# 157 entries at 6.7.10 (DERIVED, the same grep). 6.7.10 added THREE — struct_values_at_every_size_and_packed_fields_v6710
# (B7 + the ops lane), derive_codecs_share_one_classifier_v6710, instrumentation_belongs_at_the_body_start_not_in_the_prologue_v6710
# (P5-B); the rest went INTO class entries: name-based copy resolvers (the third instance, `_agc_operand`), the
# emitted-bytes class (its eighth: `_sc_pre`'s unrecorded lea), `_cfo` (the defensive zeroing), cybs (three 1-for-1
# swaps at the ceiling), the seven forks (`_cov_tail`; the Windows spawn arm's environment), counter-model (H7), the
# environment reader (the shared scratch; `_pp_env_rung`), the exit-code runner (no record = a named failure).
# 154 entries at 6.7.8 (DERIVED with `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'`; this line read 134 against a live 152 — the
# 6.7.0–6.7.7 entries never moved it). 6.7.8 added TWO (checked_dyn_trait_objects_v678,
# typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678); the rest of 6.7.8 went INTO class entries per the
# admission rule: the `_cfo` class (its seventh), the emitted-bytes class (its seventh: a raw slice base), the
# green-gate class (8: a guide that is compiled, not run; 0 NOPs read as a failure), the seven-forks class (the
# shared PREPROCESS; two spawn arms, one flag list), top level has no frame (dyn), the boolean stamp (`_PK_MARK`,
# `_PKD_MARK`), the recorded-bit entry (the slice and declared-scalar bits, the lanes' bit-62 collision, value
# bands), two passes (a refused type in both passes, pass-1 carrier records, the latching reporter), the tuples
# entry's #11 (cx r0:r4), the cybs entry (extract before you grow; the NOP compactor's caps).
# 134 entries (still 134 at 6.6.17 — cybs's dropped leading `_` and the 2048-fn seed break went INTO
# the cybs entry; the lane-isolation lessons went to methodology.cyml). 134 re-DERIVED at 6.6.16 after six: the top-level-has-no-frame CLASS (refuse / global /
# frame — never fall back), the environment reader that stops at a buffer, the bound type-parameter code
# that is not a width, scope depth vs block depth, the futex word with two kinds of waiter, and the
# deps-join traversal CLASS (the include filename-capture overflow bug/the deps-header dot-dot path bug/the deps-tag dot-dot path bug); the rest of 6.6.16 went INTO existing entries: cybs's
# seventh parameter is never stored (the cybs entry; fixed 6.7.6), a mask's width is a cap (the cap class, ninth),
# the late kernel replay of array lists + enum initializers and cx's double store (a guard in the loop,
# 2b), #deprecated in pass 1 (two passes), the overload dispatcher's borrowed classifier (shared
# helper, second instance), the per-callee checks + the pair-callee spellings (one call syntax), the
# handle operand kind (name-based copy resolvers, second instance), four TLS readings incl. CYRIUS-2026-0026
# (the TLS traps entry, now thirteen);
# 128 at 6.6.15 after two: the `secret var` zeroisation CLASS — five holes, the closure secret-var wipe gap/the tail-call secret-var wipe gap/the secret-var epilogue spill gap/the naked-fn secret-var gap and the 6.6.5 wrong-storage wipe, which had no home of their own — and the intern-search window; the rest of 6.6.15 went INTO existing entries: two TLS readings + the one-step-per-side lesson (the TLS traps entry), a shared-helper blind spot and the refusal-coverage census (the green-gate hub); 126 at 6.6.14 after the TLS-stack traps entry — the other 6.6.14 notes went INTO existing entries per the admission rule: libssl is main-thread only (the fdlopen TCB entry), three deadline-route instances (the one-copier guard), two green-gate instances, a shared-mapping re-derivation (counter-model), agnos's ACK-synchronous send (the agnos class); 125 at 6.6.13; 123 at 6.6.12 after the flags_reflect_rax entry — the other four 6.6.12 notes went INTO existing entries per the admission rule: cybs's six-argument CALL limit, the fifth `_cfo` occurrence, the sixth emitted-bytes instance, the eighth cap instance; 122 at 6.6.11 after the multi-line string attribution bug line-counter entry; at 6.6.10 bite 8 it read 119 against a live 120; first derived at v6.6.6 bite 21 review with `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'`, which is the only
# way this number should ever be written. v6.6.1 was an AUDIT that took it 158 -> 62; it then
# drifted to a live 63 while still reading 62, to 96 while 6.6.5 was in flight, and to 100
# while still reading 98 — the header was not re-derived when an entry was appended.)
#
#   ⛔ THIS FILE WAS APPEND-ONLY FOR TOO LONG — **158 entries / 5,566 lines / 461 KB**,
#   a volume nobody searches. A gotchas file you will not read is worth LESS than none:
#   it still costs a lookup and returns noise.
#
#   ⭐ THE FINDING THAT MATTERS. This compiler has a SMALL NUMBER OF RECURRING DEFECT
#   CLASSES, and this file was recording each recurrence as a fresh discovery. **59 of
#   the 158 entries were instances of 14 classes.** Quantified, because the count IS
#   the data — seven cap-sweep failures says the caps need DERIVING, not documenting:
#
#     8  a cap is declared in one place and believed in five
#     7  there are seven forks; the change landed in one of them
#     7  a stated cause or a carried number is not evidence
#     6  a syscall number on Darwin/aarch64 is not the number you wrote
#     5  identifier identity is fragile in this lexer
#     7  a green gate measuring nothing
#     4  inline replay is a prerequisite for SIMD residency
#     6  any pass over emitted bytes must know every site in the stream
#     3  prove the mutation actually reached the gate
#     3  agnos is an x86 host that is not Linux
#     3  a guard in the loop implies a second pass somebody has to write
#     2  aarch64 atomics: alignment is architectural, CAS is not a full barrier
#     2  the callptr spill slot is a real local and can alias one
#     2  install.sh copies are where the toolchain breaks
#
#   Recording the seventh instance of a class as a NEW entry hides that it IS a class —
#   and the class is the thing worth knowing.
#
#   ⚠ NOT everything with a common adjective is a class. Five entries share "silent
#   corruption with no diagnostic" (i64::MIN negation, the syntactic tail-call guard,
#   the prefix-compare P0, aggregate copy by type, the value-form Err return) and were
#   deliberately LEFT SPLIT: the mechanisms are unrelated and each is independently
#   actionable. Merging on a shared symptom would cost specificity and teach nothing.
#
#   How it got here — nothing deleted:
#     158 -> 149  · 13 merged into 4
#     149 -> 105  · 44 archived to `retros/resolved_traps.cyml`
#     105 ->  76  · 28 merged into 6, 7 more archived
#      76 ->  62  · 19 merged into 5
#   **62 live traps + 51 archived = 113 distinct entries preserved**, with 59 originals
#   consolidated into 14 class entries that keep every instance as evidence.
#
#   Also fixed: ~500 lines of v5.11.x BACKSTORY compressed to trap-plus-pointer
#   (`retros/v511x.cyml` already held it); 1 entry FACTUALLY WRONG IN ITS OWN NAME
#   (asserted the reserved-token count is "67 not 51"; live count is 76); 1 whose flat
#   claim had become FALSE (`cyrius has no inliner` — `#inline` shipped v6.5.63); 1
#   pre-existing syntax defect; 12 dead test paths.
#
#   ⭐ ONE ENTRY BECAME A REPAIR ISSUE — the double-assigned token types 79/111, a defect
#   still live that nobody had filed
#   (`docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-08-lexer-token-types-79-and-111-double-assigned.md`).
#   **A gotcha documenting a defect nobody intends to fix is a bug report nobody filed.**
#
#   ⭐ THE ADMISSION RULE, because this file regrows otherwise:
#     · TRAP or CHANGE RECORD? "v6.4.51 raised output_buf to 1 GiB" is not a gotcha.
#     · An INSTANCE of a class above? Add it as evidence INSIDE that entry and increment
#       its count. Do not open a new one. The class is the lesson; the instance is proof.
#     · Over ~40 lines? Narrative to `retros/`; only the TRAP stays here.
#     · Now impossible (hard-errored, gated, path removed)? -> `resolved_traps.cyml`.
#     · A live defect nobody intends to fix? FILE IT.
#
#   Net: **158 -> 62 entries, 5,566 -> 1983 lines, 461 KB -> 164 KB.**
#
#   Live state at 6.6.1: cycc x86_64 **1,247,608 B** (.text 1,090,832) · seed **29,024 B**
#   · check.sh **240 passed / 0 failed** · **144** shell gates · **301** .tcyr (68 crossos)
#   · **102** lib/*.cyr · api-surface **5152** · heap map **102** regions · self_compile
#   **731-734 ms** · **1** open issue · cross-OS SELFHOST_OK on ecb/ach/cass/pi.
#
[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error"
description = "UPDATE v6.3.2 — the default FLIPPED: a REACHABLE call to an undefined fn is now a hard error by default (`error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)`, exit 1, no output); the ud2 stub below applies only under `--allow-undef` or to a dead-host (DCE-unreachable) call. Pre-v6.3.2: cyrius treated an unresolved FUNCTION call and an unresolved CONSTANT/enum-value asymmetrically: a call to a function defined nowhere compiles to a `ud2;ud2;nop` (SIGILL) stub at the call site + a `warning: undefined function` and STILL exits 0 with a valid binary (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr overwrites the `e8` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` when the fn target is the -1/-2 undef sentinel — deliberate, so it's a loud localisable SIGILL not a jump into low memory); but a reference to an undefined constant/enum value is a HARD compile error (`undefined variable '…'`). CYRIUS_STRICT=1 does NOT promote the fn warning. Consequence: a stdlib fn that resolves to nothing on some target ships GREEN and traps only at runtime. Surfaced v6.2.35 on agnos (lib/sync.cyr had no agnos mutex backend → patra's mutex_* were ud2 stubs → patra_init SIGILL), and explains why the M6-chain issue's 'add inert SYS_FUTEX constants' fix was both unnecessary AND a misdiagnosis (patra ≤1.11.x referenced SYS_FUTEX as a raw CONSTANT → real hard error; 1.12.x uses mutex_lock() a FUNCTION → silent ud2 → no error)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "fixup", "ud2", "undefined-function", "undefined-constant", "agnos", "cross-target", "silent-failure", "v6.2.35"]
content = """
**UPDATE v6.3.2 (2026-06-28) — the default FLIPPED.** A *reachable* undefined-fn call is now a
**HARD ERROR by default** (`error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)`;
exit 1, no output). The check in `src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/fixup.cyr` gates on a new `_allow_undef`
global (default 0 = error) instead of `_strict_mode`. `--allow-undef` (CLI) / `--al` downgrades back
to the warn+ud2+exit-0 behavior described below. **Dead-host (DCE-unreachable) refs STAY
warn+ud2+exit-0** (filtered out of the reachable count). So the asymmetry table below now describes
(a) any undef under `--allow-undef`, or (b) a genuinely dead-host ref — NOT the default reachable
case, which is now symmetric with the constant case (both hard-error). The flip's repo-wide blast
radius (forks, cross-arch builds, aspirational tools — not just the tcyr corpus) was cleaned with
ZERO `--allow-undef` via stubs/guards; see project memory `project_v632_undef_hard_error`.

**The asymmetry** (pre-v6.3.2 default; now the `--allow-undef` / dead-host case):

| unresolved symbol | cycc behavior |
|---|---|
| **function call** | `warning: undefined function 'f'` + emit `ud2;ud2;nop` at the call site; **exit 0, valid binary** |
| **constant / enum value** | `error: undefined variable 'X'`; **exit 1, no output** |

So `return f(x)` where `f` is defined nowhere COMPILES (and SIGILLs at
runtime if reached); `return X` where `X` is an undefined enum value does
NOT. `CYRIUS_STRICT=1` does not change the function case.

**Why** (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr ~170-194): for a function-call fixup
whose target is the undef sentinel (<0), cycc overwrites the 5-byte
`e8 XX XX XX XX` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` = `ud2; ud2; nop`.
Intentional — a SIGILL at the exact call site beats the disp32 resolving
to an arbitrary low address (the sandhi M2 2026-04-24 jump-into-entry bug).
Runtime confirmation: such a binary exits 132 (128+SIGILL) at the call.

**Where it bit (v6.2.35, the M6 persistence chain on agnos):**
`lib/sync.cyr` defined `mutex_new/lock/unlock` only under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` — no agnos branch. patra includes
`sync.cyr` (not `thread.cyr`/`thread_agnos.cyr`), so on `--agnos` those
calls were undefined → ud2 stubs, compiling GREEN. `patra_init()` calls
`mutex_new()` UNCONDITIONALLY, so patra_init itself SIGILLs at runtime on
agnos. The filed fix ("add inert `SYS_FUTEX`/`FUTEX_*` to the agnos peer")
was a misdiagnosis: those constants are only referenced by stale patra
≤1.11.x's `_patra_lock`, which used `SYS_FUTEX` as a RAW CONSTANT (→ hard
error → that's the error the reporter actually saw). patra 1.12.x moved to
`mutex_lock()` (a FUNCTION → silent ud2), so once 1.12.3 is folded nothing
references the constants. Real fix: an agnos `#ifdef` no-op mutex backend in
sync.cyr (single-core cooperative-iron — no contender, lock is a no-op).

**Recurring class — `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`-only stdlib helpers / raw
Linux syscall numbers.** This is the same shape, hit THREE times now:
v6.2.33 (io.cyr's `xopen`/`xstat`/… substrate), v6.2.35 (sync.cyr mutex), and
**v6.2.36 (io.cyr's `file_lock`/`file_unlock`/`file_trylock`/`file_lock_shared`/
`file_append_locked` — Linux-only + raw `syscall(73)`, so undefined `ud2` stubs
on agnos+macOS and the wrong number on aarch64; descent's `persist_init` →
libro FileStore → `file_append_locked` trapped at BOOT).** The fix pattern is
always the same: **route through the portable `x*` wrapper** that centralizes
the per-target syscall number (`xflock` → #59 agnos / #92 macOS / #73 x86 / #32
aarch64), and where a target lacks a feature the others have, branch it (agnos
has no kernel `AO_APPEND` yet → `xlseek` SEEK_END under the lock instead of
`O_APPEND`). PREVENTION: periodically grep `lib/` for `#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` blocks that define `fn` (without sibling agnos/macОS
branches) and for raw `syscall(<small Linux num>)` outside the peer files —
each is a latent off-Linux `ud2`.

**How to recognize / guard it:**
- A consumer "compiles clean for `--agnos`" (or any target) but crashes
  early at runtime → grep the build stderr for `warning: undefined function`.
  Those are live landmines, not noise.
- A regression GATE for this class must NOT just check `exit 0` — it must
  assert the symbols are DEFINED (compile a probe that references them and
  FAIL if stderr contains `undefined function '<name>`). See
  scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh probe 1f.
- Mirror of the macОS-rot placebo: self-host + check.sh never exercise
  agnos codegen, so undefined-fn stubs ship "found by ports."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "element_typed_array_slot_spelling_v621"
description = "Bare `var a[N]` is byte-sized in a fn / slot-sized at top level — an accidental divergence that silently corrupts any address-taken local written with the `store64(&a + i*8)` slot idiom (the daimon route-404 class). v6.2.1 adds `var a: T[N]` (N elements of T) as the unambiguous slot/typed spelling; the audit found the class live in cycc itself (PARSE_SWITCH/PARSE_MATCH jump tables) + stdlib + folded libs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "var-array", "byte-vs-slot", "0x12A000", "daimon", "v6.2.1"]
content = """
**The trap**: the SAME syntax `var a[N]` reserves different sizes by
scope. In a function (`PARSE_ARRAY`, parse_decl.cyr) it's `(N+7)&~7`
**bytes**; at top level (`PARSE_GVAR_ARR`) it's `N*8` (N i64 **slots**).
Both register into the one var-size table at `0x12A000`. A
function-local slot array written with `store64(&a + i*8, …)` therefore
runs off its byte-sized backing — `var parts[4]` is 8 bytes (1 slot),
so `store64(&parts + 24, …)` corrupts the next static object. Layout-
sensitive, so it stays dormant until an unrelated change shifts the
layout (daimon: deleting dead code moved `parts` 24 B before sandhi's
`" "` literal → every HTTP route 404'd).

**The fix (v6.2.1)**: `var a: T[N]` = N elements of T, sized
`N*sizeof(T)` identically in BOTH scopes. `i64[N]` = N slots (the slot
spelling), `u8[N]` = N bytes, widths i8..i64/u8..u64/u128. Pure
**frontend** change — the element width threads through `scalar_type`
(in-fn, `PARSE_ARRAY(S,noff,ew)`) / `ann_scalar` (top-level,
`PARSE_GVAR_ARR(S,noff,sti,ew)`) into the `0x12A000` byte size, and
every backend (x86/aarch64/macho/pe/cx) inherits it by SUMMING that
table — no backend edits. Bare `var a[N]` keeps its legacy per-scope
meaning (best reserved for byte buffers).

**Two-step bootstrap REQUIRED when migrating cycc's OWN arrays**:
because the OLD build/cycc byte-sizes the new `i64[N]` annotation,
`build/cycc → stage_a` gives stage_a byte-sized internal tables;
`stage_a → stage_b` (stage_a has the feature) gives slot-sized tables.
So `stage_a != stage_b`; the fixpoint is `stage_b == stage_c`. cycc's
own source had no >32-arm switch/match, so the under-sized `ends[256]`
(32 slots) self-hosted by luck for years — the classic latent-until-
layout-shift signature.

**Audit lesson**: run the audit against the lib SOURCE repos, not the
vendored fold — the fold-copy scan missed sigil `snp_report_verify`'s
`parsed[4]` that the source check caught (same `memset(&x,0,32)`-on-an-
8-byte-buffer bug as its sibling `inters[4]`). The ecosystem libs
(sigil/sakshi/agnosys) are the language's own stdlibs: patch each
lib's source, hold for release, then re-fold — never hand-edit the
`# Do not edit` vendored copy.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack"
description = "A large function-local `var buf[N]` in cycc is allocated in `.bss` static storage, NOT the stack frame — and a `var[]` sitting in a statically-dead region (e.g. after a `#ifdef`-gated early `return`) STILL reserves its `.bss` even on the path that never reaches it. Diagnose via section deltas, not `sub rsp` scanning."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "bss", "var-array", "agnos", "stack", "v6.1.12"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/io.cyr` `getenv()` had an 8 KB `var buf[8192]`
*after* a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` early `return`. The agnos
build never touches `buf`, but the agnoshi consumer #PF'd and the
filed issue blamed an "8 KB stack frame reserved past the early
return." Plausible — but WRONG about the mechanism.

**What actually happens**: in cycc a function-local `var[]` of
this size is emitted into **`.bss`** (static storage), not as a
stack-frame allocation. Verified on the real `--agnos` build by
diffing section sizes before/after guarding the buffer behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`:

  .bss   0x2f18 -> 0xf08   (-8208 B = buf + align)
  .text  -928 B            (the now-dead environ reader)
  max stack frame: unchanged at 0xd0 — no `sub rsp, 0x2000`
  ever existed.

So scanning the disassembly for a big `sub rsp` finds nothing and
can make you think "the buffer isn't there" — it IS, just in
`.bss`. Always check `llvm-objdump -h` section deltas, not just
prologue `sub rsp`, when chasing a "phantom buffer" on a
constrained target.

**Why it still faulted on agnos** (mechanism the issue got wrong):
an oversized `.bss` grows the PT_LOAD memsz the loader must
zero-map; the agnos #PF is `.bss`-image / loader-mapping related,
NOT init-stack overflow. A "widen the ring-3 init stack" fix
aims a layer off.

**The fix**: guard the whole reader (buffer + parse loop) behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` so the dead region contributes
nothing to the agnos image. The broader compiler-side want —
elide storage for a `var[]` that's provably never live on the
taken path — stays a (low-priority) follow-on; the preprocessor
guard fully resolves the case. Related: the v6.0.79 `cyml.cyr`
oversized-`var[]`-on-constrained-target overflow.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open"
description = "libssl's OPENSSL_init_ssl uses a recursive pthread mutex whose __kind field lives in the glibc TCB. cyrius's dynlib_bootstrap_tls zeroed TCB reads __kind=0 (non-recursive); same-thread re-entry deadlocks on futex. Use lib/fdlopen.cyr (foreign-dlopen) instead. The same TCB rule makes libssl MAIN-THREAD ONLY: a cyrius worker's thread pointer is cyrius's own block, so lib/tls.cyr refuses every libssl call off the main thread since 6.6.14"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["dynlib", "tls-loader", "fdlopen", "libssl", "threads", "glibc-tcb", "v5.6.37", "v6.6.14"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius has two ways to load shared libraries at
runtime:

1. `lib/dynlib.cyr` — pure-cyrius ELF .so loader via mmap +
   GNU hash table walk. No libc, no glibc dlopen. Bypasses
   glibc's TLS/TCB setup entirely.

2. `lib/fdlopen.cyr` — foreign-dlopen wrapper. Calls into
   glibc's actual dlopen via the AT_BASE+offset trick, which
   means the loaded library gets glibc's full TLS/TCB setup
   including pthread_*.

For libssl specifically, dynlib_open works for getting symbol
addresses — but calling `OPENSSL_init_ssl()` deadlocks on a
futex inside libcrypto's pthread_mutex_lock. Why: libssl's
init uses `PTHREAD_RECURSIVE_MUTEX_INITIALIZER_NP`. The mutex
struct has a `__kind` field in glibc's TCB. cyrius's
dynlib_bootstrap_tls (the minimal TLS setup that dynlib_open
provides) zeros the TCB. `__kind == 0` means "non-recursive"
in pthread's encoding. Same-thread re-entry to a mutex that
the same thread already holds → futex wait forever (because
non-recursive).

**Rule**: if a library's init function uses pthread mutexes
internally, you NEED fdlopen (real glibc dlopen → real glibc
TLS/TCB), not dynlib (cyrius minimal loader). The list of
libraries that hit this includes:

- libssl (OPENSSL_init_ssl)
- libpthread (everything)
- libgomp (OpenMP runtime)
- libc-internal locking primitives (rare in pure cyrius use,
  but anything that touches TLS-cancellation hooks)

Conversely, dynlib is fine for:

- Pure C libraries with no pthread internals (libz, libcrypto's
  primitive routines if you skip OPENSSL_init_*)
- Libraries you call only for symbol addresses (dlsym then
  call manually)

**Recognize again**: any "dlopen succeeds, dlsym returns valid
pointer, calling that pointer hangs forever" shape on a
shared library that uses pthread mutexes.

**The same rule, one thread over: libssl is MAIN-THREAD ONLY (6.6.14).**
fdlopen gives glibc a real thread block on the thread that bootstrapped
it. A `thread_create` worker is a raw `CLONE_THREAD` clone whose thread
pointer holds cyrius's OWN thread-local block, so libssl called from it
reads garbage through `%fs`: measured on 6.6.13, `tls_available()` on main
then one fetch on a worker was a SIGSEGV, and a worker's cold first use
hung. Since 6.6.14 every libssl arm of lib/tls.cyr checks
`gettid() == getpid()` first and refuses without touching libssl
(`TLS_ERR_WRONG_THREAD` -23, 0 or -1 — language/stdlib_modules.cyml
`tls_module_contract_v6614`). Read both FRESH, never a cached pid: a
forked child inherits the cache, and once the parent dies its pid can be
recycled as a worker's tid. Anything else brought in through fdlopen has
the same limit.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment"
description = "SysV ABI requires rsp 16-aligned at every CALL (so callee sees rsp+8 16-aligned at entry). Cyrius's ECALLPOPS for n>6 args used `add rsp, 48` unconditionally; for odd nextra (N=7,9,11) this left rsp 8-aligned at CALL, propagating misalignment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "sysv", "alignment", "v5.6.41"]
content = """
**The trap**: SysV AMD64 ABI requires rsp 16-byte aligned at
every CALL instruction (so the callee sees rsp+8 16-byte
aligned at entry, with the saved rip below it). Cyrius's
codegen for fn calls with >6 args uses ECALLPOPS to clean up
stack arguments after the call:

```cyr
# n > 6 args, n_extra = n - 6
# Push n_extra extra args
# call helper
# add rsp, n_extra * 8  ← cleanup
```

Pre-v5.6.41, the cleanup was `add rsp, n_extra * 8`
unconditionally. For odd `n_extra` (N = 7, 9, 11 args), this
leaves rsp 8-byte-aligned at the next CALL. Misalignment
propagates downstream until something does SSE on a stack-
saved value (libssl/libc prologs commonly do `movdqa xmm,
[rsp+N]`) — SIGSEGV at first instruction.

**Fix**: align rsp to 16 BEFORE pushing extra args:

```cyr
if ((n_extra & 1) == 1) {
    # Pre-pad rsp by 8 to absorb odd n_extra count
    push rax  # any value; we sub rsp, 8 effectively
}
push n_extra args
call helper
add rsp, (n_extra + (n_extra_odd ? 1 : 0)) * 8
```

⛔ **6.6.5 GENERALISES THIS AND RETRACTS ITS LAST LINE.** Two
corrections, both measured:

* The parity is `(n_extra + DEPTH) & 1`, not `n_extra & 1`.
  `n_extra` alone assumes the call is emitted with nothing else
  pending on the expression stack — true between statements,
  false inside an expression, where every enclosing operator and
  argument has pushed a value. At depth 0 the new rule reduces to
  exactly the old test, which is why statement-level 7-arg calls
  are byte-identical across 6.6.5.
* **"Win64 path was already correct" was wrong twice.** Its
  `framesize & 15` branch only rounds the frame; the parity pad
  beside it (`n & 1`) was equally depth-blind. And underneath
  both, the PE BASE was inverted — Windows enters an image with
  the return address pushed (rsp ≡ 8) and cyrius never
  re-aligned, so on Windows the STATEMENT-level calls were the
  misaligned ones.

See `nested_call_alignment_stack_machine` below for the whole
story.

**Recognize again**: any "SIGSEGV on the first SSE instruction
of a libc / libssl entry" shape. Common triggers: 7-arg fn
calls, OpenSSL's EVP_DecryptInit_ex (8 args), recvmmsg
(7 args). Always check rsp alignment via `gdb p $rsp & 0xF`
at the failing instruction.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment_violation`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions"
description = "Cyrius requires top-level `var X` to be declared before any fn body that references X. Applies to programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr's symbol-table backing buffers too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "lsp", "global-init-order", "v5.7.39"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's parser does a single-pass top-down
walk of source. When a fn body references a top-level
`var X`, that `var X` must already be declared at parse time.
Forward fn calls work (the parser pre-scans fn signatures);
forward GLOBAL VAR references DO NOT work.

This is the v5.7.32 cyrlint warning class
(`global-init-order forward-ref`) that mabda surfaced. The
compiler ALSO doesn't catch every shape — the warning is
opt-in via `cyrius lint`.

**The cyrius-lsp specific instance** (v5.7.39): the LSP
extension added cross-file go-to-def + documentSymbol via a
symbol-table backing buffer. The buffer was a top-level `var
symbol_table[N]`. The fns that read it (`SYMBOL_LOOKUP`,
`SYMBOL_INSERT`) were defined before the `var` line in the
source layout.

Pre-fix: the parser saw the fn defs first, registered them,
parsed their bodies → reached `symbol_table` reference →
"undefined variable." Post-fix: moved `var symbol_table[N]`
above all fn defs that reference it.

**Recognize again**: any "undefined variable" error on a top-
level var that VISUALLY appears in the source somewhere.
Check it appears BEFORE the fns that read it.

**Generalize**: any new top-level `var X` declared in
cyrius's own source (or any cyrius project) MUST go above the
fn bodies that reference it. The parser is single-pass for
globals.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions_v5_7_39`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption"
description = "RETRACTED v6.6.4: the lib-edit snapshot-refresh recipe corrupted released install slots; its copy-back premise expired at v5.11.17"
category = "cyrius"
---
Surfaced 2026-05-03 mid-v5.8.23 bite #2 when `lib/tagged.cyr`
edits reverted between Edit calls during the sum-type migration.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

Two-step ping-pong loop:

  1. `version-bump.sh` runs `install.sh --refresh-only` which
     copies `lib/*.cyr` from the repo INTO
     `~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/lib/` and `~/.cyrius/lib`.
  2. Subsequent `cyrius deps` resolution (e.g., during
     `check.sh`) copies the snapshot version BACK into the
     repo.

If you edit a `lib/*.cyr` file AFTER a `version-bump.sh` ran
(snapshot is now stale), the next `check.sh` invocation will
overwrite your edit with the prior snapshot.

== SYMPTOM ==

You make an Edit. You verify the change. You run `check.sh`.
Tests pass. But the next time you read the file, your edit is
GONE. cycc is also gone — the snapshot's version was pulled back
in along with the lib file, AND the snapshot's lib is what's
actually in `~/.cyrius/lib`.

== MITIGATION — RETRACTED (v6.6.4) ==

The 3-step recipe that stood here (copy the edited `lib/<file>.cyr`
into `~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/lib/`, or run the
same-version `version-bump.sh`) is WITHDRAWN. Two things were
wrong with it:

  1. Its premise expired. Since v5.11.17 `cyrius deps` in the
     cyrius source repo resolves `./lib` directly
     (`_dep_find_stdlib_dir` branch (a)), and since v6.4.77
     `cmd_lib_sync` refuses to run in the source repo — so
     nothing copies a snapshot BACK over a repo edit any more.
     The loop this entry describes cannot occur at HEAD.
  2. The write it prescribes is a CORRUPTION. Between a tag and
     the next bump, `$(cat VERSION)` still names the RELEASED
     version, so the copy lands in the released slot — the thing
     every consumer pin resolves. That is exactly how the
     installed "6.6.2" stdlib became 6.6.3's (12 files; one file's
     08:49 mtime on 2026-09-12 was attested, the rest inferred) and "6.6.1" gained three
     6.6.2 files. hisab filed it after a `cyrius build` under an
     unchanged pin silently re-vendored and re-locked the
     mutated files.

What replaced it: `install.sh --refresh-only`, `cyrius pulsar`
and `cyrius lsp` REFUSE to write a released slot from a drifted
tree (CYRIUS_REFRESH_RELEASED=1 for throwaway homes only);
`check.sh` stages its own throwaway CYRIUS_HOME from the tree,
so mid-slot lib/cbt changes are tested without touching the
store; every refresh stamps `SOURCE_COMMIT`; and
`scripts/verify-store.sh` (with `--restore <v>`) compares every
tagged slot against its tag. Re-cutting a version at a new
commit is `git tag -f <v> HEAD` FIRST, then refresh.

THE GENERAL LESSON, stronger than the specific one: a mitigation
recipe outlives its premise silently. This entry told agents to
perform a write for seven minors after the loop it guarded against
had been closed, and the write became the defect. When a recipe
says "copy X into the live store", check whether the tool has
since learned to read X itself.
[[entries]]
name = "foldin_symlink_vs_regular_file"
description = "When folding a sibling distfile into cyrius's lib/ via the sandhi pattern, the vendored file MUST be a regular file (mode 100644), not the absolute-path symlink that `cyrius deps` creates by default. Symlinks point into the user's local cache (/home/<user>/.cyrius/...) and dangle on any other machine — including CI."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "deps", "ci", "sandhi-pattern", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius deps` resolves a `[deps.<name>]` entry by
populating `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>/<tag>/` and then creating
`lib/<name>.cyr` as a SYMLINK to that cache path:
```
lib/sakshi.cyr -> /home/<user>/.cyrius/deps/sakshi/2.2.3/dist/sakshi.cyr
```
The symlink target is an ABSOLUTE PATH in the resolving user's
home. On the user's local machine this works because the cache
is populated. On any OTHER machine — including GitHub Actions
CI runners (whose home is `/home/runner/...`) — the symlink
dangles.

When folding a dep into stdlib (sandhi pattern), naive `git
add lib/<name>.cyr` commits the symlink. Git records mode 120000
(symlink). On checkout elsewhere, the symlink resolves against
a non-existent path → file effectively missing.

**Surfaces as**: tcyrs that `include "lib/<name>.cyr"` fail to
compile with "file not found" → cycc exits non-zero → under
`bash -e` (GHA's default shell mode), the test loop terminates
silently mid-loop. No `FAIL:` print, no summary, just exit 1.
Easy to misdiagnose as "test crashed the runner."

**The fix at v5.8.65** (the slot that surfaced this):
1. Convert the symlink to a regular file:
   ```
   cp --remove-destination "$(readlink lib/<name>.cyr)" lib/<name>.cyr
   ```
   `--remove-destination` is essential — `cp` without it follows
   the symlink and writes through to the cache, not the repo.
2. Commit the type change. `git diff` shows mode 120000 → 100644.
3. The cyrius distlib walker (`scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`) skips
   files matching `^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$` in the first
   5 lines — those byte-snapshot bundles must not be reformatted.
   See also `distlib_marker_styles_v5865`.

**Recognize again**: any time you fold a `[deps.X]` into `lib/`
under the sandhi pattern, audit `git ls-tree HEAD lib/<X>.cyr`
for mode 120000 BEFORE pushing. The v5.7.0 sandhi precedent
(`lib/sandhi.cyr`) is the canonical example — mode 100644,
never a symlink.

**Why it wasn't caught locally**: the user's local machine
HAD the symlink target populated. `check.sh` self-host green,
all 127 tcyrs pass via `cyrius test`. CI is the first place
the absolute path doesn't resolve.

The fix is one shell loop, but the diagnosis took multiple
re-pushes (audit-walk marker fix, then ci.yml inline marker
fan-out, then sakshi-specific bundle.sh marker style). Saved
here so the next foldin slot starts with the de-symlink step
in the playbook.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "preprocess_out_vs_str_data_caps"
description = "Two distinct heap caps gate compilation. str_data caps the bytes the COMPILER writes for tracked string literals (raised to 2 MB at v5.8.59). preprocess_out caps the bytes of EXPANDED SOURCE the preprocessor produces from include resolution (still 2 MB since v5.6.40). Hitting one isn't the same as hitting the other, and the workarounds are different."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "preprocessor", "str_data", "v5.8.60"]
content = """
**The trap**: at v5.8.60 (NFKC/NFKD), the original design
used 116-char fixed-width compat-decomp records. 3833 records
× 116 chars = ~445 KB hex-encoded source — well under the
v5.8.59-bumped str_data 2 MB cap. Compiled fine in isolation.

But every consumer tcyr that includes lib/unicode/normalize.cyr
(transitively pulling in _normalize_data.cyr's ~445 KB blob)
also includes the rest of stdlib via auto-prepend. The
*expanded source* — the post-preprocessor concatenation of
all includes — overflowed `preprocess_out` (the 2 MB buffer at
S+0x44A000):
```
error: expanded source exceeds 2MB (2114828 bytes)
```
str_data has plenty of room. preprocess_out is the wall.

**These are two DIFFERENT walls**:
- `str_data` (`0x21A000`, 2 MB at v5.8.61): bytes the compiler
  writes for tracked string literals during codegen. Cap
  applies per-translation-unit.
- `preprocess_out` (`0x44A000`, 2 MB since v5.6.40): bytes of
  the source after include resolution. Cap applies BEFORE the
  lexer even runs.

A consumer that auto-prepends 14 stdlib modules + 6 sibling
distlib modules can sit at 1.6 MB expanded source even before
tcyr-specific code. Adding a 445 KB data table easily blows
the cap.

**The fix at v5.8.60**: switch the data-table encoding from
fixed-width 116-char records (445 KB total) to a 2-table
split — sorted IDX (12 chars/record × 3833 = 46 KB) +
variable-width DATA blob (count + cps, ~41 KB). Total ~87 KB,
80% reduction. Same lookup semantics (binary search IDX → data
offset → read count + cps), but consumer-side preprocess_out
stays well under cap.

**Recognize again**: any new fold-target that adds large
data blobs to lib/ should be sized against BOTH caps:
- Single-file size vs 64 KB string-literal cap (token
  encoding's u16 length field).
- Aggregate across ALL auto-prepended stdlib + the consumer's
  own source vs preprocess_out 2 MB cap.

The 2 MB preprocess_out cap is the easier one to forget
because it's not in the compiler's diagnostic surface during
single-file edits — only manifests when consumers compile.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149"
description = "OVMF firmware treats COFF Characteristics RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001) as 'load at exact ImageBase or fail'. UEFI Application binaries must NEVER set this bit, even with zero actual fixups. Standard UEFI loaders (rEFInd, systemd-boot, GRUB-EFI) all clear it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "uefi", "ovmf", "efi-application", "relocs-stripped", "coff-characteristics", "v5.11.49", "gnoboot", "silent-rejection"]
---
**THE TRAP.** OVMF firmware treats the COFF Characteristics bit
`RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001)` as *"load at exact ImageBase or fail"*. A UEFI
Application must **NEVER** set it — even when the image genuinely has zero fixups
and setting it looks accurate. Every standard loader (rEFInd, systemd-boot,
GRUB-EFI) clears it.

**RECOGNIZE AGAIN**: a flag that is *descriptively true* about your output but is
read by the consumer as a *demand*. The structural gate passed — Subsystem 0xA,
NX_COMPAT, Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed, a valid `.text` — because it checked what the
bits SAY, not what a firmware DOES with them. Boot it.

⟳ Full narrative in `retros/v511x.cyml`.
[[entries]]
name = "portable_sleep_no_raw_nanosleep_off_linux"
description = "Raw syscall(35) nanosleep faults off Linux: Darwin has NO plain nanosleep BSD syscall (and on aarch64 macho, 35 COLLIDES with unlinkat), and PE never routed 35. Portable sleep = poll(NULL,0,ms) on Linux+macOS (poll 7→230 is routed on both Mach-O backends) + a kernel32 Sleep reroute on Windows. WaitForSingleObject on a process/thread pseudo-handle does NOT block as a sleep."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "windows", "syscall", "nanosleep", "poll", "sleep", "esysxlat", "v6.0.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `syscall(35, &timespec, 0)` (Linux nanosleep) in a
stdlib/consumer sleep helper works on Linux and faults or
misbehaves on every other target:
- **macOS**: XNU has no plain `nanosleep` BSD syscall (libsystem
  builds it over semaphore primitives). Worse, on aarch64-macho
  the number 35 COLLIDES — ESYSXLAT maps aarch64 `35` to
  `unlinkat`→`unlink`, so `syscall(35, ts, 0)` tries to unlink a
  garbage path. (The x86-nanosleep-35 vs aarch64-unlinkat-35
  clash is the same multiply-defined-number swamp as the .63
  getdents 61-vs-217 bug.)
- **Windows (PE)**: 35 is not a routed reroute → fault.

**The portable fix** (v6.0.65, `lib/chrono.cyr` `sleep_ms`):
- Linux + macOS: `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` — `poll` (x86 7 → Darwin 230)
  is ALREADY routed on both Mach-O backends (the .59 socket port),
  and `poll` with 0 fds + a timeout is the portable ms-granular
  sleep. Verified ≈ms-accurate on Linux + ecb + ach (the constant
  ~300ms "overhead" the first time on macOS is one-shot
  codesign/AMFI launch, not poll — baseline-subtract to confirm).
- Windows: a real kernel32 `Sleep(ms)` reroute (0xF00F), 1-arg,
  mirroring the SRWLock reroutes.
- agnos/cx: no-op (no sleep syscall / single-threaded VM).

**Windows dead-end worth knowing**: `WaitForSingleObject(handle,
ms)` on the current-PROCESS (-1) or current-THREAD (-2)
pseudo-handle returns IMMEDIATELY — it does NOT block for the
timeout (verified on cass: 0 ms). It only blocks on a genuinely
non-signaled object. So there's no "sleep via the already-routed
WaitForSingleObject" shortcut; you need a real `Sleep` reroute.

**Measurement gotcha (cmd.exe)**: `cmd /c "echo %TIME% & prog &
echo %TIME%"` prints the SAME time twice — `%TIME%` expands at
parse time (once), the `%errorlevel%` gotcha applied to TIME. Use
`cmd /v:on /c "... !TIME! ..."` or PowerShell `Measure-Command`.

**Recognize again**: any consumer doing raw `syscall(35)` for a
sleep on macOS/Windows; or a new "syscall not routed on Mach-O"
warning naming 35. The fix is always: route through
`lib/chrono.cyr sleep_ms`, never hardcode nanosleep.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macos_owns_thread_pointer_register"
description = "macOS owns TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) — Darwin saves+restores it as thread state and overwrites any userspace `msr TPIDR_EL0` / arch_prctl on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius cannot durably own the thread-pointer register for thread-local storage on macOS."
detail = """
v6.0.84. Symptom: sigil's crypto-scratch bank (`cbank()` → `thread_local_get`,
lib/thread_local.cyr) SIGSEGV'd on real ecb (Apple Silicon) AFTER an ECDSA
sign — but every ingredient (sha256, hmac, fl_alloc, the secret vars, the
rfc6979 reject loop) ran clean in isolation. The .76 issue chased it to a
`&_fl_heads` freelist codegen bug via an early lldb; THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG —
the `&global` adrp+add path is provably correct (EVADDR emits a fresh
adrp+add per site, FIXUP_ADRP_ADD masks 0x9F00001F so it can't corrupt the
opcode, var-address resolution is deterministic per index).

Real cause: thread_local.cyr installs a per-thread block via `msr TPIDR_EL0`
(arm64) and reads it back via `mrs TPIDR_EL0`. On Linux the kernel lets EL0
own TPIDR_EL0. On Darwin the kernel uses it for its own thread self-data and
RESTORES it on every context switch. Decisive repro on ecb: install the
block (TPIDR=0x1xxxxxxxx), run a 2-billion-iteration pure-compute loop with
ZERO syscalls, read TPIDR again → 0x200d (a Darwin thread value). Pure
preemption, no syscall, clobbers it. `cbank()` lazy-inits ONCE
(`_crypto_tls_inited` guard), so the first preemption during crypto poisons
every later `thread_local_get`. This is why it looked "context-dependent,
after a preceding svc" — it's preemption, and a long crypto op always gets
preempted.

How to recognize: lldb shows the fault at `ldr xN, [x1, x0, lsl #3]` right
after `mrs x1, TPIDR_EL0`, with x1 a tiny value (~0x2010), NOT a heap addr.

Fix: a macOS process-global slot array (no TPIDR), like the Windows TlsAlloc
path. Safe because cyrius threads don't run on macOS at all — thread.cyr is
clone(2)-only (no Darwin bsdthread path) — so crypto-scratch is
single-threaded. Since preemption (not a syscall) is the trigger, NO
"re-msr before access" scheme is reliable; you must not use the register.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_fmt_macho_before_pe_precedence"
description = "When deriving an output-format selector (`_emit_fmt`) from the _TARGET_* flags, test Mach-O BEFORE PE — matching the pre-existing EMITELF ladder's order. _TARGET_MACHO and _TARGET_PE come from INDEPENDENT, unguarded env vars (CYRIUS_MACHO / CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN; main.cyr has no mutual-exclusion guard), so the combined state is reachable. The old code let Mach-O win; flip the order and you silently change the conflicting-combo output (Mach-O → PE)."
detail = """
v6.0.89 (the _TARGET_* consolidation first bite: collapse the format fan-out +
entry-base ladder, duplicated in BOTH x86/fixup.cyr and aarch64/fixup.cyr, into
_emit_fmt(S)→{0=ELF,1=Mach-O-x86,2=Mach-O-ARM,3=PE} + _entry_base(S)).

An adversarial-review workflow caught a DRAFT _emit_fmt that checked `_TARGET_PE==1`
first: for `CYRIUS_MACHO=1 CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1` the old EMITELF (macho-first) emitted
Mach-O 8192B, the draft emitted PE 1536B — a real divergence, not a false alarm,
even though no sane build sets both. Fix: `if(_TARGET_MACHO==1)…;if(_TARGET_MACHO==2)
…;if(_TARGET_PE==1)…` so Mach-O wins, byte-identical for that combo too.

VERIFICATION SHAPE (this is the most fixup-fragile code — entry feeds every absolute
fixup): build .PREV-vs-.NEW compilers and diff EMITTED binaries across EVERY mode —
ELF-user, kernel ELF32+ELF64, shared, object, EFI (real source), AGNOS, PE, Mach-O
x86, Mach-O ARM, AND the Mach-O+PE conflict. A single-format self-host run only
exercises ELF; the macho/pe/kernel paths need the explicit matrix + ecb/ach/cass.
TEST-HARNESS TRAP that wasted a cycle: zsh does NOT word-split an unquoted `$var`, so
`env $combo sh -c …` with combo="A=1 B=1" set ONE malformed var and silently tested
the no-flags case — the output's ELF magic (not Mach-O) gave it away. Use literal
`env A=1 B=1 …`, and ALWAYS check the actual output bytes, not just the exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "per_fn_local_tables_indexed_by_slot_not_var"
description = "The four per-fn local tables (fn_local_names / fn_local_depths / fn_local_types / fn_local_slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame SLOTS, NOT vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 filler slots — so any frame larger than ~2 KB silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables (the locals-cap bug). v6.1.40 relocated all four to the heap top at 16384 slots each + added a loud SFLC cap."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "local-tables", "sflc", "heap-cap", "overflow", "cve-24", "v6.1.40"]
content = """
**The trap (the locals-cap bug)**: cyrius's four per-function local tables —
`fn_local_names`, `fn_local_depths`, `fn_local_types`,
`fn_local_slice` — are indexed by `SFLC` (the stack-frame local
count). `SFLC` counts stack-frame **SLOTS**, not user-visible
variables. A `stack var buf[N]` (a byte array on the frame) doesn't
register as one entry — it registers **N/8 filler slots** (one per
8-byte slot the array occupies). So a single `stack var buf[2048]`
burns 256 table entries by itself.

The old tables were sized 256 / 576 entries. Any function whose
frame exceeded ~2 KB of slot-arrays silently ran `SFLC` past the
table end and wrote past the region — a heap overrun with no
diagnostic. Self-host never tripped it (cycc's own fns stay small),
so it was invisible until a consumer with a large stack buffer (the
daimon report) hit it.

**The fix (v6.1.40)**: relocate all four tables to the heap top,
each sized **16384 slots**:

  - fn_local_names   @ 0x5D9D000
  - fn_local_depths  @ 0x5DBD000
  - fn_local_types   @ 0x5DDD000
  - fn_local_slice   @ 0x5DFD000

plus a LOUD `SFLC` cap check (error + exit, not silent truncation)
so a future overrun reports the slot count and the file to raise,
instead of corrupting the heap.

**The footgun behind the report — local vs global array sizing**:
the array-size convention differs by scope, which is exactly what
let the frame grow past expectation:

  - **local** `var a[N]` = **N BYTES**, rounded UP to 8 (so
    `var a[5]` still consumes one 8-byte slot; `var a[2048]`
    consumes 256 slots).
  - **global** `var a[N]` = **N i64 SLOTS** (N*8 bytes).

So a buffer that looks identical in source (`var a[2048]`) is 2 KB
on the stack but 16 KB in `.bss` as a global. Size local arrays by
BYTE count; size globals by slot count. Mixing the two mental
models is how the daimon frame quietly grew past the old cap.

**Recognize again**: any "large stack buffer corrupts compiler
state / heap" shape, or a consumer whose fn has a multi-KB
`stack var buf[N]`. Check `SFLC` against the table cap; the slot
count is `frame_bytes / 8`, not the var count. Related:
`large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack` (the dual — big
fn-local arrays land in `.bss`, not the stack frame).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap"
description = "Two mechanics that bite when migrating cycc's fixed tables to growable: (1) a relocating buffer (codebuf) is only safe if every access re-derives from the base gvar and never holds a captured pointer across an EB — and the disp32 patch sites do an 8-byte read-modify-write that bypasses EB's cap-check, so the grow needs a guard band; (2) the 8192 fn-cap was a magic literal scattered across 6 subsystems and textually colliding with 5 UNRELATED 8192-capped tables, so the swap had to be surgical per-context, never a global replace."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "codebuf", "fn-tables", "relocation", "magic-number", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**Relocation safety (codebuf)**: `_codebuf_grow` `alloc()`s a new
buffer and repoints `_codebuf_base`. That is only correct because
EVERY codebuf access re-derives `_codebuf_base + offset` fresh:
- the disp32 patch sites (`var a = _codebuf_base + GCP(S); S64(a, …)`)
  capture-and-store with NO `EB` in between, so no grow can run
  between the capture and the use;
- the DSE/LASE/regalloc peephole passes read+patch already-emitted
  code AFTER all emit, when the buffer is stable.
A pattern that captured `_codebuf_base + cp` and then called `EB`
(which can grow → relocate) before using it would dereference the
freed old buffer. There is none today — keep it that way.

The disp32 stores also do an 8-byte read-modify-write at `cp`
(`S64(a, (L64(a) >> 32 << 32) | disp)`), writing 4 bytes PAST the
4-byte slot, and they bypass `EB`'s cap-check entirely. So
`_codebuf_grow` triggers at `cp >= _codebuf_cap - 64` (a 64-byte
guard band), not at the exact cap, or the last RMW before a grow
would write past the buffer end. The cx bytecode backend has its
OWN code region — since 6.7.12 `_cx_code_base`, an off-heap 4 MiB
`alloc` (it was a fixed 512 KB heap region at `0x54A000`, now
FREED) — and in the cx fork `_codebuf_base` is only that region's
break-chain shadow (an `alloc(cap + 8)`: the shadow takes the same
8-byte read-modify-write at an instruction start). See
a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five… for the five
places cx's program size is bounded.

**The scattered magic number (fn-tables)**: `8192` (the fn-cap)
was hard-coded in ~6 independent places — `REGFN`'s cap-check,
the `fn_name_hash` + `fn_start_hash` probe/mask/clear bounds, the
`live[1024]` DCE bitmap, and the 85 %-full warning — with NO single
source of truth. Worse, the literal `8192` also appears in 5
UNRELATED capped tables (var table, IR pool, PE reloc, struct
field pool, the TS scratch stack). A blind `8192 → _fnt_cap` would
have corrupted those. The migration was surgical per-context:
`& 8191 → & _fnt_hash_mask` (hash masks only), `probes/sh_clr <
8192 → _fnt_cap` (hash bounds only), `live[1024] → live[4096]`
(the DCE bitmap, sized to the 32768 ceiling), REGFN's check → the
grow call. Everything now reads one `_fnt_cap`.

**Recognize again**: any "raise/remove a fixed cap" task where the
cap value is a bare literal. Grep first; a literal that doubles as
an unrelated table's size will silently break that table.

**6.7.14 (IN-1, IN-4, IN-7) — A GROW THAT DROPS PART OF A TABLE DOES
NOT CRASH, AND A READER LEFT ON THE FIXED BASE READS ANOTHER TABLE.**
The IR arena (and the WPNR / WPSW registries) moved from fixed `S + 0x…`
bands to an `alloc()`'d table that doubles and copies — the release's
heap-layout change, proven by the two-step bootstrap. A grow mutant that
skipped the CP copy or the BB copy passed every exit-code test and
cycc's own IR=3 self-host: a zeroed node CP makes LASE / fold skip the
span (`sz > 0 && sz < 16`), zeroed BB entries find nothing to eliminate,
a lost WPNR record leaves a correct but LARGER binary. Test a grow with
the same per-fn shape at a size that never grows and one that grows, and
require the pass statistics (LASE, applied bytes, DSE) to be an EXACT
multiple; for a registry, "every byte NOP-filled is removed", not "the
binary runs". And when a band becomes growable, grep EVERY reader of the
old base: `EFNREDIRECT` still scanned the jump-source registry at
`S + 0x60000`, though since 6.6.20 the registry moves to `alloc()`'d
storage past 49,152 records — beyond that index the fixed region is the
run registry, so a `#naked` body's own record was missed and repaired
twice (IN-7).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable"
description = "Cross-OS self-host on REAL hardware — ecb (macOS arm64), cass (Windows PE), pi (aarch64 Linux), one ssh away each — is a STANDING release gate, not a closeout-only or CI-only check. A GREEN CI CHECK IS NOT VERIFICATION. Two v6.3.x bugs shipped SILENTLY behind green smoke and were caught ONLY by running on the hardware: (1) v6.3.43 — Windows sys_write/sys_read on FILE handles (EWRITE_PE always translated the fd → GetStdHandle, so only std handles 0/1/2 ever worked; a CreateFileW handle wrote to garbage) — never caught because no test had ever written to a FILE on Windows. (2) v6.3.44 — getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (untranslated syscall 172) — a callable-only smoke passed on it. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only smoke is a PLACEBO; the actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware IS the test. The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently ~9 minors behind a CI job that only ran hello-world."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["cross-os", "self-host", "ecb", "cass", "pi", "release-gate", "found-by-ports", "smoke-is-placebo", "v6.3.43", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**A checkmark is not the compiler running on the hardware.** After ANY
compiler-backend or stdlib change, cycc must self-host BYTE-IDENTICAL and
the platform tests must PASS with real assertions on real macOS (ecb,
arm64), real Windows (cass, PE), and real aarch64 Linux (pi) — each one
`ssh` away via `~/.ssh/config`. This is a STANDING per-release gate (the
LIBTEST `vr01_` glob + `SELFHOST_OK`/`LIBTEST_OK` on ecb/cass/pi), not a
closeout-only or CI-only step.

**Two silent-rot cases from v6.3.x, both found only by running:**
  - **v6.3.43, Windows file I/O.** `EWRITE_PE` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr)
    ALWAYS translated the fd into a *standard handle* via GetStdHandle
    (`neg;sub 10`), so `sys_write`/`sys_read` only ever worked for fd
    0/1/2 — a real `CreateFileW` handle (a large value) mistranslated into
    a bogus std-handle index → WriteFile to an invalid handle → the whole
    file round-trip silently failed. Never caught because NO test had ever
    written to a FILE on Windows (tests/win/ was all stdout). Fix: an
    unsigned `cmp rax,2; ja` fd-branch (fd>2 = handle direct), mirroring
    the fix EREAD_PE already had since v6.0.45.
  - **v6.3.44, aarch64-macOS getpid.** getpid returned positive GARBAGE
    (an untranslated syscall 172) — see
    `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`. A callable-only
    smoke passed on the garbage.

**The precedent.** The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently for ~9
minors (v5.3.13 → v6.0.32, 400+ patches) behind a CI job named "Mach-O
ARM64 Native ✓" that only ran hello-world / exit-code programs and never
once built or self-hosted cycc. It surfaced only when a human installed on
a Mac and got a broken toolchain. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only
smoke is a PLACEBO — the compiler self-hosting on the target, and the
actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware, IS the test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "vex_encoding_vvvv_ones_complement_and_fma_cpuid_leaf"
description = "The v6.4.8/.9 SIMD arc emitted the FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain has ever produced (f32v8 256-bit: 2-byte C5 VEX for vaddps/vsubps/vmulps at v6.4.8; 3-byte C4 VEX for vfmadd231ps + vextractf128 at v6.4.9). Two traps bit here that hand-rolled hex-literal encoders on the SSE side never had. (1) THE VEX vvvv FIELD IS THE 1's-COMPLEMENT OF THE src1 REGISTER — the non-destructive 3-operand form encodes src1 in the inverted vvvv nibble, so a wrong-polarity vvvv assembles to a VALID instruction that silently reads the WRONG register (no crash, no decode error — just wrong math). It is INVISIBLE to a size/self-host check and can even pass a coarse value test if the mis-read register happens to alias. The only defense is to round-trip EVERY new VEX op through a real disassembler (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump) and diff the mnemonic+operands against intent — a disasm gate, not an eyeball. (2) FMA NEEDS A DIFFERENT CPUID LEAF/BIT THAN AVX2 — the runtime feature probe for vfmadd231ps is CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12 (simd_has_fma), NOT the AVX2 probe of leaf 7, EBX bit 5 (simd_has_avx2). They are genuinely separate capabilities (a CPU can have AVX2 without FMA in principle), so a wrapper that gates FMA on the AVX2 bit is wrong; each 256-bit op checks the RIGHT bit and falls back (2×SSE, or scalar for FMA). AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline — the CPUID probe + fallback is mandatory, not optional. Corollary from the same arc: decode.cyr's VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided with a pre-existing SYSCALL/CPUID mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (filed P3) — the disassembler gate uses external llvm tooling, not the in-tree decoder."
category = "cyrius"
---
The v6.4.4–.9 SIMD compute arc took x86 codegen from SSE-only into
**AVX/VEX** territory for the first time: v6.4.8 emits 2-byte (C5) VEX
for the f32v8 elementwise ops (vaddps/vsubps/vmulps on ymm), v6.4.9 adds
3-byte (C4) VEX for `vfmadd231ps` + the 8-lane `vextractf128` reduce.
Two traps here have NO analogue in the older hand-rolled SSE hex-literal
emitters (EMIT_F32V_LOOP etc. in src/backend/x86/float.cyr).

**TRAP 1 — the VEX `vvvv` field is the 1's-COMPLEMENT of src1.** The
non-destructive 3-operand VEX form encodes the first source register in
the `vvvv` nibble **inverted** (`~reg & 0xF`). Get the polarity wrong
and you STILL assemble a valid instruction — it just reads a DIFFERENT
register. No SIGILL, no decode failure, no assembler complaint: silently
wrong math. It is invisible to a size/self-host check (the bytes are a
legal encoding) and can even sneak past a coarse value test if the
mis-read register happens to hold a compatible value. **The only defense
is a disassembler gate:** round-trip every new VEX op through real
external tooling (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump), diff the decoded
mnemonic+operands against intent. Eyeballing the hex is not enough — the
whole point of the trap is that the wrong bytes look plausible.

**TRAP 2 — FMA and AVX2 are DIFFERENT CPUID capabilities.** The runtime
probe for `vfmadd231ps` is **CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12**
(`simd_has_fma`), which is a DIFFERENT leaf AND bit from the AVX2 probe
**CPUID leaf 7 (ECX=0), EBX bit 5** (`simd_has_avx2`). They are not
interchangeable — a part can advertise AVX2 without FMA — so gating the
FMA path on the AVX2 bit is a real bug. AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline;
each 256-bit wrapper MUST probe the correct bit and branch to a fallback
(2×SSE for elementwise, scalar for FMA) when absent. This is mandatory,
not a nicety — a plain `-march=x86-64` box will `#UD` on an unguarded
`vaddps ymm`.

**Corollary — the in-tree decoder was NOT extended for VEX.** During the
arc, `decode.cyr`'s VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided
with a PRE-EXISTING mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (SYSCALL/CPUID),
filed P3 (issues/archived/2026-07-05-decode-len-mislengths-no-modrm-0f-opcodes.md).
So the disasm gate deliberately uses EXTERNAL llvm tooling, not the
in-tree `decode.cyr` — don't reach for the in-tree decoder to validate
VEX bytes; it can't, and fixing it is a separate tracked item.

**Recognize again.** Any first-of-a-new-encoding-family codegen
(VEX→EVEX, a new prefix, a new 3-operand form) where the encoding packs
an operand in a non-obvious way (inverted, split across bytes): assume a
plausible-but-wrong encoding will assemble cleanly and pass self-host,
and add a disasm round-trip gate BEFORE trusting value tests. And any new
ISA-extension op: find its EXACT CPUID leaf+bit (they are not grouped by
"AVX-ness") and wire a runtime fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "two_step_bootstrap_for_toplevel_array_sizing_and_legit_codegen_diff"
description = "Two paired differential-testing / bootstrap gotchas from the v6.4.x SIMD-and-fixes minor. (1) A LAYOUT change to how cycc sizes its OWN data needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP, same as a heap-map or W^X change. v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level `var X[N]` was silently 8× UNDER-sized (N bytes, the fn-local default) instead of N×8 (the PARSE_GVAR_ARR slot size) when the array was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement — because pass-2 PARSE_ARRAY fell through to the fn-local N-byte path. Fixed in parse_decl.cyr (~61): a bare NON-fn-local array gets N×8. Because cycc's OWN top-level arrays were mis-sized under the old compiler, flipping the sizing needs the two-step (old cycc → cc5b, then cc5b compiles itself; cycc==cc5b) — a plain one-step fixpoint can bake in the old layout. This joins W^X-default-flip and array-convention as the recurring 'cycc's own storage layout changed → two-step' class. (2) A BUG FIX legitimately shows differential codegen-diff>0 WITH status-diff=0 — the opposite of a pure refactor (which must be codegen-diff=0). When a fix CHANGES emitted bytes for the affected inputs (e.g. correct array sizing, a sign-extension fix, a correct register), the differential corpus SHOULD show codegen-diff>0 on exactly the affected programs while status-diff stays 0 (nothing that passed now fails); a codegen-diff=0 on a supposed behavior fix means the fix didn't actually fire. Read the two axes separately: status-diff guards regressions, codegen-diff measures blast radius."
category = "cyrius"
---
Two paired gotchas about READING differential/bootstrap results, from the
v6.4.x minor's fixes (not the SIMD codegen itself).

**(1) A change to how cycc sizes its OWN top-level data needs a TWO-STEP
BOOTSTRAP.** v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level
`var X[N]` was silently **8× under-sized** — it got N BYTES (the
fn-local array default) instead of N×8 (the `PARSE_GVAR_ARR` slot size)
**whenever it was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement**,
because at that point pass-2 `PARSE_ARRAY` fell through to the fn-local
N-byte path instead of the global slot path. Fix (parse_decl.cyr ~61): a
bare **non-fn-local** array is sized N×8. The catch: **cycc's own
top-level arrays were affected**, so the sizing flip cannot be validated
by a one-step fixpoint alone — the old (mis-sizing) cycc could bake the
old layout into the new binary. Run the **two-step**: old cycc → `cc5b`,
then `cc5b` compiles itself, assert `cycc == cc5b` (+ seed-derive). This
is the SAME class as the W^X default-on flip and the local-vs-global
array-convention gotcha: **whenever a change alters cycc's own storage
layout / data sizing, it's a two-step, not a one-step.**

**(2) A real behavior fix SHOULD show differential codegen-diff > 0 with
status-diff = 0** — and that is CORRECT, not a red flag. The differential
harness reports two independent axes over the corpus:
  - **status-diff** — did any program's pass/fail status change? For a
    fix this MUST stay 0 (nothing that compiled+ran now breaks).
  - **codegen-diff** — did the emitted BYTES change, and for which
    programs? For a fix that changes what's emitted (array sizing, a
    sign-extension, a corrected register) this SHOULD be > 0, on exactly
    the affected programs.

The trap is conflating the two. A pure REFACTOR must be
codegen-diff = 0 (identical bytes). A BUG FIX is the opposite: if a
supposed behavior fix shows codegen-diff = 0, the fix **did not fire** —
you changed a path nothing exercises, or guarded it wrong. So read the
axes separately: status-diff is the regression guard; codegen-diff is the
blast-radius measurement. `codegen-diff > 0 && status-diff = 0` on the
programs you expected is exactly the signature of a landed, contained fix.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_transcendental_polyfill_dispatch_and_dekker_dd_range_reduction"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
aarch64 has no native transcendentals — x86 gets them from x87 (fsin/fcos,
f2xm1/fscale for exp/exp2, fyl2x for ln/log2, fpatan for atan). Every f64
transcendental on aarch64 is therefore a lib/math.cyr POLYFILL that the aarch64
backend dispatches to. The dispatch pattern (v5.7.31 exp/ln → v6.4.16 sin/cos →
v6.4.25 exp2/atan) is identical: EF64_<OP>(S) in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr does
`var pf = _FINDFN_CSTR(S, "_f64_<op>_polyfill", <len>); if (pf<0) ERR_MSG(...requires
include lib/math.cyr...); EPUSHR(S); ECALLPOPS(S,1); ECALLFIX(S,pf);` — i.e. it emits
a 1-arg call into the stdlib fn resolved by name. The x86 EF64_<OP> in
src/backend/x86/emit.cyr keeps emitting native x87 bytes and is NOT touched. THREE
consequences that trip you up:

(1) x86 cycc stays BYTE-IDENTICAL across a pure-aarch64 transcendental change. The
x86 fork (src/main.cyr) includes ONLY backend/x86/emit.cyr — not the aarch64 emit —
and cycc does NOT include lib/math.cyr (it's a stdlib, not compiler source). So
adding a polyfill + rerouting the aarch64 EF64_* changes ONLY the three aarch64 forks
(main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr) and lib/math.cyr. The x86 self-host fixpoint and
the differential (343 inputs, codegen-diff=0) are trivially green — that is CORRECT,
not a missing test. The real verification is the aarch64 self-host fixpoint (build the
x86-hosted aarch64-emitting cross-compiler `main_aarch64.cyr | cycc`, then `| itself`,
cmp — byte-identical under qemu) PLUS running the actual polyfill on real hardware
(pi + ecb) via a vr01_ tcyr. A green x86 run proves nothing about the aarch64 path.

(2) The polyfill must NOT recurse into the builtin it backs. _f64_exp2_polyfill uses
round/sub/mul/add/to (NOT f64_exp2); _f64_atan_polyfill uses gt/div/sub/add/mul/neg +
bit ops (NOT f64_atan). Each polyfill compiles on x86 too (arch-independent body) but
is only INVOKED on aarch64 — x86 keeps the x87 path.

(3) Large-argument trig range reduction on a machine with only f64 add/sub/mul (no
scalar FMA builtin) requires a Dekker double-double. The single-constant reduction
`r = x - round(x*2/pi)*pi_2` catastrophically cancels for large |x| (rel err ~5e-2 at
1e15). The fix is a NO-FMA Dekker TwoProduct: split via (2^27+1), form x*(2/pi) and
frac*(pi/2) as hi+lo words with the constants pre-split into _SH/_SL halves so the
product is exact without fma. Validated numerically (Python float64 == IEEE754) BEFORE
touching the compiler — double-double is exact to ~1e-16 through |x|≈1e15; triple-double
is NOT needed. GATE it (|x| >= 8192) so the cheap small-angle path is untouched and
stays BYTE-IDENTICAL — the issue's "small path unchanged" is a byte-identity
requirement, not a vibe. The dd quadrant k must come from the dd sum (round(ph+plo)),
NOT a naive round(x*2/pi) which is off-by-one for large x — so the dd block writes k_f
locally, and (no multi-return in this compiler) the ~14-line block is inlined verbatim
in both sin and cos, same as their small-path reduction already is. Keep each emitted
Horner/dd line under 120 chars up front (cyrlint gate) by accumulating into a stepwise
var, not one deep f64_add(f64_add(...)) nest — cyrfmt won't split it for you.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_kernel32_reroutes_fixed_sub_rsp_only_aligns_on_right_parity_closehandle_faults_on_misalign_wine_cannot_repro"
description = "Windows PE kernel32 reroutes must DYNAMICALLY 16-align rsp before the call — a fixed `sub rsp, 0x28` is a latent bug. Win64 requires rsp = 0 (mod 16) immediately before a `call`; a fixed sub-rsp achieves that only when the caller's rsp lands on the exact parity the offset assumes, which depends on the function's frame size + expr-stack depth + call chain — so it silently works or faults by context. CloseHandle (and any kernel32 fn with an internal aligned SSE access, e.g. movaps) faults 0xC0000005 on an 8-misaligned stack; GetExitCodeProcess/WaitForSingleObject happen to tolerate it, which is why the crash localizes to CloseHandle though its siblings share the bug. EXPOSED at v6.4.44 by the async subprocess resume (lib/async_win.cyr _async_process_task): main -> task_join -> _async_pump -> _async_step -> fncall1 -> resume -> CloseHandle is a deep stack, and TWO sequential async_run_process calls crashed on real cass while a SINGLE call and the BATCHED (spawn-all-then-join) form worked — pure alignment-parity luck. CreateProcessW's ECREATEPROC_PE was already rbx-anchor-aligned at v6.0.51, but its own comment asserted 'the <=4-arg reroutes tolerate a misalign this 10-arg call does not' — that generalization was FALSE. FIX: route each of the 8 fixed-offset reroutes (ECLOSE_PE/EWAIT_PE/EGETEXIT_PE/ESETHANDLE_PE/ECREATEPIPE_PE/EGETCMDLINE_PE/EGETTICKS_PE/EMMAP_PE) through the existing rbx-anchored helpers (_pe_call_0/2/3/4arg_aligned, _pe_srw_call_1arg: push rbx; mov rbx,rsp; and rsp,-16; sub rsp,0x20; call; mov rsp,rbx; pop rbx). cycc SHRANK ~4 KB (inline reroutes -> shared helpers). HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a real-Windows-ONLY 0xC0000005 that WINE cannot reproduce (WINE aligns leniently -> returned 42 throughout) AND flips crash<->pass with call-depth / caller-frame changes -> suspect a fixed-`sub rsp` reroute, not the callee. Localize with a WriteFile (syscall(1,1,...)) trace bracketing each op; bisect step-0 vs resume vs the post-fncall1 loop code with manual GQCS + fncall1 replays. Self-host + seed-derive stay byte-identical (PE-emit-only; cycc's Linux codegen never calls these). See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_offbyone_prologue_postloop_missing_pp_shift_f32v8_make_lanes_567"
description = "On Win64 PE a by-value SIMD/struct return uses a HIDDEN RETPTR in RCX, so every user arg shifts down one ABI slot (a,b,c -> rdx/r8/r9; d,e,f,g,h -> stack). The callee prologue homes params in TWO places and they must agree on that retptr shift: the IN-loop homing (parse_fn.cyr ~3321) computes `_pp_shift = (_cur_fn_ret_stash>0 && !aarch64) ? 1 : 0` and calls ESTOREPARM(int_pc+_pp_shift, li) -- pidx shifted for the source Win64 slot, lidx contiguous for the dest local slot. But the POST-loop that homes the DEEP stack params (pidx>=6, parse_fn.cyr ~3404) did `ESTOREPARM(spi, spi, pc)` -- using spi as BOTH pidx AND lidx with NO shift. Correct only with no retptr. With one, it homed each deep stack param ONE LOCAL SLOT TOO DEEP (dest lidx = true+1) and stopped one param short (spi<pc never reached the last source idx), so the first excluded stack param was never homed, the rest got the previous param's value, and the last param's incoming bytes were ignored. The body reads each param at the contiguous -0x30-lidx*8 slot via FINDLOCAL, so it diverged. SYMPTOM: f32v8_make(a..h) returned lanes 0-4 correct but lanes 5,6,7 = garbage/shifted on real cass; Linux/SysV all 8 lanes correct (SIMD returns via the xmm PAIR there, no retptr, so _pl_shift==0 and the emit is textually the old code -> self-host byte-identical). FIX: mirror _pp_shift in the post-loop -- `pidx = spi + _pl_shift` (shifted source), `lidx = spi` (contiguous dest), start `spi = 6 - _pl_shift`, guard `pc + _pl_shift > 6` (also catches the exact-6-param retptr case pc>6 dropped). PRE-EXISTING since v6.4.31 (Win64 value-form SIMD params/returns); latent for many minors because vr01_f32v8_ctor.tcyr carried a stale comment saying it 'skips cass', but v6.4.31 made CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS true on PE so the native cass cycc actually RAN the f32v8 assertions -- surfaced only when the v6.4.44 permanent Windows async gate ran the full vr01_ corpus on cass. HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a value-form-SIMD/struct-returning fn with >6 params whose DEEP args (3rd+ stack arg) are wrong on Win64 but correct on Linux -> suspect the retptr-shift being applied inconsistently between the two prologue-homing sites. Confirm by disassembling BOTH the prologue homing (`mov rax,[rbp+src]; mov [rbp-dst],rax`) and the body read (FINDLOCAL disp = -((lidx+1)*8)-regalloc*8-ret_stash) -- they must land on the same local slot. The caller (ECALLPOPS / parse_decl retptr push) was CORRECT; the bug is callee-side. Same '_cur_fn_ret_stash disp<->idx off-by-one' class as project_v6431_win64_valueform_simd, here the one remaining site. See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "shift_right_split_gtgt_stays_logical_gtgtgt_is_arithmetic_reverse_of_js_never_flip_gtgt"
description = "v6.4.46 added `>>>` as the ARITHMETIC (sign-preserving) right shift and KEPT `>>` LOGICAL (zero-fill) — the REVERSE of JS/Java (there `>>` is arithmetic, `>>>` logical). The split is NOT arbitrary: `>>` MUST stay logical because two load-bearing sites depend on it. (1) CRYPTO ROTATES: keccak/sha/chacha rotate via `(x << n) | (x >> (64-n))` — logical `>>` is required or the high bits sign-extend and the rotate is wrong; flipping `>>` to arithmetic silently miscompiles SHA-3, cascading into TLS (tls_native_alpn went red). (2) THE COMPILER'S OWN UNION FLAG: `ISUNION()` is `fcount >> 63` — logical `>>` gives 1 for a tagged (high-bit-set) fcount, arithmetic `>>` gives -1 and the union sizeof/layout miscompiles (structs went red). BOTH were caught ONLY by check.sh's corpus — the self-host fixpoint AND seed-derive stayed GREEN because cycc's own source didn't exercise the exact miscompiled path until the corpus ran (a reminder that green self-host is necessary, not sufficient). LESSON: do NOT 'fix' `>>` to arithmetic to match JS/Java — it is a deliberate, load-bearing choice; route signed-shift needs through the NEW `>>>` (IR_ASHR=26; x86 `sar rax,cl` = 48 D3 F8; aarch64 `asrv` = 0x9AC12800; cx op 37). The drishti bug that motivated it was a signed value silently zero-filled by `>>`; the fix is `>>>`, never a `>>` semantics flip."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["shift", "logical-shift", "arithmetic-shift", "operator", "crypto", "rotate", "union", "isunion", "sign-extension", "green-selfhost-not-sufficient", "v6.4.46"]

[[entries]]
name = "cfo_const_fold_rewind_class_four_occurrences_grep_the_shape_not_the_operator_and_every_tier_that_reenters_the_fold"
description = "THE most-repeated codegen defect of the v6.x line — four occurrences (a fifth, 6.6.12: a factor arm that parses its own call and cleared the flag BEFORE it), each fix scoped to the tier its repro landed in and each declared complete. MECHANISM: the constant folder keeps `_cfo` (fold armed), `_cfv` (folded value) and `_cfp` (the code pointer captured at the START of the foldable region). A parse path clears `_cfo = 0` BEFORE calling a lower tier that RE-ARMS it — `PARSE_FACTOR` sets `_cfp = GCP(S)` then `_cfv = val; _cfo = 1` for a bare NUM — so the flag comes back on with `_cfp` pointing PAST code that was already emitted. The next operator then takes the const-fold branch with a live-but-stale fold and `SCP(S, _cfp)` rewinds the code pointer over that already-emitted code, replacing it with a constant. The symptom is always a silently wrong constant: no error, no warning, valid binary. OCCURRENCES: (1) 2026-06-11, cyrius-doom, `A * B * 4` -> 800 (the EIMUL path). (2) v6.4.74 — 17 sites in the `PARSE_TERM` tier (`/ % << >> >>>`): `100 >>> 1 + 1` == 2 (want 51), `100 >> 0 + 1` == 1 (want 101). `>>` and `<<` mostly escaped because their own fold path handles a literal RHS, so it bit exactly where that fold could not apply (shift count 0, or a value outside the 0x10000 window); a parenthesised RHS also masked it, since the paren path never re-arms. (3) v6.4.80 — 16 sites one tier UP in `PEXPR` (`+ - & | ^`): `1 - 2 + 3` == **5**, `2 - 5 + 10` == 15, `3 + 1 - 5 + 2` == 7. The emitted code is the expression with the leading `a -` deleted. Trigger is the `cfr >= 0` test in the subtract fold — a NEGATIVE intermediate fails it and takes the runtime fallback, and the fallback was the buggy one, which is exactly why non-negative intermediates were always right (`5 - 3 + 1` == 3) and it hid. Measured blast radius: 40 of 400 (10 %) systematic 3-term constant expressions wrong. (4) v6.4.81 — one tier DOWN in `EMIT_OP_DISPATCH`, struct operator overloading: it parses its RHS with `PARSE_TERM` (re-arms) and ends with `SESTYPE(S, 0)`, so the following `+`/`-` rewound over the emitted operator CALL — the overload silently deleted and replaced by a constant. `p * 3 + 1` == 4 (want 1004). The tell was a 2-of-4 asymmetry in four structurally identical lines: `add` (parse_expr.cyr:2809) and `sub` (:2837) already cleared `_cfo`; `mul` (:2550) and `div` (:2610) never did. THE LESSON: **grep the SHAPE, not the operator — and grep EVERY TIER that calls back into the fold, not just the tier the repro landed in.** A single `grep -c \"_cfo = 0; E.*PARSE_TERM\"` at .74 would have found the .80 sites eight releases earlier; at .80 the sweep recorded 'zero occurrences of the shape remain in parse_expr.cyr' and that was true only of PEXPR — the dispatch helper one level down was untouched. THE OTHER LESSON: **251/251 tcyr were byte-identical across the .80 fix, and that is the finding, not reassurance** — the corpus contained zero expressions of the failing shape, so 10 % of constant arithmetic was wrong with every gate green (self-host fixpoint, seed-derive, check.sh, cross-OS on four hosts). A byte-identical corpus proves a change did not perturb what the corpus covers; it says nothing about what it does not. COVERAGE TRAPS recorded while building the regression (`tests/tcyr/const_chained_multiply_fold.tcyr`, now 39 assertions and the single home of this one mechanism): an operator-overload helper whose body ALIASES the folded answer (`OpV_mul` returning `a * b`) passes while broken — make helpers return values plain integer arithmetic cannot produce; and getting a mutation to fail took three rounds, because `1 - 2 & 3` fires the SUB fallback not the AND one, AND needs a left operand folding to 0 (`0 & 1 & 1`), OR/XOR need a ZERO right-hand operand (`1 | 0 ^ 1`), and ADD is reachable only via i64 overflow (`9223372036854775807 + 1 + 1`) since a literal `crv` is never negative. Before that work 8 of the 16 .80 sites had no coverage at all. When a mutation PASSES, fuzz good-vs-mutant to find a discriminator rather than concluding the site is dead. Found at .80 by an adversarial doc-sweep verifier RUNNING the compiler to re-check an unrelated vidya claim about precedence — not by reading code. (5) 6.6.12 — a NEW factor arm that parses its OWN call, `_call_field` (a `.field` on a call result, src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr), is the same mechanism entered from the other side: the arm has to clear `_cfo` AFTER the call, not before it. A LITERAL ARGUMENT is parsed by PARSE_FACTOR, which arms the fold (`_cfo = 1`, `_cfp` at that literal); clearing before the call does nothing, the call and the field load are emitted, and the following operator folds by rewinding to the literal — deleting the call and the load. `mkp(3).y + 1` became 4 and `mk8(4).a * 10` folded from the argument literal. The arm now ends with `_cfo = 0;` after the load. RULE, stated for the next arm: any parse path that CONSUMES a sub-expression the fold may have armed — a call's arguments, a subscript's index, a parenthesised operand — clears `_cfo` once that sub-expression is fully emitted, i.e. as its LAST act; test it with a literal inside the sub-expression and an operator after it. (The name still says four; five is the count.) See CHANGELOG [6.4.74], [6.4.80], [6.4.81], [6.6.12] B04. (6) 6.7.4 — the if-expression's value join let a following `+ 3` rewind to the last branch's literal (value_join_resets_and_unparsed_scanners_v674, point 2). (7) 6.7.8 — the SLICE READ `_slice_sub_read` (v5.8.15 to 6.7.7): it parsed its index (a literal arms the fold), emitted the `_slice_idx_get_W` call, and returned with the flag still armed, so `return s[0] + 10;` folded `0 + 10` and rewound over the call — whose recorded fixup then patched the return's jump into a jump to the getter (exit 134 'slice bounds violation'; `var y = s[0] + 10;` SIGSEGV; CYRIUS_DCE=1 refused the build, 'corrupted 2 live function bodies'), on every target since 6.7.5. The index's OTHER last-value words leaked the same way: its pointer step (`s[ix()] + 1` with `ix(): *i32` added 4) and its struct type (`s[n] + 10` dispatched `Idx_add`). THE RULE, stated for the class: a factor's result state is set by the LAST thing it emits — every arm that emits after a sub-parse ends with the factor-entry resets (`_cfo = 0`, the step, the estype), as `_arr_sub_read` already did. The CP/TI-STAMPED marks (`_FBR_MARK`, `_BX_MARK`, `_W128_MARK`, `_PK_MARK`) cannot go stale this way; `_cfo` is not stamped, which is why it keeps recurring. Seven is the count. CHANGELOG [6.7.8]. 6.7.10: the execution-coverage hit and dump call each zero `_cfo` (the replay's hit follows an argument evaluation that may have armed a fold) — DEFENSIVE, not mutation-provable: every factor that can precede a fold clears or re-arms `_cfo` itself, so removing either line changes no emitted byte on any shape found (MY2 / MY3 green); say so in the ledger rather than claim a pin. 6.7.11 (ST-12, ST-13), the 6.7.8 rule again for the struct type: the expression state is the LAST operand's, so every integer arm — `% << >> >>> & | ^`, comparisons, `&& ||`, saturating / checked ops, `~`, unary `-` — leaked its right operand's struct type and slot, and the next `+` dispatched on it (`n - s + 10` called `S_add`); 6.7.9 fixed the same leak for the pointer step at the same sites. `_int_st` / `_int_res` clear it; an f64 type is kept by design. An operator that produces a plain value must WRITE the result's type. 6.7.14 (FE-16) — PEEK, DON'T EMIT THEN REWIND: teaching the run-time fold a constant NAME on the right meant rewinding over more than the left operand's own `mov` (a `push`, the right operand), and that leaves IR nodes recorded at rewound code positions (`ir_emit` stores GCP per node; IR=3 patches by position). Read the right operand's value from the TOKENS first (`_cf_fac_at`, `_cf_term_at`) and fold exactly as the literal path does — a rewind may only undo what the fold itself just emitted."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["_cfo", "_cfp", "const-fold", "code-pointer-rewind", "SCP", "PARSE_TERM", "PEXPR", "PARSE_FACTOR", "EMIT_OP_DISPATCH", "operator-overload", "grep-the-shape-not-the-operator", "byte-identical-is-not-coverage", "mutation-proof", "aliasing-helper", "_call_field", "clear-after-the-call", "v6.4.74", "v6.4.80", "v6.4.81", "v6.6.12", "_slice_sub_read", "v6.7.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt_the_cycc_fixpoint_does_not_substitute_for_seed_derive"
description = "v6.4.74's constant folder first spelled its arithmetic shift `v >>> _CF_FACTOR(S)`. It compiled, self-hosted, and FIXPOINTED byte-identical on `build/cycc` — then `scripts/seed-derive-cycc.sh` failed at step [3/5] with a bare `syntax error` and nothing else. Cause: **cybs, the hand-assembly bootstrap compiler the 29 KB seed assembles, cannot lex `>>>`** — `bootstrap/cybs.cyr`'s `lexer_shr` knows only `>`, `>=`, `>>`, so it tokenised `>>` `>`. The `>>>` operator has existed since v6.4.46; cybs never learned it. Fixed by writing `_CF_ASR`, which sign-extends using only `>>`/`<<` and masks the count to 6 bits to match the backend's `sar`. This is the cleanest available demonstration of Release Gate item 2: **the cycc self-host fixpoint does NOT cover the seed chain.** `build/cycc` is a full v6.x compiler; cybs is far more limited and fails SILENTLY, or with an unattributed one-line error, on constructs `build/cycc` compiles fine — too many global/string-literal references in one function (the ceiling that forced `_var_grow` into its `_grow_g1..g7` tail-call chain, that made v6.4.77 split the 67-entry `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` out of `TOKNAME`, and that made v6.4.81 split `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` out of an already-heavy `PREPROCESS`), tail calls, and now newer operators. ⭐ **6.6.12 — cybs refused a CALL WITH MORE THAN SIX ARGUMENTS** (measured then: `f(1,2,3,4,5,6,7)` was a bare `syntax error`, rc 1; the same program with six arguments compiled — RETIRED at 6.7.6, see the end of this entry). ⛔ **6.6.16 — AND A 7-PARAMETER DEFINITION WAS WORSE, NOT FINE:** cybs accepted it, but `emit_store_param` (bootstrap/cybs.cyr) handled param indices 0..5 only and jumped past the store for any other, so the seventh parameter was NEVER STORED and read garbage (`fn f(a,b,c,d,e,g,h) { return h; }` called with six arguments read 0). This entry used to say a 7-param definition was accepted, as if that were harmless; measured at 6.6.16 when a hunk added a seventh parameter to PARSE_GVAR_ARR: `build/cycc` self-hosted byte-identically and seed-derive died at [3/5] with the bare `syntax error` (the call site), and the definition alone would have miscompiled silently. It surfaced as the same bare `syntax error` at seed-derive step [3/5] while the preprocessor's aarch64-region helper was being written; the fix packed two of its arguments into one (`_pp_a64_open`'s kind and state); 6.6.16's derived the extra value from what was already passed (the annotation is the token after `var NAME :`, so `_gai_ann(S, sti)` reads it back) — derive it or pass it through a small helper; a global set before the call is safe only if nothing re-enters between. `build/cycc` takes any arity (the retired ≤6-args rule was a Win64 codegen bug, fixed v6.4.64); cybs did not until 6.7.6 (6.7.0 refused a 7+ argument call BY NAME), so compiler SOURCE kept every call at six arguments or fewer until then. PRACTICAL RULES: run `seed-derive-cycc.sh` for ANY `src/` change on EVERY release, not only at closeouts (a doc/lib-only bump is the sole exception); when writing compiler source prefer the OLDEST spelling that works; when a new helper carries a large literal table, expect the ceiling and split the fn before the gate tells you to; and note that gen1 (cybs's output) differing in size from `build/cycc` is NORMAL — only `gen2 == build/cycc` matters, do not chase it in either direction (this entry said '~72 KB smaller'; the sign has flipped since: at 6.6.12 gen1 is 1,545,384 B against `build/cycc`'s 1,470,944 B, ~74 KB LARGER, and gen2 == build/cycc). RECOGNITION (through 6.7.10): a bare `syntax error` from step [3/5] with no file, no line and no token is the signature — read it as cybs hitting a ceiling, not as a defect in your source. Since 6.7.11 the line names the class and the expanded-stream line. See CHANGELOG [6.4.74]. ⭐ **6.6.17 — cybs DROPPED A LEADING `_` from every name** (its lexer dispatch had no `_` arm; `lexer_skip` discards the byte), so in gen1 `_x` and `x` were ONE name: a local `_fi` was the local `fi`, a local `_x` captured reads of a global `x`, and `fn _aq` / `fn aq` were one fn (measured `aq()*10 + _aq()` = 22, not 21). `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL` declares the fn index `fi` and a compaction counter `_fi`, so gen1 wrote every fn's code-end record (`SFNE`) at a wrong index — invisible in the bytes for many releases (gen2 == build/cycc held; only gen1's DCE note differed, 57 unreachable fns against cycc's 73) because the fn tables sat in fixed 8192-slot regions and the stray writes landed in slack. The compiler's 2049th fn made the tables grow into packed buffers, the write landed in `_fnt_structmask`, a tail call lost its TCO, and seed-derive went RED on changes that FIXPOINTED byte-identically — three 6.6.17 lanes hit it independently. RECOGNITION: seed-derive red right after a change that only ADDED fns, cycc fixpoint green; bisect with `CYRIUS_SYMS` on gen1 vs build/cycc compiling src/main.cyr (the first fn whose address moves is where gen1 diverges). FIXED AT THE ROOT in bootstrap/cybs.cyr (a two-instruction `_` arm; the seed closure holds because asm.cyr has no leading-underscore identifier) and gated by tests/gates/toolchain/cybs_leading_underscore.sh; seed-derive gained step 6/6 (gen1's diagnostics must equal gen2's) because byte-identical output proves gen1 emitted the right bytes, not that it computed everything right. A lane's gate that instead FORBADE names differing by a leading `_` was dropped at the merge — a bootstrap limit written down as a rule is the same mistake as the retired six-argument rule. A fn-count threshold (2048, `_fnt_cap`; `CYRIUS_STATS=1` prints `fn_table`) is a seed-chain event. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (c1a, b3-seed, a5-seed). ⭐ **6.7.6 — cybs PASSES 7+ ARGUMENTS AND REFUSES WHAT IT DOES NOT LEX.** The caller leaves the arguments past the sixth on the stack in cycc's order (the last at `[rsp]`, so one convention holds and lib/fnptr.cyr's fncall8 matches both compilers) and the callee, having counted its parameters first (`count_params`), copies parameter i >= 6 of n from `[rbp + 16 + (n - 1 - i) * 8]`; the 6.7.0 by-name refusal is retired, so `src/` may use 7+ argument helpers and fncall6..8 compile faithfully under cybs. The lexer's fall-through no longer discards a byte it has no arm for (the path that ate the leading `_` above): a lone `!` and every byte cybs does not lex outside a string or a comment are refused by name — they compiled as if absent (`return !x;` as `return x;`, `@a = 5;` as `a = 5;`). A call through a global fn-pointer variable no longer clobbers argument 4 (rcx). Still cybs limits: syntax it does not support (`const`, `loop`, `do`, if-expressions, unary minus, `>>>`, `>>=`) — a bare `syntax error` with no location through 6.7.10, since 6.7.11 a refusal that LISTS that class and prints the offending line of the expanded stream (grep it to find the `src/` file), an unknown name named (`cybs: undefined variable or function (cybs has no const): <name>, at: <line>`) —, the per-fn reference ceiling, and the seed's SILENT caps (131,072 input bytes, 512 labels, 64 KB of code — row S of cybs_call_arity_named.sh guarded the first two, all three since 6.7.11). Gates: tests/gates/toolchain/cybs_call_arity_named.sh, cybs_lexer_drops_nothing.sh; field note flag_skip_by_position_nested_parse_state_and_the_bootstrap_rung_v676. CHANGELOG [6.7.6]. ⭐ **6.7.8 — EXTRACT BEFORE YOU GROW A GIANT.** A fn at the per-fn reference ceiling is shrunk by moving WHOLE ARMS out — each becomes one call in the giant — BEFORE a feature adds to them; prove it with the byte-identical corpus differential plus seed-derive, and record the reference count before and after: C2-1 moved the slice read, the `*expr` dereference and a typed-pointer global's step out of `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` (773 -> 748 references), and C2's subscript / `*p` / step work then grew the small fns, the giant untouched. A side effect worth knowing: the per-fn NOP compactor (parse_fn.cyr) skips any fn with >= 1024 NOP runs or >= 1024 jump sources, so a giant keeps its regalloc NOPs — `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` carried cycc's last 499 until the extraction (.text -1,680 B), and ir_nop_harvest.sh then read the IR=3 build's 0 NOPs as 'llvm-objdump missing?' (it now proves the tool by the instruction count). Same release: cybs cannot parse `dyn`, so lib/alloc.cyr — which src/main.cyr includes — cannot move its Allocator vtable to a dyn. CHANGELOG [6.7.8]. 6.7.10: three fns AT cybs's per-fn reference ceiling took new behaviour through 1-for-1 call swaps (PARSE_PROG -> `_cov_body` x2, ESUBRSP -> `_cl_frame_open`, `_macho_warn_unrouted` -> `_sc_args_done`), the counts measured before and after. 6.7.11 (INF-2 .. INF-4): (a) A REGISTER-NOW-DEFINE-LATER PLACEHOLDER IS A SENTINEL THE FIXUP MUST CHECK — cybs registers an unknown statement-called name with entry -1 and `fixup_fn_call` patched `entry - (site + 4)` unchecked, so a never-defined name (or a fn-pointer variable called as a statement) became a call to one byte before the code: exit 0, SIGSEGV at run time; it now refuses by name (`cybs: undefined function: <name>`). (b) THE KERNEL `syscall` CLOBBERS rcx AND r11, and cybs keeps its parse cursor in r11: an error path that writes a message and then needs the current token reads r11 BEFORE the first `syscall` (copied into rbx). (c) cybs compiles every fn body BEFORE the top-level statements, so a top-level `const N = 3;` a fn reads is reported as the undefined name `N` at the READER's line, while the same const with no reader is a syntax error at its own line. (d) THE SEED'S 512-ENTRY LABEL TABLE IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT and has no bounds check: cybs.cyr is at 506 / 512 after 6.7.11 (6 left). A fall-through label nothing jumps to costs a slot like any other — 6.7.11 freed five (`_start`, the entry being the first code byte; two dead fns behind a `ret`; two fall-throughs kept as comments). Count labels before adding cybs code, keep only labels a jump targets, and size a feature to the headroom (the per-construct refusal and `file:line` did not fit — filed to the seed-rotation minor). The token-position array cybs needed lives in a cybs-private region (`r15 + 0xA000000`), off cycc's heap map, so no two-step bootstrap. (e) AN UNREACHABLE CAP IS STILL WORTH A ROW: the seed's 65,536 B code cap cannot be reached while the input cap holds (no fill directive: ~0.19 B of code per input byte), so no real over-cap input can be built; the row (assembled size minus EELF's 120 B header) is proven RED by lowering the cap in a scratch copy of the gate, not by a fixture. 6.7.14 (IN-11, IN-12): (a) IN cybs `loop`, `const` AND `do` LEX AS NAMES, so resolving the identifier before the fall-through to `parse_err` on ANY next token made `loop {` / `const N = 3;` / `do {` print `undefined variable or function: loop` instead of the class message that names what cybs lacks; the name report is narrowed to the filed shape (a name before `[`). Also: cybs keeps its local table after a fn, so a top-level name that was the LAST fn's local reads as known. (b) CYBS'S PREPROCESSOR CAPS ARE REFUSED BY NAME since 6.7.14: 15 MB of stdin, a 2 MB slot per include depth (`r15 + 0x9000000 + depth x 2 MB`), 8 depths, a 16 MB expansion; `src/main.cyr` expands to ~4.4 MB, its largest file ~0.95 MB (`parse_fn.cyr`), 3 deep. Row S2 of cybs_call_arity_named.sh watches the headroom, and its awk walker must EQUAL cybs's own expansion length (a patched copy of cybs writes it), so the walker cannot drift. Two traps: a check after the fact is not enough when the overflow runs past the brk end first (the expansion is checked at each line start); and the seed assembles only the SIGNED jcc set (je jne jl jg jle jge — no jae / ja). The cap side is in a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer (6.7.14). 6.7.15 (IT-3, IT-4): (a) THE LAST FN'S LOCALS ARE GONE AT TOP LEVEL — `parse_fn_def` cleared `in_function` but left `fn_local_count` standing (the 'Also' of 6.7.14 (a) above), so `zz[1] = 5;` after `fn f() { var zz = 1; .. }` got the class message; the tail now zeroes the count too (three instructions, no label) and cybs names `zz`. (b) cybs's INCLUDE-ONCE LIST (`r15 + 0x7710000`, 256 entries of 256 B, ending where depth 0's include name buffer begins) was unchecked: the 257th distinct include marked itself onto its own name, a 256+ character path ran into the next entry, and `pp_mark`'s QWORD stores wrote 7 bytes past each NUL — out of the LAST entry into depth 0's name, so the 256th include with a 249 – 255 character path was skipped silently. Both caps are refused by name through the shared `pp_cap_err` (no new label) and the stores are `movb` (infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715 #2, #3). The seed's label table: 508 / 512 after 6.7.15 (row S; 4 left); input 120,567 / 131,072 B."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cybs", "seed-derive", "bootstrap-chain", "gtgtgt", "arithmetic-shift", "_CF_ASR", "release-gate-item-2", "silent-failure", "global-reference-ceiling", "fixpoint-is-not-enough", "call-arity-six", "seventh-param-never-stored", "v6.4.74", "v6.6.12", "v6.6.16", "leading-underscore", "lexer-skip", "fn-table-2048", "seed-derive-step-6", "v6.6.17", "stack-arguments", "count_params", "unknown-byte-refused", "v6.7.6"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_heap_map_is_machine_read_prose_on_the_map_line_is_parsed_as_the_size_and_unit_suffixes_were_invisible"
description = "`src/main.cyr`'s heap map is NOT documentation — `tests/heapmap.sh` parses it with awk and it IS the overlap gate. Two parser holes made that gate blind for years, both fixed at v6.4.81, and both are things to know BEFORE editing a map line. (a) **Unit suffixes did not exist.** The size pattern was `/\\[([0-9]+)\\]/` — a bare integer only — so every entry written `[16 MB]`-style was skipped ENTIRELY and the region did not exist as far as the auditor was concerned. That hid **20.02 MB of live heap**: `ir_nodes [16 MB]`, `ir_cp [4 MB]`, the lex_pp file scratch `[16KB]` and three PP `#derive` scratch regions. The failure mode is worse than a missing row — the auditor then reported a **phantom ~21.4 MB free gap** above `ir_live_out` that is in fact fully occupied, so a future region placed 'in the free space' would land inside `ir_nodes` and the gate would report PASS. The pattern now accepts `KB/MB/K/M`. (b) **It took the LAST bracketed number on the line**, so prose after the size won. The `fn_param_struct_mask` line ends `(v6.3.36 issue [5]: bit N = …)` and was parsed as **FIVE BYTES** — off by 13,107x. The same trap immediately bit the v6.4.81 `include_fname` correction: the corrected line carried the old `0x190500 [256]` in an explanatory parenthetical and the region was re-parsed as 256 B. The parser now takes the FIRST bracketed size after the name. **DURABLE RULE: a map line carries offset, name, size and a short label — keep every explanatory parenthetical, historical address and issue reference on CONTINUATION lines that contain no `0x… [N]` pattern.** This is the third instance of the class: v5.5.40 fixed a whitespace pattern that had silently dropped every region past 0xFC000, because 7-char hex offsets left only ONE space before the name. A heap auditor that cannot see a region cannot guard it, and it reports green either way. State after the fix: **100 regions, 0 overlaps** — numerically the same as through v6.4.74 but by a different route (94 at .75, when six fn-indexed side tables went lazy-alloc and their fixed bands were freed; back to 100 at .81 when the parser learned to see unit-suffixed entries). Do not read the recurring '100' as the old number having been right all along. Mutation-prove any change here by planting a probe region inside an existing one and requiring exit 1. See CHANGELOG [5.5.40], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "heapmap.sh", "awk-parser", "unit-suffix", "prose-on-the-map-line", "continuation-lines", "phantom-free-gap", "ir_nodes", "fn_param_struct_mask", "region-count-100", "mutation-proof", "v5.5.40", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_heap_map_entry_can_document_a_region_no_code_has_ever_written_and_the_auditor_will_validate_the_fiction"
description = "The preprocessor include / `#ref` filename scratch was recorded in the heap map as `0x190500  include_fname [256]`. **No code has ever written 0x190500.** Every real use is `0x190400`, and it was UNBOUNDED — three separate copy loops (`include`, post-macro-expansion `include`, `#ref`) with no length check. So `tests/heapmap.sh` spent three minors proving non-overlap for a region that exists only in a comment, one page above a live region it therefore never checked. The live one could be walked straight through the include-count cell at `S+0x197F00`: `include \"zzz/<31490 A's>.cyr\"` **SIGSEGV'd cycc (exit 139)**, while 31480 bytes still gave a clean error — an exact boundary, with everything below it corrupting preprocessor state silently. That is the include filename-capture overflow bug (High), fixed at v6.4.81 by bounding all three loops at 4096 B through a shared `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` helper — split into its own fn because `PREPROCESS` was already near cybs's per-fn literal/global ceiling and seed-derive is the gate that would have caught the overflow. THE GENERALISATION: **the heap map is a machine-read CLAIM ABOUT the code, and nothing cross-checks the two directions.** A gate over the map proves the MAP is self-consistent; it says nothing about whether the code obeys it. So when auditing the map, do not read down the list checking arithmetic — **grep the tree for each base address, confirm a writer exists at exactly that address, and confirm its writes are bounded.** An entry with no writer is either dead (delete it) or a decoy sitting one page away from a live unbounded region (fix the code first, then the map). Companion instances from the very same audit, all three passing the gate: the map claimed a 13.3 MB TS frontend reservation that does not exist at all; `src/main.cyr` and `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` both quoted a stale TS base of `0x1D0B000` that lands inside `fixup_tbl`; and the live TS base overlapped `tok_types` by 10 MB. Sibling CVEs found the same way and worth the same treatment — `READFILE`'s `CYRIUS_HOME/lib/` fallback composed into `var fbuf[512]` with NEITHER copy loop bounded (the READFILE path-composition overflow bug), and `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is a BARE TOP-LEVEL array so it is 256x8 = 2048 B per the v6.4.10 contract, with a `HOME=` copy bounded only by `elen` and ~32 further bytes appended unconditionally afterwards (the long-HOME overflow bug). cycc compiles untrusted source by design; every one of these is on the wrong side of that boundary. See CHANGELOG [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "include_fname", "the include filename-capture overflow bug", "the READFILE path-composition overflow bug", "the long-HOME overflow bug", "unbounded-copy", "preprocessor", "PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG", "gate-validates-fiction", "grep-for-the-writer", "cybs-ceiling", "untrusted-source", "bare-toplevel-array-is-Nx8", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "win64_ecallpops_five_register_shuttle_broke_at_ten_args_and_the_bug_was_written_down_as_a_language_rule"
description = "**The Win64 caller-side stack-arg path corrupted argument 1 AND never wrote the stack args at all for any call with >=10 arguments** — silently, on a Tier-1 target, for about a year. Fixed v6.4.64. Win64 passes 4 args in `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` and the rest at `[rsp+32..]`; because cyrius pushes args left-to-right (aN on top) but Win64 wants a5 at the LOWEST stack slot, the caller must REVERSE the extras. The PE branch of `ECALLPOPS` did that by shuttling them through a FIXED 5-register table (`r10/r11/r14/r15/r12`) with `if (nextra >= 1..5)` pops and `if (nextra == 1..5)` write-backs. With `nextra = n - 4`, `n >= 10` fell off the end of its own table two ways at once: only 5 of the >=6 extras were popped, so the following `pop r9/r8/rdx/rcx` read SHIFTED slots and `rcx` (argument 1) got the wrong value; and no `nextra ==` branch matched, so the stack args were never emitted and the callee read garbage. Both silent — wrong answers, no error, no warning. Measured on real cass: arity 5-9 correct, 10/11/12 corrupt. SysV had been migrated off the identical register shuttle to rsp-relative addressing at v5.6.24/v6.0.57 for exactly this bug class — **the Win64 branch was never brought along**, which is the durable structural warning: when one ABI branch is rewritten to fix a class, open the sibling branch in the same commit. FIX SHAPE if you touch it again: load `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` IN PLACE via `_emov_rsp_disp` (disp8/disp32 auto-select, no pops); carve the frame BELOW the pushed args so the reversed copy's destination is strictly below its source and cannot overlap at ANY arity (an in-place reversal cannot be done by a single ascending or descending shift — every order clobbers a slot a later read still needs); reverse-copy through `r10`, which is CALLER-saved (the v5.6.24 lesson — r12-r15 are callee-saved and regalloc may pin caller locals there); and share one frame formula between `ECALLPOPS` and `ECALLCLEAN` (`_pe_call_frame`: 32 shadow + nextra*8, 16-rounded, +8 parity pad for odd `n` — the old path got alignment free by popping all `n` slots first, so an in-place path must pay for it explicitly). **WHY IT SURVIVED: it had been written into CLAUDE.md as a LANGUAGE RULE** — 'fns take <=6 args cleanly (register ABI); args 7+ go on the stack and have shown corruption — restructure instead.' Wrong in BOTH directions (7-9 were always fine; the cliff was 10, on one target), it read as settled so nobody re-measured, and on 2026-07-14 it was cited to file an issue **against sigil**, asking a stdlib to restructure around a defect in the compiler that compiles it. The release gate also could not see it structurally: sigil sits outside cycc's include closure and cycc's own PE calls happen to stay <=9 args. The live consumer was sigil 3.12.0 — `argon2id_into` takes 10 args, `argon2_hash_into` 17 — i.e. a password KDF returning a wrong-but-plausible hash, and sandhi's HTTP client (11 fns taking >=10 args, 15 call sites passing >=10) was miscompiled on Win64 in both 1.8.2 and 1.9.0. **THIS IS THE LANGUAGE REPO: when the compiler cannot compile valid cyrius, fix the compiler. If a rule here tells you to work around codegen, treat the rule as the bug report.** Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/win64_stack_args.tcyr` (arity 5/9/10/17 + nested), mutation-tested on real cass — old codegen 2 passed / 3 failed, fixed 5 / 0. See CHANGELOG [6.4.64]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["win64", "PE", "ECALLPOPS", "ECALLCLEAN", "stack-args", "nextra", "arity", "_pe_call_frame", "r10-caller-saved", "sysv-migrated-pe-was-not", "codegen-bug-as-language-rule", "sigil-argon2", "sandhi-http", "vr01_win64_stack_args", "v5.6.24", "v6.4.64"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_temporal_invariant_can_guard_a_spatial_collision_invisibly_ts_arena_overlapped_tok_types_by_10mb"
description = "The TS frontend arena was a FIXED base — `S + 0x298B000`, with `TS_HEAP_SIZE = 0xD81000` — so it ran `0x298B000..0x370C000` and **overlapped `tok_types` entirely plus 1.6 MB of `tok_values`: 10,027,008 bytes.** Nothing was ever corrupted, because all three TS modes (`--lex-ts`, `--parse-ts`, `--emit-js`) `SYS_EXIT` before `PREPROCESS` and `LEX`, so the token arrays are never populated while the arena is live. **A TEMPORAL invariant guarding a SPATIAL collision** — held by nothing but call ordering, written down nowhere, and one added 'lex the TS then keep going' path away from a 10 MB heap smash. TWO THINGS MADE IT UNDETECTABLE. `tests/heapmap.sh` structurally could not see it: the base was a **code literal at the call sites, not a map entry**, and the auditor only knows the map. And the map actively said something else — it claimed a 13.3 MB TS reservation between `fixup_tbl` end (`0x207B000`) and `tok_types` (`0x2D7C000`), which does not exist and is 524,288 B smaller than `TS_HEAP_SIZE` regardless. THE ORIGIN IS THE DURABLE PART: `0x298B000 - 0x207B000 = 9,502,720` — **exactly the 9.06 MB the v5.11.68 reorg shrank the heap by.** That reorg moved ~35 region bases and MISSED THIS ONE LITERAL, which had been the pre-reorg `fixup_tbl` end. Which is the entire argument for the v6.4.82 fix: the arena is now `alloc(TS_HEAP_SIZE)`, taken only when a TS mode is actually active, so a normal compile pays nothing and the brk carries no TS carve-out at all. `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` had documented `alloc()` as the contract the test drivers use all along — the fixed base was the deviation. **A literal is what the next reorg misses too.** Relocating to another fixed base was never really available anyway: it needs ~14.2 MB contiguous, which does not exist below the arena end, so a fixed-base fix would have been a brk/heap-LAYOUT change requiring the two-step bootstrap — which is why v6.4.81 deliberately filed it rather than bundling it into the tail of an already-gated release. LESSONS: when auditing the heap, grep for `S + 0x` literals in CALL ARGUMENTS, not just map lines; a subsystem reachable only in an early-exit mode is exactly where a spatial invariant rots unnoticed, because no test ever runs past the exit; and prefer `alloc()` to a new fixed base for anything not required to sit at a fixed offset. See CHANGELOG [5.11.68], [6.4.81], [6.4.82]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "ts-arena", "TS_HEAP_SIZE", "temporal-invariant", "spatial-collision", "tok_types", "heap-layout", "alloc-over-fixed-base", "v5.11.68-reorg", "missed-literal", "heapmap-blind-spot", "two-step-bootstrap", "early-exit-mode", "v6.4.82"]

[[entries]]
name = "toktyp_reads_past_gtcnt_as_token_type_0_never_12_eof_so_every_eof_test_fails_and_recovery_cannot_terminate"
description = "`TOKTYP` (`src/backend/common/tokens.cyr`) is an unchecked `L64`. Past `GTCNT` it reads zeroed heap and returns token type **`0`, never `12` (EOF)**. Once panic-mode multi-error recovery existed (v6.4.62) that had a compounding consequence: every `t == 12` EOF test in `_sync_skip` and in the block loops silently failed, recovery could never terminate normally, and `PARSE_STMT`'s forced-progress guard was disarmed too — it only advances while `GTI < GTCNT`, which is exactly the condition that is false out there. Input ending mid-construct — a clipped file, an unclosed `fn`, EOF mid-expression or mid-arg-list — produced **166,670 stderr lines** before the v6.4.62 watchdog aborted. Clamped to EOF inside `PEEKT` at v6.4.78: 166,670 -> 5 lines (unclosed `fn`: 3). **THE PERF OBJECTION THAT GOT IT DEFERRED DID NOT SURVIVE CONTACT WITH THE FIX.** It was filed as its own slot because `PEEKT` is THE hottest function in the parser and a compare+branch there is a hot-path change. But `PEEKT` ALREADY has an `if (_had_error == 1)` guard for the watchdog, and the runaway is exclusively a POST-ERROR phenomenon — LEX unconditionally appends an EOF token and every parse loop stops on type 12, so a well-formed parse never advances past it. Putting the clamp INSIDE that existing guard therefore costs nothing on a clean compile: measured **-0.07 %** (672.8 vs 673.3 ms, interleaved medians on one box), size-neutral, 251/251 tcyr byte-identical. Clamping unconditionally in `TOKTYP` would also work and would tax every lookahead for no benefit. LESSON: **before deferring a hot-path fix on cost, check whether an existing guard already gates the path** — the cheap placement often already exists, and the fix that was 'too expensive to bundle' turns out to be free. The v6.4.62 watchdog is kept deliberately alongside the clamp — a `_had_error`-gated stall detector in `PEEKT` (>500,000 reads with `GTI` unmoved, far above any legal recursive-descent depth so it never false-fires) that bounds ANY future desync the clamp does not anticipate, and it is what broke the VR-02 fuzz wall that had stopped two prior attempts at multi-error recovery. GATE THE SHAPE THAT MATTERS: a garbage-TOKEN input can happen to recover — the pre-existing case did, and missed this entirely — so the gate uses inputs ending MID-CONSTRUCT, asserts <=200 stderr lines, no hang, no SIGSEGV, no output, **and that the watchdog does NOT fire** (its firing is itself the regression signal). Independently fuzz-verified with 60 deterministic random truncations of `lib/str.cyr`: worst case 166,680 -> 18 lines, 0 hangs. See CHANGELOG [6.4.62], [6.4.78]. 6.7.14 — TWO MORE HALVES OF THE SAME CLASS. (a) THE cybs HALF (IN-10): cybs's pass-0 skippers `shallow_fn` / `shallow_global` / `shallow_g_col` stepped over the name (and the type name) with an unconditional `inc r11`; at the end of the input that stepped PAST the EOF token, and every later EOF test read beyond the token array. A skipper must test the token it consumes; an input that ends right after `fn` or `var` is now refused. (b) THE DIAGNOSTIC HALF (FE-7): every `ERR_EXPECT(S, 14)` in parse_ctrl.cyr was followed by a step that moved the cursor PAST the EOF token, so the next block's error read a slot with no line or offset and printed `error:0:1:`. One clamp in `_err_head_at` and `_err_excerpt` (`_err_ti`) covers every emitter — clamp where the token is READ for a report, not at each step."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["TOKTYP", "PEEKT", "GTCNT", "EOF-token-12", "token-type-0", "panic-mode", "_sync_skip", "PARSE_STMT-progress-guard", "watchdog", "hot-path-objection", "existing-guard", "VR-02-fuzz", "truncated-input", "v6.4.62", "v6.4.78"]

[[entries]]
name = "textual_includes_mean_per_item_file_identity_must_be_stamped_at_preprocess_time_and_the_parser_is_two_pass"
description = "Per-item FILE IDENTITY is the whole substrate under `public`/`private` (v6.5.0), and cyrius includes are TEXTUAL — by parse time there is one flat expanded buffer and the origin file of each fn/var has to have been recorded on the way through. Three traps, all of which shipped green before being caught. (1) **The preprocessor must emit a RESUME marker after an include closes, not just an ENTER marker before it opens.** With enter-only markers every fn declared in the enclosing file AFTER an include is attributed to the INCLUDED file, so `alloc_init` and `atomic_cas` — different files — collided on id 19 and were mutually visible under `private`. **My Phase 1 gate passed anyway because the fixture put its includes first and declared nothing after them**, which is the shape that cannot observe the bug; the rebuilt gate uses a nested include AND code-before-include, and was run RED first to prove it. If you add file tracking to a textual-include preprocessor, the fixture must have code after an include or the gate is decorative. (2) **The parser is TWO-PASS and a pending-modifier flag leaks across the boundary.** `_pub_pending` was consumed in `PARSE_FN_DEF` (pass 2) but not in `_prescan_fn_sig` (pass 1), so the flag set by a `public` in pass 1 survived into the next file — `private` worked in a main source and SILENTLY did nothing when the file arrived via an include. Any per-item modifier needs consuming in BOTH passes; stamp the fileid in both too. (3) **A byte-identical binary is a TELL, not a reassurance.** The Phase 2b var stamp was inserted inside the annotated-only branch (`ann_scalar < 8`), so it fired only for `var x: i32` and never for a bare `var x = 7;`. Nothing failed — the rebuild just came back byte-identical when a real behaviour change had been made, which is the signal that the edit is on a path the compiler's own source never takes. Move the stamp before the width branch (`SVCNT`) and it changes. RECOGNITION for the class: when adding cross-file semantics to a language whose includes are textual, every question of the form 'which file did this come from' is answered at PREPROCESS time or not at all — the parser cannot recover it later. See CHANGELOG [6.5.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "parse_return_tail_path_is_the_third_place_found_missing_what_parse_fncall_has"
description = "The overload-suffix dispatch carried TWO independent defects from v5.10.25 to v6.5.1, and the pair is a lesson about WHERE to look rather than about overloads. (1) **The redirect never checked the TARGET's arity** — it decided on argument 1's type alone, so `base(oneArg)` could be rewritten into a call to a two-parameter `base_str`, surplus parameter bound to whatever occupied the register, reported as a WARNING with the binary emitted anyway. (2) **`PARSE_RETURN`'s tail-call fast path never consulted the overload tables at all** — it emits epilogue+jmp itself — so `var r = println(myStr)` routed to `println_str` while `return println(myStr)` silently called the cstr base. **The same call, spelled two ways, ran two different functions.** RECOGNITION: when a transformation lives in ONE call-parsing function, enumerate every path that emits a call without going through it — cyrius has at least three (normal `PARSE_FNCALL`, the `PARSE_RETURN` tail path, inline replay), and this is the THIRD time the tail path was found missing something the normal path had (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, now overload dispatch). The fix is the same each time and it is NOT to duplicate the logic: set `tc_has_addr = 1` to divert onto the normal path. **THE MEASUREMENT THAT MATTERS**: the arity fix changes **0 of 253** .tcyr while the tail divert changes **81** — attribute a differential per-fix instead of reporting one total, because '0 files changed' is not reassurance that the fix is inert, it is proof the CORPUS HAD ZERO COVERAGE OF THE SHAPE, which is exactly why the defect survived five minors. Identical lesson to v6.4.80 (251/251 byte-identical across a real wrong-answer fix). Also: escalating the arity check from warning to error was verified safe by measuring FIRST — the whole repo (253 .tcyr, all 7 main forks, programs/) emits zero such warnings — and it deliberately does NOT set `_panic`, since an arity mismatch is not a token-stream desync and all bad sites should report in one compile. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1]. 6.7.11, a fourth and fifth time (ST-6, ST-7): `return mk();` of ANOTHER struct and `return gp();` of a `: *S` callee were emitted as a tail `jmp` before any struct check; `_tc_small_sret_divert` sends both onto the normal path — the same fix as each time before."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "qemu_user_reports_a_false_thread_va_leak_because_proc_self_statm_measures_the_host_process"
description = "`tests/tcyr/crossos/thread_detach.tcyr` (v6.5.8) asserts that 100 detached threads grow virtual address space by **exactly 0** — before the fix they grew it by **210,124,800 B**, i.e. 100 x 2,101,248 (`THREAD_STACK_SIZE + THREAD_GUARD_SIZE`) to the byte, with `vm.max_map_count` (65530) as the ceiling, so a thread-per-request server degraded to `thread_create` returning 0 after ~65K spawns. **DO NOT TRUST `qemu-aarch64` FOR THIS TEST — IT REPORTS A FALSE LEAK.** Under qemu-user the file fails with ~1,665 residual pages per detached thread, which is not even the 513-page stack: qemu maps its OWN per-thread emulator state into the same address space, and `/proc/self/statm` measures the HOST process, not the guest. On real pi the identical binary reports a delta of exactly 0 and the file passes 115/0. Same class as the v6.4.42 aarch64 epoll bug that only real pi could surface — **if a VA/mapping assertion goes red under qemu, run it on pi before believing it.** THREE MORE MEASUREMENT TRAPS baked into that file, each of which cost a false reading while it was being written. (1) **VA, not RSS.** Thread stacks are touch-on-demand, so the leak showed as ~200 resident pages while VA grew 51,300 — an RSS-based test misses it entirely. (2) **Poll until it settles; never sample at a fixed instant.** A spin loop is not a synchronisation primitive: on a slow machine the children simply have not reached their `munmap` yet, which reads as a leak. The property that actually distinguishes fixed from broken is that VA comes BACK DOWN AT ALL — with the leak it never does, so a bounded retry loop (400 x 5 ms) separates 'slow' from 'broken' where a fixed sleep cannot. (3) **Let the warm-up threads finish BEFORE sampling the baseline**, or they unmap during the measurement and you get a NEGATIVE growth of -513 pages — exactly one thread — the measurement racing the thing it measures. **AND ASSERT ZERO, NOT 'LESS'**: the detached child unmaps its stack AND the TLS block carved from the top of that same mapping and allocates no handle at all, so 0 is the correct number and a threshold like '< 20 MiB' would still pass with the TLS or the handle leaking. See CHANGELOG [6.4.42], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["qemu-user", "false-positive", "proc-self-statm", "host-vs-guest", "thread_create_detached", "VA-not-RSS", "poll-until-settled", "baseline-race", "assert-zero-not-less", "real-pi-hardware", "vr01_thread_detach", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_does_not_inline_so_a_one_load_accessor_on_a_dispatch_path_is_a_real_call_frame"
description = """⟳ **CORRECTED v6.6.1 — the flat claim below is no longer true.** `#inline` became a
REAL directive at v6.5.63 (token 163) and the inline-replay machinery (`SFINL`,
`_INLINE_OK`) is live. What remains true is the DEFAULT: `_INLINE_OK = 0`, so ordinary
calls are still not inlined unless a fn is marked, and the accessor cost below is still
what you pay on an unmarked dispatch path. Read the measurement, not the absolute.

**cyrius does not inline ordinary calls BY DEFAULT, so every unmarked accessor is a real frame — and an accessor whose entire body is one `load64` costs strictly more than the load it wraps.** `alloc_via` (`lib/alloc.cyr`), the hot path of the whole `_a` allocator convention, cost **15.1 ns** while the `arena_alloc` bump underneath it is about eight instructions: **5.1 ns of that was two accessor CALLS** — `allocator_alloc_fn(a)` to perform one `load64(a)` and `allocator_state(a)` to perform one `load64(a + 32)` — against ~6.0 ns for the bump itself. A third level was pure trampoline: `_arena_alloc`, whose entire body was `return arena_alloc(state, size)`. v6.5.10 reads the vtable inline in the four dispatch helpers (`alloc_via` / `realloc_via` / `free_via` / `reset_via`) and registers `&arena_alloc` / `&arena_reset` directly: **15-16 -> 12 ns** from the inlining, **-> 11 ns** with the trampolines gone. The accessor inlining is the large half. **THIS IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE `allocator_new` FIXES THE LAYOUT** — reading `a + 0` / `a + 32` directly is exactly as safe as calling the accessors, which remain as public API; only the hot path stopped calling them. **DO NOT GENERALISE THE INLINING INTO A DELETION.** `_bump_alloc` and `_bump_reset` look like the same trampoline shape and are NOT: they ADAPT ARITY (`alloc(size)` and `alloc_reset()` take fewer arguments than the vtable signature passes), so they genuinely translate rather than pass through, and removing them corrupts the call. The distinction — a frame that only forwards vs. a frame that changes the argument list — is the thing to check before deleting any wrapper, and `tests/alloc_via_no_plumbing.sh` asserts both directions. **WHY A FEW NANOSECONDS EARNED A RELEASE SLOT:** `_a` threading multiplies the cost by the size of the object graph, which is the entire point of the `_a` families. agnosai counted **112 allocations on one route — 32 % of the request** — with a counting allocator wrapped around the arena's own vtable, and that was AFTER a hoisting pass; `bayan_json_v_obj_new_a` alone is three `alloc_via` calls. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."""
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["no-inlining", "alloc_via", "allocator-vtable", "dispatch-plumbing", "trampoline", "_bump_alloc-adapts-arity", "arity-adapter-vs-passthrough", "_a-convention", "allocator_new-fixes-layout", "alloc_via_no_plumbing", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "negating_at_i64_min_is_a_no_op_so_every_while_n_gt_0_decimal_formatter_printed_a_bare_minus"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a **NO-OP at `i64::MIN`** — two's-complement absolute value overflows back to itself — so a formatter written as 'emit the sign, negate, then `while (n > 0)` peel digits' emits the sign byte **and nothing else**. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string `\"-\"`, and bayan then serialised `{\"n\":-}`, which is not JSON, **with no error raised at any layer**. Both the `n == 0` special case and the digit loop are skipped, because the negated value is still negative. Fixed at v6.5.8 across **12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named**: four in `lib/fmt.cyr`, plus `string.cyr`, `log.cyr`, sakshi (fixed UPSTREAM at 2.4.8 and re-vendored, per 'fix the source repo, not the fold'), **two inside the compiler itself that the roadmap never listed** — `PRNUM` (`src/common/util.cyr:849`) and `_emit_decimal` (`src/main.cyr:888`) — and three shipped demos. **THE PART WORTH CARRYING IS THAT THIS EXACT BUG HAD ALREADY BEEN FIXED IN THE FUNCTION DIRECTLY ABOVE.** `fmt_hex` sits immediately below `fmt_int` in the same file and was corrected for the same `> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at v6.4.69 — **the decimal siblings were never brought along**, which is the same one-branch-at-a-time shape as the Darwin `AT_*` flags and the macOS `Stat` enum. When you fix a loop-termination predicate in one formatter, grep the whole tree for the predicate, not the function name. PREREQUISITE VERIFIED BEFORE RELYING ON IT: truncation toward zero for negative `/` and `%` was confirmed on x86_64, aarch64 **and** cx, since the digit-peel for a still-negative `n` depends on it. See CHANGELOG [6.4.69], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["i64-min", "twos-complement", "0-minus-n-is-a-noop", "fmt_int", "fmt_hex", "PRNUM", "_emit_decimal", "gt-0-vs-ne-0", "sakshi-2.4.8", "fix-upstream-then-fold", "truncation-toward-zero", "grep-the-predicate-not-the-fn", "v6.4.69", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "an_allocator_returning_0_is_not_an_error_channel_when_the_type_system_has_no_option"
description = "**The arena primitives all handled exhaustion CORRECTLY and that was not the bug.** `arena_alloc` returns 0, `str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. The bug is what the 0 does NEXT: a `Str` of 0 is **indistinguishable from a valid `Str`**, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and the first thing that dereferences it faults. 'Arena too small' was observable as a **SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.** **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MADE IT SERIOUS:** before threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on the no-free global bump; after threading — a change whose entire purpose is to stop leaking — the same response was a *crash*. **Adopting the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter**, and a 5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of items and nothing about that shape ever probes the ceiling. v6.5.9 ships one **policy field** (`a + 24`) rather than three features: `ARENA_FULL_NULL` (0, today's behaviour and still the default, so no existing arena moves) / `ARENA_FULL_GROW` (1, chain another chunk — `arena_new_growable` / `arena_allocator_growable`) / `ARENA_FULL_SPILL` (2, serve overflow from the global allocator) / `ARENA_FULL_ABORT` (3, die loudly at the allocation instead of faulting three layers away). FOUR THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TOUCHING IT. (1) **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`** — the bump allocator underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible, and it is not what an arena wants anyway: reset rewinds to the first chunk and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water mark and then allocates nothing (verified: 500 allocations twice over produce identical total capacity). (2) `arena_used` cannot show the whole chain once it has grown — that is what `arena_capacity_total` is for. (3) `arena_free` previously cleared only base/ptr/end and **left the policy set**, so a 'freed' growable arena would take the grow path and **succeed**; it now clears the policy back to `ARENA_FULL_NULL`. (4) **MUTATION TESTING CHANGED THE CODE HERE, NOT JUST THE GATE**: the mutation 'grow ignores an oversize request' PASSED, because `arena_alloc` returned the post-grow pointer **without re-checking the bound** — a silent heap overflow, not a failed allocation — and the test could not see it either, asserting only `!= 0`. Both were fixed: a defensive bound re-check, and a first/last-byte write-and-read-back that only survives if the memory is really there. `lib/alloc.cyr` is inside cycc's include closure and on the seed path, so all of this carried the full fixpoint + seed-derive gate. See CHANGELOG [6.5.9]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arena", "exhaustion-policy", "ARENA_FULL_NULL", "ARENA_FULL_GROW", "ARENA_FULL_SPILL", "ARENA_FULL_ABORT", "zero-is-not-an-error-channel", "no-option-type", "regression-direction", "grow-retains-chunks", "arena_capacity_total", "arena_free-left-policy-set", "mutation-changed-the-code", "seed-path", "v6.5.9"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_distlib_deps_sidecar_scanned_includes_not_the_declaration_and_a_wrong_sidecar_switches_off_cyriuss_own_consumer_check"
description = "⚠ SUPERSEDED in cyrius 6.6.18 (P4 option 2): the union below was REMOVED — the sidecar is now derived only by the compile-verify fixpoint over six targets, which catches the setu case on its own (see `a_verify_that_only_adds_to_a_seed_hides_every_gap_the_seed_happened_to_cover`). Kept for the disabled-check lesson. `dist/<lib>.deps` says it lists 'stdlib leaves this fold requires in scope', but until v6.5.10 it was built **ONLY by scanning bundled module sources for literal `include \"lib/X.cyr\"` lines** — so it captured what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the manifest declaration. setu 0.8.2 emitted **8** leaves against a declared **12**; the four missing (`result`, `net`, `chrono`, `args`) are all referenced at top level in `src/client.cyr`. **THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT A MISLEADING FILE — IT IS A DISABLED CHECK.** `cyrius deps` validates a consumer's `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's sidecar, correctly and loudly. The filer measured both directions: with a corrected 12-leaf sidecar an under-declaring consumer got a hard error naming `chrono` and `args`; with the raw 8-leaf one **the same consumer built `OK`, silently**, on agnos. A wrong sidecar switches OFF the machine check that exists to catch exactly that mistake. Fixed by making the sidecar the declared `[deps] stdlib` **UNIONED** with the include-scan, not either alone — the scan can legitimately catch a leaf the author forgot to declare, so the union can never under-report relative to either source. **THE CONSTRAINT THAT KEEPS THE FIX FROM BEING WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: base bundle only.** A `[lib.X]` PROFILE is a narrow subset of modules, so its true dep set is a SUBSET of the declaration; unioning the whole declaration into e.g. a `sha` profile would OVER-report and could fail a legitimately-narrow consumer against the very check this re-enables. Profiles keep the pruned inference, and `tests/distlib_deps_sidecar.sh` asserts that asymmetry explicitly. **THREE HYPOTHESES WERE MEASURED AND FALSIFIED BY THE FILER BEFORE FILING** — it is not the declaration, not an inline-comment parse bug, not a direct-symbol scan — and they are recorded in the gate so none of them gets re-investigated. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "deps-sidecar", "cyrius-deps-validation", "under-report", "union-declaration-with-scan", "base-bundle-only", "profiles-keep-pruned-inference", "over-report-hazard", "setu", "falsified-hypotheses-in-the-gate", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_tail_call_guard_was_syntactic_so_a_pointer_laundered_through_a_local_freed_the_frame_under_the_callee"
description = "`return f(args)` takes cycc's tail-call path: the epilogue tears the frame DOWN and JMPs, so the callee executes on released stack. v5.8.16 §8 already knew this and guarded it — **by scanning the call's argument list for a literal `&` token**. That scan is SYNTACTIC, so it only ever saw the direct spelling. `return f(&buf)` was guarded from v5.8.16; `var p = &buf; ... return f(p)` walked straight past it and read dead stack. **Same source semantics, opposite results, no diagnostic — a silent wrong answer, not a crash.** Fixed at v6.5.14 by replacing the syntactic test with a semantic one: `_fn_local_addr` is set at the SINGLE `ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` site in `parse_expr.cyr` wherever a fn materialises the address of one of its own locals, reset per fn in `PARSE_FN_DEF`, and read by the guard in `parse_fn.cyr`. One site covers every laundering path — through variables, arithmetic, structs, globals — because they all have to go through `&local` first. A fn that never takes a local's address keeps the flag at 0, so its codegen is byte-identical (cycc itself grew **16 bytes**). **THE ARITY IS WHY IT HID FOR YEARS AND IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING:** cycc already declines TCO above **6 arguments** (stack args are clobbered by the epilogue), so the defect is reachable ONLY at ≤6 args — the identical helper called with 7 args was fine. Downstream that does not look like a compiler bug, it looks like a cursed *buffer*. sigil hit exactly this: its 10-arg RSA verify wrappers localised cleanly at 3.12.3 while their 6-arg SIGN twins 'broke the KATs', so **three consecutive sigil releases (3.12.3, .4, .5) each shipped a source comment saying localisation regressed tests for reasons not understood** — and the shared `cbank()` lanes that comment justified were an **authentication bypass** (1 of 800 forged PSS signatures accepted under lane aliasing). **THE DIAGNOSTIC LESSON IS SHARPER THAN THE FIX.** 3.12.3 recorded that 'callee-clobbers-caller was ruled out'; that came from a probe window over the buffer's first 8 bytes, and the corruption was at **offset 223**. A probe that cannot see the defect is not evidence of absence — it froze a security fix for three releases. The measurement that finally settled it was two addresses printed side by side: a callee's local sat ABOVE every one of the caller's locals, which is only possible if the caller's frame is already gone. **This is the FIFTH thing found missing from this tail path** (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, v6.5.1 overload dispatch, v6.5.2 the literal-to-cstring error, now this) — when you touch `PARSE_FNCALL`, ask what the tail path does NOT do. Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/tailcall_frame_escape.tcyr`, in `crossos/` because the epilogue exists on every backend and the frame it frees is ABI-shaped; acceptance is **aliased ≡ direct**, not aliased ≡ a literal. See CHANGELOG [6.5.14], sigil [3.12.6]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["tail-call", "TCO", "frame-escape", "_fn_local_addr", "v5.8.16-section-8", "syntactic-vs-semantic-guard", "ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR", "6-arg-threshold", "silent-wrong-answer", "sigil-3.12.6", "rsa-pss", "authentication-bypass", "probe-window-too-narrow", "offset-223", "fifth-miss-on-the-tail-path", "crossos-gate", "v6.5.14"]

[[entries]]
name = "visibility_enforced_at_reference_not_insertion"
description = "A feature enforced only where a name is USED is not a boundary — private fns collided for five minors (v6.5.0-.37)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: `private` was implemented as a CHECK AT
THE REFERENCE SITE layered on top of an UNCHANGED global symbol table. That
makes the feature look complete — the error message fires, the gate is green,
`nm` output shrinks — while the thing it claims to guarantee is absent.

What actually happened (v6.5.0 through v6.5.37): the fn table held exactly one
entry per NAME. A second definition reused that entry and overwrote its body,
so a private helper was silently replaced by any same-named fn in another file,
INCLUDING for its own file's internal calls. Declaring `private` on BOTH sides
changed nothing, because neither declaration touched INSERTION.

    a.cyr:  private fn _helper(s) { return 1; }
            public  fn a_entry(s) { return _helper(s); }
    b.cyr:  private fn _helper(c) { return 0; }
            public  fn b_entry(c) { return _helper(c); }

    a_entry(0) * 10 + b_entry(0)  ->  exit 0.   Correct is 10.

⭐ THE TELL WAS IN THE DOCS THE WHOLE TIME. The guide promised "private fns are
omitted from the exported symbol table". That is a statement about the SYMBOL
TABLE, and nothing in the implementation touched the symbol table. When a
feature's documented guarantee names a data structure the code never modifies,
the feature is a check, not a boundary.

⚠ AND THE WARNING ACTIVELY MISLED. The collision surfaced as
`duplicate fn ... last definition wins`, which reads as benign shadowing and
usually is. It is not benign when the two definitions disagree about arity,
struct layout, or RETURN POLARITY — and nothing in the warning says which case
you are in. The filed instance (vyakarana + sankoch, both defining a private
`_stream_grow`) disagreed about all three; a wrong binding there reports every
successful buffer grow as a FAILURE, inside a tokenizer, on file content.

⚠ ZERO ADOPTION HID IT. 0 of 292 first-party files across seven repos declared
`private`, so nothing in the ecosystem exercised the boundary for five minors.
A feature with no users generates no bug reports — that is not evidence it
works. The first consumer to actually try it filed this within days.

⚠ THE GATE COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT, for a mechanical reason worth copying:
the v6.5.37 gate builds every probe by CONCATENATING sources into one file. The
defect keys on ORIGIN FILE, so every concatenated probe gets a single file id
and exercises nothing. A multi-file defect needs a multi-file gate, on disk,
with real `include`s.

FIX: split same-named definitions into separate symbols at insertion when a
private one is involved and the origin files differ; resolve preferring the
caller's own private definition, then the first non-private one, then fall
through so the precise privacy error still fires rather than "undefined
function". See tests/gates/frontend/private_scoped_at_insertion.sh (v6.5.38).

[[entries]]
name = "one_shot_process_registration_closes_the_raw_thread_path"
description = "bsdthread_register is one-shot per process and libSystem always takes it first, so arm64-macOS cannot install its own thread trampoline (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: a kernel facility can be PRESENT, correctly
ROUTED, and still UNUSABLE, because something else in the process already consumed
a one-shot resource. Routing proves reachability, not availability.

v6.5.43 routed all five Darwin concurrency syscalls on both Mach-O backends and
proved them reaching the kernel on real ecb and ach. Every one of those routes is
correct. And `bsdthread_create` is STILL unusable on arm64-macOS, because:

  * `bsdthread_register` installs the process-wide thread-start trampoline and is
    ONE-SHOT. A second call returns EINVAL.
  * cyrius's arm64 Mach-O writer emits a dyld/LC_MAIN PIE that links
    /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib, so libpthread's initializer registers first.
  * Measured from a REAL cyrius-compiled binary on ecb: exit 22 (EINVAL).
  * A libSystem-free process is not an escape either — on macOS 26 a static
    LC_UNIXTHREAD arm64 binary is SIGKILLed by AppleSystemPolicy.

⭐ THE ASYMMETRY IS THE INTERESTING PART, and it is a property of the two WRITERS,
not of the OS. The x86 Mach-O writer emits a static MH_NOUNDEFS/LC_UNIXTHREAD binary
with no dyld and no libSystem — so on Intel-Mac registration DOES work and the raw
bsdthread path is the right one, while on Apple Silicon it is closed. One "#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS" spans two targets whose thread primitives cannot be the same.

WHAT WORKS INSTEAD, and it needed no new machinery at all: __got[5] had been bound
to `_pthread_create` since v5.6.6 and never used. No GOT growth, no new symbol, no
bind-opcode arithmetic. And no trampoline: a Darwin start routine is
`void *(*)(void *)`, which is exactly the shape of a cyrius `fn body(arg): i64` —
arg 1 in x0, result in x0, stock AAPCS64 prologue.

⚠ THE TEMPTING WRONG TURN was to hand-build a pthread_t and use libpthread's
already-registered trampoline. It WORKS — it was prototyped end to end — but it
depends on sig@0x00 (validated against a per-process ptr_munge cookie), fun@0x90,
arg@0x98, tsd@0xE0 and a PAC modifier, none of which is stable ABI. Working is not
the bar; surviving an OS update is.

[[entries]]
name = "check_which_manifest_key_CONSUMERS_write_not_the_one_your_repo_writes"
description = "v6.5.51: v6.5.49 taught `cyrius build` to fall back to `[build] src` / `output` in ./cyrius.cyml when the positional arguments are omitted. Measured across ~/Repos at v6.5.51: **120 of 125 cyrius.cyml files declare `entry`, and only 5 declare `src`** — one of the five being the cyrius repo itself, which is where the implementer read the key from. So the convenience shipped INERT for 96 % of the ecosystem: a pre-fix CLI handed an `entry=` manifest prints the usage text and exits 1, which reads as 'this feature does not exist'. ⚠ AND ITS GATE PASSED, because the gate's fixture manifest was written with the same key the implementation read. That is the transferable failure: a gate authored alongside an implementation tends to encode the implementation's assumptions, so it confirms the code does what the code does rather than what users need. LESSON: when a feature reads a config/manifest key, count the key across real consumer repos BEFORE implementing (`awk '/^\\[build\\]/{f=1;next}/^\\[/{f=0}f&&/^(src|entry)/' ~/Repos/*/cyrius.cyml`), and make the gate's fixture use the key CONSUMERS write. Fixed by accepting both with `entry` first, plus a gate axis for each and one for the both-keys precedence."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "copy_semantics_beats_lifetime_analysis_for_unboxing_a_sum_type"
description = "v6.5.55: the way to stop boxing a payload enum was NOT to find a safe place to put the box — it was to stop having one. Two relocations were disproven; the register pair was never in that set."
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.15 tried to stop cyrius boxing `Ok(v)`/`Err(e)` by RELOCATING the 16-byte box to a
per-call-site anonymous global. It passed every gate and shipped a compiler that "reported a
failed file open as SUCCESS in a retaining loop", and was reverted. The project's standing note
then read: caller-frame is too short-lived (`?` in an unannotated helper returns the box out of
that helper's frame), a global is too shared, therefore "no relocation-based design can work;
the survivors are escape analysis or a scope-tied arena with reclaim."

⭐ THAT ENUMERATION SILENTLY DROPPED THE OPTION THAT WORKS, and the dropped option needed no
new machinery: return `(tag, payload)` in the multi-return register pair the language already
had. **Copy semantics kills both failure modes at once** — each binding is its own copy of two
registers, so two values built at one call site cannot alias, and there is no storage for
anything to dangle into. Verified before designing anything: a probe of
`fn mk2(x): (i64, i64)` called at ONE call site in a loop returned distinct values with
`alloc_used()` delta of exactly 0.

The general trap: **when every candidate is a variant of "where do we put the object", the
question to ask is whether there needs to be an object.** Lifetime analysis is the expensive
answer to a question that value semantics deletes.

⚠ WHAT IT COST TO NOT SEE THIS: the standing note was framed as a closed set of survivors, so
each revisit re-derived escape analysis and re-concluded it was too big. A closed-set claim in
a design doc deserves the same premise-check as a perf number — ask what it EXCLUDED and why.

⚠ SECOND LESSON, ON SCOPE. The default was NOT flipped, and that was the right call rather than
timidity: the boxed layout is the documented API. The guide spells `Ok(42)` as "16-byte heap
alloc — tag at +0, payload at +8", `?` desugars to `load64(rax + 8)`, and ~300 ecosystem sites
read those offsets by hand. A register pair read as a pointer is a plausible-looking address,
so a default flip breaks them SILENTLY. Opt-in via a keyword that was ALREADY RESERVED (`stack`,
token 111, from `stack var buf[N]`) costs no lexer change and cannot collide with an
identifier — adding a `value` keyword would have broken any program using `value` as a name.

[[entries]]
name = "a_property_held_by_an_unrelated_invariant_breaks_silently_when_that_invariant_moves"
description = "v6.5.56 P0: cyrius's identifier dedup was a PREFIX compare that was exact only because bucketing put one length per chain. Changing the bucketing removed the invariant and nobody added the check it had been standing in for."
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius's `LEXID` deduplicates identifiers through a hash chain. The compare walked `klen` bytes of
a stored pool entry and never checked that the stored entry ENDED there — a PREFIX compare. It was
nonetheless correct for years, because `bucket = klen` put exactly one length on each chain, so
two entries in a chain could not differ in length. **The exactness was a side effect of the
bucketing, not a property of the compare.**

v6.5.50 replaced `bucket = klen` with a content hash — a real fix for an O(N²) chain walk — and
thereby removed the invariant, without adding the terminator check the invariant had been standing
in for. Result, live for six releases: a shorter identifier that is a prefix of a longer one in the
same bucket took the longer one's pool offset and **the two names became one symbol**.
`var ah = 7; var ahxaa = 99;` then read `ah` as **99**. Exit 0, no diagnostic. 127 repos under
`~/Repos` carried at least one colliding pair.

⭐ THE COMMENT ASSERTED THE PROPERTY AND THAT IS PART OF THE FAILURE. Three lines above the compare
sat "dedup remains an exact byte-compare … verified: self-host fixpoint + differential corpus". It
was false. A reader checking whether the hash change was safe found a sentence saying it was.

⛔ THE SELF-HOST FIXPOINT IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THIS ENTIRE CLASS, and this was PROVEN, not
argued: a compiler with the fix deliberately removed **still reproduces itself byte-identically**
while miscompiling consumer code. The reason is that the defect is a function of the INPUT's
identifier set — cycc's own source has 0 colliding prefix pairs out of 54,089 — and the aliasing is
deterministic, so it reproduces identically across generations. **Byte-identity after a hashing or
interning change proves nothing about consumer output.** Any change that alters which inputs collide
needs a gate on a known-colliding input, not a fixpoint.

⚠ THE RECOGNITION RULE, worth more than the fix: when you remove or replace an invariant, ask what
was RELYING on it — not just what it was for. `bucket = klen` was introduced for lookup speed; it
was silently also providing compare exactness. Grep for code that reads the same state and check
each one against the NEW invariant. A helper whose correctness argument mentions a property you are
about to change is the code to re-derive first.

**Instance 2 — v6.6.6 bite 16, `_cur_fn_pslots`, and the same comment failure.** The variable
answers "is this frame slot a parameter", and `parse.cyr`'s comment said it was "saved/restored
around the async constructor exactly like `_cur_fn_ix`". It was not: `_async_emit_constructor`
saves ELEVEN pieces of enclosing state and this was not one of them. It was correct only because
`_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`'s teardown zeroes it FOUR LINES before it emits the constructor — a LINE-ORDER
dependency, documented as a save/restore. Move either line and the constructor inherits the body's
parameter span, at which point every slot in it reads as an address-passed struct parameter, with
no test that would notice. Same shape as the prefix compare: a property held by something else,
with a comment asserting the property directly. Fixed by making the comment true (`sv_psl`), not by
rewriting the comment — when the cheap option is "describe the accident accurately", prefer
"remove the accident".

[[entries]]
name = "regalloc_param_exclusion_is_by_count_not_slot"
description = "The picker excluded params by param COUNT while its frame-slot map is by SLOT — a scalar after a by-value vector param got promoted to a register and read as 0"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.62. The register-allocator picker (an inline block inside `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`,
`src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr`) skipped parameters when choosing promotion candidates with:

    var iv_li = pc;        # pc = PARAMETER COUNT

but the frame-slot indices it walks are SLOTS. A by-value vector parameter occupies
`nslots` slots — `nslots-1` anonymous fillers (name sentinel -1) and then its NAMED slot,
which carries the SLTYPE. So for `fn f(v: f64v4, s)`:

    slot 0,1,2 = fillers for v      slot 3 = v (named)      slot 4 = s
    pc = 2  ->  the loop started at slot 2  ->  s was a "local"

`s` became a promotion candidate and was held in a callee-saved register, while the
prologue's homing store still wrote the incoming argument to its frame slot. The callee
read the register. `s` came back as 0 — silently, exit 0, from ordinary user code.

THE FIX is to capture the real boundary where the parameter list closes, because GFLC
there counts exactly the param slots:

    var ra_pslots = GFLC(S);     # right after the `)` of the param list
    ...
    var iv_li = ra_pslots;       # instead of `pc`

For a function with no wide params this equals `pc`, so nothing moves: 406 corpus inputs
byte-identical, 0 codegen diffs.

HOW TO RECOGNISE IT. Four things must coincide, and dropping any one makes it vanish —
which is why it survived so long and why a narrow probe can "disprove" it:
  1. a by-value VECTOR param (both widths reach it; f64v4 reliably, f64v2 whenever the
     picker actually spends a register on the slot),
  2. another param AFTER it (scalar-first is fine),
  3. a body the inline-replay path REFUSES, so a real call happens. A FLAT body inlines
     and dodges it entirely,
  4. the picker enabled.

⭐ THE ONE-COMMAND OWNERSHIP TEST. `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0` recompiles with the
picker disabled — ⚠ it is a COMPILE-time knob: `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 cycc < x.cyr > x`,
never `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 ./x` (the 6.6.2 gate did the latter and its "off" axes
silently re-tested the ON binary until 6.6.5). If the wrong answer becomes right, the picker owns the bug; if it stays
wrong, the picker is exonerated and you are looking in the wrong subsystem. Run this
BEFORE reading any allocator code. In the same session it exonerated the picker for a
different SIMD defect (v6.5.61's value-form binding bug, which was in `PARSE_VAR`) and
then convicted it for this one — two bugs with overlapping symptoms in the same wrappers.

⚠ AND THE TRAP THAT ALMOST BURIED IT: the same release flattened `lib/simd.cyr`'s
wrappers for performance, which makes `f64v4_scale` INLINE — and an inlined callee never
takes the broken path. The perf change would have made the symptom disappear while the
compiler stayed broken for every consumer writing their own non-flat wrapper. When a
library change makes a wrong answer go away, find out whether it fixed the bug or only
stopped reaching it: rebuild the PRE-change library against the FIXED compiler and check
it passes on its own.

[[entries]]
name = "simd_value_form_cost_is_store_to_load_forwards"
description = "The cost of a chained SIMD value-form expression is 16-byte store-to-load forwards, NOT instruction count — removing 22 of 28 instructions per link measured 1.00x"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.64. A value-form vector chain like

    var t1: f32v4 = f32v4_add(a, b);
    var t2: f32v4 = f32v4_add(t1, b);

expands, per link, to 28 real instructions and ~15 memory ops for exactly ONE `addps`. The
obvious conclusion — "delete the scaffolding" — is WRONG, and it was measured wrong before
the code was written.

A hand-written asm replica that removes exactly that scaffolding (3 lea + 3 pointer spills +
3 reloads + a bounded loop that runs one iteration) and changes nothing else runs **33.99 ms
against the current 33.96 ms**. Twenty-two instructions per link removed, zero time saved.

WHY. The critical path is made of 16-byte STORE-TO-LOAD FORWARDS, and the pointer machinery
is not on it. A calibrated cost model over five variants predicts within 3.6%:

    time(2M iters) = 4.111ms x (critical-path store->load forwards) + 1.370ms x (packed ops)

Per link there are two forwards: the inline replay's copy of a param into the callee slot
(reloaded by the kernel), and the kernel's result store (reloaded by `return r`). What
actually bought 1.48x was making the store and its reload ADJACENT so the v6.5.53 SLASE
peephole could collapse them — the peephole matches at exactly +8, and before this change the
pair sat ~0x31 bytes apart, which is why SLASE's own comment recorded it deleting nothing.

MEASURE THE REGIME, NOT JUST THE SHAPE. Two of my own baselines were mis-framed the same way:
a benchmark chain that is DEAD at the end of each iteration lets consecutive iterations overlap
in the out-of-order window, so the dependency stall reads as ~20%. Carry the chain across
iterations (feed the accumulator back) and the same code shows ~2.3x. If you are measuring
dependency latency, the value must be live into the next iteration; otherwise you are measuring
throughput and will under-read the prize by a factor of two.

Staged prize for a vector register class, all measured:
  direct form alone .................. 1.00x
  + elided return reload ............. 1.59x   (v6.5.64)
  + elide replay param copy .......... ~2.07x latency / 5.20x throughput
  + full register residency .......... 8.26x latency / 9.48x throughput
And spilling the result once per iteration is FREE (4.114 vs 4.111 ms) — a register class only
has to kill the per-link RELOAD, not all stores.


[[entries]]
name = "aggregate_copy_by_type_not_by_occupancy_clobbers_neighbours"
description = "v6.5.57 P0, live SEVENTEEN releases: `X = Y;` between two struct-POINTER locals copied STRUCTSZ/8 slots and wrote over neighbouring locals. The discriminator is the FILLER SENTINEL (name -1), not the type — and the fixpoint was structurally blind because cycc's own source never uses the shape"
category = "cyrius"
---
`X = Y;` where both are locals of a struct type copied **the number of slots the
TYPE implies** rather than the number the VARIABLES occupy. For two inline
aggregates that is right. For two **pointers** to a struct it is catastrophic: the
locals are one slot each, so the copy walked `STRUCTSZ/8` slots off the end and
overwrote whatever sat beneath them — in yukti, a loop-bound PARAMETER, which then
walked its buffer off into the process stack.

**The discriminator is not the type.** An inline aggregate OWNS the slots beneath
its named one, and the frame marks them with a **filler sentinel** — name `-1`. A
pointer-to-struct local does not. So the guard is: before copying `nslots`, walk
both source and destination and refuse unless every slot beneath the named one is
a filler:

    while (ac_k < nslots) {
        if (L64(names + (dst_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        if (L64(names + (src_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        ac_k = ac_k + 1;
    }

⛔ **WHY IT SURVIVED SEVENTEEN RELEASES, and this is the part to carry.** The
byte-identical self-host fixpoint could not see it: **cycc's own source never uses
the shape.** Guarded and unguarded compilers emit `src/main.cyr` byte-identically
(1,247,608 B) — so the fixpoint agreeing proves the compiler does not *exercise*
the path, never that the path is correct. Treat "the fixpoint is green" as coverage
ONLY for constructs cycc itself contains.

**How it was actually found**, after instrumentation kept making it vanish: bisect
the TRACKED compiler binaries. Every tag carries `build/cycc`, so

    git show 6.5.56:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_56    # PASS
    git show 6.5.57:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_57    # FAIL

is an instant, download-free bisect over the real artifact. One fix then cleared
failures in TWO downstream repos that had been written off as pre-existing.


[[entries]]
name = "value_form_return_payload_alone_reads_as_success"
description = "v6.6.0 migration fail-open: `return res;` on an Err path returns the PAYLOAD by itself, so Err(77) reads downstream as tag=77 / is_err=0 — an error that reads as SUCCESS. 19 real sites in yukti alone"
category = "cyrius"
---
When `Result`/`Option`/`Either` became the value form (v6.6.0), a payload variant
returns `(tag, payload)` in a register pair. The natural-looking propagation

    var t, v = inner();
    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return v; }     # ⛔ WRONG

returns the **payload alone**. The caller then binds `t` from rax — which now holds
the payload — so `Err(77)` reads as **tag=77, is_err=0**: the error is silently
reclassified as a success carrying 77. Correct:

    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return (t, v); }

**This is the migration rule's own fail-open**, and it is worse than the bug the
flip fixed: an allocation leak is a resource problem, an error that reads as success
is a correctness one. Measured: **19** real sites in yukti (→2.3.10) and 3 in vani.

A mixed-return function — some paths returning a pair, some a single value — now
raises a compiler **warning**. It is deliberately not fatal (the dual-mode shape is
legitimate and tested both ways in vani), so the warning must actually be read.
⚠ Do NOT "fix" a warning by wrapping the single-value path in a constructor without
checking the tests: that was tried on vani and broke 3 of them, because those
returns were raw-by-design.


[[entries]]
name = "compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not"
description = "v6.6.1: CYRIUS_DCE=1 shrinks the code buffer, but PE geometry is computed at the TOP of FIXUP from the pre-elimination length — so the import payload was written at the new cursor while the section header named the old offset. The loader mapped the IAT from zero padding and the first call *IAT(%rip) faulted 0xC0000005 before main"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.72 made `CYRIUS_DCE=1` physically REMOVE dead bodies (`wp_compact`) and shrink
`GCP(S)`. But `_pe_layout(S)` runs at the **top** of FIXUP off the **pre-elimination**
length, so every PE geometry field — section sizes, RVAs, `PointerToRawData`, and the
IAT RVA the ftype=4 fixups patch against — described a layout the emitted code no
longer had. `EMITPE_EXEC` then writes `.idata` at the **post-compaction cursor**
(`o = o + cp`) while the header still names the pre-compaction offset.

Measured on the filed repro: the import payload moved from file offset `0x220F6` to
`0x2AF6` — **128,512 B earlier**, against the 128,555 B DCE reported — so the loader
mapped the Import Address Table from what had become zero padding, every import
resolved to 0, and the first `call *IAT(%rip)` faulted `0xC0000005` **before main**.

⚠ **Two independent breakages, and fixing one is not enough.** The displacement was
ALSO wrong on its own terms: patched against the old geometry, then the instruction
moved, so it resolved to RVA `0x39DD` for an IAT the header puts at `0x23000`. A
compaction pass that repairs geometry but not rip-relative displacements — or the
reverse — produces a binary that looks fine and faults later.

**Two tells worth recognising.** (1) The flag reported "N bytes eliminated" while the
FILE SIZE did not change — output written earlier inside a fixed-size buffer looks
identical from the outside. (2) The crash read a function pointer whose bytes were
ASCII (`0x7465470000000057` = `W`…`Get`): an IAT slot holding hint/name-table content
means the loader never resolved it.

**Current state (6.7.11)**: PE and x86 Mach-O DECLINE compaction, exactly as `_pie_mode`
already did and for the same stated reason — a rip-relative reference shape the pass
does not repair. They keep NOP-fill (correct, size unchanged), as do `--pie` (a `--pie`
kernel included), `shared;` and aarch64. x86_64 Linux ELF eliminates for real (123,048 →
16,552 B, −86.5%) — a W^X executable since v6.5.72, and a `kernel;` image and a
`CYRIUS_WX=0` executable since 6.7.11 (the data moved with the code; below).

⛔ **The filing said "PE / `--win` target only" and that was wrong.**
`main_x86_macho.cyr` includes the same `x86/fixup.cyr`, so Intel-Mac Mach-O took the
identical path and SIGSEGV'd on real hardware — absent from the report only because
the reporter does not build that platform. **A filing's target list is a report about
what the reporter builds, not about the bug.** Reproduce on every host that shares
the code path before scoping the fix.

**6.7.11 — WHOLE-PROGRAM COMPACTION MUST KNOW WHETHER THE DATA FOLLOWS THE CODE (INF-5).** FIXUP patches every
data address against the PRE-compaction code end. Under W^X the data segment is frozen at the boundary FIXUP
patched against, so compaction may ignore data; a kernel image and `CYRIUS_WX=0` put the data right after the code,
so it moves by the code's shrink and every patched data imm64 must move with it. The move must be a MULTIPLE OF 16,
or every array FIXUP padded for m128 against the old base becomes 8 mod 16 — the filed repro's plain move was 744
(8 mod 16); the fix pads the code end by 8 zero bytes when needed. History: 6.5.72 – 6.6.19 moved the data and not
the addresses (agnos: NUL strings, death before the banner); 6.6.20 declined compaction there (correct, no shrink);
6.7.11 moves both. A doc sentence about which layouts compact must ENUMERATE the declines that remain (`--pie`
kernel, aarch64) — the review caught the guide and platform-status claiming "every x86 kernel".

**SURVIVES COMPACTION AND AIMS INTO REMOVED CODE MEANS A KEPT DEAD BODY.** In the post-patch pass both can be true
only for a dead body that was KEPT (the length decoder could not size an instruction — an `in` / `out` in `asm`): a
live caller keeps its callee. Shifting such a rel32 like any other lands it on whatever moved into the hole — an
unrelated live fn, or the body's own entry. Neutralise it (`ud2; ud2; nop`; an `&fn` becomes 0) and refuse any
other opcode by name. `_wp_inside` (strictly inside a removed run) is the wrong test for a CALL target — a call aims
at a run's FIRST byte; `_wp_removed_at` includes it.

**A REPAIR PASS MUST NOT READ A SENTINEL BACK AS A DISPLACEMENT.** Under `--allow-undef` FIXUP writes `0f 0b 0f 0b
90` (`ud2; ud2; nop`) over a call with no callee; compaction's stage 1b read those bytes back as a rel32, "adjusted"
them, and wrote `0f 5f 0f …` (`maxps`): SIGSEGV where SIGILL was right. A pass that re-reads bytes it did not write
must first ask whether the site holds a sentinel (`_fnt_offsets[idx] < 0`).
Gates: dce_kernel_compaction_boots.sh (a multiboot1 boot under QEMU), the kept-dead gate. Known at 6.7.12 and
closed in 6.7.14 (below): W^X compaction left stale old bytes in `.text`'s alignment pad (never executed);
`CYRIUS_SYMS` was written before compaction.

**6.7.14 — THREE MORE POSITIONS COMPUTED BEFORE A MOVE (IN-3, IN-6r, IN-8).** (a) A RANGE RECORDED BEFORE THE IR=3
SEAM LIES AFTER IT (IN-6r, the lane's own regression). IN-6 recorded a redefined fn's losing-body ranges at PARSE
time so the DCE seed would skip them (ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714 #2); under
`CYRIUS_IR=3` the seam's `wp_compact` moved the code before FIXUP read them. A range left pre-move does not just go
imprecise — it lies over whatever moved INTO it (the winner's redirect jump, a later top-level call), so live fns were
listed dead and removed (SIGILL). Any table of code positions recorded before the seam is shifted by `wp_compact`
(`_wp_fnr_shift`, as stage 3 shifts `_fnt_offsets`); `dead_code_floor.txt` carries `_wp_fnr_shift` on the forks that
never compact (IN-6r2). The lane's staged note first called it "Low / sound / pre-existing" — the review found it was
none of the three. (b) `CYRIUS_SYMS` WAS DUMPED AT THE TOP OF FIXUP (IN-8), before the sweep and `wp_compact`; moving
the call was not enough, because stage 3 shifts a REMOVED fn's offset onto the next survivor — the dump needs the
sweep's removed set (a skip bitmap). (c) FIXUP'S REALIGN DOES NOT WRITE (IN-3): `SCP(align8(cp))` only moves the
cursor, so the pad past the moved code kept pre-compaction bytes, on both compaction seams; `.text` after a compaction
now ends in zeros.


[[entries]]
name = "guard_must_precede_a_range_reduction_not_sit_inside_it"
description = "v6.6.1: f64_exp/f64_exp2 returned NaN for ±inf in BOTH implementations. The reduction computes `inf - inf`, AND the exponent it assembles comes from f64_to(inf) saturating to i64::MIN — two ways to produce garbage, both upstream of the actual exponential"
category = "cyrius"
---
`f64_exp(+inf)` and `f64_exp(-inf)` returned **NaN**. The range reduction subtracts a
multiple of the argument *from the argument*, so for an infinite input it computes
`inf - inf` = NaN. A guard placed *inside* the reduction cannot fix this, because
there is a **second** failure on the same path: the exponent is assembled from
`f64_to(inf)`, which **saturates to `i64::MIN`**, so `(n + 1023) << 52` is not an
exponent either. The check has to come **first**, before any arithmetic touches the
argument.

    if ((x & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) == 0x7FF0000000000000) {
        if (((x >> 63) & 1) == 1) { return 0; }    # exp(-inf) = +0
        return 0x7FF0000000000000;                  # exp(+inf) = +inf
    }

⚠ **There are TWO implementations and a consumer hits whichever its target selects**:
the `_f64_exp_polyfill` / `_f64_exp2_polyfill` pair in `lib/math.cyr`, and the emitted
x87 `f2xm1`/`fscale`/`frndint` sequence in `src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`. Fixing only the
polyfill leaves every x86 build wrong. The backend guard is emitted ahead of the x87
sequence and its two exits are patched after the x87 pop.

**NaN deliberately gets NO guard.** It already propagates correctly through both
paths, and adding one would only create a branch that has to stay correct forever.
Not every special value needs handling — check which ones actually break.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_replay_is_a_prerequisite_for_simd_residency_and_it_binds_params_by_slot"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four v6.5.52-.53 entries. A SIMD value chain cannot keep a vector in a register across a call because SysV has NO callee-saved XMM — so inlining is a PREREQUISITE, not an optional optimisation. Admitting SIMD params to the inline-replay path then exposed four defects at once, rooted in the replay binding params to ADJACENT 8-BYTE slots that a 16-byte value overruns"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces four** (`no_callee_saved_XMM_on_SysV…`,
`inlining_is_a_PREREQUISITE…`, `inline_replay_binds_params_to_8_BYTE_slots…`,
`an_inline_replay_that_binds_params_BY_NAME…`). One arc, one lookup.

**The ABI fact that sets the order.** SysV has **no callee-saved XMM registers**.
So a vector CANNOT survive a call, and a value-form SIMD chain that emits a `call`
per link must spill and reload around every one of them. The roadmap and state.md
both pinned the blocker as "the allocator's picker only recognises REX.W mov to/from
`[rbp+disp32]`, so no xmm/ymm local is a candidate" — a TRUE statement about the
code and the WRONG lever. Removing the calls (inlining) has to come first;
teaching the picker about xmm is downstream of it. **Inlining is a prerequisite,
not a nicety.**

**What admitting SIMD params to inline-replay then exposed — four defects, and the
instructive part is that each one ALONE still looked nearly working**, so every
single fix "almost" succeeded:

- The replay binds a callee's params to **temporary local slots** —
  `var p0_slot = saved_flc; var p1_slot = saved_flc + 1;` — i.e. ADJACENT **8-byte**
  slots. A 16-byte SIMD param written into one **overruns into the next**, so two
  vector params collide.
- `SFINL` packed the two param NAME offsets from local slots 0 and 1, which is only
  correct when the callee's first two locals ARE its params.
- The replay's param COPY was read back two instructions later — a 16-byte
  store-to-load FORWARD that the hardware cannot elide; the emitter must read the
  CALLER's local instead (v6.5.65, 1.27×).
- Dead inline-param copies left an `xmm0` clobber that stopped SLASE collapsing the
  inter-link pair; harvesting them is what finally gave register residency across
  chain links (v6.5.66, 2.50–2.60× cumulative).

⚠ **Measured, not assumed**: deleting the value-form scaffolding alone — 28
instructions for ONE `addps` — moved 33.99 → 33.96 ms, i.e. **ZERO**. The cost was
never the instruction count; it was the store-to-load forwards and the calls.


[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_only_proves_something_if_you_prove_the_mutation_reached_it"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from three v6.5.50-.54 entries. A mutation applied with a sed pattern that never matched left the tree UNCHANGED and the gate printed PASS; some gates need a source revert rather than a compiler swap; and a comfortable hypothesis about a flaky gate was wrong. Mutation-prove the gate AND check the mutation was actually applied"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces three** (`a_mutation_that_silently_fails_to_apply…`,
`some_gates_need_TWO_different_mutations…`, `test_the_comfortable_hypothesis…`).

- ⛔ **A mutation that does not apply reports PASS and proves nothing.** A gate was
  mutation-proven with `sed -i 's/                        if (IR_ENABLED(S) != 0) {/…/'`
  — **twenty-four** spaces of indentation against the file's **twenty**. The pattern
  never matched, the tree was never modified, the gate ran against the ORIGINAL code
  and printed PASS. Always assert the file CHANGED (`cmp`, or a grep count that moves)
  before believing a mutation result.
- **Some gates need a source revert, not a compiler swap.** Swapping `build/cycc` for
  a pre-fix compiler is not always the mutation: a gate axis that BUILDS from the
  working-tree source (`cycc < src/main_aarch64.cyr`) and runs the result is testing
  the SOURCE, so only a source revert perturbs it. Pick the mutation that reaches the
  axis you are proving.
- **Test the comfortable hypothesis before fixing it.** A gate went red once under
  load and never reproduced; the filing's leading explanation (a sampler counting a
  SIGKILLed-but-unreaped process) was checked and REFUTED. Fixing the comfortable
  story would have left the real cause live and closed the investigation.

⭐ Companion traps already known and still true: a gate fixture whose decoy sits
AFTER the real key is never reached (the scan stops at the first match), and a gate
whose fixture shares the implementation's assumption agrees with it forever — the
v6.5.49 manifest-key case, where the feature shipped inert to 96 % of consumers and
its gate stayed green.


[[entries]]
name = "install_sh_copies_are_where_the_toolchain_breaks_use_temp_plus_rename"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. install.sh has broken the toolchain twice by HOW it copies: `cp -L` over a symlink pair aborts with 'same file' under set -e before the symlink-update block (v5.10.37), and `cp` writes THROUGH the directory entry so installing over the running binary is ETXTBSY (v6.6.1). Both leave a half-written tree; rename() replaces the entry instead"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces two** (`install_sh_cp_L_same_file_silent_fail`,
`installing_over_a_running_binary_needs_rename_not_cp`). Same file, same class:
**how install.sh copies is the thing that breaks the toolchain.**

**Failure 1 — `cp -L` on a symlink pair (v5.10.37).** The `--refresh-only` lib-copy
loop uses `cp -L lib/*.cyr`. When `lib/<dep>.cyr` is a `cyrius deps`-resolved symlink
AND the snapshot dir holds a parallel symlink to the SAME real file, `cp -L` exits
with "same file". `set -e` makes that fatal, so install.sh dies BEFORE the
symlink-update block — `~/.cyrius/bin` and `~/.cyrius/current` stay pointing at the
PREVIOUS version. The user-visible symptom is a stale `cycc --version` in the shell
prompt, which reads as prompt/config corruption rather than a failed install.
⚠ Still live: `grep -c 'cp -L' scripts/install.sh` → 2.

**Failure 2 — `cp` over a running binary (v6.6.1).** `cp` writes THROUGH the existing
directory entry, so overwriting a currently-executing binary is `ETXTBSY`. `cyriusly`
re-execs the pinned toolchain, so it is **always running during its own install** —
structural, not a race. The install then aborted part-way, which is why deleting
`cyriusly` and re-running the installer did not recover it: the tree was already
half-written.

**The fix for both shapes**: write a temp file beside the target and `rename` it over.
`rename` replaces the DIRECTORY ENTRY; a running process keeps its open inode and
finishes, and the next exec picks up the new file. A per-file failure warns and
continues rather than aborting mid-tree (`_install_file`, `scripts/install.sh:90`).

⚠ **v6.5.3 had already fixed the ETXTBSY shape in ONE of THREE copy paths.** The
tarball path — the one `cyriusly install` actually uses — survived and was reported
from a clean machine three minors later. When a fix is "make this write atomic",
sweep every writer of that kind in the same pass.
⚠ The installer is frozen into each release's immutable tag, so a broken one cannot
be hot-fixed for an already-published version.

[[entries]]
name = "a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from seven entries; an eighth instance at 6.6.12 (a hand-kept write length beside its literal), a ninth at 6.6.16 (a per-slot fact kept in a 64-bit mask), a tenth at 6.7.12 (cx's program size bounded in five places), three more at 6.7.14 (the DCE run cap restated in five places; the stub scan's silent 64-entry tables; cybs's preprocessor buffers), a fourteenth at 6.7.15 (include-once's 127-byte entries against a whole-spelling compare). Every cap in cycc is declared once and then RE-STATED as a guard, a comment, a scratch size, a clear-loop bound and a doc line. Raising the declaration without sweeping the restatements is the single most repeated defect class in this compiler — it has produced a silent miscompile, an unsound DCE, and a SIGSEGV, across v3.3.17 through v6.5.50"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries** (`stale_fixed_cap_drift`, `any_fn_indexed_side_table_must_reach_32768…`,
`a_declared_region_size_becomes_the_bound_someone_guards_at`, `a_cap_raise_must_sweep_every_consumer_of_the_cap`,
`scratch_sized_for_an_old_cap_fails_SILENTLY…`, `stdlib_alloc_grow_step_must_cover_request`,
`identifier_pool_raises_in_place_only_below_the_0x100000_ceiling`). They were the same
lesson seven times, which is itself the evidence that it keeps happening.

**THE CLASS.** A cap lives in more places than the one you edit: the allocation, the guard
that compares against it, a `while (i < N)` clear-loop, a scratch region sized from it, the
heap-map comment, and the docs. Raise the declaration alone and the rest keep enforcing the
old number — and because the extra capacity exists, nothing faults at the boundary. The
failure is always *silent and late*.

**The instances, because the variety is the point:**

- **A clear-loop left behind.** `live[]` was widened 4096 → 16384 bytes when the fn ceiling
  went 32768 → 131072, but the clear loop still zeroed 4096. Three quarters of the DCE
  reachability bitmap started as stack garbage: sometimes "live" (a missed elimination,
  harmless), sometimes "dead" (the fn is NOP-filled and the program faults). ⚠ **This was
  the SECOND time** — v6.4.75 had fixed the same loop for the previous ceiling.
- **Side tables that did not follow the table.** Six fn-indexed side tables stayed at 8192
  while the fn table grew, so index 8192 aliased a neighbour.
- **A declared size became someone's guard.** Three capture loops guarded at 4095 because the
  heap map said `[4096]` — but two live things sat inside that span. The MAP was the source
  of the bound, and the map was fiction.
- **Scratch sized for an old cap.** `EMITELF_OBJ` carved sub-regions from a size that had
  moved; it failed silently long before it crashed.
- **A grow step that does not cover the request.** A fixed `0x100000` delta cannot satisfy an
  allocation larger than the step — grow logic must cover the REQUEST, not a constant.
- **A ceiling that is physical, not arbitrary.** The identifier pool at `S+0x60000` can only
  be raised in place while its END stays under `0x100000`; above that the compiler hangs
  rather than erroring.
- **A byte count kept by hand beside the string it measures (6.6.12).** The PE "routed
  numbers" note (`_pe_warn_unrouted`, src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr) is one literal
  written with `syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2, "  note: CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1 routes ... .\n", 1584);` —
  the length is a second, hand-kept statement of the literal. Adding the 0xF04B GetLastError
  reroute grew the literal and the first build printed the note WITHOUT the new entry: the
  stale 1550 simply cut the write short. No warning, no error, a plausible-looking message.
  It is the same class at its smallest — one number restated once — and the fix was to
  recount (1550 -> 1584, measured by counting the literal's bytes). Every
  `syscall(SYS_WRITE, fd, "<literal>", N)` in the compiler carries this hazard; when you edit
  such a literal, recount N in the same edit, and check the OUTPUT (the whole note printed,
  as tests/gates/platform/pe_last_error_reroute.sh does), not just that it compiled.
  6.7.12 grew the note twice more (1587 -> 1639 -> 1686 B, the 0xF04C / 0xF04D LockFileEx pair, then
  0xF04E GetFinalPathNameByHandleW). The pattern that keeps it honest: the NEWEST reroute's gate pins
  that the note ENDS with its entry, and the previous gate's END check becomes "present and followed"
  (`pe_last_error_reroute.sh` -> `pe_lockfileex_reroute.sh` -> `embed_link_race_pe.sh`); a mutant that
  leaves the count at the old value turns the new gate's note axis RED.
- **A mask's WIDTH is a cap (6.6.16).** `_cur_fn_ptrp` (6.6.10) recorded `p: *T` parameters as
  `1 << li`, indexed by frame SLOT, and answered 0 for li >= 64 — and slots outrun parameters (a
  value-form vector owns 2-4; on Win64 sixteen `f64v4` params reach slot 64). Harmless while it
  only steered generic inference; once 6.6.16 copied every by-value struct parameter in the
  prologue unless `_is_ptr_param` said otherwise, a `*T` at slot 64 would have been COPIED and its
  writes lost. The review refused the planned "refuse a struct param at slot >= 64" — a table
  limit written down as a language rule — so the fact moved into the slot's own depth word
  (`SLPTRP` / `GLPTRP`, bit 56, util.cyr): no width, cleared on slot reuse because every
  registration writes SLDEP first, carried by the closure snapshot with the rest of the word. Two
  traps: the `*` is seen before the slot is registered, so the param loop keeps a PENDING mark
  (`_cur_fn_ptrp` = slot + 1) that `_param_record` moves onto the slot; and `SLAGG` rewrites bits
  32-63, so the bit is written AFTER MARK_SMALL_STRUCT. Still 64-bit and still open: the
  per-ORDINAL masks (`_fnt_structmask` / strmask / the 2-bit simd_mask) — 6.6.16 backlog.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** When you change a cap, `grep` the NUMBER — the old one and the new one —
across `src/`, the heap map comment block, and the gates, and fix every hit in the same
commit. A cap raise is never a one-line change. And prefer deriving a bound from the
declaration (`sizeof`-style) over restating the literal, so there is nothing to drift.

**6.7.11 — a write length is what the lexer STORES (TL-3, the tool side; this entry's 6.6.12 instance was a
hand-kept write length).** A lint comparing a write's length argument to its literal must count bytes as
`src/frontend/lex.cyr`'s string arm stores them — a raw byte one byte (never decoded; an em dash 3), a
two-character escape one byte, `\xHH` one byte, `\u` its UTF-8 length — and prove it against the compiled binary's
output (`cyrlint_overrun_and_write_length.sh` axis E). Counting `\x1b` as three bytes ("collapse only two-character
escapes") warns on every correct `"\x1b[0m", 4`; decoding the literal first (a unicode-escape round trip)
miscounts every non-ASCII byte (23 false hits at v6.5.33). The rule found a live one at once: the shadow_pam SKIP
line had lost its `s\n`.

**6.7.12 — A FIXED cx REGION AND ITS CONSUMERS MOVE TOGETHER (LIB-7, D12; the tenth).** cx's program size is
bounded in FIVE places: the compiler's bytecode buffer; its break-chain shadow (an 8-byte read-modify-write at an
instruction start, so sized cap + 8); cxvm's `.cyx` read cap; cxvm's guest memory, which the image is loaded into;
and cxvm's VM stack-entry count, which 6.6.12 sized so that the guest segment, not the host arrays, is the recursion
limit. Growing only the first two (512 KiB -> 4 MiB) left a compiled program cxvm could not load ("exceeds 1MB cxvm
code buffer"); growing the guest memory (1 -> 8 MiB) without the stacks changed which trap a runaway recursion met
first (at 65536 entries the call-stack trap fired before "guest stack overflow"; now 524288). Grow all five
together and gate each: `cx_crossos_rows_run.sh` R19-R21; `cx_tailcall_and_vm_traps.sh` D3-D9 at the new boundary,
D5b for memory now ordinary, B3 / B4 at depth 524288; a mutation per cap. AND: a shared capacity STRING in
`src/common/util.cyr` changes every fork's bytes — the cx line alone moved the native aarch64 compiler by 8 bytes,
so release-gate step 1b goes RED until `cyrius pulsar` regenerates `build/cycc-native-aarch64`. A string dead for
cycc is still in cycc's image.

**6.7.14 — THREE MORE (the eleventh to the thirteenth).** (a) IN-4, IN-4r: `CYRIUS_DCE=1` declined to compact past
4,096 dead-code runs, and that cap lived in FIVE places — the decline message, the heap-map row, a gate's reasoning
(`dce_data_vaddr_frozen.sh`, which could only be structural while the cap stood), the unset hint's comment and the
x86 sweep's comment. The run and switch-table registries now grow; the review found the last restatement (the sweep's
comment). (b) GN-3: A SILENT TABLE CAP IS A PARSE ERROR SOMEWHERE ELSE. The generic stub scan's per-fn `_gcr` / `_gsn`
/ `_bnr_tab` held 64 entries and dropped the 65th without a word; the symptom surfaced lines later as "expected ';',
got '.'" or "no struct type in scope". A fixed-size table grows (`_tab_grow`, alloc'd, doubling) or refuses BY NAME at
its cap; the gate rows exercise two growths (130 entries), and each table's own mutant turns RED only its own rows.
(c) IN-12: cybs's preprocessor buffers (15 MB stdin, 2 MB per include depth, 8 depths, 16 MB expansion) are refused by
name, and the headroom row's walker must equal cybs's own expansion length — cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt… (6.7.14).

**6.7.15 — THE FOURTEENTH: A WIDTH THE STORE BELIEVES AND THE COMPARE DOES NOT (IT-5, IT-4).** cycc's include-once
table kept 127 bytes of each spelling (`PP_MARK_INCLUDED`), while `PP_ALREADY_INCLUDED` compared the whole spelling, so
a spelling of 128+ characters never matched its own entry and was included again — silently, on every target since
v4.8.4-alpha2. cybs's 256 B list had the same width with no check at all. A fixed-width key table must REFUSE past its
width or STORE WHOLE: cycc now stores a pointer to the whole spelling (the same band, no layout change), cybs refuses by
name — infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715 #3.


[[entries]]
name = "there_are_seven_forks_and_two_symmetric_backends_a_change_lands_in_one_of_them"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from seven entries. `ls src/main*.cyr` is SEVEN per-target forks, and several subsystems exist as symmetric pairs (x86/aarch64 emit, the two Mach-O backends). A change applied to the fork or backend you were testing compiles everywhere and is WRONG everywhere else — the compiler cannot tell you, because the other fork's stub returns 0 rather than failing"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries** (`new_pe_emitter_needs_aarch64_strict_stub`,
`new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx…`,
`cross_compile_arms_in_main_cyr_are_a_separate_fork…`, `a_diagnostic_that_exists_on_one_backend_only`,
`apply_a_consolidation_to_both_symmetric_places_or_neither`,
`one_ifdef_two_backends_different_pointer_contracts`,
`heap_region_shared_by_all_forks_must_relocate_to_arena_top…`).

**THE CLASS.** cyrius has **seven** per-target entry forks (`main`, `main_win`,
`main_x86_macho`, `main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}`, `main_cx`) plus symmetric emitter pairs.
There is no shared dispatch helper, so a change is applied by hand N times — and N-1 is the
normal failure. Two mechanisms make it invisible:

1. **Return-0 stubs.** A new `E*_PE` reroute needs a matching `fn E*_PE(S) { return 0; }` in
   the aarch64 and cx backends purely for symbol resolution. Those stubs make a missing
   implementation COMPILE and emit NOTHING — clean build, clean run, wrong answer, no
   diagnostic. Only `cass`'s `cycc_cx` caught one such miss.
2. **The cross-compile arm is a different code path from the native driver**, and a `.tcyr`
   run on the host can never reach it. Compiling FOR a target and running ON it are two
   different programs.

**Instances worth keeping:**
- A diagnostic (the unrouted-syscall warning) existed on arm64 Mach-O and **not** on x86
  Mach-O for ELEVEN minors — an asymmetry nothing was checking.
- One `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` covers **two** Mach-O backends whose reroutes disagree
  about whether an argument is a pointer. One ifdef, two contracts.
- A consolidation (the query-mode route table) was applied to one of two symmetric backends;
  the other kept 279 hand-written cases. **Apply it to both places or neither** — a half-done
  consolidation is worse than none, because the next reader believes it is done.
- A heap region SHARED by all forks must relocate to arena top: the forks' arenas diverge, so
  a fixed offset that is free in one is occupied in another.

**6.7.8 — two ways not to have N copies.** (a) A whole-program switch decided in the SHARED
PREPROCESS reaches all seven forks with no copy: `_bnd_setup` (lex_pp.cyr) runs after PP_NEUT_PASS
and before PP_PASS evaluates the first `#ifdef`, so its `CYRIUS_BOUNDS` predefine is seen by every
file. CYRIUS_POISON, read in each fork's driver, had seven copies — and they read `0` and an empty
value as ON where CYRIUS_BOUNDS / CYRIUS_DCE read them as off / no rung (resolved 6.7.11: decided once in
the shared PREPROCESS through `_pp_env_rung` — `_poison_setup`, parse_cov.cyr — and `poison_env_rung.sh`
S1 / S2 refuse any fork-level read). (b) The class
outside the compiler: cbt's two spawn arms (POSIX envp; Windows, which has no envp and sets the
CLI's own environment) each kept a flag list, and the Windows one rotted for three flags
(`--strict-pin` v5.11.58, `--poison` v6.5.29, `--pie` v6.6.5), each added to the POSIX list only.
One list both arms read (`_cc_flag_inject`), plus a gate that reads the arm FROM THE SOURCE and
fails on any hand-set name, is what stops a fourth (compile_flag_list.sh axes 4-6) — the env twin
of 6.6.20's argv `_cc_flags`.

**6.7.10 — a per-fork tail line is a load-bearing line.** `_cov_tail` (the execution-coverage mode) is ONE
line in all seven forks, and it does more than its name: it sizes the counter band and bakes the fn map too.
A fork without it loses even the exit hook's dump, and on x86 Mach-O its hits overran the 8-byte band
(SIGSEGV; P5B-7's hardware mutants). The two Mach-O forks run ONLY on macOS, so ecb / ach are the gate. The
spawn-arm lesson again: the Windows arm sets the compiler's environment on the CLI's OWN process, so the NEXT
program inherits it — delete what only the compiler should see (`_covx_win_unset`).

⭐ **THE HABIT.** After any emitter, predefine, or driver change: `ls src/main*.cyr` and
account for every one. The gate is byte-identical self-host **on all four hosts** plus
seed-derive — a green x86 build proves nothing about the other six.

**6.7.11 — three more fork facts.** (a) A TARGET SELECTOR IS HONOURED ONLY BY THE FORKS THAT READ IT (TL-10):
`CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` / `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL` are read by `src/main.cyr` alone; the aarch64 forks
(`main_aarch64.cyr`, `main_aarch64_native.cyr`) read `CYRIUS_KERNEL` only; the PE and both Mach-O forks read none
of the three. A host CLI whose compiler cannot emit the target must REFUSE the selector, not pass it — a dropped
selector that exits 0 writes the host format under the target's name (the 6.6.5 one-argument rule). The first
cut's docs said agnos builds from "a Linux host (x86_64 or aarch64)"; pi showed the aarch64 Linux CLI writing an
aarch64 ELF for `--agnos` (filed, High). (b) A DRIVER FORK THAT LACKS A SHARED EPILOGUE BLOCK IS SILENT (GF-19):
the cx fork missed v5.9.37's `fn main` auto-call until 6.7.11, as PE did until 6.0.54 — and a test keeping its
asserts in `main` passed vacuously there (a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6).
(c) A SELF-BUILD IS NOT A USER BUILD (TL-9): a CLI that builds a compiler or tool it KEEPS (`cyrius pulsar`, the
cx arc's `cycc_cx` / `cxvm`) must scrub the compile-mode switches as the self-build scripts do (6.7.9), or an
exported `CYRIUS_POISON=1` yields a poison-mode tracked compiler whose stages agree (the self-host check passes) and
gets installed. POSIX: hand the child a filtered envp (`_envp_self_build`). Windows has no envp to hand a child —
save, clear around the spawn, and RESTORE, because a later user compile in the same process must still see them.


[[entries]]
name = "a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64_is_not_the_number_you_wrote"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from six entries. Between ESYSXLAT, EMACHO_SYSXLAT and per-arch stdlib peers, a raw syscall(N) reaches the kernel as something else — and the SOURCE number is arch-specific too, so the same wrapper name means different numbers in different files. Renumbering alone is never enough: VALUES and FLAGS diverge as well"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces six entries** (`macho_syscall_number_dual_definition_61_vs_217`,
`macho_socketpair_dual_number_199_vs_53`, `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`,
`darwin_at_star_flags_diverge_from_linux…`, `the_macos_x86_stat_enum_was_a_mix_of_two_structs…`,
`esysxlat_is_a_linear_cmp_chain…`).

**FOUR INDEPENDENT WAYS THIS BITES**, and fixing one does not fix the others:

1. **The SOURCE number is arch-specific.** x86-macho feeds `syscalls_macos.cyr` (BSD-numbered:
   getpid 39) while aarch64-macho feeds `syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` (getpid 172). So a fix
   reasoning only about the x86 `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` table misses the aarch64-macho stdlib
   ENTIRELY. Same shape for `SYS_GETDENTS64` (217 vs 61) and `socketpair` (199 vs 53).
2. **ESYSXLAT is a linear cmp-chain, so ORDER is semantics.** An entry must sit BELOW every
   entry that compares the number it PRODUCES, or its output is re-caught and re-translated.
   The aarch64-Linux arm must stay LAST in its chain.
3. **VALUES diverge, not just numbers.** `AT_FDCWD` is **-2** on Darwin, not Linux's -100 — and
   a pure renumbering translation passes a Linux-valued FLAG straight through, where it is
   simply invalid: EINVAL, forever, silently. ESYSXLAT renumbers NUMBERS, never VALUES.
4. **A struct definition can describe two structs at once.** `syscalls_macos.cyr`'s `Stat`
   enum mixed fields from two layouts because only the field that prompted a fix was
   corrected. Fix the WHOLE struct or none of it.

⚠ **A private alias band exists for the unwinnable case** (v6.5.7): when BOTH the native
number and the x86 number are already owned, use `1000 + native` — numbers ≥1000 are
cyrius-private and no OS will mint them.

5. **An UNNAMED number is invisible to the diagnostic built for it** (added v6.6.5). The
   ELF-aarch64 raw-literal warning derives its table from `SYS_*` names declared in BOTH Linux
   peers, so the case it exists for — a number nobody ever named — produces NOTHING. Measured
   under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` at 6.6.4: raw 77 (x86 `ftruncate`) ran **tee**, raw 46
   (`sendmsg`) ran **ftruncate**, raw 319 was ENOSYS, and the build was clean and silent.
   Naming a call is therefore not cosmetic; it is what makes the diagnostic able to see it.
6. **THE GENERATED TABLE IS THE DIAGNOSTIC; ESYSXLAT IS HAND-WRITTEN AND NEVER REGENERATED**
   (added v6.6.5, and the filing that prompted it had this backwards).
   `src/common/syscall_xlat.cyr` is produced by `programs/gen_syscall_xlat.cyr` from the two
   peers and is consulted only to WORD A WARNING. The renumbering is the `cmp x8 / b.ne /
   movz x8` chain in `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`. **Naming a syscall in both peers
   renumbers nothing.** Rows are added by hand, in an order the chain makes load-bearing.
7. **A NEW NATIVE DECLARATION CAN SILENTLY DELETE A DIAGNOSTIC ROW** (added v6.6.5). The
   generator drops any x86 number that is also a valid native aarch64 number, because a
   literal there is plausibly correct. So declaring `SYS_CAPSET = 91` on the aarch64 peer
   removes the row that warns about raw x86 `fchmod`(91) — quietly, with every gate green.
   `tests/gates/platform/syscall_peer_kernel_agreement.sh` PINS that ambiguous set for
   exactly this reason.
8. **A SHIFT ROW DROPS arg0, SO AN at-CALL RUNS AGAINST THE CWD** (added 6.7.12, WN-3). arm64 Mach-O's
   `_esx_arm_shift(S, src, dst, n)` renumbers an at-call onto its BSD path call by shifting the arguments
   down — DROPPING the dirfd. `openat` 56 -> `open` 5 ran every dirfd-relative open against the cwd,
   silently (ecb: `sys_openat(dirfd, "leaf")` read the cwd's file). A straight row to Darwin `openat` 463
   had been refused at 6.6.19 because raw callers pass Linux's `AT_FDCWD` -100, not Darwin's -2 (item 3).
   Fix: `_esx_arm_atfd` keeps the dirfd and translates the one VALUE — `cmn x0, #100; b.ne +8; movn x0, #1`
   (x0 = -2) before `movz x16, #463`; encodings from llvm-mc, and the body must pass `_esx_body_bad`
   (fold-safe). Still open: the other shift at-rows — `sys_mkdirat(dirfd, "x")` made `./x` in the cwd and
   returned 0 on ecb, `sys_fchmodat` looked in the cwd (filed, 6.7.14). Any `_esx_arm_shift` row whose
   source takes a dirfd is suspect. CLOSED in 6.7.14 (PL-5): mkdirat 34 -> Darwin 475 and fchmodat 53 -> Darwin
   467 go through `_esx_arm_atfd` too, so every arm64 Mach-O at-row keeps x0. The arg-shift emitter `_esx_arm_shift`,
   whose `b.ne` displacement depended on the shift count, is gone, and `_esx_arm` emits its three words itself; an
   at-row is 6 words where a shift row was 5.
9. **A RAW-ISSUED ALIAS IS INVISIBLE TO A NAME-LEVEL PARITY CHECK** (added 6.7.12, DW-5). A Darwin private
   alias (1000 + the BSD number: 1202, 1329, 1360-1366, 1515, 1516, …) is issued as a RAW number by a macOS
   arm, never as a `SYS_*` name both peers declare, so macho_route_parity's name-level drift axis cannot see
   it: deleting either backend's alias row passed the whole gate. Axis 2b now compares the alias band
   row-for-row on both Mach-O backends (deleting one row fails 2b and nothing else). A Darwin alias is TWO
   edits — `_esx_arm` in ESYSXLAT's Mach-O arm and `_msx32` in EMACHO_SYSXLAT; the x86 one sits in a
   cybs-compiled fn near its per-fn reference cap, so seed-derive is required.
10. **GIVING A RAW NUMBER A PUBLIC NAME HANDS IT TO EVERY PROGRAM'S ENUM** (added 6.7.14, PL-6). `fd_wait_ready`
   issued a raw 7; to satisfy the region rule it now issues `SYS_POLL`. "Last definition wins" is GLOBAL, so a
   program's own `SYS_POLL = 39` after the include makes the stdlib's `fd_wait_ready` call getpid (measured:
   `fd_wait_ready(999, 0, 0)` answered 1 where -9 is right) — the same reason the comment above it keeps `POLLIN` /
   `POLLOUT` literal. Prefer a name only where the respelling is required, and expect the duplicate-symbol warning to
   be the only signal.
11. **A PROGRAM'S RAW `SYS_OPEN` DOES NOT COMPILE FOR aarch64 LINUX** (added 6.7.14, PL-9). The aarch64 peer declares
   no `SYS_OPEN` (that kernel has only openat). `programs/cyrld.cyr` used `syscall(SYS_OPEN, …)` and so never built
   for aarch64; nothing noticed until the release tarball built every `[release]` bin (D59). Use the wrapper
   (`sys_open`): its x86 output is byte-identical.
12. **A VM THAT PASSES A GUEST'S NUMBER THROUGH IS RENUMBERED BY ITS OWN HOST COMPILER** (found 6.7.14 in the stdlib
   lane; FIXED 6.7.15, PL-11 / PL-12). cxvm issued the guest's x86-Linux number with a RUN-TIME `syscall(sn, …)`, and
   each host compiler renumbers a run-time number through the chain built for that host's own stdlib numbering
   (ESYSXLAT's aarch64-Linux arm, the Mach-O chain, the PE argc router). On aarch64 Linux a guest lseek (8) ran
   getxattr, kill (62) ran lseek, `exit_group(77)` exited 0 and fork (57) ran close, so the parent took the child
   branch; on arm64 macOS kill ran lseek and an unmatched number SIGSYS-killed cxvm; on both Macs open's Linux flags
   read as other Darwin flags. THE FIX: serve each guest call through the HOST's own stdlib wrapper, as
   `_cx_host_getrandom` / `_cx_host_clock_gettime` have since v6.6.8 — `_cx_host_sys` (read / write / open / close /
   lseek / exit / … / fcntl), `_cx_host_posix` (getppid, kill, dup, dup2, flock, the uid / gid getters, fork; -38 on
   Windows), `_cx_host_linux` (socket, epoll_create1, … on Linux hosts only); every other number -38 (6.7.14's D55).
   RULES: (a) an interpreter's dispatch contains NO runtime-numbered syscall at all — cxvm's PE build's "syscall(<non-
   literal number>) has no Windows-routable syscall" warning was the tell, and it is gone. (b) FLAGS AND STRUCTS CROSS
   THE BOUNDARY FIELD BY FIELD: `open` / `F_SETFL` bits map to the host's O_* names and `F_GETFL` maps back
   (`_cx_open_flags` / `_cx_flags_back`) — passed raw, x86's O_DIRECTORY is arm64's O_DIRECT, and arm64's O_LARGEFILE
   0x20000 read back raw is x86's O_NOFOLLOW; a guest `struct stat` is refilled at the guest ABI's offsets from the
   host's own (x86 st_mode at 24, aarch64-generic at 16, Darwin's 16-bit st_mode / st_nlink); mkdir / unlink / rename /
   stat / … had answered -38 (PL-12). (c) A FIXTURE THAT CARRIES RAW x86 NUMBERS (the `.cyx` ABI) MUST SAY IT NEVER
   REACHES aarch64-Linux: `raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh` reads `#ifndef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` that way, and cx
   predefines only `CYRIUS_TARGET_CX`, so the guard costs the cx and x86_64 builds nothing (byte-identical). (d) SAY
   WHAT IS NOT CLOSED: Darwin errno values and signal numbers still reach the guest unmapped, and `F_GETFL` off x86_64
   Linux shows only the nine mapped bits (each filed, 6.7.16) — the contract is NOT "x86_64 Linux's answers on every
   host" (PL-11r corrected exactly that wording). On cass `close(-1)` answers 0: -1 is the current-process
   pseudo-handle, and the filed table's 1 was the BOOL PL-7 folds (pe_errno_stubs_region_declarations_and_the_ctl_
   signature_hash_v6714 #7). CHANGELOG [6.7.15] PL-11, PL-12, PL-11r.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** A wrapper that COMPILES on five targets is not a wrapper that RUNS. Put a
file in `tests/tcyr/crossos/` and run it on real ecb/ach/cass/pi — that is what turned up
seven wrapper defects at v6.5.7, five of which were half-fixes that stopped at the first
symptom.

⭐ **AND THE CHECKS THEMSELVES SHARED A SOURCE** (v6.6.5). Four gates guarded this machinery
and every pair of them was circular: the generated table re-derives FROM the peers, the
routed-literal allowlist derives FROM the emitter, the shadow gate compares the two peers TO
EACH OTHER, and the row-order gate checks the chain against ITSELF. A check that shares a
defect with the thing it checks reads GREEN. The fix was an OUTSIDE fact — committed kernel
UAPI tables under `tests/data/syscalls/`, the way `tests/data/ucd` is committed — against
which every declared number must MEAN the call its name claims. It found a live disagreement
on its first run.


[[entries]]
name = "a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing_and_the_ways_it_happens_are_enumerable"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four entries into the gate-proof lesson. A gate can pass BECAUSE of the bug it guards; a corpus can be unable to distinguish the thing it asserts from its absence; a test environment can make the code under test unreachable; and a path that never executes during self-host is untested no matter how green the fixpoint is"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces four entries** (`a_gate_can_pass_because_of_the_bug_it_guards…`,
`a_corpus_that_cannot_tell_real_threads_from_inline_calls`,
`an_empty_test_environment_makes_environment_code_unreachable`,
`phase0_growable_grow_path_self_host_invisible`). Companion to
`a_gate_only_proves_something_if_you_prove_the_mutation_reached_it`.

**SEVEN DISTINCT MECHANISMS (four at the v6.6.1 merge; 5-7 found at 6.6.5, 6.6.11, 6.6.15 — this
line still said FOUR at six). Each produced a green gate over a real defect:**

1. **The gate depended on the defect.** Remove the bug and the gate fails — so it was
   asserting the broken behaviour. Also in this family: byte-pattern gates made vacuous by
   ASCII collisions, where the pattern matches something unrelated.
2. **The corpus could not tell the assertion from its absence.** Six concurrency tests passed
   on macOS for four minors while thread bodies ran **serially** — every assertion was
   honestly true, because a serial fallback satisfies every property the tests checked. The
   corpus could not distinguish a real thread from an inline call.
   ⟶ 6.6.14: a "this verb refuses off the main thread" test compared each verb's result with
   its refusal value; deleting each of 31 guards in turn left 8 GREEN, because the real call
   answers the same 0 (a fresh connection's `tls_get_session`; a setter whose libssl symbol is a
   C macro and never resolves). Two of the 8 were a real regression — an unguarded worker CLAIMED
   a one-time latch with null pointers and main's SPKI read returned 0 for the life of the
   process. Assert on what the defect changes: a counting spy in EVERY pointer slot the library
   holds (resolved or not), a "made no call" row per verb, the shared state afterwards — and
   require every guard deletion RED.
   ⟶ 6.6.15: a test whose BOTH ends run the code under test agrees with any version of it.
   Replacing RFC 8446's message_hash(ClientHello1) with ClientHello1 itself left all 523
   native↔native assertions GREEN — client and server call the same `_tn_transcript_after_hrr`,
   derive the same wrong secret, and every Finished verifies. It went RED only on the OpenSSL
   gate's HelloRetryRequest rows (an independent peer, Linux only) and on a KAT computing the
   construction from the RFC with sigil's one-shot sha256 / sha384 (every host). When a mutant of
   shared code survives the native matrix, the matrix is not its test: write the construction
   down from the spec. (Same root as a 1.2 signature only your OWN signer made — TLS entry item 2.)
3. **The environment made the code unreachable.** The suite ran every test with `envp={NULL}`
   for reproducibility, so nothing could exercise `getenv` at all — the tests were green
   about a code path they never entered.
   ⟶ 6.6.14: the Windows trust-store test read the real `Disallowed` store — EMPTY on cass —
   so it passed with the Disallowed check deleted. Build the state instead of borrowing the
   host's: `CERT_STORE_PROV_MEMORY` stores filled with exactly the certificate and properties
   each row names (Windows stores ANY blob for a property id, so every malformed and fail-closed
   path is reachable, deterministically, with no admin rights and no machine state touched).
4. **Self-host does not exercise it.** The growable pressure tables never grow during cycc's
   own compile, so the grow path had zero coverage from the fixpoint. Force it with a
   low-cap build. ⚠ Generalise this: **the byte-identical fixpoint is coverage ONLY for
   constructs cycc's own source contains** — that is exactly why the v6.5.57 aggregate-copy
   P0 shipped for seventeen releases.
5. **The knob was set on the wrong process (6.6.5).** The 6.6.2 SIMD gate's "picker OFF" axes
   ran `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 ./probe` — but that variable is read by **cycc at compile
   time**, never by the program. Both axes re-ran the picker-ON binary: against 6.6.1 they
   reported 139 (the ON crash) where a real picker-off build returns 10, the silent mode the
   issue file said was "now pinned". Any compile-time knob (`CYRIUS_*` read by cycc) goes on the
   COMPILER invocation. The repair adds the self-check the original lacked: the ON and OFF
   builds must differ in bytes, or the gate fails.
6. **The gate said SKIP and exited 0 (6.6.11).** A gate that could not run its check (a missing
   tool, host, fixture, /proc, wine) printed `SKIP: …` and exited 0 — the shape
   methodology.cyml's `ci_drift_detection_silent_skip_is_false_green` had prescribed as the
   "better" one. But both runners graded the EXIT CODE: the check driver's `_gate` mapped 0 to
   PASS and check.sh's `_chk_gate` knew only PASS / TIMEOUT / FAIL, so every such gate scored
   PASS and the SKIP line was prose nobody read; `CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP`, CI's strict switch,
   reached only the driver's own rows. A runner that reduces a gate to a number cannot see a
   word. Fix: exit **77** means "could not run" and both runners score it as a SKIP (a FAIL
   under `CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP=1`); a census axis
   (`tests/gates/toolchain/check_driver_skip_is_not_pass.sh` axis 11) fails any script under
   tests/gates that prints SKIP and then exits 0, so the old shape cannot come back one gate at
   a time.
7. **The coverage claim was written from the FEATURES, not from the SITES (6.6.15).** A new TLS
   suite said it covered "every client and server refusal, each with its alert". A reviewer
   deleted each refusal of the new parsers once: 12 stayed green. An independent verifier then
   found 4 more — the reviewer's set had been CHOSEN, not swept. Deleting every refusal statement
   in the functions the work wrote, one build each (144 sites), turned 52 RED; every survivor the
   docs named got a row, and the remaining 68 are classified in the test's ledger (role guards,
   framing the driver checked first, guards the callers make unreachable). Two traps make rows
   that LOOK like coverage: (a) a row whose input a LATER check also refuses, with the same
   alert, pins neither — the "wrong-length share" row sent a 64-byte P-256 share that sigil's
   point check refuses anyway, so with the length check deleted it stayed green while a 31- or
   33-byte x25519 share (never point-validated) was ACCEPTED: build each refusal's input well
   formed in every other respect, so deleting the check makes the peer TAKE it; (b) a deleted
   check that a later one backs up with a DIFFERENT alert is caught only by asserting the alert,
   not the failure. Cheap form: `grep -c` the refuse / fail helper over the function, one
   single-line mutant per hit, one ledger line per mutant in the test. Writing the rows also
   found a real defect (TLS entry item 8): the row for a check is how you learn the check works.
8. **A gate that COMPILES a documented example proves it compiles, not that the text is true
   (6.7.8).** guide_examples_compile.sh compiles each include-less block, so a wrong stated value
   (`# 40` on a program that returns 39) or a reworded quoted diagnostic ships green. The 6.7.8
   sections RUN theirs: dyn_checked.sh TT10 runs the guide's dyn examples for the values their
   comments state; bounds_mode_checked.sh TL4 extracts the bounds-mode example, compares its stated
   stderr line byte for byte, and compares each quoted diagnostic with what cycc prints (mutation-
   proven by rewording the guide). A diagnostic the guide quotes is a second copy of the compiler's
   string — pin it like code. THE INVERSE, same release: ir_nop_harvest.sh read the best result
   (0 NOPs) as "llvm-objdump missing?" and went RED — prove a tool ran by an independent measure (the
   disassembly's instruction count), never by the measured quantity being nonzero.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** For any new gate ask: what would make this fail? If you cannot state the
mutation, the gate is decorative. Then apply it and confirm the tree actually changed.

**6.7.11 — A FAKE THAT DEFAULTS TO SUCCESS MAKES EVERY NEW PROBE VACUOUS.** `tests/fixtures/agnos_sctrace.cyr`'s
`_st_answer` answers 0 to any number it does not script, so a new syscall's capability probe (`#109(conn, buf, 0, 0)
== 0` → supported) read "supported" in EVERY existing mode: every axis that pinned the old `#48` path would have
silently switched to the new path and stayed green. Fix: script the new number in every existing mode as the OLD
kernel answers it (#109 → -1), so those axes stay the fallback path, and add modes for the new kernel (`dl0` / `dl3` /
`dl3dead`). The same for a pass-through row: #110 had to answer 1, not 0, or a wrapper that drops the kernel's
answer reads the same 0 and passes. Default unknown numbers to the OLD kernel's answer.
**6.7.11 — A RULE NO BEHAVIOUR TEST CAN SEE IS ENFORCED BY A SOURCE CENSUS THAT CHECKS ITSELF.** A preprocessor walk
left on the bare lexical machine (INF-1) is invisible to every behaviour test, because the belt and braces
(`PP_NEUT_BOLMARK`) still catches the forge (tree mutant M11: every behaviour axis green). So
`file_marker_forge_refused.sh` axis 26 ties every call to a guard by occurrence counts, both arms agreeing — and runs
itself on two one-site mutants so it cannot match nothing and read green.
**6.7.13 — A GREP PATTERN THAT STARTS WITH `-` IS AN OPTION (VC-12).** `grep -qF "- got .."` reads the pattern as
an option and exits 2 — which `if grep -q …` / `! grep -q …` read as "no match", so the row grades on an error, not on
the text. Write `grep -qF -e "- got .."`. Run a new row's grep once against text that MUST match before trusting its
"absent". And a static axis that pins a CALL SITE but not the CALLEE's answer is blind to the callee: windows_flock_
relock_and_lock_state_in_the_kernel_v6713 #3 (LIB-1r).
**6.7.14 — THREE MORE.** (a) MECHANISM 4 AGAIN, FOR A GROW (IN-1, IN-4): a grow mutant that skipped part of the copy
(the IR arena's CP or BB array, a WPNR record) passed every exit-code test AND cycc's own IR=3 self-host — a lost copy
only makes a pass skip work or a binary larger. Pin the pass statistics at a size that grows against one that does not
(phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap, 6.7.14). (b) MECHANISM 1, A ROW THAT PASSES THROUGH THE BUG BESIDE IT
(PL-2): `struct_value_codegen.tcyr` F21 seeked fd 1, a std fd that `ELSEEK_PE` passes as a raw HANDLE, so the seek
always failed and read as 0 — the row was 0 with AND without the ECONDCMP mutant it existed to catch. When a fix flips
an unrelated row, read the row's fixture before "fixing" the expectation: the row now seeks a real handle and is RED
again under the mutant. (c) A GATE'S PASS LINE IS SHELL TOO (GN-5r, VC-17): unescaped backticks inside a
double-quoted `echo` EXECUTED their text on every green run — `var h: S` (`var: command not found`) in a generics gate,
`*V` in `simd_pointer_param.sh` ("line 1524: *V: command not found") — the verdicts unaffected, so nobody read it.
Escape backticks in gate messages, and read a gate's FULL output, not only its verdict line. The shell-semantics
companion (a gate green under `sh` that stops under `bash -eo pipefail`) is methodology.cyml
a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_pipefail_v6714.

**6.7.15 — AN EMPTY BUILD OUTPUT CMP-EQUALS ITSELF (the wave-2 integration).** A shell's `>` creates the output file
BEFORE the compiler runs, so a failed compile leaves an EMPTY file, not a missing one. `chmod +x` it and run it from a
shell: execve fails with ENOEXEC, and sh / bash fall back to running the file as a SHELL SCRIPT — an empty script, exit
0, no output (measured 2026-10-19: `: > e1; chmod +x e1; ./e1 < /dev/null > e2` gives rc 0 and an empty e2, and
`cmp e2 e3` of the next stage is EQUAL). So a fixpoint chain `c1 = cycc(src); c2 = c1(src); c3 = c2(src); cmp c2 c3`
whose first stage failed reads as a FIXPOINT over three empty files, and an install step after it copies nothing over
`build/cycc`. It happened: two lanes changed one fn's contract (forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_
str_destinations_v6715 #13), the first build of the merged tree failed, and an unguarded build chain copied the empty
output over `build/cycc` — restored from git (`git checkout HEAD -- build/cycc`, as methodology.cyml
a_parallel_agent_fleet_can_exhaust_tmpfs_and_corrupt_the_repos_build_cycc (b) says) before anything read it. Every later
rebuild went through a stage-checking script: EACH stage must exit 0 AND produce a NON-EMPTY output (`[ -s ]`), c2 ==
c3 is required after that, and the install is `cp` to a temp beside `build/cycc` then `mv`, only once all of it holds.
The same family as cycc compiling EMPTY stdin to a runnable binary at exit 0 (unhandled_flag_falls_through_to_compile_
stdin_6_6_6) and a flat glob over the recursive tests tree matching nothing (a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_
still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus): an empty input is a valid input to every tool in the chain, so the CHECK must
test for it.


[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomics_need_8_byte_alignment_and_are_not_a_full_barrier"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. On aarch64 ldxr/stxr MANDATORILY SIGBUS on a non-8-aligned address, and atomic_cas is NOT the full barrier the stdlib comment claimed — true on x86 (lock cmpxchg), false on aarch64"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces two entries** (`aarch64_atomic_on_global_needs_8_aligned_var_base`,
`aarch64_atomic_cas_is_not_a_full_barrier`). One subsystem, two traps, both aarch64-only.

1. **ALIGNMENT IS ARCHITECTURAL, NOT ADVISORY.** `ldxr`/`stxr` — what `atomic_cas` and
   `atomic_fetch_add` compile to — fault with **SIGBUS** on a non-8-aligned address. On x86
   the same code merely runs slower, so an unaligned global is invisible until it reaches ARM.
   Any global an atomic touches needs an 8-aligned base.
2. **THE BARRIER CLAIM WAS FALSE ON ONE ARCH.** `lib/atomic.cyr` documented `atomic_cas` as
   "a full barrier on both arches". TRUE on x86, where `lock cmpxchg` is a full fence. FALSE
   on aarch64, where the LL/SC pair orders only itself. Code relying on the documented
   guarantee is racy on ARM and correct on x86 — the worst possible split, because the
   development host is the one where it works.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** A stdlib comment asserting a MEMORY-MODEL property is a claim about two
architectures and must be verified on both. Run it on real `pi`; qemu-user will not surface
either of these reliably.


[[entries]]
name = "the_callptr_spill_slot_is_a_real_local_and_can_alias_one"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. Compiler-emitted indirect calls spill the callee address to a frame slot; that slot must survive the Win64 shadow space AND must not alias an &-taken local — it is a real frame slot competing with the ones the user declared"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces two entries** (`callptr_indirect_call_frame_slot_spill_survives_win64_shadow`,
`callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local`). Same slot, two ways it goes wrong.

`callptr` lowers to `IR_CALL_INDIRECT` — x86 `call [rbp-disp]`, aarch64 `ldr x6; blr x6` —
which requires spilling the callee address into a **frame slot**. That slot is not
bookkeeping; it is a real local, and it participates in every rule real locals do:

1. **It must survive the Win64 shadow space.** The callee may write the 32-byte shadow area;
   a spill slot placed inside it is destroyed by a conforming callee.
2. **It must not ALIAS an `&`-taken local.** When a function passes `&x` and also makes an
   indirect call, the spill slot can land on `x`'s storage — the callee writes through the
   pointer, or the spill overwrites the value, depending on order. Neither faults.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Any compiler-allocated frame slot must be reserved through the same
allocator the user's locals use, never carved from "space that looks free". The same class
produced the v6.5.61 regalloc bug, where params were excluded by COUNT while the frame map is
by SLOT.

[[entries]]
name = "identifier_identity_is_fragile_in_this_lexer_five_ways"
description = "CLASS — 5 recurrences merged v6.6.1. Reserved words are a CLASS of 107 tokens at 6.6.20, not a list; the set cannot be grepped (two keyword paths, >8-char names matched by u64+load8 tails); two token types were DOUBLE-ASSIGNED until 6.6.2 so a type could not recover its spelling; and a struct field name that shadows a global silently reads the global. Every count ever written down for this set has gone stale"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces five entries.** They are one subject: **the lexer's notion of what a name IS,
and the several ways it goes wrong.**

**1 — Reserved words are a CLASS, not a short list.** `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` (`src/common/util.cyr`)
is the source of truth: **79** builtin/intrinsic names (76 at v6.6.1; 6.6.13 added
`f64_le` / `f64_ge` / `f64_trunc`), disjoint from the **28** tokens `IS_KEYWORD_TOK`
enumerates itself (26 statement keywords + `f64_sqrt` 136 / `callptr` 137, which TOKNAME
names) — **107** reserved tokens at 6.6.20 (the v6.6.1 figure, 102, also left those two
out). Any of them as an identifier is refused. `fn` cannot be a parameter name; `mod` cannot be a var or param.

**2 — The set CANNOT be grepped, and every written count has rotted.** Two reasons a regex
sweep under-counts: the lexer has TWO keyword paths (`LEXKW_EXT` which returns a token, and
the inline `ADDTOK` chain), and a name longer than 8 characters cannot fit one u64 immediate
so it is matched by a u64 compare on the first 8 bytes PLUS per-byte `load8` tails — never a
single decodable literal (`f64_floor`, `f64v_fmadd`, the whole `f32v8_*` family: 16 names
invisible to a hex grep).
⛔ **Measured at v6.6.1: THREE live counts for one set** — 51 (a `util.cyr` comment), 67
(README + CLAUDE.md), **76** (the actual arm count). Derive it, never quote it:
`sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'`.

**3 — ✅ RESOLVED 6.6.2: two token types WERE DOUBLE-ASSIGNED.** Token 79 was BOTH `object`
and `f64_sqrt`; token 111 was BOTH `stack` and `callptr`. Both compiled correctly — the
grammar disambiguates by POSITION — but anything mapping a type back to a name had lost the
information. Before v6.4.77 `var f64_sqrt = 1;` reported `reserved keyword 'object'`, naming
the WRONG keyword; v6.4.77's workaround printed both spellings. 6.6.2 renumbered them
(`f64_sqrt` 136, `callptr` 137), so each names itself (re-verified 6.6.20); the issue is
archived (`docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-08-lexer-token-types-79-and-111-double-assigned.md`).

**4 — A field name that shadows a global reads the GLOBAL.** `bc.exit_code` silently read a
global rather than the struct field when the field name matched one (v6.4.24, P1). Field
access and identifier resolution are not as separate as they look.

**5 — The diagnostic must NAME the collision.** Since v6.4.77 the parser names the specific
reserved word you hit; before that, 67 tokens reported a generic `got unknown`, which cost
one consumer a whole 312-assertion suite findable only by bisection.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Read the table; never extend a list in prose. When a name is rejected and
you do not believe it, compile `var <name> = 1;` and read the diagnostic — the round-trip
through the compiler is the only sound enumeration method.


[[entries]]
name = "any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream"
description = "CLASS — 4 recurrences merged v6.6.1, a 5th at 6.6.5 (a byte classifier whose stages disagreed), a 6th at 6.6.12 (a raw emit the IR was not told about, for one width only), a 7th at 6.7.8 (a raw frame base the IR was not told about, before a parsed index), the 8th to 10th at 6.7.10 - 6.7.12 (below), the 11th to 13th at 6.7.14 (a field emit and a float literal the IR was not told about; a byte scan read as a call graph), the 14th at 6.7.15 (a fold over a span holding a node another pass had marked for a later fill). The code buffer is not pure instructions: inline switch tables, hand-emitted jumps outside any registry, and fn-boundary offsets all live in or beside it. A scanner that assumes 'instructions only' desynchronises; a mover that repairs only the tables it knows about corrupts the rest — and a length decoder that is ONE BYTE SHORT is worse than one that admits it does not know 6.7.10 (an 8th, OPS-1): an operand emitter that returned rax (-1) parked it across `_sc_pre`'s `lea`, which records no IR node, so CYRIUS_IR=3's LASE dropped the reload and the 24 B rows read garbage — the operand is emitted through `_OP_OPERAND_ADDR` (`_OPD_SV`) after the retptr push; a by-value struct-VARIABLE operand still met it (issues/archived/2026-10-08-ir3-drops-unrecorded-field-emits.md — closed by 6.7.14's IN-2, the 11th). 6.7.11 (a 9th, ARG-9): x86 `EFLADDR` (`lea rax, [rbp+d]` for a method receiver, a by-value struct argument, a hidden result buffer) recorded no IR node, so LASE took STORE_LOCAL x, the unrecorded lea, PUSH, LOAD_LOCAL x for a redundant reload and NOP-filled it — `o.with2(x)` passed `&o` as x under CYRIUS_IR=3 only. Every x86 emitter that writes rax records a node; `IR_RAW_EMIT` is the opaque marker for one with no opcode (it emits no bytes)."
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces four entries.** One subject: **the emitted byte stream contains more than
instructions, and every pass over it inherits that.**

**1 — Inline switch tables live IN the code stream.** A table-regime `switch` puts its jump
table among the instructions. Any unaligned byte scanner over a fn range — a peephole, a
compactor, a decoder — will walk into it and read table data as opcodes. A v6.5.20 P1 silent
miscompile came from exactly this, latent for multiple minors; a v6.5.53 adversarial review
of a NEW peephole found the OLD one had the same hole.

**2 — Some sites are in NO registry.** The entry trampoline's `E9` is hand-emitted (`EB(S,
0xE9)` + four raw bytes, patched later with `EPATCH`) rather than going through `EJMP0`, so
neither the per-fn `jump_src_tbl` nor the fixup table knows it exists. It never mattered while
compaction was per-function — its source and target both sit outside any fn body — and became
a SIGSEGV the moment a pass moved bytes program-wide.

**3 — The enumeration people reach for is incomplete.** "The tables the per-fn compactor
repairs" omits fn START offsets (`_fnt_offsets`), fn END offsets (`_fnt_codeend`, recorded
after the per-fn pass so it is already correct), and the trampoline. Widening a pass's scope
means re-deriving the list of stored positions from scratch, not inheriting it.

**4 — A WRONG length is worse than an UNKNOWN one.** A length decoder returning 0 fails safe:
the caller declines. One returning a length ONE BYTE SHORT desynchronises the walk and every
subsequent decode is garbage — and the resulting compiler is the SAME SIZE as the correct one,
so size comparison cannot detect it. `DECODE_LEN` had no case for `0x99` (CQO, before EVERY
integer division) and a `0F`+imm8 class that was short by one.

**5 — A pass that CLASSIFIES bytes must disqualify every form it cannot rewrite (6.6.5).** The
register picker is three stages over the same bytes: a use scan (counts `48 8B 85` / `48 89 85`,
the rax moves), an unsafe scan (disqualifies a slot), and a patch pass (rewrites exact 0x85). The
unsafe scan's trigger read the whole ModRM (`& 0xC7 == 0x85`, any reg) but its EXEMPTION read only
the prefix and opcode — so `48 8B 95`, `mov rdx,[rbp+disp32]`, was filed as a "plain mov the patch
pass will rewrite", and the patch pass never touched it. Every SIMD batch kernel reads its
operands in exactly that form (`_EMIT_LOAD_RDX_FROM_LOCAL`, 55 sites). A slot with two rax refs
was promoted, its store became a register move, and the kernel read a slot nothing wrote — silent
wrong code / SIGSEGV through all 33 batch intrinsics on every x86 picker target. It needed a way
for two uses to share a slot (a nested inline replay frees a param slot the next intrinsic
reuses) and shipped in 16 tagged releases (6.5.63 → 6.6.4) because cycc's own source has no intrinsic, so the fixpoint was
blind. The v6.5.64 comment on that very scan CLAIMED `48 8B 95` was already caught. FIX: one
predicate (`_ra_plain_slot_mov`) that all three stages call — a slot is promotable only if every
`[rbp+disp32]` reference to it is a form the rewriter handles. The byte scan is also disp32-only,
so an rbp-disp8 or SIB operand read would be invisible to all three stages; that dependency is now
a census gate (0 in 180,445 corpus functions) rather than an assumption.

**6 — A raw emit the IR is not told about, for ONE WIDTH (6.6.12).** Under `CYRIUS_IR=3` the IR
pass rewrites code around the nodes it recorded; bytes an emitter writes directly must be marked
with an `IR_RAW_EMIT` node or the pass treats rax as unchanged across them. x86's
`EFIELD_LOAD_W(S, width)` records that marker only for `width == 1 || 2 || 4`
(src/backend/x86/emit.cyr) — and a SIGNED narrow load is requested with a NEGATIVE width
(`_EMIT_NLOAD_RCX` takes -1/-2/-4 for movsx), which fails all three tests and reaches the IR
unrecorded. Signed struct FIELDS have been loaded that way since v6.3.35 and still read right
under `CYRIUS_IR=3` (measured on 6.6.12: i8 / i16 / i32 fields of a global and a local) — their
address reaches rcx through `EFLADDR_X1` / `EVADDR_X1`. The typed-array subscript
(`_arr_sub_load`) builds its address in rax out of RECORDED IR ops (`EADDR` is `IR_ADD`, then
`EMOVCA` is `IR_MOV_CA`) and follows them with the unrecorded movsx, and there an `i8` element
read wrong under `CYRIUS_IR=3` only — right in the default build. Fixed at the caller
(`if (w < 0) { _IR_REC0(S, IR_RAW_EMIT); }`; a second record for the backends that already
mark is harmless). ⚠ The recorder's guard was still unsigned-only, so the NEXT caller that
handed `EFIELD_LOAD_W` a signed width inherited the gap — and one did (6.7.14, the 11th, below:
the emitter itself now records every narrow width). Check any new path under `CYRIUS_IR=3` as well as the default build: this class
of miss is invisible to the default build by construction.

**7th instance (6.7.8): the slice read's frame base.** `_slice_sub_read` (v5.8.15) emitted the
slice's base with `EFLADDR` — raw bytes, no IR node on x86 — then pushed it and parsed the index.
Between `store i` and `load i` the IR pass saw nothing and forwarded i from rax, which by then held
`&s`: `var i = 1; return s[i] + 10;` exited 134 "slice bounds violation" under `CYRIUS_IR=3` only
(6.7.7 too; the default build, aarch64 and cx were right). `_arr_sub_addr` (6.6.12) had marked its
own base `IR_RAW_EMIT` for exactly this; the slice read now does, and so does the 6.7.8 slice store.
Any new emit of a raw base before a parsed operand gets the mark.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Before widening any pass over emitted bytes, enumerate (a) every table
embedded in the stream, (b) every stored code position, (c) every site emitted outside the
normal helper. Then mutation-prove that the pass fails when one is omitted. For a pass with
several stages (count / disqualify / rewrite), give them ONE predicate for "a form I handle" —
"disqualify anything not handled" is the only safe default — and check it with an oracle that
does not share the predicate (`objdump` decoding both a pass-on and a pass-off build, as
`simd_intrinsic_operand_slots.sh` axis C does).

**6.7.11 — the 9th (ARG-9), and two traps learned bisecting it.** LASE resets `last_store_idx` only on a
RECORDED rax clobber, and `ir_apply_lase` fills [CP(n), CP(n+1)) — so an eliminated node also swallows any
UNRECORDED bytes emitted after it. The fix records `_IR_REC0(S, IR_RAW_EMIT)` at `EFLADDR`'s head (its return
value ignored, so the bytes are emitted in every mode; nothing is recorded with the IR off, so the default build is
byte-identical). PE runs `ir_lase` as ANALYSIS only (never applied), so a LASE miscompile is ELF-only — a PE leg
cannot see one. And a pass-bisection loop under zsh must run under `sh`: `env $e cmd` with `e="A=1 B=2"` does not
word-split in zsh and sets ONE variable `A` to "1 B=2".

**6.7.12 — the 10th (DW-4): AN x86 CODE OFFSET RECORDED AT EMIT TIME IS NOT STABLE.** x86 moves code after emitting
it — the per-fn NOP compaction runs on every build where the register allocator takes a local, and wp_compact runs
under CYRIUS_IR=3 — while aarch64 has no compaction, so its `ESYSX_STUBS` can keep raw code positions and the x86
twin (`EMACHO_SYSX_STUBS`, the x86 Mach-O XLAT-2 stubs) cannot: a patch list of saved CPs patches the wrong bytes,
and a `call .` placeholder left unpatched is unbounded recursion. Anything patched later goes through the
whole-program disp32 registry (`_wpjs_add_raw`; keep the registry INDEX and read the site where it is NOW) or the
fixup table, never a saved CP. Assert the placeholder before patching ("is no longer its call placeholder") and
assert the list drained at FIXUP. Proof: mutation (f) of `emacho_sysxlat_fold.sh` (patch at the emitted offset) is
refused on a register-allocated probe, so the move is real. Related: the parser's constant fold (`_cfo`) folds a
syscall number only below 0x10000, so `syscall(0x2000014)` is a run-time number to the backend.
6.7.14 (PL-1): THE SAME RULE ON PE. The Windows errno reroutes call ONE shared `EPE_ERRNO_STUBS` table emitted after
EEXIT (beside `EMACHO_SYSX_STUBS`) through a 13-byte `call .` placeholder per site, registered with `_wpjs_add_raw`;
FIXUP asserts every placeholder was patched (`_pew_stubs_drained`), so a driver that forgets the stub call fails at
compile time, by name — pe_errno_stubs_region_declarations_and_the_ctl_signature_hash_v6714 #1.

**6.7.14 — the 11th (IN-2): AN UNRECORDED EMIT BELONGS TO THE NODE BEFORE IT.** IR=3's passes NOP-fill a node's range
`[cp, next node's cp)`, so bytes an emitter writes with no `_IR_REC*` are silently part of whichever node precedes
them, and a pass that kills that node kills them: the signed field `movsx` (`EFIELD_LOAD_W` recorded widths 1 / 2 / 4
only — item 6's warned NEXT caller) went with a dead `mov rcx, rax` (DCE), and `EFLADDR_X1`'s `lea rcx` with a dead
store (DSE). The 6.6.12 instance had been fixed only in its one caller (`_arr_sub_load`); the emitter itself now records
every narrow width. Every emitter that writes bytes records a node (`IR_RAW_EMIT` when it has no opcode). How to see
it: `CYRIUS_IR=2`'s dump shows the hole (`PUSH, POP_A, ST64` for a field store whose lea is invisible), and
`CYRIUS_DCE_CAP=0` / `CYRIUS_DSE_CAP=0` name the pass. (Closes issues/archived/2026-10-08-ir3-drops-unrecorded-field-emits.md;
a signed narrow field through a pointer runs under IR=3 since — typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678
#4.)

**6.7.14 — the 12th (ST-1): A FLOAT LITERAL.** `EMIT_FLOAT_LIT`'s `movabs rax, imm64` recorded no IR node, so LASE saw
rax unchanged across it, kept the local loaded BEFORE the literal as rax, and NOP-filled the reload after it: `q.f =
2.5;` stored through 2.5's bits, and `g(2.5, a)` passed them as `a`. TRAP 1: the filing guessed an unrecorded FIELD
emit (the 11th's class) — the disassembly diff (the reload dropped after the `movabs`) found the real site, and the
`g(2.5, a)` row proves it is not field-specific. TRAP 2: record it `IR_RAW_EMIT`, never `IR_LOAD_IMM` — LOAD_IMM would
let const-fold read float bits as an integer. Siblings: EF32_FROM / EF32_TO (6.7.6), EFLADDR (6.7.11), EFIELD_LOAD_W /
EFLADDR_X1 (the 11th).

**6.7.14 — the 13th (IN-18): A BYTE SCAN FOR E8 IS NOT A CALL GRAPH, BUT THE BUFFER IS NOT PURE INSTRUCTIONS EITHER.**
The x86 dead-code reachability counted every `E8` whose rel32 landed on a fn start — including bytes inside an imm32
/ disp32 — so the dead set moved with the data addresses (the mechanism and its cost:
ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714 #3). It walks with the length decoder now — and
KEEPS the byte scan wherever the bytes may not be instructions, MEASURED not assumed: a raw `asm { 0xEB; 0x02; 0x81;
0x78; }` makes the decoder read a 7-byte `cmp` over the next call and resync exactly on the span end; a switch's jump
table after `jmp rax` swallowed the call opening case 0 in 61 of 120 case-body layouts. "The walk lands exactly on the
span end" is WEAK evidence: x86 decoding resynchronises within a few instructions, so a walk that read garbage still
ends where it should. And a fn's range is no closed box for any pass that acts on it (IN-5) — the same lane entry #1.

**6.7.15 — the 14th (IT-1): A PASS THAT REWRITES A BYTE SPAN OWNS EVERY IR NODE INSIDE IT.** The IR=3 passes talk
through node states, and a node another pass already marked — IR_ELIMINATED, to be NOP-filled LATER by `ir_apply_lase`
— keeps its pending byte-level action even when a later rewrite reuses its bytes. `ir_const_fold` matches `LOAD_IMM(a),
PUSH, LOAD_IMM(b), POP_RCX, OP` and skips eliminated nodes inside it; in `var r = 0; r = r + 10;` DSE had eliminated `r
= 0`'s 3-byte store between the first LOAD_IMM and the PUSH, the fold wrote `mov eax, 10` across the whole span, and the
deferred fill landed in bytes 2-4 of the new immediate, which compaction then deleted: `b8 0a 50 b8 64` (`mov eax,
0x64b8500a`), a fault — on 6.6.20, 6.7.0 and 6.7.13, x86_64 only (the forks never call `ir_const_fold`). The fold now
consumes (IR_NOP) every node strictly inside its span (`_ir_fold_consume_span`, a helper to stay under cybs's per-fn
reference ceiling). It shows only when two passes fire on one span, and only under `CYRIUS_IR=3`: `CYRIUS_DSE_CAP=0` or
`CYRIUS_FOLD_OFF=1` hides it, which is how to tell. The corpus gate that runs the IR=3 + DCE pair (it was on no corpus
leg): infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715 #1.


[[entries]]
name = "a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times"
description = "CLASS — 7 recurrences (4 merged at the first v6.6.1 pass, 3 more on the second). A cause that was confidently NAMED was wrong about cycc's compile time twice, about a regalloc guard, about what a cap was hiding, and about an IR cost that was off by 20x. A carried binary SIZE is not identity. Instrument or count the thing itself"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries.** The recurrence count IS the lesson: this is the most repeated
*methodological* failure in the project.

**Wrong causes, confidently named:**
- cycc's compile-time superlinearity was blamed on `_fnt_grow` (costs 4–9 ms across an entire
  120,000-fn compile, not the 1,145 ms attributed to one call), on a rehash that DOUBLES and
  is therefore amortized-linear by construction, and on a DCE walk that is OFF by default and
  never ran in any measurement. The real causes: a hash keyed on **LENGTH ALONE** (20,168 ms →
  1,005 ms once the key includes content) and an open-addressed table grown only when **FULL**.
- **A cost jump at a doubling boundary is not evidence the doubling is the cost.** 60,000 fns
  737 ms → 70,000 fns 2,078 ms was read as "one grow costs 1,145 ms". The boundary was where
  LOAD FACTOR crossed.
- The roadmap asserted for months that the cross-BB regalloc defect was "live and localised to
  ONE line". That line was a **deliberate v5.6.22 correctness guard**, and deleting it alone
  fails **69 of 282**. The real blocker was an unnamed second one: a LIFETIME cap of 5.
- **Removing a cap unmasks whatever the cap was hiding.** Lifting the allocator's lifetime cap
  was correct and immediately exposed an O(n²) safety scan the cap had been concealing. A
  correct change can still make things worse; measure after.

**Carried numbers, never re-taken:**
- `CYRIUS_IR=3` was planned around "+3.6 % compile time (670 → 694 ms)" for several releases.
  Measured live: **13,967 ms** vs 672 — off by **20×**. The SIZE half of the same note was
  accurate, which is exactly why the time half survived: a half-right number reads as checked.
- **An identical binary SIZE is not byte identity.** ELF padding absorbs small additions — a
  one-line xlat entry landed while 872,790 bytes moved and the size did not change. Use `cmp`,
  never `ls -l`.
- **…and a size DELTA under page rounding is not a delta (6.7.12, DW-3r).** A commit message claimed the
  arm64-macOS compiler shrank 2,097,660 -> 2,081,276 B; the review rebuilt each tree from its own directory
  with its own cross compiler and got 2,081,276 B both times. The 1,152 B of code removed (36 `cmp x16,#42`
  words) hides under Mach-O's 16 KiB page rounding, while the CLI does cross a page (901,628 -> 885,244 B).
  For a Mach-O size claim count the instruction words or `.text`, and build every side from its own tree.

**A brief's premise about a kernel call is a verdict, not evidence (6.7.12, DW-5 — the eighth).** The first
macOS `sys_sigprocmask` shim picked Darwin's `sigprocmask` (48) because the brief said it "acts on the calling
thread", and the single-thread tests passed (round-trip, exec child). XNU's 48 goes through
`block_procsigmask` / `unblock_procsigmask` / `set_procsigmask`, which walk EVERY uthread — it is process-wide;
the per-thread call is `__pthread_sigmask` (329), the one libSystem's `pthread_sigmask` uses. What showed it: a
TWO-thread probe (the main thread blocks SIGUSR1, a second thread reads its own mask) — 48 gives
`thread_after=536870912` on ach and ecb, Linux and 329 give 0. The alias now routes to 329, pinned by a row
asserting another thread's mask is unchanged, with `THREADS_CONCURRENT == 1` asserted on macOS so the row cannot
pass vacuously. A single-thread test cannot tell a per-thread call from a process-wide one.

**6.7.14 — THREE MORE NAMED CAUSES, EACH WRONG.** (a) ST-1: the filing blamed an unrecorded FIELD emit for `q.f =
2.5;` storing garbage under IR=3; the disassembly diff showed the reload dropped after a float literal's `movabs`, and
a `g(2.5, a)` row with no field at all proved it (any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…, the 12th). (b) VC-15:
the filing said an explicit instance's `**T` parameter was pinned without its binding; measuring `g<S>(..)` beside
`g(..)` showed the explicit instance RIGHT and the implicit call never instantiating, because `_gs_param` reads ONE
`*`. Measure the two spellings side by side before blaming the instance. (c) IN-6r: the lane's own staged note called
the IR=3 losing-body-range regression "Low / sound / pre-existing" — the review found it was none of the three (live fns
removed, SIGILL; compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not, 6.7.14). A triage verdict carried into a
note is a claim to re-measure, not a finding.

**6.7.15 — FIVE MORE.** (a) A FILING'S LOCATION (ST-19): the top-level `o.mk().p[0] = 5;` second error was filed as
written "inside PARSE_FIELD_LOAD's chain"; it came from `_stmt_qmark` -> `_stmt_call_sub` and the factor's `_sub_post`
(forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715 #12). (b) A BRIEF'S GUESSED RETURN VALUE
(RC-2): the inner reporter was said to return 0 on the double-report path; it returns 2, so the mark goes on the note,
not on the return value. (c) A TRIAGE'S COUNTS, CARRIED ACROSS WAVES (the vectors lane): waves 0 and 1 landed between
the triage and the lane, and the filed repros printed ALL their lines on the lane's base where the triage had seen two —
re-measure every carried count on the base the lane actually forks from (and IT-4's triage plan said 255+ refused where
the issue said 256 / 255; the issue's numbers shipped, rowed both sides). (d) A BRIEF'S PREMISE ABOUT A HELPER (GN-24):
`_fn_src_name` was said to keep a free spelling's mangled name; measured, it renames `S_two(..)` to `S.two`. (e) A CLAIM
THE LANE'S OWN COMMIT DOES NOT SUPPORT, found by the review each time: IT-9's text called its gate green under `bash -eo
pipefail` (it stopped after L1; IT-9r withdrew the claim), VC-1's bullet gave one before-value for every target (VC-1r:
43 on x86_64 and PE, 84 on aarch64), VC-6's newly-refused list omitted `Vec` / `Result` / `Option` (VC-6r), GN-15's text
said "struct" for a reader that also peels scalars (GN-15r). A bullet in the lane's own CHANGELOG is a claim like any
other: re-run it before it is written, on every target it names.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Instrument or COUNT the thing before designing around a stated mechanism,
and re-take any number you are about to plan against. Several of these survived multiple
releases and two prior investigations apiece.


[[entries]]
name = "agnos_is_an_x86_host_that_is_not_linux_and_the_predefines_do_not_say_so"
description = "CLASS — 3 recurrences merged v6.6.1, a 4th at 6.6.14 (its sock_send waits for the ACK inside the call, so no user-space deadline bounds a write). `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` is predefined on EVERY x86-hosted build including the AGNOS ring-3 target, so `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` alone silently selects Linux syscall numbers on agnos. Add that agnos has no /proc and a stub written against an older kernel ABI rots silently, and agnos needs a precedence check in three separate places"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces three entries.** One subject: **agnos looks like x86 Linux to every mechanism
that asks a coarse question.**

**1 — The arch predefine does not distinguish it.** `main.cyr` unconditionally does
`PP_PREDEFINE(S, "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86")` — the AGNOS ring-3 target included, because agnos IS
x86. So a stdlib file selecting syscall numbers on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
`CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` **alone** silently takes the Linux branch and emits Linux syscall
numbers, which compile clean and corrupt at runtime. **The fix shape is a PRECEDENCE branch:**
test `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` FIRST, then fall through to arch. Surfaced v6.2.33 by
sakshi's logger. Still live by design — this is the pattern, not a bug to fix.

**2 — A `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;` stub rots silently.** Written against an
EARLIER kernel ABI, it keeps returning 0 after the kernel grows the real call. The stub's
comment naming a kernel version is a TIMESTAMP, not an invariant. ⚠ The v6.6.1 clock case is
the sharp version: the v6.2.6 "fix" bound `clock_now_ns()` to `uptime_ms` (#40, `timer_ticks`)
which is **FROZEN with interrupts cleared** — replacing a stub that returned 0 with one that
returns a plausible CONSTANT, strictly harder to spot. Now `uptime_us` (#95, rdtsc).

**3 — agnos has no /proc**, so `getenv()` walks the exec-stack envp from `_agnos_init_rsp`.
Anything that reaches for `/proc/self/*` is a Linux assumption wearing portable clothes.

**4 — Its sockets are not Linux sockets (6.6.14, measured with a fake-kernel probe whose #48
takes 8 s).** `sock_send#48` is ACK-SYNCHRONOUS: `tcp_send_ex` sends one segment and blocks INSIDE
the call until it is ACKed, up to `TCP_PROGRESS_US` = 8 s, and #48 takes no time argument (a4 is
unused). There is no writability readiness for a conn (`epoll_wait#21` reports EPOLLIN only, and
only for `VFS_SOCK` fds) and no non-blocking send, so no user-space deadline can cut a write
short: native TLS under a +1 s deadline returned `TLS_ERR_TIMEOUT` ~8.25 s late, and smaller
chunks do not help (each waits for its own ACK). This entry said "the fix is kernel-side — #48
honouring a4 as a µs bound": SUPERSEDED at agnos 1.57.13 / cyrius 6.7.11, below — "no user-space
deadline bounds a write" now holds only for a kernel before 1.57.13. (A dead connection answers -1,
never a signal.)
**4b — 6.7.11: a NEW number, not a new argument.** agnos minted `sock_send_dl`#109 rather than an a4
on `#48`, because on this ABI an unused argument register carries a STALE value: every shipped
three-argument `#48` caller leaves whatever r10 held in a4 (`agnos_peer_fake_kernel.sh` axis 12 pins
a call made right after a 7-argument call that left r10 = 5) — widening a live call's arity is not
backward compatible. THE TRAP: #109 reads `timeout_us = 0` as NO ceiling. THE FIX:
`_agnos_sock_send_dl` passes the time LEFT, breaks at -1, and never passes time's up as 0; support
is probed once (a zero-length #109) and cached — and cached as "no" only after the `#48` fallback
answers >= 0, because #109 answers -1 both on a pre-1.57.13 kernel and for a dead or foreign conn.
The peers since 6.7.11: #109 `sock_send_dl`, #110 `console_mode`, #111 `fd_kind` (the next free is
#112; platform_abi `agnos_two_sided_abi_mirror`).

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Ask "is this check TARGET-specific or ARCH-specific?" — on agnos the two
disagree, and only the target question is safe. Gate with
`tests/gates/platform/folds_agnos_parity.sh` (every folded stdlib that builds for Linux must
also build for agnos) and verify against the live kernel ABI doc, not the stub's comment.


[[entries]]
name = "a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write"
description = "CLASS — 3 recurrences merged v6.6.1, two more at 6.6.16. A correct in-loop guard that DEFERS work to a later pass is only half an algorithm, and the other half gets forgotten: closures dropped every parameter past the 6th, gvar inits were emitted after a pass that never returns (and at 6.6.16 the same late replay still held every array list and every enum-naming initializer), and #derive bodies landed after the user's tail. The guard makes the code look finished"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deferred-pass", "kmode", "kernel", "global-init-order", "static-init", "replay", "EMIT_GVAR_INITS", "enum", "array", "cx", "prestore", "efi", "v6.5.46", "v6.6.16"]
---
⟳ **Replaces three entries.** One shape: **an in-loop guard that says "handle this later"
where the later never got written** — and because the guard itself is correct, review passes
over it.

**1 — Closures dropped every parameter past the 6th.** The in-loop guard correctly stopped at
the register-argument boundary and implied a deferred stack-param pass. That pass was never
written, so params 7+ were silently discarded. Fixed v6.5.46 by writing the second half.

**2 — Global-var inits were emitted after a pass that never returns.** In `kmode==1` the emit
order puts gvar-init AFTER `PARSE_PROG`, so top-level `var X = <literal>;` got its store
**code-emitted but never executed** — agnos's `XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS = 10000000` read as 0. A
timeout of zero does not crash; it looks like a hardware fault. Fixed with static-init tables
that bake the value into the image, removing the second pass entirely.

**2b — 6.6.16: the static-init table covered only what pass 1 could fold, and the rest kept the
late store.** The table took literals (v5.11.64) and constant expressions (v6.4.74). An array
list (`var t: i64[3] = {1, 2, 42};`, C1) and an initializer naming an enum constant
(`var X = A;`, C6) were still stores in the late replay — so `kernel; var B[1] = {0x77, 0x66}`
put its stores behind the asm and `var X = A;` read 0 in agnos-shaped kernels. (A comment claimed
byte lists "don't suffer the kmode-entry init-order bug"; measured, they did.) THE ORDER, x86
`kernel;` only (`src/main.cyr`'s `_init_km == 1` branch — the v5.7.19 multiboot invariant, agnos
depends on it, do not reorder): entry -> enum stores -> PARSE_PROG -> EMIT_GVAR_INITS -> exit.
Every other build, and every OTHER fork's kernel build, replays BEFORE PARSE_PROG.
⭐ **AN INITIALIZER HAS TWO CLOCKS:** where its NAMES resolve and where its VALUE is read. Pass 1
sees only what is declared ABOVE, so folding there is wrong twice — `var X = A; enum E { A = 3; }`
(forward, no fold) and `enum E { A = 3; } var X = A; var A = 9;` (the program reads 9; pass 1
would bake 3). The REPLAY is the one point where every name is registered and resolves exactly as
the replayed store will (`_findvar_core`, a kernel build's program globals hidden). So the fold
sits there (`_gvk_pre` for scalars, `_gai_replay` for lists, `_cf_enum = 1` only there), the value
lands in a blob (`_vgsi_base`, `_vgai_base`) that every image writer copies into the var area
(`_EMIT_GVAR_STATIC_INITS` -> `_gai_bake`), and the replayed store stays (code bytes unchanged).
⚠ **"EVERY WRITER" INCLUDES cx — check the writer before trusting "this target has no image".**
The first cut believed the 6.6.6 prestore comment ("cx has no file image") and turned the blob into
run-time stores on cx; a `.cyx` DOES carry its var data (main_cx.cyr wrote zeros, cxvm copies the
body into the guest), so cx alone read a forward list as 0. And cx stores a constant global
TWICE at run time (`_gv_cx_prestore` ahead of the replay, then its own deferred entry), so a bake
needs both accounted for: the prestore skips a slot a live list bakes (`_gai_cx_blobs` makes
only the LIVE lists' blobs, first), and a list redeclaration supersedes the name's earlier
deferred entries (`_gv_supersede(S, noff, 1)`, keeping earlier LISTS so two overlay). Either alone
left `var F1 = 5; var F1[1] = {0};` reading 5 on cx (cx_array_initializer.sh mutations 4, 5).
The same double store hides clobbers: a canary right behind an array on cx is re-written by its
own entry and repairs itself — a canary must not be written by anything that runs after the code
under test. A blob is allocated by the entry that writes it, not at pass 1, or a superseded entry's
zeroed blob lands over the later value.
⚠ **THE WARNING FOR WHAT STAYS LATE MUST BE TRUE FOR THE BUILD IT FIRES IN:** gate it on a flag
set in main.cyr's `_init_km == 1` branch (`_gvk_arm`), never on `kernel_mode` (an aarch64 kernel
is kernel_mode and replays first); exempt an EFI application with `efi_main` (gnoboot — efi_main
runs after the late replay); check AFTER the arm's own diagnostics (a failing build warns nothing).
**TEST IT WITHOUT A KERNEL:** a `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1` build is an ELF64 at 0x100000 that Linux
runs as a process (program, late replay, exit) — check the globals and exit with the first wrong
row. Prove a bake by DIFFERENTIAL, not grep: build each kernel with the lists and with the arrays
declared bare, require the same image size and every differing byte after the program's marker
(kmode_array_initializer_baked.sh, kmode_enum_initializer_baked.sh). Survey at landing: agnos x86
= 2 warnings (string globals its own comments already mark unsafe), gnoboot 0. CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

**3 — `#derive` must emit generated bodies BEFORE the user's tail.** The remedy has an
ordering requirement: flattening generated bodies onto the struct's closing brace keeps the
construct's expansion line-neutral, but they must precede whatever the user wrote after.

⚠ **`EMIT_GVAR_INITS` is a HAND-DUPLICATED parallel copy of `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT`** — the same
two-pass shape, maintained twice. A change to one that is not applied to the other is the
fork problem (see the seven-forks entry) at function scope.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** When you write a guard that defers, write the deferred pass in the SAME
commit or leave the guard failing loudly. "Correct as far as it goes" compiles, runs, and
produces wrong answers with no diagnostic.


[[entries]]
name = "a_compat_row_that_turns_fail_closed_into_fail_open_ships_with_its_decline"
description = "v6.6.4. A raw x86 syscall number in arch-neutral code is not 'unsupported' on aarch64 — it is a DIFFERENT syscall, and the raw-literal diagnostic cannot see that class by construction (a valid aarch64 number is silent by design). Four shipped: cbt's getppid(110) ran timer_settime and killed every `cyrius run/test` child on native aarch64 for 59 releases; hashseed's time(201) ran listen(0); sigil's uname(63) ran read; dynlib's arch_prctl(158) ran getgroups — which on REAL hardware returns the group COUNT (qemu-user EFAULTs it), so the `rc == 0` caller failed open exactly on group-less processes. Two habits: derive the allowlist from the emitter, and land a decline in the same commit as any ESYSXLAT row that would make an x86-only path start 'working'."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["esysxlat", "aarch64", "raw-syscall", "fail-open", "getgroups", "getppid", "qemu-user", "cross-os", "v6.6.4"]
---
**The class.** `syscall(<x86 literal>, …)` in `lib/` or `cbt/` reaches aarch64-Linux through
ESYSXLAT, which renumbers the x86 numbers it has rows for and passes the rest through
UNCHANGED. A passed-through number is not an error on aarch64 — it is whatever aarch64
assigned that number to. The v6.5.51 raw-literal diagnostic derives its table from the aarch64
PEER's declared `SYS_*` names, so a number the peer declares for a *different* syscall is silent
**by design** (`syscall_xlat_generated.sh` axis 5 pins that 63 = x86 `uname` / aarch64 `read`
stays quiet). The most dangerous class is therefore the one the diagnostic is built to ignore.

**What shipped that way, all found in one sweep (v6.6.4):**
- `cbt/build.cyr`: `syscall(110)` (`getppid`) after `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` → aarch64 `timer_settime`
  → `-EINVAL` ≠ ppid → child exits 1 before `execve`. EVERY `cyrius run/test/tests/bench/fuzz`
  child on native aarch64, 6.5.19 → 6.6.3 (59 releases). The pi gate self-hosted the COMPILER each
  release and never built or ran the CLI; from 6.5.51 on, the CLI's own aarch64 build printed a
  warning for that line. Note the fix is the SYMBOL, not a row: each Linux peer declares its own
  `SYS_GETPPID` (110 / native 173) and ESYSXLAT carries no 110 row.
- `lib/hashseed.cyr`: `syscall(201, 0)` (`time`) → aarch64 `listen(0, …)` → `-ENOTSOCK` as the
  hash seed.
- `lib/sigil.cyr`: raw 63 (`uname`) → aarch64 `read`.
- `lib/dynlib.cyr`: `syscall(158, 0x1002, tls)` (`arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)`) → aarch64
  `getgroups(4098, tls)`.

**⚠ The qemu trap.** The filing traced under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` and reported every wrong
call as failing closed. qemu-user's `getgroups` range-checks the whole `4098 × 4` destination
before the call and returns `-EFAULT`. The real kernel on pi writes the process's supplementary
GIDs into the TLS block and returns their COUNT — and the caller took only `rc == 0` as success,
so it failed closed BY ACCIDENT on a process with supplementary groups (pi: 2) and failed OPEN
on one with none (`setgroups(0)` daemons, minimal containers): a TLS block the CPU never
installed, handed back as installed. Two lessons in one row: a cross-OS finding traced under an
emulator is a hypothesis about the hardware — run it on the host before writing "fails closed"
into a severity — and "the wrong syscall returned an error" is not the same as "the caller
failed closed": read what the caller does with the return value.

**⚠ The fail-open a fix can create.** The obvious repair — add ESYSXLAT rows for `fstat` 5 and
`lstat` 6 — would have made `_fdlopen_mmap_elf`'s `fstat` start WORKING on aarch64, so an x86-only
ELF loader that had been failing by accident would proceed to `mmap` an aarch64 object and jump
into it. A compat row that turns an accidental fail-closed into fail-open **ships in the same
commit as the decline** (`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64 return -1`), plus the belt the decline
implies (`e_machine != 62` refused on every arch). The rows alone would have been a regression
that every gate called green.

**The habit that catches the class.** Do not maintain an allowlist; DERIVE it. `cmp x8,#N` on
aarch64 is `0xF100001F | N<<10`, so every `EW(S, 0xF1……1F)` word in ESYSXLAT's ELF arm is a
routed source number. `tests/gates/platform/raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh` decodes them (44
rows) and scans every raw `syscall(<literal>, …)` in `lib/` + `cbt/` (325 sites) against the
set, tracking `#ifdef`/`#ifndef`/`#else` target guards so a literal under
`CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` is judged native. A new row is admitted automatically; a new raw literal
fails until it is routed, spelled `SYS_*`, or guarded. And the cross-OS leg now builds and RUNS
the CLI on pi — a wrapper that compiles on five targets is not a wrapper that runs.

[[entries]]
name = "nested_call_alignment_stack_machine"
description = "A stack-machine codegen is rsp-aligned only BETWEEN statements. cycc padded nothing for values pending inside an expression, so any call nested in an argument or operand entered its callee 8 bytes off — and on PE the base itself was inverted, so it was the statement-level calls that were wrong."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "alignment", "sysv", "win64", "codegen", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The trap, in one sentence**: if your codegen evaluates
expressions on the machine stack, "the frame is 16-aligned" is a
statement about the gaps BETWEEN statements, and a call emitted
inside an expression is aligned only by luck.

cycc pushes one 8-byte value per pending operand (`push rax` in
EPUSHR/ESPILL). The only alignment invariant it had was the frame
rounding `fsz = (flc*8+15) & -16`. So:

```cyr
store64(pp, create_buffer(dev, desc));   # arg 2: 1 pending -> rsp 8 off
var buf = create_buffer(dev, desc);      # statement level  -> aligned
store64(pp, buf);
```

Both x86 ABIs require rsp ≡ 0 at the CALL. A C callee that spills
SSE with `movaps`/`movdqa` — what gcc emits for ordinary code —
takes a #GP. Found by mabda 4.1.3 as a SIGSEGV inside NVK's
`create_buffer`; whether it faults depends on the callee, so it
reads as intermittent and vendor-specific.

**Two properties that make the source text a bad guide:**

* The shift is INHERITED. A cyrius fn entered misaligned runs its
  whole body misaligned, so its statement-level calls are off too,
  to any depth. A second misaligned nesting cancels it. So the
  question is never "is this call nested", it is "can anything
  that reaches C be reached from a nested position".
* The same text can have opposite alignment in two positions.
  `fncallN(...)` in expression position was LOWERED (callee
  spilled to a frame slot); as a bare statement it was a plain
  call into lib/fnptr.cyr with the callee pushed as argument 1.

**PE was inverted, which is the part worth remembering.**
Windows (and UEFI firmware) enter an image the way they call any
function: return address pushed, rsp ≡ 8. Linux enters at ≡ 0.
cyrius never accounted for that, so the whole PE base ran one
parity off — every statement-level call entered its callee at
≡ 0 and the ODD-depth nested calls were the aligned ones. It
survived because cyrius code uses no aligned SSE, but the seven
fixed-frame kernel32 reroutes (EOPEN_PE/ECREATEDIR_PE/EDELETEF_PE
0x258, EEXIT 0x28) had each been hand-tuned to that base over
years — so `f(0, open(path))` faulted inside kernelbase's own
`movaps` in CreateFileW.

⭐ **That is also why the v6.0.71 entry seed "destabilised cycc,
don't retry".** It did — because the seed alone leaves those
frames compensating for a base that no longer needs it. Seed and
retune ship together or PE breaks; neither half is testable
without the other.

**Fix shape (6.6.5)**: a compile-time counter `_xdepth` of
pending 8-byte pushes, maintained by every push/pop consumer, and
ONE parity rule at the call site per ABI (SysV n<=6 `push rax` /
`pop rcx`; SysV n>6 `(nextra+d)&1`; PE n<=4 folded into the
shadow sub; PE n>4 `_pe_call_frame(n, d)`), plus the PE/UEFI
landing seed and the retuned reroutes.

**Recognize again**: any "crashes only sometimes, inside a
vendor library, on its first SSE instruction" report; any
`gdb p $rsp & 0xF` = 8 at a callee entry; any fix that works when
you hoist the call to its own statement. And structurally: a new
target whose loader enters with a return address pushed needs a
landing seed, or every alignment fact you have is inverted there.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "counter_model_gate_shares_the_defect"
description = "A gate that asks the compiler's own model to check that model reads GREEN. The 6.6.5 alignment work measured one mutant that produced zero compile-time desync reports and 24 CPU-measured misaligned calls."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "verification", "methodology", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
6.6.5 added a compile-time counter (`_xdepth`) modelling how many
values are pushed on the expression stack, plus `_xd_check` at the
parser chokepoints to report a desync. That check is useful and it
is NOT a gate for the bug it was built alongside.

**Measured, not reasoned — and the FIRST version of this note got
the mutant wrong, which is itself the lesson.** The mutant that
produces the zero-desync result is: ECALLCLEAN stops EMITTING the
`pop rcx` that releases the alignment pad **but still decrements
`_xdepth`**. ECALLPOPS increments, ECALLCLEAN decrements, the
model balances perfectly, and the emitted code leaks 8 bytes per
padded call:

* compile-time desync reports: **0**
* CPU-measured misaligned call sites in the gate driver: **24**,
  then SIGSEGV on the `movaps` leaf

⚠ Deleting the whole line — the emitted byte AND the decrement —
is a DIFFERENT mutant and DOES desync: **200 reports** on a cycc
self-compile, because ECALLPOPS's increment is then unmatched.
The first draft of this note (and of the gate header, and of the
CHANGELOG) attributed the zero-desync result to that one, without
rebuilding it. Both were then rebuilt and measured. A mutation
ledger you did not run is a claim, not evidence.

The gate that catches it computes `(rsp+8) & 15` inside a
gcc-assembled leaf — the CPU, reading the frame the caller really
built — and compares against the ABI constant 0. Two independent
derivations; the compiler's counter is not consulted.

⚠ This is the SIXTH instance of this file's "a green gate
measuring nothing" class, and it is kept as its own entry only
because the mechanism is new and measured: the check and the
checked share a model, so one edit moves both.

**The general rule this instance supports**: compute the expected
value a DIFFERENT way from the actual one. A model-checking
assertion is a good tripwire for the cases you did not think of,
and it is worthless against a change that edits the model and the
code in one motion — which is what every "fix" to that area will
look like.

Related: the v6.3.26 FFI gate called `fncall4..7` at TOP LEVEL
only, so it proved alignment for the one position this bug never
touched, and its pass was then cited in the guide and in
docs/ffi/fncall-abi.md as proof of the general claim. A gate
proves the position it tests.

**6.6.14 — an "independent" re-derivation that shared the MAPPING.** The
Windows trust-store test re-derived the exported bundle from the `ROOT`
store certificate by certificate, with its own reading of the root-program
properties — and read property 104 as "distrusted for certificates issued
after this date", exactly as the code did (104 is the Disable date; the
issuance bound is 126). The two agreed, the row was green, and the first
cut exported the three Entrust roots as full TLS anchors. A different way
means a different SOURCE for the fact (wincrypt.h's names, a dump of the
host's real properties), not a second copy of the same belief.

**6.7.10 — a mode-off check that compares only mode-off spellings shares the defect.** The
execution-coverage switch's S10 compared unset / `=0` / empty with ONE compiler, so "the hit is
emitted with the mode off" (M17) made all three identical and S10 green. The absolute anchor is
H7: the mode-off binary holds none of the dump's bytes (`cyrius-coverage`, `__cyrius_cov`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "uefi_exit_must_restore_the_entry_rsp_not_adjust_it"
description = "Under UEFI the exit is a bare `ret` to firmware and `ret` pops [rsp] ITSELF, so the exit must RESTORE the entry rsp — an `add rsp, 8` give-back only fixes the one depth it was computed for. 6.6.5 shipped the give-back first: `syscall(60,x)` inside a fn body #GP'd under OVMF and every gate was green over it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["uefi", "pe", "abi", "alignment", "gates", "emission-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
6.6.5 put a 4-byte `sub rsp, 8` at the PE/UEFI landing so that
depth 0 means 16-aligned on every target (Windows and UEFI
firmware enter an image with the return address already pushed,
rsp ≡ 8; Linux enters at ≡ 0). On Windows that is the whole
story — `EEXIT` there is `call [ExitProcess]; int3` and never
returns.

**Under UEFI it is not.** The entry point IS an MS-x64 function
and firmware reads its `rax` as the EFI_STATUS, so `EEXIT` emits
a single `0xC3`. `ret` pops `[rsp]` ITSELF, so it is correct only
where rsp is EXACTLY the rsp firmware entered with.

⛔ **THE FIRST FIX PAID THE SEED BACK (`add rsp, 8`) AND THAT IS
THE WRONG SHAPE.** A give-back corrects one known displacement;
the exit has to be right at every displacement. rsp is 8 low at
top level, and a whole frame plus its locals low inside a fn
body. Measured on OVMF (edk2, q35):

```
var q = 7; syscall(60, q);                      # top level
  6.6.4:        returns cleanly to BdsDxe
  seed only:    X64 Exception Type - 06(#UD)
  give-back:    clean

fn f(){var a=3; syscall(60,a); return 0;} var r=f();
  6.6.4:        survives by luck
  give-back:    X64 Exception Type - 0D(#GP), RIP inside the stack
  (with a ConOut print first, 6.6.4 #UDs too — the class was
   broken on BOTH compilers)
```

**The shipped fix parks and restores.** The landing emits
`lea r13, [rsp]` BEFORE the seed and `EEXIT` emits
`mov rsp, r13; ret`. Depth-independent, so there is nothing
per-site to track. r13 is reserved from the register allocator on
that target (`_ra_cap = 2` in parse_fn.cyr) — a parked register
that regalloc may assign to a hot local is not parked.

⚠ The park is a 4-byte `lea` and not the 3-byte `mov r13, rsp` on
purpose: the landing must stay an EVEN number of bytes or every
inline CHAR16 literal in a hand-written-asm image (efi_probe,
gnoboot) loses its 2-byte alignment.

⛔ **THE SECOND TRAP IS EMISSION ORDER, AND IT IS THE REUSABLE
ONE. fn BODIES ARE EMITTED BEFORE THE LANDING.** The give-back was
gated on a flag set at `EPATCH(jmp_patch)` and cleared per fn body
by PARSE_FN_DEF — and the top-level definition pass runs FIRST, so
that flag read 0 inside every fn body no matter what the clear
did. The per-body clear looked like it was doing the work. Any
"set a flag at the landing, read it while emitting" is wrong for
anything a fn body emits; derive the condition instead. The
shipped EEXIT reads kmode (`L64(S + 0x18FCA0)`) at the point of
emission for its one remaining choice, executable vs
object/shared mode.

FOUR routes reach that firmware `ret` and a fix has to cover all
four:

1. `main.cyr`'s fall-through exit at the end of the image.
2. A top-level `syscall(60, x)`, reaching EEXIT through the PARSER.
3. A `syscall(60, x)` INSIDE a fn body — not exotic: `lib/alloc.cyr`
   and `lib/bounds.cyr` abort that way, so a UEFI image takes this
   route on OOM or a bounds trip with no explicit exit in user code.
4. `programs/efi_probe.cyr`, whose hand-written asm returns from
   its OWN `ret` and never reaches EEXIT — so it carries the
   correction itself, as a deliberately ASYMMETRIC
   `sub rsp,0x20` / `add rsp,0x28`.

⛔ **THE GATE LESSON IS THE BIGGER ONE, AND IT REPEATED.** OVMF
TOLERATES A MISALIGNED CALL BUT NOT A MISALIGNED RETURN, and the
EFI smoke gate grepped serial output for `hello, uefi` — so it
PASSED on an image that printed and then took a #UD one
instruction later. It now also requires the absence of
`X64 Exception` and a post-return marker. **And that was still not
enough**: the only image it boots is `efi_probe.cyr`, which
returns through route 4 and therefore had NEVER tested how the
COMPILER ends a UEFI image. Route 3 shipped broken under a green
smoke, a green byte gate and a code review. There is now a second
boot (`programs/efi_fn_exit_probe.cyr`) that exits from inside a
fn, and a byte axis that COUNTS `mov rsp,r13; ret` in such an
image — two are required, because that image has two firmware
returns and a top-level-only fix produces one.

**Recognize again**: any entry-alignment seed on a target whose
exit is a `ret` rather than a syscall; any fix expressed as "undo
the N bytes we added" where the site can be reached at more than
one depth; any flag set during emission and read by something
emitted earlier; any gate whose only fixture exercises a path that
bypasses the code under test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "probe_row_reports_only_through_the_returned_argument"
description = "An alignment/value probe row measures nothing unless the callee returns the argument the probe sits in. `snd(probe(), 0)` returns its SECOND argument and is the constant 0 — four rows of the 6.6.5 alignment test and three of its shell gate were dead that way, and the fix's own test scored a perfect pass over them."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "verification", "methodology", "testing", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
When a test probes codegen by calling a measuring function from
many expression positions, the row only reports if the VALUE it
finally asserts on carries the probe's reading. It is very easy
to write rows that cannot:

```cyr
fn snd(x, y): i64 { return y; }
a(snd(align_probe(), 0), "argument 1");   # constant 0 — DEAD
a(thd(0, align_probe(), 0), "argument 2"); # thd returns z — DEAD
t = 8; if (zero < probe() + 1) { t = 0; } # true for 0 AND for 8 — DEAD
Rec_b(&rec)                                # a derived getter returns
                                           # the FIELD, never the call
```

All four shapes shipped in the first cut of 6.6.5's own alignment
test and gate, and every one of them PASSED — against the broken
compiler as well as the fixed one.

**The audit that finds them is cheap and mechanical**: rebuild
with the probe hardcoded to return the FAILING value (`mov rax,8;
ret` in place of `lea 8(%rsp),%rax; and $15,%rax`) and require
every row to report. Measured at 6.6.5: 63 of 64 tcyr rows and
56 of 56 gate rows report; the one that does not is a deliberate
loop-count control. Run it whenever a row is added — it is the
per-row analogue of the anti-vacuous twin, which only proves the
probe itself is live.

⚠ A single anti-vacuous control is NOT enough. The 6.6.5 file had
one from the start (`align_probe_8`, reading one slot higher, must
return 8) and it was green while four rows were dead: the probe
was live, the rows just threw its answer away.

⚠ Corollary for the thing being measured: some constructs cannot
report at all. A `#derive(accessors)` GETTER returns the field, so
only the SETTER's argument can carry a probe. Say so at the site
rather than leaving a row that looks like coverage.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_top_level_statement_closes_the_declaration_window"
description = "struct / enum definitions and struct-TYPED globals must appear before the first top-level statement. After one, `struct X {` is `unexpected struct`, and a `var g: T = ...` still compiles but silently loses the struct-type annotation operator-overload dispatch keys on."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["frontend", "parser", "language", "testing", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Hit while writing a test that needed a struct, an enum, an
`impl`, a `#derive(accessors)` struct and a `#naked` asm probe in
one file. Two distinct symptoms, one cause:

* **`enum Tri { ... }` or `struct Pt { ... }` placed after the
  first top-level statement** is a hard error — `unexpected
  struct` / `unexpected enum`, followed by a cascade of
  `undefined variable` for every field name. Definitions are
  collected in a pre-pass over the top of the file.
* **`var g: OpV = 5;` placed after the first top-level statement
  compiles** and is usable — but the struct-type annotation does
  not survive, and `EMIT_OP_DISPATCH` keys on exactly that, so
  `g + 3` silently becomes a plain integer add instead of calling
  `OpV_add`. No diagnostic.

The second one is the dangerous one, and it is how a test row
that looks like it exercises operator overloading exercises
nothing.

**Ordering that works**: includes → enums → probe/helper fns the
definitions need → structs / impls / `#derive` → globals →
top-level statements → `fn main`. In particular, put the fns an
`impl` body calls ABOVE the `impl`: an impl method that forward-
references gets `warning: undefined function`.

⚠ Related, and worth checking before writing any operator-overload
test: `tests/tcyr/lang/advanced.tcyr`'s `operator add` row asserts
`20 + 22 == 42` over a struct-typed LOCAL. A plain integer add
satisfies that too, and a struct-typed local does not dispatch at
all — so that row proves nothing about dispatch. A dispatch test
needs a GLOBAL receiver and a helper returning a value plain
arithmetic could not produce (the shape
`tests/tcyr/frontend/const_chained_multiply_fold.tcyr` uses:
`fn OpV_add(a, b) { return 3000 + b; }`).

**6.7.14 (FE-25, the user's D13 (a) and D14 (a)) — THE CASCADE IS
GONE, THE RULE STAYS.** A `struct` / `enum` / `union` / `trait` /
`impl` / `mod` / `use` / `pub` declaration after the first top-level
statement is now ONE error naming the rule and the first statement's
line ("a `struct` declaration must come before the first top-level
statement (line 3)"); nothing that compiled changes. An `include` of a
file that declares one is reported at the INCLUDE line of the file the
user wrote: the includer's file resumes after the inclusion with base =
include line + 1 in the file map, so the declaration found inside the
inclusion maps back to it (`_dl_incl`). Uses of the refused struct
further down still cascade (filed). And an `async fn` there is a
function, as a plain `fn` is (D14 (a): only the `async` prefix had been
left unconsumed).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "process_entry_sp_parity_varies_with_argv_env_darwin_x86"
description = "A process entry SP parity is a per-KERNEL fact, not an ABI fact. On Darwin x86_64 the initial rsp's 16-byte parity varies with the argv/env string byte count, so the same binary is aligned under one process name and misaligned under another. Never assume a kernel-aligned entry — a landing must ESTABLISH the alignment invariant, not inherit it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "abi", "alignment", "macho", "platform", "verification", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Every alignment rule in cyrius's x86 backend is stated RELATIVE
to the entry landing — "depth 0 means 16-aligned", and the pad
machinery keeps that true through pushes. So the landing itself
has to be an ABSOLUTE. In 6.6.5 THREE targets turned out to be
inheriting it from an assumption instead:

* **PE/Win64** — Windows enters with the return address pushed,
  rsp = 8 (mod 16). Seeded (`sub rsp, 8`).
* **UEFI** — same firmware entry, plus a `ret` back to firmware.
  Seeded, plus an entry-rsp park in r13.
* **x86_64 Mach-O** — **no fixed parity at all.**

The third is the one worth remembering. MEASURED ON REAL HARDWARE
(ach, Darwin 22.6.0, x86_64): XNU does not 16-align the initial
rsp of a static Mach-O executable. It falls where the argv/env
string area leaves it, so the parity is a function of the
process's own NAME and ENVIRONMENT:

```
./_l            misaligned      ./_ltxx                 aligned
./_lt           misaligned      /tmp/csa2               aligned
PADVAR=x ./_lt  misaligned      PADVAR=xxxxxxx ./_lt    aligned
```

A 20-argv0-length x 16-env-pad sweep of a `#naked` `(rsp+8)&15`
probe splits 160/160, and it tracks the PARKED entry rsp
(`r15 & 15`) ROW FOR ROW — i.e. the misalignment at every call
site WAS the kernel's entry parity, carried through the landing
untouched. It is still 160/160 with the fix in: the kernel has
not changed, the landing absorbs it.

**Fix shape**: `and rsp, -16` (48 83 E4 F0) at the landing. It
rounds DOWN, so it is correct for either parity and free when the
entry was already aligned. AFTER the init-rsp park (`mov r15,
rsp`), never before — the park is what keeps argc/argv reachable
once rsp moves. And in BOTH x86 forks (`src/main.cyr`'s
`_TARGET_MACHO == 1` arm and `src/main_x86_macho.cyr`); only the
native fork is the compiler a Mac actually runs.

**Which targets may inherit, with the evidence:** Linux ELF only
(SysV amd64 3.4.1 guarantees rsp = 0 at `_start`; measured
320/320). aarch64 is safe architecturally (measured 320/320 on
ecb, anti-vacuous control 320/320 at 8). agnos builds rsp from
two 16-aligned terms in its own loader.

⛔ **HOW IT PRESENTS, AND WHY IT COST A ROUND.** It does NOT look
like an entry bug. Every row of the cross-OS alignment test
shifts together, so it reads as a scatter of unrelated shape
failures — capturing-closure callptr at odd depth, method
dispatch, operator-overload dispatch, a derive setter argument,
for-in, while — with nothing naming the entry. The fix for that
is a test row that reads the base EXPLICITLY (a global
initialised with the probe, so it is measured in the gvar-init
block right after the landing) asserted FIRST and labelled
ENTRY.

⛔ **AND A SINGLE RUN IS NOT A VERIFICATION ON DARWIN.** The
cross-OS lib-test runner always names the binary `./_lt`, i.e. it
samples exactly ONE parity. On this host that name is the
misaligned one and the gate went red; one byte longer and the
same broken compiler scores GREEN. Any entry-alignment claim on
Darwin needs the argv0/env sweep, not a re-run.

**What can be checked without the hardware** (and what should
have been): the cross-compiler emits Mach-O from Linux, so the
LANDING BYTES are readable on the dev box — `mov r15, rsp`
(49 89 E7) immediately followed by `and rsp, -16` — with an
anti-vacuous check that the ELF build does NOT carry the pair,
plus a source-level fork-parity check on both forks. Deleting the
helper from the NATIVE fork only leaves the byte axis green,
which is why the source axis is there too.

⭐ THE GENERALISATION, which was available two targets earlier
and was not drawn: **"the kernel aligns the entry" is a claim to
be measured on that kernel, not inferred from another platform's
ABI.** Three targets, three separate discoveries, one rule.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority"
description = "When a compiler computes a fact in two passes, the passes WILL disagree, and the failure is silent. cyrius stamped fn visibility in pass 1 AND recomputed it in pass 2, and both halves were wrong in different ways: pass 1 did not stamp every definition kind (so the check fell open), and pass 2 mis-armed the marker (masked only because the pass-1 bit was never cleared). The fix is one authority — stamp per DEFINITION in pass 1, look the definition up by its own token in pass 2, and take the stamp."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "frontend", "visibility", "two-pass", "private", "deprecated", "v6.6.5", "v6.6.16"]
content = """
cycc parses twice: pass 1 walks the token stream registering
declarations so forward references resolve, pass 2 emits. Fn
visibility (`private`) was computed in BOTH.

⛔ **PASS 1 DID NOT REGISTER EVERYTHING, and an unregistered
definition is fail-OPEN by construction.** `_vis_check` tests the
callee's private FLAG first and its owner second — an unstamped
callee has neither, so the check returned at its first test. Four
kinds were unstamped: impl methods (all seven `src/main*.cyr`
forks brace-skipped impl bodies), `mod`-scoped fns (a `GMOD == 0`
guard), every fn after the FIRST TOP-LEVEL STATEMENT (pass 1
stops there while `PARSE_PROG` keeps defining fns), and anything
above the `private` line — because the marker was recorded as
pass 1 WALKED PAST it, so a definition written earlier was
prescanned while the file still read public.

⭐ **THE REVEALING PART IS NOT THE FAIL-OPEN, IT IS THE MIRROR
IMAGE.** The filing said "only on an include order normal code
never produces". The same root produced, in ORDINARY order:

* **False refusals.** Pass 1 kept one entry per NAME, so a second
  file's same-named helper overwrote the first file's owner,
  arity and private bit before either body was parsed. Two
  private files each with a `_h` gave `'_h' is private to its
  file` AND `'_h' expects 2 arguments, got 1` on a legal program.
* **A public fn reported private.** The private bit was only ever
  SET, never cleared, so a public definition inherited a stale
  stamp — the language guide's own two-file example was REFUSED
  when the public file was included first.
* **Silent SIGSEGV.** No pass-1 signature means no parameter
  masks, so a forward call passing a >8-byte struct took the
  mask-0 ABI and pushed it BY VALUE into a callee that derefs the
  parameter as a pointer. rc=139, no diagnostic, x86 + aarch64 +
  PE.
* **Diagnostics naming the wrong symbol.** The method-call path
  registered an UNCOMMITTED scratch name (the mangler writes at
  `GNPOS` and deliberately does not advance it), so the next mint
  overwrote it and `q.nosuch()` was reported as
  `undefined function 'Q7_seven'` — a method that exists.

**The shape to remember: a stamping gap does not present as "the
check is too permissive". It presents as a scatter of unrelated
bugs — wrong ABI, wrong arity, wrong symbol name, wrong refusal —
because everything downstream reads the same empty record.**

**Fix shape, generalisable:**

1. Do the file-level marking ONCE, before either pass (a single
   walk at the end of LEX). Anything recorded DURING a walk makes
   the result depend on position in the file.
2. Register every definition kind in pass 1, per DEFINITION, not
   per name. The identity rule ("is this existing entry mine?")
   becomes one shared function both passes call, so they cannot
   drift.
3. Pass 2 finds the entry pass 1 gave THIS definition by its own
   `fn` token and takes the stamp — but only while nothing has
   claimed that entry yet, because two same-file definitions of
   one name legitimately share one entry and it holds ONE bit.
4. CLEAR the bit when a definition is public. A flag that is only
   ever set is a latch, and a latch across a shared entry is how
   a public fn becomes private.
5. Keep a fail-CLOSED-LATER backstop: a reference whose callee has
   no owner AND no body is DEFERRED and re-judged once everything
   is stamped. It reports nothing on legitimately-unknown callees
   (enum constructors, DCE stubs) and it is what catches the NEXT
   definition kind someone forgets to walk. Measured: with the
   relaxed-ordering prescan disabled, the deferral still refuses
   the call — but the program MISCOMPILES, because the ABI comes
   from the masks, not from the visibility check. A backstop is
   not a substitute for the stamp.

**Another instance (6.6.16, `#deprecated`).** The attribute was
recorded only in pass 2 (PARSE_FN_DEF moves the pending flag onto the
fn), so every call parsed BEFORE the definition — a helper above the
fn, anything in a file included earlier — read flag 4 as 0 and was
silent. Pass 1 now records it (`_tl_deprecated(S, 0)` ->
`_prescan_dep_take`, its OWN pending pair: reusing the pass-2 globals
would be the `_pub_pending` leak again).

⚠ **Recording in pass 1 means pass 1 must hand the attribute to the
SAME fn pass 2 does, and "the next fn" is not the same rule in both.**
Pass 2 gives it to the next PARSE_FN_DEF call. PARSE_PROG accepts a fn
inside a TOP-LEVEL block (`if (c) { fn a() {..} }`), so after
`#deprecated("x")` that nested `a` is the one marked — while pass 1
skips the whole statement and would have marked the next top-level
`fn b` instead: every call of `b` warning falsely. The tail scan now
drops the pending when a skipped statement holds a `fn`. A third
spelling still disagrees and is PASS 2's bug: a statement between the
attribute and its fn that instantiates a generic (`y = g<i32>(1);`)
hands the pending to the INSTANCE (`_instantiate_generic_fn` saves the
per-fn state, not the pending attributes) — `#must_use` is lost the
same way. When a fact moves to pass 1, write the pass-2 consumption
rule down and test each place the two walks differ: nested blocks,
re-parses, impl bodies, unnamed definitions.

**6.7.8 instances.** (a) REFUSING A TYPE POSITION WHOLE TAKES BOTH
PASSES AND THE REPLAY. Pass 1's parameter count (`_prescan_params_scan`)
stepped one token of `[u8]` and stopped counting at `u8`, so a call
above the definition met an arity error after the slice parameter's
refusal; the refused type is now consumed to its last token in pass 1
(`_param_type_step_q`), pass 2 (`_param_type_step`) and the zone replay
(`_gvi_ann`, or EMIT_GVAR_INITS reports `expected '='`). Give a refused
declaration the shape it claimed (a refused slice parameter's slot reads
as a slice) so its uses add nothing. (b) A PASS-1 RECORD CANNOT RESOLVE
WHAT IS DECLARED BELOW: a generic struct carrier (`fn gx<T>(b: Box<T>)`)
is prescanned before a `Box` declared under the fn is registered, so it
records G's NAME TOKEN as an offset from the `(` and looks G up at the
call. (c) A TOKEN-PASS READER RUNS BEFORE FN-LOCAL GENERIC INSTANCES
EXIST — read the declaration's `Name<..>` tokens, never the instance
table or the base struct (the pair scans followed the carrier into
nothing: rc 139). (d) A pass-2 declaration checker that reports through
a LATCHING reporter (ERR_EXPECT) swallows every later member's error —
move the cursor, report, clear the latch, put the cursor back
(`_tn_refuse`'s pattern) — and never re-check what another path parses
whole (an inherited trait default is PARSE_FN_DEF's), or one token
reports twice (6.7.8 trait signatures).

**6.7.14 instances.** (a) EVERY ATTRIBUTE A CALL SITE READS IS
RECORDED IN PASS ONE (FE-21). `#deprecated` (6.6.16), `const fn` (6.7.2)
and now `#io` / `#alloc`: a call site reads fn_flags, which pass 2
writes only in source order, so `#pure`'s check saw no flag on a callee
defined BELOW its caller. Arm a pass-1-only pending flag where pass 1
steps the attribute — in EVERY pass-1 walker (the top-level scan, an
impl body, the tail scan, a top-level block) — never the pass-2 one
(the `_pub_pending` cross-pass leak). (b) A FACT WITH THREE SOURCES
NEEDS ALL THREE IN PASS 1 (RC-15, RC-22). A fn is pair-returning by
`return Ctor(..)`, by `return pairfn(..)` and by a `?` on a pair call
(pass 2's `SFLG(.., | 256)`); with the third seen only in pass 2, a
caller ABOVE a fn whose only pair route is `?` saw a word — the verdict
depended on definition order (the 6.7.11 impl-method and 6.7.13
trait-default fixes were the same class). And a scan that books a `?`
must step a brace-less closure body (`|a| parse(a)?`):
`_skip_nested_body` stepped `{ }` only (RC-22, caught by the SECOND
oracle — `header_spellings_and_size.sh`, where `cyrius header`'s own pair
fixpoint and cycc must agree). (c) A ZONE GLOBAL'S TYPE MUST EXIST AT
THE END OF PASS 1 (RC-14): fn bodies compile (pass 2) before the
declaration zone's replay, so a body reading `G.a` of an untyped `var G
= mk1();` needs the type first — `_gu_reg` notes the declaration,
`_gu_sweep` types it from the tokens (`_gz_call_rs`) at the end of pass
1. A generic struct INSTANCE is not made yet there (`_gen_resolve_call`
runs in pass 2), so a `: G1<T>` callee binds nothing in the zone
(filed). (d) A READER SHARED BY PASS 1 MUST NOT REPORT WHAT PASS 2
KNOWS (GN-6, GN-11): `_gtype_sid` answers the base SILENTLY so a struct
declared below a fn still resolves, and the check sits at the pass-2
call sites only — a wrapper there (`_gtype_sid_def`), never a flag
inside the shared reader; a generic's return-type reader likewise stays
quiet in pass 1 and refuses nothing pass 2 can know, gated on
`_targs_resolvable` as `_ptr_step_peek` is (the `**` pin path still
reads in pass 1 — filed).

**6.7.15 instances (both filed halves above closed).** (a) A SECOND READER OF ONE RETURN (GN-8): the `**` pin read
(`_ek_pin` -> `_pp_psid` -> `_gen_ann_sid`) was the path (d) left open; one predicate (`_ret_ptr_unres`) now gates both
readers, so pass 1 leaves a `: **G<..>` return's pin to pass 2 while its type arguments do not resolve
(type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715 #4). (b) A ZONE GENERIC INSTANCE MINTED AT THE
SWEEP (RC-14): (c)'s `: G1<T>` callee binds in the zone now — the sweep reads the type arguments as `_gen_resolve_call`
/ `_gen_infer_tp` do and mints through pass 2's own `_instantiate_generic_struct`, whose name dedup hands pass 2's call
the same `G$..`; and the sweep's verdict and the replay must agree, looping walk and recheck to a fixpoint (RC-13:
receives_known_callee_records_assignment_judge_and_zone_sweep_v6715 #5, #7). (c) A PASS-1 RECORD KEYED ON FINDSTRUCT
IS RE-READ ONCE EVERY STRUCT IS REGISTERED (ST-1): CO-01's per-fn ABI facts were recorded as pass 1 met the fn, before
a struct declared below it (or below it by include ORDER) existed, and a forward call read that record (139, false
refusals). The re-read touches only a definition that is still its fn's record and names a struct registered after its
note, re-runs no one-time part, and is proven idempotent by re-reading EVERY definition byte-identically
(forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715 #1). A fact keyed on a TOKEN scan of
struct names is order-free by construction; one keyed on a registry lookup is only as current as the registry.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_cannot_pack_reason_deserves_the_same_scrutiny_as_the_bug"
description = "'It needs a design decision' is the cannot-pack reason that survives longest unexamined. A cyrius filing deferred a fix on the grounds that it required a name-pool design decision across seven compiler forks; the decision took one 18-line function, and the fork obligation was two one-line calls each. Cost the fix a release."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["process", "deferral", "compiler", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The filing said, in its own *Why it was not packed* section:

> deciding how the pass-1 mangle coexists with pass 2's — a design
> decision on the name pool, with seed-derive exposure. That is
> the "cannot pack" reason CLAUDE.md names.

It reads like diligence: the reason is named rather than left as
prose, which is what the rule asks for. But nobody had tried it.

**The answer was mechanical.** Mint the mangled name at `GNPOS`
WITHOUT committing; if an fn of exactly that name is already
registered, reuse ITS string and leave the pool untouched;
otherwise commit. Name lookup compares by STRING, so a string
minted in either pass serves both, and pool growth is unchanged
because the second mint is discarded. Eighteen lines, and it
removed a duplicated mangler rather than adding one.

The other two clauses were no better:

* **"seed-derive exposure"** — real, and the reason to RUN
  seed-derive, not a reason to defer. It was GREEN.
* **"a 7-fork obligation"** — two one-line calls into shared code
  per fork, guarded by a static parity axis in the new gate that
  derives the fork list with a glob.

⭐ **The test to apply: can you state the design decision as a
question with a small number of answers?** If yes, it is not a
design decision, it is a thing you have not tried. A genuine one
("what should the language DO here") has a user in it.

⚠ And the cost is not one release of delay. The defect stayed
open long enough that its severity line — "Medium, only on an
include order normal code never produces" — was quoted forward as
if it had been measured, while the same root was refusing
legitimate programs and miscompiling struct arguments in ordinary
include order the whole time.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "routing_a_new_caller_through_a_shared_helper_inherits_its_latent_bug"
description = "The fix for a call path with no ABI handling was 'route it through the shared helper the other paths use'. The helper had its own latent defect — it took a local's ADDRESS unconditionally, which is wrong for a pointer-mode struct — so the new caller traded a loud SIGSEGV for a silent wrong value. Test the helper against the shape the new caller brings, not just against the bug you are fixing."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "abi", "struct", "refactor", "overload", "str", "closures", "v6.6.5", "v6.6.16"]
content = """
cyrius's method-call path (`s.m(arg)`) pushed every argument as a
bare value, with no reference to the callee's parameter masks. A
`>8`-byte struct argument therefore arrived BY VALUE at a callee
that derefs the parameter as a pointer: SIGSEGV. The obvious fix,
and the one written in the plan, was "resolve the callee first and
route each argument through `_try_push_struct_addr_arg`" — the
helper `PARSE_FNCALL` and four other paths already use.

⛔ **That helper pushed `&slot` UNCONDITIONALLY.** A struct-typed
local in cyrius is one of two things:

* **inline** — the slot IS the struct, so `&slot` is its address;
* **pointer-mode** — the slot HOLDS the struct's heap address, so
  the callee wants the slot's VALUE.

Taking the address of a pointer-mode slot hands the callee a
pointer-to-pointer, and it reads the address itself as field 0.
Measured on the unfixed compiler with `var b: HB = alloc(24);
b.a = 10; …; f(0, b)`: it printed **140545185808389** where 42 is
right. No crash, no diagnostic. So the method path would have gone
from *SIGSEGV on inline / correct on heap* to *correct on inline /
silent garbage on heap* — strictly worse in the dimension that
matters, since a wrong number travels further than a fault.

⭐ **WHY IT SURVIVED:** the defect is invisible from the helper's
existing callers. They were all reached through code paths whose
own tests used inline structs, and the ONE in-tree corpus (316
tests, 131 programs, 408 raw-compilable ecosystem sources) contains
zero instances of the shape — because it had never worked, so
nobody wrote it. A shared helper's blast radius is measured over
what people DO call it with, which is exactly the set that avoids
its bugs.

**What to do instead of routing-and-hoping:**

1. Write the new caller's shape as a probe FIRST, run it against
   the helper as-is, and read the value — not the exit code. This
   one exits 0.
2. Check the helper against the OTHER polarity too. The rule here
   already existed twice in the same compiler (field access and a
   method's `self` both discriminate on a -1 slot marker plus a
   `<= 8 B` single-slot rule); argument passing was the third user
   and the only one that had never asked.
3. Fix the HELPER, then measure the blast radius on both sides. In
   this case the byte-identity differential over four backends and
   the ecosystem was zero, which is what says the change is a
   strict improvement rather than a behavioural swap.

⚠ And exempt what the rule does not describe. A value-form SIMD
local is always inline and its type tag lives in a descriptor band
where the struct-size table has no entry, so it must SKIP the test
rather than answer it wrong — `_is_simd_any` first, then the rule.

⭐ **SECOND INSTANCE (6.6.16, the overload dispatcher).** `PARSE_FNCALL`'s
dispatcher typed its first argument by hand and never routed a Str
global or field to `<base>_str`. The 6.6.13 I11 warning already had a
correct-looking classifier for exactly that question (`_str_arg_kind`),
so the planned fix was a one-line swap: route when the classifier
answers "a Str". It passed a fixpoint, seed-derive and a 0-diff corpus
differential.

⛔ **The classifier was written for a WARNING, and it typed a name the
way no codegen path reads it.** Inside a closure body a captured
enclosing local is not in the local table, so the classifier fell
through to the GLOBAL of the same name. For a warning that is a false
positive; for a dispatcher it is a miscompile: with a `var s: Str`
global, `fn f() { var s = "x"; var c = |k| show(s) + k; .. }` reached
`show_str`, which read the cstr's bytes as a {data, len} header. The
old dispatcher never routed globals at all, so the swap would have
CREATED a memory-unsafe path while fixing the filed one. No corpus
file has the shape — the same blind spot as the first instance. The
fix was in the helper, in codegen's order: a single name is typed the
way PARSE_FACTOR reads it (local, capture, global — `_arg_name_sid`),
the base of `x.f` the way PARSE_FIELD_LOAD does (`_recv_sid`).

⛔ **The same swap also LOST a shape.** The old hand-typing routed
`println(s.cat(t))` by accident — `s` was a Str, it never asked what
`cat` returns — and the swap dropped it, so that call printed the
header's pointer bytes; the corpus had no such call, review caught it.
The classifier now types a call or method RESULT by its own declared
return (`x.m(..)`, `f(..).m(..)`, `f(..).f`, `g<T>(..)`), which also
fixes the accident's other half: `s.len()` had been routed to `_str` by
the same receiver rule. Gate: tests/tcyr/lang/overload_str_dispatch.tcyr
(its capture rows are red on the one-line swap).

**Generalisation:** when a decision moves from a diagnostic to codegen,
the diagnostic's false positives become miscompiles and its blind spots
become regressions. Re-read the helper against every name-resolution
rung the codegen path has — locals, captures, globals, shadowing each
way — and list every shape the OLD code answered, including the ones it
answered right by luck, and test each before reusing the helper.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_call_syntax_is_not_one_call_path_enumerate_the_masks"
description = "cyrius has FOUR argument-marshalling paths for what a reader sees as one call: PARSE_FNCALL, the method-dot path, the tail-call path and the PE SIMD-return path. Each consults the callee's parameter masks independently, so a mask honoured by one and not the others is a silent wrong value that differs between two spellings of the same call in the same program. Enumerate the masks, not the bug."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "abi", "call-paths", "tail-call", "simd", "str", "deprecated", "visibility", "v6.6.5", "v6.6.16"]
content = """
A cyrius callee records what each parameter wants in per-fn bitmask
tables: `_fnt_structmask` (>8-byte struct, pass its address),
`_fnt_strmask` (`: Str`, wrap a string literal in `str_from`),
`_fnt_simdmask` (vector, load into XMM/V *after* the integer args are
popped, never push it), `_fnt_cstrmask`, `_fnt_ovstr` / `_fnt_ovint`
(overload dispatch).

`PARSE_FNCALL` reads all of them. **Three other paths marshal
arguments too**, and each one reads whatever subset its author needed
that day:

* the method path `s.m(a)` (`parse_decl.cyr`) — read NONE until 6.6.5,
  and had no arity check either: `q.one(5, 99)` on a 2-parameter method
  BUILT and returned 6 where the identical free call is a hard error;
* the tail-call path `return f(a);` (`parse_fn.cyr`) — emits its own
  epilogue + `jmp`, so it never enters PARSE_FNCALL at all;
* the Win64 hidden-retptr vector RECEIVES — and that is not one path but
  FOUR loops (`_try_vector_call_assign`, the `_f2c` / `_f4c` receives, the
  PE SIMD-return `_rc` path), each `_TARGET_PE == 1` only. ⚠ They are
  invisible to every x86-Linux run: the same source gave 5 on ELF and 0 on
  PE, under wine, on two consecutive cuts of the same fix.

⚠ **COUNT THE LOOPS, NOT THE PATHS.** "Four paths" is how this was written
down after 6.6.5 round 1 and it was already an undercount — `grep -n
'_try_push_struct_addr_arg(' src/frontend/` lists the argument loops, and
there were seven.

⭐ **THE TELL IS TWO SPELLINGS DISAGREEING IN ONE PROGRAM.** Not a
crash, not a diagnostic — two lines that should be the same value:

    var r = f("abcde");    # 5
    return f("abcde");     # 0   -- no str_from wrap on the tail path

    h.vmix(2, v, 3)        # 927 -- vector pushed as an int arg, so
    free_vmix(0, 2, v, 3)  # 923    every LATER argument shifts a register

The tail path's history is the whole lesson: v6.3.36 added a divert
for plain-struct params, v6.4.53 for value-form SIMD params, v6.5.1
for overload dispatch, v6.5.2 for the integer-literal-to-cstring
error, and 6.6.5 for the `: Str` literal wrap. **Five separate
discoveries of the same structural gap**, each found by a user hitting
one spelling.

**What to do when you touch any of these paths:**

1. List the mask tables from their definition site, not from the path
   you are editing. `grep '_fnt_[a-z]*mask' src/frontend/` is the
   enumeration.
2. For each, write the two-spelling probe (`var r = f(x)` against
   `return f(x)`, free fn against `obj.m(...)`) and compare the
   VALUES.
3. Prefer EXTRACTING PARSE_FNCALL's arm into a shared helper over
   copying it. 6.6.5 lifted `_try_push_str_literal_arg`,
   `_simd_arg_record` and `_simd_arg_second_pass` out precisely so the
   method path cannot drift from the free-fn path again.
4. A divert (`tc_has_addr = 1`, forcing the general path) is simpler
   than teaching the special path a new mask, and it is what the four
   previous fixes did — **but its PREDICATE must match PARSE_FNCALL's
   exactly, and "over-covering is the safe direction" is FALSE here.**
   6.6.5's first cut armed the `: Str` divert on a literal at ANY paren
   depth and wrote "over-diverting costs a tail call" in the comment.
   It does not: losing TCO on a SELF-RECURSIVE tail call turns bounded
   recursion into unbounded stack growth, and a correct program that ran
   on 6.6.4 started SIGSEGVing. Read the predicate off the helper you
   are diverting TO — `_try_push_str_literal_arg` opens
   `if (PEEKT(S) != 30) return 0`, i.e. an argument's FIRST token, i.e.
   paren depth 1 — and gate on that. A predicate copied from the general
   path cannot under-cover by construction, and does not over-cover.

⚠ **AND THE CONTROL CAN BE THE BROKEN ONE.** The 6.6.5 tail-call
defect was found because a new test asserted `obj.m("lit")` equalled
the identical free fn — and the free fn had been written as
`return f(...)`, so it was wrong too. Both sides agreed at a wrong
value until a third expectation (the literal's length, computed in the
test) was added. Compute the expected value a DIFFERENT way from the
actual one, and make sure "different way" is not just a different
spelling of the same path.

⛔ **6.6.6: THE ENUMERATION GREP ITSELF UNDERCOUNTED — IT FINDS ONLY LOOPS
THAT ALREADY CALL A GATE.** `grep '_try_push_struct_addr_arg('` lists the
loops that run *that* gate; a loop that runs NO gate is invisible to it.
PARSE_VAR's two struct-valued receives (`var p: S = f(..)` — the >16 B
retptr `asv` loop and the 9-16 B `asv_pair` loop) were bare
`PCMPE + EPUSHR` per argument and so never showed up: a vector argument
shifted every later int, a struct argument went by value (SIGSEGV), a
`: Str` literal was not wrapped, and the arity check never ran. Enumerate
by the thing EVERY call must emit — `grep -n 'ECALLPOPS(' src/frontend/`
— and then ask each hit which gates it runs. 6.6.6 moved the per-argument
gates into ONE helper, `_call_arg_one`, which the method loop and both
receives now share.

⭐ **A HIDDEN ARGUMENT IS PART OF THE CALL TOO.** The same bite found that
only `var p: S = f(..)` supplied the >16 B struct's hidden RETPTR; an
expression statement, an argument, `x = f(..)` and `return f(..)` (the
TAIL path) marshalled the user args as if there were none, and the callee
wrote its result through argument 1. The matrix to walk is (call syntax) x
(return class: scalar / rax:rdx pair / retptr / vector), not just
(call syntax) x (parameter mask) — and top level, where there is no frame
to give the result, is a syntax too (v6.6.6).

⛔ **AND "CALL SYNTAX" HAS MORE ROWS THAN THE FIX'S DESTINATIONS COULD SEE.**
That first cut gave the retptr a destination at every site that could
RECOGNISE a struct call by its tokens — `IDENT (`, the callee named by
the next token. Two call syntaxes never look like that: a METHOD call
`b.mk(2)` (the callee is resolved from `b`'s TYPE) and an OVERLOADED
OPERATOR `a + b` (the callee `V3_add` is never written at all). Both emit
their own call, so `var r: P3 = b.mk(2)`, `r = b.mk(5)`, `h(b.mk(1))` and
`var c: V3 = a + b` still SIGSEGV'd after the fix, while the identical
`B_mk(b, 2)` worked — and the guide the same commit shipped said the call
"may be written anywhere". The fix that scales is the reverse direction:
the EMITTER (which knows its callee) allocates the temp, passes the retptr
and RECORDS the token span it covered; a destination arms the token it is
about to parse and takes the temp only when the record spans EXACTLY its
expression (`_sc_pre` / `_sc_post` / `_sc_whole`, v6.6.6). Begin AND end
must match — `k + a * b` ends where `a * b` ends. When a fix keys on the
SHAPE of source text, list every syntax that reaches the same codegen and
ask which ones have that shape.

⭐ **SAME CLASS, A DIAGNOSTIC THIS TIME (6.6.16).** The per-callee CHECKS
drift exactly like the masks do. `_vis_check` (`private`) had 14 call
sites after the v6.6.4 sweep; `_DEPRECATED_WARN` had 3. So `#deprecated`
was silent through `&old_f`, `o.m()`, both struct receives, both operator
dispatches and the four PE-only vector paths — every path the visibility
sweep had found and the deprecation warning never heard about. Two lists
of "the call paths", maintained separately, are two lists that disagree.
The fix is the extraction rule applied to the checks:
`_callee_site_checks(S, fi, noff, ti)` runs both, and every site calls IT
(`grep -n '_vis_check(' src/frontend/` now finds only the helper). Two
traps met on the way:
* **Name the call's own token, not the cursor.** The tail path warns after
  it has consumed `);`, so `_err_head(S)` printed the NEXT statement's line
  (`return old_f(x);` on 14 reported as 15:1); `&f` has consumed the name.
  Each path passes the token of ITS call (`_callee_site_checks_here(S, fi,
  noff, back)` for the cursor-relative ones — one call, so PARSE_VAR /
  _PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL grew no references under cybs's ceiling).
* **"Exactly once" needs a definition of a site.** The same tokens are
  PARSED more than once — an `#inline` body per expansion, a generic body
  per instance, a vector-returning fn's `return f(..)` twice on x86,
  aarch64 and cx — and each re-parse warned again. The (token, fn) pair is
  the site; it must be a token unique to the call (operator dispatch named
  its left operand's first token, which chained dispatches share, until it
  named its operator).

⭐ **AND THE THIRD SPELLING SET (6.6.16): a `: stack` pair callee.** Every
asker of "does this call return a pair" (`?`'s lowering, the lossy-bind
refusals, `return` propagation, the destructure, the forward prescan)
tested for `IDENT (` only, so `g<i32>(..)?` and `p.div(d)?` took the BOXED
lowering and read the tag as a pointer (rc 139). One resolver now answers
for `name(`, `name<..>(` and `x.m(`
(field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml `pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aggregate_layout_is_recorded_never_inferred_from_a_neighbour_slot"
description = "cycc guessed whether an aggregate LOCAL was inline (the slot IS the struct) or pointer-mode (the slot HOLDS its address) from `is the slot below named -1?`. That marker is not a discriminator: SCOPE_POP writes -1 over every local of a CLOSED BLOCK, and callptr / bitset / SIMD-reserve / closure-env temporaries are registered -1 by construction. So any pointer-mode struct local declared after a closed block or after one of those temporaries read its fields OFF ITS OWN FRAME SLOT — silent since 5.8.17, live in a shipped consumer (stiva let a 512 MB node pass a 1024 MB constraint). Fixed v6.6.5 by RECORDING the slot span at registration (SLAGG/GLSPAN, high 32 bits of the existing per-slot depth word) and giving every consumer ONE reader, `_local_struct_is_ptr`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "layout", "struct-locals", "scope-pop", "silent-miscompile", "discriminator", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The class, stated generally: a property that is DECIDED at one place and RE-DERIVED at
another will disagree, and the re-derivation is the one that ships wrong.** Five separate
consumers each asked "is this local inline or a pointer?" by reading a NEIGHBOURING slot's
name, and the writer of that name (`SCOPE_POP`, util.cyr) had no idea anyone was reading it:

```cyrius
fn f(c) {
    if (c == 1) { var t = 5; }   # SCOPE_POP renames `t`'s slot to -1 when the block closes
    var p: P = mk();             # ...so `p` "looks inline". It is a POINTER.
    return p.y;                  # 6.6.4: 6. Correct: 22.
}
```

The same guess is defeated by `callptr` (181 for 22), by `bitset` temporaries, by SIMD
reserves and by closure-env slots — all of which legitimately register name -1.

**How to recognise it again.** A predicate whose INPUT is a side effect of unrelated code is
not a predicate. Ask: *who writes the thing I am reading, and do they know I read it?* If the
answer is "several places, for their own reasons", the property has to be RECORDED at the
point where it is actually known — here, at the variable's registration.

**The cheap place to put a recorded bit.** Depths are small non-negative counters, so the
per-slot depth word had 32 unused high bits. Putting the span there means no new table, no
heap-map change, and it CLEARS ITSELF on slot reuse because every named registration writes
`SLDEP` before anything else. A new side table would have needed a per-fork base
initialisation; the high-bits trick needed none.

**Companion trap in the same release:** the aggregate's WIDTH, not just a flag, is what the
coroutine emitters need. A stack frame's slot addresses DESCEND with the index; a coroutine's
heap frame ASCENDS. So `&agg + k*8` walks off a multi-word aggregate in a coroutine, and
`EFLADDR` can only recover a SINGLE-slot local there. Storing the span rather than one bit is
what let that be answered exactly instead of guessed again.

**6.7.8 — a per-slot side table is trusted only behind a bit the registration rewrites.** The
slice-width table (positive = a slice local, since v5.8.14) is reused per fn and per closure, and a
fn / closure / SIMD parameter's registration never clears it — so an untyped parameter in a slot an
EARLIER fn's slice had used was a "slice" to every reader (`y[0]` a slice read of `&y`, `y.len` a
frame read, `y += 1` refused). The fix is a bit, not "clear the width at every registration" (~50
sites, and the next new one forgets): `SLSLICE` writes the width AND a depth-word bit, `_slice_lw`
reads the width only behind it, and the bit dies at the next `SLDEP`. dyn's declared-scalar table
(`_dsc_tn[slot]`, the annotation a dyn formed from a scalar names) got the same validity bit (`SLDSC`).
⚠ **The high bits are a shared, finite resource:** the two 6.7.8 lanes, planned apart, both took bit
62 — merged as they stood, every declared scalar would have read as a slice. Before taking a "free"
bit, grep the accessors AND every whole-word reader (a signed compare of the depth word sees bit 63)
across the lanes in flight. **A value can ride an existing word in a BAND no other writer produces**,
read only behind a bit its readers already test: an array's exact N sits in its named slot's
slice-width word as `0 - 2^48 - N` (below every pointer mark `0 - 0x7E00 - (ps | ed << 40)` and the
signed-narrow -1 / -2 / -4), trusted only behind the array descriptor; a global's N sits in its sign
word's bits 16-47 — and that word is also a REDECLARATION SHAPE KEY, so the key must mask the new bits
or `u8[5]` / `u8[6]` (one 8-byte storage) stop folding. ⚠ The slice width is sizeof(T) and a struct
element passes 255: never byte-mask it — the slice's element kind went above bit 31 (`w | ed << 32`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "compiler_generated_temporaries_must_not_be_globals_or_named_zero"
description = "`switch`, `match`, both `?` lowerings and `for ... in` each stashed their subject in a GLOBAL slot registered under NAME OFFSET 0. Three silent consequences: recursion reloaded the outer call's value, threads shared one word, and name offset 0 is the FIRST LEXED WORD of the program — so FINDVAR (a flat last-match table) RETURNED those slots for that identifier and `&setup` changed value after an intervening fn containing a switch. Frame slots named 0 aliased the same way through FINDLOCAL, which matches any `sn >= 0`. Fixed v6.6.5: `_HTEMP`/`_HTSTORE`/`_HTLOAD` — an anonymous FRAME slot inside a fn, a dead-marked global at top level."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "lowering", "hidden-temporaries", "findvar", "recursion", "threads", "name-offset-0", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**It was a HABIT, not a lowering.** Five constructs each open-coded the same four lines —
`GVCNT`, two `S64` writes, `SVCNT` — to get one scratch word, and each passed name offset 0
because "it has no name". Copy-paste made the defect five times over, which is why the repair
shipped with a CENSUS gate (`tests/gates/codegen/hidden_temp_census.sh`) rather than only a
behaviour test: the behaviour test pins the five that exist, the census pins the SHAPE so the
sixth is caught at the source.

**Name offset 0 is not "no name".** The lexer resets NPOS to 0, so offset 0 is the first
identifier the program lexes. `FINDVAR` walks names backward with no scope or owner filter, so
a hidden temporary was a perfectly good match for it. `FINDLOCAL` is worse: it accepts any
stored name `>= 0`, so a FRAME slot named 0 aliases the same identifier — which is how the
slice/u128 high half, the `secret` flag and the `defer` flag each became reachable. The
anonymous convention in this tree is `0 - 1`, and it has to be written that way everywhere.

**The recursion trap when you go to TEST it.** A recursive fn that finishes its loop BEFORE
recursing passes on the broken compiler — the shared bound has already done its job. The
recursive call has to be INSIDE the loop body, and a `match` has to be re-entered from an arm
PATTERN (a call in the pattern position), i.e. after the subject is stashed and before it is
compared. A first cut of the gate asserted the obvious shape and went green on 6.6.4.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_fn_local_static_array_must_not_take_a_global_name"
description = "An array local over the per-fn frame budget (~120 KB), or any array under CYRIUS_STACK_ARRAYS=0, falls back to shared STATIC storage — and was registered with an ordinary GLOBAL NAME, so it captured every later reference to that identifier anywhere in the program. It was also invisible to FINDLOCAL, so an outer LOCAL of the same name won inside its own fn. v6.6.5 keeps the storage static (raising the budget is a heap-layout change) and scopes only the NAME, and turns the `note:` into a WARNING that says the storage is shared across calls and threads."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "arrays", "storage-class", "stack-arrays", "secret", "scoping", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**Two separable questions, and conflating them is what made this hard to fix for two years:**
*where does the memory live* (a heap-layout decision, expensive) and *what does the name mean*
(a scoping decision, cheap). The filing framed it as one. Only the second had to move.

**The `secret` half is the sharper lesson.** `secret var buf[N]` decided local-vs-static from
the stack-arrays FLAG rather than from where the array ACTUALLY went, so an over-budget
`secret var` was refused outright — and, with an outer local of the same name in scope,
`FINDLOCAL` found the wrong thing, the zeroise ran against the wrong storage, and the buffer
was never cleared. A security feature that silently does nothing is worse than one that errors.
Ask the SLOT-COUNT DELTA (`GFLC` before vs after), which is the fact, not the intention.

**The diagnostic class matters.** `note:` reads as informational; the consequence here is
semantic — ONE buffer for every call and every thread — so it is a `warning:` that says so.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_variable_two_frames_the_half_fixed_address_of"
description = "6.6.5. A coroutine's locals live in a heap frame, not the stack. `EFLADDR`/`EFLADDR_X1` (field access, method self) were taught that frame; `ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` (the `&name` path) was not — so `q.v = 11` stored to `r11+0x28` and `peek(&q)` read `rbp-0x40`, and the variable silently answered 0. The un-fixed half had a recovery written for it at v6.5.70 that HAD NEVER ONCE FIRED, because it was guarded on `_cur_fn_regalloc == 0` and an async fn body reserves the five callee-saved slots like any other fn."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["coroutines", "async", "codegen", "x86", "regalloc", "address-of", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The general shape: when one VARIABLE is reachable through two emitters, fixing one of them
is worse than fixing neither.** Before the fix both halves addressed the stack, which was
wrong but CONSISTENT — and it read as correct for years because a resumed coroutine frame
usually lands back on the same bytes. The moment one half learned the heap frame, the
variable split down the middle and the answer went from accidentally-right to silently 0.
Grep for every emitter that can produce an address for the same storage BEFORE changing one.

**The dead guard is the second lesson.** v6.5.70's recovery read:

    if (_cur_fn_regalloc == 0) { if (_cur_fn_ret_stash == 0) {
        var _ai = (0 - adisp) / 8 - 1;

`_cur_fn_regalloc` is 5 in every coroutine body, so the branch was unreachable — and the
displacement inside it did not subtract the shifts either, so it would have computed the
wrong slot if it had run. A comment above it asserted "both of which are ZERO in a coroutine".
**A guard nobody can reach is not conservative, it is untested code that will one day run.**
Subtract the shifts instead: `(0 - adisp - _cur_fn_regalloc * 8 - _cur_fn_ret_stash) / 8 - 1`.

**A multi-word aggregate needs its LOW word, and refusing it was a capability regression.**
The stack descends with slot index and a coroutine's heap frame ASCENDS, so basing a struct
at its NAMED slot puts word k on a different local. The first cut of the fix made that a hard
error — but `var q: P2;` in a suspending fn, and even a struct used only BEFORE the first
suspend, compiled on the previous release and returned the right answer, so the diagnostic
took working code away. Base at the aggregate's LOWEST-INDEX slot instead: the block owns the
same `n` contiguous words in both frames, and the internal word ORDER being reversed is
invisible because every consumer either addresses the block through that one base or walks
BOTH sides by slot index (a permutation applied identically to source and destination copies
the same word to the same word).

**A diagnostic is the right answer only for a gap that cannot be closed.** Check which of the
refused shapes compile on the PREVIOUS release before shipping a refusal.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "tightening_one_side_of_a_symmetric_operation_creates_an_asymmetry"
description = "6.6.5. `_try_aggregate_copy_assign` opened `if (GINFN(S) != 1) return 0;` and then FINDLOCAL'd both sides, so `B = A` between two inline struct GLOBALS copied EIGHT BYTES while the identical assignment between two locals copied the whole struct. The local half had just been tightened onto the recorded layout; the global half had never been reachable at all."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "codegen", "globals", "aggregates", "struct-copy", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**When a fix tightens one operand class of a symmetric operation, enumerate the other classes
in the same change.** `dst = src` has four arms — local←local, local←global, global←local,
global←global — and only the first was implemented. Fixing it made the SAME source line answer
differently depending on where the two variables lived, which is worse than both being wrong,
because the wrong one is now the one nobody is looking at.

**A global slot index is NOT a word index.** `SVCNT` advances by ONE per variable while
`_vars_base` holds its BYTE size, so `EVLOAD(gvi + 1)` reads the NEXT VARIABLE. Every other
global-aggregate consumer goes through `EVADDR(gvi) + byte offset` for exactly that reason,
and the first cut of this fix did not — it produced 0 for a two-word copy and looked like a
different bug entirely.

**Globals are packed by EXACT byte size** (`x86/fixup.cyr` sums `_vars_base` with no round-up
below 8), so a 12-byte struct global is followed at +12. A word-granular copy of
`ceil(sz/8)` words clobbers 4 bytes of its NEIGHBOUR — the same overrun v5.9.36 fixed for the
literal's own field stores. Copy the tail at 4/2/1. The gate row that catches this is a
neighbouring global asserted to still hold its own value, not the copy's own fields.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "metadata_recorded_after_the_code_that_reads_it_splits_one_variable_in_two"
description = "6.6.5. `_STRUCT_LIT_LOCAL` recorded the aggregate's slot span (SLAGG) at the BOTTOM of the fn, after the zero-fill and every initialiser store was already emitted. On the stack that is invisible; inside a coroutine the emitters consult the span to find the block's base, so the literal's own stores based one place and every later read based another — and the stores ran past the block into the next local."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "codegen", "coroutine", "aggregates", "struct-literal", "registration-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**Record a fact about a slot BEFORE emitting any code that consults it.** The registration
order here was: reserve the slots, emit the zero-fill, parse and emit the initialisers, THEN
name the slot and record its span. Naming last is deliberate and correct (`var cfg = P { cfg, 2 };`
must see the OUTER `cfg`) — but the SPAN is not the name, nothing resolves through it, and
recording it late made the emitters answer two different questions about the same variable:
the stores went to `EFLADDR_X1(named)` with span 0 and the reads to the same call with span
`n`, which in a heap coroutine frame is a different base. `var p = P2{5,6}` across a suspend
read 5 where the previous release read 56, silently.

**A typed declaration and a literal are DIFFERENT registration paths.** `var p: P2;` records
its span at registration and was correct throughout, so a gate written only against the typed
form passes over a broken literal. When a feature has two spellings that reach the same
emitters by different routes, the gate needs a row for each — the first cut's axis had typed
rows only and read GREEN.

**The visible half is the overrun, not the wrong value.** A block based `n-1` slots off writes
past itself over whatever local follows, so the cheapest gate row is a `guard` local declared
after the aggregate and asserted to still hold its seed — it catches the neighbour damage even
when the aggregate's own fields happen to read back plausibly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "moving_a_symbol_to_a_new_resolution_rung_drops_whatever_the_old_rung_set"
description = "6.6.5. Making `var a = Num{1}` a frame local moved it off the global rung and onto the closure-CAPTURE rung, which emitted the right value but never called SESTYPE — so `|x| a + x` compiled to an integer add where the same source had dispatched `Num_add`, and with the operator fn DELETED it compiled clean where the previous release refused the program."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "closures", "operator-overloading", "capture", "storage-class", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**A storage-class change is a RESOLUTION change, and every rung sets different side data.**
`PARSE_TERM`'s identifier ladder has four rungs — closure local, closure capture, enclosing
local, global — and each one emits a value AND records expression metadata (`SESTYPE`,
`SESVAR`, `SEXW`, `SPSC`). Only three of them set the struct type. Moving a declaration from
global storage to a frame slot silently re-routed every read of it to the rung that did not.

**The tell is not the wrong number, it is the LOST DIAGNOSTIC.** `3` instead of `102` looks
like arithmetic. Delete the operator fn and compare: the old compiler refuses the program
(`reachable undefined function`), the new one compiles it. A fix that turns a compile error
into a silent answer is a capability regression however right the number would have been.

**Read the capture metadata from the SNAPSHOT, not the live tables.** By the time a closure
BODY is parsed, the shared per-fn slot tables hold the closure's own locals, so slot index
`esl` no longer means the enclosing variable. `_cl_snap_span` / `_cl_snap_type` read the
saved copy; anything reading `GLSPAN(esl)` there is reading a different variable.

**When you add an encoding band, check every decoder of the old one.** The capture operand is
encoded `-1000000 - capi` and a local is `-2 - li`; a capture is therefore ALSO `<= -2`, so
`_OP_OPERAND_ADDR`'s local arm silently accepted it as a nonsense slot index until the capture
band was tested first.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_warning_raised_after_its_subject_was_consumed_names_the_next_statement"
description = "6.6.5. The over-budget array-local warning fires once `]` and `;` are consumed, and WARN reads the parse CURSOR — so `var bigbuf[200000];` on line 3 printed `:4:5:` with the excerpt and caret under the following statement, while the release note told downstreams the warning carries a file:line for the declaration."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["diagnostics", "parser", "warnings", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`WARN` / `ERR` / `_err_excerpt` all read `GTI(S)`. That is right for a parse error — the cursor
IS the problem — and wrong for anything raised after the parser walked past its subject. The
repo already had the fix pattern twice (`_err_head_at`, and `_priv_per_item_err`'s save-cursor /
`STI` / restore), so the rule is: **a diagnostic about a construct saves that construct's token
index when it enters and reports against it**, rather than growing a second excerpt path.

**Pin the LINE, not just the text.** A gate that greps the message body passes over a warning
pointing at the wrong statement. Put the declaration in a probe and derive the expected line by
grepping the probe file — never by writing the number in the gate.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_generated_tables_two_halves_drawn_from_one_source_cannot_intersect"
description = "6.6.5. The raw-syscall diagnostic's better clause — 'on ELF-aarch64 that number is `ptrace`' — was DEAD CODE for 13 minors. Both halves of the generated table were derived from the same file, and the emit predicate for one half was the exact complement of the other, so the two lookups could never both hit. 43 numbers warned; 0 ever carried the text."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["diagnostics", "codegen", "generated-code", "aarch64", "syscalls", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`src/common/syscall_xlat.cyr` is generated from the two stdlib syscall peers. `_SYSX_MEANT(n)`
answers "what x86 call is n", and `_SYSX_ACTUALLY(n)` answers "what does n do on aarch64". The
generator emitted a `_SYSX_MEANT` row only when `n` is **not** a value the aarch64 peer declares,
and built `_SYSX_ACTUALLY` from **exactly those declared values**. Two sets, one complement of
the other. `parse_expr.cyr` then asks both, and the second could not answer for anything the
first admitted — so the clause that names the call the number really invokes never printed once
between v6.5.51 and v6.6.4.

⭐ **THE TELL IS THE PREDICATE, NOT THE OUTPUT.** Nothing looked wrong: the table was large, every
row plausible, the warnings well-formed. It surfaced only from compiling a probe containing all
43 rows and grepping for the second clause — 43 warnings, 0 matches. **When a generator emits two
lookups that a consumer chains, compile a probe with every key and count how many reach the
second one.** A row count proves the table exists; it does not prove any row is reachable.

**The fix is a DIFFERENT SOURCE for the second half.** The peer cannot name calls the stdlib has
no wrapper for, which is most of the interesting ones. `_SYSX_ACTUALLY` is generated from the
committed kernel table (`tests/data/syscalls/aarch64.tbl`) now, restricted to the `_SYSX_MEANT`
keys so every row is reachable by construction: 35 of 43 carry the text, the other 8 are
genuinely unassigned on aarch64 and the message says so. The table SHRANK by 47 lines, because
~82 of its rows had been unreachable.

⚠ **A generator that reads an external fact must HARD FAIL when the fact is missing**, never emit
an empty table: an empty table compiles, links, answers 0 for everything, and re-derives cleanly
against itself.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_arch_guarded_native_syscall_number_is_a_shadow_anywhere_not_just_in_the_peers"
description = "6.6.5. ESYSXLAT's shadow class (a compat row stealing a NATIVE aarch64 number) had three gates watching it, all reading the same two stdlib peer files. `lib/yukti.cyr` declared `SYS_STATFS = 43` under `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` and the `43 -> 202` socket row made every call `accept()`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "syscalls", "gates", "vendored-stdlib", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The class was found twice before (v6.5.36, v6.5.37) and each time the sweep that found it was
written against `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr`. But the defect is a property of an
**arch-guarded syscall NUMBER**, not of that file — and vendored sibling stdlibs declare their
own. Measured under `qemu-aarch64 -strace`: `yukti_filesystem_usage` issues
`accept(6293618, 0x7f254c000000, [0]) = -1 errno=14` and reports "statfs failed: errno 14" on
every ARM host. Older than the release that found it.

⭐ **WHY THE OTHER FOUR SYSCALL GATES COULD NOT SEE IT, and this is the generalisable part.**
`raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh` EXEMPTS arch-guarded literals **by construction** — writing the
native number under a guard is the supported spelling, so it cannot flag them. The other three
parse the two peer files. Every gate was doing its job; the union of their scopes had a hole
shaped exactly like a vendored fold.

**The discriminator has to come from outside the repo.** "Is this guarded value a row source?" is
not the question — the peers deliberately declare x86 numbers the chain renumbers
(`SYS_TRUNCATE = 76`, routed `76 -> 45`). The question is whether the row's DESTINATION really is
that call on aarch64, answered against a committed kernel table. That separates yukti's
`43 -> 202 = accept` (wrong) from the peers' `76 -> 45 = truncate` (right).

⚠ **A sweep whose real corpus is legitimately tiny needs a POSITIVE CONTROL, not a floor.** Six
guarded declarations exist across 658 in-tree files, so "at least N found" means nothing. The gate
runs its scanner over a fixed synthetic buffer every time and requires it to flag exactly the
known-bad lines. That control caught the first cut anchoring its regex at line start, which a
one-line `enum M { SYS_PPOLL = 73; }` walked straight through.

⛔ **CORRECTION (same release, review round 2): that sentence was half right and the half it got
wrong let the gate read green over an EMPTY WALK.** A positive control proves the scanner; it
says nothing about whether the corpus was visited at all. See
`a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus` — the device that
actually closes it is re-deriving a KNOWN live hit a different way, with corpus floors behind it.
The same round added axis 3, because this axis's `SYS_*` regex could see neither consumer shape
the release names (`GWL_NR_FTRUNCATE = 46`, `n = 83;`).

⛔ **And do not fix it in the fold.** A patch to cyrius's vendored `lib/<dep>.cyr` evaporates at
the next `cyrius deps`. The gate names the live instance on every run instead, so it cannot go
quiet while the upstream fix is pending.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_tracked_cross_compiled_binary_no_gate_rebuilds_goes_stale_then_wrong"
description = "6.6.5. `build/cycc-native-aarch64` is committed, shipped into every install slot, and rebuilt by nothing — not install.sh, not the release gate, not CI. It was 60 releases stale, and a syscall renumber in the same release turned stale into actively wrong: `sys_unlink` traced as `fanotify_mark`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["bootstrap", "aarch64", "release-gate", "install", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The binary exists because ARM hardware self-host needs a compiler that already runs on ARM, so it
cannot be regenerated by the x86 build. `install.sh`'s case for it is `: ;` with a comment
claiming the binary stays "in lockstep"; the pi gate leg builds its compilers **fresh from
source**, so nothing in the gate ever touches the tracked one. Meanwhile
`install.sh --refresh-only` copies it into `versions/<v>/bin/` and `verify-store.sh --restore`
writes it from the tag. It had been 940,536 B from three months earlier against 1,505,480 B from
the tree.

⭐ **STALE IS TOLERABLE; DISAGREEING WITH THE STDLIB IS NOT.** The release moved the aarch64
peer's `SYS_UNLINKAT` from 35 to 263 and added the matching `263 -> 35` ESYSXLAT row. The old
emitter has no such row, so `sys_unlink` compiled against the new stdlib traced as
`fanotify_mark(-100, <ptr>, 0, 0)`. **Any release that touches `src/backend/aarch64`, the
ESYSXLAT chain or the syscall peers must regenerate it** — and it is deterministic and local,
no ARM hardware in the loop:

    ./build/cycc < src/main_aarch64.cyr > build/cycc_aarch64
    build/cycc_aarch64 < src/main_aarch64_native.cyr > build/cycc-native-aarch64

`qemu-aarch64` is enough to self-host the result to a byte-identical fixpoint as a local check
(an emulator, not the pi). ⚠ Generalise it: **a tracked artifact that no gate rebuilds has no
freshness signal at all** — its mtime is the only evidence, and nobody reads an mtime.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_x86_hosted_cross_compiler_takes_the_hosts_arch_ifdef_arm"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. Changing code under `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` in cyrius's own source produced ZERO byte delta in all SEVEN forks, including `main_aarch64.cyr` — because those forks are x86-HOSTED cross compilers and take the x86 arm. Only `build/cycc-native-aarch64` moved, by exactly two bytes."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["bootstrap", "aarch64", "verification", "cross-compile", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`cat src/main_aarch64.cyr | ./build/cycc` produces an **x86 binary that EMITS aarch64**. The
arch macros come from the COMPILER doing the compiling, not from the fork's name — `build/cycc`
predefines `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, so every `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` inside the compiler's own
source is dead in all seven of those builds. The only build where it is live is the NATIVE ARM
compiler, produced by running `build/cycc-native-aarch64` on `src/main_aarch64_native.cyr`.

⛔ **THE TRAP IS THE REASSURANCE.** Deleting an `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` arm from
`src/backend/common/runtime.cyr` gave `ctl == new, IDENTICAL` for all seven forks. Read
naively that says "the change is a no-op, nothing to verify". It says the opposite: the
verification that matters had not run yet. Under qemu the native compiler differed in exactly
two bytes (`cmp -l` → offsets 144529/144530: `0x0E20` → `0x1C80`, i.e. the `movz` immediate
113 → 228) and self-hosted to a byte-identical fixpoint.

⭐ **The rule.** A compiler-source change under an ARCH guard is verified by building the
native compiler for that arch, not by the x86 fork sweep. Corollary: an x86-hosted fork-parity
check can never regress on an arch-guarded change, so it is not evidence about one.

The companion check is cheap and direct — compile a probe with the cross compiler and read the
kernel's own answer:

    cat probe.cyr | build/cycc_aarch64 > probe.a64 && qemu-aarch64 -strace ./probe.a64

which printed `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, …) = 0` and settled that raw 228 reaches
`clock_gettime` on ELF-aarch64 through the `228 -> 113` ESYSXLAT row.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. A sweep gate had a synthetic positive control for its SCANNER and floors on its inputs (rows, peer declarations, kernel facts) — and still printed PASS and exited 0 when its tree walk matched ZERO files, taking the one live defect it names out with it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "testing", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`aarch64_syscall_shadow.sh` axis 2 walks the tree for arch-guarded syscall declarations. It had
every anti-vacuity device the repo knows: a floor on decoded ESYSXLAT rows, a floor on parsed
peer declarations, a floor on kernel-table rows, and a **positive control** running the scanner
over a fixed synthetic buffer and requiring it to flag exactly the known-bad lines.

Measured: change the file-extension tuple so it matches nothing and the gate prints

    PASS: … axis 2: 0 arch-guarded declarations over 0 files, 0 known / 0 new

and exits 0. The `⚠ KNOWN LIVE DEFECT lib/yukti.cyr:58` line — the only thing keeping a real,
unfixed, cross-repo bug audible on every run — vanishes with it.

⭐ **WHY THE CONTROLS MISSED IT.** A positive control proves the SCANNER works. Floors on inputs
prove the INPUTS parsed. Neither says anything about the CORPUS in between. Three separate
guards, all green, over a walk that visited nothing.

⭐ **WHAT ACTUALLY CLOSES IT: re-derive a known live hit a DIFFERENT WAY.** The gate now opens
each entry of its KNOWN table by that entry's own path and regexes it directly. If the
declaration is still in the file, the walk MUST have reported it; a broken walk fails loudly
instead of going quiet. It needs no threshold, and it retires itself the day the upstream fix
lands and the declaration disappears. Corpus floors sit behind it as a second line, not as the
answer.

⚠ Generalise: **"my check has a positive control" is not the same claim as "my check ran over
the thing".** Ask separately what proves each of scanner, inputs, and corpus.

**6.7.12 — A DOCUMENTED COMMAND THAT SELECTS NOTHING RUNS NOTHING (WN-2).** `CYRIUS_CROSS_OS_FULL=1 sh
scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh cass`, the command the filing gave, ran NO lib-test: the full-corpus switch only
widened a selection that arg 2 had to START, and with no arg 2 the bundle carried no `tests/tcyr`. The same class
as a flat glob that matches nothing: a switch that widens a selection must also start it (since 6.7.12
`CYRIUS_CROSS_OS_FULL=1` alone starts the lib-test), and a policy two callers share (release-gate step 4,
`cyrius audit --internal=platform-check`) lives in the one script both call (`CO_FULL_HOSTS`). Since 6.7.14
(PL-4, PL-11) `CO_FULL_HOSTS` holds all four hosts: the release gate's cross-OS leg runs the whole tcyr corpus on
cass too, not only the `crossos/` set.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "assert_lte_zero_on_a_syscall_wrapper_accepts_every_failure"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. `assert_lte(sys_sched_yield(), 0, …)` is true for success (0) AND for every negative errno, so the only assertion covering that wrapper could not fail: mis-declaring its syscall number still scored 50/50, exit 0."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "syscalls", "vacuity", "crossos", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The wrapper returns 0 on success and `-errno` on failure, so a `<= 0` assertion is a tautology
over its entire range. Measured: set the aarch64 peer's `SYS_SCHED_YIELD` 124 → 300, rebuild the
aarch64 fork, run the cross-host test under qemu → **50 passed, 0 failed, exit 0**. The wrapper
was in the release's headline list of new names and had a line in the test with its name on it.

⭐ **The tell is the shape, not the subject.** A bound that a failure satisfies is not an
assertion. `assert_lte(x, 0)`, `assert_gte(x, 0)` on a value that is `-errno` or a fd,
`assert_ne(x, <a value it never takes>)` — all of them read like tests and cover nothing.

The fix is `assert_eq(…, 0)` per target: Linux and Darwin both return exactly 0 (Darwin through
the zero-timeout `poll` the wrapper composes), and PE declines with `-38` by design, which is a
separate `assert_eq` in the Windows group. Two exact assertions instead of one that spans both.
The same mutation then scores 51 passed / 1 failed, exit 1.

⚠ A per-target exact value is more work to write than one loose bound that "covers every
target" — and the loose bound is how a wrapper ships with no test at all.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "bench_min_over_windows_net_of_a_mean_floor"
description = "6.6.5. A MEAN clock cost subtracted from every window, whose MINIMUM is then reported, reads low by the clock's own jitter and reaches 0 for real work — and subtracting the minimum read cost instead does NOT fix it, because an empty window is not a lower bound on in-window clock cost."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["benchmarking", "statistics", "clock", "stdlib", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`lib/bench.cyr` calibrated the **mean** cost of one `now_ns()` read, subtracted that one scalar
from every timed window, clamped at 0, and fed the result into `min`. The minimum over windows
of `op + c_i - mean(c)` is `op - (mean(c) - min(c_i))`: low by the clock's window-to-window
spread, and **0** as soon as that spread reaches the op. Measured on an hpet box: `getpid` timed
one per window reported `min=0` in 3 of 3 runs while its mean (370/373/581 ns) and its 128-op
batched figure (262/309/320 ns) agreed. mabda filed it; cyrius's own `bench_tagged` and
`bench_float` were printing `min=0ns` on 5 of 14 rows at the time.

⛔ **The obvious repair is refuted by measurement.** "Subtract the MINIMUM single-read cost when
computing minima" re-nets the same raw windows to 70 / 0 / 0 ns against a true ~280. The reason
is in the data: the minimum raw `getpid` window (907 ns) came out EQUAL to the minimum EMPTY
window (907 ns) in 2 of 3 runs. Clock cost inside a window that does work is not drawn from the
same distribution as an empty window, so it dips below any empty-window minimum. **No scalar
floor rescues a per-window minimum.**

⭐ **The fix is RESOLUTION, not a better floor.** A window may claim a per-op extreme only if its
net duration is at least 100 x (one read + one TICK) — i.e. only when the instrument can be
wrong by at most 1 % of it. Short windows still count toward the total and the mean. When
nothing resolves, report the MEAN and expose a `resolved` flag; never 0, and never `n/a` if
consumers parse the field as a number.

Three corollaries that each cost their own bug:
  1. **Net at READ time, not per window.** A per-window clamp at 0 biases the MEAN upward (88 to
     3,584 of 20,000 windows clamped) and freezes rows against a later floor correction.
  2. **Error is floor PLUS tick.** They are different numbers and on three of five targets the
     tick is the larger one. Floor-only sizing sized for 0 on a host whose floor calibrates to
     0 against a 15 ms tick.
  3. **The adaptive path's PILOT and TAIL windows are short**, so through 6.6.4 they set the
     extremes for every `bench_run` row: `bench_run(noop, 1e5)` read `min=0` in 91 of 200 runs.
     Under the resolution rule they are excluded automatically, with no special case.

⚠ Generalise: **do not subtract a mean from a quantity whose minimum you then report.** If the
instrument's error is comparable to the measurement, the honest answer is the mean plus a flag,
not a smaller number.

⛔ **And do not answer a review finding by widening the promise.** The first cut of this fix
wrote "never 0 for real work" into four places. It is false: when a row's windows do not in
total outlast the clock reads that bracketed them, the netted total clamps at 0 and the mean —
and with it the unresolved min and max — goes to 0. Measured at `bench_run(noop, 16)`: min=0 in
69-317 of 500 reps. That case cannot be repaired by arithmetic (reporting the raw mean would
report the CLOCK as the op, which is the inflation the release before had removed), so the
answer is to NAME it: a `sub_floor` predicate, the words "below the instrument" in the report
line, and a closed-form test leg beside a control that must NOT be flagged. A measurement that
the instrument cannot make should say so in its own vocabulary; it should not be rounded into a
number that looks like the bug you just fixed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "gate_a_statistics_defect_with_a_scripted_clock_not_a_measurement"
description = "6.6.5. Three attempts to gate a timer defect with live host-jitter statistics: one unsatisfiable by construction, one passing two runs in three, and one that measured the defect and declared it correct. A seam that makes the clock return scripted time gives closed-form expected values on every host."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "benchmarking", "gates", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`tests/tcyr/crossos/bench_timer_floor.tcyr` has carried three gate shapes for one subject:

  1. `bench_min_ns(b) * 2 < raw` where raw was a MIN over 200 no-op windows — unsatisfiable on
     any host whose tick exceeds a no-op (`0 < 0`), RED on ecb and ach on every run.
  2. A difference of totals across two separate executions — signal `N x floor`, noise the
     run-to-run spread of the same loop, and the noise was larger: 3 of 20 runs came out
     NEGATIVE on correct code.
  3. A one-sided `_btf_agree`, loosened until it stopped failing — and its own comment then
     recorded `min via bench_run : 0 0 3 22 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 (0 in TEN of twelve)` on real pi
     and concluded **"That 0 is not a defect."** Five weeks later a consumer filed exactly that
     number.

⭐ **A statistic whose noise exceeds its signal cannot be rescued by moving the threshold** —
loosen it until it stops failing and it has stopped discriminating. Each repair made the gate
looser, and the third repair wrote the defect down as correct behaviour, which is worse than no
gate: it is a standing instruction not to look again.

⭐ **The shape that works: a seam.** `var _bench_clock_fp = 0;` checked first in `now_ns()`, so a
test hands the library scripted virtual time — a stated per-read cost, jitter, op and tick — and
every expected value is CLOSED FORM from those four numbers. Identical on ecb, ach, cass, pi and
x86 Linux, at any load, in 0.1 s. Nine mutants (min over all windows, per-window clamp,
integer-ns per op, no recheck, recheck adopting a higher floor, one calibration round, error
without the tick, truncating round, unresolved min of 0) each turn it RED; the same nine ran
GREEN against the three live shapes above.

⚠ **Uninstalling the seam is more than clearing it.** Dropping `_bench_clock_fp` leaves the
CALIBRATION CACHE holding the script's numbers — the 15 ms axis leaves a floor of 0 and a 15 ms
tick — and every live axis after it then measures the host against those. Measured: two live
axes went RED on a correct library the first time the scripted axes were added. Force a real
recalibration and ASSERT it happened.

Keep live assertions, but one-sided and property-shaped ("a real op never reports a zero
minimum"), never as the primary gate.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_stdlib_module_that_calls_a_wrapper_it_does_not_include_stops_being_self_sufficient"
description = "6.6.5. Routing lib/chrono.cyr's and lib/bench.cyr's Windows clock through lib/syscalls_windows.cyr's sys_qpc_ns() broke every PE consumer that declared only \"chrono\" — and no in-repo test could see it, because every in-repo consumer pulls lib/syscalls.cyr."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["stdlib", "self-sufficiency", "windows", "pe", "deps", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`lib/chrono.cyr` includes only `atomic.cyr`; `lib/bench.cyr` includes NOTHING. Every platform
arm in both files is a bare `syscall(...)` builtin, which is what makes them stand alone. The
6.6.5 Windows-clock repair replaced `syscall(228)` with a call to `sys_qpc_ns()`, defined two
files away in `lib/syscalls_windows.cyr` — and a consumer whose `[deps] stdlib` lists
`"chrono"` without `"syscalls"` stopped cross-building for Windows:

    undefined function 'sys_qpc_ns'

⛔ **The defect is invisible in this repo by construction.** Every in-repo consumer includes
`lib/syscalls.cyr`, so the whole corpus, the whole gate set and the whole cross-OS leg build
fine. It only appears one repo over, at the pin, in the shape nobody here compiles.

⛔ **And the vendoring closure cannot rescue it.** `_distlib_union_declared_stdlib`
(`cbt/commands.cyr`) derives `dist/<lib>.deps` from the declared `[deps] stdlib` plus literal
`include "lib/X.cyr"` lines in the bundled sources. Neither file has such a line for syscalls,
so there is no chrono→syscalls edge to derive and nothing adds one.

The fix is to spell the route raw the way the module's other arms already do —
`syscall(61496, &c)` / `syscall(61497, &c)` in a private `_chrono_qpc_ns` / `_bench_qpc_ns` —
not to add an include. (PE reroute numbers ≥ 0xF000 are exempt from
`raw_syscall_literals_routed`, which judges arch-neutral ELF numbers.)

⭐ **The gate lesson is separate and sharper.** The first anti-vacuous check for the new gate
axis read the PE import table: "the binary must import QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency".
It passed over a mutant whose Windows arm had been reverted to GetTickCount64 outright —
because the now-unreachable raw helper STAYS IN THE BINARY and keeps its imports. An
import-table assertion measures what was LINKED, not what is REACHED. The check that kills
that mutant derives the arm's callee from the source and requires it to be defined in the same
file; the import check is kept only as the second axis.

Rule of thumb: when a module's header says "Requires: X" but the module compiles standalone
today, that header is a wish, not a constraint — the constraint is whether a consumer that
declares only this module can still build, on every target. Cross-build a single-module
consumer in the gate; it is one `cycc` invocation.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_check_that_reads_the_thing_it_guards_is_not_a_check_git_diff_index"
description = "6.6.5 / the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item. cyrius deps verified a dep cache with `git diff-index --quiet HEAD`, which answers from that cache's own index and config. It refused 138 of 138 untouched checkouts after one `cp -a`, and accepted seven shapes of real tampering. The fix hashes the tree into a private throwaway index."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "git", "cve", "supply-chain", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`cbt/deps.cyr` proved a cached dep checkout matched its tag with:

    git -C <cache> diff-index --quiet HEAD

Every input to that answer lives **inside the cache being judged**: the index's cached stat
data, its `assume-unchanged` / `skip-worktree` bits, and `core.fileMode` / `core.trustctime` /
`core.checkStat` / `core.fsmonitor` / `refs/replace`. Anything that can write the cache can
write the evidence.

⛔ **It was wrong in both directions at once, which is why one half hid the other.**

  * **False refusal** (what got filed, by a consumer): `diff-index` never refreshes the stat
    cache, so `touch`, `cp -a`, or a `git status` run under `unshare -r` makes every entry
    mismatch. One `cp -a` of `~/.cyrius/deps` refuses **138 of 138** real checkouts. It looks
    intermittent because any porcelain command (`git status`, `git diff`) rewrites the index
    and silently "fixes" it — the first repro attempt hid the bug that way.
  * **Silent accept** (nobody filed it, because nothing surfaces): an in-place edit with the
    mtime restored, `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, a local commit with no lock,
    `refs/replace`, a missing `.git`, and a `core.fsmonitor` hook all vendored the tampered
    bytes at exit 0. Untracked files are invisible to `diff-index` entirely.

⭐ **The rule.** A check must compute its answer from something the subject cannot write. The
fix keeps using git, but builds the evidence itself: `read-tree HEAD` into a **private
throwaway index** (a fresh index holds no stat data, so `update-index --refresh` HASHES every
file), then `diff-files`, `ls-files -o`, and a populated-gitlink scan. The cache supplies
objects; it no longer supplies the verdict.

Five git facts this cost, each measured rather than read:

  1. **`--connectivity-only` does not catch a forged object.** `read-tree` never verifies that
     an object hashes to the name it is stored under, so a forged subtree spliced in under the
     original tree's name is read back whole and `diff-files` then agrees with the planted
     bytes. `fsck --connectivity-only` exits **0** on that repo. It appeared to catch it only
     because the cache's own `.git/index` carried a cache-tree naming the mangled object —
     delete the index (a state that is otherwise perfectly benign) and the detection vanishes.
     Only a FULL `git fsck` hashes objects: 11 ms on a typical dep, 70 ms on a 392-object one.
  2. **An explicit `--git-dir` SKIPS git's `safe.directory` ownership check.** Naming the
     repository to stop discovery therefore turns OFF a security check. Use
     `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=<parent>` instead: discovery stays on, ownership is still
     enforced, and it cannot climb out of the cache.
  3. **Discovery keeps CLIMBING past a `.git` that exists but is invalid.** A gutted `.git`
     directory is not "not a repo, stop" — git walks up to the next candidate. With
     `CYRIUS_HOME` inside a git workspace that means an ENCLOSING repo answers `rev-parse
     HEAD`, and its commit gets written into `cyrius.lock` as the dependency's pin.
  4. **Git exports `GIT_INDEX_FILE` into every hook** (an absolute path to `.git/index.lock`),
     and `GIT_DIR` too in a linked worktree. A tool that forwards its environment to `git`
     children and is run from a pre-commit hook will operate on the USER'S commit index. Here
     the cold-cache `git clone` rewrote it: the hook reported success and the user's
     `git commit` died with `invalid object … Error building trees`. The scrub list is
     `git rev-parse --local-env-vars` (plus `GIT_NAMESPACE`) — and `GIT_CONFIG*` is
     deliberately NOT on it, because CI injects credentials that way and a command-line `-c`
     outranks it.
  5. **`core.fsmonitor` is a program git RUNS.** Reading a config value out of an untrusted
     directory can be code execution, not just a wrong answer. Measured: two executions per
     status check, zero with `-c core.fsmonitor=false`.

⚠ And the shared-cache constraint ruled out both remedies the filing proposed: `update-index
--refresh` and `git diff --quiet HEAD` each WRITE the cache's index, and `scripts/check.sh`
aliases the live `~/.cyrius/deps` into its staged home — the "fix" would have corrupted the
maintainer's cache on every suite run. Verification must be able to run against a directory it
may not touch. Proof is a filesystem snapshot (`find .git -printf '%p %T@ %s'`) before and
after, not a git query.

Gate: `tests/gates/toolchain/deps_git_cache_verified.sh` (65 axes, 34 mutants — DERIVE those
from the file, they grew twice during review). See
`docs/audit/2026-09-03-security-audit.md` the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_axes_that_exist_because_the_ledger_could_not_prove_their_check"
description = "6.6.5. A 39-axis gate looked complete until the mutation ledger ran: two of the guarantees it claimed — GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES and a FULL fsck — reddened NOTHING when removed. An unprovable check is either dead weight or an untested one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "mutation-testing", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`deps_git_cache_verified.sh` shipped its first green run with 39 axes covering, among other
things, a refusal caused by a forged git object and a refusal caused by a cache inside an
enclosing repository. Both passed. Then the ledger ran, and two mutants reddened **no axis at
all**:

  * removing `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` — because the axis that was supposed to need it
    (`.git` deleted) is stopped one step earlier by the `.git`-is-a-real-directory check;
  * downgrading the full `fsck` to `--connectivity-only` — because the forgery axis left the
    cache's own index in place, and that index's cache-tree names the mangled object, so
    connectivity-only exits 8 for a reason that has nothing to do with the check.

Two guarantees in the shipped code, neither one measured. The remedy is not to delete the
mutants: it is the two axes each mutant needed in order to be provable.

  * **R19** — a `.git` that EXISTS but is gutted (git's discovery keeps climbing past an
    invalid one), inside a git-repo home. Only the ceiling stops that.
  * **R20** — the same forgery with the cache's index removed, a state another (benign) axis
    already proves is normal. Only a full fsck stops that.

⭐ **The ledger is not a report card you write after the gate is done; it is the last design
step.** A mutant that reds nothing is telling you either that the code is dead or that your
fixture is proving the right answer for the wrong reason — and both of those get written down
as "verified" if the ledger is skipped. Run the ledger, and treat every NO-AXIS-RED row as a
missing axis until proven otherwise.

**UPDATE (same release, review round 2): four such axes, and one deliberate exception.** Two
more checks turned out to be unprovable as first written — `.git/info/attributes` (the other
laundering axes also carried a config key and refused on that) and a `core.worktree` hidden in
`.git/config.worktree` (a `--local` config scan cannot see that file at all). Both got an axis.
The exception is worth the precedent: dropping the `GIT_WORK_TREE` the same release added reds
NOTHING, because every work-tree redirect we could build is ALSO refused by the config scan. It
is kept anyway, with the ledger line saying so in the gate header, because it is the layer that
does not depend on having enumerated the right config key — which is precisely the assumption
the previous cut got wrong. "Treat NO-AXIS-RED as a missing axis **until proven otherwise**" has
a second answer besides "write the axis": two layers that genuinely overlap, where the redundant
one is the one that survives a wrong assumption. Say which it is in the ledger, or someone will
delete it on the strength of a green line.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "overriding_an_untrusted_config_with_dash_c_is_not_enough"
description = "6.6.5 / the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item, the SECOND cut. Having moved the dep-cache verification into a private index, the fix pinned five `-c` values on the cache's own git config and considered the config handled. Review then found eight more shapes that still vendored tampered bytes at exit 0 — every one of them a knob `-c` cannot reach."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "git", "config", "cve", "supply-chain", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The private-index rewrite fixed WHERE the evidence came from. It still let the subject vote,
through its own `.git/config`, because the code overrode a fixed list of keys:

    -c core.fsmonitor=false -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null -c core.fileMode=true
    -c core.symlinks=true  -c core.untrackedCache=false

A fixed list can only override keys whose NAMES you know. Measured against that build, each of
these resolved at **exit 0 with the tampered bytes vendored**:

  1. **`core.worktree`** — points every content command at a different directory. Park a
     pristine copy beside the tampered cache, and `diff-files` compares the copy while the
     resolver vendors from the cache. The code unset `GIT_WORK_TREE` and never SET it, so
     discovery took the work tree from this key.
  2. **`filter.<name>.clean`** — a program git RUNS over the working tree while hashing it, and
     whose output is what the comparison sees. A driver printing the honest bytes made an
     edited file compare equal. ⛔ `-c` cannot neutralise this one **in principle**: the
     driver name is chosen by the attacker, so there is no fixed key to override. It also ran
     twice per resolve — the untrusted cache choosing a binary for us to execute, the same
     class as the `core.fsmonitor` hook the first cut DID gate.
  3. **the same filter behind `include.path`** — git follows includes when it reads the config,
     but `git config --local --name-only --list` does **not** print what they pull in (measured:
     the listing showed only `include.path`, and the included filter laundered the edit anyway).
     A scan that greps the listing for `filter.` misses it.
  4. **`.git/info/attributes` alone** — no filter needed: `* text eol=crlf` makes git convert
     while hashing, so a CRLF-only edit compares equal.
  5. **`core.autocrlf` in the cache's config** — same laundering, and it CANNOT be forced off:
     a user whose GLOBAL autocrlf is on has a legitimately CRLF working tree, so forcing `lf`
     would refuse an honest cache.
  6. **`extensions.worktreeConfig` + `.git/config.worktree`** — a SECOND config file whose keys
     `--local` does not list either. Anything from 1-5 can hide there.
  7. **a `--depth 1` clone of a DIFFERENT repository carrying the same tag** — the cache
     directory is keyed on dep NAME and TAG only, so it is simply reused, and the foreign
     commit is written into `cyrius.lock`.

⭐ **The rule: partition the knobs.** What the command line CAN override, override. What it
cannot, REFUSE by name — and refuse it *before* running the command that would honour it,
because by the time a filter has laundered the bytes it has also already executed. Refusing
needs a cost check, not a guess: all 138 live checkouts carry exactly the six keys `git clone`
writes (`core.repositoryformatversion`, `core.filemode`, `core.bare`, `core.logallrefupdates`,
`remote.origin.url`, `remote.origin.fetch`), so the deny list costs nothing.

⚠ **An allowlist is the tempting inversion and it is wrong here**: git writes platform-dependent
extras at clone time (`core.ignorecase`, `core.precomposeunicode`, `core.symlinks` on Windows),
so an allowlist derived on Linux false-refuses on macOS — which is the very defect the release
was fixing. Useful fact for either shape: `git config --name-only --list` lowercases the section
and variable halves of a key (`core.workTree` prints `core.worktree`), so prefix matching on
lowercase is exact; only the subsection keeps its case.

⚠ **And narrow the claim afterwards.** Even with all of that, the check proves the checkout is
internally consistent with the tag it CLAIMS — not that the objects came from the declared
remote. Commit the tamper locally, `git tag -f` onto it, and nothing offline can tell (measured).
The lock file's commit pin is the real bound, and it is trust-on-first-use. The audit entry, the
guide and the gate comments all said "proves the checkout IS the tag"; all three were corrected.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "hardening_that_forces_a_setting_must_ask_what_the_tool_was_telling_it"
description = "6.6.5. Two ways the dep-cache hardening could itself have bricked a legitimate dependency permanently, both found in review: reading `git fsck`'s POLICY complaints as integrity failures, and forcing `core.fileMode=true` over git's own filesystem probe. Both are the same mistake — overriding a tool's answer without asking what the answer meant."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "git", "false-refusal", "portability", "gates", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
A check that refuses is only as good as its ability to refuse the right thing. Two instances,
one release, same shape:

**1. `git fsck` exits 1 for things that are not corruption.** Measured on git 2.55, all on an
otherwise perfect repository: `missingEmail`, `badEmail`, `missingSpaceBeforeEmail`,
`missingSpaceBeforeDate`, `badDate`, `zeroPaddedDate`, `missingNameBeforeEmail`,
`missingAuthor`. A dependency whose tag commit predates modern git hygiene would have been
refused as "object-store damage" **for ever** — and the recovery the message printed
(`rm -rf <cache>`) re-clones the same objects and refuses again. Fix: pass those ids as
`fsck.<id>=warn`; leave everything that bears on whether an object hashes to the name it is
stored under as an error. ⚠ An id a git version does NOT know is `fatal: Unhandled message id`,
exit **128** — which would brick every resolve on that git — so the run falls back to a
policy-free fsck on 128. (`fsck.missingName` is exactly such an id on 2.55. The list has to be
probed, not copied out of documentation.)

**2. Forcing `core.fileMode=true` overrides git's own filesystem probe.** On vfat / exfat /
NTFS-3g / 9p — a USB stick, a WSL `/mnt/c`, a VM share — `git clone` writes
`core.filemode=false` because the exec bit cannot be stored. Forcing `true` made every 755 entry
in the tag read as mode-changed, so the dep refused permanently and `reset --hard` could not
repair it: it cannot set a bit the filesystem has no room for. 104 of 138 live checkouts here
carry a 755 file.

⭐ **The fix is not "trust the config" — it is to ASK THE SAME QUESTION YOURSELF.** Probe the
filesystem the way git probes it (create a 0600 file, `chmod` it 0700, `stat` it) and force the
probed answer. That keeps the security half — an attacker's `core.fileMode=false` is still
overridden on a filesystem that CAN store the bit — while a mode-only difference stops bricking
the dep on one that cannot. ⚠ Use `stat`, not `access(X_OK)`: they disagree on a `noexec` mount,
where the bit IS stored, and `stat` is what git itself asks.

⚠ **The probe's own first cut then walked into the neighbouring trap**: it read `st_mode` at a
hand-written offset, which is byte 24 on x86-Linux, **16** on aarch64-Linux and **4** on
macOS-arm64's stat64 — the exact thing `lib/io.cyr`'s `is_dir` carries a warning about, after it
once reported every directory as not-a-directory on ARM. Measured under `qemu-aarch64` (not
hardware): with the hand-written 24 the ARM build read st_uid and **accepted** a mode tamper,
and through `xstat` + `STAT_MODE` it refuses. If the stdlib has a wrapper for a struct field,
using it is not style — the offsets differ per ABI and the host suite cannot see the difference.

⚠ Both of these are refusals that only fire on other people's machines, so the gate has to reach
for them deliberately: a tag commit built with `hash-object --literally` for the fsck policy, and
— since a vfat mount needs root — driving the probe-impossible path by making the probe's own
directory read-only, then asserting BOTH halves (a mode-only difference is accepted, content
tampering in the same state is still refused).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_identifier_two_legal_spellings_is_not_a_string_compare"
description = "6.6.5 / the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item, review round 2. The dep-cache check compared `remote.origin.url` against the manifest with a plain string equality. `…/x` and `…/x.git` are ONE repository on every forge, so it refused 19 live declarations across 11 repos — and the refusal had no fixed point."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "git", "false-refusal", "url", "cve", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The hardening added "the cache must CLAIM to come from the url the manifest declares", because
the cache directory is keyed on dep NAME and TAG only and a foreign checkout parked there was
silently reused. Correct. The comparison was `_cstr_eq(cache_origin, declared_url)`.

A forge serves `https://host/o/x`, `https://host/o/x.git` and `https://host/o/x/` as the SAME
repository, and both spellings are in normal use. Measured across the 126 `cyrius.cyml`
manifests on this box against the 138 live caches: **19 declarations in 11 repos** differ from
their cache by exactly that suffix — one dep+tag (tyche 1.0.1) is declared both ways by eight
different consumers.

⛔ **The shape that makes it worse than a one-off refusal: there is no fixed point.** The message
says `rm -rf <cache>`; do that and the next consumer re-clones with ITS spelling, and now the
first project refuses. Two consumers alternate for ever. A false refusal that the advertised
recovery converts into a different false refusal is not a bug you can tell a user to work around.

⛔ **And the SAME comparison sat one function over, where the failure mode is worse.** The
the release-integrity hardening item commit pin is looked up by `(name, url, tag)` from `cyrius.lock`; an exact compare there
does not REFUSE, it silently TOFU-RE-PINS — respell the url in your manifest and the
moved-tag check the pin exists for is skipped for that resolve. The first fix normalised only
the cache's origin, because that is where the reviewer had measured it. ⭐ When you fix a
comparison, grep for the SHAPE (`_cstr_eq(<a url>, …)`), not the site.

⭐ **Rule: an identity check between two things written by different people needs the identity
relation, not `==`.** Normalise what the domain says is insignificant (here: one trailing `/`,
then one trailing `.git`), then compare — and prove the check still refuses a genuinely
different url, or normalisation has just deleted the check.

⚠ **And the CLAIM about it shared the blind spot.** The release note said "every origin url
matches what the manifests declare", because it had been checked against the CACHES (138 of
them) rather than against every DECLARING manifest (210 rows). The subject and the verifier were
the same population — the v6.6.2 lesson (`a check that shares a defect with the thing it checks
reads GREEN`) applied to a sentence in a CHANGELOG. Compute the claim from the other side.

⚠ Related, unfixed and now written down instead: two consumers on one box declaring the same dep
NAME+TAG from genuinely DIFFERENT urls (a fork) still alternate refusals, because the cache path
carries no url component. The refusal is correct in each direction; the recovery fixes one
project by breaking the other. Keying the directory on a hash of the url is the fix when someone
needs it.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "could_not_measure_is_not_measured_false"
description = "6.6.5 / the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item, review round 2. Three fail-opens and one hard-fail in one release, all the same shape: a check that could not ASK its question treated the failure to ask as an answer. An unwritable directory read as 'this filesystem cannot store the exec bit'; a fork that does not exist on Windows read as 'git says your cache is broken'."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "windows", "probe", "fail-open", "cve", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The dep-cache hardening probes its environment in several places. Each probe has three possible
outcomes and the code only had two.

**1. The exec-bit probe.** To decide whether `core.fileMode` comparison is meaningful, it writes
a 0600 file beside the cache, chmods it 0700 and stats it. When the file could not be CREATED it
returned *not storable* — i.e. "this filesystem has no exec bit" — and memoised that per PROCESS
from the first dep's parent directory. So a read-only `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>` silently disabled
mode comparison for every dep in the run: measured, a `chmod -x` tamper accepted at rc 0, no
warning, no attacker control of the cache config needed. Fix: a third answer, UNKNOWN, which
forces nothing and falls back to the value git's own clone-time probe wrote into that cache —
the other measurement of the same filesystem — and a memo keyed on the directory probed.

**2. `sys_fork` on Windows.** `lib/syscalls_windows.cyr` defines it as `return 0 - 1`. The
verification runs git through `fork`+`execve`, so on PE **every** git command returns -1. The
release had just closed a fail-open by making "no HEAD" a refusal — which on Windows turned into
a hard refusal of a pre-populated cache (the only way a git dep can exist there), naming a reason
that is false ("its .git is missing") and a recovery that cannot run. Measured under wine: rc 1
with the module missing, where 6.6.4 vendored it. Fix: ASK the platform (`_git_exec_available`,
one `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`), warn once per dep that the cache was not verified, and stay out
of the check — rather than inferring a verdict about the cache from the failure of the tool.

⭐ **The rule: every probe has three outcomes — yes, no, and I-could-not-ask.** Collapsing the
third into "no" is a fail-open when the check is a security check, and collapsing it into "yes"
is a permanent false refusal. Name the third state and decide it deliberately; the right
fallback is usually another measurement of the same thing (here: what git itself recorded), not
a guess.

⚠ The gate axis for the first one had encoded the WRONG semantics and passed: it asserted that
an unprobeable directory accepts a mode difference. It now asserts both halves against the
cache's own recorded value — `false` (a vfat clone) accepts, `true` (this box) refuses — which
is a property, not a behaviour the implementation happens to have.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_argument_parser_per_verb_is_a_defect_per_verb"
description = "6.6.5. A filing about ONE flag on ONE verb (`cyrius lint --strict-deferrals`) was ~60 spellings across every verb and every delegated tool, because each branch hand-rolled its own argument loop. Several were SILENT AND MUTATING: a typo'd flag rewrote a file, deleted build/, or ran a real resolve, all at exit 0."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cli", "cbt", "arguments", "fail-open", "dry-run", "windows", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
mabda filed two rows against `cyrius lint`: the flag-first spelling handed cyrlint the FLAG as
its file (usage text, exit 1), and the flag-last spelling DROPPED the flag and exited 0 with the
gate off. The second is the one that costs you — it prints the same notes as a plain lint and
nothing says the flag was ignored, so three untracked deferrals shipped in three mabda releases
behind a green CI step.

**It was never a lint bug.** ~35 verb branches in `cbt/cyrius.cyr` each had their own loop, and
no two agreed. The shape has four faces:

1. an undeclared `-` token becomes the OPERAND (`lint --strict-deferrals f` opens
   "--strict-deferrals"; `build s --strict` writes the binary to a file NAMED `--strict`);
2. a flag after the operand is dropped (`fmt f --chekc` REWRITES the file, exit 0);
3. extra operands are dropped (`lint a b` lints `a` and reports its verdict for the pair —
   ranga's CI linted 1 of 41 files that way);
4. the branch indexes from a literal `argv(2)` rather than `cmd_idx + 1`, so a global `-q`/`-v`
   shifts every operand (`cyrius -q clean --dry-run` DELETED `build/`).

⭐ **The severity is not in the flag, it is in what the verb does when it proceeds anyway.**
Measured on 6.6.4: `clean --dryrun` deleted; `lib sync --dry` synced 110 files; `deps --dry-run`
and `deps --verfy` really resolved; `pulsar --dry-run` did the real rebuild-and-install. Sorting
the ~60 rows by SILENT vs LOUD and by MUTATING vs not is what turns "low severity, there is a
working form" into the actual picture.

⭐ **`--dry-run` was set too late to mean anything.** It was assigned inside the build loop,
which runs AFTER `_auto_deps`, so a dry-run build in a `[deps]` project printed "dry-run:
compile" having already created `lib/`. And the deps branch never set it at all — which means
`cbt/deps.cyr`'s dry-run early return had existed for releases and had **never once been
reached from the CLI**. A branch no caller can reach is not a feature; grep for the flag that
selects it before believing it works.

⭐ **A cap in the plumbing silently shapes the caller.** `run_tool` forwarded at most 3
arguments, so `cmd_lint` was *written* to forward only `--strict` and the file: three flags plus
a path is four. The caller looked like a policy decision and was an arity limit. Same shape one
level down in `lib/flags.cyr`, whose positional array stopped at 128 and returned SUCCESS —
kriya re-implemented operand counting by hand (`src/lib/args.cyr:781`) rather than being told.

⚠ **`run_tool` used fork/waitpid, which are -1 STUBS on PE.** `cyrius lint/fmt/doc/vet/deny/
api-surface` therefore never spawned their tool on Windows at all; under wine the PE CLI did not
return in 15 s. The Windows path is `_win_create_process`/`_win_wait_close` with each argument
quoted (a PE command line is ONE string). If a CLI feature delegates to a helper binary, check
how it spawns before assuming the feature exists on every target.

⚠ **`var argv[8]` is 8 BYTES, one slot.** `run_cx` stored its NUL terminator at `+8`, which
landed on the next local's stack slot (a probe measured `&argv + 8 == &fd`). It had worked by
luck since the cx arc.

**The fix is one classifier, not 35 repairs** (`cbt/cli_args.cyr`): flags in any position, an
undeclared `-` token is an error that NAMES it, operand counts enforced, indices from
`cmd_idx + 1`, effects applied before `_auto_deps`, and `--help` printed from the SAME spec
tables the parser reads so the two cannot drift. Adding a flag to a table registers it with both.

⭐ **What the round-2 review found in the FIX — the same family, one level out:**

* **A wrapper that re-implements its tool's semantics will disagree with it.** The first cut
  spawned cyrlint once per file and took the MAX exit; cyrlint itself SUMS. On three
  100-warning files `cyrlint a b c --exit-with-count` exited 255 and `cyrius lint a b c
  --exit-with-count` exited 100. The fix is not a second summing loop — it is ONE invocation
  over every file, so the tool's semantics are the only semantics.
* **When the exit code IS the answer (a count), a hard failure needs its own carrier.** The new
  multi-file tail returned the summed count and threw away `rc`, the only variable holding
  "could not read file 2" — so `cyrlint good.cyr missing.cyr --exit-with-count` printed the
  read error and exited **0**. A fail-open written by the fix for a fail-open. `_lint_hard`
  now records it separately.
* **A per-target arm applied to one binary out of nine.** `_ccname`/`_sn` had resolved
  `cycc.exe` on Windows since v6.0.85; every OTHER tool resolved as `make_path(_tools_dir,
  "cyrlint")` with no suffix, and `run_tool_vec` refuses on `file_exists` before CreateProcess —
  so on the shipped Windows layout every delegated verb said `tool not found`. The new spawn
  worked (measured under wine by copying `cyrlint.exe` to `cyrlint`) and was unreachable.
  `_tool_path` now carries the `.exe` probe for all of them, and the gate fails on any tool
  lookup that bypasses it. Two of the tools were not even in the Windows tarball.
* **Forwarding arguments to something that ignores them is a drop.** `cyrius run p.cyx a b c`
  handed `a b c` to cxvm, which reads nothing but the program on stdin (cx has no guest argv
  ABI), and exited with the guest's code. Rejected by name now.
* **The fix's own new integer parser wrapped.** `_cli_int` existed to stop `atoi` coercing
  garbage and accumulated past 63 bits: `soak 99999999999999999999` became
  7766279631452241919, passed the `> 0` guard and never terminated.
* **Sweep every binary with a dispatcher, not the one in the filing.** `cyriusly` (a shipped
  `[release].bins` entry) had the filed shape verbatim — `cyriusly use 6.6.4 --globl` DROPPED
  the typo and took the LOCAL-pin path, exit 0 — and `cyriusly install --dry-run` installed a
  version literally named `--dry-run`.

⭐ **Round 3 found the family again, in three new places:**

* **A count-as-exit mode must still carry the verdicts.** `cyrlint --exit-with-count` returned
  the warning count BEFORE the strict checks ran, so `--exit-with-count --strict-deferrals` on a
  file with 0 warnings and an untracked deferral exited **0** — the filed flag, finally
  forwarded, switched off by its neighbour. The exit code is now max(count, 2). When one
  channel carries two answers, decide which wins before the first early return.
* **"Last one wins" is a drop.** The classifier's value accessor kept the last occurrence, so
  `--features a --features b` resolved `b` alone while `--features a,b` resolved both, and
  `--target=js --target=cx` silently meant cx — the FLAG_LIST defect fixed in `lib/flags.cyr`
  the same week, rebuilt one directory over. Single-valued repeats are refused by name now;
  `-D` and `--features` accumulate. Same shape in `cyrius-init` (`init alpha beta` scaffolded
  beta alone), `ts_test_runner` (last path walked), `cyrld` (a second `-o` replaced the first)
  and `cyrius_api_surface` (`--snapshot` twice).
* **A dry run must fail where the real run fails.** `deps --dry-run`, reachable for the first
  time, returned before the manifest check: in an empty directory it said "would resolve deps
  from cyrius.cyml" and exited 0 while `deps` exited 1.
* **Windows, three layers deep.** The spawn fix made `lint` reach its tool, and then the
  shipped layout (cycc.exe present) failed CLOSED: the private temp dir was a literal `"/tmp"`
  plus a raw `syscall(39)`, which is not a PE reroute and returns -38 on every call, so every
  run raced for `/tmp/cyrius--38[-N]`. Under the temp dir sat `run_binary_timed`, still
  fork/waitpid, so `run/test/tests/bench/fuzz` never ran the program on Windows and decoded an
  UNINITIALISED status (`run argc a b c` → 111). And a CreateProcessW with no application name
  appends `.exe` to an extensionless program, so the temp binary had to be named `run.exe`.
  Measure a Windows fix on the SHIPPED layout — the round-2 claim was measured with cycc.exe
  absent, which is the one layout where the pre-pass never needs a temp dir.
* **Plan change, recorded:** the fix plan said the delegated tools would move onto
  `lib/flags.cyr`. They got in-place loops instead, and the loops had already drifted (cyrsign
  printed `unknown option` without the token). What is enforced is the RULE, by one gate over
  every binary — not which parser implements it; `lib/flags.cyr`'s header had claimed those
  tools used it and was corrected.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "gate_a_cli_with_a_hash_the_shell_computes"
description = "6.6.5. How to gate argument handling without the gate sharing the defect: a tree hash the SHELL computes, a second oracle from a different binary, and the defect in the LAST operand so a fix that only handles operand 0 cannot pass."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "cli", "anti-vacuous", "mutation", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`tests/gates/toolchain/cli_args_never_dropped.sh`, 579 assertions after the round-3 review (478
after round 2, 310 in the first cut). The techniques are reusable for any CLI-shaped gate:

⛔ **Round 3 found this gate passing on the 6.6.4 CLI in three ways — each worth a rule:**

* **Grep for the ERROR CLASS, not the token.** Axis 2 checked that stderr contained
  `--zz-cli-probe`. On 6.6.4 every verb whose operand is a path printed `no such file:
  --zz-cli-probe` — the flag-taken-as-a-file defect itself — and the row read green. It now greps
  `unknown option '--zz-cli-probe'`.
* **A refusal row is vacuous on a fixture where the real operation fails anyway.** `deps
  --verfy` in a dir with no manifest, `fuzz --poisn` with no harness, `pulsar --dry-run` outside
  a cyrius tree: 6.6.4 ran all of them for real and they failed for unrelated reasons, rc != 0,
  green. Each typo row now runs where the real operation SUCCEEDS, proven by an anti-vacuous
  twin without the typo, and hashes CYRIUS_HOME as well as the working dir (pulsar/lsp/update
  write the store, not the project).
* **A throwaway CYRIUS_HOME is not hermetic if the compiler reads $HOME.** cycc's include
  fallback reads `$HOME/.cyrius/versions/<ver>/lib` and ignores CYRIUS_HOME, so five fixture
  rows compiled against the LIVE store; with an empty HOME they went red. The gate now points
  HOME at a throwaway whose `.cyrius` is the staged home.
* **A Windows leg under wine needs its own prefix.** wine overrides `TEMP` from the prefix
  registry (a `TEMP=` in the environment does not reach the process), so every PE run that makes
  a temp dir writes into `~/.wine`. The leg runs under `WINEPREFIX=$T/wine` (winemenubuilder,
  mono and gecko disabled), then `wineserver -k` and removes its `/tmp/.wine-<uid>/server-…`
  socket dir. It SKIPs, loudly, when wine is absent; cass runs the same rows on hardware.

**Never ask the tool what it did.** Side effects are measured with
`{ find . ; find . -type f | xargs sha256sum ; } | sha256sum`, taken before and after each
probe. ⚠ A `find . -type f` alone is NOT enough: it misses `cyrius deps`' symlinks and a bare
`mkdir lib`. That exact blind spot hid a real defect during development — `lib sync --dry-run`
mkdir'd `./lib` and the hash did not move, because an empty directory has no files in it. The
NAME list is what caught it.

**Two oracles for the filed row.** Axis 1 asserts exit 2 both against the literal and against
the rc of `cyrlint --strict-deferrals` run directly — a different parser in a different binary.
And the `--exit-with-count` expectation is parsed out of STDOUT's `<n> warnings` line, a
different channel from the exit code it predicts.

**Put the defect in the LAST operand.** Axis 4 lints/formats N files where only the last is
bad. With the defect in the first file, a mutant that iterates only operand 0 passes every row —
which is precisely the mutation (M3) this arrangement exists to catch. It also runs 130 files so
a silent cap anywhere in the chain shows up.

**A census axis, so a verb added later cannot escape.** The gate derives the verb list from
`cbt/cyrius.cyr`'s own `streq(cmd, "…")` dispatch sites with awk and FAILS on any verb with
neither a fixture row nor a reasoned exemption. Mutation M11 (adding a `streq(cmd, "zz")`
branch) turns it red. ⚠ **"Has a row" must MEAN "is probed".** The first cut kept COVERED as a
second hand-typed list beside the probe loop, and it drifted at once: `build`, `run` and
`--version` passed the census and were never probed, under a comment claiming every verb was.
COVERED is now the probe list itself (mutation M19: drop `build` from it and the census fails).
The same census runs over `cyriusly` and `ark` from their own dispatch functions.

**Equivalence needs an absolute anchor.** "`V F x` == `V x F`" compares a binary with itself: a
flag dropped in BOTH positions keeps it green (mutation M23 — `doc --check` ignored everywhere —
passed every equivalence row). Each verb also needs one row whose expected value comes from
outside the tool: `doc --check` over a fixture of N undocumented fns must exit N, where N is the
shell's loop counter.

**Anti-vacuous rows next to every assertion.** `plain lint exits 0` beside `lint --strict-
deferrals exits 2`; `build without --dry-run DOES write` beside the seven dry-run rows; `a real
clean DOES remove the file` beside `-q clean --dry-run leaves it alone`; `argc()==1 with no args`
beside `argc()==4 with three`.

**Record the mutations as MEASURED, not as intended.** The ledger in the header carries the fail
COUNT per mutation. One row is honest about not doing what was planned: restoring `lib/flags.cyr`'s
128-positional cap leaves this gate GREEN and turns `tests/tcyr/crossos/flags.tcyr` red, because
the CLI does not use the stdlib parser — two parsers, two gates. Writing that down is worth more
than quietly dropping the row.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_line_scanner_forgets_what_the_language_carries_across_lines"
description = "6.6.5. cyrlint ran every rule on ONE physical line, and four things in cyrius cross a line break: a comment a formatter wrapped, an initializer that continues, a string with a raw newline, a call whose arguments wrap. Each produced a miss — one of them a real silent zero with no warning — and widening the unit of MATCHING must not widen the unit of EVIDENCE."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrlint", "lint", "deferral", "init-order", "strings", "measurement", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
mabda filed one row: `# The immediate-offset form is a later` / `# bite.` passed
`cyrlint --strict-deferrals` while the same words on one line exited 2. Three untracked
deferrals shipped that way. The same design produced three more misses nobody had filed:

1. **init-order** — the initializer scan stopped at the line end, so `var A = 1 +` /
   `    B;` over a later `var B = g();` drew no warning while the binary read B as 0
   (exit 1; the same program in declaration order exits 3). Prove a silent zero at
   RUNTIME before asking the linter — the gate compiles both orders and checks the exit
   codes first, so the lint assertion rests on a fact, not on the linter's own opinion.
2. **brace depth** — cyrius strings may hold a raw newline (src/main.cyr has two). Three
   hand-rolled trackers reset "inside a string" at every line end, so every main*.cyr fork
   drew a false `unclosed braces`, and a `}` inside such a string took a real top-level
   `var` out of the init-order rule's sight. One shared helper (`_lx_line`) now carries the
   state; three copies had already drifted.
3. **call notes / enum members** — `sys_open(p,` / `0, 0)` and one-line enums were missed.
   The enum grammar (`PARSE_ENUM_DEF`) makes the `;`/`,` separator OPTIONAL, so "a member
   follows `{` or `,`" — the plan's rule — would have missed sankoch's `;`-separated enum.
   Read the parser, not the plan.

⭐ **Join the TEXT, keep the EVIDENCE line-granular.** The filing proposed accepting a
tracking pointer "anywhere in the joined block". Measured over a corpus before choosing: that
hid one of the filing's OWN three cases (a `docs/` path three lines up "tracked" a `for` /
`now` it had nothing to do with) and silently un-flagged 98 of 179 existing notes. The rule
shipped: a pointer counts only on a physical line the occurrence touches — every single-line
verdict is unchanged and all three filed cases are caught. When a fix widens what a matcher
SEES, check separately what it lets EXCUSE.

⭐ **A per-occurrence re-scan is quadratic on a hostile file.** The first join rescanned the
pointer vocabulary per occurrence: 11–27 s on 1 MB inputs. A per-line cached verdict makes it
76–263 ms, and the gate pins that with `timeout 10` on a 960 KB tracked line (mutating the
cache away turns it red). The same applies to cross-line CALL walks: one walk per LINE with a
line cap, not one per `syscall(` (3 s → 0.25 s on 90K unclosed calls).

⭐ **A classification nothing can observe is dead weight.** The plan made `#`+letter lines a
"directive" class that breaks a paragraph. Measured: removing it changed 0 notes across
24,241 ecosystem files — a directive's keyword (`ifdef`, `derive`) always sits between the
phrase halves — and its only visible effect was hiding `#now`-style wraps. It was dropped.

Oracles in the gate: literal tables typed from the fixtures, plus an awk re-implementation of
the OLD per-line rule for the single-line fixtures (invariance, checked by a different program
in a different language), plus the runtime exit codes. The cyrlint under test is compiled in
the gate from the tree — `build/cyrlint` and the store can both be stale.

⭐ **Enumerate the SHAPES the grammar allows, not the ones you happened to write.** Review
round 2 found six more statement shapes the first cut had not tried, each confirmed by
compiling it: `var A` / `= 1 + B;` (the `=` opens the next line, past comment lines — a silent
zero, exit 1), `pub var` / `public var`, a second decl on a line as a forward-ref TARGET,
`sys_open (p, 0, 0)` and `syscall` / `(…)` — and the snake_case rule, which matched a
line-initial `fn ` only, had never checked one of the ecosystem's 28,652 `pub fn` lines. Every
one was measured at 0 live instances
across 24,241 files — and so had the shape the bite was filed for, until mabda wrote it. Ask
the parser what it accepts (`var A` with no `=` and no `;` is an error, so the look-ahead is
safe to take at the first significant byte) and cover each shape the rule claims.

⭐ **A `#` is not always a comment.** The lexer turns ten byte prefixes (`#naked`, `#inline`,
`#pure`, `#io`, `#alloc`, `#must_use`, `#regalloc`, `#deprecated`, `#assert`, `#pe_import`)
into TOKENS and keeps lexing the line, so `#naked fn isr() {` opens a real brace. cyrlint read
the line as a comment (15 false `unmatched closing brace`, and a negative depth that blinded
init-order to a real silent zero after it), and cyrfmt had the SAME reading: `cyrius fmt`
rewrote that fn's body flush left. Both now mirror the lexer's own table. The lexer matches
with no word boundary, so `#ioctl numbers` does not compile — a lexer defect the tools mirror
on purpose until it is fixed (issue 2026-09-19-lexer-attribute-prefix-swallows-comments).
When a tool re-implements the lexer's idea of "comment", copy the lexer's table, not the
one-byte rule everyone remembers.

⭐ **A check that expects "nothing" must prove the tool RAN.** The first gate compared
cyrlint's stderr against `''` / `0` and discarded its exit status, so a cyrlint that SEGFAULTED
on all seven src/main*.cyr forks read PASS 77/77 (a crash prints nothing, and nothing matched).
Every run now goes through one wrapper: a timeout, a signal death or a missing `<n> warnings`
trailer is a hard failure, and after the first timeout every later run fails at once, so a
looping build costs one timeout instead of hanging check.sh. Mutation-prove the harness too:
a crashing mutant and a looping mutant are ledger rows now.

⭐ **Round 3: the unit is the STATEMENT, not the line — and order is by OFFSET.** Round 2's
look-ahead accepted an `=` only when it OPENED the next line; the grammar lets the header wrap
anywhere: `var` / `A = 1 + B;`, `var A:` / `i64 = …`, `var A` / `: i64 = …` — each a silent
zero (exit 1, twins 3). Two more statement STARTS were invisible: a `var` after a `}` that
closes a fn on the same line, and a `var` after a `;` that ends a WRAPPED initializer. And a
line-number compare cannot order two declarations on one line: `var A = 1 + B; var B = g();`
reads B as 0 and drew nothing. The rule now walks the header across lines to its `=`/`;`,
takes the name from the next line after a bare `var`, continues the `;` chain across lines,
tries a declaration after every `}` that returns to depth 0, and compares declaration
OFFSETS. Every walk ends where the next declaration can begin and a "frontier" skips any
keyword inside an already-walked statement, so the walks never overlap: 50K lines of
`{} var vN = (` finish in 0.16 s (without the frontier each line scans to EOF — quadratic).
0 changed notes over 24,241 ecosystem files; every new shape is pinned with a runtime twin.

⭐ **A buffer sized for the INPUT is not sized for the OUTPUT.** cyrfmt's output buffer was the
input cap (1 MB) with no bound check, and formatting can multiply size: 20K lines of an
unclosed `syscall(a,` indent line i by 2·i spaces, so `--check`/`--write` SEGFAULTED (at 6.6.4
too) and stdout mode streamed >100 MB. Bound the writer, stop the indent loop at the cap, and
fail LOUDLY — the per-line quadratic work disappears with the output.

⭐ **A silent truncation makes a gate judge a PREFIX.** cyrdoc read every file into a fixed
64 KB buffer. sigil's CI requires "0 undocumented" in its 1,106,698-byte dist bundle: cyrdoc
saw 69 of its 1,168 fns (6 %) and passed; read whole, 429 are undocumented. cyrius's own
stdlib doc gate had judged three >64 KB files on a prefix. The buffer now grows until a read
comes up short of it. Grep every fixed-size read in a checking tool: `file_read_all(p, buf,
N)` returning exactly N is a truncation, not a file of length N.

⭐ **The PREPROCESSOR had the same defect one layer down — fixed 6.6.6.** A line inside a
multi-line string that began with `#ifdef` / `#endif` / `#define` was EXECUTED: `"ab` /
`#ifdef NOPE` / `cd` / `#endif` / `ef"` compiled to the bytes `ab`, four newlines, `ef`, exit 0,
no diagnostic. Three separate passes each carried an inlined copy of a state machine that reset
`in_string` at every newline, justified in a comment by the premise "cyrius source uses
single-line strings" — false about `src/main.cyr`'s own error messages. ⚠ **The reset was a
blast-radius bound, so deleting it is not the fix.** It bounded a `"` the machine MIS-READ, and
both real sources of that had to become states first: a `"` in a `#` comment and a `"` in a char
literal (`var q = '"';`). Remove the reset without the char state and one apostrophe poisons the
whole buffer. Fixed as `PP_LEXST`, one machine for all the line-oriented passes.

⛔ **COUNT THE INSTANCES BY SHAPE, NOT BY NAME — there was a FOURTH pass.** The first cut
fixed the three passes that HAD a state machine and said so in the commit, the CHANGELOG, the
gate header and the issue's resolution. `PP_IS_HOST_ONLY`, 2,300 lines up in the same file,
was missed because it had NO state machine to find: just `var bol = 1` and a column-0 byte
match. Its own comment named the column-0 requirement as the safeguard ("so prose that merely
mentions the directive cannot trip it") — and a raw LF inside a string is exactly what puts an
arbitrary byte at column 0, so an included file holding `var doc = "intro` / `#host_only` /
`end";` made every bare-metal build refuse it. Grep the SHAPE (a `bol` flag, a first-byte-of-line
decision), not the names you already know; a pass with no state is the one a "which passes carry
this state?" search cannot return.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_sites_that_rederive_one_mapping_drift_record_it_where_it_is_decided"
description = "6.6.6. PARSE_FN_DEF homed register params INSIDE the parameter loop (with each param's true int ordinal and frame slot) and stack params in a SECOND pass after the loop that re-derived ordinal, slot AND the caller's int-class total from `pc`, the all-class param count. A value-form vector next to 6+ ints, or an x86 retptr with 6+ params, desynchronised it on every backend — silently. v6.4.44 had already patched ONE input of that pass (the Win64 retptr ordinal) and left the other two. Fix: record (ordinal, slot) where the loop classifies the param; the pass only executes what was recorded."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "calling-convention", "stack-args", "simd", "retptr", "param-homing", "rederivation", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Shape.** A deferred pass cannot run where the facts are known (SysV/aarch64 stack slots
count DOWN from the caller's int-class TOTAL, which is only known after the whole list is
parsed), so it was written to RE-DERIVE the facts afterwards — from `pc`. Three inputs, one
proxy:

| input | re-derived as | true value |
|---|---|---|
| stack total | `pc` | int-class params + retptr (`int_pc + _pl_shift`) |
| arg ordinal | loop counter `spi` | that param's `int_pc + _pp_shift` |
| frame slot  | loop counter `spi` | that param's `li` (a vector owns 2-4 slots) |

The proxy is exact for the common case (every param int-class, one slot, no retptr), so
323 `.tcyr` and 99 programs never exercised a difference — the fix moved ZERO corpus bytes.
`n6(v: f64v2, 1..6)` read `e` as 0; `mk6(1..6): P3` read its sixth param from the return
address; x86, aarch64, Win64 and cx were all wrong, each differently.

**Why a patch on one input is not a fix.** v6.4.44 found the Win64 retptr case of this
same pass (vidya `win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_...`) and taught it the
ordinal shift — the one input that had a symptom on the host in hand. The total stayed
`pc` (wrong on SysV with a retptr) and the vector's ordinal/slot stayed `spi`. Any pass
that recomputes what an earlier site already decided will drift again the next time the
earlier site learns a new case; the durable fix is to RECORD at the deciding site
(`_stkp_defer(ordinal, li)`) and have the pass consume the record (`_stkp_flush(total)`).
Recording inside the loop's own guards also stopped the pass homing params the loop had
refused (a coroutine impl's).

**How to find the next one.** Grep for a second pass over the same list keyed by a
counter the first pass did not use as its key: `while (spi < pc)` next to a loop that
tracked `int_pc` / `li`. The caller paths the filing exercised — plain call, method call,
call in `return` position — were already right on every target (and the filing's "a
per-target convention change" was wrong) — bisect caller vs callee by disassembling the
prologue homing store against the body's FINDLOCAL read first. ⚠ Do not generalise that to
"every caller": the struct-valued-assign paths (`var p: P3 = f(v, ...)`, parse_decl.cyr
`asv` / `asv_pair`) push a value-form vector as an INT at any arity — a separate caller-side
defect, filed at 6.6.6 (`2026-09-19-struct-valued-assign-call-pushes-simd-args-as-ints`).
Name the caller paths you measured; "all of them" is a claim nobody enumerated.

**Gate.** Differential gates (method arm vs free fn) cannot see a defect on the shared
callee half — both arms agree while both are wrong. `stack_param_homing_matrix.sh`
GENERATES the shape matrix (class x position x 5..9 ints, retptr) with expectations built
as digit strings by awk while the callee computes them arithmetically.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "redeclared_name_new_slot_orphans_per_slot_data_and_name_replays_drift"
description = "6.6.6. A top-level `var` redeclaration was registered as a SECOND slot while every reference bound to the last one (FINDVAR is last-match). Anything written PER SLOT for the first declaration — its baked static-init value — was orphaned, so `var a = 5; var b = a; var a = 5;` read b as 0, and the collision warning's '(last definition wins)' described a semantics nothing implemented. A replay that re-resolved the name at emit time (instead of using the slot recorded at declaration) could land on a LATER declaration in kernel builds. Fix: one slot per name in the declaration zone, value rule stated and gated, replay entries carry their slot."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["globals", "static-init", "symbol-table", "last-match", "redeclaration", "diagnostics", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Shape.** Two mechanisms each correct on their own: (1) static-init bakes a constant
into the image AT THE SLOT INDEX being registered; (2) name lookup returns the LAST slot
with that name. Put together, a redeclared name's first value lives in a slot no reference
can reach. The v5.11.64 static-init even had a "shadow opt-out" — but it opted out the
LATER declaration (to stop an earlier runtime store clobbering it), never the earlier one,
whose value was the one being orphaned.

**The warning was the spec nobody tested.** `CHKDUPVAL` printed "(last definition wins)"
since v6.2.11, and no gate ever read the value back. A diagnostic that states a semantics
is a claim: gate the claim (read the value from an earlier initializer, a fn, and after
init), not just that the diagnostic fires.

**Replay by name drifts.** EMIT_GVAR_INITS replayed each deferred initializer by
re-resolving the name. In a normal build the replay runs before the top-level program, so
it happened to find the declaration's own slot; in a kernel build it runs AFTER, and found
the program's later `var` of the same name. Record the slot when the entry is registered
(`_gv_ent_set`) and replay by the record — the same lesson as the stack-param homing entry
above: record where it is decided, do not re-derive.

**Where the rule stops.** After the first top-level statement a `var` is a statement and
redeclaration is lexical shadowing (in-tree tests re-declare a scratch buffer at a new
size). Merging there would change a relied-on semantics; the defect was the declaration
zone. Measure the corpus before widening a fold: a probe that printed every shadowing
registration found 53 in-tree, all same-shape — but it had not instrumented the top-level
array path, and the full `.tcyr` old-vs-new loop is what surfaced the two size-changing
script-zone arrays the first cut (which folded both zones) broke.

**Review round (same release) — three more instances of "resolved by name at the wrong
time".** (a) Turning a deferred store into a static one REORDERS it against every other
startup store: the enum pass-2 init (`PARSE_ENUM_DEF(S, 1)`) runs before the deferred
replay and resolves the variant name last-match, so once a `var` over an enum constant
became static the enum's store overwrote it (`enum E { K = 5; } var K = 7;` read 5 for
every read). Before making anything static, list every OTHER store that can target the
slot and when it runs. (b) Recording the replay's STORE target fixed half of the kernel
case: names the replayed initializer READS still went through FINDVAR after the program
had registered its own globals — the fix ranks those below declaration-zone matches while
the replay runs (`_fv_hidden`). A "record, don't re-derive" fix has to cover every lookup
the replayed code makes, not just the one that motivated it. (c) An opt-out whose hazard
you have just removed is now only a restriction: the v5.11.64 shadow opt-out existed
because the replay re-resolved its store onto the later slot; with recorded slots it only
kept a different-owner shadow on the runtime path, where an earlier read saw 0.
And a real consumer was hit: agnos declares one register index twice with the same value;
in kernel mode the second (deferred) store is never reached, so it read 0 for the life of
the kernel.

**6.7.14 (FE-19) — A PER-SLOT MARK WRITTEN AFTER THE FOLD JUDGES THE WRONG DECLARATION.**
GVBOOL is per slot and was written by `_gv_last_marks` AFTER the redeclaration fold, so a
reader between two declarations was judged by the OTHER declaration's mark: `var G = 5;
var G: bool = G;` built a bool holding 5. Write the incoming mark before `_gv_fold` and
compare it in `_gv_same_shape` — a global redeclared with another bool-ness is now refused
as a type change. Any per-slot fact of a redeclaration is compared AT the fold, never
stamped after it.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "declaration_zone_skip_stops_at_first_semicolon_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius parses a top-level `var` TWICE (pass 1 registers it, pass 2 steps over it) and both passes used to find its end by scanning to the FIRST `;`. That was correct only while no expression could contain a `;` — a premise closures broke in v6.3.7. `var f = |x| { return 7; };` before the first top-level statement therefore stopped BOTH passes inside the closure body, at its `}`, which ends every top-level loop: the rest of the program was silently dropped and the binary exited with the closure's address. Fixed at 6.6.6 by one shared brace-aware skip (`_skip_gvar_decl`) used by PARSE_GVAR_REG's two arms and by all seven `src/main*.cyr` forks."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "declaration-zone", "closures", "token-skip", "silent-failure", "forks", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class, which is the reusable part: a lexical SKIP encodes an assumption about the
grammar, and a later syntax extension can falsify it without touching the skip.** The
skip-to-`;` in `PARSE_GVAR_REG` (and the copy in every fork's pass-2 `var` arm) was written
when a `var` initializer was an expression and an expression could not contain a `;`. Block
closures (v6.3.7) made that false. Nothing failed at the closure — it failed three releases
later, in unrelated code, as "the second half of my file isn't in the binary".

**What made it invisible for so long.** The failure has no error path: the top-level loops
END at `}`, so a skip that stops mid-body looks to both passes like a well-formed end of
input. A program with no `main` exits with the last global's value, so the status was the
closure's ADDRESS (186 = 0x…ba) — a plausible-looking non-zero number, not a crash. And
the two positions people actually test were both fine: an expression-bodied closure
(`|x| x + 1`) carries no `;`, and a closure AFTER a top-level statement is parsed by
`PARSE_PROG` as a statement. `tests/tcyr/lang/closures.tcyr` keeps every closure inside a
fn, so the corpus never covered the broken position at all.

**Two consequences worth separating, because a partial fix hides one of them.** Pass 2
stopping early drops the program; pass 1 stopping early drops the REGISTRATION of everything
below the declaration — globals, structs, enums. Fix only pass 2 and the program compiles
but a `var` declared after the closure does not exist. That asymmetry is why the gate row
for the destructure arm needed a global declared BELOW it to detect a pass-1-only revert:
without it the mutant passed every row.

**Search the shape, not the name.** `grep` for the loop shape — `while (… PEEKT(S) == 5 …)`
— found NINE copies: two in `PARSE_GVAR_REG` (the plain arm and the `var a, b = f();`
destructure arm) and one in each of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks. Seven forks means a
fix applied to six of them is broken on exactly one target, with no local gate to notice;
the gate therefore carries a static parity axis that derives the fork list from
`src/main*.cyr` rather than listing it.

**Only BRACE depth may carry a skip past a `;`.** Counting parens and brackets too looks
more thorough and is worse: a malformed `var x = f(1;` then swallows the rest of the file,
and every diagnostic below it disappears — trading a silent miscompile for a silent
mis-diagnosis. `_prescan_skip_stmt` (6.6.5) tracks all three because it skips STATEMENTS,
where `for (;;)` is legal at paren depth; a DECLARATION never has one.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "unhandled_flag_falls_through_to_compile_stdin_6_6_6"
description = "cycc's fall-through for an invocation it does not recognise is COMPILE STDIN. So a driver missing a `--version` arm does not error — it compiles EMPTY input and writes a runnable binary to stdout with exit 0. Four of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks were in that state at 6.6.6 (`cycc --version` on an ARM install wrote a 65,888-byte ELF; `cycc_cx` wrote 24 bytes of .cyx), and the starship prompt segment `scripts/install.sh` installs runs `cycc --version | awk '{print $2}'`, so inside any cyrius project an ARM or cx install showed a field of that binary as the version in the shell prompt. Same release: `src/main_cx.cyr` predefined no `CYRIUS_TARGET_*` macro at all, so every per-target `#ifdef` arm in the stdlib compiled to NOTHING on cx."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cli", "forks", "silent-failure", "empty-input", "preprocessor", "ifdef", "cx", "macos", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Two different mechanisms, one shape: the failure mode of "unhandled" is SILENT SUCCESS,
so the absence of a feature looks exactly like the feature working.**

**1. An unrecognised flag is not an error — it is an empty compile.** cycc reads stdin and
compiles it; a flag it does not match is simply ignored. With nothing piped in, stdin is
empty, an empty program is valid, and cycc emits a runnable binary and exits 0. Nothing in
the pipeline can tell that apart from a successful `--version`: exit code 0, output on
stdout, no stderr. This is why four forks could go years without the arm. ⚠ The general
rule: when a tool's default action is "do the thing", a missing flag handler is a SILENT
WRONG ACTION, and the gate has to assert the OUTPUT SHAPE (here: no ELF/MZ/Mach-O/CYX magic
on stdout, exactly one line, field 2 == the VERSION file), not just the exit code.

**2. A per-target `#ifdef` stdlib compiles to NOTHING on a target that predefines no
macro.** `lib/alloc.cyr` and `lib/syscalls.cyr` are `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,AGNOS,
LINUX}` arms. `src/main_cx.cyr` predefined only `CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS` — so on cx NO
arm matched, those modules contributed no functions, and `include "lib/assert.cyr"` failed
with `undefined function(s) called (cx backend): alloc, vec_get, alloc_reset, sys_exit`. The
consequence is the one worth remembering: **no `.tcyr` had ever run on that target**, and
the five cx gates that existed all proved codegen SHAPES rather than that the stdlib works.
"Compiles on five targets is not runs on five targets" applies to the STDLIB, not only to
syscall wrappers.

**Why the fork axis has to be DERIVED.** A gate that lists the forks it checks is a gate
that an eighth fork walks straight past. `tests/gates/toolchain/fork_version_parity.sh`
derives the list from `src/main*.cyr` with a floor, and then RUNS each fork's built
compiler where the host can run it — x86 and the host cross stages directly, PE under wine,
aarch64 under qemu. A static grep alone is not enough and the mutation ledger records why:
flipping the `--ve` test byte 118 -> 119 leaves the `_VERSION_STR_` write in the source, so
the grep row stays green while the binary goes back to emitting an ELF.

**macOS forks cannot use the `/proc` arm, and the dead copy looks alive.**
`main_aarch64_macho.cyr` carried a verbatim copy of the Linux `/proc/self/cmdline` scan from
v5.11.63. On Darwin the `open()` fails and returns a POSITIVE errno in rax (no carry check),
so the `if (_vn > 0)` guard written for Linux's negative-errno convention FIRES on the errno
and the walk runs harmlessly over a zeroed buffer — matching nothing, reporting nothing. So
`--strict` and `--allow-undef` had never worked there either. argv on macOS comes from the
entry stack the driver parks (x28 on arm64, r15 on x86 — the base `_read_env` has used since
v6.0.33/v6.4.59); at 6.6.6 that reader moved to `src/common/util.cyr`, because util.cyr is
the ONE module all seven drivers include and `main_cx.cyr` deliberately does not include
`backend/common/runtime.cyr`.

**6.7.14 (PL-9) — the same fall-through, for a flag a fork does not have.** The aarch64
drivers skip any flag they do not know: `--parse-ts` on aarch64 compiles STDIN as cyrius and
exits 0 on valid cyrius. The TS front end was in `src/main.cyr` only, so the aarch64
`ts_test_runner` failed every file. An unknown flag must be refused by name in
every fork, not skipped.

**6.7.15 (PL-13, 6.7.15's D38) — the TS case closed the other way: the forks GAINED the
flags.** `--lex-ts` / `--parse-ts` / `--emit-js` now run on the aarch64 Linux cross and native
compilers, both macOS compilers, `cycc.exe` and its Linux-hosted cross (not `cycc_cx`);
`ts_test_runner` passes on pi. How the move kept `src/main.cyr`'s cycc byte-identical is
patterns.cyml byte_identity_through_a_driver_refactor_needs_the_blocks_own_positions_v6715. The
general rule above still stands for every OTHER flag a fork lacks.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "argv0_is_not_a_self_path_on_windows_6_6_6"
description = "On Linux a tool finds its own install root with readlink(\"/proc/self/exe\"); on macOS with fcntl(F_GETPATH). Windows has neither, and the tempting substitute — argv(0) — is NOT a self path: on Windows argv[0] is whatever the PARENT wrote on the command line, so it can be relative or a bare name resolved through PATH. programs/cyrius-init.cyr derives its templates directory from its own path's GRANDPARENT, so an argv0 fallback yields a directory that does not exist. The real answer is kernel32!GetModuleFileNameW, added at 6.6.6 as PE reroute 0xF03A. The trap is that the argv0 version COMPILES, ships, and passes a --dry-run."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "self-path", "argv0", "packaging", "silent-failure", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class: a portability arm that compiles is not a portability arm that works, and the
cheapest-looking fallback can be wrong in a way no build-time check can see.**

`cyrius-init` did not cross-compile to PE at all until 6.6.6 — `_self_path` reached an
unguarded `sys_readlink` and the port driver an unguarded `sys_rename`, neither defined in
`lib/syscalls_windows.cyr`, so the build refused with two reachable undefined functions and
a 0-byte file. `cyrius init` and `cyrius port` simply did not exist on a Windows install.

The filing offered two repairs. One was the reroute; the other was "fall back to the
`argv(0)` the function already has". **Measured, the argv0 route fails**, and it fails in
the worst possible shape:

  * it compiles clean, so every build gate is green;
  * `--dry-run` passes, because a dry run never resolves the templates;
  * a real `init` launched the way a test launches it — absolute path, cwd next to the
    binary — also passes;
  * and it fails the moment the exe is invoked by a relative path, or off PATH, from some
    other directory. Then the grandparent walk lands somewhere that does not exist and the
    tool scaffolds `error: missing template` for every file.

So the gate (`tests/gates/toolchain/cyrius_init_builds_for_pe.sh`) invokes the binary as
`..\\bin\\cyrius-init.exe` from an unrelated working directory. That one detail is the whole
difference between a gate that proves the fix and a gate that proves the mutant.

**Two reusable mechanics from the reroute itself:**

1. **A wide-string Win32 reroute should return the RAW UTF-16 and let cyrius narrow it.**
   `syscall(0xF006)`/GetCommandLineW already worked this way — `lib/args.cyr`'s
   `_args_w2u8` does the conversion, where it is pure, host-testable and shared. Following
   that split made `EGETMODFILENAME_PE` a plain 3-register Win64 call (rcx=NULL,
   rdx=buffer, r8d=capacity) with no hand-assembled conversion loop at all. Reroutes that
   widen inline (EOPEN_PE, EMOVEFILEEX_PE) each carry ~25 instructions of loop that only
   real Windows can test; prefer the split when the API hands you a buffer.

2. **The PE reroute chain in `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` is at cybs's per-function global/call
   reference limit.** New arms go in their own small helper fn (`_PE_ROUTE_MODULEPATH`,
   next to `_PE_ROUTE_PERFCOUNTER`) with ONE call from the chain. cybs fails SILENTLY past
   that limit and losing seed → cybs → cycc is days of repair.

**And the half nobody lists in an acceptance criterion: the installer.** Shipping the
binary in the tarball was not enough — `scripts/install.ps1` copied only `bin\\` and
`lib\\`, so `programs/cyrius-init-templates` would have reached the tarball and stopped
there, and the binary would have shipped, run, and reported `missing template` for
everything. When a tool resolves data relative to its own install root, the packaging
question is not "is the binary in the tarball" but "does the installer put the DATA beside
the binary, at every level the binary can be invoked from" — here both `versions\\<v>\\`
and the active home, because Windows copies rather than symlinks.

**Postscript, and it is the same class as the `-1` fork stub in the next entry: the first
cut of this fix also added `sys_readlink` to the Windows peer as an honest `-38`/ENOSYS,
and review removed it.** With the scaffolder's call moved behind `#ifndef
CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, the stub was measured to change nothing about the PE build — same rc,
same `MZ`, same 155,648 bytes, zero undefined functions — so it bought no capability. What
it cost was the only thing that made this bug findable: the v6.3.2 reachable-undef HARD
ERROR. `readlink("/proc/self/exe")` is the precise shape of this entire filing, and with a
stub present that line COMPILES for PE and then silently degrades to argv0 — the exact
half-fix the gate's relative-path axes exist to catch, waved through one step earlier.
**Define a POSIX name for a target only where the definition is USED; a stub whose only
effect is to let the wrong code build is a diagnostic you deleted.** Callers that genuinely
want a degrade have the portable façade (`lib/io.cyr`'s `xreadlink`, `-1` behind its own
`#ifdef`); the gate now pins the ABSENCE, with the mutation "put the stub back" reddening
it while leaving every build axis green.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_minus_one_fork_stub_succeeds_wrongly_on_pe_6_6_6"
description = "lib/syscalls_windows.cyr defines sys_fork and sys_waitpid as `return 0 - 1` WRAPPERS, not as unrouted syscalls returning -ENOSYS. So a POSIX fork/wait on PE does not FAIL, it SUCCEEDS WRONGLY: pid is -1, the parent takes the `pid != 0` branch, sys_waitpid answers -1 without touching the status buffer, and the caller decodes an UNINITIALISED STACK SLOT as the child's exit status. Whenever that garbage reads as WIFEXITED with status 0, the code reports success for a child that never existed. Measured on real cass at 6.6.5: `cyrius self` printed its header and exited 0 having compiled nothing, and stayed exit 0 with the compiler source deleted. The cyrius-guide had written this down as safe — \"code paths that are dead on Windows (e.g. POSIX fork/execve) can compile without routing them\" — and that sentence licensed twelve unguarded sites in cbt/."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "fork", "waitpid", "silent-failure", "uninitialised", "per-target-source", "gate-design", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class: a stub that returns a sentinel is not a stub that stops you.** -ENOSYS from an
unrouted `syscall(n, ...)` is loud enough to notice; a wrapper that returns `-1` is not,
because the POSIX idiom around fork does not test the pid before using it. `pid == 0` is
the CHILD test, and `-1` is not 0, so the parent branch runs. `sys_waitpid` then returns -1
having written nothing, and `WIFEXITED(load32(&status_buf))` reads whatever was on the
stack. The failure is not a crash and not an error code — it is a *plausible verdict*.

Two lessons that generalise past Windows:

1. **Do not reason about a platform arm from the syscall table.** "That number is not
   routed, so the path is dead there" is only true when the call site really reaches the
   raw `syscall()`. Everything in `lib/syscalls_<target>.cyr` is a named wrapper that may
   answer with a value. Grep for the WRAPPER, not the number.

2. **A per-target verb must use the per-target SOURCE.** The Windows arm of `cyrius self`
   first self-hosted from `src/main.cyr`, the Linux fork, because that is what the POSIX
   shell script names. `cycc.exe` compiles it happily — the frontend does not care — and
   produces a PE built from Linux syscall numbers, which page-faults the instant step two
   executes it (`Unhandled page fault on write access ... at 0000000140001060` under wine).
   The right source is `src/main_win.cyr`. The same rule the cross-OS legs already follow
   for ecb (`src/main_aarch64_macho.cyr`).

**And the gate-design half.** A "no silent nothing" property is satisfied by
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN return 0; #endif`, which is *the defect*, reading greener than
the bug. So `tests/gates/platform/cbt_fork_sites_have_pe_arm.sh` derives the fork-site list
from `cbt/*.cyr` (a new site cannot be added without an arm), requires each arm to NAME
what it does, and — the half that turned out to matter — requires every `fn _win_*` DEFINED
in `cbt/` to be REACHABLE in the cross-built PE binary, from cycc's own DCE listing. The
arm-derived list cannot see a helper go dead, because an arm that stops calling its helper
drops the name from that list; a mutation that replaced a spawn call with a constant read
GREEN until the definition-derived half existed.

⚠ Its own detector had two defects that each made a mutant pass: an awk comment strip
(`sub(/#[^i].*/, "", code)`) ate `#endif` as a comment, so every arm stayed open and the
scan found 1 site of 17; and a shell glob `*_win_*` matched a LOCAL named `cap_win_rc`,
admitting an emptied arm. Both were found only because the mutation ledger was actually
run. Match a helper as a CALL (`name` followed by `(`), never as a substring.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_guard_inside_one_copier_covers_only_that_copier"
description = "v6.5.21 closed the `#@file` forgery hole INSIDE PP_PASS's copy loop; three other routes carried source bytes to the preprocessor output and stayed open for four minors (CYRIUS-2026-0007). Where a defence goes decides what it covers — put it at the ENTRY POINT of the untrusted bytes, not in one of the things that moves them."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE HOLE. `private` visibility is decided from the preprocessor's
`#@file "NAME" BASE` markers, and `FM_BUILD` accepts one at ANY offset —
no byte-0 rule, no beginning-of-line rule, unlike `#@incdir`. So any
bytes that reach the preprocessor's output can mint a file span and claim
to be another file, which is a direct route to that file's private items.

v6.5.21 saw that and fixed it — inside `PP_PASS`'s copy loop, with a
careful comment explaining that the loop was the right place because it
is the one that knows about string literals. The reasoning was sound and
the code was correct. It covered one route of four.

MEASURED AT 6.6.5, each one a clean build and `exit 42` where the honest
program is refused with `'SECRET_ADD' is private to its file`:

  1. an INCLUDED file  — `READFILE` writes it STRAIGHT into `out`, in
     both passes. It never meets the loop at all.
  2. a `#define` macro BODY — the `#define` line is consumed by the
     directive handler (so it never meets the loop either) and the stored
     body is written out later by `PP_EXPAND`.
  3. a `#derive` line's TAIL — `PP_COPY_TAIL` copies it verbatim.

⚠ THE PASS THAT DOES (2) CARRIED A 17-LINE COMMENT HEADED "v6.5.21 —
NEUTRALISE a user-authored `#@file` marker", describing in detail a guard
that had never been in it. A reader auditing the route would have read
that and moved on. A comment that describes a defence is not a defence,
and a copied comment is the cheapest way to make a hole look closed.

THE FIX SHAPE. Neutralise at the ENTRY POINTS — one pass over the raw
source before anything reads it, plus one call at each include's
`READFILE` — instead of at one of the places that move bytes around.
There are two ways into the pipeline and four ways through it; guarding
the two is complete by construction, guarding one of the four is not.
The v6.5.21 inline guard was REMOVED rather than left alongside: two
mechanisms for one invariant is how the first came to be believed
complete (the same failure the `source_marked` one-shot caused for the
file map at v6.5.3).

MECHANICAL NOTE worth copying: the neutraliser OVERWRITES the `@` with a
space rather than inserting a byte, so the region keeps its LENGTH and no
line or column shifts. The v6.5.21 version inserted, and had to carry a
caveat about shifted columns inside comments.

**6.6.14: THREE MORE, ONE SHAPE — a protection placed in one of the two routes through a verb,
the plain one and the deadline's.** The native SIGPIPE fix had two write loops to cover (the
plain one and `_tn_nb_write_all`); a mutant reverting only the deadline loop failed only the
deadline rows. The libssl sticky-error wrapper was missing from the deadline branch
(`_tls_ssl_io`) and read GREEN until every row was re-run with a deadline set. The libssl
SIGPIPE hold's first live suite reached neither the deadline loop nor the 0-RTT pair — twelve
call sites in all, pinned only when spies stood in for every `SSL_*` pointer. Fix shape each
time: the protection lives on the LEAF every route shares (`_tn_os_write`, `_tls_ssl_call`), and
each test row runs once per route.

[[entries]]
name = "a_degrade_becomes_a_permission_once_rules_are_written_around_it_6_6_6"
description = "lib/io.cyr's xrmdir returned -1 on PE for four minors because no RemoveDirectoryW reroute was wired, and the -1 was documented as an honest degrade. By 6.6.6 THREE separate rules had been written around it: a crossos test asserted its own xrmdir only `if (rd == 0)`, the same test kept a FIXED directory name under #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN because a per-pid one could never be cleaned up, and a tree-write gate carried a `win_guarded` allowlist RULE whose stated reason was the gap. Each rule made the gap cheaper to live with and the fix look bigger; the one target where the call could fail was the one target not asserting it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "rmdir", "degrade", "allowlist", "gate-design", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class.** A `-1` / no-op degrade on one target is honest exactly while nothing depends
on the call succeeding. The moment something does — here `_cbt_tmpdir_cleanup`, which had
to remove the CLI's private temp directory — the degrade is a defect, and it has usually
been a defect for a while without anybody noticing, because the workarounds accumulated
quietly and each one looked local and reasonable.

Watch for these three shapes; they are what a live degrade grows:

1. **A conditional assertion.** `var rd = xrmdir(d); if (rd == 0) { assert_eq(...); }` —
   written so the one target that cannot succeed does not turn the suite red. It also
   means the only target where the call can fail is the only one not asking, so the gap is
   structurally invisible to the test that covers it.
2. **A per-target fixture.** The same test kept a FIXED directory name on PE while every
   other target used a per-pid one, because a per-pid name that can never be removed turns
   one reused leftover into one per run. Perfectly sound reasoning — about a defect.
3. **An allowlist RULE, not just an entry.** A gate grew a whole `win_guarded` rule
   (detector, self-test, ledger row) whose written justification was the missing reroute.
   A rule is more durable than the defect it excuses: entries get re-checked for staleness,
   rules get re-used.

**The fix is all four places, in one go.** Wiring the reroute (`syscall(0xF03A)` →
kernel32!RemoveDirectoryW, an exact copy of `EDELETEFILEW_PE` with a different import —
same 1-argument shape, same inline UTF-8→UTF-16 widener, same rbx-anchored `and rsp,-16`
frame, same BOOL→0/-1) is the small part. Deleting the three permissions is the part that
stops it coming back, and it is the part a reviewer will not ask for, because each one
reads as a sensible local accommodation.

⚠ When a fix removes a carve-out, delete the RULE, not only the entry. A rule with no
users is a standing permission waiting for its next user.

**Measured, so the premise is not a story.** On real cass (Windows 11) the pre-fix CLI
took `%TEMP%`'s `cyrius-*` count from 6 to 8 over five verbs and the fixed one left it at
6; the crossos test went 6/7-with-one-skipped to 7/7 with nothing left behind. A gate row
that only asserts "no directory was left" is satisfied by a CLI that never made one, so
the companion row probes the primitive directly — five `xrmdir` behaviours, each its own
exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "grep_the_shape_not_the_name_a_rule_applied_inline_once_is_the_tell_6_6_6"
description = "A filing named ONE defective predicate (#io matched as a byte prefix, so `#ioctl numbers` was parsed as code). Fixing the ten it listed left NINE more instances of the same shape in the same subsystem, found only across three review rounds: PP_IS_HOST_ONLY, three ISDERIVE* probes, a fourth reader in cyrius_api_surface.cyr, and ISENDIF/ISENDPLAT/ISSRCLINE — the last of which let a COMMENT close an #ifdef so that skipped code was compiled in silently. ISELSE had carried the boundary inline all along, which was the tell that the invariant was already known and applied exactly once."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "preprocessor", "word-boundary", "audit-method", "review", "silent-failure", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The method.** When a filing names a defective predicate, the name is a sample, not the
population. Grep the SHAPE — here "a `#`-directive name compared byte by byte and then
acted on, with nothing checking that the name ENDED". One `grep -n '^fn IS'` over
`src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`, printing each predicate's LAST byte comparison, separated the
bounded ones (the comparison is `!= 32`, a space, so the name ends by construction) from
the unbounded ones in about a minute. That one command found three defects three review
rounds of prose had not.

**⭐ A rule applied INLINE exactly once is the strongest tell there is.** `ISELSE` carried
this, in the source, since long before 6.6.6:

    # Terminator: whitespace, newline, or EOF. Reject identifier
    # continuation so "#elseif" (hypothetical typo) doesn't match.

Someone understood the invariant, wrote it out, applied it to the predicate in front of
them, and did not generalise. Everywhere you find a hand-written guard with a comment
explaining WHY it is needed, ask which siblings did not get it. That comment is a defect
report about its neighbours.

**Severity inverts as you go down the pipeline.** In the LEXER the defect was loud — a
comment failed to compile with a diagnostic pointing at its second word. In the
PREPROCESSOR the same shape is silent and worse:

- `#host_onlyish note` marked a module host-only and refused every bare-metal build
- `#derive(Serialize)x note` ARMED the derive machinery: 4472 B vs the twin's 4456 B, rc=0
- `#endifoo note` CLOSED a conditional, so code inside a skipped `#ifdef` was compiled in
- `#@srclinex 10` shifted every diagnostic in the file by one line

So "the compiler errors, so we would have noticed" does not transfer from one pass to the
next. It is a property of where the predicate sits, not of the bug.

**Score the comment against its SPACED TWIN.** Never ask the compiler what a comment should
produce. Compile `# ioctl numbers` — which no prefix rule can ever read as a directive —
and require the unspaced form to produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL binary. The oracle is a different
program, and it catches the case where both forms compile but to different bytes, which is
precisely the silent half. Pair every such axis with an ARM axis: the wrong fix is "make
every `#` a comment", and it passes every comment axis.

**One deliberate exception, written down.** `#assert(8 == 9)` is rejected out loud today
(`#assert: expected constant expression`). Making `(` a non-boundary there would have made
it a silent comment — a DROPPED compile-time assertion. It keeps its paren boundary, and a
gate axis pins the exception so a later "tidy up the special cases" pass cannot quietly
remove it. A loud error on rare prose beats a silent hole in a check.

**6.7.14 — THE SAME SHAPE IN THE ASSEMBLER (IN-14, D16).** Every `ASM_MNEMONIC` arm compared
an `L64` of the name MASKED TO ITS LENGTH — a prefix match — so `clix` assembled as `cli`
for years (and `inb` was the only way to reach `ASM_IN`): a misspelt privileged instruction
emitted a different one, silently. Mask through the NUL (len + 1 bytes) for a whole-name
compare; an unknown mnemonic is now refused by name, as the aarch64 backend already did.
The keyword half (`in` never reached the dispatch) is in
ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714 #4.

**6.7.14 — A CENSUS OF WRAPPERS IS A CENSUS OF NAMES (LIB-5).** 6.7.12's D13 made the
pointer-taking WRAPPERS honest on cx (`cyr_mmap`, `sys_pipe`, `sys_fstat`, `sys_unlink`);
`mmap_stack` (lib/thread.cyr) and `_fl_mmap` (lib/freelist.cyr) issued `syscall(SYS_MMAP, …)`
DIRECTLY and on cx handed back a host address as success. When a fix is "every path that
does X", grep the raw number and the `SYS_` name too, not only the wrappers.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_test_whose_oracle_lives_in_the_file_the_defect_damages_cannot_fail_6_6_6"
description = "The preprocessor's macro pass expanded a macro name inside a STRING LITERAL, rewriting the program's own data. The obvious test — assert_streq(lit, \"MIB(5) text\") — passes on the BROKEN compiler, because the expected literal sits in the same file and is mangled identically. Every oracle for a defect that damages source text has to be a control the defect CANNOT reach: a different spelling, a different file, or a value the program computes at runtime. The first cut of this coverage was a .tcyr whose binaries came out byte-identical from the fixed and unfixed compiler, and it took a deliberate old-compiler run to notice."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "macro", "testing", "vacuous-gate", "oracle", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The trap.** A defect that rewrites SOURCE TEXT damages the test's expected value as
readily as it damages the thing under test. `#define ID(a) (a)` plus

    assert_streq(lit, "ID(5) literal")

is green on the broken compiler: both strings are mangled to `"(5) literal"` and they still
match. The assertion is not wrong, it is UNREACHABLE — there is no compiler for which it
fails.

**The rule.** The oracle must be something the defect cannot touch:

- a CONTROL SPELLING the predicate cannot match (`XIB(5) text` against `MIB(5) text`, same
  length, not a macro) — then compare LENGTHS, not contents
- a TWIN PROGRAM with the trigger renamed, required to compile BYTE-IDENTICALLY
- a value the program derives at RUNTIME (a `while (load8(s + n) != 0)` loop), never a
  number written into the test

**And always run the unfixed compiler against the new test.** `CYRIUS_CC=<old> sh
<gate>` is two seconds and it is the only thing that distinguishes "my fix works" from "my
test cannot fail". Here it turned a 7/7 green .tcyr into an obviously vacuous one: the
fixed and unfixed compilers produced the SAME BYTES for it, which no correct test of a
codegen-visible fix ever does.

**A corollary about mutation ledgers.** Mutating the FIX is not enough on its own —
every mutant is built from a tree that still contains the rest of the change. Run the
whole pre-fix compiler too, and record what it scores (here 4 of 7 axes red); that is the
one run that proves the axes are about the defect and not about the shape of the patch.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_safety_argument_about_what_an_operation_writes_says_nothing_about_what_it_reads_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's macro pass expands inside `#` comments. The written justification for that — 'a #define body cannot contain a newline, so an expansion inside a comment cannot change the emitted binary' — was reasoned about, believed, and copied into six places in one release. It is a claim about the bytes an expansion WRITES. The bytes it READS were unbounded: PP_EXPAND scanned to the matching `)` wherever that was, so `# TODO: fix M(` swallowed every statement up to the next `)` and the program compiled to exit 0 with an empty stderr. Fixed by BOUNDING the scan to the comment's line, never by excluding comments — the exclusion regresses attribute lines, which the state machine also reads as comments."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "macro", "safety-argument", "unbounded-scan", "doc-rot", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** Any transform has two sides: what it EMITS and what it CONSUMES. A safety
argument that only covers one side reads as a complete argument, and it is the kind of
wrong that survives review — the sentence is *true*, it just does not cover the case.

    "a `#define` body cannot contain a newline, so an expansion inside a
     comment cannot change the emitted binary"

Every clause of that is correct about emission. Consumption was never mentioned, and
consumption was unbounded:

    #define M(a) 0
    # TODO: fix M(
    var r = 42;
    var t = 1;
    syscall(60, r + t);        → exit 0, empty stderr. 43 is correct.

**How to check one.** For a transform, ask the two questions separately: *what can this
write?* and *how far can it read?* If the answer to the second is "to the next delimiter",
name the delimiter and ask what happens when it is missing, or when it is somewhere the
author did not mean. Here the answer — "to the matching `)`, anywhere in the file" — makes
the defect obvious in one sentence.

**BOUND, do not EXCLUDE.** The reflex fix is "then do not expand inside comments at all".
Measured, that regresses real code: the preprocessor's lexical state has no notion of
ATTRIBUTES, so `#inline fn f(): i64 { return N(5); }` is "a comment" to it and that macro
legitimately expands. Telling the two apart needs a list of attribute names kept in sync
with the lexer's if-chain, and the direction that list fails in re-opens the defect. The
narrow rule — *an expansion that starts in a comment must also END in it* — needs no list
and leaves the legitimate case alone. The over-correction is worth a mutation of its own
in the gate; here it is the one mutant that turns exactly the attribute axis red.

**And a note on where the claim lived.** It was in a source comment, a gate header, the
gate's registration string, the CHANGELOG, the language guide and the doc-health ledger —
six copies, all written in the same release, all propagated from the first one. Re-reading
any of them would never have caught it, because they agreed. Only running the shape did.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_that_discovers_loops_by_shape_judges_the_fn_you_inline_into_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6's self_host_src_per_target.sh DISCOVERS self-host loops instead of naming them: any fn that both asks `_self_host_src()` and runs something, and it then requires that fn to be one of two shapes — ask and name no fork, or pin build/cycc. Bite 24b fixed `cyrius capacity`'s per-target default by inlining `var s = _self_host_src(); if (file_exists(s)) ... elif (file_exists(\"src/main.cyr\")) ...` into cbt/cyrius.cyr's main(). main() also runs a compiler (the pinned-toolchain re-exec), so the change made main() a self-host loop that both asks AND hard-codes a fork, and a gate from an adjacent bite went red on a correct behavioural fix. The resolution belongs in a named helper; the helper is load-bearing, not tidiness."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "cbt", "self-host", "shape-detection", "per-target", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** A gate that finds its subjects by NAME can only ever judge what it was told
about; a gate that finds them by SHAPE judges everything that fits the shape — including
code you add later for an unrelated reason. That is the whole point of the shape-detector,
and it is also its one surprise: *where you put a line changes whether a gate is about it.*

The detector (`self_host_src_per_target.sh` axis 6) admits a fn when

    (asks _self_host_src()  AND  runs something)   OR   (names a fork  AND  compares bytes)

and then demands one of two legitimate shapes:

  (a) it ASKS and writes no fork path of its own — the loop runs THIS HOST's compiler;
  (b) it is pinned to `build/cycc` — the tracked x86-64 Linux compiler, whose own fork IS
      `src/main.cyr`, so naming that fork is correct and ASKING would be wrong.

`main()` in `cbt/cyrius.cyr` runs a compiler — the `_try_redirect_to_pinned()` re-exec —
so the moment a per-target default was inlined into it, it satisfied the left-hand prong
and failed both shapes at once: it asked, and it named `src/main.cyr` as a fallback. The
behaviour was right. The placement was not.

**Why the fallback could not just be dropped.** `capacity`'s literals are not a leftover:
a NON-cyrius project has no per-target fork, so on ARM the host-fork test simply misses and
`src/main.cyr` / `src/lib.cyr` must still answer. Deleting them to satisfy the gate would
have broken `cyrius capacity` for every ARM and macOS project — a far bigger blast radius
than the bug. The mutation that deletes them is in the bite's own gate for that reason.

**The rule.** When a per-target question needs both an ASK and a literal fallback, it goes
in a fn of its own that asks and returns — no execs, no verdicts — so the shape-detector
classifies it as a resolver rather than a loop. Check the neighbouring gates BEFORE
choosing where to put the code, not after the commit: this was caught by running
`self_host_src_per_target.sh` one bite later, and it had already shipped in between.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_reroute_existing_is_not_a_reroute_being_used_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: cycc.exe saw NONE of its 20 CYRIUS_* knobs because _read_env fell through to `return 0` on PE — while the GetEnvironmentVariableA reroute it needed had existed since v6.0.85 and the comment above it still said the Windows lookup was 'queued for v5.5.x tail'. The stdlib hit the SAME shape at v6.5.45, that half was fixed, and the compiler's own copy one call away was not. The import table is the check that cannot be fooled."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "env", "audit-by-class", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** A backend reroute is *capability*, not *use*. `syscall(0xF015)` →
`GetEnvironmentVariableA` has been emittable since v6.0.85, and for the whole of that time
`_read_env` in `src/backend/common/runtime.cyr` — the compiler's own environment reader —
ended its Linux and macOS arms and fell straight to `return 0` on PE. So `cycc.exe` could
not see `CYRIUS_STATS`, `CYRIUS_DCE`, `CYRIUS_SYMS`, `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH` or any of the other
20 names, and every one of them failed the way a knob you did not set fails: silently, with
the default.

**Why nobody noticed.** The comment directly above the function said the Windows lookup was
"queued for v5.5.x tail" — written before v6.0.85, never revisited after it, and therefore
read for five minors as a statement about the present. A stale *plan* comment is worse than
no comment: it answers the question you were about to investigate.

**⭐ The class, which is the actual lesson.** `lib/io.cyr`'s `getenv` hit this EXACT defect at
v6.5.45 and its fix note says so in as many words: *"Windows has the reroute but NOTHING IN
THE STDLIB CALLED IT."* That fix stopped at the stdlib. The compiler's own copy of the same
mechanism, in the same repository, one call away, stayed broken for another minor — because
the two were investigated as two bugs rather than as one class. When you find "the primitive
exists, the caller never called it" anywhere, the next move is to grep every OTHER caller of
that primitive in the tree before closing the bite. Same family as the macOS `_read_env` gap
that caused the v5.3.13→v6.0.32 self-host rot.

**The check that cannot be fooled.** Do not grep the source for the arm and do not `strings`
the binary — cycc carries the literal `"GetEnvironmentVariableA"` inside its own
reroute-warning text whether or not it imports it, so a strings scan reads 2 hits on the
BROKEN compiler. Read the PE **import directory**:

    objdump -x cycc.exe | sed -n '/DLL Name/,$p' | grep -c GetEnvironmentVariableA
    # 0 on 6.6.5, 1 on 6.6.6

An import entry only exists if the emitter actually emitted a call, so this distinguishes
"the code is written" from "the code is reached" — which a source grep cannot.

**Scope correction worth keeping.** The filing assumed `cyrius capacity` was dead on Windows
"for the same reason". It is not: `cmd_capacity` spawns the compiler with `sys_fork()` +
`/bin/sh -c`, and `sys_fork` returns **-1** on PE. Wiring `_read_env` changes nothing there.
Verified by building `cyrius.exe` and running the verb: `error: no stats output from
compiler`, with the fixed compiler in place. Two defects that produce one symptom.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cmd_set_var_1_ampersand_puts_the_space_in_the_value_6_6_6"
description = "Verifying an env-gated compiler knob on cass: `cmd /c \"set VAR=1 & prog\"` sets VAR to \"1 \" WITH the trailing space. cyrius knobs test load8(v)==49 && load8(v+1)==0, so the value fails to match and a correctly fixed binary reads exactly like the unfixed one. Write `set VAR=1&& prog` — no space before the ampersands."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "cass", "verification", "cmd", "gotcha", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The trap.** `cmd.exe`'s `set` takes everything to the end of the token as the value,
including whitespace that looks like it belongs to the shell:

    cmd /c "set CYRIUS_STATS=1 & prog"     REM -> CYRIUS_STATS = "1 "   (trailing space!)
    cmd /c "set CYRIUS_STATS=1&& prog"     REM -> CYRIUS_STATS = "1"

**Why it bites here specifically.** Every `CYRIUS_*` knob in the compiler is tested as
`load8(v) == 49 && load8(v + 1) == 0` — the byte `'1'` followed immediately by NUL. `"1 "`
fails the second half. So the variable IS set, `GetEnvironmentVariableA` DOES return it, and
the knob still does nothing.

**Why that is expensive.** It is indistinguishable, from the outside, from the bug you are
there to verify. During bite 18b's hardware check on cass the first run of a correctly fixed
`cycc.exe` produced a zero-byte stderr — exactly what the broken 6.6.5 binary produces —
and the reading "still broken on hardware" was one step away from being written down. The
same command with `&&` and no space printed the full meter block.

This sits alongside the other two cass gotchas already in CLAUDE.md
(`cmd /c "x & echo %errorlevel%"` lies because `%errorlevel%` expands at parse time; a
`prog < in > out 2>nul &` chain corrupts the redirect). All three are the same underlying
thing: **cmd.exe parses the whole line before running any of it**, so anything that looks
like shell punctuation may already have been folded into a value or an expansion.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_pass_that_reads_a_buffer_the_previous_pass_stopped_copying_back_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's preprocessor passes share two buffers under a documented 'write to preprocess_out, copy BACK to input_buf' contract. PP_IFDEF_PASS dropped the copy-back for a good reason and said so in its own header; PP_MACRO_PASS still read input_buf. Result: one function-like #define anywhere in a file put stripped #ifdef arms back into the build and truncated the source at 1 MB. The dropped-contract pass and the assuming pass were 50 lines apart and each was individually correct."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "buffers", "lex_pp", "pipeline", "gotcha", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape, which is the reusable part.** A pipeline stage is optimised to skip a copy that
the pipeline's contract requires. The optimisation is correct *for that stage* — nothing it
produces is wrong — and its header documents the deviation honestly. A LATER stage, written
earlier or by someone reading a different file, still relies on the contract. Neither stage
is wrong in isolation; the pipeline is.

**The instance.** `src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`. `PREPROCESS` runs PP_PASS -> PP_IFDEF_PASS ->
PP_MACRO_PASS. The heap map (`src/main.cyr`) says of input_buf@0x7400000: *"Passes write to
preprocess_out and copy BACK here."* PP_IFDEF_PASS's own header says the opposite, with a
reason: *"NO copy-back to S+0 at the end — LEX reads from S+0x459D000, and PP_MACRO_PASS
short-circuits for programs without macros."* That last clause is the whole bug. It is true
only while `_pp_macro_count == 0`, which is the pass's entry condition — so the moment a file
contains ONE function-like `#define`, PP_MACRO_PASS reads input_buf (PP_IFDEF_PASS's INPUT,
truncated to its 1 MB helper window) and writes it over preprocess_out. The `#ifdef` filtering
is undone and everything past 1 MB is gone.

**What it looked like from outside.** Nothing to do with macros:

    #define M(a) (a)              <- never used
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    -> error: lib/atomic.cyr:94:20: unexpected identifier 'x0'

`x0` is an aarch64 register inside `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`, in an x86 build. With
`lib/assert.cyr` instead it was `expected '}', got end of file` at a line number from the
wrong buffer. An OBJECT-like `#define` was fine — it never sets `_pp_macro_count`. So the
feature worked in every small test that had no `#ifdef` beneath it, and the failure was
content-dependent on which stdlib you happened to include.

**How to recognise it again.** When a stage documents "I skip X because the next stage
doesn't need it", the claim is about the NEXT STAGE'S CODE, not about this stage's, and it
rots the moment that code changes — or, as here, the moment its short-circuit does not fire.
Grep the consumer before believing the comment. The fix is one call (`PP_SYNC_SRCB`), placed
behind the consumer's own short-circuit so a program with no function-like macro pays nothing.

**The neighbouring lesson, recorded because it cost time.** The gate for this is a
BYTE-FOR-BYTE binary differential: the same program, with and without an unused
function-like `#define`, must compile to identical bytes. That axis needs no knowledge of
which stdlib file carries an `#ifdef`, and it survives the stdlib changing. A row that greps
for `unexpected identifier 'x0'` only works until atomic.cyr is rewritten.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_fields_of_a_foreign_api_constrain_each_other_and_the_emulator_does_not_enforce_it_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6 mapped POSIX open() flags onto CreateFileW by deriving dwDesiredAccess and dwCreationDisposition INDEPENDENTLY — a table per field, which is how such a map is naturally written. Win32 ties them: TRUNCATE_EXISTING is refused with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER unless the access word carries GENERIC_WRITE (FILE_WRITE_DATA does NOT satisfy it). wine does not enforce that rule, so the gate's wine axis read 30/30 while real Windows ran 25/30 — inside a bite whose whole subject was Windows file semantics."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "createfilew", "wine", "cross-os", "translation-table", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** When you translate one API's argument onto another's, the natural structure is
one mapping table per field. That structure silently assumes the target's fields are
independent. A foreign API's fields very often are not, and the coupling is not in the
argument's own documentation — it is a sentence under the *other* argument.

Measured on real cass (Win 10.0.26200), `CreateFileW` with an existing file:

    acc=GENERIC_READ                    disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_READ|FILE_WRITE_DATA    disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_READ|FILE_APPEND_DATA   disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_WRITE                   disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> OK, size 0
    acc=GENERIC_READ                    disp=CREATE_ALWAYS      -> OK, TRUNCATED to 0

Note the last row: `CREATE_ALWAYS` truncates happily with no write access at all, so the
coupling is not even uniform across the dispositions — exactly one of them carries it. And
`FILE_WRITE_DATA`, the minimal bit you would reach for, does not satisfy it; kernel32 tests
for `GENERIC_WRITE` itself.

**What it cost in cyrius.** `open(p, O_RDONLY|O_TRUNC)` and `open(p, O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC|O_APPEND)`
— both `O_TRUNC` WITHOUT `O_CREAT`, i.e. both `TRUNCATE_EXISTING` — returned fd = -1 on
Windows where Linux truncates. The fix is one rule, not two special cases: when the ladder
selects `TRUNCATE_EXISTING`, force `GENERIC_WRITE` into the access word.

**⭐ The part worth carrying to any other target.** *wine accepted all three refused forms.*
The local behaviour gate passed the broken compiler 30/30 under wine; real cass ran 25/30.
An emulator implements the *operations*; the cross-field VALIDATION is precisely the kind of
thing it skips, because skipping it makes more programs work. So for a foreign-API argument
map, a green emulator run is weaker evidence than usual — weaker than it is for codegen,
where wine at least executes the same instructions. Two practical consequences:

1. **Probe the target API directly before writing the map.** A PowerShell `Add-Type`
   P/Invoke matrix over the argument pairs costs one SSH round-trip and produces a table you
   can paste into the emit function's comment. Deriving the map from documentation and then
   testing it under an emulator produces a map that is wrong in exactly the places the
   documentation is thin.
2. **Give the shape axis a signature for the coupling.** cyrius's
   `pe_open_flag_translation.sh` now greps the disassembly for `or $0x40000000,%r9d` and
   requires it once per open reroute — an emitter-shape check is an odd thing to lean on,
   but when the behaviour axis structurally cannot see a defect class, the shape axis is the
   only LOCAL guard, and the mutation ledger says so in the row where the mutant passes wine
   and fails hardware.

**6.7.12 — FOUR MORE, each a wine pass and a real-Windows fail.** (1) Byte locks: `LockFileEx` locks are
MANDATORY on Windows, and wine does not enforce a byte lock against another handle's reads and writes — the
sentinel-at-offset-0 mutant of the `xflock` emulation passes the `.tcyr` under wine and fails 5 rows on cass; only
the gate's static axis catches it locally (the mutation ledger records the wine-passes row). (2) wine maps POSIX
absolute paths onto the Unix host through Z:, so a fixture that writes `/tmp/x` or reads `/etc/passwd` passes under
wine and fails on Windows (seven cass corpus failures were /tmp fixtures; pwd_grp passed under wine and died
0xC0000005 on cass). (3) wine's crypt32 has no CurrentUser\\ROOT store in a default prefix and does not keep
root-program properties (84 / 104 / 126) on memory stores — never a reference for trust-store rows. (4) wine
enforces no MAX_PATH, so a long-path row is smoke there. The rule stays: wine is a pre-sort, never a verdict.
**6.7.13 — A FIFTH: a lock HAND-OFF race.** The kernel grants a pending `LockFileEx` waiter AT an unlock —
deterministic on real Windows, only sometimes under wine: 6.7.12's `xflock` re-lock arm (unlock, then re-take)
failed the re-lock `.tcyr`'s threaded rows on 4 of 4 cass runs and on 1 of 4 wine runs. A wine pass proves nothing
about lock-handoff races; the deterministic local guard is the static axis (windows_flock_relock_and_lock_state_in_
the_kernel_v6713).
**6.7.14 — A SIXTH: CREATE_NEW OVER A DIRECTORY (PL-3).** Real Windows 11 answers ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED; wine answers
ERROR_FILE_EXISTS. The triage planned to delete `lib/io.cyr`'s Windows exclusive-create classification as dead once open
said -17 (under wine); on cass that deletion turned `file_create_exclusive(dir)` into -13, and the atomic writer failed
on a directory planted at its next temp name. The arm now classifies -13 by asking the name. A plain `CreateFileW` of a
directory without FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS is ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED too. Code that looks dead under wine is not dead.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_integrity_check_that_is_conditional_on_an_input_you_never_ship_verifies_nothing_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's macOS install pillars scp'd a release tarball to a real Mac and ran the real install.sh against it for 30+ releases without ever verifying a hash. install.sh DOES verify a local tarball — fail-closed, on a mismatch it aborts — but only IF a `.sha256` sidecar sits beside the file. The builders wrote the sidecar; the scp never copied it. Both gates were green the whole time, and the Intel-Mac pillar had verified a hash on no release since it was added."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["install", "checksum", "cross-os", "macos", "gates", "fail-open", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape, and why it survives review.** An integrity check written as

    if [ -f "$ARTIFACT.sha256" ]; then verify_or_abort; fi

reads as *strict* — the body is fail-closed, aborts on a mismatch, refuses when no
SHA-256 tool is present. Every line you look at is correct. The defect is not in the
branch; it is that **nothing on the calling side guarantees the branch is ever taken.**
A caller that does not ship the sidecar silently selects the no-verification path, and
there is no output to notice: a skipped optional check prints nothing, so a verified
install and an unverified one differ only by one line that is *absent*.

**What it cost here.** `_cross_os_selfhost()` (cbt/commands.cyr) carries the ecb
(Apple Silicon, v6.0.38) and ach (Intel, v6.4.59) install pillars inline — the gates whose
entire purpose is "the published artifact installs on a real Mac". Both `scp`'d the
tarball alone. Measured at 6.6.6 on both real Macs, with a tarball one byte longer than
its sidecar says:

  * sidecar shipped -> `checksum mismatch … aborting`, rc 1, nothing unpacked
  * sidecar NOT shipped -> **zero** mentions of a checksum, and `binaries installed`
    **from the tampered file** before an unrelated later failure

The sidecar had been sitting in the build output directory the whole time
(`sha256sum "$STAGE.tar.gz" > "$STAGE.tar.gz.sha256"`, in every tarball builder since
they were written). One `scp` argument.

**The three-line rule this earns.**

1. **Grep the CALLERS of every conditional check, not the check.** When the condition is
   "an optional input is present", the question is not "is the body right" but "who
   supplies the input, and does anything fail if they don't". Reading the check teaches
   you nothing about how often it runs.
2. **The far side must REQUIRE the input.** Shipping the sidecar is not enough: an `scp`
   that silently drops it puts you straight back on the unverified path with everything
   green. The remote command now starts `[ -f …tar.gz.sha256 ] && …`, so an absent
   sidecar is a RED gate rather than a quiet skip.
3. **A gate for this needs the negative case.** The static "does the scp name a sidecar"
   axis is a string check and proves nothing on its own. The load-bearing axis runs the
   real installer three ways — intact+sidecar, tampered+sidecar, tampered+NO sidecar —
   because only the third measures what the old pillars were actually doing. Without it
   a reviewer has to take "install.sh verifies only if the sidecar is there" on trust,
   which is the same kind of unchecked claim as the bug.

⭐ Related but distinct from "a check that shares a defect with the thing it checks reads
GREEN" (v6.6.2). Here the checker is correct and the *feeder* is missing — which is
harder to see, because auditing the checker is the thing a reviewer naturally does.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: the cross-OS lib-test runner judged tests/tcyr/crossos/ by exit code alone. A binary that executes NO user code exits 0, so a compiler bug that dropped the whole top-level program scored a PASS on real ecb/ach/cass/pi — over the very defect the test was named for. Measured: the 6.6.5 compiler turned macro_expansion_with_include.tcyr into a 43,512-byte binary that printed nothing and exited 0. The seeded-non-zero trick inside the test cannot help, because the seed is part of the text that vanished."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "cross-os", "gates", "exit-code", "silent-pass", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class.** cyrius already knows the empty-CORPUS version of this: `cycc` on empty stdin
exits 0 and emits a runnable binary, so an unmatched glob scored a fake PASS (CLAUDE.md's
`tests/` note, v6.5.11). This is the same trap one level down — an empty PROGRAM rather than
an empty corpus — and it survived the first fix because the corpus was no longer empty. The
file was there, it compiled, it produced a binary, the binary ran, and it exited 0.

**Why in-file defences do not reach it.** The natural guard is what the test already had:

    var mei_rc = 92;          # seeded non-zero
    mei_rc = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, mei_rc);

That catches a build which emits a binary but drops SOME of the program. It cannot catch one
that drops ALL of it, because the seed, the summary call and the exit syscall are all in the
text that disappeared — and with nothing left to run, the process exits 0 by default. There is
no statement you can put in the file that survives the file being removed. **The judgement has
to come from outside the binary.**

**What that looks like.** Every `.tcyr` ends in `assert_summary()`, which prints
`N passed, M failed (T total)` to stdout. So the runner captures stdout and, for a test whose
SOURCE calls `assert_summary`, requires that line with N >= 1. Two properties are worth
copying:

* **Derive the requirement from the artifact under test, not from a list.** `grep -q
  'assert_summary(' "$t"` self-scopes and cannot rot when a test is renamed — the failure mode
  of the `vr01_` prefix all over again. Measured at 6.6.6: 332 of 333 `.tcyr` call it and all
  332 print a line with N >= 1, so the check is not selective in practice, and the single
  opt-out opts ITSELF out by not calling it.
* **N >= 1, not an exact count.** A test may assert inside a loop or behind a platform guard.
  The claim being enforced is "user code ran and reported", which is exactly what a dropped
  program cannot fake, and nothing more.

**The recognition rule.** Whenever a harness reduces a run to one number, ask what that number
is for the degenerate case — no input, no output, no work. If "did nothing" and "succeeded"
map to the same value, the harness is reporting on a set that includes the failure it exists
to find. Exit code 0, an empty summary line `__LIBTEST_SUMMARY__ 0 0`, and `0 warnings` from a
linter that never parsed the file are all the same bug.

**6.7.10, the same rule for coverage.** An instrumented run that exits 0 with NO coverage record
fails by name (`exited 0 but left no coverage record`): a silent zero would read as unexecuted code
(an `asm` exit, a run-time exit number, an exec). A hardware leg grades each remote run as its own
ssh, keeps every exit code, pulls the output back and names every miss.

**6.7.11, the same rule for a fork that never calls `main`.** A crossos `.tcyr` that left `fn main` to the entry's
auto-call was vacuous on cx: cxvm printed nothing and exited 0 — read as a pass (the returns lane's gate). Check the
PRINTED summary line, not the exit code. Turning the cx auto-call on (GF-19, D16) exposed seven .tcyr that had never
run on cxvm: five hit the guest contract (a host-address `mmap_anon`, guest-offset `pipe` / `fstat` / `unlink`
buffers) and two have no cx arm.

**6.7.12 — the honest arms, and zero assertions is a failure.** (a) cx (LIB-8, D13): the four wrappers refuse
honestly on cx (`cyr_mmap` -1, `sys_pipe` / `sys_fstat` / `sys_unlink` -38); the seven files assert the REFUSAL and
print a named SKIP, and `cx_auto_call_main.sh` promoted X1 / X2 to T4 / T5 and requires BOTH the `N passed, 0 failed`
line and the SKIP line, with N derived per leg — a vacuous run still reads RED. (b) The `.tcyr` side of the SKIP
rule (WN-2): the cross-OS leg grades a run with 0 assertions as a FAILURE, and a target arm that only prints SKIP
counts nothing. Three cass / Mac corpus failures were exactly that (tls_native_ccs_deadline and
process_run_capture_args on cass, async_alloc_refused on the Macs — no macOS rows at all). An inapplicable arm
asserts its PREMISE by name ("Windows refuses socketpair", "cyr_mprotect declines -38").

**6.7.13 — a harness that reuses ONE output path reads its own ETXTBSY.** A per-file `.tcyr` loop that wrote every
binary to one path read 134 / 157 "cannot execute" failures (most likely a still-running earlier binary held the
file); one output path per test: 621 / 621. A runner's own plumbing can fail tests that pass — give each test its own
binary, and read a "cannot execute" as the runner's before the test's (the vectors lane, VC-*).

**6.7.14 — two more.** (a) A TEST WHOSE EVERY ARM CAN SKIP MUST ASSERT THAT AN ARM RAN (PL-5, 08a190069):
`at_family_dirfd_relative.tcyr` had no assertion on its Windows / cx / agnos SKIP arms, so the cass runner (which
refuses a 0-assertion run: "assert summary reports 0 assertions") failed it — the one cass failure of its first
cross-OS run. Give each inapplicable arm an assertion of its premise. (b) A VM'S GUEST-FAULT EXIT CAN EQUAL THE
EXPECTED EXIT (LIB-1): cxvm exits 1 on a guest trap, so a cx row whose expected exit is 1 must also read cxvm's stderr,
or a trap reads as a pass — the filed repro's row 1 was caught only by the stderr read.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_synthesized_comparison_that_is_accidentally_right_for_rhs_zero_hides_forever_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: cx has no lte/gte opcode, so ESETCC synthesized `(a<b)|(a==b)` — and wrote the strict half into the wrong register with its operands swapped. For a right-hand side of 0 and a clean scratch the wrong sequence is ACCIDENTALLY CORRECT, and `if (x <= 0)` is the commonest shape in the tree, so it survived cx's entire life. It surfaced as three unrelated-looking bugs: assert_gte always failing, vec_get aborting on a populated vec, and a `size <= 0` guard flipping after an unrelated `g * 8` — which reads as the CALLER corrupting argument 1."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "codegen", "comparison", "silent-miscompile", "emulated-opcode", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**What was emitted.** `lte` had to be built from two opcodes and an `or`:

    lt  r2, r0, r1      # the strict half, into the scratch the `or` reads
    eq  r0, r0, r1
    or  r0, r0, r2

What was actually there was `lt r0, r1, r0` on the first line — destination `r0` instead of
`r2`, operands swapped. So the strict half was computed backwards, immediately overwritten by
the `eq`, and the `or` folded in whatever `r2` held from an EARLIER emit.

**Why nobody noticed for the target's whole life.** Work the wrong sequence through with a
right-hand side of `0` and a zero `r2`:

    (0 < a) == 0   ==   a <= 0        # correct, for this one operand

`if (x <= 0)` and `if (x >= 0)` are the commonest shapes in the corpus, so the emitter looked
right on exactly the cases anyone tried. It went wrong for every other right-hand side, and
for `0` too once anything had left the scratch non-zero — the shift-by-immediate emitter
parks its shift amount there, so a `g * 8` anywhere earlier in the program poisoned every
later `<=`.

**The three symptoms were one emitter.** This is the part worth keeping. They were filed as
three separate defects and two of them pointed at the wrong subsystem:

* `assert_gte` / `assert_lte` failed for every input while `assert_gt` / `assert_lt` passed —
  which reads as a bug in the assert library.
* `vec_get` aborted `index out of bounds` on a populated vec (`0 >= 1` scored TRUE) — which
  reads as a bug in the vec library, or in its length bookkeeping.
* `zalloc(64)` returned 0 because its `if (size <= 0)` guard flipped after an unrelated global
  multiply — which reads as **the caller corrupting the callee's first argument**, and sends
  the reader into the call ABI, where nothing is wrong.

**The recognition rule.** When a target lacks an opcode and the compiler synthesizes it from
two or three others, check the synthesis against an operand that is NOT the common one. An
emulated operator is a small program, and a small program that is right for the argument
everybody passes is indistinguishable from a correct one. Two cheap tests catch it:
a non-zero right-hand side, and running the same comparison after something that dirties the
scratch register the synthesis reads. Both are one line in a gate; neither had ever been
written for cx (`tests/gates/codegen/cx_relational_or_equal.sh` now holds them, with every row
cross-checked against the same source through the x86 backend).

**And the corollary for triage:** three consumer-visible symptoms in three different
subsystems, all appearing on one target, is evidence of ONE thing in that target's backend —
not three. Look for the shared emitter before fixing any of them.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_refusal_added_at_the_bottom_of_a_multi_arm_parse_fn_only_covers_what_reaches_the_bottom_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: PARSE_RETURN has THREE arms that handle a `return` themselves and `return 0` out of the function before the bottom of the fn is reached — the tail-call path, the multi-return (tuple) arm, and the vector `return IDENT;` branches. A new type refusal was added near the bottom; it covered one of the three. The bite found the tail-call arm (because a test happened to use that spelling) and shipped with the tuple arm open, while the source comment said 'EVERYTHING ELSE' and the CHANGELOG said 'refuse anything the ABI cannot carry'. Both were false. Grep for the EARLY RETURNS, not for the shape you are refusing."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parse-return", "refusal", "early-return", "multi-arm", "incomplete-fix", "silent-miscompile", "simd", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** `PARSE_RETURN` in `src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr` is ~700 lines and is not one
decision. It is a sequence of arms, each of which recognises a `return` spelling, emits the
whole thing itself, and `return 0`s out:

    if (GINFN(S) == 1) { ... tail call: epilogue + jmp ... return 0; }     # line ~613
    if (PEEKT(S) == 10) { ... multi-return: ret2/ret3 ... return 0; }      # line ~909
    if (_cur_fn_ret_sid > 0 ...) { ... big-struct retptr copy ... return 0; }
    if (_cur_fn_ret_pair == 1) { ... rax:rdx pair load ... return 0; }
    if (_is_simd128(...)) { ... return IDENT; byte copy ... return 0; }
    if (_is_simd256(...)) { ... same, 32 bytes ... return 0; }
    <the new refusal>                                                     # line ~1242
    <the PE retptr arm, then the generic scalar PCMPE path>

A refusal placed at `<the new refusal>` is not a refusal for the language. It is a refusal for
the spellings that fall through six arms to get there.

**What that cost.** v6.6.6 bite 21e refused everything the vector return ABI cannot carry, and
correctly noticed the tail-call arm — it diverted `return f(args);` to the normal path so the
refusal could see it. It did not notice the multi-return arm, so:

    fn bad(): f64v2 { return (41, 7); }

still compiled clean, exit 0, no diagnostic, and the caller read 0 — on f64v2, f32v4, i64v2 and
the arity-3 form into f64v4. The ret2/ret3 convention is rax:rdx[:r3], which is the ABI of a
9-16 byte STRUCT, not of a vector on any target. Meanwhile the legacy spelling of the same
thing, `ret2(41,7); return 0;`, WAS caught — because the trailing `return 0;` reaches the
bottom. Two spellings of one construct, opposite outcomes, and the one that worked is the one
that made the fix look complete.

**Why the tail-call arm got found and the tuple arm did not.** The tail-call arm was found by a
test that happened to be written as `return sc();`. Nobody wrote `return (a, b);` because it is
not a shape anyone uses for a vector — which is exactly the property that makes an unrefused
shape dangerous rather than harmless. A refusal exists for the code nobody writes.

**The recognition rule.** Before adding a check to a long parse function, enumerate its EARLY
RETURNS, not its inputs:

    awk '/^fn PARSE_RETURN/,/^fn [^_]/' src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr | grep -n 'return 0;'

Every one of them is a path your check does not see. Decide for each whether it can carry the
thing you are refusing; the ones that cannot need the check at their own head (or a divert to
the common path, which is what the tail-call arm got). Then write ONE gate row per arm and
mutate each guard SEPARATELY — a mutant that reddens every row proves only that you have one
check, not that you have the right number of them. The 6.6.6 gate
(`tests/gates/codegen/simd_return_shapes.sh`) has m2/m3/m4 for exactly this: revert the
PARSE_RETURN refusal and the tuple rows stay GREEN, which is the signal that they are a
different site.

**The corollary for prose.** The same review found the source comment claiming 'EVERYTHING
ELSE' and the CHANGELOG claiming 'refuse anything the ABI cannot carry'. A completeness claim
in a comment is a claim about control flow, and it is cheap to check and cheap to get wrong.
Do not write one until the early-return enumeration above is done.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_arm64_unrouted_syscall_reran_stale_x16"
description = "On arm64-macOS an unrouted syscall number did not fail: ESYSXLAT's Mach-O chain writes x16 (Darwin's syscall register) only on a MATCH and nothing defaulted it, so the svc silently re-ran whatever x16 last held — normally the PREVIOUS syscall — with the new arguments, returning a plausible value. The class shipped at least eight times and was fixed row by row until cycc 6.6.8 defaulted x16 to 0xFFFF at the chain head (xnu → nosys: SIGSYS, or -78 with SIGSYS ignored). A translate-by-chain emitter needs a DEFAULT for the no-match case, not more rows."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "aarch64", "macho", "darwin", "syscall", "esysxlat", "silent-failure", "cross-target", "v6.6.8"]
content = """
**Symptom.** `getpid()` then `syscall(<unrouted>, …)` on ecb returned the PID again. No
crash, no errno — the wrong call ran. `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_ioctl_x86_compat.tcyr`
passed on ecb for releases because its raw 16 had no row and re-ran ioctl out of x16 = 54,
so the test that was supposed to prove the 16 row existed proved nothing.

**Why.** `ESYSXLAT`'s `_TARGET_MACHO == 2` arm is a chain of `cmp x8,#linux; b.ne; movz
x16,#bsd`. Each row writes x16 only when it matches. ESYSCALL preserves x16 across `svc`
(the pipe/fork fixups rely on it), so after any syscall x16 holds that call's BSD number,
and an unmatched number simply inherits it. Other x16 writers exist too (large-offset
scratch, every `__got` reroute's `adrp/ldr/blr x16`), so "stale" means "whatever last wrote
x16". x86-macOS never had the hole: an unmatched rax reaches Darwin UNCLASSED and SIGSYSes.

**Fix (6.6.8).** One word at the chain head, guarded on query mode like every row:
`movz x16, #0xFFFF`. xnu maps a number >= nsysent to nosys. The compiler's "syscall N not
routed … will fault on macOS" warning had promised that all along; it was false on this
backend until the default existed.

**x86-macOS had a quieter twin.** With SIGSYS IGNORED, an unclassed number came back with rax
unchanged — `syscall(4000)` returned 4000, a positive "success". EMACHO_SYSXLAT now ends with
`_msx_tail`, classing a leftover number as Unix 0xFFFF, so both Macs answer -78.

**The general lesson.** A translation table implemented as a chain of conditional writes has
an implicit DEFAULT — whatever the destination held before. If that default is "the last
value", every missing row is a silent wrong answer instead of a loud one, and each one gets
found late, on hardware, and fixed by adding one more row. Give the chain an explicit
failing default first; THEN the missing rows announce themselves (the ioctl test died rc 140
on ecb the moment the default landed without the row).

**Related 6.6.8 finding — a compile-time diagnostic keyed on the number alone.** The Mach-O
reroutes for 228 / 35 / 1700 fire at ONE argument count, but the route query answered by
number, so `syscall(228, id)` built clean and faulted. `_macho_warn_unrouted` now checks the
arity. `tests/gates/platform/darwin_syscall_literals_routed.sh` probes every in-tree literal
site through the compiler at its REAL argc, and compiles every shipped program and crossos
test for both Macs, failing on any `not routed`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "float_literal_rational_divided_at_runtime"
description = "Until cycc 6.6.10 a decimal float literal was an i64 numerator/denominator pair (numer = int*10^fd + frac, denom = 10^fd) that the backends divided at RUNTIME (cvtsi2sd/scvtf + divsd/fdiv). Three silent failures fell out: numer overflowed once the digit string reached 2^63; denom overflowed past 18 FRACTION digits whatever the significance (`0.0000000000000000001` was NEGATIVE, `9.999999999999999999` was -8.2); and the runtime divide double-rounded once numer passed 2^53, so ~3%/24%/21% of 16/17/18-digit literals were 1 ulp off, identically on every host. The fix converts at compile time with integer bignums (round-half-even, sticky, subnormals, +inf) and each backend loads the bits as one immediate. Integer literals >= 2^64 wrapped the same way and are now an error."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "float", "literal", "rounding", "bignum", "double-rounding", "overflow", "silent-failure", "cross-target", "seed-derive", "v6.5.28", "v6.6.10"]
content = """
**Symptom.** No error and no warning, only a different number. `9.999999999999999999` was
0xc020e4bba3a48757 (-8.2). `12345678901234567890.0` and `0.0000000000000000001` came out
negative. dhvani's `0.9222578791123097` ended in ...808b instead of ...808c. x86, aarch64
(qemu), real pi and ecb all produced the same wrong bits, so no cross-host comparison could
see it.

**Why it hid.** `tests/tcyr/crossos/float_literal_precision.tcyr` scaled each literal by a
power of ten and truncated with `f64_to`, which cannot see a 1-ulp error. A test that rounds
away the property it claims to check will pass. It compares the IEEE-754 bit patterns now
(38 rows, expected values computed independently in Python).

**The trigger was misreported.** It is not "19+ significant digits". `3.141592653589793238`
(19 digits, below 2^63) was right, while a ONE-digit literal with 19 fraction digits was
garbage. The overflow was in the denominator.

**The mechanism: two roundings of one value.** numer and denom are each rounded to a double
(cvtsi2sd), and then the quotient is rounded again. Both are correctly rounded operations,
but the composition is not. Correct rounding needs the exact rational and one rounding step,
which is why the conversion now happens in the lexer, where the digits still exist.

**Bignums under cybs.** `_flit_bits` and its helpers in src/frontend/lex.cyr use 32-bit limbs
in 8-byte slots. That way no product needs 128 bits, and every shift is correct whether `>>`
is logical or arithmetic. The quotient is 56 bits, computed by restoring shift-subtract, so
there is no bignum-by-bignum long division. Every helper is small and uses few globals,
because cybs fails silently past its per-fn limits. seed-derive's gen1 was checked on a
3,000-literal sweep, since cycc's own source has no float literals and the fixpoint never
runs this code.

**The size bound is an argument, not a cap.** Only 800 significant digits are kept. A nonzero
tail becomes one trailing `1`, which cannot change the result, because every double and every
halfway point between two doubles has at most 767 significant digits. Values below 1e-324 or
at or above 1e309 are decided before any bignum is built.

**Integer twin.** LEXDEC, LEXHEX and LEXOCT had no overflow check either: `18446744073709551617`
was 1, and so was `0x10000000000000001`. Only one such literal existed in the ecosystem,
cyrld.cyr's `r_add - 0x10000000000000000`, and it was a no-op that compiled BECAUSE of the
wrap. The hard error and that line's deletion had to land in the same change, or cyrld would
stop building.

**Lesson.** A number the lexer builds into a fixed-width field needs an overflow check at
the point it is accumulated. v6.5.28 fixed the 32/32 packing of this same literal and did
not look at the i64 arithmetic one line above it. Gates:
`tests/tcyr/crossos/float_literal_precision.tcyr` and
`tests/gates/frontend/integer_literal_overflow_refused.sh`. See CHANGELOG [6.5.28] and
[6.6.10].
"""

[[entries]]
name = "file_attribution_is_a_line_lookup_so_the_lexer_line_counter_is_a_security_boundary"
description = "`private` is enforced on FM_FILEID(line): a token's FILE is a range lookup of its LINE in the `#@file` span table, so every input to that lookup is part of the security boundary. Three instances, three different inputs: the span arithmetic (v6.5.3, an unmasked base collapsed every line to one file), the markers (the file-marker forge (private visibility) bug, a forged `#@file`), and at cycc 6.6.11 the lexer's own line counter — LEX's string loop did not count a newline inside a string literal, every later token sat one line high per newline, tokens near an include boundary resolved to the NEIGHBOURING file, and a call to another file's private fn compiled (the multi-line string attribution bug). Nothing was forged; the only input was an ordinary multi-line string."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "line-numbers", "private", "visibility", "FM_FILEID", "file-map", "security", "the multi-line string attribution bug", "the file-marker forge (private visibility) bug", "v6.5.3", "v6.6.11"]
content = """
**The chain.** The preprocessor flattens every `include` into one buffer and brackets each
file with a `#@file "NAME" BASE` marker; `FM_BUILD` turns the markers into spans (start line,
line count, source base) and `FM_FILEID(S, line)` answers a token's file by finding the span
its LINE falls in. `private` is decided on that answer. The line is whatever LEX stamped on the
token, so a lexer that miscounts lines moves tokens between files without touching a marker.

**The miscount.** LEX's string loop stored a raw LF, and the LF of a backslash-newline escape,
without calling `SCLINE`. After a literal holding N newlines every later token was N lines
high. Symptoms, mildest first:
  * diagnostics one line high per newline — "errors in backend/x86/fixup.cyr are one line
    high" was filed separately (N3); its trigger was the one raw-newline literal in
    src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr, and the `#@file` bookkeeping it was blamed on was correct;
  * line 1 of the next include reported on that include's own marker line, as a bare
    `error:4:12:` with no file name;
  * a call after a multi-line string near an include boundary attributed to the DEFINING
    file, so a cross-file call to a `private` fn COMPILED, and a privacy error, when one
    fired, landed on an innocent name.

**Why it is a CVE and not a diagnostics bug (the multi-line string attribution bug, P2).** No marker was wrong and nothing
was forged. Any source file could bypass `private` with a string literal. Same impact as
the file-marker forge (private visibility) bug, different vector.

**The class: three inputs to one lookup, each fixed on its own.**
  1. v6.5.3, the span arithmetic. `FM_BUILD` packs the source base into the high 32 bits of
     the line-count word; `FM_FILEID` read it unmasked, `start + lines` overflowed, the first
     span matched every line, and every token collapsed to one file id (access wrongly
     ALLOWED).
  2. the file-marker forge (private visibility) bug (6.6.6; the first copier was guarded at v6.5.21), the markers. `FM_BUILD` accepts
     `#@file` at any offset, so an included file, a `#define` body or a `#derive` tail could
     mint a span and claim to be another file.
  3. the multi-line string attribution bug (6.6.11), the key: the line numbers the lookup is indexed by.
Each fix guarded the input its repro used. None asked what else the lookup reads.

**The rule.** When a security decision is DERIVED (file = f(line), owner = f(offset)), list
every input to the derivation and treat each one as boundary code: the table, the arithmetic
over it, and the key. Here the key is the lexer's line counter, which nobody reads as security
code. Every path in the lexer that consumes a newline byte has to count it.

**Fix and pin.** Both string-loop paths call `SCLINE`; the string token keeps its OPENING line
(the column its diagnostic head prints). `tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh`
axis 6 pins, after a multi-line string, the diagnostic's line, the include's file name and the
`private` refusal of a cross-file call that compiled on 6.6.10. Mutations recorded in its
header: dropping the raw-LF `SCLINE` turns axis 6 red; dropping the escaped-LF one turns
`ml_bslf` red. The same bite closed the escape list (an unknown escape is a lex error). See
CHANGELOG [6.6.11] (B05, N1/N2/N3) and language/features.cyml
`string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "flags_reflect_rax_is_emitter_state_so_a_load_after_an_eaddr_must_clear_it"
description = "x86 `_flags_reflect_rax` says 'the CPU flags already reflect rax', and the condition path then SKIPS its `test rax, rax`. Arithmetic emitters (EADDR = add rax, rcx) set it; a narrow field load through `EFIELD_LOAD_W` deliberately leaves it alone. So a new path that computes an address with EADDR and then loads through it must clear the flag itself, or `if (x)` branches on the ADDRESS (6.6.12, the typed-array subscript). ⚠ Since 6.7.6 ECONDCMP also requires the setter to be the LAST emit (`_flags_cp`), which closes the class for every emitter"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["x86", "peephole", "flags", "_flags_reflect_rax", "ETESTAZ", "EADDR", "EFIELD_LOAD_W", "subscript", "v5.6.8", "v6.6.12"]
---
**The mechanism (v5.6.8, "flag-result reuse").** A bare condition `if (x)` needs ZF/SF to
reflect rax. The condition path (src/frontend/parse_ctrl.cyr) emits `test rax, rax` via
`ETESTAZ` — unless `_flags_reflect_rax == 1`, meaning the LAST emitter was an arithmetic op
that updated rax in place and therefore already set the flags. x86 emitters maintain it:
`EADDR` (`add rax, rcx`), `ESUBR`, the logic ops, `xor rax, rax` set it to 1; a `mov`, a
multiply, a compare and `EVLOAD_W`'s narrow load set it to 0. aarch64 emits non-S add/sub
and never sets it, and cx declares the variable and never sets it either, so on both the
test always fires — the skip, and this trap, are x86 (and x86 Mach-O / PE) only.

**The trap.** `EFIELD_LOAD_W` — the narrow `[rcx]` load the field path uses — deliberately
does NOT touch the flag (its comment says so: the field-load path must leave it as the caller
set it). That is right for the paths it was written for, and wrong for a new caller whose
address arithmetic ends in `EADDR`: the flag is still 1 from the `add`, the load replaces rax
with the element, and the condition skips its test and branches on the flags of the ADDRESS
computation — nonzero, so `if (a[i])` was taken for every element. It was found while building
the 6.6.12 typed-array subscript: `_arr_sub_addr` ends with `EADDR` (&a + i * sizeof(T)),
`_arr_sub_load` does `EMOVCA` + `EFIELD_LOAD_W`, and now ends with `_flags_reflect_rax = 0;`
(src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr). The default build — no IR mode needed.

**Recognition.** A condition on a freshly loaded value that is taken (or not) regardless of
the value, while `var t = <expr>; if (t)` — which stores and reloads — behaves. Disassemble:
the `jz`/`jnz` follows the load with no `test rax, rax` in between.

**Rule.** The flag is emitter STATE that describes the last instruction, and every emitter
that changes rax without setting the flags must clear it. When you add a load path, either
go through an emitter that clears it (`EVLOAD_W`) or clear it yourself after the load; when you
add an emitter, decide its effect on the flag explicitly. A helper that "leaves the flag as the
caller set it" is a promise about its callers — read that comment before you become one.
Gate: tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_array_subscript.tcyr's `if` / `while` on an element rows
(reverting the clear is one of the five mutations in its header).

⛔ **6.7.6 — the rule above was not enough, and is now backed by position.** A class of emitters kept
missing the hand-kept "clear it" contract (a narrow local / field load, `mulh64`, `~`, `&x`, a string
literal, `f64_floor`, every PE syscall reroute), so `x = x + 1; if (h.m)` branched on the add's ZF.
`_flags_set(S)` now records the setter's code position in `_flags_cp`, and ECONDCMP skips the test only
when `GCP(S) == _flags_cp` as well — any emit after the setter forces it. Clearing the flag is still
correct; forgetting to is no longer a miscompile. See
flag_skip_by_position_nested_parse_state_and_the_bootstrap_rung_v676.

[[entries]]
name = "packed_globals_misalign_every_later_word_and_aarch64_atomics_sigbus"
description = "Global data used to be laid out PACKED: a `var a: u8[363]` (or a lone `u8` / `i16` / odd-sized struct global) left the next 64-bit global at an odd address, and every one after it until another odd size happened to realign. x86 tolerates it; aarch64 `ldaxr` / `stlxr` / `ldar` raise an alignment fault (SIGBUS) on a Pi 4 and on Apple Silicon. Since 6.6.13 every global starts at its NATURAL alignment (1/2/4 for narrow scalars, 8 for everything else) on every backend (6.6.13, I9)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "globals", "alignment", "aarch64", "atomics", "SIGBUS", "ldaxr", "GVOFF", "v6.6.13"]
---
**How it surfaced.** agnostic 0.1.7's aarch64 binary died with SIGBUS at startup on a Pi 4: the folded
sankoch declares three `u8` arrays (630 bytes, ≡ 6 mod 8), and 1,101 of agnostic's 1,962 globals came
after them misaligned — among them a dozen of sigil's atomic init flags, so the first hash faulted in
`atomic_cas` (`ldaxr x4, [x0]` with x0 = 0xa200f6). It had been recorded downstream as "SIGBUS under qemu,
needs real hardware" for four releases.

**The trap.** The var-area offset is a PREFIX SUM, and it is computed in several places that must agree:
x86 FIXUP (both the object-mode and the main loop), aarch64 FIXUP, PE `_pe_layout`'s mirror, and
`main_cx.cyr` — plus re-sum walks that recomputed the offset instead of reading GVOFF. Change the rule in
one and the image disagrees with the code that addresses it. 6.6.13 puts the rule in one helper
(`_GV_ALIGN`, `src/common/util.cyr`) and replaces the re-sum walks with GVOFF.

**Why natural alignment and not "8 for everything".** Narrow scalars stay packed against each other, so
an over-width store into one still clobbers its neighbour — which keeps M1-class defects (an 8-byte store
into a `u8` slot) DETECTABLE by narrow-narrow tests instead of being masked by padding.

**Recognise it again:** an aarch64-only SIGBUS (or a split-lock fault on x86 under
`split_lock_detect=fatal`) in an atomic on a global — print `&g % 8`. Gate:
tests/tcyr/crossos/global_alignment.tcyr (run natively on pi and ecb by the release gate).

[[entries]]
name = "name_based_copy_resolvers_need_the_capture_rung"
description = "Struct-copy paths that resolve their SOURCE by name (`_fsc_name_src`, `_try_aggregate_copy_assign`, `_try_struct_copy_init`) looked a name up as local, then global. Inside a closure a captured struct is NEITHER — so the resolver found nothing, fell back to an 8-byte store, and copied the struct's ADDRESS into the field (`b.v = p` with a captured `Pt p` returned 80 instead of 34). The lookup order must be local, CAPTURE, global (6.6.13, M3). Second instance 6.6.16 (C2): a pointer-mode struct (a HANDLE) was an operand kind `_agc_operand` did not know, so every handle <-> value assignment fell to the same one-word store"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closures", "capture", "struct copy", "aggregate", "_fsc_name_src", "_agc_operand", "handle", "pointer-mode", "rebind", "v6.6.10", "v6.6.13", "v6.6.16"]
---
**The shape.** 6.6.10 made a field store of a struct copy its bytes (`_fsc_*`). Every resolver that
answers "is this name an inline struct, and where is it?" was written against two tables — the frame's
locals and the globals. A closure body sees a THIRD kind of name, its captures (the env object), and the
resolvers did not consult it: the silent fallback was the 8-byte scalar store, i.e. the address.

**Fix.** A capture rung between FINDLOCAL and FINDVAR in each name-based resolver, plus the enclosing
fn's pointer-slot count carried in `_cl_save_locals`' trailer so a capture can tell an inline struct from a
pointer-mode one. A copy between DIFFERENT struct types through a capture is now refused by name, as it
is outside a closure.

**Recognise it again:** any new "resolve the source by name" helper — ask what happens when the name is a
capture. Gate: tests/tcyr/crossos/closure_capture_struct_copy.tcyr and
tests/gates/diagnostics/closure_capture_struct_copy_mismatch.sh.

**SECOND INSTANCE (6.6.16, C2): an operand KIND the probe does not know falls to the one-word store,
silently.** `_agc_operand` / `_agc_src_operand` (parse.cyr) classify each side of a struct assignment:
inline local (`_agc_li` = slot >= 0), inline global (`_agc_gvi` >= 0), address-passed by-value param
(`_agc_pp` = 1), closure capture (`_agc_li` = -2). A HANDLE — a pointer-mode local / global or a `*T`
param, whose slot holds the struct's address — was none of them, so `q = a` stored a's ADDRESS into
q.x and `a = q` stored q.x into a's slot (the next `a.x` SIGSEGV'd). 6.6.16: a handle local / `*T` is
`_agc_pp = 2` (`_AGC_DREF(li)`), a pointer-mode GLOBAL is `_agc_gvi = 0 - 1000 - gvi` (the deref band
`_agc_addr_x1` reads as `[G]`), `_agc_hdl()` asks "is a handle". Traps: a consumer of the
address-passed band must test `_agc_pp != 0`, not `== 1`, and a global consumer must tell `>= 0`
(SLVAR it) from `!= -1` (address it); ONE operand probe feeds FOUR arms (`_try_aggregate_copy_assign`,
`_try_struct_call_assign`, `_pcmpe_struct_assign` + its field arm, `_fla_assign`), and the field arm
is armed only when `_agc_t < 0`, so a kind reported with `_agc_t = 0` skipped it (`a = b.v` SIGSEGV'd
for exactly that); the handle <- handle REBIND must return BEFORE `_AGG_ASSIGN_TYPE_ERR`, or every
legal heap-handle rebind between two struct types becomes `cannot copy ...`; a `*T` parameter passes
`_local_is_sptr_param` (its slot looks exactly like an address-passed by-value one) — `_is_ptr_param`
tells them apart, else `a = b` between two `*T` params COPIES; `_agc_copy_bytes` takes six params
(cybs's cap), so the band rides in the index, not a flag. And cx already answered every row right —
a passing cx run is not evidence the image targets agree. User-facing rule:
language/features.cyml `struct_assignment_handle_and_value_v6616`.
⟳ 6.7.10, the third instance: the copy-assign operand classifier (`_agc_operand`) answered only NAMES —
locals, globals by their GVPM, captures — so any `*T` that was not a local or parameter (a `*T` global,
GVPM 0; a `: *T` call; a `*T` field) fell to the one-word store at every size. The fix did NOT grow the name
classifier: it reads the source AFTER it is parsed (rax = the pointer) from the compiler's own typing
(`_hl_pchain`), as the 6.7.10 declaration arm does. And a size-keyed predicate is the same family:
`_agc_plain_big` made a `*T` over 8 B a copy handle only because of the struct's SIZE; one predicate
(`_small_sptr`, beside `_small_hdl` in `_agc_handle_local`) switched every `_agc_*` consumer at once. See
struct_values_at_every_size_and_packed_fields_v6710.

[[entries]]
name = "tls_the_obvious_reading_of_a_protocol_field_was_wrong_seven_times"
description = "Nine traps in cyrius's own TLS stack (lib/tls_native_*.cyr, the libssl shim, the Windows store reader), seven met in 6.6.14 (CYRIUS-2026-0019/CYRIUS-2026-0020/CYRIUS-2026-0022 and the libssl follow-ups) two more in 6.6.15 and four in 6.6.16 (CYRIUS-2026-0026 and three libssl / call-site readings), each a check that stopped at the obvious reading of a protocol field: possession read as identity, a 1.2 signature scheme read as a curve (it bit in BOTH directions), supported_groups read as a key-exchange list only, the ctx's current state read as an event, OpenSSL's ZERO_RETURN read as a clean close, Windows root-store membership read as trust, any `*.` read as a wildcard, a key_share read as a version, legacy_session_id read as inert, a fixed-length verify read as length-checked, a CA's purpose read as the leaf's alone, an OpenSSL macro read as a symbol, and a failed handshake read as over. What the OTHER implementation (OpenSSL, the Windows chain engine) reads the field as decides both interop and security"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["tls", "tls_native", "libssl", "mtls", "x509", "ecdsa", "ecdhe", "wildcard", "windows", "crypt32", "trust-store", "supported_versions", "middlebox-ccs", "hrr", "openssl-interop", "security", "ed25519", "eku", "session-cache", "CYRIUS-2026-0019", "CYRIUS-2026-0020", "CYRIUS-2026-0022", "the ECDHE key zeroisation gap", "the all-zero x25519 check item", "CYRIUS-2026-0026", "v6.6.14", "v6.6.15", "v6.6.16"]
---
1. **Client authentication is THREE checks, in every version the ctx accepts (CYRIUS-2026-0019).** The 1.3
   server checked the CertificateVerify against whatever leaf arrived — proof of POSSESSION — and
   never chained it, so a self-signed leaf was an identity and a server with no roots accepted
   everyone; the 1.2 server never sent a CertificateRequest, and a server ctx accepts 1.2 by
   default, so a client offering only 1.2 connected unauthenticated under FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT.
   The checks: ASK (1.2 and 1.3); verify the chain to the SERVER's own roots with the CLIENT
   purpose, failing closed with no roots; verify the CertificateVerify over the right transcript
   (1.2: every handshake message before it — Ed25519 signs the messages, so a running hash cannot
   stand in). One verifier serves both roles: `_tn_verify_chain(ctx, leaf, inters, now, purpose)`.
   Review rule: a server "verify" flag that only decides whether a message is REQUESTED is not
   verification — find the call that verifies the chain with the client purpose.
2. **TLS 1.2's ECDSA scheme names the HASH, not the curve** (RFC 5246 §7.4.1.4.1).
   `ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256` (0x0403) is P-256 + SHA-256 in 1.3, but `{sha256, ecdsa}` for ANY
   ECDSA key in 1.2: OpenSSL signs SHA-256 with a P-384 key when the peer lists 0x0403 first. It
   bit once per direction — 6.6.14 the 1.2 server's CertificateVerify check, 6.6.15 the 1.2
   client's ServerKeyExchange check, invisible until the client listed secp384r1 and then failing
   every P-384 server certificate with `TLS_ERR_AUTHN`. One helper now serves both,
   `_tn_ecdsa_verify_12`: digest from the scheme, curve from the key; 1.3 keeps the binding. Test
   it with a signature made OUTSIDE your code (tls12_client_ecdhe.tcyr embeds `openssl dgst
   -sha256 -sign <p384 key>`): your own signer always pairs a curve with its natural hash and can
   never produce the case.
3. **supported_groups is a promise about the KEY EXCHANGE — and in TLS 1.2 a bound on the ECDSA
   CERTIFICATE's curve** (RFC 8422 §5.1). OpenSSL refuses a 1.2 ECDSA certificate, the client's
   own included, on a curve the client did not list — even when the exchange itself is on a
   listed curve (`s_client -tls1_2 -groups P-384` against a P-256 certificate: "Peer Temp Key:
   ECDH, secp384r1", then "wrong curve"). So "wrong curve" is about a certificate: read WHICH side
   printed it. 6.6.14 listed the client certificate's curve without doing ECDHE on it, so a
   server ranking that curve first failed; 6.6.15 did the real fix — ECDHE on P-256 / P-384
   everywhere, over sigil 3.13.8's constant-time ECDH — and the native 1.2 SERVER now refuses a
   client whose list omits its certificate's curve (handshake_failure) instead of sending a
   certificate the client will reject. Interop rows that test the KEY EXCHANGE use an Ed25519
   server certificate, which the list does not bind.
4. **Publish an identity on the EVENT, not the current STATE.** The client's leaf is kept once its
   chain verifies, BEFORE its CertificateVerify, so the getter cannot answer from the slot. The
   first cut answered "while the ctx is not ERROR": that hid a failed handshake's leaf, but any
   later I/O error also sets ERROR, so a server lost a verified identity when its client left
   without close_notify. A latch set where the handshake completes and never cleared
   (`_tn_server_connected`) is the answer. Same release: three 1.3 encrypted-read sites returned
   an error without FAILING the ctx (`LAST_ERR` 0) — every negative result must fail it.
5. **OpenSSL's re-read after a fatal alert is `SSL_ERROR_ZERO_RETURN`.** It sets
   RECEIVED_SHUTDOWN on any alert and leaves `warn_alert` at 0 — close_notify's code — so a naive
   mapping reports an attacked stream as a clean end. Keep the FIRST error sticky in the shim, and
   never call `SSL_shutdown` after `SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL` / `SSL_ERROR_SSL` (OpenSSL forbids it).
6. **A Windows root store's MEMBERSHIP is not trust (CYRIUS-2026-0020).** CurrentUser\ROOT is a logical
   store (cass: 59 ⊇ LocalMachine\ROOT 59 ⊇ AuthRoot 47), and the root program speaks in
   PROPERTIES: purposes (9, a DER SEQUENCE OF OID; absent = all), a **Disable** date (104, scope
   122: not trusted at all after it), a **NotBefore** date (126, scope 127: what it issues after
   that is not trusted), name constraints (84), plus the separate Disallowed store. ⛔ 104 is NOT
   the issuance-date distrust — 126 is; the first cut mapped 104 that way, never read 126, and
   exported the three Entrust roots as full anchors. Read the names out of `wincrypt.h` and dump
   every property of every root on the host before writing a policy. Size-query every property (a
   fixed-size read fails ERROR_MORE_DATA and silently drops a trusted root); CRYPT_E_NOT_FOUND
   (0x80092004) means absent and any other failure fails closed; and a purpose parser serving both
   an allow-list (9) and a deny-scope (122 / 127) needs THREE answers — names TLS / well formed
   without it / malformed — because "malformed" fails closed in OPPOSITE directions for the two.
   Since 6.7.12 (WN-6) the registry's auto-updated disallowed CTL IS read (88 entries on cass at 6.7.12, 82
   applied — item 14) and binds every certificate of a path, and a NotBefore root is taken with an issuance
   bound (it anchors only a leaf whose notBefore precedes it, as SChannel does; D9). Still out of reach of
   any enumeration: roots Windows has not fetched yet (D9: fail closed — add them with
   `tls_ctx_load_verify_locations`).
7. **A dNSName is a wildcard only by OpenSSL's `valid_star` (CYRIUS-2026-0022).** Native read any `*.`
   prefix as a wildcard over the first label with a star matching any bytes: `*.com` verified
   every `.com` host and `*.example.com` verified `a_b.example.com`. Port the reference
   (`v3_utl.c` `valid_star` / `wildcard_match` under NO_PARTIAL_WILDCARDS) rather than writing the
   rule from memory, and keep two backends in step with ONE table asked of BOTH live — OpenSSL's
   column computed by `X509_check_host`, never transcribed (`tls_hostname_verdicts.tcyr`). Note
   that `X509_check_host` reads a host starting with `.` as a SUBDOMAIN pattern: give such a host
   no identity before OpenSSL sees it.
8. **A key_share does not make a ClientHello TLS 1.3; supported_versions alone does** (RFC 8446
   §4.2.1; 6.6.15). The server's dispatcher returned "1.3" for any hello carrying a key_share
   before it read supported_versions — its comment called that robustness — so it negotiated 1.3
   with clients that never offered it, and the new "a second ClientHello must still offer 1.3"
   check did not hold (probed: supported_versions [0x0303] plus the requested share completed
   1.3). The rows written for that check are what found it. Neighbouring MUSTs (§9.2): a
   key_share without supported_groups, or the reverse, is missing_extension, as OpenSSL does — so
   a hand-built test ClientHello carries supported_versions, supported_groups,
   signature_algorithms and key_share; two in-tree fixtures had relied on the leniency.
9. **legacy_session_id is not inert** (RFC 8446 §D.4; 6.6.15). When the client sends a non-empty
   one — OpenSSL's s_client always does — the server MUST send one dummy ChangeCipherSpec right
   after its first handshake message (the HelloRetryRequest, else the ServerHello). The native
   server never did, and OpenSSL tolerates the absence, so no interop row could fail. Check it from
   the record headers `s_client -msg` dumps (`awk '/^<<< .*RecordHeader/ { getline; print $1 }'`
   gives `16 14 17 17`), not from a "ChangeCipherSpec" line: OpenSSL 3.6 prints that line, 3.0
   (Debian 12, Ubuntu 24.04) does not — the first cut passed on 3.6 and failed 7 rows on 3.0. Run
   interop gates on the OLDEST OpenSSL a consumer has, not only the newest.

10. **A fixed-length verify primitive needs its CALLER's length check** (6.6.16). sigil's
   `ed25519_verify(pk, msg, msg_len, sig)` takes no signature length and always reads 64 bytes, so
   a caller holding a wire-supplied length must check `== 64` FIRST — and bind the key type too:
   `x509_cert_pubkey` returns the cert's 96-byte key slot for every key type, so an Ed25519 verify
   against a P-256 leaf decodes the first 32 bytes of X as an Ed25519 point. The native server
   signature path (`_tn_verify_sig_scheme`) did neither: a 65-byte field verified with its tail
   ignored, a 63- or 0-byte one borrowed bytes from the next handshake message. The client path and
   sigil's own chain link already checked. When a primitive's API has a fixed size, grep every
   caller for the length check — the API is correct; the omission is at the call.
11. **A certificate PURPOSE binds the whole path, not the leaf** (CYRIUS-2026-0026, 6.6.16). `_tn_verify_chain`
   applied server/client purpose to the LEAF only and vetted CAs for keyCertSign and pathLen, never
   their own extendedKeyUsage — so a clientAuth- or S/MIME-only CA could issue a leaf the native
   client took as a TLS server, and a serverAuth-only CA in an mTLS server's trust set could issue
   a client identity (OpenSSL refuses both, error 26); the contract doc's "extendedKeyUsage
   honoured" was made to look true by the leaf check. ONE purpose predicate (`_tn_eku_purpose_ok`)
   at every depth — leaf, intermediates, anchor; absent = unrestricted; anyExtendedKeyUsage =
   either purpose (RFC 5280; OpenSSL 3.6.5 honours it at NO depth — a documented divergence). And
   put a CA constraint INSIDE the conjunction that SELECTS a path candidate (`_tn_ca_signer_ok`),
   not after the path is chosen: a wrongly purposed CA is then skipped and path building reaches a
   rightly purposed duplicate (cross-signed / re-issued, same subject and key), where OpenSSL's
   verdict depends on the order the peer lists the chain. Testing trick: give each family of CAs
   ONE subject and ONE key and vary only the EKU, so every row's single variable is the EKU
   (`tls_native_ca_eku.tcyr`). sigil records only the serverAuth bit; the clientAuth half reads
   the DER (`_tn_cert_eku_client_auth`), so a root's DER must stay live — it does on every install
   path.
12. **An OpenSSL "function" you dlsym may be a MACRO** (6.6.16). `SSL_CTX_set_session_cache_mode`
   is `SSL_CTX_ctrl(ctx, SSL_CTRL_SET_SESS_CACHE_MODE = 44, mode, NULL)` in 1.1 and 3.x (the GET is
   45); it has no symbol in libssl.so, so dlsym returns 0, and a wrapper that reads 0 as "libssl
   lacks it" is a silent no-op — `tls_ctx_set_session_cache_mode` set nothing on every OpenSSL 3,
   and the resumption probe that required the pointer read 0. When binding libssl by name, check
   `nm -D --defined-only libssl.so.3` for EVERY name; a missing one is a macro — call the ctrl with
   the ssl.h number. The sweep of the 49 names the stdlib resolves found this one plus
   `SSL_get_peer_certificate` (only the 1.1 fallback after `SSL_get1_peer_certificate`).
13. **A failed OpenSSL handshake RESUMES on the next SSL_read** (6.6.16). SSL_read / SSL_write on an
   SSL still in init run the handshake implicitly, so a wrapper that reports SSL_connect /
   SSL_accept failed (0) and then lets the caller read hands back application data on a connection
   the caller was told had failed (measured: `tls_read` returned the server's 21 bytes after a
   complete that failed on a non-blocking socket); a second SSL_connect re-drives it and against a
   late peer succeeds. The wrapper keeps its OWN sticky failure (shim +32): refuse every later
   read, write and complete without calling SSL, skip SSL_shutdown, and count WANT_READ on a
   non-blocking socket with no deadline as TLS_ERR_HANDSHAKE_FAILED, never WOULD_BLOCK (the caller
   would retry for ever). Related, measured on 3.6.5: the handshake state machine clears the error
   queue on entry, so a stale libcrypto error cannot decide SSL_connect's SSL_get_error — but
   SSL_read does NOT, so the clear before it is load-bearing (CYRIUS-2026-0016): deleting it turns 128 of
   tls_libssl_read_errors' 348 rows RED, deleting the pre-handshake one turns none RED. Same
   release: libssl-only verbs never touch a native ctx.
14. **Windows' disallowed CTL entries are public-key MD5s, not thumbprints** (6.7.12, WN-6).
   `DisallowedCertEncodedCtl` (SubjectAlgorithm "disallowedHash", 1.3.6.1.4.1.311.10.11.15) is NOT a SHA-1
   thumbprint list: on cass 88 entries, 82 of 16 bytes (the MD5 of the certificate's subjectPublicKey —
   crypt32's prop 25), 6 of 48 bytes (form unknown at 6.7.12 — SINCE 6.7.14 (PL-10, D57): prop 15 of SHA-384-signed
   certificates, the six DarkMatter intermediates, each `CERT_TRUST_IS_EXPLICIT_DISTRUST` on cass with both Disallowed
   stores empty; applied; prop 15's length follows the certificate's own signature digest, and the 20 / 32 / 64-byte
   forms are not applied yet — filed, Low, latent: today's CTL holds none), none of 20. Established against DigiNotar Root
   CA (`CertGetCertificateChain`: `CERT_TRUST_IS_EXPLICIT_DISTRUST`, the Disallowed store empty) and the
   2024-2025 Fina CA 1.1.1.1 certificates. Trap: `CertFindSubjectInCTL`, which uses prop 15, does not find
   them — only the chain engine does; prop 15 (CERT_SIGNATURE_HASH) is the TBS hashed with the certificate's
   OWN signature digest (16 / 20 / 32 / 48 bytes). Hash it without allocating: `lib/sha1.cyr`'s one-shot
   allocates per call, a per-connect leak on the no-free heap, so the verifier carries a private `_tn_md5`.
15. **ProtectedRoots is applied by crypt32, and a user root needs a desktop** (6.7.12, WN-6).
   `ProtectedRoots\Flags` are applied by crypt32 inside `CertOpenStore` of the logical ROOT store, read ONLY
   from `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\SystemCertificates\Root\ProtectedRoots` (the non-Policies key
   changes nothing) — a loader that opens the logical store already honours them, and reimplementing them (a
   second key, a LocalMachine switch) can only diverge. Measured: `Flags = 8` took the store from 59 roots to
   12 at the next open. Trap for tests: a CurrentUser root cannot be added from a non-interactive session
   (`certutil -user -addstore Root` -> 0x80070032 ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED, as administrator and as SYSTEM), and a
   root planted straight into the HKCU registry is purged at the next open — user-root behaviour on Windows
   needs an interactive desktop. A probe that sets machine policy is visible to every process for its
   duration: opt-in, administrator only, never touching an existing key.

(The name still says seven; fifteen is the count.)

**AND ONE STRUCTURAL LESSON FROM 6.6.15: one step written once per side drifts.** The 1.2 path
checked x25519's all-zero result (RFC 8446 §7.4.2 MUST — sigil's `x25519` does not flag a
low-order point) and neither 1.3 side did (the all-zero x25519 check item); the 1.2 client's new ECDHE zeroed its secrets
and the other three handshake paths never had (the ECDHE key zeroisation gap — found by comparing them). Both closed by
collapsing the step into one helper for every side and version (`_tn_ecdhe_shared`,
`_tn_ephemeral_keygen`, `_tn_wipe_ephemeral`). When one path of four does a check, the review
question is why the other three don't — not whether the one is needed.

CHANGELOG [6.6.14] [6.6.15] [6.6.16]; cyrius `docs/development/lib-tls-contract.md`. User-facing behaviour:
language/stdlib_modules.cyml `tls_module_contract_v6614`.

[[entries]]
name = "secret_var_zeroisation_skipped_four_ways_and_copied_around_once_enumerate_every_exit_and_every_epilogue_store"
description = "CLASS — five holes in one guarantee, 6.6.5 to 6.6.15. `secret var` is zeroised by the defer walker (`_defer_emit_walk`) on the way out, so the guarantee is exactly as good as two enumerations: every way control leaves the fn passes through the walker, and nothing the epilogue stores outlives the wipe. Four holes were exits or targets that skipped it (wrong storage, a closure attributed to its encloser, a tail call, a `#naked` fn — the closure secret-var wipe gap, the tail-call secret-var wipe gap, the naked-fn secret-var gap); one was the walker's OWN register save, released uncleared after the wipe (the secret-var epilogue spill gap) — created by the 6.6.7 fix for the walker's previous hole"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["secret", "secret-var", "defer", "epilogue", "_defer_emit_walk", "EDEFER_RESTORE", "zeroisation", "naked", "tail-call", "closure", "security", "x86_64", "aarch64", "the closure secret-var wipe gap", "the tail-call secret-var wipe gap", "the secret-var epilogue spill gap", "the naked-fn secret-var gap", "v6.6.5", "v6.6.7", "v6.6.15"]
---
`secret var buf[N]` registers a synthetic `defer` whose body is a memset(0); the epilogue walker
runs it. Every instance below compiled clean, exited 0 and left the key in memory.

| # | release | hole | shape |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6.6.5 | an over-budget `secret var` fell back to static storage and `FINDLOCAL` found an outer local of the same name, so the zeroise cleared the wrong storage (`a_fn_local_static_array_must_not_take_a_global_name`) | skipped — wrong target |
| 2 | 6.6.7, the closure secret-var wipe gap | a `secret var` in a closure body registered on the ENCLOSING fn | skipped — wrong fn |
| 3 | 6.6.7, the tail-call secret-var wipe gap | `return f(..);` took the tail-call path, which jumps past the walker — and `return Ok(v);` is that shape | skipped — exit outside the walker |
| 4 | 6.6.15, the naked-fn secret-var gap | a `#naked` fn has no epilogue; the walker emitted after its body was unreachable (since `#naked` landed, 6.2.27) | skipped — no epilogue |
| 5 | 6.6.15, the secret-var epilogue spill gap | the walker saves the whole return convention in 64 bytes below sp (x86_64 rax, rdx, r8, the entry rsp, xmm0, xmm1; aarch64 x0-x3, q0, q1), runs the blocks, reloads, and released the area UNCLEARED — after the wipe | copied around |

**#5 is the shape to recognise.** The save exists so that a call inside a defer body cannot
destroy the return value; 6.6.7 widened it from rax alone (which the caller receives anyway) to
every return register. **A save whose job is to preserve registers ACROSS the wipe is, by
construction, a copy of them the wipe never sees.** In sigil 3.13.7, `ecdsa_p256_sign` returned
with xmm1 still holding the HMAC_DRBG's last SHA-NI state — the RFC 6979 nonce k — and its own
`secret var` epilogue parked it 212-224 bytes below the frame, directly above its 8 KB stack
burn; k plus one signature is the private key (d = r^-1 (s*k - e)). Fix (`EDEFER_RESTORE`,
`src/backend/x86/float.cyr` / `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`): reload, take the entry rsp into
r11, eight `mov qword [rsp+8i], 0` (immediates — no return register touched), `mov rsp, r11`;
aarch64 four `stp xzr, xzr` before `add sp, sp, #64`. Every fn with a walker, a plain `defer`
included: +74 B (x86_64) / +16 B (aarch64) each; other fns byte-identical. cx is unaffected in
the guest-visible sense (its save is pushes on cxvm's private data stack).

**#4** is now refused by name, as `return` always was: `defer is not allowed in a #naked fn`,
`secret var is not allowed in a #naked fn`. On cx `#naked` is inert (framed), so all three work.
⚠ A plain `var` in a naked body still compiles and stores through the CALLER's rbp — declare
nothing there; put key material in a framed wrapper that calls the naked fn.

⭐ **THE HABIT, for any change that adds an exit or an epilogue store.** List every way control
leaves a fn — fall-off, `return`, the tail path, `?`, a coroutine's completion, a naked body's
own `ret` — and check the walker is on each; list every store the epilogue makes AFTER the walker
and check each is cleared or holds only the return value. That survey is how #4 was found beside
#5: the regalloc restore and `leave; ret` only load, a coroutine completion pushes only the
returned value and its heap slot, and a `secret var` fn never tail-calls (since #3).

**TEST SHAPE that catches a copy-around** (`tests/tcyr/crossos/secret_epilogue_save_area.tcyr`):
inline asm puts a distinct marker in EVERY saved register as the fn's last statement, the fn falls
off its end, and a sibling scans its own uninitialised window. Scrub the stack between rows (a
previous row's residue reads as this row's); keep an anti-vacuous control that leaves the markers
one frame down (the window must SEE them), a no-walker control, and value rows proving the return
value, `(a, b)`, `(a, b, c)`, f64 and f64v4 survive the clear. Pre-fix it found 7 of 7 markers on
x86_64 and 8 of 8 on aarch64, on every host.

What `secret var` never covered — a callee's dead frame, vector registers, heap buffers, timing —
is the user-facing half: language/features.cyml `secret_var_compound_ops`. CHANGELOG [6.6.5]
[6.6.7] [6.6.15]; cyrius docs/audit/2026-09-03-security-audit.md the closure secret-var wipe gap, -47, -69, -72.

[[entries]]
name = "an_intern_search_that_writes_the_candidate_before_searching_must_stop_at_the_committed_end"
description = "cycc interned a string literal by comparing its bytes plus terminator against each pool entry, walking entries NUL to NUL, with the new literal already written, uncommitted, at the pool's end. A literal holding a NUL could match a window that ran past the committed pool into its own copy, so it was aliased onto bytes the NEXT literal overwrote: after var A = \"a\", the literal \"a<NUL>a\" read 'z' at offset 2 once \"zzzz\" followed. Silent; measured by sankoch on cycc 6.6.4, fixed 6.6.15 — and meanwhile carried downstream as a coding rule (no NUL in any literal), the pattern of Win64's old six-argument rule"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "strings", "interning", "embedded-nul", "miscompile", "boundary", "off-by-one", "codegen-bug-as-language-rule", "sankoch", "v6.6.4", "v6.6.15"]
---
**THE TRAP, GENERALLY.** A dedup / intern table that appends the candidate BEFORE searching (so a
miss costs nothing) must bound every comparison window by the COMMITTED end — never by a
terminator convention the data can violate. Here the loop is now
`while (si + slen < sstart)` (src/frontend/lex.cyr), `sstart` being where the candidate sits.

**WHY ONLY NUL-BEARING LITERALS.** To reach `sstart` a window must straddle the previous entry's
terminator, which would have to equal one of the candidate's bytes — impossible unless the
candidate holds a NUL and is periodic over the pool's tail. So the fix changes nothing for
NUL-free literals: an instrumented pre-fix compiler logged zero cross-boundary matches across
the 560 in-tree files; fixpoint and seed-derive held. (Also why no gate saw it: cycc's own
source holds no such literal.)

**THE BOUNDARY IS STRICT.** At `si + slen == sstart` the window's terminator byte is the
candidate's own FIRST byte, so `"~r\0\0Q12"` then `"\0Q12\0"` aliased under `<=` and lost its
terminator to the next literal. Pin an off-by-one fix with a row exactly ON the boundary — in
tests/tcyr/frontend/string_interning_embedded_nul.tcyr the `<=` mutant fails that row only.

**THE CONSUMER COST.** Until 6.6.15 sankoch carried ADR 004 ("no string literal may contain a
NUL byte"), a lexer-accurate CI gate enforcing it, and a generator that PROVES its one exempt
literal — RFC 7932's 122 KB Brotli dictionary, 70 NULs — is not periodic at any NUL. All of it is
retirable. An ecosystem survey at the fix (4,291 NUL-bearing literals) found one hit, rekha
`programs/hint_unit_vectors.cyr` 326 / 327, benign by luck. When a consumer writes a rule around
the compiler, the rule is the bug report — see
`win64_ecallpops_five_register_shuttle_broke_at_ten_args_and_the_bug_was_written_down_as_a_language_rule`.
CHANGELOG [6.6.15].

[[entries]]
name = "top_level_has_no_frame_so_a_lowering_that_needs_one_must_refuse_or_bring_its_own"
description = "CLASS (6.6.16). Top-level cyrius code runs with no stack frame (its vars are globals; rbp / x29 / cx fp hold whatever the loader left), so any lowering that spills to FRAME SLOTS must either refuse top level by name, give its storage another way, or open a frame of its own. The trap is the fourth option — gating the lowering on 'inside a fn' and letting top level silently FALL BACK to an older path. 6.6.16's instance: a top-level `fncallN` skipped the closure-aware indirect-call lowering and called lib/fnptr.cyr, which jumps to a capturing closure's bit-63-tagged value (SIGSEGV / 0xC0000005), and on cx returned 0 for every callee"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["top-level", "frame", "closures", "fncall", "callptr", "PINDIRECT_CALL", "cx", "pe-stack-probe", "alignment", "v6.6.16"]
---
**THE ANSWERS SO FAR.** REFUSED by name — the SIMD intrinsics (6.6.2, `_SIMD_RESERVE`), `bitset` /
`bitclr` (v5.10.35), a struct-returning call (6.6.6, "a struct result needs storage in a fn's
frame"), `defer`. GIVEN STORAGE ANOTHER WAY — a top-level `for x in` variable and every hidden
temp are GLOBALS at top level (6.6.8, `_HTEMP` / `_HTNAMED`). Both honest. SILENTLY FALLEN BACK —
`fncallN` from 6.5.17 to 6.6.15: `PINDIRECT_CALL` (spill the callee, read bit 63 to pick "closure
or plain pointer") was gated `_cur_fn_ix >= 0` in TWO places (`_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL`,
`_PARSE_STMT_IMPL`), and top level kept the pre-6.5.17 meaning. The gate's comment called that path
"the only thing that ever worked there anyway" — true for a closure LITERAL (which needs a frame to
be built), false for one a fn built and RETURNED, stored in a global, or passed through memory.
`callptr`, the same lowering under another name, had been REFUSED at top level the whole time: two
spellings of one call disagreed about whether top level was legal.

**HOW IT HID.** Every row of the 6.5.17 gate (`closure_escape_dispatch.tcyr`) called from inside a
fn. And lib/fnptr.cyr has no cx arm, so on cx EVERY top-level fncallN returned 0 — three
crossos/stdlib files had been failing on cxvm for releases, and no gate runs cx over the crossos
corpus.

**THE FIX THAT SCALES: bring the frame.** At top level `PINDIRECT_CALL` saves + zeroes GFLC and
`_flc_hwm`, emits `ETLFRAME_OPEN` (x86 `push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; sub rsp,imm32`; aarch64 / cx EFNPRO
+ ESUBRSP), runs the unchanged body, patches the size from max(GFLC, `_flc_hwm`) — the high-water
matters because hidden temps and the inline replay release GFLC — and closes. In-fn codegen is
byte-identical. Three details that are easy to get wrong:
  * x86 PARITY: size the frame so 8 (saved rbp) + size == 0 mod 16. Round to 16 instead and every
    top-level indirect callee lands 8 bytes off — invisible to cyrius callees, fatal to an
    SSE-spilling extern-C one (the .tcyr compares `&local & 15` reached directly and indirectly).
  * PE STACK PROBE: the size is only known at the CLOSE, so the open always emits the page walk,
    with the size in r10 so rax survives. On cass with the frame forced to 512 KiB: probed runs,
    unprobed 0xC0000005 (wine does not discriminate).
  * IR: record the open/close as IR_RAW_EMIT, never IR_PROLOGUE / IR_ALLOC_FRAME.

**6.7.8 (checked dyn) — all three answers in one feature.** Forming a dyn builds it in a frame
temp, so at top level a FORM into an assignment, a field or an argument is REFUSED by name ("a dyn
formed at top level has no frame to be built in - …"); a top-level `var G: dyn S = ..;` GIVES IT
STORAGE (the global); a call through a dyn at top level BRINGS THE FRAME (`_dyn_call_top` opens
`PINDIRECT_CALL`'s micro-frame). And a fourth-option instance found on the way, reported: a
declaration-zone global initialised from a 9-16 B METHOD result (`var G2: Q = Q0.mk();`) — the zone
replay refuses only a whole FREE call and stored rax, the first word, into the pointer-mode global
(SIGSEGV at startup, 6.7.4 – 6.7.8); for a dyn destination it is refused by name.

**RECOGNISE IT AGAIN:** grep for `_cur_fn_ix >= 0` / `GINFN(S) == 1` used as a GATE on a lowering
(not on a lookup). Each must end in a refusal, a global, or a frame — never in "the old path".
User-facing: language/features.cyml `indirect_calls_at_top_level_since_6_6_16`. CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

[[entries]]
name = "an_environment_reader_that_stops_at_a_buffer_is_a_silently_missing_variable"
description = "v6.6.16 (C10): cycc's include fallback read /proc/self/environ ONCE into 4,096 bytes and matched only HOME= — while its comments said CYRIUS_HOME — so a 5,000-byte variable ahead of HOME made the store fallback vanish, and CYRIUS_HOME was never honoured at all. The CLI had fixed the same class at 6.6.11 (J4, an 8 KB cap on the child envp); the compiler's three readers were each written separately and each capped differently"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["environment", "proc-self-environ", "include", "CYRIUS_HOME", "fixed-buffer", "truncation", "comment-is-not-evidence", "v6.6.11", "v6.6.16"]
---
**THE SHAPE.** A fixed-size read of /proc/self/environ looks like "read the environment" and is
really "read the first N bytes of it". Nothing fails: a variable past the cut is simply absent,
and one straddling it is TRUNCATED, which for a path means a DIFFERENT directory. CI runners carry
large environments, so the cut is hit in CI and not on a laptop.

**THE READERS (6.6.16):** `_init_cyrius_lib` (lex.cyr) — fixed: chunked read to EOF, match state
carried across reads, values bounded at 1,984 B and REFUSED (never truncated) past it. Still capped
(backlog): `_read_env` (src/backend/common/env.cyr, 8,191 B, values cut to 255 B) and
`_env_var_is_1` (lex.cyr, 4,096 B — CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT=1 is ignored past 4 KB, measured).

**THE TEST SHAPE.** Put the variable behind padding in an `env -i` run (5,000 B, 9,000 B) AND split
its NAME and its VALUE across the read boundary (pad lengths that land `NAME=` at offsets 4085,
4091, 4079 …). A per-chunk matcher passes the padding rows and fails the split rows; a single read
fails both. tests/gates/toolchain/include_fallback_cyrius_home.sh.

**AND THE COMMENT.** The fallback's comments said "CYRIUS_HOME" for years while the code read HOME,
and a gate (distlib_sidecar_verified.sh) pinned HOME to a throwaway to work around it. A comment
describing the intended behaviour is not evidence of the behaviour; the gate's workaround was.
⚠ **AND A FALLBACK THAT ANSWERS HIDES WHAT NEEDED IT.** The same release found 36 shell gates that
derived their root from `$0` and never `cd`'d there (G1); 14 of them were invisible to the sweep
because their only cwd-relative include was `lib/...`, which the store fallback resolved — against
the STORE's lib, not the tree's — whenever the slot existed. Probe cwd-dependence under a HOME with
no `.cyrius`; the check driver now runs every gate from `/`.
6.7.10: `_read_env` hands back ONE shared 256 B scratch, so a value kept past the next read must be COPIED
out (`_cov_path` keeps CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE); and the 0 / 1 rung is one fn now, `_pp_env_rung(S, name)`
(lex_pp.cyr), shared by CYRIUS_BOUNDS and CYRIUS_COVERAGE — a third copy of the rung is how two flags came
to read `0` / empty differently.
User-facing: language/core.cyml `syntax` INCLUDES. CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

6.7.11 (TL-8): "IS IT SET" IS NOT "IS IT ON". `_read_env` returns a pointer for any set variable, an empty one
included, so each fork's `if (_read_env("CYRIUS_POISON") != 0)` turned poison on for `0`, `''` and `no`, while the
two switches already on the rung read `0` / empty as off and refused a word. The third switch now goes through the
same rung, once, in PREPROCESS (before `PP_PASS`, so before the first `#ifdef`). Under wine and cmd.exe an empty
variable reads as UNSET, so a Windows leg cannot tell the old rule from the new on the empty row.

[[entries]]
name = "bound_tparam_code_is_not_a_width_v6616"
description = "A bound type parameter's _tp_resolve value is a RETURN-TYPE code (-9 = f64), not a width — three readers took it as one (fixed 6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["generics", "type-parameters", "encoding", "sizeof", "f64", "_tp_resolve", "v6.6.16"]
---
`_tp_resolve(S, noff)` answers with `_classify_return_type`'s encoding: a positive sid, -1/-2/-4/-8
for i8..i64, **-9 for f64**, the SIMD band. It LOOKS like the `0 - width` encoding scalar_type
uses, and three readers treated it so: sizeof(T), the `[T]` / `slice<T>` element width, and
PARSE_VAR's `var y: T` arm (`scalar_type = 0 - _ltc`). With T = f64 each saw a 9-byte type — sizeof
9, `T[N]` reserving 9 per element, an untyped 9-"wide" local — and with T = i8 the local lost its
sign (the encoding has no sign; the old ladder took it from the NAME, which is `T`).

6.6.16 converts the code ONCE (`_tn_from_rc`, parse_types.cyr) into the resolver's band and reads
it only through the accessors (`_tn_iw`, `_tn_sgn`, `_tn_flt`, `_tn_sid`, `_tn_vec`, `_tn_bytes`).
Rule: never do arithmetic on a `_tp_resolve` / `_classify_return_type` value; convert it. The first
cut converted two of the three readers — the third was in a function the same bite rewrote, found
by review. The one vocabulary: language/core.cyml `type_names_v6616`.

[[entries]]
name = "scope_depth_is_not_block_depth_an_elif_body_opens_no_scope"
description = "To ask 'is this statement at true module scope?' cycc's GSDEP (scope depth) is the wrong question: an `elif` body (and its `else`) is parsed by PARSE_PROG with no SCOPE_PUSH, so GSDEP is 0 inside it — and a switch arm, an @unsafe body and a for-init clause are parsed by PARSE_STMT with no PARSE_PROG at all. 6.6.16 counts PARSE_PROG's own nesting (`_pp_depth == 1` is module scope) and routes the PARSE_STMT-only bodies through a wrapper that bumps it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "scope", "block", "elif", "switch", "unsafe", "top-level", "module-scope", "v6.6.16"]
---
**THE SITES.** src/frontend/parse_ctrl.cyr PARSE_ELIF calls PARSE_PROG for its body and its `else`
with no SCOPE_PUSH (PARSE_IF's `if` / `else` push one); `for`'s body is `_loop_body(S, 0)` because
the `for` already opened its scope. So "GSDEP == 0" is true inside some top-level blocks.

**AND PARSE_PROG IS NOT EVERY BODY.** A `switch` case / default arm (both the chain and the x86
jump-table loops), the `@unsafe { }` body and a `for`'s init clause parse nested statements with
PARSE_STMT directly. A counter kept in PARSE_PROG alone reads 1 inside them — the first cut baked
`case 0: var b: i64[2] = {1, 2};` at a top-level switch whose arm never ran, and took `@unsafe { }`
while refusing a bare `{ }`. They go through `_PARSE_STMT_NESTED` (parse.cyr), a wrapper so
`_PARSE_STMT_IMPL`, a cybs-sensitive hot fn, gains no global reference. When adding a statement
form whose body is not a PARSE_PROG block, route its inner statements through that wrapper.

**WHAT TO USE.** `_pp_depth` (incremented at PARSE_PROG's entry, decremented at its end) is 1
exactly at module scope; with `GINFN(S) != 1` that is "a statement outside every fn and every
block". tests/gates/frontend/array_initializer_refusals.sh: with GSDEP in place of `_pp_depth`
only the `elif` row fails (it is the row that justifies the counter); with the wrapper's bump
removed the switch-arm, `@unsafe` and for-init rows fail. CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

[[entries]]
name = "one_futex_word_two_kinds_of_waiter_wake_one_strands_the_other_kind"
description = "Parking senders AND receivers on one futex word with wake-one deadlocks a channel with several producers and consumers; give each kind its own seq word (an eventcount). lib/thread.cyr's channel had the bug from v5.5.31 to 6.6.15 because every test had one producer and one consumer"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["threads", "futex", "channels", "deadlock", "eventcount", "lost-wakeup", "mpmc", "testing", "v6.6.16"]
---
lib/thread.cyr's channel parked receivers ("empty") and senders ("full") on the SAME word,
count@32, and every operation woke ONE thread there. With several producers or consumers that
wake can reach the wrong kind: a receive wakes a receiver parked on "empty", which finds the ring
empty and re-parks, while the sender parked on "full" sleeps on with room in the ring. Nothing else
wakes it, and the process hangs with the mutex free. Measured at 6.6.15: four producers and four
consumers deadlocked on every run on x86_64 (40/40 at cap 1, 27/27 at cap 3); one producer and
three consumers on cap 1, a saturated worker pool, hung its producer on pi. It lived the channel's
whole life because every test had one producer and one consumer, and the only multi-consumer user
(sandhi) sized its channel so senders rarely waited.

A second defect shared the word: `closed` was not part of it. A receiver that saw "open and
empty", unlocked, and lost the CPU before FUTEX_WAIT slept through chan_close's wake.

**THE FIX IS AN EVENTCOUNT, not "wake all":** each kind of waiter gets its own seq word plus a
count of registered waiters, all under the lock; a waiter registers, reads its seq, unlocks, and
parks on that value; a waker that finds the other kind registered advances THAT kind's seq under
the lock and wakes one after unlocking. The parked value then changes whenever the state the
waiter waits for changes (close included), so the kernel's value check closes the race window —
and an uncontended operation makes no syscall (before, every send and receive made a FUTEX_WAKE).

**The general rule:** a futex word with heterogeneous waiters needs WAKE_ALL, or one word per
waiting condition. Wake-one is correct only when any waiter can use any wake. Test a channel with
N > 1 on BOTH sides, with a deadline (field_notes/compiler/patterns.cyml
`a_test_that_may_hang_must_fail_not_hang`). User-facing: language/features.cyml
`chan_blocking_capability_v6616`. CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

[[entries]]
name = "deps_manifest_field_becomes_a_path_every_joined_field_needs_its_own_traversal_guard"
description = "CLASS — the include filename-capture overflow bug, the deps-header dot-dot path bug, the deps-tag dot-dot path bug. `cbt/deps.cyr` builds the clone dir as `<home>/deps/<name>/<tag>`, and three releases each guarded ONE field of that join and left the next: v6.2.51 (the include filename-capture overflow bug) the modular sub-module / index-leaf names, 6.6.13 (the deps-header dot-dot path bug) the `[deps.NAME]` header, 6.6.16 (the deps-tag dot-dot path bug) the `tag`. Each was found by a later review measuring a NEIGHBOURING feature, not by a sweep of the join"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "cbt", "path-traversal", "manifest", "security", "the include filename-capture overflow bug", "the deps-header dot-dot path bug", "the deps-tag dot-dot path bug", "v6.2.51", "v6.6.13", "v6.6.16"]
---
Two non-obvious shapes, both measured on 6.6.15:
- git creates a clone dir's LEADING components before it rejects the ref, and removes only the
  last one it created — so "git refused it, exit 1" still leaves `<home>/../esc` behind.
- A traversal value that names an EXISTING directory skips the clone (`is_dir` is true) and lands
  in the tampered-cache refusal, which printed `rm -rf <that path>` as repair advice. A refusal
  message that prints a derived path can be weaponised; validate the inputs so the path is always
  inside the cache, and never print one built from an unvalidated field.

**RULE when adding a manifest key that becomes a path component:** validate it right after the key
loop, before any gate, mkdir, clone or is_dir, with a check that refuses `..`, a leading `/` / `-`,
a `.`-led component, `\`, control bytes and (if the key is present) an empty value — and guard the
CALL on the key being present (`dep_tag != 0`), or every tagless dep is refused. Print the refused
value with non-printing bytes escaped. Then grep the JOIN, not the field you were asked about:
every field it concatenates is a member of this class. Still open (backlog): the read side —
`modules` entries and a transitive `path`. CHANGELOG [6.6.16]; gate
`deps_modules_default_or_warned.sh` D8/D9. See also dependencies.cyml (the include filename-capture overflow bug).

[[entries]]
name = "fold_a_hand_written_chain_by_simulating_it_in_its_own_order_and_prove_it_by_interpreting_it"
description = "6.6.18 XLAT-1/2. ESYSXLAT, the x86 → aarch64 syscall renumbering chain, is a linear cmp/b.ne list whose ORDER is semantics, and it was inlined at every aarch64 syscall site (36 % of cycc-native-aarch64). The fold does not re-derive the mapping: it EMITS the chain, decodes it, and SIMULATES it in emission order with cur = N, keeping only the row bodies that would run — so every ordering rule holds by construction and the row SOURCE the gates read is untouched. The proof was an interpreter, not a corpus: 772 numbers × 1–7 args × ELF / Mach-O × literal / variable, folded result vs the chain interpreted row by row — 0 differences"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["esysxlat", "aarch64", "syscall", "compile-time-fold", "equivalence-check", "interpreter", "naked", "x30", "v6.6.18"]
---
Measured: cycc-native-aarch64 2,042,184 → 1,323,392 B (−718,792, −35.2 %); 0 `cmp x8,#imm`
words left; a variable number calls one shared per-class stub (`ESYSX_STUBS`, between EEXIT and
FIXUP).

**The row-shape invariant (checked on every fold — a violation is an internal error naming the
row, never a silent fallback).** A row is `cmp x8,#src` / `b.ne +4k` / k-1 body words; it writes
x8 only as its LAST word (`movz x8`); on Mach-O it ends in `movz x16`; it reads flags only after
setting them in its own body; it branches only inside its own body; no ADR/ADRP/LDR-literal/BL,
FP/SIMD, system instructions or BC.cond. Adding a row that breaks this stops EVERY aarch64 build.

**Why an interpreter beats the corpus here.** The corpus exercises the numbers cyrius happens to
call; the fold must be right for every number the chain could see. Interpreting the emitted
chain word by word for the whole number space, at every arity and on both object formats, is
cheap and exhaustive — and it found the arm64-macOS 7-argument variable site that kept its
`bl .` placeholder (an infinite loop), which led to refusing `syscall()` past six arguments on
every target. Known blind spot: a fold that skips its `cur` update is invisible while no live
re-capture row exists (backlog).

**x30 is live in a `#naked` fn.** Any emitter that introduces a `bl` into user code must keep a
frameless fn on an inline path — XLAT-2's stub call does. And the stub `bl`s are RESOLVED with
no fixup-table entry: a future aarch64 code-moving pass (DCE compaction) must repair them.
CHANGELOG [6.6.18] (aarch64 — the ESYSXLAT compile-time fold).

6.7.12 — THE x86 MACH-O TWIN (DW-4, XLAT-1 + XLAT-2 on x86) AND XLAT-3 (DW-3). `_msx_fold` replays EMACHO_SYSXLAT
in a query mode (`_msx_qon == 2`) the same way — the rows untouched, so the route gates still read the truth: a
literal site is one `mov rax, imm32`, a run-time number calls one shared stub per arity class
(`EMACHO_SYSX_STUBS`, between EEXIT and FIXUP); the x86-macOS compiler 2,883,584 -> 1,937,408 B (−32.8 %). On x86 a
stub site must NOT be kept by its code offset — x86 compacts after emit (any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…
the 10th). And the fold now REPORTS the x16 it leaves so ESYSCALL drops the run-time pipe / fork fixups at a literal
site (XLAT-3): field_notes/compiler/patterns.cyml fold_once_report_the_fact_to_every_consumer_v6712.

[[entries]]
name = "a_verify_that_only_adds_to_a_seed_hides_every_gap_the_seed_happened_to_cover"
description = "6.6.18 distlib D1/D3. While the `.deps` sidecar was seeded from the producer's `[deps] stdlib` (v6.5.10 union), two blind spots in the compile-verify were invisible, because the declaration covered them. Removing the union (P4 option 2) made both publish broken sidecars — measured before shipping, so they became prerequisites D1 and D3"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "deps-sidecar", "compile-verify", "family-directory", "ifdef-arm", "agnos", "masked-defect", "v6.6.18"]
---
1. **The depth-0 family blind spot (D1).** The verify's snapshot index read only `lib/*.cyr`, so a
   symbol from a family directory (`lib/unicode/`) read as "not a stdlib symbol — the consumer's
   problem" and the fixpoint skipped it. niyama carried `unicode` only because its declaration was
   unioned in. Fix: a family directory is ONE index entry owned by the family name.
2. **An in-unit declarer is not proof of definition (D3).** When a name was still undefined on
   target T and a file declaring it was already in the unit, the verify called it "the target's own
   gap". But that declarer demonstrably does NOT define it on T (an `#ifdef` arm): mabda includes
   `lib/syscalls.cyr`, whose peers declare `O_RDWR`, yet on agnos only `lib/io.cyr`'s agnos arm
   defines it. Skip the in-unit declarer that failed and look for the one that does not.

The general shape: an inference that only ever ADDS to a hand-written seed inherits every
coverage gap the seed papered over. Before deleting the seed, run the inference ALONE on the
real producers (here: 11 folds × 6 targets) and see what it misses. Supersedes the v6.5.10 entry
`the_distlib_deps_sidecar_scanned_includes_not_the_declaration_…`. CHANGELOG [6.6.18].

[[entries]]
name = "a_changes_rerun_set_must_include_the_check_drivers_own_rows_not_just_the_shell_gates"
description = "6.6.18 D4 review BLOCKER. The distlib change re-ran 'all 33 gates that invoke distlib' — every tests/gates/*.sh naming it — and the merged regression suite still went RED: `programs/checks/deps_init.cyr`'s `_deps_sidecar_gate` is a CHECK-DRIVER ROW, compiled into the check program, not a shell gate, and it asserted the old contract (the umbrella-only `keccak` in the sidecar)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["check.sh", "programs-checks", "regression-suite", "rerun-set", "review", "distlib", "v6.6.18"]
---
`grep -rl distlib tests/gates` is not the set of things that test distlib. The check driver
(`programs/checks/main.cyr` and the files it includes — `deps_init.cyr`, `heap_audit.cyr`, …)
carries its own assertions, and they run only under the full `check.sh` / its `regression`
selector. When building the re-run set for a behaviour change, grep `programs/checks/` as well as
`tests/gates/` and `tests/tcyr/`, and run the selector that executes the driver rows. The row now
asserts the option-2 contract (7a63c931). CHANGELOG [6.6.18] (Distlib, review fixes).

[[entries]]
name = "two_check_sh_selectors_in_one_worktree_race_on_build_cyrius_check"
description = "6.6.18 integration. check.sh builds its driver to a FIXED path, `build/cyrius_check`, so two selectors started in parallel in ONE worktree overwrite each other's driver mid-run. Parallel check.sh runs are safe only across worktrees; within one, run selectors sequentially. (The 6.6.18 plan's 'in parallel if you like' was wrong.)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["check.sh", "worktree", "parallel", "race", "fixed-output-path", "process", "v6.6.18"]

[[entries]]
name = "interning_by_scan_was_quadratic_and_an_index_reproduces_it_only_for_nul_free_literals"
description = "6.6.19 B0b. LEX interned each new string literal by scanning EVERY segment of the string pool, so N literals cost N x pool bytes: 9,000 small literals 4.98 s, and one 1.9 MB literal ahead of 3,000 small ones 11.4 s (0.92 s placed after them) — exactly the shape `[embed]` makes, since cbt writes embeds first. An FNV-1a index of the pool's NUL-delimited segments made it ~0.9 s and self_compile −6.7 %, with BYTE-IDENTICAL output — but only because of a property worth knowing before touching it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lex", "interning", "string-pool", "quadratic", "fnv", "byte-identical", "embed", "perf", "v6.6.19"]
---
The scan returns the FIRST offset whose bytes match. For a literal with no NUL, that first match is
always the first pool SEGMENT of equal content, so an index keyed on each segment's first offset
reproduces the scan byte-for-byte. A literal holding an embedded NUL can match ACROSS segment
boundaries (`"b"` after `"a\0b"` lands at X+2), which no segment index sees — so those keep the old
committed-window scan (`_lex_intern_scan`, verbatim). The index is `alloc`'d (no heap-map / brk
change), catches up segment-aligned before each lookup, and rebuilds from 0 if the pool is wound
back below it (a `#deprecated` message is).

How it was proven, the recipe for any "faster, same output" compiler change: two-step self-host
fixpoint (old-built and new-built compilers emit the same compiler); every tcyr + 135 programs /
fuzz / bench / cbt sources compiled by BOTH compilers with every binary AND every diagnostic
compared; seed-derive (cybs, the hand-assembly bootstrap, also got fewer references from LEX losing
its loop); a scaling gate with RATIO rows (N vs 2N < 3.0x; 1.9 MB before vs after < 2.0x) that the
old scan fails (3.8x / 13.8x) and a mutant restoring it fails (3.7x / 11.6x). Measure scaling as a
ratio on one box, never an absolute time. CHANGELOG [6.6.19] (Compiler — B0b);
`tests/gates/frontend/string_intern_scale_linear.sh`.

[[entries]]
name = "trait_names_and_scratch_at_gnpos_v670"
description = "Two compiler gotchas met building 6.7.0 traits: a lookup name may be BUILD_METHOD_NAME's uncommitted scratch AT GNPOS (interning there overwrites it), and a lookup can run with the cursor far from the call (pre-scans), so a FINDFN-side diagnostic has no site."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.0", "traits", "FINDFN", "name-pool", "diagnostics"]
---
1. SCRATCH AT GNPOS. BUILD_METHOD_NAME writes `T_m` at GNPOS WITHOUT committing (lookup only). Any
helper that INTERNS at GNPOS (`_tr_join`, `_tl_use_alias`'s pattern) while that name is live
overwrites it: the ambiguity diagnostic first printed `'P_A_A_A_'` for `P_size`. Rule: inside a
FINDFN path, build temporary names in scratch FAR past GNPOS (6.7.0's `_tr_scratch(.., off)` at
+4096 / +8192) and intern only in pass 1, where nothing is pending.

2. NO SITE IN FINDFN. A name is looked up from pre-scans and probes with the cursor elsewhere (a
direct `P_size(p)` was first looked up with GTI in lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr). An error raised
inside FINDFN's miss path therefore reports the WRONG position, and a "cursor is right after the
name" heuristic misses the direct call. 6.7.0 reports the method form at its call (the cursor IS
past the method name there) and adds the explanation to the undefined-function report for the
direct form (`_tr_undef_note`). CHANGELOG [6.7.0].

3. ONE PRE-SCAN beats order-dependent registration: a trait declared below its impl, and a name
collision with an impl further down, are only knowable before pass 1 registers the first method —
`_tr_prepass` walks the token stream once (brace depth 0) and records every trait and impl.

[[entries]]
name = "generic_bounds_tokens_and_base_v671"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.1 trait bounds: key a per-call-site fact by its TOKEN index (the base, instances and inline replays re-parse the same tokens); an instance minted mid-definition corrupts the outer struct's field slice; the eager i64 base needs a 'not a valid instance' stub rule; and cybs's six-argument limit bites new helpers."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.1", "generics", "bounds", "field-pool", "cybs", "monomorphization"]
---
1. A CALL-SITE FACT KEYED BY TOKEN. A generic's body is parsed many times — the i64 base, every
instance (`_instantiate_generic_fn` re-invokes PARSE_FN_DEF from the def-start token), every inline
replay at a call site — always over the SAME tokens. A fact decided once in pass 1 about a call in
the body (6.7.1: "this `v.show(` is bound to trait Show") is therefore keyed by its method-name token
index (`_bs_find(ti)`), and every parse finds it; a per-parse context flag would have to be saved and
restored around every nested instance.

2. NEVER REGISTER A STRUCT INSIDE ANOTHER'S DEFINITION. REGSTRUCT sets `field_base[si] = pooltop`;
ADDFIELD then fills field_base + fc. An instance (`Box<i32>`) instantiated while the outer struct's
fields are being added appends ITS fields at pooltop — inside the outer's slice — and the outer's next
fields overwrite the pool (the mutation SIGSEGVs cycc). Instantiate every field type in a pre-scan
BEFORE REGSTRUCT (`_sdef_gen_prescan`); `_vec_field_elem` skips its nested args for the same reason.

3. THE EAGER BASE. A generic's base (T -> i64) is emitted at its definition. Any check that refuses
"T = i64" would then refuse the DEFINITION of a generic only ever called with good types. The rule
that already existed for `p.field` on a T (`_gen_stub`: the base is a dead `rax = 0` stub and a scalar
call is refused) extends: a base that is not a valid i64 instance (`_bnd_base_bad`) is a stub, and is
refused where used. The same for a generic struct's base definition reading `Box<T>`: record it
quietly (`_bnd_quiet`, `_sdef_bb`) and refuse W's base where W's base is used.

4. CYBS TAKES SIX ARGUMENTS (until 6.7.6). A seven-parameter helper (`_bnd_check(S, blk, tp, gnoff, g,
c0, c1)`) compiled with build/cycc and broke seed-derive; the cybs_call_arity_named gate runs cybs over
src/main.cyr in check.sh. 6.7.1 derived the extra values from a key (`_bnd_check(S, g, c0, c1)`).
⚠ Since 6.7.6 cybs passes stack arguments in cycc's order, so a 7+ argument helper in src/ is fine
(flag_skip_by_position_nested_parse_state_and_the_bootstrap_rung_v676).

5. `>>` CLOSES TWO LISTS. `<T: Show<i64>>`: skipping the refused bound's `<i64>` consumed the `>>`,
which also closed the type-parameter list; the capture loop then ran to EOF. A skip that can close
the enclosing list has to say so (`_ctp_closed`).

[[entries]]
name = "compile_time_evaluation_v672"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.2's const evaluator: no host FPU under cybs (software IEEE, NaN refused), mirror the parser's STATIC typing not runtime values, one interpreter for check + run (skip mode), a lazy pending flag behind GVECP, and reading a const context twice means evaluate once and replay by token."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.2", "const fn", "evaluator", "softfloat", "cybs", "GVECP"]
---
1. NO FLOAT IN THE COMPILER. cybs (the seed's compiler) has no f64 builtin and no float literal, so the
compiler cannot borrow the host FPU to fold f64: the evaluator uses an integer-only binary64 module
(`_sf_*`, 64x64->128 from 32-bit halves, long division with a sticky bit). Prove such a module
DIFFERENTIALLY against the hardware (millions of random pairs biased to halfway / subnormal / overflow,
plus an edge matrix, on x86 AND aarch64) and with mutations (no ties-to-even: 81,577 add mismatches).
NaN bits differ by target (x86 default NaN is negative, ARM positive) — refuse NaN rather than bake one.

2. MIRROR STATIC TYPES, NOT VALUES. At run time `var x = 2.0; x + 1.0` is an INTEGER add of the bit
patterns (an untyped var is an i64) and `f + 3` (f: f64) adds 3's BITS. A compile-time evaluator that
tracks "this value is a float" dynamically gives a different answer. Track the parser's static type:
the declared type of a local / param / return, the literal's type, the LEFT operand's type for an op.

3. ONE INTERPRETER FOR CHECKING AND RUNNING. The definition check of a const fn is the evaluator in SKIP
mode over the whole body (both arms of every if, loop bodies once, calls not executed but the callee
must be a const fn). A separate checker grammar would drift from the evaluator; skip mode cannot.

4. A LAZY CONSTANT BEHIND AN EXISTING FLAG. Top-level consts get a size-0 global slot with GVECP = 2
(pending); GVECP() evaluates on first read and leaves 1 (int: an ordinary enum-constant fold) or 0
(f64 / string, read through `_cst_vkind`). Every existing enum-constant reader took int consts for free.

5. READ TWICE, EVALUATE ONCE. A switch's case labels are read by its pre-scan and again by its body
paths; enum values in pass 1 and pass 2. Evaluating each time reports a bad label twice — evaluate in
the first reader and replay by TOKEN index (`_clm`), trimmed when the switch ends (nested ones stack).
And seven-argument helpers still broke seed-derive at 6.7.2 (cybs passed six) — no longer since 6.7.6,
which gave cybs stack arguments.

[[entries]]
name = "boolean_stamp_and_bool_typing_v673"
description = "Gotchas met adding 6.7.3's checked bool: a per-expression type flag would leak (GESTYPE is not cleared by integer ops); stamp the value by (code pointer, token index) instead; a lexer that silently drops a byte is a CVE; the rough return-type scan defaults unknown names to the retptr ABI; a fall-off value must precede the defer trampoline."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "bool", "stamp", "_BX_MARK", "lexer", "retptr", "defer"]
---
1. DON'T RIDE THE ESTYPE. GESTYPE is reset at each factor start but the integer `+ - * /` arms never
clear it, so after `a + ok` it holds the RIGHT operand's type — and EMIT_OP_DISPATCH reads any nonzero
estype as a struct id (operator overloading). A "this is a bool" estype would mark `n + ok` boolean
and send `ok + 1` to overload dispatch. Instead STAMP the value: every boolean producer records
(GCP, GTI) at its last emission (`_BX_MARK`); a write site asks whether both still match (`_BX_IS`).
Anything that ends in a boolean operand either emits after it or consumes a token (even `ok + 0`,
which folds to nothing, consumes two), so the stamp dies by itself — nothing has to remember to clear
it. Parens re-stamp the token across `)` (`_paren_post`). Same pattern as 6.6.10's `_FBR_MARK`.
6.7.8 added two. `_PK_MARK` / `_pk_word` (C2's `*p` at the element's width): the pointer-scale word
GPSC means "the LAST factor's", so after `x == q`, `m & q`, `1 * q`, `-q`, `~q` or `mk(q).n` it still
held q's word and a narrow `*p` would have read those at q's width; the (code pointer, token, word)
stamp is written where a pointer value is complete — the PARSE_FACTOR wrapper (judged by the tokens
the factor ended on: `-(q)` and `~(q)` end in `)` too and are NOT calls — a call is `name(`, `.name(`
or `name<..>(`), pointer arithmetic after its last emit, the if-expression's join — and only the KIND
is stripped from an unvouched word, never the step, so all arithmetic stays byte-identical.
`_PKD_MARK`: a fact ONE reader needs without the step every other reader would see — a one-byte
`n + p` keeps no step (6.6.17), and giving it one would have turned `(1 + p8) - q32` into an error;
a second stamp that only the dereference reads made `*(1 + p)` narrow and changed no other byte.
Compute a stamp-checked fact BEFORE the next emit: `_deref_store` reads its element before it pushes
the address — after the push the stamp no longer matches (every narrow store reverted to a word).

2. A LEXER THAT DROPS A BYTE IS A SECURITY BUG. `!` was silently skipped (`else { p = p + 1; }`) for
years: `if (!ok)` meant `if (ok)`. the dropped-at-sign lexer bug was the same shape for `@`. Every unknown byte must be a
token or an error, never nothing.

3. THE ROUGH RETURN-TYPE SCAN. PARSE_FN_DEF peeks `: T` BEFORE the parameters to decide the retptr
ABI, and an unrecognised name defaults to the retptr — which reserves arg-reg 0 and shifts every
parameter by one (x read from rsi: `fn f(x): bool` returned the wrong value). Every new scalar return
type must be added to that scan (`_rs_scalar_name`), not only to `_classify_return_type`.

4. A FALL-OFF VALUE GOES BEFORE THE DEFER TRAMPOLINE. With defers, the body's fall-through jumps round
the trampoline straight to the return landing; a `mov 0` emitted after the trampoline is skipped.

5. CHECK ONCE PER TOKEN. A generic base and its instances parse the same tokens; key the refusal by
the value's token (`_tn_once`) and skip it inside #inline replays (GIDEP > 0) and under --syntax-only.

[[entries]]
name = "set_so_far_flag_inside_a_loop_v673"
description = "A flag set when the parser REACHES a construct cannot answer a whole-body question inside a loop: 6.7.3's tail call before `x = &s` kept its jmp and handed the callee a freed frame. Decide such sites behind a patchable selector at the end of the OUTERMOST loop statement."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "tail call", "_fn_local_addr", "loop", "selector jmp", "TCO"]
---
1. THE TRAP. `_fn_local_addr` is set when the parser reaches the address, so a lexically earlier
`return f(..);` in the same loop body read 0 and kept its `jmp`. At run time the address is taken
BEFORE the next iteration's return. Only back-edges make "so far" differ from "whole"; outside loops
the old answer was right. (`while (i < 2) { if (i == 1) { return rd(x, k); } x = &s; i = i + 1; }`
gave 5 on x86 where 7 is right, since 6.5.14.)
2. WHY NOT PRESCAN. A lexical prescan (6.6.7's defer fix) works only when the token says everything;
since 6.6.20 most frame-address sources depend on types and callee signatures. A shadow parse would have
to roll back code, fixups, instances and closures.
3. THE PATTERN. Emit the site behind a patchable selector `jmp`, record what the alternative needs
(callee, argc, the marshalling's `_xdepth` / `_xcall_pad` / `_xcall_d`), and decide when the answer is
final: at the end of the OUTERMOST loop STATEMENT (a C-style `for` emits its step after the body).
Kept: EPATCH the selector to the next instruction. Diverted: EPATCH it to an out-of-line call stub at
the fn end. Nested bodies (closures, generic instances) carry their own range in `_fnst_save`.
4. BACKSTOP. An unresolved selector is `jmp +0` on x86 (the old wrong value, silently) but a
branch-to-self on aarch64 and cx — a missed hook must be an internal error (`_tcp_drop`).

[[entries]]
name = "toolchain_helpers_never_resolve_from_the_cwd_v673"
description = "A helper the toolchain runs comes from the INSTALL, never from the current directory and never from beside the binary: the cyriusly cmdtools CWD-script bug (compiled `cyriusly cmdtools` ran a checkout's `scripts/cyriusly`) is the Windows bare-name process-start bug class. A verb that only works in the dev tree is resolving something from the CWD."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "the cyriusly cmdtools CWD-script bug", "cyriusly", "install store", "CWD", "untrusted search path"]
---
The compiled `cyriusly cmdtools` ran its shell twin as the literal `scripts/cyriusly`, a CWD-relative
path: inside any cloned repository shipping one, a version-manager verb ran that repository's code
("PWNED from the checkout: cmdtools list", rc 0). The tell: the verb only ever WORKED inside a cyrius
checkout — nothing installed held the helper. The rule: resolve from `<home>/versions/<current>/...`
with `<home>` absolute and `current` checked as a version's shape (`../evil` is a path); refuse by name
when absent, never fall back; never beside the binary's own path (a binary run from a shared directory
would find a planted helper). Then EVERY store writer ships it (install.sh refresh-only / tarball /
source-bootstrap, the release builders) and scripts/verify-store.sh judges it. Gate:
tests/gates/toolchain/cyriusly_version_operand_refused.sh axes 7 and 9.

[[entries]]
name = "tool_mirrors_of_the_lexical_state_machine_v673"
description = "A tool that copies the preprocessor's lexical state machine must also copy PP_LEXST_AT's attribute rule: an attribute line is CODE, so a string opened on it spans lines; a copy that reads every `#` as a comment stays a quote out of step for the rest of the file."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "cyaudit", "api-surface", "PP_LEXST_AT", "LEXATTRWORD", "mirror"]
---
1. THE RULE THE COPIES MISSED. Since 6.6.20 PP_LEXST_AT asks LEXATTRWORD before treating a `#` in code
as a comment; the ten attribute words keep their line in code, string literals included.
2. BOTH DIRECTIONS. After `#assert 1 == 1, "x<LF>y"` cyaudit missed the next include (vet `no
dependencies`, deny `0 violations`, rc 0) on a file the compiler refuses; a VALID program whose attribute
string holds a raw line beginning `include "` got a false MISSING (rc 1). Probe both, compiler as oracle.
3. SIX HAND COPIES of the attribute list (cyrlint, cyrfmt, cyrdoc, cbt/srcscan, api-surface, cyaudit):
each needed a gate that DERIVES the words and their `(` rule from LEXATTRWORD's rows
(cyaudit_include_directives.sh axis 7 was the pattern). RESOLVED 6.7.11 (TL-1) by removing the copies, not
by censusing them: `LEXATTRBOUND` / `_lex_attr_is` / `LEXATTRWORD` moved into a pure `src/frontend/lex_attr.cyr`
that lex.cyr and the six tools INCLUDE. A census holding a copy to the original only DETECTS drift (and only for
the copy it covers — cyaudit's was the one held); an include removes it. The cost: the tools build only from a
checkout's root (a `src/` include), which every shipped build path already does. Gate:
lexer_attribute_word_boundary.sh C0 refuses any hand row.
4. THE OVER-CORRECTION GUARD: keep rows where a comment only STARTS like an attribute (`#ioctl "notes`)
and where `(` ends only three of the ten words (`#io("notes` a comment, `#deprecated(` code).

[[entries]]
name = "value_join_resets_and_unparsed_scanners_v674"
description = "Gotchas met adding 6.7.4's if-expression: a VALUE-producing join must reset every per-emit tracker that describes only the last branch (flags-reflect-rax, the rewinding peepholes, the constant-fold flag), classify a branch's type by SHAPE (estype leaks from intrinsic arguments), and every scanner that walks an expression unparsed must use the evaluator's grammar, not line breaks."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.4", "if expression", "join", "_flags_reflect_rax", "_cfo", "peephole", "scanner", "#derive"]
---
1. `_flags_reflect_rax` (x86 ECONDCMP skips its `test rax, rax` when the last emit set ZF from rax) is
true at a join only for the LAST branch; the then-path arrives with the CONDITION's flags. An
if-expression used as a condition branched on stale flags. Reset it at every value join (EPATCH does not).
2. `_cfo` / `_cfp` (a constant-foldable literal) let a following `+ 3` REWIND the code pointer to the
last branch's literal and re-emit — across the then-path's patched jump. The rewinding peephole
trackers (`_last_push_cp`, `_last_emovca_cp`, `_last_movca_popr_cp`) are the same hazard.
3. GESTYPE is not a reliable type: a name intrinsic (`mulh64(3, n)`) leaves its last argument's struct
type behind (filed; fixed 6.7.6 — the result is untyped). Decide "is this a struct value" by shape: a bare name, a whole call, a whole
method call (`_sc_whole`), a whole field (`_fpk_*`).
4. Pass-1 scanners that skip an expression (#assert, #derive enum values) must stop where the
evaluator stops. A line-break heuristic stopped `#assert 1<LF> == 1` mid-expression (6.7.2), and an
atom-only #derive value walk dropped every member after `A = 1 + 1`. Mirror the grammar (operand
(binop operand)*, balanced groups, if-chains), and ignore newlines as the evaluator does.
5. A forward const read in an UNTAKEN arm (skip mode) must not be forced (false cycle) and must not be
guessed (false accept): kind 4 = unknown, recorded and re-checked when every const is known.


[[entries]]
name = "new_loop_forms_and_lvalue_ladders_v675"
description = "Gotchas met adding 6.7.5's loop / do and field compound assignment: every loop form must DECLARE its continue mode, reset the store-elimination tracker and emit the IR landing pad; a do's continue is a forward jump and its tail-call verdict comes after the condition; a contextual keyword needs a name test in every scanner keyed on token types; an address ladder must mirror the base resolver; struct-copy sources key on the statement terminator."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.5", "loop", "do while", "continue mode", "_efl_store_cp", "IR_NOP", "contextual keyword", "compound assignment", "for step"]
---
1. A NEW LOOP FORM must set three things itself — none is inherited: the continue mode (0x18F898; 0 = jump
to top, 1 = the forward `_cont_open` / `_cont_close` chain — `_fnst_save` does not save it, so a loop that
forgets it inherits an enclosing for's mode and `continue` jumps to the for's step: the 6.6.3 class); the
x86 store-elimination tracker `_efl_store_cp = 0 - 1` at the back-edge target (else a reload of a variable
stored just before the loop is skipped — v6.3.30); the IR_NOP landing pad under CYRIUS_IR (v5.6.14).
6.7.5 put all three in one `_loop_open` shared by `loop` and `do`.
2. `do … while`: `continue` must reach the CONDITION, emitted after the body — a forward jump (the C-style
for's mechanism), never `jmp top`. The in-loop tail-call verdict (`_tcp_loop_end`) must be taken AFTER the
condition: an `&local` in a do condition counts, and stamping before it keeps a `jmp` whose callee reads a
freed frame (the 6.7.3 class).
3. A CONTEXTUAL keyword (`loop`, like `kernel`) is an IDENT token, so every scanner keyed on statement token
TYPES misses it: the statement dispatch (before the identifier path), the const-fn `_ce_stmt`, the #inline
eligibility scan, the if-expression branch check (both runtime and const), the closure capture prescan (else
an enclosing local named `loop` is captured), error recovery. `_TOK_IS_LOOP` (name + `{` next) is enough
only where a statement start is KNOWN (the dispatch, the evaluator, the if-expression branch); a scanner that
meets tokens in ANY position needs the positional `_TOK_IS_LOOP_AT` (previous token `;` `{` `}` or a case `:`)
— position-blind, `for i in 0..loop {` / `match loop {` in a closure skipped the capture and read a GLOBAL
`loop` (100 where 5 is right; the 6.7.5 review). A lexer-level rule would misfire on `struct loop {` and
`var p = loop { 3, 4 };`.
4. A compound field assignment's address ladder (rax) must make EXACTLY the decisions of
`_resolve_field_base_addr` (cx boxing, pointer-mode globals, closure captures) — a divergence loads one place
and stores another — and must NOT set `_fn_local_addr` (the address never escapes; setting it diverts every
in-loop tail call in the fn).
5. Struct-copy source recognisers (`_fsc_name` / `_fsc_call` / `_fsc_expr`) key on the statement terminator.
A for STEP ends at `)`, not `;`: without the terminator threaded as a parameter, a struct-valued field step
silently copies ONE word (the 6.6.20 class, for variables).
6. cybs's per-fn reference ceiling fails SILENTLY: each feature added exactly one helper call to
`_PARSE_STMT_IMPL` and `_ce_stmt`, and moved inline blocks (the `*p` store, field-store tails, the for-step
replay) OUT into helpers — each touched giant fn ended with fewer references than before.

[[entries]]
name = "flag_skip_by_position_nested_parse_state_and_the_bootstrap_rung_v676"
description = "Gotchas met in 6.7.6 (Break 1): an x86 flag-reuse skip is safe only when the flag setter was the LAST emit (`_flags_cp`) — the hand-kept 'every rax writer clears it' contract missed a whole class of emitters; parse state held in single globals must be a STACK once a closure body can nest a declaration inside it; a name intrinsic must not inherit its argument's type; a decision question needs a probe that confirms its premise; and cybs needs cycc's argument order, its parameter count before any store, no clobbered argument register, and respect for the seed's silent caps."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.6", "_flags_reflect_rax", "_flags_cp", "ECONDCMP", "parse state", "closure", "_scv_vec", "GESTYPE", "premise check", "cybs", "stack arguments", "count_params", "seed caps"]
---
1. STALE FLAGS — DECIDE BY POSITION, NOT BY CONTRACT. `_flags_reflect_rax` lets x86 ECONDCMP skip its
`test rax, rax` when the last emitter set ZF from rax. The rule "every emitter that writes rax without
setting the flags clears it" was kept by hand and a class missed it: a narrow typed local / field load,
`mulh64`, `~`, `&x` / `&g` / `&f`, a string literal, `f64_floor` and every PE syscall reroute (a raw
`pop rax` + an IAT call). `struct H { n; m: i8; }` … `x = x + 1; if (h.m)` took the branch with h.m == 0.
6.7.6: `_flags_set(S)` records the setter's code position (`_flags_cp = GCP(S)`) and ECONDCMP skips
only when `_flags_reflect_rax == 1` AND `GCP(S) == _flags_cp` — any emit after the setter, flag-preserving
or not, forces the test. The class is closed for every emitter, present and future; cycc's own code is
byte-identical (the skip still fires wherever it was right). aarch64 and cx declare `_flags_cp` and never
set it. GENERAL: a peephole that trusts "the last emit did X" must key on the CODE POSITION it recorded,
not on every other emitter remembering to reset a flag. This supersedes the "a new load path must
clear the flag" rule in flags_reflect_rax_is_emitter_state_so_a_load_after_an_eaddr_must_clear_it.
Gate: tests/gates/codegen/struct_value_codegen.sh row F (mutation: ECONDCMP without the GCP test -> RED).

2. SINGLE-GLOBAL PARSE STATE NESTS THROUGH A CLOSURE. The parenthesised-struct paths keep their state in
globals: `_scv_pk` (the parentheses peeled), `_scv_term` (the token that ends the source inside them),
`_sc_fcw`, `_fla_want`. A closure body inside the initialiser or operand runs PARSE_VAR re-entrantly:
`var q: K = (mkk(fncall1(|x| { var y = x; return y; }, 2)));`. The inner arm zeroed them and its disarm
set the others to -1, so the outer wrap never closed (`expected ';', got ')'`) and the outer call
recorded no temp — its first word passed as its address, SIGSEGV. Fix: each arm saves the four on a
stack (`_scv_vec`) and its disarm restores them; the returned word packs the previous `_sc_want` with
the save's depth, so a disarm reads its OWN save even after an unbalanced inner one — as
`_op_big_arm` / `_op_big_disarm` (the >8 B operand temp) save and restore theirs. RULE: a parse-time global that
an expression arms and a later token disarms is a stack the moment the expression can hold a closure
(a closure body is a whole fn body; every statement form recurs inside it).

3. A CALL'S RESULT TYPE COMES FROM THE CALLEE. `mulh64(..)`, `fncall0..8(..)` and `callptr(..)` left
GESTYPE at their LAST ARGUMENT's struct type (filed by the 6.7.4 note), so `fncall1(&f, n) + 1` with a
struct `n` dispatched `P_add` instead of adding 1 (100 is right). Reset the type after the intrinsic;
keep only the case the old typing got right (callee written `&f`, `f` declared `: f64`, an f64 last
argument -> an f64 result). Leftovers of parsing the arguments are never the result's type.

4. A DECISION QUESTION NEEDS A PROBE THAT CONFIRMS ITS PREMISE. The first Break 1 question told the user
`b = b + 1` on a u128 already carried and only `b += 1` did not. Neither carried: the planning probe
misread a PRE-FILLED high word (a local u128 initializer then stored its value into BOTH halves) as a
carry. Lane D's probe found it and the user re-decided the same day; further rounds then came back for
comparisons and truth tests, which the first question never measured. Each fact a question states needs
a probe that would FAIL if the fact were false — start from a known high word (0), check that
`load64(&b + 8)` moves by exactly the carry, test every spelling — and enumerate the whole operator
surface (assign, compare, truth test, match / switch, OP=) before asking, not one question per surprise.

5. CYBS — THE TRUSTED ROOT'S FIRST RUNG (bootstrap/cybs.cyr; the seed assembles it):
  a. ONE ARGUMENT ORDER. cybs passed only the six register arguments (6.7.0 refused a 7+ argument call
     by name). 6.7.6 passes stack arguments in CYCC's order — the LAST at `[rsp]` — so one convention
     holds and lib/fnptr.cyr's hand-written `fncall8` matches both compilers. The first cut used the
     SysV order (argument 7 at `[rsp]`), and the disagreement hid behind a fixture whose callee was
     SYMMETRIC in its stack arguments (a swapped pair summed the same). Weigh every argument by
     POSITION in such a fixture (`.. + h * 10 + i`) so a swap is red under either compiler.
  b. THE CALLEE NEEDS n FIRST. With the last argument at `[rbp + 16]`, parameter i >= 6 of n lives at
     `[rbp + 16 + (n - 1 - i) * 8]`, so a parameter's slot depends on the COUNT: cybs counts the
     parameter IDENTs up to `)` before storing any (`count_params`; with it mutated out, n stays 0 and
     the gate's 7- / 9-argument program returns 0 — RED).
  c. NOTHING BETWEEN THE ARGUMENTS AND THE CALL MAY TOUCH AN ARGUMENT REGISTER. A call through a global
     fn-pointer variable loaded its callee with `movabs rcx, &g; mov rax, [rcx]` AFTER the argument
     registers, so argument 4 (rcx) arrived as the global's address (`gp = &f4; gp(1, 2, 3, 4)` gave
     1230 + junk). Now `mov rax, [&g]` (48 A1 moffs64) — through rax alone.
  d. LEX EVERY BYTE OR REFUSE IT. cybs's lexer skipped any byte it had no arm for: `return !x;` compiled
     as `return x;`, `g()?` as `g()`, `@a = 5;` as `a = 5;` — the dropped-bang / dropped-at-sign lexer
     bugs, in the bootstrap. A lone `!` and every byte cybs does not lex outside a string or comment are
     refused by name (`cybs: the bootstrap compiler cannot lex this byte outside a string or comment: @`).
  e. THE SEED'S CAPS ARE SILENT. bootstrap/asm reads at most 131,072 input bytes and DROPS the rest with
     exit 0 (3 bytes over assembled a cybs one byte short); its label table holds 512 entries with no
     bounds check (520 labels overwrite the first ones); its CODE buffer (S+0x20000) is 65,536 B with
     unbounded stores. At 6.7.6 cybs.cyr is 112,175 B and 501 labels, the cybs binary 21,780 B. Row S
     of cybs_call_arity_named.sh guards the input and label caps in check.sh — and since 6.7.11 the code
     cap too (input 115,974 / 131,072 B, labels 506 / 512, code 22,200 / 65,536 B). A real fix is a new seed — a new trusted root, the user's call. Measure size and labels
     before adding to cybs.cyr: every cybs feature spends a budget whose overrun fails silently.
GATES: tests/gates/toolchain/cybs_call_arity_named.sh (rows A S B C E D + mutation ledger),
cybs_lexer_drops_nothing.sh; tests/gates/codegen/struct_value_codegen.sh. CHANGELOG [6.7.6].

[[entries]]
name = "tuples_anonymous_structs_and_the_multi_value_bridge_v677"
description = "Gotchas met adding 6.7.7's tuples (B4): `.N` is a selector by the PREVIOUS token, not the byte; a struct is registered only BETWEEN struct definitions; a tuple declaration is never pointer-mode; `_pwrap_k` counts a literal's own `(`; a literal argument's temporary is a frame address, so a tail call diverts; a multi-value call has no storage for an address-passed parameter; a capture pushes every return register before any store; `return t;` must not check the caller's signature inside an #inline replay; a refusal that peeks a callee can EMIT, so it sits right before its PCMPE; a refused type-parameter binding recovers through the base under the panic latch; aarch64 has two pair conventions; a check on every `return` must be linear."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.7", "tuples", "B4", "_lex_dot", "REGSTRUCT", "pointer-mode", "_pwrap_k", "tail call", "MULTIRET_SID", "_tup_drain_push", "#inline replay", "PCMPE", "_gen_infer_tp", "panic latch", "aarch64 x0:x2", "linear scan"]
---
1. `.N` IS A SELECTOR BY THE PREVIOUS TOKEN. LEXDEC builds a float from `digits.digits`, so before 6.7.7
`t.0.1` lexed IDENT DOT FLOAT"0.1" — the parser cannot split a float, so the LEXER owns `.N`. `_lex_dot`
(lex.cyr) emits the DOT, then, when the next byte is a digit AND the token before the DOT is an IDENT (2), `)`
(11) or `]` (29) — the tokens a field selector follows — lexes the digit run with LEXID (`t.0.1` = two
selectors). The guard is the previous TOKEN, not the byte before the `.`: a range's second `.` follows a DOT
token, so `a..5` / `0..10` are untouched; `1.5` is one LEXNUM token and never reaches the DOT arm; `1.0.5`
stays a float and a stray `.`. Drop the guard and `for i in 0..10` reads `10` as a name ("undefined variable
'10'"). The selector is LEXID's whole alnum run (`p.0x1` is field '0x1'). `_tok_start` moves to the digit so the
IDENT's column is right. And once `.N` is a name, `p.0()` resolves as the METHOD `P_0` — a user `fn P_0(self)`
was called — so `x.0(..)` is refused on ANY receiver (`_tup_meth_chk`). Rows L1-L5, R13e-R13i; mutations M1,
M1b, M13d.

2. REGISTER A STRUCT ONLY BETWEEN STRUCT DEFINITIONS. REGSTRUCT sets the new struct's field_base to the packed
field pool's top and ADDFIELD fills field_base + fc in order, so registering a struct while another is
mid-definition lands the inner struct's fields inside the outer one's slice — both corrupt. A tuple type is
interned (registered) on first use, so PARSE_STRUCT_DEF interns every `name: ( .. )` field type in a pre-scan
BEFORE its REGSTRUCT (`_sdef_tup_prescan`, the twin of `_sdef_gen_prescan` for `Box<T>` fields) and the field
arm only looks the type up (`_tup_type(S, 1)`): a miss there is an internal error, never a silent REGSTRUCT.
Every other interning site (a var annotation, pass 1 or 2) is outside any definition. Row S2, mutation M2.

3. A TUPLE DECLARATION IS NEVER POINTER-MODE. A struct over 8 B declared from an expression (`var p: P =
mk();`) is POINTER-mode: its slot holds the struct's address. No expression yields a tuple's address, so
`var t: (i64, i64) = 5;` reaching that path would store a scalar into a slot read as a pointer.
`_tup_ptr_refuse` (at `_sl_ptr_type` and the two global SVTYPE sites, `_tup_svtype`) refuses it by name; every
tuple source (a tuple, a tuple field, a literal, a capture) must claim its path BEFORE that backstop. Rows
R12e-R12i, mutation M10b.

4. `_pwrap_k` COUNTS A LITERAL'S OWN `(` AS A WRAP. 6.7.6 made parentheses around a whole struct source
transparent: `_pwrap_k` counts the `(`s that close right before each other and the destination steps inside.
For `((1, 2))` it counts TWO, the literal's own `(` included, so an unwrap that stepped inside parsed `1` and
then "expected ')', got ','". Every accepting position tests `_tup_lit_at` (the innermost counted group has
commas) BEFORE its unwrap — `_tup_asg` heads `_try_aggregate_copy_assign`, `_tup_sarg_lit` heads `_sarg_paren`,
`_tup_fsc_lit` heads `_fsc_paren`, `_tup_var` runs before PARSE_VAR's peels — and the peelers that are not
destinations (`_ret_peel`, `_scv_peel`) decline a literal so its factor refuses it by name. Rows S1, PW1-PW9;
mutation M5.

5. A LITERAL ARGUMENT'S TEMPORARY IS A FRAME ADDRESS. `return take((1, 2));` builds the literal in an
`_agg_temp` and pushes its address; the tail call's jmp would free that frame before the callee's prologue
copies the tuple (measured 52 where 60 is right). `_tup_sarg_lit` calls `_sarg_escapes(fi, argc, tail)`, which
sets `_fn_local_addr` for a tail call's own argument, so `_tc_after_args_divert` makes it a real call (in a loop
too). tuple_values.tcyr's tailcalls rows, mutation M6.

6. A MULTI-VALUE CALL HAS NO STORAGE FOR AN ADDRESS-PASSED PARAMETER. A struct call gets a temp
(`_struct_call_into_temp`, `_sc_pre`); a MULTI-VALUE call (`: (i64, i64)`, GFRS = MULTIRET_SID) has none —
`_arg_call_class` answers 0 and the caller pushes rax, so a tuple parameter received the call's FIRST VALUE as
its address (SIGSEGV). Refused by name: a free call before `_arg_call_class` (`_tup_arg_call`), a method or
operator after its parse (`_sc_post_small` stamps the multi-value result's span, `_mrc_note`;
`_push_struct_expr_arg` matches it against the whole argument). The same shape into a NAMED by-value struct
parameter over 8 B is a pre-existing silent SIGSEGV, reported by the lane. Rows R17a-R17h; mutations M14, M14b.

7. A CAPTURE PUSHES EVERY RETURN REGISTER BEFORE ANY STORE. `var t: (i64, i64, i64) = f();` / `t = f();` /
`a, b, c = f();` drain the multi-value registers (x86 / PE rax:rdx:r8, aarch64 x0:x2:x3, cx r0:r4:r5) with
`_tup_drain_push` — push rax; `EMOVRA_RDX`, push; `EMOVRA_R3`, push (`EMOVRA_RDX` leaves r3 intact) — then pop
each value into its slot, the last first. Computing a slot's address between two register reads is what the
destructure's own drain had to order by hand; pushing first makes the order irrelevant. A tuple's slots are
stored through `_resolve_field_base_addr` + `EADDIMM_X1` + `ESTOC`, never EFLSTORE (the regalloc picker would
rewrite an aggregate slot's store into a register, and a coroutine's heap frame orders an aggregate's words from
its LOW slot, `_ECORO_AGG_OFF`); a re-assigned scalar takes `_asg_store_slot`. The local block is reserved with
its span BEFORE the call is parsed and NAMED after the stores (an argument reads an outer `t`); a global after the
first statement is registered AFTER the call (a registration inside the call takes its own index). `a, b = f();`
keeps its resolved targets in its own locals across the call's parse (a multi-value assignment inside a closure
among the arguments re-uses the module table, `_masg_ix`). Rows AS2, A; mutations M8a (stores in push order),
M8b (no `EMOVRA_R3`), M23 (targets re-read after the call: 26 where 813 is right).

8. `return t;` CHECKS THE CALLEE'S SIGNATURE — NOT INSIDE AN #inline REPLAY. The replay re-parses a callee's body
in the CALLER's fn context (`_cur_fn_ix` is the caller's), so a check against "the current fn's declared return"
reads the wrong fn there. `_ret_tup_ok` skips its signature check when `GIDEP != 0` (the definition already
checked its own body, `_bx_live`'s rule) and only emits; an `async fn` body is told by its minted name (`f$impl`,
`_fn_async_impl`), since `_cur_fn_coro` is set only for a body that suspends. A 9-16 B struct fn's `return t;`
is refused by its own class first (`_ret_struct_pair`), so the tuple refusal stays quiet there.

9. A REFUSAL THAT PEEKS THE CALLEE CAN EMIT — PUT IT RIGHT BEFORE THE STORE'S PCMPE. `_refuse_lossy_pair` (6.7.7
T5b: a `: stack` pair into one slot) resolves the callee through `_pair_callee_at`, and for a generic call that
is `_gen_resolve_call` — which INSTANTIATES the instance, emitting its body inline (a jump over it) at the current
code position. `_PARSE_TERM_IMPL` does the same peek (`_tpair`) at the start of every term, so a refusal placed
immediately before the store's PCMPE instantiates where the term would have: every accepted program compiles
byte-identical. Placed before an emit (in `_asg_compound_op`, before its `EPUSHR`) the instance's bytes would move
ahead of the push — correct, no longer identical; placed before `_W128_IS` (which compares GCP with the u128
load's) an instantiation would make a u128 `b += g(..)` read as no u128 place. Hence the u128 compound's refusal
lives in `_w128_binop`, right before its own PCMPE. stack_enum_lossy_context.sh axis 15; mutations M24a-M24i.

10. A REFUSED TYPE-PARAMETER BINDING RECOVERS THROUGH THE BASE — SO LATCH THE STATEMENT. `first(t)` with a tuple
`t` is refused in `_gen_infer_tp`, the one place every generic call path reaches (the call head,
`_gen_resolve_call`'s own-call paths and lookaheads, the `: T` return peek, the tail-call divert). T binds i64 —
the base, as a refused bound's call does — and the base is not written for that argument: a struct-reading body
is a stub, a small one is inlined (`x.0` on an i64), a `T` return is one value that a tuple capture declines (and
PARSE_VAR's generic-callee path then drops the annotation, so `u.0` on the NEXT statement cascades). So the
refusal sets the panic latch — the statement's cascade is swallowed and `_sync_skip` resyncs — and
`_tup_cap_check` treats a latched callee as refused. The latch is set on every parse of the call (an #inline
replay re-parses it; `_tn_once` reports it once), never under --syntax-only (lint's pre-pass resolves as before:
row X7). Recovering through the OLD instance instead is worse: minting it parses its body at once, a nested
parse that resyncs and CLEARS the latch, so the capture's "returns struct" refusal follows as a second error.
Rows R4e-R4r; mutations M17i, M17p, M17r, M17s.

11. AARCH64 HAS TWO TWO-REGISTER RETURN CONVENTIONS. `E*_STRUCT_INT_PAIR` returns a 9-16 B struct in x0:x1
(aarch64/emit.cyr); a multi-value `return (a, b)` puts its second value where EMOVRA_RDX reads it, x2. x86 / PE
use rax:rdx for both, so the two never disagree there. Where they meet on aarch64 — `return (a, b)` in a
struct-returning fn, or `var a, b = mk16();` of a struct-returning call — the second word was lost (55 / 50 where
56 is right, on pi and ecb too): issues/archived/2026-10-09-aarch64-struct-pair-and-multi-value-registers-disagree.md,
whose fix the user placed in 6.7.7 itself (2026-10-09). A tuple uses ONLY the multi-value emitters (its return,
its capture's drain), and the capture and `a, b = f();` refuse a struct-returning callee by name, so tuples never
reach the seam (`a, b = f();` took one until the B4 review: 50 on aarch64 where 59 is right). GENERAL: a
convention two features "share" on x86 because both happen to use rax:rdx is two conventions on a target where
they differ — test the pair on aarch64, not only x86. cx (6.7.8, dyn D1) put the 9-16 B struct
pair's high word in r4 — its multi-value second slot — so there the two conventions are one, with no
sync. ⚠ A test of that crossing must not pass the producer's high word as argument 1: cx's r4 IS
argument 1, so `mk(a, b)` leaves b in r4 and hides a mismatch (aarch64's x1 is the same trap).
6.7.13 (MV-12): the crossing now runs in ONE direction only — a `return p;` read as two values, where
EFLLOAD_STRUCT_INT_PAIR writes x2 as well as x1 on aarch64. The other direction (a multi-value landing read as the
struct pair, the x1-from-x2 sync ESTRUCT_PAIR_HI_SYNC) has no producer in a program that compiles, since the tuple
literal (6.7.11) and `ret2` (6.7.13) are refused in a struct fn; the def is gone from all three backends, and a lane
that calls it will not link.

12. A CHECK ON A PATH EVERY STATEMENT OF A KIND TAKES MUST BE LINEAR. `_ret_tuple_var` runs for EVERY `return` (it
must recognise `return t;` before the scalar path); a `_pwrap_k` peel in its wrap scan made
recursion_depth_bounded.sh's `return ((..((1))..));`, 65536 deep, take 99 s — a timeout, caught by that gate's
`nested parens @ 65536` row. The scan is one linear pass over the tokens now.
6.7.13 (MV-19) — THE SAME TRAP ONE LEVEL UP: A LOOKAHEAD THE PARSER ASKS AT EVERY NESTING LEVEL MUST BE LINEAR IN THE
DEPTH. D19's `_pair_unwrap` took the wrap's level count from `_pwrap_k`, which runs `_call_close` from each level to
its `)` — quadratic — and the parser asks `_callee_returns_pair_at` at every `(` it descends, BEFORE the 256-level
nesting bound can fire: 16,384 levels ran for minutes where the base refused at once ("nesting too deep"). Caught only
by recursion_depth_bounded.sh axis 1 at the full check.sh — no row in the bite's own gates measured time. Fix: the
inside is ONE call, so the k `(` before it match the k `)` right after its close; read those k tokens (bounded by
GTCNT): 7 / 55 / 205 ms at 1,024 / 16,384 / 65,536. Rule: any helper reachable per nesting level gets a deep-input
timing check before it lands.
6.7.14 (RC-21) — A THIRD TIME, FOR A WHOLE-WRAP PEEL: a store whose whole value is parenthesised and starts with a pair
call needed the depth of the wrap that encloses the WHOLE value; `_pwrap_k` is quadratic in the depth, and the
nesting-bound row timed out. Count the leading `(`, read the call's close and the run of `)` after it, then ONE
`_call_close` from the innermost whole wrap — monotone: if wrap j closes at c0 - j, every shallower one does.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/tuple_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger); tests/tcyr/crossos/tuple_values.tcyr;
stack_enum_lossy_context.sh axis 15. CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "defaults_named_arguments_and_the_one_marshaller_v677"
description = "Gotchas met adding 6.7.7's default and named arguments (B6): a parameter-list walker skips a default with ( [ { only (the const evaluator's two counts are the exception and must agree); a walker's end-of-input return must not be its own start; one marshaller keyed by parameter index; the tail arm never fills (a divert plus a loud backstop); a reordered named call spills to _HTEMP and records a vector at its parameter-order ordinal; a fill resets the expression type; the evaluator judges file scope at the PARSER cursor; a pass-1 refusal leaves the panic latch alone; the #inline replay binds by position; a plan's premise must be checked in code; the forward-arity gap was fn bodies; compiler-called helpers take no default; a named argument's label is not a capture read."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.7", "default arguments", "named arguments", "B6", "_pl_next", "_pl_dflt_end", "_arg_next", "_owncall_args_at", "_call_arg_one", "_tc_args_divert", "_CHECK_ARITY", "_HTEMP", "_simd_ord_at", "_cst_emit_val", "_pd_eval", "_panic", "_fnc_no_inline", "_cl_prescan_ident"]
---
1. A PARAMETER-LIST WALKER SKIPS A DEFAULT WITH ( [ { BALANCE ONLY. In a parameter's TYPE `<..>` nests
(`m: Map<K, V>`; `>>` / `>>>` close two / three); in its DEFAULT only `( [ {` do (`b = A < B`). A walker that
counted `<` as a bracket inside a default lost the list's end. One rule, written once: `_pl_next` / `_pl_tdepth`
(a parameter), `_pl_dflt_end` (a default), used by `_bx_pname`, `_gs_params`, `_bnd_sites`, `_tr_arity`, both
passes' parameter loops, and `_prescan_skip_fn` (which skips the whole `( .. )` before looking for the body, so
nothing in a default is taken for it). EXCEPTION: the const evaluator's two counts (`_ce_nparams` at the
definition check, `_ce_bind_params` at the bind) are paren-depth only and MUST agree, so `_ce_bind_params` steps
with `_tok_item_end`, not `_pl_next` — a `<`-aware bind refused an uncalled `const fn f(p: Pair<i64, i64>, b)`
that compiles (const_checked.sh C3). Rows S3, C1; mutations M10, M11, M17, M22.

2. A WALKER'S END-OF-INPUT RETURN MUST NOT BE ITS OWN START. A walker written `i = _pl_dflt_end(S, i);
continue;` loops forever on `fn f(a, b = 1` at EOF if the helper hands `i` back. The B6 walkers return the EOF
token, so a caller that continues from the result always moves.

3. ONE ARGUMENT MARSHALLER, KEYED BY PARAMETER INDEX. `_owncall_args_at(S, fi, noff, int0, p0, line)` ->
`_call_arg_one(S, fi, pi, ..)` carries every callee-mask gate (the SIMD record, the cstring literal error and
Str warning, the Str literal wrap, the struct address push, PCMPE + bool / f32). PARSE_FNCALL, the method form
(p0 = 1: `self` is parameter 0) and the struct-valued / Win64 vector-retptr receives all call it, so a call-site
obligation — B6's fills and names — is written once, and a named argument passed its PARAMETER's index inherits
every gate. (Bite 1a, a refactor with identical output: 647 files byte-identical on every backend.)

4. THE TAIL ARM NEVER SEES A CALL THAT FILLS OR NAMES — A DIVERT, PLUS A LOUD BACKSTOP. The tail arm
(PARSE_RETURN) has missed a call-site obligation five times, so B6 gives it none: `_tc_args_divert` asks
`_pd_call_fills` first (a named argument, or fewer written arguments than max, counted by `_arg_next`) and sends
such a `return f(..)` to PARSE_FNCALL — the one marshaller, the only site that fills. `_CHECK_ARITY` mode 3 (the
arm's literal `1` -> `3`: no new reference in a giant fn at cybs's ceiling) turns a predicate miss into
"internal: tail call to 'f' reached the tail arm short of its defaults", never a jmp with a parameter unset.
Mutations M1 / M2 show both halves; T1 pins the full-arity jmp.

5. A REORDERED NAMED CALL SPILLS TO _HTEMP; A VECTOR'S REGISTER IS ITS PARAMETER-ORDER ORDINAL. Each argument is
evaluated as written through its parameter's gates, its int-class word popped into `_HTEMP` (a frame slot in a
fn; a dead hidden global at top level — never a frame slot there: mutation M14 SIGSEGVs), then every parameter
pushed in parameter order. A value-form vector is RECORDED at `_simd_ord_at(fi, pi)` (the widths of the vector
parameters before it), never pushed — the running ordinal hands `vtwo(b: y, a: x)` its registers swapped (M7). A
named call is checked WHOLE before anything is emitted (`_pd_nscan`, cursor-free), so a refusal is one line and
the rest is parsed without a push (`_pd_nskip`), never a cascade. Every scanner that reads an argument by
POSITION before the call is parsed (`_gen_infer_tp`, `_gs_arg_named`, `_tok_gen_ret_sid`) goes through
`_call_arg_for`, which falls back to `_call_arg_start` when nothing is named — so positional calls are
byte-identical and `g(n: 2, x: p)` infers like `g(p, 2)`.

6. A FILL RESETS THE EXPRESSION TYPE. `_cst_emit_val` (a const read, and a default's push) of an f64 sets ESTYPE
to f64. The method form has no type reset of its own (issue
2026-10-09-method-call-result-keeps-last-arg-f64-type), so `p.m() * 2` with `d: f64 = 1.5` read 0 until the fill
ended with `SESTYPE(S, 0)`. Row L1, mutation M16.

7. THE CONST EVALUATOR JUDGES FILE SCOPE AT THE PARSER CURSOR — EVALUATE A DEFAULT ON ITS OWN TOKEN. FINDFN's
same-name file scoping and a private const's visibility are judged at the parser cursor, not the evaluator's. A
default is evaluated with GTI put on its own token (`_pd_eval`), so it resolves in its definition's file whatever
file the call is in (row E2: 149, not 199; mutation M19). A const context does not check a CALLED const fn's
`private` at all, and defaults inherit that, as `const X = pk();` does. And evaluating every default at the END
of pass 1 (`_pd_sweep`) is what keeps a caller's locals out: with a lazy evaluation at the call, row E1 read the
caller's local const (99 where 29 is right — M20 with M18).

8. A PASS-1 REFUSAL MUST LEAVE THE PANIC LATCH AS IT FOUND IT. ERR_* set `_panic` and pass 1 never resyncs it, so
a second bad const in one file goes unreported. The default refusals print through `_pd_eo` / `_pd_ec` (one line
per token, `_tn_once`; the latch untouched), and an evaluator refusal clears the latch it set — every bad default
in a file is reported, each once.

9. THE #inline REPLAY BINDS BY POSITION — A NAMED CALL MUST NEVER REACH IT. `_fnc_no_inline` sends a named call
to the normal path; without it the replay read `b:` as a variable (M4). A defaulted fn is never inline-eligible
(`_inl_why = 7`), so no per-call count is needed (M3, row W1).

10. CHECK A PLAN'S PREMISE IN CODE. The B6 plan's D17 ("a fn defined inside a top-level block is not prescanned,
so refuse its defaults") was false since 6.6.17: `_prescan_block_fn` records it, so such a fn takes defaults like
any other and a call above the block reaches the record (row R17 is a PASS). `_pd_late`'s refusal stays as a
backstop for a definition pass 1 did not record; the earlier definition of a REDEFINED fn also reaches that
branch (pass 1 keeps only the last start) and is D15's. Its mutation (M21) has no killing row, by construction.

11. THE FORWARD-ARITY GAP WAS A CALL FROM A FN BODY. A top-level STATEMENT's call to a fn defined below it was
already checked before 6.7.7 (that fn's body is emitted first); the gap (fork F2, user 2026-10-09) was a call
from inside a fn body, or to a fn inside a later top-level block. `_CHECK_ARITY` now checks whenever pass 1
recorded the callee (GFDS). A gate row for a forward call must sit in a fn body (rows C1-fwd, R24 / R24b;
M9 = M23).

12. A HELPER THE COMPILER CALLS WITH A FIXED COUNT MUST NEVER DECLARE A DEFAULT. The synthesized calls (for-in
vec_len / vec_get, closure and enum alloc, str_from, future_force / future_pending, `_slice_idx_get_N`, the
`_chk_*` / `_sat_*` overflow modes, the aarch64 `_f64_*_polyfill`s, f64v2_make / f64v4_make) pass a FIXED number
of words; a default there would never be filled. Row S-R5 derives the names from src (`_FINDFN_CSTR` /
`EMIT_OVF_CALL` literals plus vec_len / vec_get / alloc / str_from) and fails on a lib/ definition with a default
(MT9).

13. THE CLOSURE CAPTURE PRE-SCAN READS NAMES BY TOKEN — A NAMED ARGUMENT'S LABEL IS NOT A READ.
`_cl_prescan_ident` captures any free name it meets in a closure body. `|n| w3(1, c: n)`'s label `c` matched the
enclosing fn's local `c` (a capture the body never uses: refused without lib/alloc.cyr), and at top level
whatever the local table still held from the last fn compiled — the callee's own parameter, so the closure
captured a dead frame slot (SIGSEGV on x86 / aarch64). `_cl_is_arg_label`: `IDENT :` just after `(` / `,` whose
innermost unclosed bracket is a `(` — the bracket test keeps a struct literal's field (`Pt { x: n }`, in a `{`)
and a `case A, B:` as they were. Rows N-cl, the tcyr's top-level A21; mutation MU4 (the B6 review).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/default_named_args_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger);
tests/tcyr/crossos/default_named_args_values.tcyr; const_checked.sh C3 / M2. CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677"
description = "Three classes both 6.7.7 lanes met (tuples B4, default / named arguments B6): two passes that read one construct must read it by ONE rule (a tuple parameter type, a `u128` / `*fn` type token, a declaration-zone generic call) — a disagreement is a silent ABI split; a separator comma must be found by ONE walker that steps a closure's bars and a generic's type arguments; and every hand-written signature reader in the tools must count brackets and skip literals."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.7", "two passes", "_prescan_params_scan", "_pd_pc_check", "_gcall_syn", "_arg_next", "_tup_commas", "cyrius header", "cyrius-lsp", "api-surface", "cyrdoc", "type-audit"]
---
1. TWO PASSES, ONE RULE — A PARAMETER LIST STOPPED SHORT IN PASS 1 IS A SILENT ABI SPLIT.
`_prescan_params_scan` (pass 1) records a fn's parameter count and per-parameter masks for every call parsed
before its definition; pass 2 (PARSE_FN_DEF) reads the list again. Twice in 6.7.7 they disagreed:
  a. B4: pass 1 stepped over `name: IDENT<..>` only; at a tuple type's `(` it stopped, so a forward call saw one
     parameter and no struct-mask bit (an arity error — or, with the counts agreeing, the tuple's first word
     pushed where the callee dereferences an address). A new parameter-type spelling needs its pass-1 arm
     (`_tup_pscan`) in the same change as its PARSE_FN_DEF arm (`_tup_param`), and pass 1 must also predict the
     prologue copy (`SFBVCP`, `_tup_bvcp`) or a tail call after an ordinary call to a callee declared below is
     diverted for nothing. Rows S3, the tailcalls rows; mutations M3, Mbv.
  b. B6 review: pass 1 took a parameter's TYPE only when it was a NAME; pass 2 takes any one token. At
     `a: u128` / `a: *u128` / `a: *fn` (keywords) pass 1 stopped, so its count was short: a forward call lost the
     fn's `: f64` / struct return (the return-type scan read the parameter — exit 0, SIGSEGV; pre-existing), F2
     refused a RIGHT-count forward call, and a default after such a parameter was never recorded while pass 2's
     count let the short call through (an unset register). `_pd_pc_check` now refuses a defaulted fn whose passes
     disagree, so the fill and the range check never read two maxes. Rows R24c / R24d / U1-U5; mutations MU1, MU2.
  c. B4 review: the declaration zone's PARSE_GVAR_REG sizes a global before the fns and structs after it exist;
     EMIT_GVAR_INITS replays the initializer with everything declared. A tuple literal's comma detector skipped a
     generic call's type arguments only through `_gcall_open` — a KNOWN generic — so with the generic declared
     below, `pk<i64, i64>(1, 2)`'s comma was a tuple comma in pass 1 and none in the replay: the global was sized
     as one shape and initialized as another. Pass 1 now reads `name<..>(` by its tokens (`_gcall_syn`), and where
     the readings can still disagree (a `<` comparison spelled like a type list) the replay refuses by name
     (`_gvi_tup_slot`, `_gvi_tup_odd`) — never a store into a slot of the other shape. Rows ZG1-ZG11; Mz1-Mz4.
RULE: two counts of one list come from one rule; anything pass 1 decides from tokens that name things declared
later must be decided by a reading the replay RE-CHECKS.

2. ONE WALKER FINDS A SEPARATOR COMMA. A tuple literal is a `(` group with a comma at its own depth, and an
argument ends at a comma — but `(|a, b| a + b)` and `(pk<i64, i64>(1, 2))` hold commas that separate nothing.
PARSE_RETURN's paren-depth scan counted both, so `return (|a, b| a + b);` was taken for a two-value return
("expected ','"). `_arg_next` (B6) steps a closure's bars — EVERY leading head, since a closure body that opens
with `|` / `||` can only be another closure literal (no unary `|`): `|a| |b, d| ..` is one argument (stepping
only the first head counted `|b, d|`'s comma; the B6 review, rows A24, MU3 / MU3b). `_tup_commas` (B4) walks a
group element by element with `_arg_next` / `_tok_item_end` and, where an element's end falls INSIDE an explicit
generic call's type arguments, resumes after that call (`_tup_gen_past`). ONE detector serves every tuple
position (PARSE_RETURN, the five accepting positions, the PARSE_FACTOR stray refusal, the const evaluator), so
they cannot disagree (rows C1-C4, M21). For B6 the count decides a tail call: counted as two arguments,
`return ap(|a, b| a + b);` to `fn ap(c, n = 5)` would jump with `n` unset (M2). The three older scanners that did
not step the bars (`_CALL_ARGC_PEEK`, `_call_arg_start`, `_tc_str_literal_arg` — a false generic refusal, and a
`return f(|a, b| .., "lit")` tail call into a `: Str` parameter that passed the raw literal) are
issues/archived/2026-10-09-closure-literal-comma-counted-as-argument.md, whose fix the user placed in 6.7.7 itself
(2026-10-09).

3. A TOOL THAT READS A SIGNATURE BY HAND MUST COUNT BRACKETS AND SKIP LITERALS. Before 6.7.7 no parameter held a
`,` or a `)`, so the tools read a list as "to the first `)`, split at every `,`". A tuple type (`t: (i64, i64)`)
and a default (`b = f(1, 2)`, `s = ",)"`, `c = ')'`) broke them silently: `cyrius header` printed `cyr_val i64`
as a parameter and dropped the ones after it (and `cyr_val b = 2`, not C); the LSP's semantic tokens coloured
`i64` as a parameter all through the file and lost later parameters; api-surface counted a string's `,` and read
`#` as a comment; type-audit's `)` in a string closed the list; cyrdoc ended a signature at a `{` in a string.
All five now balance `( [ {` and skip string / char literals; none balances `<` / `>` (in a default it is an
operator, as in `_pl_dflt_end`). A depth-aware end has a hazard the old one did not: an inner `)` the name scan
stops at must still be STEPPED, or the walk never advances (the header steps every byte of a parameter to its
depth-0 `,`). api-surface records `name/MIN-MAX` for a defaulted fn (`name/N` otherwise): a fn gaining a
trailing default reads as a widening, still a changed signature (a `fncall1(&f, ..)` caller with the old count
is refused). Rows tuple_checked.sh T1 / T1b / T2 (MT1a-c, MT2a-c) and default_named_args_checked.sh S-hdr /
S-asf / S-doc / S-lsp / S-ta (MT1-MT8).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/tuple_checked.sh, default_named_args_checked.sh. CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

4. 6.7.11 — THE SAME CLASS, THREE MORE TIMES.
  a. ARG-3: the two parameter scans (`_prescan_params_scan` and `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`) must consume the SAME span for
     every parameter type, or a forward call's arity / defaults drift (`_pd_pc_check`). One reader whose END is a
     function of the tokens alone — never of what it reported — keeps them together even on a refused type
     (`_pty_end`; `_tn_once` dedups the report, because a generic's instances re-read the signature). The mutation
     that proves it: pass 1 left at one token — the forward-call rows go RED.
  b. TL-2: point 3's "none balances `<` / `>`" was wrong for a TYPE. A tool counting parameters by depth-0 commas
     meets `p: Pair<i64, i64>` and splits it in two (a header cc rejects, a wrong api-surface arity, the LSP colouring
     every `i64` a parameter). The fix is the compiler's own rule (`_pl_next` / `_pl_tdepth`): `<` opens and `>` /
     `>>` / `>>>` close one / two / three levels from the parameter's start to its depth-0 `=`; after the `=` (a
     default) `<` is the less-than operator (`c = 1 < 2` is one parameter). The 6.7.7 B6 hand-off left `<` out on
     purpose because of the default — the line is the `=`, not "never".
  c. GF-12 / GF-13: the const evaluator is a second interpreter of the parser's tokens. Two walkers over one grammar
     drift — walk a list with the parser's own walker (`_pl_next` for parameters, `_arg_next` for arguments): a
     `Pair<i64, i64>` parameter counted as two made a const fn's const-context arity disagree with its run-time
     one. CHANGELOG [6.7.11].

5. 6.7.12 — A SECOND PREDICATE THAT RESTATES WHAT A READER REFUSES DRIFTS WHEN THE READER GROWS, AND A TOOL MIRRORING
   A RULE READS ITS WHOLE GRAMMAR.
  a. FX-4: `_pd_type_refused` ("keep this parameter's default, its type was refused") listed slices and unresolved
     names; 6.7.11's ARG-3 taught the type reader to refuse `a: 5` / `a: fn` / `b: "s"`, and the list was never
     taught — so the default was dropped and every short call refused again (two error lines for one mistake).
     Fix: ask the reader itself, quietly (`_pty_end` with `loud` 0, its `_pty_bad` saved and restored). The
     cross-lane hand-off that named this was left unapplied at the 6.7.11 merge.
  b. TL-21: cyrlint's bare-array overrun rule took a literal N only; the guide's N is "any integer const
     expression" — an integer literal, a `const NAME = <literal>;`, an enum constant with a literal value (bare or
     `E.X`), or `+ - *` over those — and a top-level bare `var g[N]` is N × 8 bytes, checked in every fn whose local
     or parameter does not shadow it.
  c. TL-8: `cyrius type-audit` skipped a generic fn — a `<..>` sits between a fn's name and its `(`, and a
     signature reader must step it. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-4, TL-8, TL-21.

6. 6.7.13 — ONE QUESTION, SEVERAL ASKERS: A SPELLING RULE LANDS IN EVERY ASKER THROUGH ONE HELPER.
  a. MV-8: "does this call return a pair" has TWO askers — the parser's `_callee_returns_pair_at` (feeding the live
     flags: `?`, the bind / store refusals, PARSE_RETURN's propagation flag) and the token passes' `_tok_ret_callee` /
     `_ret_is_variant` / `_tok_q_is_pair` (the pair prescan that flags a forwarder defined BELOW its caller, and the
     mixed-return warning run after parsing). D19 (parentheses are transparent) had to land in both through ONE helper
     (`_pair_unwrap`). With the rule in the parser alone, measured: a flag the parser sets was judged "a single value"
     by the warning pass (a NEW false warning on `fn fw(x) { return (f(x)); }`), and the forward case depended on
     definition order (`fw` above its `fw(x)?` caller ran 38, below it still crashed).
  b. MV-1 / MV-6 / MV-18 — THE MULTI-VALUE PREDICATES DO NOT AGREE ON WHAT A MULTI-VALUE CALLEE IS: pass 1's count
     (`_prescan_ret_arity`) counts `return (..)` STATEMENTS (a scalar-declared fn's too, D23 B), but `ret2(..)` is an
     expression builtin, so a ret2 body has no count and three names bind a stale register; `_mv_callee` (R17's and
     the named-struct refusals' predicate) answers declared-or-counted but excludes a scalar-declared callee, so
     `take(g2(3))` into a tuple parameter still crashes; the destructure (`_dt_rhs_check_t`) takes `callptr (` now, but
     the tuple capture reads the call through the shared `_dt_call_open`, deliberately left unwidened because it has
     other callers — so the capture still refuses it. When a fix lands in one caller instead of a shared helper, list
     the helper's other callers' surfaces in the same pass and file what is left (all three filed, 6.7.14 — and all
     three closed there: a ret2 body counts as 2 values, RC-16 / D42; a scalar-declared multi-value call into a tuple
     parameter is refused, D44; the capture takes `callptr`, RC-19).
  c. VC-7 — A REFUSAL KEYED ON "A NAMED LOCAL OF TYPE V" MISSES THE OTHER VECTOR VALUES: a call declared to return V (by
     name, `g<T>(..)`, `&f`, a method), a `*V` dereference and a closure's capture each reach the argument routes
     separately — the address-passed `_try_push_struct_addr_arg`, the value-passed `_sarg_byval_small`, the
     dereference park `_dlo_vec` — and each route needs the whole set. A route that judges a dereference BY ITS TOKENS
     (`_svo_vderef`: `* NAME`) misses every other `*V` operand (`**ppb`, `*GPV`, `*h.pv`, `*&b`: those still fault into
     `*i64`, filed); the address-passed route types the operand AFTER its parse and gets them all.
  CHANGELOG [6.7.13] MV-1, MV-6, MV-8, MV-18, VC-7.

7. 6.7.14 — TWO MORE READINGS, AND 6c CLOSED.
  a. VC-16 — A CLASSIFIER BOTH PASSES CALL MUST READ FACTS BOTH PASSES HAVE. Pass 1's struct table is still filling as
     it scans, so a name decision keyed on FINDSTRUCT differed between the prescan of a fn ABOVE a struct's declaration
     (a vector: 2 / 4 slots, the SIMD masks) and the definition (the struct) — the measured MV2 mutant: a refusal on
     four legs, exit 1 on PE (D41 A: a program's struct named like a vector type is the struct everywhere). The token
     stream is complete before either pass: read it ONCE (`_cpt_vscan`), cached by token count (an `#ifdef`'d-off
     declaration is not in it).
  b. FE-13 — A REPLAY READER MUST STEP A SHAPE AS ITS REGISTERING PASS DOES. EMIT_GVAR_INITS's `_gvi_ann` read one `*`
     and then one token, where pass 1's `_ptr_annot` reads every `*`: `var P: **i64` in the declaration zone left the
     replay's cursor on `i64`. The zone's other half (a second parser that must take every statement shape) is
     struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #9 (6.7.14).
  c. 6c's `*V` operands other than `* NAME` (`**ppb`, `*gp(pb)`, `*h.pv`, `*GPV`, `*(pb + 0)`, `*&b`) are refused by
     name since 6.7.14 (VC-10) — vector_values_receives_arguments_and_dereferences_v6713 #9.
  CHANGELOG [6.7.14] VC-10, VC-16, FE-13.

[[entries]]
name = "checked_dyn_trait_objects_v678"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.8's checked `dyn`: a token fold must precede every index-keyed prepass; keep a refusal to one report by rewriting the token; a destination's exemption from a value refusal must ask the destination's TYPE; read every classification before the source is parsed; a vtable is filled where the dyn is formed; a bodiless prototype feeds the one marshaller but needs its arity and value class set by hand; the callee is spilled before the arguments; only a parameterless tail caller proves a temp-argument divert; 'holds a dyn' is judged on the complete struct; inference through a pointer changes what compiling code computes; and the call path was proven on hardware before it was written."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.8", "dyn", "trait objects", "vtable", "token fold", "_dyn_fold", "_sc_want", "prototype", "_CHECK_ARITY", "GFLG 1024", "ECALLIND", "tail call", "_sarg_escapes", "generic inference", "D0"]
---
1. THE TOKEN FOLD PRECEDES EVERY INDEX-KEYED PREPASS. `_dyn_fold` turns each `dyn X` into ONE identifier token
(compacting the three token arrays in place) as the FIRST line of `_tl_pass1`: `_tr_prepass` and `_cst_prepass`
record token INDICES, and a fold after them left an impl read one token off (mutation M-T2). The fold runs only
when the lexer counted a `dyn` (`_lex_dyn_n`), so every other unit's tokens are untouched — that is what made every
bite's whole-corpus differential byte-identical.

2. ONE REPORT PER REFUSED TOKEN — REWRITE THE TOKEN AFTER THE REPORT. When a later parser would report the same
token again and has no `_tn_once` of its own, change the token: a bare `dyn` in a type position becomes the
identifier `dyn` after its report (the parameter-type arm took ANY one token: `o: dyn)`, `Vec<dyn>`, `*dyn`
compiled), and a dyn type in a VALUE position becomes `0` (it was also "undefined variable 'dyn Show'").

3. A VALUE REFUSAL'S DESTINATION EXEMPTION MUST ASK THE DESTINATION'S TYPE. "a dyn result used as a value" sits in
the factor, and the destinations that take a whole dyn (`var o: dyn S = mk();`, `o = mk();`, an argument) arm
`_sc_want` before the factor runs — so the exemption was `_sc_want == ti0`, exactly (every destination peels its
own parentheses; `var x = (mk());` arms at the `(` but infers no struct and kept the data word). The review found
it still leaked: an assignment arms `_sc_want` for ANY target, a declaration for any annotation, and a tail
`return mk();` never reaches the factor — `x = mk();` into an i64, `var y: u8 = mk();`, a top-level `var G =
mk();` each kept the data word. The exemption is now RECORDED and each such destination asks, after its parse,
whether it is that dyn (`_dyn_dst_chk`); a dyn-returning tail callee from a fn of another type is diverted to the
normal path, whose factor refuses it. A pair-class `return mk();` passes the call through the scalar path UNARMED
— record it as a destination (`_dyn_rw`).

4. READ EVERY CLASSIFICATION THE EMITTERS NEED BEFORE THE SOURCE IS PARSED. The FORM emitters read the pair's impl
after parsing the source, so `var o: dyn Show = pick(n);` — `pick` taking a `dyn Num`, formed in its argument —
built the outer dyn with the INNER pair's vtable (x86 49 / qemu 100 / cx 36 / wine 100 where the row expects its
own value; M-F14, caught inside the bite). `_dyn_emit` reads the impl first.

5. A VTABLE CANNOT LIVE IN DATA. No target relocates a data word — x86 fixups patch code only, Mach-O arm64 is
`MH_PIE` with an empty rebase table, PE's `.reloc` lists code sites — so the hidden table global is FILLED AT EVERY
FORMING SITE (per slot a fixup-type-3 `ELOAD_FN_ADDR`, stored addressed; every site writes the same words). It
also means a `kernel;` build never waits on the late replay. A slot method is therefore address-taken exactly as
`&f` makes a fn, so CYRIUS_DCE=1 keeps it even when its only forming site is dead code (`&g` in a dead fn keeps
`g` the same way) — the plan's "DCE stays exact" was wrong, measured at D7.

6. A BODILESS PROTOTYPE FEEDS THE ONE MARSHALLER — AND NEEDS BY HAND WHAT A BODY WOULD SET. The call needs a callee
fi for `_owncall_args_at` / `_sc_pre_m` / `_sc_post` / `_call_ret_marks`, so `dyn Show.m` is a fn-table entry made
lazily from the trait member's tokens with pass 1's own recorders (`_prescan_params`, `_prescan_ret_type`), owned
by the trait's file (or `_warn_undefined_prepass` names it), never in a call fixup (or the undefined-call gate
counts it). Three things a body would have given it: (a) ARITY — `_CHECK_ARITY(.., need_off = 1)` skips a callee
with no offset and no definition, so the call counts its arguments and checks them itself; (b) the VALUE CLASS —
GFLG 256 (pair) / 1024 (one value) come from a body scan, so without them `o.div(d)?` on a `: Result` member took
the boxed `?` lowering and dereferenced the tag (rc 139) and a destructure read a stale register: every reader of
those bits (`?`'s `_tpair`, the lossy-bind refusals, the wrapper flag, `_dt_arity_check`) needs them set from the
DECLARATION; (c) the LOOKAHEADS — `_meth_fn_at` resolves `T_m` and was blind to a dyn receiver until it learned
the prototype (an inferred `var r = o.rq8();`, a destructure, a `Str` result, a `*T` result forming a dyn), and it
must not make one before pass 1 has registered every struct (`_dyn_p2`).

7. THE CALLEE OF AN INDIRECT CALL THROUGH MEMORY THE ARGUMENTS CAN REWRITE IS LOADED AND SPILLED FIRST — to a
fresh frame slot, before `self` and any argument (an argument may reseat the dyn: row C-ORD). The call is then
`ECALLIND` (`call [rbp+disp32]` / `blr`) on every target, PE included — never `ECALLPTR_PE`.

8. A FRAME TEMP PASSED TO A TAIL CALL DIVERTS THE CALL (6.7.7's tuple literal argument, again) — and only a tail
caller WITHOUT parameters proves it. A dyn formed in place as an argument lives in an `_agg_temp`; `_sarg_escapes`
makes the tail call a real call. The planned row (a tail caller WITH parameters, outside a loop) survived the
mutation: its temp sat below the slots the callee homes into, intact by luck. The killing row is a parameterless
tail caller, whose temp sits in the callee's own parameter slots.

9. "HOLDS A DYN" IS JUDGED ON THE COMPLETE STRUCT. Struct locals are not zeroed, so an unformed dyn — or a struct
local holding one — is refused. A mark set at the struct's definition missed a generic instance: `Box<Hd>`
re-parses `v: T` through the binding arm, never `_add_named_field`, so `var b: Box<Hd>;` slipped through. `_dyn_held`
walks the complete struct's fields, memoized per sid.

10. INFERRING THROUGH A POINTER CHANGES WHAT A COMPILING PROGRAM COMPUTES. Binding T from a `*Box<T>` parameter's
pointee turned a program that ran the i64 base (and read `Box<Pt>` with `Box<i64>`'s layout: 4) into one running
`gx$Pt` (8). The user approved that for the `*G<T>` carrier only (fork F1); a pointer passed to a by-value `b:
Box<T>` binds nothing on purpose (gate row R18e pins the old 4), and the review found the carrier's field-chain
form (`gx(h.bp)`) still on the base — a silent wrong layout until `_gsc_chain` walked the chain to its leaf.

11. A SOURCE TOOL THAT READS A TYPE AS ONE WORD MEETS `dyn Tr`, the first type with a space in it. `cyrius header`
skips a parameter's type to its own depth-0 `,` (6.7.7's tuple fix), so it holds; a one-word skip reads `Tr` as a
parameter (mutation MTT4: `cyr_val total(cyr_val a, cyr_val Area, …)`).

12. THE CALL'S ABI PATH WAS PROVEN BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN (D0). A scratch-only probe compiler routed `callptr(&f,
..)` through the planned sequence (callee in a frame slot, `_sc_pre_m`, `self`, `_owncall_args_at`, `_sc_x8`,
`ECALLIND`, `_sc_post`) and ran i64 / f64 / 16 B / 24 B (aarch64 X8) / 7-13-argument / top-level rows on x86,
qemu, cxvm, wine AND ecb / ach / cass / pi; proof the probe took the path was the same rows FAILING under the base
compilers and the PE binary's `ff 95` count. Nothing of the probe was committed. See the class entries for the
rest of 6.7.8's dyn notes: aggregate_layout_is_recorded_never_inferred_from_a_neighbour_slot (the declared-scalar
validity bit, and the bit-62 collision with C2), two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority
(pass-1 carrier records, the trait-signature latch), top_level_has_no_frame_so_a_lowering_that_needs_one_must_refuse_or_bring_its_own,
tuples_anonymous_structs_and_the_multi_value_bridge_v677 #11 (cx r0:r4).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/dyn_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger M-T*, M-F*, M-C*, MR*, MTT*);
tests/tcyr/crossos/dyn_objects.tcyr; generic_struct_inference.tcyr; struct_arg_type_refused.sh R18*. CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

[[entries]]
name = "typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.8's C2 (p[i] on *T, *p at the element's width, slice stores, the bounds-checked mode): the element kind rides the pointer's step word and must be masked at a closed list of readers; one subscript path branches only on the base; a postfix on the factor belongs in the PARSE_FACTOR wrapper and a base is judged by its declaration, never the scale word; a signed slice read routes through the store's address; a replayed fragment checks its own terminator; a whole-program switch is decided once, in the shared preprocessor; a check that is a call into injected cyrius needs no per-target emitter; @unsafe is a token range; the constant rule reuses the const evaluator behind a pre-screen; a scrub must unset."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.8", "C2", "subscript", "GPSC", "step word", "element descriptor", "_sub_post", "_slice_idx_addr", "for step", "_bnd_setup", "bounds check", "@unsafe", "#inline", "_read_env", "CYRIUS_POISON"]
---
1. THE ELEMENT KIND RIDES THE STEP WORD, MASKED AT A CLOSED LIST OF READERS. `*p` / `p[i]` need T's kind (signed,
float, bool) wherever a `*T` value is, and the step sizeof(T) already reaches every such place — the slot mark,
the field and return tables, the global table, then GPSC, which pointer arithmetic writes back from its operand.
Packing the descriptor as `ps | ed << 40` gives the kind exactly the step's reach, with no second name resolution
to drift. The price is a READER CENSUS: every GPSC reader either tests `== 0` / `> 0` (unchanged: ed is nonzero
only when the step is) or uses the value as a NUMBER and must mask (`_ps_step`: EPTR_SCALE, `_ptr_add`,
`_int_plus_ptr`, `_ptr_diff`, the if-expression's merge) — a missed one is silent, so each has a row and a
mutation. A SHAPE KEY (GVTYPE, compared by `_gv_same_shape`) must NOT carry the kind: `*u8` / `*i8` globals still
fold, so the kind went to a side byte map. Count the SETTERS by reading the factor, not the plan: the plan listed
six; a step-8 `*T` local (`*i64`, `*f64`, `*bool`) keeps SLTYPE 8 and loads through the factor's narrow-scalar
arm, a seventh (found by a debug compiler printing GPSC at each `*expr` over every source x kind).
6.7.12 (D27) — THE STEP WORD GREW A THIRD FIELD: A `*V`'s VECTOR. A vector pointee has no element kind, and
`_hl_psid` types only structs, so `rd(*pv)` could not tell an `f64v2` from an `i32v4` pointee by step (both 16).
Bits 48-55 of a typed pointer's step word now carry the vector descriptor less 2048 (`_ek_vd` / `_ps_vw` /
`_ps_vdesc`), written at every place a `*V` is declared (local, parameter, field, `: *V` return, global —
`_gv_pvd`, a lazily allocated table) and carried by captures and `pv + n`. The trap is this item's again, one field
wider: everything that compares step words for EQUALITY (`_ps_merge`, `_pk_word`'s stamp) now sees two `*V` of
different vectors as different words with the same step; every numeric reader must mask (`_ps_step`), every kind
reader must mask bits 40-47; `_pk_post` vouches a word whose kind OR vector is set. (6.7.11's ARG-8 — a one-byte
struct pointer's step word carries no kind — is the same class.) Proof: the corpus stayed byte-identical (x86_64
and PE) with the new bits set.
6.7.13 (D9 (a), ST-19, VC-11) — A FOURTH FIELD: THE POINTER-TO-POINTER PIN. The map is now step 0-39 / kind 40-47 /
vector 48-55 / pin 56-62: an index 1..126 into `_pp_tab`, the interned pointee word of a `**T`. Bit 63 must stay CLEAR
(every reader tests `> 0`). Two traps of this item again: a DEREFERENCE's result word must be stamped (`_PK_MARK`), or
`_pk_word` strips its kind and `**pp` reads a word; and `*pp OP= n` needs `_asg_cstep` set to the pointee's step, or
`*pp += 1` steps a byte while `*pp = *pp + 1` steps an element. A field `**S` of the struct being declared pins S late
and is sized at the dereference, as a late `*S` is. And A STEP WORD CANNOT NAME A STRUCT: a pin interned by its step
word alone conflates two structs of one size (`**Q3` accepted as `**P3`, mutation M-DR3) — key the pin by its
struct; the 126 cap then counts structs (filed, Low).
6.7.14 (VC-1) — A FIELD SIZED FOR ITS FIRST KEY BECOMES A CAP WHEN THE KEY WIDENS. Keying the pin by struct made two
same-size structs take two pins where one had served, so the 7-bit field (126 pins) refused 127 distinct two-word
structs taken through `**S` — programs 6.7.12, which had no pins, compiled: a 6.7.13 regression on every target.
Re-derive a field's width when its intern key changes. Free bits can be found where the word's other fields are
PROVABLY 0: a `**T`'s pointee is a pointer, so its word names no vector, and bits 48-55 were always 0 there. The map
for a pinned word is now bit 62 (pinned) + a 14-bit pin in bits 48-61 (`_ps_pinned` / `_ps_pin`; `_ps_vdesc` answers 0
for a pinned word): 16,383 pointees, `_pp_tab` growing by doubling, a `**T` global's pin in 16 bits. The intern was a
linear search; 6.7.15 (VC-3) hashes it and keeps first-come numbers (vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_
pointer_typing_v6715 #12).

2. ONE SUBSCRIPT PATH: RESOLVE THE NAME, THEN BRANCH ONLY ON THE BASE. `_arr_sub_addr` resolves a name in the
factor's order (local, capture, global) and reads one element descriptor — an array's from its declaration, a
pointer's from its step word — and everything after is shared (the index, EPTR_SCALE, the load / store helpers
`_esub_load` / `_esub_assign`); only the base differs (an array's ADDRESS vs a pointer's VALUE). `*p` (C2-5) and
the slice writes reuse the same helpers. A FLOAT ELEMENT HAS NO INTEGER WIDTH TO NARROW FROM: an i64 element read
sets GEXW 8, so storing it into an f32 element warned "narrowing" where an f32 local / field warns nothing —
`_esub_assign` clears GEXW for a float element.

3. A POSTFIX ON THE FACTOR BELONGS IN THE PARSE_FACTOR WRAPPER, JUDGED BY THE TOKENS IT ENDED ON. `_sub_post` runs
after `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` when the next token is `[`: the token before it says what the base is (`.name` a field,
`)` a call whose `(` lies past the factor's first token, `]` a second subscript) — no arm in the giant, no second
resolution. NEVER JUDGE A BASE BY THE SCALE WORD ALONE: a call's arguments are parsed inside the factor and
`_field_load_type` writes the word only for a `*T` field, so after `mk(q).n` (q a `*i32`) the word still held q's
step; a field base reads its OWN declaration (trusted only when the field load ended at the cursor, `_flt_ti`), a
direct call its callee's `_fn_rps`. A STATEMENT'S CALLEE IS IN THE TOKENS: statement position sets no scale, and a
closure body among the arguments re-enters the statement arms and overwrites any recorded callee — `_sub_callee`
walks back from the `)` to its `(` (and over `<..>`).

4. ONE ELEMENT, ONE LOAD — ROUTE THE READ THROUGH THE STORE'S ADDRESS. The v5.8.15 getters return a ZERO-extended
value from inside a library call, and no "sign-extend rax" emitter exists on every backend, so a signed slice read
is the store's checked address call (`_slice_idx_addr`) plus the typed-array load (`_esub_load`, IR-recorded) — the
same code `OP=` uses, so the read and the compound cannot disagree; only signed kinds take it (the differential's
three codegen inputs were exactly the signed-slice files). A test comparing against a signed narrow field THROUGH A
POINTER could not run under CYRIUS_IR=3 until 6.7.14 (issues/archived/2026-10-08-ir3-drops-unrecorded-field-emits.md); it can
since IN-2, which records every narrow width in `EFIELD_LOAD_W` itself (any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…,
the 11th).

5. A GRAMMAR FRAGMENT REPLAYED OUT OF PLACE MUST CHECK ITS OWN TERMINATOR. The classic-for step is skipped by paren
depth before the body and re-parsed after it through the statement's assignment helpers, which leave the
terminator to their CALLER — and in the replay the replay IS the caller, while PARSE_FOR restores the cursor after,
so junk vanished (`i += 1 2` built; a struct `a = b 2` stored ONE word). Check where the value ends, and NOT after a
helper that consumed the `)` itself (a second check refuses every valid struct step: "expected ')', got '{'"). A
check after an already-reported refusal is swallowed by the panic flag — pin those paths with "exactly one error
line" rows.

6. A WHOLE-PROGRAM SWITCH IS DECIDED ONCE, IN THE SHARED PREPROCESS (seven-forks class). Its line is judged on the
RAW entry, string-aware as PP_PASS reads it, and every judged line is BLANKED (newline kept) so line numbers stay
exact and PP_IFDEF_PASS — which walks the main source again — never judges it twice. The target refusal (kernel /
EFI / `shared;` / `object;`) cannot run there (`kernel;` is read in pass 1), so it sits at the END of the shared pass
1, and is FATAL at once: the appended checker otherwise reached pass 2 and EFI refused its write import as a second,
misleading error. cbt must hand a manifest `false` down as `CYRIUS_BOUNDS=0`, not as silence: the source's
`#bounds` is a rung BELOW the manifest that only cycc can read.

7. A CHECK THAT IS A CALL INTO INJECTED CYRIUS NEEDS NO PER-TARGET EMITTER. An inline unsigned compare + branch was
rejected: aarch64 / cx EJCC map signed conditions only, cx's EJCC overwrites r0, x86's push/pop peephole is not
reset across EPATCH, two patches per site would fill the 1023-entry jump table, and the failing block would need
`_xdepth` patched by hand. A CALL (the slice read's `ECALLPOPS` / `ECALLFIX` / `ECALLCLEAN`) keeps `_xdepth` exact
with no branch, so Win64 shadow space and SysV / aarch64 alignment come with it, and the checker text (plain cyrius
appended to the FINISHED unit) lowers through each target's existing write / exit — agnos's numbers chosen from the
PP flag table, since `_TARGET_AGNOS` is set after PREPROCESS. The per-file names go into the string pool AFTER
lexing: every backend reads GSPOS at fixup time, and the lexer's intern index re-syncs from its own `upto`.

8. `@unsafe` IS A TOKEN RANGE, NOT A DEPTH. `_unsafe_depth` is dynamic: an `#inline` fn's body replayed inside an
`@unsafe` block would inherit it and lose its own check (the 6.7.7 `return t;` lesson again: a replay runs in the
CALLER's dynamic context). The `@unsafe {` .. `}` ranges are found once and a site asks whether its `[` lies inside
one. ⚠ `return f(x);` is a TAIL CALL, never an inline replay — a row for "an `#inline` fn inside `@unsafe`" must call
it as `v = f(x);` (the `return` form passed with the depth mutation in place). A site's LINE is its `[` token's
(`GTLINE`), so every re-parse — a generic instance, an `#inline` / `defer` replay, a for step — names where the
subscript is written.

9. THE CONSTANT RULE REUSES THE CONST EVALUATOR BEHIND A TOKEN PRE-SCREEN: only literals, `+ - *`, parentheses and
names resolving — local const, local, capture, then const / enum constant, the factor's order — to an INTEGER
constant reach it, so the evaluator can neither report nor emit; its latches (`_ce_err`, `_ce_k`, `_ce_steps`) and
the cursor are put back. (The one lane test that stored `s[0 - 1]` on purpose now spells that index at run time: a
constant out of range warns in every build.)

10. A SCRUB MUST `unset`. `_read_env` treats an EMPTY value as set — `CYRIUS_POISON=` switched the poison mode on
until 6.7.11 (the three mode switches now read empty as off, through `_pp_env_rung`; `unset` stays the right scrub for
any variable read raw),
and lib/alloc.cyr (which src/main.cyr includes) reads it at compile time, so an exported one built a poison-mode
compiler and seed-derive went RED. `V=` keeps V exported. When the suite must keep the variable, scope the scrub to
the self-builds: check.sh's `_chk_selfbuild() { ( unset V; "$@" ); }`.

11. A DIAGNOSTIC OVER 120 COLUMNS IS TWO LITERALS. cyrlint flags lines over 120 characters, so a refusal's text is a
head + a tail (`_sub_err`) — and a gate that greps the message must match across the join.

Class entries holding the rest of C2: cfo_const_fold_rewind_class… (the slice read, seventh), any_pass_that_walks…
(the slice base's IR mark, seventh), boolean_stamp_and_bool_typing_v673 (`_PK_MARK`, `_PKD_MARK`),
aggregate_layout_is_recorded… (the slice bit, the N band, the shape key), two_passes… (a refused type in both passes),
there_are_seven_forks… (the shared PREPROCESS, two spawn arms), a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing… #8 (a quoted
diagnostic is a second copy; 0 NOPs), cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt… (extract before you grow; the NOP compactor's caps).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/{pointer_subscript_checked, bounds_mode_checked, slice_position_refused,
slice_signed_read, slice_read_left_operand, for_step_terminator_checked}.sh, toolchain/selfbuild_poison_scrubbed.sh,
toolchain/compile_flag_list.sh (each + mutation ledger). CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_at_every_size_and_packed_fields_v6710"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.10's B7 (the handle line at every size, packed u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 fields) and its ops lane (a field / dereference as an operator operand, `*T` copy sources): size-keyed classifiers are a family; a new storage class needs the whole consumer-arm inventory; a one-shot flag is reset at entry and cleared on read; the declaration zone cannot judge a global's initialiser when it is registered; type an operand from the parser's own records AFTER it is parsed, never from its token shape; an argument arm keyed on the first token skips what it does not list; pointer step 1 is ambiguous; the ftype cell carries only a width; an operand parked in rax across an unrecorded `lea` loses its reload under IR=3."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.10", "B7", "handle line", "packed fields", "u8", "f32", "_CL_CAP_BASE_RA", "_agc_plain_big", "_small_sptr", "_sci_sval", "_gz_sweep", "_hl_psid", "_flo_note", "_dlo_park", "_var_rhs_post", "_hl_p1", "_fld_uns", "_struct_same_layout", "_OP_OPERAND_ADDR", "LASE"]
---
1. SIZE-KEYED CLASSIFIERS ARE A FAMILY — when a class boundary moves, grep every `STRUCTSZ > 8` / `< 8`.
The handle line moved "a handle" from "a struct over 8 B" to "a struct not declared from a struct value", and
three classifiers still keyed on the size: `_CL_CAP_BASE_RA` (a closure capture's base) keyed on
`STRUCTSZ > 8`, so registering the small handle alone gave the issue's repro 5, not 237 — the env word was
copied as the value; `_agc_plain_big` made a `*T` over 8 B a copy-assign handle only because of its SIZE — the
fix is one predicate (`_small_sptr`, beside `_small_hdl` in `_agc_handle_local`), so every `_agc_*` consumer
(copy, call / result into, field into, dyn / tuple destination checks) switched at once (one knock-on: dyn_checked
V4m, `sp = pass(o)` into a `*S1`, is now refused by the copy's type check, the 16 B twin's message); and a
global's read keyed on its SLOT size (`var_sizes < 8` = an i8 / i16 / i32 scalar) silently stopped a VALUE
global under 8 B from dispatching its operator (B7-2r).

2. A NEW STORAGE CLASS NEEDS THE CONSUMER-ARM INVENTORY. Every by-value consumer — an argument, a `: S` return,
a right operand, an if-expression branch, both sides of a copy, in a fn and at top level — had to learn the
small handle; one missed arm is a silent wrong value, not an error. The odd sizes (3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B) copy through
a temp so no byte past the object is read; the last arm found reading past it was an odd-sized by-value `self`
loaded rounded up to 4 / 8 bytes (`_self_value_ra`, B7-1r). The guide's known defect — a dereference of a 1-7 B
`*S`, and a whole 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B field, used as an operand loaded as 8 bytes — closed in two steps: the dereference
in 6.7.11 (ST-1), the field and every temp's padding in 6.7.13 (ST-1, ST-3; struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_
replayed_returns_v6711 #1).

3. A ONE-SHOT FLAG IS RESET AT ENTRY AND CLEARED ON READ. `_sci_sval` ("this initialiser was a struct value")
left at 1 by a declaration that never read it marked the NEXT handle inline.

4. THE DECLARATION ZONE CANNOT JUDGE A GLOBAL'S INITIALISER WHEN THE GLOBAL IS REGISTERED: the right-hand side is
parsed by the replay, after every fn body, and a call in it may name a fn declared below. The end-of-pass-1
sweep (`_gz_sweep`) judges it from the TOKENS — an expression typer for calls, generics, method chains,
overloaded operators dispatched on the left operand's struct, if-expressions — and repeats until nothing
changes; the replay (`_gz_late`) re-checks against what the parse recorded and REFUSES a disagreement. A token
answer must be the parser's answer or a loud refusal, never a silent wrong layout. At top level a struct temp is
a hidden global (`_HTEMP`'s top arm, `_hl_gtemp`).

5. TYPE AN OPERAND FROM THE PARSER'S OWN RECORDS AFTER IT IS PARSED, NEVER FROM ITS TOKEN SHAPE.
  (a) B7-1d: a struct-value test written against `* IDENT ;` missed every other spelling of the same value —
  `(*p)`, `*h.p`, `*gp()`. `_hl_psid` types the dereference's operand instead: one pointer-aware chain reader
  shared by locals, the declaration zone and the late arm.
  (b) OPS-1, the 6.7.6 call-result stamp generalised: a whole struct-typed field read stamps its span, base and
  offset with the code pointer after its load (`_flo_note`), and `_op_lst` takes it as the left operand only when
  it spans the whole operand with nothing emitted since. A dereference needs its POINTER after the load, so it is
  parked in a hidden temp BEFORE the load whenever an overloadable operator follows (`_dlo_park` — a superset;
  harmless when the dereference turns out not to be the left operand). OPS-5 reuses it: a `*` at the start of an
  address-passed argument or a right operand is "wanted" (`_dro_want`) and its consumer takes the parked pointer
  under the same span test — one mechanism for the left operand, the right operand and the argument.
  (c) OPS-2: an untyped binding's type is inferred AFTER the initialiser (`_sc_post_op` notes the record as an
  operator's; `_var_rhs_post`). The peek-before-parse inference (`_var_rhs_call`) can see only the leading call —
  which is why `var r = mk3(4) - s;` with an integer-returning `P3_sub` is still typed `P3` (open item 1 of
  issues/archived/2026-10-08-struct-operator-results.md); the post-parse inference overrides it whenever the whole
  initialiser is a struct-returning operator.
  (d) OPS-3: the copy-assign operand classifier answered only NAMES (`_agc_operand`), so a `*T` global, call or
  field fell to a one-word store at every size — the fix reads the source after it is parsed (rax = the pointer)
  from `_hl_pchain` rather than growing the name classifier (name_based_copy_resolvers_need_the_capture_rung).
  (e) OPS-6: TWO TYPERS OF ONE TOKEN SHAPE DRIFT. `_hl_psid` already read `p ± n` / `n + p`; OPS-3's source reader
  stopped at `_hl_pchain`, so one typed `*(p + 1)` and the other did not type `l = p + 1`. Route the source
  through the one that reads arithmetic.

6. AN ARGUMENT ARM KEYED ON THE FIRST TOKEN SILENTLY SKIPS EVERY SHAPE IT DOES NOT LIST (OPS-4).
`_try_push_struct_addr_arg` took a name, a call and a `(`, so `take(*p + a)` — an argument starting with `*` —
fell to the value push (the result's first word as an address: a crash) even after the operator path learned to
dispatch on it. The expression arm (`_push_struct_expr_arg`) is the safe default for any other first token.

7. POINTER STEP 1 IS AMBIGUOUS. An i8 / i16 / i32 local reads with pointer step 1 (v6.3.34, so `n + 2` adds 2)
exactly as `&x` does, so an "is this argument a pointer?" test on GPSC alone dereferenced narrow integers.
`_hl_p1` decides step 1 by the LEFTMOST term of the top-level `+` / `-` chain (`+` keeps its left operand's step)
and treats a primary that reads a narrow local as a value. Two edges stay the word by the compiler's own typing:
an if-expression of pointers of DIFFERENT steps (`_ps_merge` gives no step) and `n + pb` with a narrow `n` left.

8. THE ftype CELL CARRIES ONLY A WIDTH. Every reader decoded `ft < 0` as "narrow and signed", so u8 / u16 / u32
needed a side map (`_fld_uns`, one byte per field-pool entry, alloc()'d — no heap region); the two places that
negate a width by hand (`_field_load_on`, `_fld_compound`) are the only readers of the sign.
`_struct_same_layout` compared ftype alone, so `i8` / `u8` and `i32` / `f32` would have compared EQUAL once they
shared a width — it compares the kind too (the redefinition warning). And dyn decision 2 meets the packed layout:
`C4 { u8 x4 }` shrinks from 32 B into the `self: T` refusal.

9. AN OPERAND PARKED IN rax ACROSS AN UNRECORDED `lea` LOSES ITS RELOAD UNDER CYRIUS_IR=3. OPS-1's first cut
returned "rax" (-1) for the left operand; `_sc_pre`'s `lea` records no IR node, so LASE dropped the reload and
the 24 B rows read garbage. The operand is emitted by `_OP_OPERAND_ADDR` (`_OPD_SV`) after a >16 B result's
retptr push. A by-value struct-VARIABLE operand still meets the class (any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…;
issues/archived/2026-10-08-ir3-drops-unrecorded-field-emits.md, with the 24 B method-on-a-field-through-`*W` shape).

GATES: tests/gates/codegen/small_struct_handle_line.sh, tests/gates/codegen/struct_value_codegen.sh,
tests/gates/frontend/narrow_fields_checked.sh, tests/gates/codegen/hidden_temp_census.sh (26 `_HTEMP` users);
the crossos rows of language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710.
CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-1 .. B7-2, OPS-1 .. OPS-6).

[[entries]]
name = "derive_codecs_share_one_classifier_v6710"
description = "Gotchas met adding u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 / cstring fields to #derive(Serialize) and fixing its Str decode in 6.7.10: three codecs each kept their own byte ladder (now one classifier over the parser's type vocabulary); bayan's pairs form cannot tell `\"null\"` from `null` by the value bytes; a 16-byte stack Str passed by name to a `s: Str` parameter is its data word; two decoders of one field type disagreed; the scanner's skip of a nested struct is not depth-aware."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.10", "derive", "Serialize", "JSON", "cstring", "Str", "PP_DFIELD_EK", "_tn_class", "bayan", "_cy_json_skipval", "PP_DQSTR_SCAN", "str_json_unescape"]
---
1. ONE CLASSIFIER OVER THE PARSER'S TYPE VOCABULARY. The three codecs (`_to_json`, `_from_json`,
`_from_json_str`) each carried their own byte ladder for i8..i64 and f64, and the offset table its own (B7-2) —
so a type the parser learned could be missed by one codec. They share `PP_DFIELD_INT` / `PP_DFIELD_FLT` over
`PP_DFIELD_EK` = `_ek_of_code(_tn_scalar(..))` now. i64 and u64 share an element descriptor (ek 8), so u64 is
excluded by its CLASS (`_tn_class == 2`), not by its width.

2. THE PAIRS FORM CANNOT TELL "null" FROM null BY THE VALUE. bayan's pairs keep a quoted value WITHOUT its
quotes, so the string `"null"` and the literal `null` are the same 4 bytes there. The cstring decoder reads the
byte BEFORE the value (a quoted value's is its opening `"`), and only for a 4-byte `null` text — coupled to
bayan's borrow representation: a refold that copies values turns derive_cstring_field.sh's T rows RED, not
silent.

3. A 16-BYTE STACK Str PASSED BY NAME IS ITS DATA WORD. The encoder passes its stack Str by ADDRESS (`&t`): by
name an `s: Str` parameter received the cell's first word (mutation M29 SIGSEGVs) — the B7-F by-name trap
meeting lib code.

4. TWO DECODERS OF ONE FIELD TYPE DISAGREED. The scanner decoded only `\n \t \r` and the pairs form kept every
escape raw (and returned a borrow into the parsed document) — row S6. Both call one lib helper now
(`str_json_unescape`, a fresh Str; `str_json_unescape_cstr` for a cstring), and the scanner's Str arm is the
same escape-aware quoted walk as its cstring arm (`PP_DQSTR_SCAN`), which removed ~35 emitter lines
(cycc −568 B).

5. A SKIP THAT IS NOT DEPTH-AWARE STOPS INSIDE THE VALUE. The scanner walks a non-string cstring value with the
shared depth-aware `_cy_json_skipval`, so an object value does not end at its first inner `,`. Its NESTED
`#derive`d struct arm still scans to the next `,` / `}` without depth, so it stops inside the nested object and
every field after it is lost (left 0) — the open known defect; the pairs form decodes all of it.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/derive_cstring_field.sh, tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_narrow.tcyr,
tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_cstring.tcyr. User-facing: language/features.cyml derive_str_fields.
CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-3, B7-4, B7-4b).

[[entries]]
name = "instrumentation_belongs_at_the_body_start_not_in_the_prologue_v6710"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.10's execution-coverage mode (P5-B): a per-fn hit belongs at the BODY start, not in EFNPRO (live argument registers, no fn index, non-fns open frames there); a closure's hit after ESUBRSP; an #inline replay is a body with no call; cx's EADDRA_IMM clobbers r1; compiler-made program data goes in an SVGAI image blob; the exit hook runs after the arguments and before every target lowering; exit predicates are per target; only a builtin / EEXIT exit is visible; and the CLI side — the preflight chain, a quiet manifest probe, a capture file its user must remove."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.10", "P5-B", "execution coverage", "CYRIUS_COVERAGE", "EFNPRO", "ESUBRSP", "#inline", "EADDRA_IMM", "SVGAI", "exit hook", "_cov_body", "_cov_tail", "_cli_preflight", "_mf_probe"]
---
1. NOT IN THE PROLOGUE. At EFNPRO the arguments are still live — Win64 coroutine SELF in rcx, the aarch64 x8
retptr, a closure's env register — EFNPRO does not know the fn index, and it also opens NON-fns (the aarch64 /
cx top-level micro-frame, `_cyrius_init`, the async / enum constructors). At the BODY start (`_cov_body`: after
the frame, the parameters and the by-value struct copies) every register is dead, so the hit is the ordinary
accumulator emitters, with no backend change.

2. A CLOSURE'S HIT BELONGS AFTER ESUBRSP. A push before the frame exists clobbers parameter slot 0 (the v6.2.41
shape; P5B-5's mutant MC3 crashed 46 files). The swap is `_cl_frame_open`: ESUBRSP, then the hit.

3. AN #inline REPLAY IS A BODY WITH NO CALL, so a counter at the definition alone never sees it: the hit belongs
at each replay's start too (`_cov_body` with ti = -1, PARSE_FNCALL's one PARSE_PROG swapped).

4. cx's EADDRA_IMM CLOBBERS r1, so the hit keeps the counter's address on the stack.

5. COMPILER-MADE PROGRAM DATA GOES IN AN SVGAI IMAGE BLOB, which every writer bakes — no string-pool or fixup
surgery for the counter band and the fn map (`_cov_greg`).

6. THE EXIT HOOK RUNS AFTER THE ARGUMENTS ARE EVALUATED and before every target lowering: a hook before them
loses `syscall(60, f())`'s `f` (mutant M12). Its call (`_cov_call_dump`) clobbers only the accumulator, and its
pushes are balanced by ECALLPOPS / ECALLCLEAN from `_xdepth`, so the syscall's pushed arguments survive.

7. EXIT PREDICATES ARE PER TARGET. Raw 231 is an exit on x86 / cx only — `munlockall` on ELF-aarch64, -38
(unrouted) on PE; a wrong predicate dumps TWICE (M9 / M16). Only an exit lowered through the syscall builtin or
EEXIT, with a compile-time-constant number, is visible: an `asm` exit, a run-time number, a `#naked` exit, exec,
a signal are the documented residual (the CLI names such a run).

8. HOOKING INSIDE A GIANT FN: three fns sit at cybs's per-fn reference ceiling, so new behaviour went in through
1-for-1 call swaps (cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt…) — the counts measured before and after. 6.7.12, two more instances
(WN-1, WN-4): the new PE reroutes could not join `_PE_ROUTE_SOCK`, so they live in a new `_PE_ROUTE_FILELOCK`
called from its end (0xF04E then joined that one), and `_pe_wp_slashes` was split out of `_pe_wp_target`. Put a new
route group in a chained dispatch fn from the start; seed-derive after each such change.

9. THE CLI SIDE. (a) A verb that joins the AUTO_DEPS chain conditionally (`coverage --exec`) must also join
`_cli_preflight`, or every refusal it names lands AFTER lib/ and cyrius.lock are written. (b) Asking the
manifest a question that HAS an answer without it ("is the mode on?") must not report its read failure:
`_mf_probe` reads quietly and forgets the failure, so the reader that needs the manifest names it once. (c) A
per-process capture file in a temp dir that is rmdir'd (never swept) must be removed by its user — keeping it
"for later" leaked the whole dir (`_dep_wt_err` → `_dep_wt_why`). (d) The reader takes a record's NAME field
only, whole (`_src_ids_add` per line): tokenising would split `sh_gen$i` at the `$` and make the `$` cut a no-op.

Class entries holding the rest of P5-B: cfo_const_fold_rewind_class… (`_cfo = 0` after the hit — defensive,
not provable), there_are_seven_forks… (`_cov_tail` in all seven; the Windows spawn arm's environment),
counter_model_gate_shares_the_defect (H7, the absolute mode-off anchor), an_environment_reader… (the shared
256 B scratch; `_pp_env_rung`), a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code… (no record = a named failure),
methodology.cyml a_gate_whose_fixture_cannot_express_the_bug… (M7b's two-line probe).
GATES: tests/gates/codegen/coverage_exec_hits.sh, tests/gates/toolchain/coverage_exec_cli.sh,
tests/gates/memory/coverage_mode_corpus.sh (`# check: serial`). User-facing: language/tooling.cyml
execution_coverage_mode_v6710. CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (P5-B).

[[entries]]
name = "multi_value_returns_one_value_proof_and_the_producer_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's returns lane (RET-1 .. RET-10: the one-value proof through loops, counted undeclared returns, the declared producer, struct words as return registers, a multi-value call is not a struct, pair-method prescans, literal elements, parenthesised receives, async multi-value refusals, one error per statement): a token-walk proof trips on the do-block's `}`; a new per-fn flag bit must be checked against the reset mask; judge before the arms and report after them; classify a struct callee by ABI before any counted arity; every named-struct destination keyed on GFRS > 0; a prescan resolves, never registers; scope a panic latch to its statement."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "returns lane", "multi-value return", "_body_ends_in_return", "_brk_inner_end", "GFLG", "_bx_fn_reset", "GFRN", "_prescan_ret_arity", "_ret_agg_class", "_dt_zone_check", "_pm_copies", "_gen_decl_check", "_fn_by_defti", "_spi_expr", "_scv_peel", "_fn_async_impl", "_sync_skip", "_pair_sync_ti", "_ptr_refd_ti"]
---
1. THE ONE-VALUE PROOF IS A PASS-1 TOKEN WALK (RET-1). `_body_ends_in_return` records the first token of the body's
last top-level statement. Trap: the do-block's `}` restarts the statement, so `do { } while (1);` reads as a final
`while` statement with the `do` the statement BEFORE it; and a `break` inside a switch / match leaves THAT
construct, not the loop. Fix: match the `} while ( 1 ) ;` tail; the break test steps every nested break target's
body (`_brk_inner_end`), plus closures and nested fns.

2. A NEW PER-FN FLAG BIT MUST BE CHECKED AGAINST `_bx_fn_reset`'S MASK, NOT THE HEAP MAP ALONE (RET-2). GFLG bits
4096..32768 were 6.7.3's bool flags, cleared by `_bx_fn_reset` (`0 - 61441`) but missing from the heap map — the
planned 16384 / 32768 would have been wiped. The count went to bits 16 / 17, and the map now lists 12-17. Also:
GFRN is the DECLARED arity (`_dsg_save` / `_dsg_check` read it) — a pass-1 inference must not write it.

3. JUDGE BEFORE THE ARMS, REPORT AFTER THEM (RET-3). A new refusal at PARSE_RETURN's entry would pre-empt the arms'
own, more specific refusals (several report through `_dyn_err`, which does not latch `_panic`). The wrapper judges
first and reports after the arms parse the expression, only if `_had_error` did not move; in a file that has already
failed it reports up front, where the latch keeps the statement to one error.

4. CLASSIFY A STRUCT CALLEE BY ITS ABI BEFORE ANY COUNTED ARITY (RET-4). `_prescan_ret_arity` counts a `: P` fn's
crossing arms (`return (x, x + 1, 9)`) as three values, so the counted arity accepted three names from a two-word
struct. `_ret_agg_class` (2 retptr / 1 rax:rdx / 0 one word) decides first. Second trap: the declaration-zone replay
has no statement resync, so a report there cascaded into the call's own top-level refusal — `_dt_zone_check` hands
its latch to the replay, which clears it after the statement's PCMPE.

5. EVERY NAMED-STRUCT DESTINATION KEYED ON GFRS > 0 (RET-5). A declared multi-value return is MULTIRET_SID (negative)
and an undeclared one 0, so all five destinations (argument, method argument, declaration, field, assignment) fell to
the value path and took the FIRST value as the struct's address. At a call site `_pm_copies` is what tells a by-value
struct parameter from a `*P` one (both set the struct-mask bit). `_gen_decl_check` reset a generic callee's declaration
to untyped whenever the callee returned no struct.

6. A PRESCAN MUST RESOLVE, NEVER REGISTER (RET-6). The pair prescan reads impl methods from `_tr_prepass`'s tables and
resolves each with `_fn_by_defti` on the pass-1 definition start: a method's mangled name is not its token text, and a
FINDFN-or-REGFN of the bare name mints an entry the undefined-call check then reports (the bayan 17-warning
regression). An untyped impl `self` has no annotation for the token-level typer (`_tok_name_sid`), so the scan
carries the impl's struct (GFISELF). The trait-DEFAULT body is not in `_tim_tab` (filed, High).

7. A POSITIONAL LITERAL'S NESTED STRUCT FIELD PARSES ITS FIRST LEAF ELSEWHERE (RET-7). The carried scalar of
`H { f(7), 3, 5 }` (with `p: P` first) is parsed in `_spi_expr`, not `EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD_W`, so a per-element check
needs both sites (mutants M31a / M31b split 17f from 17g).

8. PARSE_VAR HAS TWO FIRST-TOKEN READERS OF AN UNTYPED INITIALISER (RET-8): the > 8 B receive and
`_ti_inferred_sid`, plus `_var_rhs_msid` for methods. A wrapped call is taken by the typed path's `_scv_peel` once
pscale is inferred — never by the asv retptr / pair paths, which consume `= IDENT(` themselves. A refused declaration
that KEEPS its annotation gives an accurate follow-on `.field` error; one that took the inferred struct made `x + k`
link-fail on `P2_add`. A generic base parses `var x: T` with T the i64 base (`_inst_name` 0): a refusal keyed on the
annotation must skip it and let each instance judge.

9. "IN AN ASYNC BODY" IS `_fn_async_impl` ALONE (RET-9). An async fn's body is `f$impl` (a coroutine's too), so
`_cur_fn_coro` adds nothing and a closure is its own fn. `ret2` returns from the fn it is written in (`_mret_land`
jumps to the epilogue) even as an expression statement, so `ret2(a, b);` in an async body is a multi-value return.

10. AN IN-SYNC REFUSAL MUST NOT HAND ITS PANIC LATCH TO THE STATEMENT WRAPPER (RET-10). `_sync_skip` then consumes the
NEXT statement through its `;`, its own error with it. Clearing the latch at the report is not the fix either — the
same statement's later checks (a bool destination's "not a bool") then add a second error about the same value. Scope
the latch to the statement: record where it was set and let the statement's own end clear it (`_pair_sync_ti` /
`_stmt_resync`).

11. A REFUSED TYPE SHOULD LEAVE ITS POINTER STEP IN PLACE (RET-10). Returning 0 from `_ptr_annot` silences the
declaration's warning but moves one to every use; mark the refused token (`_ptr_refd_ti`) and skip that one warning
instead.
(The lane's vacuous-on-cx gate is in a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6.)
User-facing: language/features.cyml ret2_rethi § SINCE 6.7.11, struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611 (6);
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml multi_value_returns_and_struct_words_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG [6.7.11]
(RET-1 .. RET-10).

[[entries]]
name = "fn_pointer_results_parameter_classes_and_argument_readers_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's call-arguments lane (ARG-1 .. ARG-9: `fncallN(&f, ..)` struct / vector results, a value-form vector local, `p: *V`, the parameter type grammar, the size-independent by-value check, generic carriers through a wrap or a dereference, the kind-4 argument warning, one-byte struct pointers): a parameter's class is decided at its type NAME; a fn-pointer call's result rides the method call's struct hooks, and a decide-before-parse destination needs a token route; a spelling made transparent to one argument reader must be transparent to all three; a one-byte struct pointer's step word carries no element kind; a class a CALL reads is recorded in pass 1 too; judge a dereference argument after its parse, from tokens. (ARG-3 and ARG-9 went into their classes.)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "callargs lane", "fncallN", "callptr", "_param_class", "_pvec_param", "_sc_pre", "_sc_x8", "ECALLPTR_PE", "_icall_sid_at", "_expr_struct_sid", "_gsc_arg", "_tok_gen_sc_sid", "_pwrap_k", "_hl_psid", "_iep_", "_param_float", "_fnt_pbool", "_ess_deref", "_gz_split"]
---
1. A PARAMETER'S CLASS IS DECIDED AT ITS TYPE NAME, BY BOTH SCANS (ARG-4). Anything that strips a `*` before
classifying must ask whether one stood there: `p: *f64v2` was classified as its bare vector name and became a
value-form vector in both passes. `_param_class` reads the token before the name. The call-site address push needs
the parameter's pointee from the definition's tokens (`_pvec_param`), since no per-parameter table records a vector
type behind a pointer.

2. A FN-POINTER CALL'S STRUCT / VECTOR RESULT RIDES THE METHOD CALL'S STRUCT-CALL HOOKS (ARG-1, ARG-2). `_sc_pre`
runs after PE's callee push and is counted in ECALLPTR_PE's argc (a misorder pops the result pointer as the callee:
exit 5 on PE ONLY — keep a PE leg on the gate); `_sc_x8` sits before ECALLIND on aarch64. The decide-before-parse
destinations (an argument, `return`, the declaration zone's sweep) cannot see the parse's struct record yet, so each
needs a token-level route (`_icall_sid_at` / `_icall_rs_at` / `_icall_open_at`) that the parse's record then
confirms — without the zone's route the replay's agreement check refused an 8 B global as "the declaration zone
misread". The callee is known only as `&f`; a variable callee stays one word (D31). On Win64 a vector result needs
the caller's hidden pointer at EVERY call site: the receive passes it, a DISCARDED result still does not (filed, High).

3. A SPELLING MADE TRANSPARENT TO ONE READER OF AN ARGUMENT MUST BE TRANSPARENT TO ALL OF THEM (ARG-5, ARG-6,
ARG-5r). Three readers key on an argument's first token: the struct CHECK (`_expr_struct_sid`), the generic
INFERENCE (`_gsc_arg`) and its token mirror in the pair scans (`_tok_gen_sc_sid`). 6.7.6 gave the `_pwrap_k` peel to
the address-passed arms only; the by-value check missed it until 6.7.11, and when the check learned the wrap and the
whole dereference the inference had not, an argument of the parameter's own generic struct met a false "different
struct type" (the base `W1` vs `W1<i32>`); with the inference fixed and the scans not, a `?` above a wrapper typed
through `unbox((b))` died rc 139 at run time. Inside a wrap a name / call must END at the innermost `)`, or a
tuple literal's first element reads as the whole argument (`bs1((t, 3))`).

4. A ONE-BYTE STRUCT POINTER'S STEP WORD CARRIES NO ELEMENT KIND (ARG-8). The step word alone cannot tell `pb`
(`*B1`) from `&x` or a narrow local's step 1, so `_add_word` / `_hl_p1` read the operand's tokens (`_hl_psid`) for
it. And an if-expression's merged step word answers "same step?", not "every branch a pointer?" — a by-value consumer
needs the latter, so it is a side record (`_iep_*`), leaving the merge word, its warning and `(if ..) - q` untouched.
(On the merged tree the narrow-scalar fix, D15, folds `_add_rb1` into `_add_word`.)

5. A PER-PARAMETER CLASS THAT A CALL READS MUST BE RECORDED IN PASS 1 TOO (ARG-7). `_param_float` tags only the
callee's own slot; the f64 class went into `_fnt_pbool` from both `_bx_pscan` (pass 1) and `_bx_ptype` (pass 2), or
a call above the definition misses the warning.

6. CHECK BEFORE PUSH, FROM TOKENS (ARG-6). The by-value argument judges a whole dereference AFTER the argument's
parse, from tokens only (`_ess_deref`: one `* E` inside balanced parentheses, nothing after it — `_gz_split` -1 — E
typed by `_hl_psid`), so the push and its emission stay byte-identical and a later byte-exact load of `*p` lands in
the same path untouched.
Into their classes: ARG-3 (the two parameter scans, one span) — one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677;
ARG-9 (EFLADDR's unrecorded lea under LASE) — any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream.
User-facing: language/features.cyml struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676 (fn-pointer results),
typed_simd_value_type_abi, struct_argument_type_checked_v673; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ARG-1 .. ARG-9).

[[entries]]
name = "preprocessor_walk_tax_and_kernel_compaction_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's infra lane (the preprocessor walks, cybs's diagnostics and the seed's caps, kernel / CYRIUS_WX=0 DCE compaction): a per-byte call in a preprocessor walk is a ~3 % tax only a source census sees; a multiboot1 kernel enters in 32-bit protected mode, so a boot gate needs a long-mode shim; a mode differential finds what the default corpus never runs; a helper reached only through a fn a fork never runs is dead on that fork. (The cybs points went into cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt…, the compaction points into compaction_moves_code_geometry…)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "infra lane", "PP_LEXST_AT", "PP_LEXST", "#inline", "PP_NEUT_BOLMARK", "multiboot1", "long mode", "isa-debug-exit", "CYRIUS_DCE", "dead_code_floor", "wp_compact"]
---
1. A PER-BYTE CALL IN A PREPROCESSOR WALK IS A TAX, AND ONLY A SOURCE CENSUS SEES IT (INF-1). 6.6.20's attribute-line
rule moved every PP walk onto a 4-parameter wrapper (`PP_LEXST_AT`) that loads the byte and calls `PP_LEXST` — one
extra call per byte per pass, x10 walks, ~3 % of self_compile (measured −2.8 % / −3.3 % when removed). The cost was
the CALL LAYER, not the attribute test (dropping the test and keeping the call recovered nothing). `#inline` will not
absorb it (at most 2 parameters and 32 body tokens: `#inline ignored: fn takes more than 2 parameters`). Fix: test the
rare byte inline at each site (`if (load8(B + I) == 35) { slow } else { PP_LEXST(…) }`) — the slow path differs from
the plain machine only on that byte. A walk left on the bare machine is invisible to every behaviour test (the census
that enforces it is in a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing_and_the_ways_it_happens_are_enumerable).

2. A MULTIBOOT1 KERNEL ENTERS IN 32-BIT PROTECTED MODE, SO A BOOT GATE NEEDS A LONG-MODE SHIM (INF-5). QEMU's
`-kernel` loads only multiboot1 (an ELF32 `kernel;`), which enters in 32-bit protected mode; cyrius's 64-bit code
needs long mode first. `dce_kernel_compaction_boots.sh` carries a ~150-byte shim (page tables at 0x70000, a 64-bit
GDT, `call` / `pop` + `retf` to reach the next instruction wherever compaction put it) and leaves through
`isa-debug-exit` (QEMU exit 33). Without QEMU it is a named SKIP (77), a FAIL under `CYRIUS_REQUIRE_BOOT=1`.

3. A MODE DIFFERENTIAL FINDS WHAT THE DEFAULT CORPUS NEVER RUNS (INF-5). The `CYRIUS_DCE=1` differential over the
.tcyr corpus found a pre-existing defect the default runs never touch: a generic instance is emitted inline inside
the enclosing fn's code range, so a DEAD forwarder instance's `[start, end)` contains the LIVE instance it forwards
to, and DCE removes / NOP-fills it (`lang/generic_instance_inline_binding.tcyr`: 139 under x86 compaction, a hang
under aarch64 NOP-fill; filed for 6.7.12). Verifying DCE output against `CYRIUS_SYMS`: the map is written BEFORE
compaction (filed), so take addresses from the PLAIN build's map, as the kept-dead gate does.

4. A HELPER REACHED ONLY THROUGH A FN A FORK NEVER RUNS IS DEAD ON THAT FORK (INF-5). The six new `ir.cyr` helpers are
unreachable on the aarch64 (a64 / a64m / a64n) and cx forks, like `wp_compact` itself: `dead_code_floor.sh` went RED
until `tests/fixtures/dead_code_floor.txt` listed them (`--write`, then review that it added exactly those lines).
The x86 floor stays the number the CHANGELOG records (52 fns / 10,597 B at 6.7.11).
User-facing: language/tooling.cyml compiler_env_vars § DCE, audit_test_check_pipeline § THE PRE-TAG GATE;
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml dce_compaction_moves_a_kernels_data_v6711. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (INF-1 .. INF-5).

[[entries]]
name = "tool_scanners_census_before_refusal_and_return_conventions_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's tools lane (the shared attribute list, `<..>` in parameter scanners, the cyrlint overrun / write-length rules, the distlib owner rule, the mode-switch rung, self-build scrubs, target selectors, `cyrius header` return types) and at the wave-1 merge: a rule that refuses ambiguity needs a census of the live corpus beside it — which is how a fold's top-level global was found to be program-wide; cycc's return conventions as one table; a bare `Vec` field's size depends on declaration order; a cleanup keyed on empty keeps every killed run's scratch. (TL-1 / TL-2 / TL-3 / TL-8 / TL-9 / TL-10 went into their classes.)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "tools lane", "distlib", "owner rule", "census", "_bre_err", "redeclared global", "cyrius header", "cyr_val2", "cyr_valN", "return conventions", "x0:x2", "Vec field", "FINDSTRUCT", "_cbt_tmpfile", "check_stale_home_reaper"]
---
1. A RULE THAT REFUSES AMBIGUITY NEEDS A CENSUS OF THE LIVE CORPUS BESIDE IT (TL-4). distlib's every-target owner was
the partial rule minus its in-unit skip and its tie refusal, and the reason it stayed that way — "no lib/ tie exists" —
was never checked: a census with the CLI's own declaration reader found nine (`WS_*` in ws / ws_server), and the first
refusal landed on a fold (yantra 1.0.11's own distlib). Two answers to one question drift; when they merge, count what
the stricter answer refuses BEFORE shipping it, and pin the count (`distlib_owner_rule_shared.sh` row C: an allowlist
of filed ties that goes RED on a new tie AND on a stale row). The merged rule resolves every declarer of every
undefined name, and resolving reads dispatcher files whole — memoize per snapshot leaf (582 → 287 MB on an 8,454-name
verify), the v6.6.3 arena-OOM class.

2. A FOLD'S TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL IS PROGRAM-WIDE — AND THE CENSUS ABOVE IS WHAT SAW IT (the wave-1 merge). sankoch 2.8.4's
Brotli encoder declared its overflow flag `_bre_err` at top level; niyama declares the same name. cycc accepts a
repeated same-value top-level `var` (the guide's *Declaring a global twice*), so a program including both folds
shared ONE cell — each resets it per operation, so only two threads running a niyama compile and a Brotli encode at
once could read each other's value. A redeclared enum or constant is silent too (measured with an agnos program
redeclaring `CM_NOECHO` / `enum FdKind` / `SYS_FD_KIND`); only a duplicate fn warns ("last definition wins"). The
fold gates (fold_namespace_collisions among them, which reads the compiler's warnings over four folds) passed; row C
reported `_bre_err: niyama sankoch`. Fixed at the SOURCE: sankoch 2.8.7 renames it `_bre_ovf`, refolded
byte-identical. The compiler side — a global first declared `= 0` and redeclared with another value is silent, and
cross-fold collisions are invisible — is staged as a decision for the user
(issues/archived/2026-10-14-redeclared-global-from-zero-is-silent-and-cross-fold-collisions-invisible.md).

3. CYCC'S RETURN CONVENTIONS, AS ONE TABLE (TL-11). A word in rax / x0: 8 B or less, and `Str` (a handle). 9–16 B and
a `dyn`: rax:rdx / x0:x1. Over 16 B: a result pointer — integer argument 0 on x86 SysV and Win64 (rdi / rcx), x8 on
aarch64 — left in rax / x0, the callee writing WHOLE words (a 17 B struct writes 24 B, so a C caller's buffer must be
the rounded size — `cyr_valN` is). A two-value return: rax:rdx, but x0:x2 on aarch64 (x2 is the multi-value slot;
only a 9–16 B struct fn syncs x1). Three values: rax:rdx:r8 / x0:x2:x3. Win64 C returns anything over 8 B through a
hidden pointer, so cycc's rax:rdx pair matches no Win64 C type. The trap the first cut fell into: assuming "AAPCS64
x0:x1" for a two-value return by analogy with the struct pair — measured on pi, a C caller read x1's leftover
argument.

4. A BARE `Vec` FIELD'S SIZE DEPENDS ON DECLARATION ORDER (TL-11r). PARSE_STRUCT_DEF / PARSE_UNION_DEF take a bare
`Vec` field as the file's own `struct Vec` only when it was parsed already (`FINDSTRUCT`), else the 8-byte handle:
`struct Vec` above `struct Ray { o: Vec; d: Vec; }` gives 48, below it 16, both rc 0 — while any other name declared
below its use is refused since 6.6.10 (`_sfield_named`). A scanner that mirrors the layout must mirror the order too
(the header's sizer first answered 8 for every bare `Vec` and printed a 16 B `cyr_val2` for a 48 B result-pointer
return); the compiler's order dependence itself is filed.

5. A CLEANUP KEYED ON "EMPTY" KEEPS EVERY KILLED RUN'S SCRATCH FOR EVER (TL-5). Key it on CONTENTS, with the
vocabulary DERIVED from the source that creates the files (`check_stale_home_reaper.sh` axis 16 reads every
`_cbt_tmpfile` name out of cbt/), remove only regular files it names (never a link or a directory), and keep the whole
dir on any name it does not know. Measure before assuming the leftovers come from failure paths: they all already
unlinked; the scratch came from kills of the CLI itself mid-compile.
Into their classes: TL-1 tool_mirrors_of_the_lexical_state_machine_v673 (one list, included); TL-2
one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 (`<..>` in a TYPE); TL-3
a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer (a write length is what the lexer stores);
TL-8 an_environment_reader_that_stops_at_a_buffer_is_a_silently_missing_variable and the seven-forks entry ("is it set"
is not "is it on"); TL-9 / TL-10 there_are_seven_forks_and_two_symmetric_backends_a_change_lands_in_one_of_them.
User-facing: language/tooling.cyml compiler_env_vars, compiler_flags, cyrius_cli; field_notes/language/
platform_abi.cyml cyrius_header_return_types_and_agnos_hosts_v6711. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (TL-1 .. TL-11).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's struct lane (ST-1 .. ST-21: exact small dereferences, every destination from every source, `*p = v`, returns checked by their struct, tuple-is-not-a-struct, integer operators, untyped bindings of whole struct results, `*T` method chains, wrapped literal elements, the nested derive decode): a struct under 8 B is one word and every producer must read exactly it; a token typer must agree with the instance without making it; pass 1 and the replay ask one predicate; a peek-before-parse type must ask where the peeked thing ENDS; an inline-replayed body's `return` runs with the caller's return state; a destination-only rung must not touch sources; a store through `*p` reuses the assignment arms; a reported-already mark is a code-pointer stamp; the zone typer mirrors the parser's dispatch; destinations step their whole wrap before they arm; two arms judge a chain; a generated decoder's skip is the shared walker."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "struct lane", "_deref_small", "_hl_pval_ra", "EFIELD_LOAD_W", "_hl_pchain", "_gen_call_psid", "_tp_resolve", "_gci_src", "_gci_below", "_call_ends_at", "_ret_expr_sid", "_hl_dx_end", "GIDEP", "_agc_src", "_tn_ptr_step", "_ps_is_sz", "_agc_pp", "_ifm_cp", "_gz_unary", "_refuse_vector_tuple_return", "_scv_un_peel", "_spi_pcall", "_method_chain", "_pp_derive_codec", "_pp_derive_isen"]
---
1. A STRUCT UNDER 8 B TRAVELS IN ONE WORD, AND EVERY PRODUCER OF THAT WORD MUST READ EXACTLY THE STRUCT (ST-1). The
dereference read 8 bytes (`_deref_read`'s `ELOAD64` — only a narrow scalar `*T` had a width): fixed by `_deref_small`
→ `_hl_pval_ra`. The same word's other producers: a whole 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B FIELD (`EFIELD_LOAD_W` rounds an odd width
up — filed), and `_hl_pval_ra`'s byte-copy temp, whose padding was frame garbage — zeroed (`z` = 1) for the
dereference and the new return paths, still garbage for a by-value `self`, a `*S` into a by-value parameter and a
`*S3` parameter returned (filed). TRAP: a "byte-exact" copy into a temp is only byte-exact if the temp's OTHER bytes
are defined.
6.7.13 (ST-1, ST-3) — BOTH FILED HALVES CLOSED, AND THE FLAG WAS THE BUG: the `z` flag was a per-caller switch for an
invariant EVERY caller needs, and a per-caller flag for a universal invariant is a flag someone passes 0 — the by-value
`self`, the `*S` into a by-value parameter, `_sptr_param_temp` and the captured-handle copy all did, and carried frame
garbage past `sizeof(S)` (255 after a frame-dirtying call). Fix: zero unconditionally and drop the flag. aarch64
showed the captured-handle case where x86_64 happened to read 0 — run the padding rows on EVERY leg. The 3 / 5 / 6 /
7 B field now reads byte-exact through its address (a zero-size field reads no byte).

2. A TOKEN TYPER MUST AGREE WITH THE INSTANCE WITHOUT MAKING IT (ST-4). The chain typer (`_hl_pchain`) runs before the
call's parse and in pass 1, where `_gen_resolve_call` would EMIT an instance; it reads the base's `): *T` annotation
under the binding the call instantiates with (`_gen_call_psid`) — the same tokens and binding the instance's
`_ret_ptr_record` reads, so the two agree. Gotcha: `FINDSTRUCT('T')` for a bound type parameter is 0 — resolve
through `_tp_resolve` first.

3. PASS 1 AND THE REPLAY ASK ONE PREDICATE (ST-5). `_gci_src` decides both the global's layout (pass 1) and its
initialiser (the replay); it must type every hop the replay's copy can follow (a `*T` global, a `: *T` call, a `*T`
field), or the global is laid out as a slot and initialised as a struct. A source pass 1 cannot type (declared below)
is a REFUSAL (`_gci_below`), never the value store.
6.7.14 (ST-14) — THE SAME PREDICATE DECIDES A GLOBAL'S SHAPE IN PASS 1: binding `var H: Str = gt;` at top level (a
handle to gt's static header) needs pass 1 to register H as a handle (`_gci_inline` declines via `_istr_gsrc`) AND a
replay arm (`_istr_gz`), which stores gt's address with EVADDR as start-up code — no data-image relocation, so the
Mach-O and PE hardware legs matter. Ordering guard: a source registered AFTER H must be declined, or the replay reads
an unregistered global.

4. CLASS — A PEEK-BEFORE-PARSE TYPE MUST ASK WHERE THE PEEKED THING ENDS (ST-10, ST-16, ST-6, ST-2). The untyped
declaration typed `var r = mk3(4) - s;` by the leading `IDENT (` (`_var_rhs_call`); the 9-16 B pair-return check
accepted `return mk2(1) == p;` because the call STARTED the value (`_pair_ret_call_ok`); both now ask `_call_ends_at`,
the 6.7.6 helper the receives already used — three readers of `name(` had to learn it separately. The same family:
`_expr_struct_sid` knows only an argument's terminators (`,` / `)`), so a return's value needs its own reader ending
at `;` (`_ret_expr_sid`); a dereference source ends at its `;` or the first unopened `)` of a for step / wrap
(`_hl_dx_end`). The f64 leading-call peek has the same shape (`var c = f() < 1.0;` was typed f64 — fixed in 6.7.13,
RC-5: the leading `: f64` call types the binding only while the WHOLE initialiser is still an f64). Applying
`_call_ends_at` blindly also dropped a `: f64` leading call's f64 (`var x = f() * 2.0;`) — the fix keeps the struct
check only.

5. AN INLINE-REPLAYED BODY'S `return` RUNS WITH THE CALLER'S PER-FN RETURN STATE (ST-6, ST-7, ST-15). A generic's or
`#inline` fn's straight-line body is replayed at its call, so its `return` sees the caller's `_cur_fn_ret_sid`: every
new `return` arm must stay out of a replay (GIDEP), or a callee's `return GPT;` became the caller's struct copy (rc 139
measured), `#inline fn two(a, b) { return (a, b); }` was refused inside a `: P3` fn, and a replayed generic body inside
a `: S8` fn was falsely refused. The pre-existing form (the replay refused under the caller's class) is filed —
and fixed in 6.7.14 (GN-4, below).
6.7.13 (MV-3, MV-4, MV-11r) — THE SAME CLASS, THREE MORE TIMES, AND THE GENERAL RULE: during a replay (GIDEP > 0)
`_cur_fn_ix`, `_cur_fn_ret_sid` and the return-class checks all describe the CALLER, while every `return` / `?` jump
the body records in `rp_vec` is patched to the REPLAY's END (the jump is the call's value, not the caller's
epilogue). So ANY per-fn fact recorded from a statement inside a replay must test GIDEP. The three: the v6.5.67
pair-propagation flag in `_PARSE_RETURN_ARMS` (the caller became "pair-returning" — a false "bind both" at its
callers and a false mixed-return warning); the two `?` arms (`_stmt_qmark`, `_PARSE_TERM_IMPL`), the same false flag
(the triage read a replayed `?` as leaving through the caller's epilogue; the emitted jumps say otherwise — read the
patch loop, "Patch return jumps from inlined body to here", before trusting a plan); and `_ret_struct_pair`, which
judged a replayed `return ret2(..);` by the caller's 9-16 B class (it refused MV-11r's valid callee through 6.7.12
while the `ret2(..);` statement form compiled). The tail-call arm already tested GIDEP. And a "yield" added to stop a
duplicate error (`PEEKT != 97`) is a behaviour change wherever the yielded refusal was the ONLY one on some path —
check which programs it lets through before adding it.
6.7.14 (GN-4, FE-6) — THE STRUCT CLASS, AND THE SCOPE. (a) The replay re-parses the callee INSIDE the caller's fn
context, so every per-fn global its statements read — `_cur_fn_ret_sid` / `_cur_fn_ret_pair`, the SIMD return scalar,
the defer table — describes the CALLER unless the replay swaps or masks it: `return GPA;` in a `: *T` generic replayed
in a `fn r(): P2` was judged as P2's pair return. 6.5.59 masked the SIMD class, 6.7.13's MV-3 / MV-4 the pair marks,
6.7.14 the struct class (`_inl_replay_body`); `#inline` fns share the replay and met the same refusal. A new per-fn
global read by `return` needs the same mask. (b) A REPLAY IS A SCOPE WITH NO SCOPE_POP: PARSE_FNCALL's inline path
re-parses the body one scope deeper and restores the depth itself, so every per-scope table (here `_lc_tab`, the local
consts) outlived the replay, and the second replay in one scope met the first's rows ("already declared"). Trim at the
replay's end (`_inl_scope_end`: `_lc_pop` of the body's depth). The issue's premise "`_lc_pop` has no caller" was
stale — premise-check the claims in a filing, not only its pin. Beside it, an inline replay cleared the panic latch, so
PE printed one error fewer than the replaying targets — closed in 6.7.15 (VC-2: `_inl_replay_body` puts back a latch
its caller set; vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715 #2).

6. A DESTINATION-ONLY RUNG MUST NOT TOUCH THE SOURCE SIDE; A ZERO-SIZE T STEPS 8 (ST-8). The deref band
(`_agc_gvi <= -1000`) is the "a global whose slot holds the address" answer, so a typed `*S` global in it is a handle
to EVERY arm; `_agc_src` keeps the new rung off sources, so every source's code stays byte-identical. `_tn_ptr_step`
steps a T of no bytes by 8, so a "step == sizeof" test needs `_ps_is_sz`'s zero-size arm — the field destination's
`_fsc_psrc` lacked it (fixed 6.7.13, ST-2, with `_dlo_park` and `_deref_small`, which tested the step against
`STRUCTSZ` (0) or a 1..7 range and fell to the 8-byte word for a zero-size struct). Mutation lesson: `_fsc_psrc`'s fix
alone was an EQUIVALENT mutant once PARSE_FIELD_STORE returned at size 0 — two guards covering one shape need their
mutants run TOGETHER (MZ1 + MZ2).

7. THE STATEMENT'S ASSIGNMENT ARMS CAN SERVE A DESTINATION THAT HAS NO NAME (ST-9). `*p = v` parks the pointer and
runs `_PARSE_STMT_IMPL`'s arms with an override keyed to the operand's last name, answered as a value through its
address (`_agc_pp` 1, so a `*S` / handle source copies instead of rebinding); the override is cleared for statements
nested in the source (PARSE_STMT — a closure body's own `p = m`) and for sources (`_agc_src`), and the name lookups a
real destination needs (`_tup_dst_sid`, `_bx_asg`, `_mv_dst_sid`) answer 0 for it.

8. A "REPORTED ALREADY" MARK IS A CODE-POINTER STAMP (ST-11) — the `_FBR_MARK` / `_BX_MARK` pattern again: the warned
int + f64 mix stamps where it ended (`_ifm_cp`), an operand that ends exactly there IS that result, and the stamp must
be RENEWED when the result is only carried, or the second link of a chain re-reports.

9. THE DECLARATION ZONE'S TOKEN TYPER MIRRORS THE PARSER'S DISPATCH DECISIONS (ST-13): `_gz_unary` still judged `-g` a
dispatching struct after `_neg_int` stopped dispatching, and the zone refused its own replay (*the declaration zone
misread ...*). A change to what the parser dispatches is a TWO-site change — rows in the zone AND after it (U2 / U3).
6.7.13 (ST-6) — AGAIN, FROM THE OTHER SIDE: a new struct arm in the parse's typing (`_ie_struct_sid`'s dereference
arm, an if-expression branch `{ *p }`) without its twin in the zone's `_gz_bty` made the zone refuse VALID code ("the
declaration zone misread whether the initializer … is a struct value - please report it"). The zone types a global's
initializer from its TOKENS and the replay checks the parse against it: every parse-side typing change needs its zone
twin and a top-level row in the zone and after.
6.7.14 (FE-14) — THE ZONE REPLAY IS A SECOND PARSER. EMIT_GVAR_INITS re-parses declaration-zone initialisers, so every
shape the statement path accepts must route through the SHARED body (R5, v6.4.15, said it for `Str`): the named-field
struct literal went to the positional walk. And the baker (`_spc_walk`) must learn the same shapes, or a `kernel;`
build reads 0. (The replay's pointer annotation is one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 #7b.)

10. PARSE_RETURN'S MULTI-VALUE ARM RUNS BEFORE EVERY STRUCT-RETURN CLASS (ST-15): a shape check that belongs to a class
must sit in FRONT of that arm (`_refuse_vector_tuple_return` already did for vectors; the struct check is called from
it, so PARSE_RETURN — at cybs's ceiling — gains no reference).

11. CLASS — A DESTINATION'S TEMP EXISTS ONLY WHEN ITS ARMS SEE THE CALL'S OWN FIRST TOKEN, SO EVERY DESTINATION MUST
STEP ITS WHOLE WRAP BEFORE IT ARMS (ST-18, ST-20): `_scv_peel`, `_rcv_peel`, `_ret_peel`, now `_scv_un_peel` (the
untyped declaration) and `_spi_pcall` (the literal element, sharing `_pwrap_name` with `_rcv_peel`); anything keyed
on `_sc_want` moves with the peel (6.7.8 V4's dyn exemption: `_scv_un` / `_dyn_want`). The one left — a 9-16 B method
/ operator literal element (`_spi_expr` armed at the wrap's `(`, so `_sc_pre` made no rax:rdx temp) — closed in 6.7.13
(RC-3) by stepping `_spi_expr` inside the element's parentheses. TWO consequences to pin, not discover: the
`_refuse_lossy_pair` caret moved from 11:30 to 11:31 (stack_enum_lossy_context.sh PW9c RED — refusals keyed on the
element stay at its FIRST token, `_sc_whole` on the outer start), and arming `_fla_want` inside the wrap also took a
wrapped struct-typed FIELD `(h.q)`. And every TOKEN route that reads a shape before the parse must peel the whole wrap
the parser will: RC-1's known-callee reader (`_icall_fti`: `_pwrap_in`, then the wrap's closers) made `callptr((&mk3),
..)` the known callee — and at the vectors merge `_vrcv_fptr` (the out-pointer hint's "callee held in a variable"
test) had to read through `_icall_fti` too, or a wrapped `&f` read as a fn pointer in a variable.
6.7.14 (RC-7, RC-20) — PEEL IN, CLOSE AFTER, AND PIN THE CLOSE WITH THE STATEMENT FORM. Step the cursor INTO the k
parentheses, parse the call, close the k after it (`_dt_wrap_in` / `_pw_close`): the call compiles byte-identical to its
unwrapped spelling (the destructure and the tuple capture take `a, b = (f());` since 6.7.14). A classic-for step's
replay ends at its own `)`, so a missing close there is invisible — pin it with the STATEMENT form. The peel must stay
linear in the depth (RC-21): tuples_anonymous_structs_and_the_multi_value_bridge_v677 #12.

12. TWO ARMS JUDGE A CALL'S RESULT FOR A CHAIN, AND A RESULT KIND ONE LEARNS MUST BE TAUGHT TO THE OTHER (ST-19): the
free-call arm (`_call_ptr_field`) took a `*T` result in 6.6.17; the method arm (`_method_chain`) missed it for four
minors and gave a FALSE reason (*it does not return a struct*).

13. A GENERATED DECODER'S SKIP MUST BE THE SHARED DEPTH- AND STRING-AWARE WALKER (ST-21): every hand-rolled
`while (c != ',' && c != '}')` skip in the derive emitter was this bug (Vec<struct> 6.4.x, cstring 6.7.10, nested and
unknown keys 6.7.11; the scalar arms — fixed 6.7.13, ST-4, below). Two traps on the way: `PP_PARSE_STRUCT_DEF` registers a struct
for EVERY derive, so "registered" is not "has a `T_from_json_str`" — an accessors-only inner struct referenced an
undefined decoder until the codec flag (`_pp_derive_codec`, ST-21r); and a one-variant derived enum field builds
today, but `var _nq = E_from_json_str(..)` would be refused (*a `: stack` enum returns two values*), so enums are
excluded (`_pp_derive_isen`). `<T>_from_json_str` scans to the first `{`, so a non-object value must be guarded or a
LATER object is decoded.
6.7.13 (ST-4) — AN ARM THAT CONSUMES "ITS OWN TOKEN" MUST STILL SKIP THE REST OF THE VALUE: the scalar arms stopped at
an object's `{`, and the main loop's byte step then read the inner keys as the outer struct's. TRAP: an arm that walks
to a delimiter BY HAND (`PP_DBOOL_SCAN` to the first `,` / `}`) can stop INSIDE the value, where a later depth-aware
skip cannot repair it (`4 4 4 9 …` with the skip alone). FIX: every arm ends with `_cy_json_skipval`; no arm walks by
hand.
Into their classes: ST-6 / ST-7's tail calls — parse_return_tail_path_is_the_third_place_found_missing_what_parse_fncall_has;
ST-12 / ST-13's leaked struct type — cfo_const_fold_rewind_class…; the DCE byte scan that moved the dead-code floor
(ST-9, ST-14, the merge) — generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711 point 7.
User-facing: language/features.cyml struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711; field_notes/language/
semantics_runtime.cyml struct_copies_and_operator_results_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ST-1 .. ST-21).

[[entries]]
name = "generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.11's generics + frontend lane (GF-1 .. GF-20: instance names, bounds at f64, method inference, carrier fields, nested generic calls, the overload registry, Vec<T> elements, `slice` as a user struct, a top-level `return`, const-context checks, closure scoping, plain-integer step resets, cx's `main` auto-call): instance names are the dedup key; a bounded T method call is a trait use; the method arm is its own call path; the stub scan is lexical; a generic call's type is its instance's; the token-side pair scans mirror inference by path; the DCE call graph was a byte scan (an instruction walk since 6.7.14, IN-18); the overload registry fills at first mention; SKIP_GENERICS reads nothing; a built-in type word shadows a user struct by position; a jump recorded from outside every fn is never patched; the closure pre-scan is a second scoping model; a step of 1 means a pointer to every reader."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "generics lane", "frontend", "_append_conc_name", "_gen_is_inst", "_bnd_base_bad", "_mcall_gen", "_selfbv_bit", "_gs_gdef", "_gi_sc", "_tsp", "_tok_init_sid", "DCE byte scan", "overload registry", "SKIP_GENERICS", "_sl_ann_at", "_sl_word_builtin", "rp_vec", "_callee_site_checks", "_pl_next", "_arg_next", "_call_scale_fix"]
---
1. INSTANCE NAMES ARE THE DEDUP KEY (GF-1). A fragment spelling shared by two type arguments is a silent miscompile:
FINDFN finds the first instance and the second call runs its body. f64's fragment fell through every arm of
`_append_conc_name` to a bare `i`, which is also a `struct i`'s name. Spell every scalar (`i8`..`i64`, `f64`) and mark
a struct whose name is a scalar spelling (`.`), with a byte that is not `$` (`_gen_is_inst` and the coverage CLI cut
at the first `$`). The mark does not cover a nested instance inside a two-parameter generic: its own `$` makes the
split ambiguous (still open, High). 6.7.14 (GN-1, D1 (a)): CLOSED. `Base$a$b$c` has two splits when a fragment is
itself `X$Y` (a generic struct instance's registered name), and FINDFN dedups by name — so two different instances
shared one fn and the second call ran the first's body, silently. Mark the fragment that HOLDS the separator (`<..>`:
bytes no identifier holds, not the `$` the instance readers cut at) rather than re-spelling every name — the user's
"bracket only a type argument that is itself an instance"; size the name-pool guard from the fragments' real lengths,
not a fixed slack. Gate: `generic_instance_names_distinct.sh` (the coverage dump's names, exact).

2. A BOUNDED `T` METHOD CALL IS A TRAIT USE, NEVER A STRUCT USE (GF-2). Counting it a struct use unless i64 implemented
the trait made the i64 base the dead stub, and a stub has no scalar instance — so every f64 call routed to the base
and the refusal named i64. The base's validity is a separate judgment (`_bnd_base_bad`).

3. THE METHOD ARM IS ITS OWN CALL PATH (GF-3). Every inference the free call gained (struct, carrier, f64, the stub
refusal) has to be mirrored in `_mcall_gen`. Refusing a scalar binding of a stub makes every generic that forwards T
into the method a stub too, or its eagerly emitted i64 base meets the refusal at its own definition — and the stub
scan must type the receiver however the source spells it (a name, a field chain, a call's or a method call's result:
GF-3r). A type parameter on `self` binds the receiver; its instance's ABI mark (`_selfbv_bit`) is read before
PARSE_FN_DEF binds T, so it resolves the binding itself.

4. THE STUB SCAN IS LEXICAL, AND PASS 1 RUNS IT BEFORE LATER STRUCTS ARE REGISTERED (GF-4). A rule that needs a
struct's field types reads the struct's TOKENS (`_gs_gdef`: a search for `struct G<`), not the struct table. Its
carrier side table is fixed at 64 entries per generic and drops the 65th silently (filed; fixed 6.7.14, GN-3 — the
three tables grow, a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer, 6.7.14 (b)).

5. A GENERIC CALL'S TYPE IS ITS INSTANCE'S, NEVER ITS BASE'S (GF-5). Every inference site that reads a callee's return
must resolve the inner generic call first; a base parsed below the call has no `: T` return yet (0, not "not
generic"). The lookahead MINTS the instance, so the outer inference's globals (`_gi_sc`) and the cursor (a refusal's
column) are put back around it.

6. THE TOKEN-SIDE PAIR SCANS MUST MIRROR EVERY INFERENCE SHAPE, BY PATH (GF-5, GF-5r). A one-struct-id answer names
nothing two carriers down, so a `?` above `unbox(unbox(bb))` took the boxed lowering and dereferenced the tag (rc 139).
The scans now carry a chain of (G, slot) steps (`_tsp`, pushed and popped by the frame that makes them). An untyped
`var b = mk1(p)` must be read through its call, not through `_tok_init_sid` (the generic base).

7. THE DCE CALL GRAPH IS A BYTE SCAN, AND ITS FALSE-LIVE MOVES WITH DATA ADDRESSES (GF-20; the struct lane's ST-9 /
ST-14 and the wave-2 merge met it too). An E8 / E9 byte inside an imm32 / disp32 that lands on a fn start counts as a
call; two unrelated new globals moved the cx program's data onto `ir_emit1`'s start and flipped the dead-code floor
gate, and on the merged tree the addresses moved again and the cx fork read it dead, as it is (no caller). Safe for
codegen (it only over-keeps), but a floor gate can trip on a change with no relation to the fn: rerun it after every
merge, re-record with `--write` as a one-entry diff, and never read it as a real reference (filed:
issues/archived/2026-10-14-dead-code-floor-trips-on-dce-byte-scan-false-live.md). ⚠ HISTORICAL SINCE 6.7.14 (IN-18): the x86
call graph counts a call only where an instruction starts (a length-decoder walk), so data addresses no longer move
the dead set; the byte scan is kept only where the bytes may not be instructions —
ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714 #3 and any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…
(the 13th).

8. THE OVERLOAD REGISTRY FILLS AT A NAME'S FIRST MENTION, CALL SITES INCLUDED (GF-6). "A sibling exists" meant
"someone named it", so routing hung on text order and on unrelated callers; a route target must have a recorded
definition (a body, or pass 1's defaults row).

9. SKIP_GENERICS READS NOTHING (GF-7). A type position that steps its `<..>` with it treats the text as a comment,
whatever the field form next door checks. A cursor-neutral check (read the argument, put the cursor back) adds the
reading to a position without changing what either pass consumes.

10. A BUILT-IN TYPE WORD TESTED BEFORE THE STRUCT TABLE SHADOWS A USER'S DECLARATION IN THAT ONE POSITION (GF-8). Each
ladder that knows a built-in by spelling must ask the struct table first (`_sl_ann_at`, `_sl_word_builtin`), or one
name means two types by position. The consequence runs both ways: once the struct wins, the built-in's fields on that
spelling stop compiling.

11. A JUMP RECORDED INTO A FN'S PATCH LIST FROM OUTSIDE EVERY FN IS NEVER PATCHED (GF-9). Its displacement stays 0:
`e9 00000000` falls through on x86, and the same unpatched jump branches to ITSELF on aarch64 / cx — a silent skip on
one target and a hang on another. Every rp_vec writer must be fn-only: `break` / `continue` / `defer` / `?` (6.6.7),
`return` (6.7.11); `ret2` is still open (filed).

12. THE CONST EVALUATOR IS A SECOND INTERPRETER OF THE PARSER'S TOKENS (GF-12, GF-13, GF-11). Each check the parser
makes on a path (visibility, `#deprecated`, the parameter walk) must be made by the evaluator too, or the paths
diverge silently: the one-helper rule (`_callee_site_checks`) holds only if the evaluator CALLS it. A refusal in a
const context must also set `_panic`, or the context's own parser adds a cascade; and a refused enum value must move
the cursor past the expression, or the member loop reads the call as a new member. (The walker half is in
one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677.)

13. THE CLOSURE PRE-SCAN IS A SECOND SCOPING MODEL OF THE PARSER (GF-14). Each declared name must be shadowed exactly
where the parser binds it — measure the parser in a plain fn's nested block first: `var a = a + 1` and
`var a, b = f(b)` read the outer name, a for-in's start bound and collection read the outer name, and its end bound
reads the variable. Shadowing too late over-captures (a refusal); shadowing too early drops a capture, silently when
a global has the name. 6.7.13 (D35 A, RC-13, RC-14) fixed `var a = a + n` in a closure: a `var` and a destructure
bind after the declaration's tail (`_cl_prescan_var`, mode 4), `for … in` after its start bound or collection, `const`
at its name. A NESTED closure literal's parameters are shadowed for its body only (`_cl_prescan_lit`); a literal's `|`
is told from a bitwise or by the token BEFORE it (a POSITIVE list of tokens that cannot end an operand; `|` itself
only at the walk's first token) and by the parser's own parameter shape — never by seeding "every IDENT up to the
closing `|`", which scans a bitwise or for a far-away bar. A missed position must fall back to an EXTRA capture, never
drop one: reading a bitwise `|` as a literal dropped the captures between its bars (mutant m10: KP5 "undefined
variable 'q'"). ⚠ Still open (filed, High): every WRITE arm resolves a global (FINDVAR) before asking
`_cl_snap_find`, so with a same-named global a write to a captured name lands in the global. 6.7.14 (RC-1) CLOSED IT,
AND THE PLACE MATTERED: the read path asks a closure's captures before globals, but every write arm resolved local ->
global -> capture, so `_asg_capture_refused`'s `FINDVAR(...) >= 0 -> return 0` silently skipped the capture test
whenever a global had the name. The capture is now asked before ANY lookup, at the one lvalue gate every write path
passes before it parses the value or emits a load (`_asg_lvalue_at`). The triage's site, `_asg_compound_load`, could
not tell its caller to stop: a check placed where it cannot abort the statement only adds an error beside a store that
still happens. And the pre-scan must read the grammar the parser reads (RC-4): it accepted only `| IDENT, .. |` as a
closure literal, so `|m: i64|` was not one and `m` became a capture; a type in a parameter tail also needs `<` / `>` /
`>>` / `>>>` counted, or `Map<i64, i64>`'s comma splits the list (`_cl_ptail_end`).

14. A STEP OF 1 MEANS "A POINTER" TO EVERY `> 0` READER (GF-16 – GF-18). A value that scales nothing must carry step 0,
not 1: the narrow local's 1 (v6.3.34) made it a byte pointer to every difference and warning check, and on the LEFT
of a typed pointer it became the pointer, adding the other operand unscaled. Every producer of a non-pointer value
resets the step — `await` (a call the parser emits, not parses, so `_call_scale_fix` never ran) and the overflow
operators included. The multi-word closure capture is the one kind-less step-1 producer that is no pointer the
source wrote.
(GF-19, the cx fork missing the `main` auto-call, is in there_are_seven_forks_and_two_symmetric_backends_a_change_lands_in_one_of_them.)
User-facing: language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, slice_type_v58x, bare_return_in_fn,
const_and_const_fn_v672, closures_v637_v638, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, auto_call_main_v59x;
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (GF-1 .. GF-20).

[[entries]]
name = "vector_addresses_typed_copies_and_zero_as_none_v6712"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.12's fix-first lane (FX-1 .. FX-8: a vector value into `*V` is an address, the Win64 hidden pointer at every vector call site, D28's fn-pointer refusal, the untyped typed copy, the zero-counting redeclaration warning): a Win64 vector return is a hidden-pointer return at EVERY call site, not only a receive; a refusal hooked before the initialiser's parse must mark the token reported; choose a declaration's type from its source and every downstream arm is the annotated form's; 0 as 'none' in a value table hides a recorded 0; a refusal that does not latch the panic flag reports every argument."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "fix-first", "*V", "vector", "Win64", "hidden pointer", "_pe_vret", "_scv_icall_ann", "_tn_once", "_uci_ps", "PARSE_VAR", "_gv_lit", "_vgsi_base", "redeclaration", "_vec_kind_err", "_panic"]
---
1. ON WIN64 A VECTOR RETURN IS A HIDDEN-POINTER RETURN AT EVERY CALL SITE, NOT ONLY AT A RECEIVE (FX-2). The receives
passed the pointer, so every other site — a statement, an untyped declaration, an argument, a method, an operator fn —
passed none, and the callee wrote through its first argument (exit 5; `s.mv(..)` overwrote `s`). Fix: ONE predicate
(`_pe_vret`) fed to the struct-call hooks every path already has (`_fnc_agg`, `_sc_pre` / `_sc_post`, `_icall_sret`),
never a new path; storage a frame temporary, at top level a hidden global (no frame, and nothing newly refused). Two
traps: a by-value LEFT operand of an operator whose result pointer is pushed must be parked first (the retptr push
clobbers rax: `_op_park_top` at top level); and `_ret_agg_class` must NOT change — the callee rough-scan shares it.

2. A REFUSAL HOOKED BEFORE THE INITIALISER'S PARSE MUST MARK THE TOKEN REPORTED (D28, FX-3). `_scv_icall_ann` refuses
`var x: bool = fncall0(&mk3)` before the parse; without `_tn_once` on the initialiser's first token, the bool check
that runs after the parse reports a second line ("not a bool"). Mutation N11 (the mark dropped) -> 12 rows RED. A
pre-parse judgement and a post-parse check on one token need one "already reported" bit.

3. CHOOSE THE DECLARATION'S TYPE FROM THE SOURCE, AND EVERY DOWNSTREAM ARM IS THE ANNOTATED FORM'S (D29, FX-5). The
untyped `var q = p;` fix is one line in PARSE_VAR: `_uci_ps` answers the source's `0 - sid` as the pscale, and the
declaration then runs exactly as `var q: P = p;` — copy-init, `_mark_small_init`, the closure snapshot. No closure
change was needed: the snapshot already carries SLTYPE, so `_CL_CAP_FIELD_IDX` resolves `q.y` once q is typed. The
issue's "closure bug" was a typing gap one level up (its premise was wrong: `q.y` was refused OUTSIDE a closure too).
PARSE_VAR is a cybs-ceiling fn — the logic lives in the helper; seed-derive per bite.

4. 0 AS "NONE" IN A VALUE TABLE (D30, FX-6). `_vgsi_base` stored both a global's constant and its presence, so a
recorded 0 was invisible to every reader asking "was there a constant?" — `var a = 0; var a = 5;` compiled silently,
and sankoch's `_bre_err` merged with niyama's (6.7.11's sankoch 2.8.7). Presence needs its own bit (`_gv_lit`, a lazily
allocated byte per global, set beside the value, cleared by `_gv_clear`). The same zero-is-none test hid the 6.6.6
chain's third link and decided the enum-over-var wording ("value 0 or computed"). The fold-collision gate
(`fold_namespace_collisions.sh`) now covers every fold and same-value pairs (axis 4) — the census is the only thing
that sees a same-value repeat, which stays silent by design.

5. A REFUSAL THAT DOES NOT LATCH THE PANIC FLAG REPORTS EVERY ARGUMENT (D27, FX-1). `_vec_kind_err` sets `_had_error`
but not `_panic` (the parse is in step: the value push follows), so `rp(b) + ri(b)` reports both; a latching refusal
would have hidden the second, and the filed repro asks for both.

Into their classes (no new item): the step word's third field (bits 48-55, a `*V`'s vector) ->
typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678 #1; FX-4's `_pd_type_refused` restating the type reader ->
one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 #5; FX-8's wine server dir and the single-sample timing
flake -> methodology.cyml (a_gates_scratch_space_must_be_provably_its_own…, a_flaky_gate_on_a_shared_box…).
User-facing: language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676, enum_namespacing;
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11,
generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11, a_same_value_redeclaration_is_silent_by_design_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12]
(FX-1 .. FX-8, FX-1r, FX-3r).

[[entries]]
name = "native_region_rules_and_the_macho_fold_report_v6712"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.12's darwin lane (DW-1 .. DW-6: the native-region renumber warning, XLAT-3 on arm64 Mach-O, XLAT-1/2 on x86 Mach-O, the per-thread macOS sigprocmask, the macOS tarballs' scripts): two region rules disagree — the 6.6.12 x86-name warning INHERITS a native region through include, the 6.7.12 renumber warning is FILE-LOCAL; a rescan from byte 0 per call is quadratic; a routed number has no name in the table built for unrouted ones; the module var carrying the fold's fact must reset both before and after."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "darwin", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "native region", "PP_A64_NATIVE_AT", "_esx_region_here", "_SYSX_A64", "_SYSX_ACTUALLY", "XLAT-3", "_esx_lit_x16", "_msx_fold"]
---
1. TWO REGION RULES DISAGREE: INCLUDE-INHERITED VS FILE-LOCAL (DW-1). `PP_A64_NATIVE_AT` (lex_pp.cyr; the 6.6.12 x86-name
warning's exemption) INHERITS a native region through `include`. The 6.7.12 renumber warning is FILE-LOCAL
(`_esx_region_here`: the `#@a` depth minus the depth at the file's `#@file` entry, with a file stack of entry / resume /
re-anchor), like raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh's frame for lib/. Under the inherited rule every aarch64 stdlib build
would warn twice (lib/syscalls.cyr includes the peers inside its aarch64 region, and they write x86 numbers there on
purpose: `fd_wait_ready`'s 7, `sys_epoll_wait`'s 232); under the file-local rule a consumer's per-arch file included
under the guard is missed. Which rule is the language's is filed for the user (6.7.14,
2026-10-15-native-region-warning-misses-included-files-and-author-constants.md). RULED (D61, 2026-10-16): the region
carries through include for the renumber warning too — call sites use the INHERITED region, and the stdlib spells its
deliberate x86 numbers through `SYS_*` names. DECLARATIONS are judged FILE-LOCAL, on purpose: 6.7.14's PL-6,
pe_errno_stubs_region_declarations_and_the_ctl_signature_hash_v6714 #4 (supersedes this item). 6.7.15 (PL-9, D39):
file-local only for a file included under lib/ (by the include NAME as written); any other file's declared constants
take the carried region, and a fn-local const is judged too — the same #4.

2. A RESCAN FROM BYTE 0 PER CALL IS QUADRATIC ONCE EVERY CANDIDATE IS TESTED (DW-1). `PP_A64_NATIVE_AT` rescans the
stream from byte 0 on every call — fine for one warning, quadratic once every renumbered literal asks. The new warning
scans incrementally (sites arrive in source order, and a backward offset restarts the scan): self-compile 1,087 ->
1,090 ms.

3. A ROUTED NUMBER HAS NO NAME IN A TABLE BUILT FOR UNROUTED ONES (DW-1). `_SYSX_ACTUALLY` covers `_SYSX_MEANT` —
unrouted by construction — so it cannot name what a routed number becomes on aarch64. The names come from a new
GENERATED `_SYSX_A64(n)` in `src/common/syscall_xlat.cyr` (the aarch64 kernel names of every ELF-arm row source below
1000 and every row product, 106 rows from tests/data/syscalls/aarch64.tbl; only the aarch64 forks call it), and the
`1000+N` alias the warning suggests is derived by running the chain with 1000+N — "no 1000+N alias exists" when no
1NNN row does.

4. THE FOLD'S FACT MUST BE RESET BOTH BEFORE THE FOLD AND AFTER ITS ESYSCALL (DW-3). XLAT-3 hands the x16 a literal
site's kept words leave (`_esx_body_x16`, read by walking the chain, not from the last emitted word, so a `#naked`
chain cannot fool it) to ESYSCALL through `_esx_lit_x16`, which then emits the pipe halves only for 42 and the fork
half only for 2. Mutation (h), "never reset", leaks a literal's x16 into the next variable / `#naked` site (0 / 0
fixup words where 4 / 2 are needed). The pattern: field_notes/compiler/patterns.cyml
fold_once_report_the_fact_to_every_consumer_v6712.

Into their classes (no new item): the x86 code offset recorded at emit time ->
any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes… (the 10th); Darwin's 48 read from the brief, and the Mach-O size under
page rounding -> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence… ; the raw-issued alias invisible to name parity
-> a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64_is_not_the_number_you_wrote #9; the cbt/ include order at the merge ->
tools_lane_signals_pairs_paths_and_the_lock_v6712 #9. User-facing: language/features.cyml
aarch64_xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers_and_native_region_exemption_v6612, sys_sigprocmask_on_macos_v6712;
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence,
sys_sigprocmask_works_on_macos_per_thread_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] (DW-1 .. DW-6, DW-3r, DW-5r).

[[entries]]
name = "windows_locks_links_and_fixtures_that_carry_linux_v6712"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.12's windows lane (WN-1 .. WN-6: xflock through LockFileEx, the cass corpus triage, the [embed] link race, xsymlink's long paths, the trust store): Windows byte locks are mandatory, so a flock emulation locks a sentinel byte; close a check-then-open race by asking the OPENED handle where it is; an NT symlink stores its target verbatim; a fixture that passes on Linux carries Linux; a passwd id is one signed number; a new gate runs from any cwd and its labels are read as commands."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "windows", "pe", "LockFileEx", "xflock", "GetFinalPathNameByHandleW", "embed", "CreateSymbolicLinkW", "xsymlink", "MAX_PATH", "cass", "fixtures", "pwd", "grp", "darwin"]
---
1. WINDOWS BYTE LOCKS ARE MANDATORY, SO A FLOCK EMULATION LOCKS A SENTINEL BYTE (WN-1). `LockFileEx` locks are
MANDATORY — a locked range refuses other handles' reads and writes, where flock is advisory — so locking the file's
real bytes would make patra's own I/O through a second handle fail. Lock ONE byte far past any data (offset 2^63 − 1,
length 1): it excludes other lockers only. Measured on cass's NTFS: offsets 2^63 − 1, 2^63 − 2, 2^62, 2^48 − 1,
2^64 − 2, 2^63 and 100 all accepted. Relocking a range a handle already holds blocks or stacks, so a conversion drops
the handle's lock first (flock's conversion is not atomic either) — only for a mode CHANGE: a re-lock to the mode
already held must be a no-op, because unlocking first hands the lock to a waiter (6.7.13, LIB-1;
windows_flock_relock_and_lock_state_in_the_kernel_v6713). `ERROR_LOCK_VIOLATION` 33 and `ERROR_IO_PENDING`
997 are the `LOCK_NB` "held" answers; `ERROR_NOT_LOCKED` 158 is an unlock of nothing. (wine does not enforce the
lock: two_fields_of_a_foreign_api… 6.7.12.)

2. CLOSE A CHECK-THEN-OPEN RACE BY ASKING THE OPENED HANDLE WHERE IT IS (WN-3). A path checked prefix by prefix for
links, then opened, races a second writer who swaps a directory for a link in between. A dirfd walk (`openat` +
`O_NOFOLLOW` per component) closes it on POSIX; Windows has no dirfd `openat`, and `FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT`
(O_NOFOLLOW) covers the last component only. Fix: open, then ask the OPENED handle for its final path
(`GetFinalPathNameByHandleW`, `FILE_NAME_NORMALIZED | VOLUME_NAME_DOS`; reroute 0xF04E) and require it inside the root
handle's final path (`win_handle_under`). Traps: the containment test is a prefix that must END AT A SEPARATOR
(`C:\proj` must not hold `C:\project\x`; a volume root already ending in `\` counts); compare case-blind; a final path
that cannot be had is -1 and must never read as "inside" (fail closed — an unrouted -38 included). And on arm64 macOS
the walk needed `sys_openat` to keep its dirfd (a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64… #8).

3. AN NT SYMLINK STORES ITS TARGET VERBATIM (WN-4, WN-4r). CreateSymbolicLinkW stores the target as given and
resolves it later against the link. Measured on cass: `/` is not a separator in a stored target (a
`subdir/inner.txt` target never resolves — convert to `\` first; cass and wine agree); MAX_PATH applies to an
ABSOLUTE target as well as to the link (a short link to a 296-unit `C:\…` target fails), while a relative target of
any length is stored and resolves (275 units measured). Rule: the link path takes the usual long-path treatment
(absolute + `\\?\` at 248+ units); only an ABSOLUTE target may take `\\?\` — a relative one must stay relative,
because the link resolves it later.

4. A FIXTURE THAT PASSES ON LINUX CARRIES LINUX (WN-2, the cass triage). Seven shapes met in one triage, each fixed in
the test: an absolute `/tmp` scratch path (drive-relative on Windows — use a cwd-relative name); a socket reader fed
from a FILE (Windows `recv` cannot read a file — feed it through loopback TCP); a raw Linux syscall literal (getpid;
`MAP_ANONYMOUS` 34 — SIGSEGV on Darwin; use the named constant or the wrapper); a floor sized by the dev box's
environment (> 50 trust anchors; Windows' store holds the roots fetched so far, 40 on cass); a one-time table build
counted as per-call heap (20,232 B on the first call — warm it up before measuring); a host tool's dialect (macOS
`/usr/bin/openssl` is LibreSSL: `s_server -accept` takes a port, not host:port, and binds the wildcard); host data
(Darwin's gid 0 is `wheel`). Classify every failure from its own output ON THE HOST, never its name: of cass's 26
failing files one (result_stdlib) held 2 real PE defects (filed) and the rest were PE-inapplicable or fixture
defects; the Macs' 4 included 1 real stdlib defect (pwd / grp, item 5).

5. A PASSWD ID IS ONE SIGNED NUMBER (WN-2r). Darwin ships `nobody:*:-2:-2:` (and `nogroup:*:-1:`) AHEAD of root, so a
reader that stops at the first non-digit and never checks the field's end answers uid 0 with nobody. Validate the
whole field (optional `-`, digits to the field's end); a packed `(v << 32) | span` return cannot carry a negative —
return the value through an out-pointer.

6. A NEW GATE RUNS FROM ANY CWD, AND ITS LABELS ARE READ AS COMMANDS (WN-5). Two defects of a new gate, both found only
by check.sh: a cross compiler built from the CALLER's cwd (`src/main_aarch64.cyr`'s includes did not resolve from a
foreign cwd — build from `$ROOT`), and a grading label `wine` followed by an operand, which `gates_never_write_tree.sh`
axis 9 reads as a wine call (label it `PE`). Run a new gate through `check.sh gate:<name>` and from `/` before
committing.

Into their classes (no new item): wine passing four more Windows-only failures -> two_fields_of_a_foreign_api…
(6.7.12); the routed-numbers note's byte count -> a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five… (the 6.6.12
item); the new route group at cybs's ceiling -> instrumentation_belongs_at_the_body_start… #8; the ESYSXLAT shift row
dropping the dirfd -> a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64… #8; the disallowed CTL's MD5s, ProtectedRoots and the
root store's NotBefore bound -> tls_the_obvious_reading_of_a_protocol_field_was_wrong_seven_times #6, #14, #15; zero
assertions graded a failure -> a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code… (6.7.12); the full-corpus switch that started
nothing -> a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus (6.7.12). User-facing:
language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking, the TLS trust store, the identity surface; language/tooling.cyml the
`[embed]` entry and THE PRE-TAG GATE; field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] (WN-1 .. WN-6, WN-2r,
WN-4r).

[[entries]]
name = "a_const_two_peers_share_turns_a_paste_every_peer_harness_into_a_compile_error_v6712"
description = "6.7.12 (LIB-16): `const EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE` is the first const declared in two syscall peers; a const declared twice is an error where the peers' same-name vars only warned, so a gate that pasted every macOS peer into its x86 ELF build beside the Linux peer stopped compiling — and stayed GREEN when run alone, because its include fallback read the live store's pre-lane slot."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "const", "syscall peers", "EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE", "check.sh", "CYRIUS_HOME", "staged home", "gates"]
---
THE MECHANISM. `EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE` (6.7.12, LIB-5) is declared in syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr and syscalls_macos.cyr (12
B on both: x86_64 Linux's `struct epoll_event` is packed). A const declared twice is a compile error; the peers'
same-name VARS had only warned. `dce_pe_macho_layout_declines_compaction.sh` pasted every `*_macos.cyr` into its x86
ELF build beside the Linux peer, so its axis 3 stopped compiling.

THE TRAP, IN TWO PARTS. (1) Run alone, the gate stayed GREEN: its include fallback read the live store's PRE-lane
slot, and only check.sh's staged `CYRIUS_HOME` read the lane's `lib/` — so the failure appeared only inside check.sh.
(2) The gate reported `tail -1` of stderr, which is the unreachable-fns note printed last, not the error.

THE FIX. Never include two peers, which no target's dispatch does — the gate's ELF axis drops the macOS / Windows
peers, as 6.6.18 did for async_*. Report the FIRST `^error` line. Test a lib/ change under check.sh's staged home
(`check.sh gate:<name>`), not just alone. User-facing: field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
x86_64_epoll_event_is_packed_and_the_stride_is_the_layout_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-5, LIB-16.

[[entries]]
name = "tools_lane_signals_pairs_paths_and_the_lock_v6712"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.12's tools lane (the kill handler, `cyrius header`'s pair and vector returns, the lock's in-process hash and its one-pin trailer, symlinked and Windows working directories, the target selectors, the self-build environment rule) and at the merge."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "cbt", "signals", "rt_sigaction", "SA_RESTORER", "sa_tramp", "cyrius header", ": stack", "cyrius.lock", "sha256", "getcwd", "GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES", "current_cc", "dead_code_floor", "CYRIUS_POISON", "gates_never_write_tree"]
---
1. INSTALLING A SIGNAL HANDLER FROM A CYRIUS BINARY (TL-24, cbt/sigclean.cyr is the worked example). x86_64 Linux
`rt_sigaction` (#13) refuses delivery without `SA_RESTORER` (0x04000000) and a restorer address — the process dies of
SIGSEGV instead (the crossos row's "no SA_RESTORER" mutant: 12 RED); aarch64 (#134) returns through the vDSO. Darwin
has no libc `_sigtramp` in a cyrius binary, so the handler must also be `struct __sigaction`'s `sa_tramp` (24 bytes:
handler, tramp, mask u32, flags i32, through ESYSXLAT 13 / 134 -> 46) and must never return — exit from it. Give each
signal its own zero-argument handler: the kernel's argument registers differ (Linux handler(sig, info, uctx), Darwin
tramp(catcher, style, sig, info, uctx)). Query the old action first and keep an inherited SIG_IGN (nohup; a `&` job
starts with SIGINT ignored — a gate that sends SIGINT to a background job needs `env --default-signal=INT`). In the
handler: fixed tables and raw syscalls only; signal a tracked child only if a WNOHANG wait says it is unreaped (a
reaped pid may be reused); a forked child must take over (or clear) the tables it inherited before its handler may
touch them. Exiting 128+N makes the parent see WIFEXITED, not WIFSIGNALED — 6.7.12 only; since 6.7.13 the
handler re-raises (tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_passes_v6713 #5).

2. A `: stack` ENUM'S PAIR IS rax:rdx ON x86_64 BUT x0:x2 ON aarch64 — AND PAIR-NESS IGNORES THE ANNOTATION (TL-22).
The aarch64 emit's `EMOVRDXRAX` is `mov x2, x0`: a constructor (`Ok`, `Err`, `Some`, a payload variant) returns the
tag in x0 and the payload in x2 (measured on qemu, pi and ecb: x1 is 0), so it is NOT the AAPCS64 16-byte pair x0:x1 a
9-16 B struct return uses; the issue assumed x0:x1. The same x2 slot as 6.7.11's two-value return
(tool_scanners_census_before_refusal_and_return_conventions_v6711 #3 — add the stack-enum pair to that table). And a
fn's pair-ness propagates through its body whatever its annotation says: `fn ai(x): i64 { return Ok(x); }` compiles
and returns two values (`var z = ai(3);` is refused "bind both"); the compiler has a third route, `?` on a pair call,
which a source scanner reading only `return` shapes misses (filed). A scanner deciding "is this a pair" must follow
the compiler's propagation, with cycc's single-bind refusal as its oracle.

3. A SPAWN YOU REMOVE CAN BE THE CHILD ANOTHER CHECK WATCHES (TL-17 -> TL-29). Hashing the lock in process deleted the
only child `cyrius deps` spawned under `env -i`, and `scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh`'s macOS environment probe watched
exactly that child (a fake `sha256sum` first on PATH): both Mac legs read ENV_FAIL from that commit until the probe
asked the cycc child instead. It failed loudly; the same probe written as "if the fake ran, check it saw PATH" would
have read GREEN with nothing tested. When a bite removes a process, grep the gates and scripts for what watched it.

4. cyrius.lock HAS ONE PIN FOR EVERY ROW, AND A RESOLVE REWRITES IT ONLY WHEN IT OWES A WRITE (TL-11, TL-12). The
`cyrius` trailer names one pin for all rows, and `cmd_deps_lock` re-hashes all of lib/, so any writer at a moved pin
stamps the new pin over rows it did not re-vendor (D19 refuses it for a resolve; `deps --lock` and a scoped
`lib sync` did not refuse through 6.7.12 — since 6.7.13 they refuse too, D42). And the lock is rewritten only when the
resolve owes a write (a named dep resolved, a pin change, a newly vendored leaf, `--relock`): through 6.7.12 a file
deleted from lib/ kept its row through any number of plain `cyrius deps` runs (even after `rm -rf lib/`), and
`--verify` then failed "cannot hash" — since 6.7.13 a stale row is itself a trigger that re-writes the lock (TL-9).

5. getcwd IS PHYSICAL, `$PWD` IS LOGICAL — COMPARE PATHS BY IDENTITY (TL-15). Under a symlinked cwd a unit spelled
`$PWD/tests/x.tcyr` never byte-matches getcwd's answer; find the cwd inside a path as the shortest prefix whose stat
dev / ino equal `stat(".")`'s. The stat layouts differ per target: Linux x86_64 and aarch64 dev@0 / ino@8; x86_64
macOS's legacy stat dev@0 (u32) / ino@4 (u32); arm64 macOS's stat64 dev@0 (u32) / ino@8. And a git child's
`GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` must be the parent of the PHYSICAL directory (chdir, read it back), or git walks up from the
link target into an enclosing repository and reports its HEAD and dirt.

6. getcwd HAS NO PE ROUTE (TL-18). `syscall(79)` on CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN returns -38 (and the build warns "not routed"),
so every path a PE tool made absolute stayed relative. The route that exists: kernel32!GetCurrentDirectoryW through
GetModuleHandleA / GetProcAddress (0xF013 / 0xF014) and `callptr`, narrowed by `_fs_narrow` — no compiler reroute
needed. A Windows "absolute" path is drive-rooted (`C:\x`, `C:/x`) or UNC; `C:x` and a rooted `\x` are drive-relative
and stay outside a project (the plant class).

7. `current_cc()` PICKS THE COMPILER FROM `_arch` ALONE (TL-10, TL-30). Any arm that execs a compiler must check the one
it will exec, not the host fork's capability: `_target_cc_has_js` asked the host fork, then `_emit_js` exec'd
`current_cc()`, which `--aarch64` had made cycc_aarch64 (it read stdin as the source). On an aarch64 Linux host
`_arch` stays ARCH_X86, meaning "the host's own cycc" — flipping it to ARCH_AARCH64 would select cycc_aarch64, an
x86_64-hosted cross compiler no aarch64 host ships — so the progress label comes from `arch_name`, not `_arch`.
Since 6.7.13 (TL-2) an aarch64 Linux host sets `_cc_arm = _cc`, so ARCH_AARCH64 selects the host's own cycc there;
`_arch` itself still defaults to X86, which is why a `[deps.X] target` read x86_64 there (filed, 6.7.14; FIXED in
6.7.14, TL-2: the deps arch axis asks `_dep_arch_is_a64()`, never `_arch` — see
tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_ignores_v6714 #2).

8. A BODY COMPILED INTO A NEW FORK BRINGS ITS DEAD ARMS WITH IT (TL-28, TL-31). Dropping `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`
from the pin drift check put its `else` openat arm (dead where `SYS_OPEN == 2`) into cycc_win, which warned "syscall 2
with 4 argument(s) is not routed" on every build — guard the dead arm `#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, as READFILE's shims
are. And the dead-code floor lists per fork which fns are dead: a fn that becomes live in a fork turns
`dead_code_floor.sh` RED until `--write` regenerates it (review that the diff is exactly those rows).
`CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1`'s dead list is a ready oracle for "does this fork call it" (strict_pin S2).

9. TWO LANES GREEN ALONE, RED MERGED: A NEW cbt/ DEPENDENCY (TL-24; the darwin lane's integration find, c0b17300).
cbt/build.cyr now calls cbt/sigclean.cyr's `_cbt_scratch_track` and friends; cbt/cyrius.cyr includes sigclean.cyr
before build.cyr, but a `.tcyr` that includes cbt/build.cyr directly (the windows lane's
`tests/tcyr/crossos/embed_open_under_root.tcyr`) stopped compiling at the merge ("undefined function
'_cbt_scratch_track'") on every target, and the release gate's full-corpus legs read 610 / 611. A file that includes
cbt/ modules must mirror cbt/cyrius.cyr's include order; a full-corpus leg after every merge catches it.

10. THE CLI'S ENVIRONMENT RULE (TL-3, completing 6.7.11's TL-9). A SELF-build — a compiler or tool the CLI builds from
the checkout, kept (`pulsar`, the cx builds, `cyriusly setup`) or only verified (`self`, `soak`) — runs without
`CYRIUS_POISON` / `CYRIUS_BOUNDS` / `CYRIUS_COVERAGE` / `CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE`; a USER compile keeps them. A verb that
does both (soak: two self-host steps, then the user's tests and `.scyr` harnesses) scopes the scrub to the self-build
steps — at verb entry it would strip the user's own compiles, and on Windows (where the four are cleared around a
spawn) the hosts would disagree.

11. THE STATIC TREE-WRITE DETECTOR READS LITERALS (TL-24, TL-27). `gates_never_write_tree.sh` takes
`( cd "$S/up" && sed -i … cyrius.lock )` as an in-place edit of the TREE's cyrius.lock (a relative path that exists at
the root) and a `"/tmp/<name>"` literal as a fixed name concurrent runs share, whatever the shell context makes of
them. Write a scratch file's path absolute, and build a per-process /tmp name from pieces (`"/tmp/" + "ckh…"`).

Into their classes (no new item): TL-21's const-expression sizing and TL-8's `<..>` skip ->
one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 #5; TL-2's silent gate legs -> methodology.cyml
ci_drift_detection_silent_skip_is_false_green (a leg skipped with no line and no count read PASS). User-facing:
language/tooling.cyml cyrius_cli, compiler_env_vars, lib_sync_relock_v6617, lsp_and_plugins;
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml cyrius_header_return_types_and_agnos_hosts_v6711;
field_notes/language/shell_runtime.cyml. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] (TL-1 .. TL-31).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_one_dereference_typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.13's structs lane (ST-1 .. ST-20: exact bytes and zero-size structs, `*(&p)`, a struct dereference as an if branch and a tuple element, `&x.f` refused, `return &s;`, 6.7.11's D24 at top level, a struct value into a word pointer, `var q = G;`, the inline `Str` address, `gp().n = 5;`, the bare `Vec` field, `*pp` of a `**T`): route every dereference consumer through ONE operand typer and never teach the pointer typer; a top-level refusal reached from two paths reports once; which predicate answers 'is this an inline struct'; a closure's shadowing comes before FINDVAR; an implicit `&t` must do what an explicit one does; keep a decision to its wording; a statement-form store through a parked pointer; a special-name arm bypasses the common field rules; a new scratch slot fails the hidden-temp census."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "structs", "_hl_dpsid", "_hl_psid", "_hl_dstep", "_hl_damp", "_amp_vd", "_tn_once", "_scv_top_big", "_hl_amp_sid", "_hl_name_ty", "_uci_glob", "_cl_snap_find", "_fn_local_addr", "_lfi_base", "_istr_cpmark", "_cpf_try", "_fst_on", "_ptr_park", "_add_named_field", "hidden_temp_census", "_HTEMP"]
---
1. ROUTE EVERY DEREFERENCE CONSUMER THROUGH ONE OPERAND TYPER; NEVER TEACH THE POINTER TYPER ITSELF (ST-5; VC-11 the
same, for vectors). `_hl_psid` answers 0 for `&x` (6.7.11's D15: `&x + n` / `n + &x` is a BYTE pointer), so `*(&p)`
was a word load at every consumer. Teaching `_hl_psid` `&x` would also have changed `&x + n` and `return &s` (other
rules). FIX: `_hl_dpsid` — `& IDENT` of an inline struct answers its struct, else `_hl_psid` — asked by every
dereference consumer (`_hl_dstar_t`, `_gz_deref`, `_deref_small`, `_dlo_park`, `_ess_deref`, `_gsc_deref`,
`_ret_lopd`, `_dst_struct`), later by the return arms (ST-10) and the if-expression (ST-6). TRAPS: the arm brings
step 1 (`&x`'s), so every step test needed `_hl_dstep`; and the same typing turned `*(&h) += 10` into 6.7.11's
compound refusal — a shape outside the filed surface, held back (`_hl_damp`) and filed. The vectors lane met it
independently: typing `&b` itself as a `*V` (its plan) would re-scale `&b + n` to 16-byte steps; `_amp_vd` types it
only for `*&b`. Type `&x` ONLY where a dereference consumes it.

2. A TOP-LEVEL REFUSAL REACHED FROM TWO PATHS MUST REPORT ONCE (ST-11). 6.7.11's D24 at top level is reached from PARSE_VAR
(`_scv_arm`) and from the declaration zone (`_gvi_expr`), and sits beside a `: bool`'s own check: mark the
initialiser reported (`_tn_once`) or a `: bool` prints "not a bool" too; and a result over 16 B is left to its
existing no-frame refusal (`_scv_top_big`). One error per mistake.

3. "IS THIS NAME AN INLINE STRUCT?" IS `_hl_amp_sid` (WHAT `&` RESOLVES), NOT `_hl_name_ty` (ST-12). The name typer
answers for a handle and a `Str` too; asking it refused handles and `Str`s whose word IS the address (mutant: RED 2).

4. INSIDE A CLOSURE, A NAME AN ENCLOSING FN'S LOCAL HOLDS SHADOWS THE GLOBAL (ST-13). `_uci_glob` must ask the
closure snapshot (`_cl_snap_find`) before `FINDVAR`, or a captured handle named G is copied as the global G. (The
WRITE arms still ask FINDVAR first — the filed High bug in generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_
v6711 #13.)

5. AN IMPLICIT `&t` MUST DO EVERYTHING AN EXPLICIT `&t` DOES (ST-14). Passing an inline `Str`'s header address
without setting `_fn_local_addr` let `return f(t);` compile as a tail `jmp` out of t's frame (mutant n9: exits
8-248) — `_fn_local_addr` (the tail-call guard's "a local's address was taken") is set where `&t` is parsed, and
the implicit address (`_istr_ra`) set nothing. Any lowering that synthesises an address must set what the token
would.

6. KEEP A DECISION TO ITS WORDING; NEVER EDIT A PINNED ROW TO FIT A WIDENING (ST-14r). D7 (1) is worded for `var t:
Str = Str { .. }`; taking in the uninitialised `var t: Str;` broke 6.6.16's pun (0 where 5), and the lane had edited
the pinned row instead of stopping. The narrow fix reuses the per-slot byte table `_lfi_base` (value 2; the float
flag's 1 never meets a `Str` slot) and must mark a COPY of such a slot too (`_istr_cpmark`), or `var h: Str = t;
f(h)` reads h's stale length.

7. A STATEMENT-FORM STORE THROUGH A PARKED POINTER (ST-15). `gp().n = 5;` is parsed by the call-field reader, which
parks the returned pointer (`_ptr_park`); the statement forms arm `_cpf_try` (`_cpf_st`) so the field chain's `=` /
`OP=` runs PARSE_FIELD_STORE's body (`_fst_on`) on the parked pointer — the address computed ONCE for `OP=` (the call
runs once).

8. A SPECIAL-NAME ARM BYPASSES THE COMMON FIELD RULES UNLESS IT ROUTES THROUGH THE NAMED-FIELD HELPER (ST-16, ST-17).
The bare-`Vec` arm called `FINDSTRUCT` + `ADDFIELDTYPED` directly, skipping `_add_named_field`'s order and self
tests, so a `struct Vec` declared below the field was taken, and `struct Vec { x; v: Vec; }` made `STRUCTSZ` recurse
until cycc died (rc 139; found beside ST-16). Any "special name" arm in PARSE_STRUCT_DEF / PARSE_UNION_DEF is a bypass
of 6.6.10's order and self rules.

9. A NEW SCRATCH-SLOT SITE FAILS THE HIDDEN-TEMP CENSUS (ST-20; VC-10 the same). `_ret_addr_field` (ST-10r) parks
through `_HTEMP`, and `hidden_temp_census.sh` axis 3 counts every site ("total want 32 got 33"); the vectors lane's
`_vrcv_demit` did the same ("want 33 got 34"). Add the census row, with its why-line, in the SAME bite as the site —
the lane gate's full check.sh is where it surfaces otherwise. (34 `_HTEMP(S)` sites at 6.7.13.) 6.7.14, twice more, each
registered in its own bite: the structs lane's `_istr_rax_into` (parking a `Str` handle into an inline `Str`, ST-12)
and the vectors lane's `_dvi_park` (`var v = *p;` of a `*V`, VC-13 — the spec `_vrcv_dparse:1 _dvi_park:1`).

Into their classes (no new item): the byte-exact temp's padding and the 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B field (ST-1, ST-3), the
zero-size step (ST-2, the MZ1 + MZ2 mutants), the derive scanner's scalar arms (ST-4), the zone twin of a parse-side
typing (ST-6) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #1, #6, #13, #9; the step word's fourth
field, the pin (ST-19) -> typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678 #1; the PE leg's wine server dir
(ST-9) -> methodology.cyml a_gates_scratch_space_must_be_provably_its_own…. User-facing: language/features.cyml
struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710, struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711,
if_expression_v674, tuples_v677, struct_field_widths, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617,
struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616; language/core.cyml type_names_v6616; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13. CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (ST-1 .. ST-20, ST-10r, ST-14r).

[[entries]]
name = "multi_value_fall_off_lands_at_the_fn_end_and_the_replay_end_v6713"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.13's multi-value lane (MV-1 .. MV-19: ret2 judged as the literal, `while (true)`, the fall-off, one declared type, parentheses transparent to the pair checks, the async forward, ESTRUCT_PAIR_HI_SYNC removed): a fall-off rule has TWO landing sites, the fn end and the `#inline` replay's end; the rest of the lane's lessons went into their classes (the replay's caller context, two askers of one question, a per-level lookahead that must be linear, the predicates that disagree on a multi-value callee, the one-direction struct-pair crossing)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "multi-value", "ret2", "fall-off", "_mret_falloff", "_mret_falloff_fi", "_bx_falloff", "_bx_falloff_fi", "_defer_emit_init", "EDEFER_SAVE", "#inline", "GIDEP", "_pair_unwrap", "ESTRUCT_PAIR_HI_SYNC"]
---
1. A FALL-OFF RULE HAS TWO LANDING SITES (MV-17, D28 C). The fn end: PARSE_FN_DEF, after `_bx_falloff` and BEFORE
`_defer_emit_init` — every return jumps past it, and with defers the fall-through is routed round the trampoline;
EDEFER_SAVE / RESTORE keep the zeroed registers across a block's calls. AND the `#inline` replay's end: PARSE_FNCALL,
after `_bx_falloff_fi` with the CALLEE's fi, before its return jumps are patched. The pairs are `_bx_falloff` /
`_bx_falloff_fi` (6.7.3, bool) and `_mret_falloff` / `_mret_falloff_fi` (6.7.13, multi-value). A new fall-off rule
that lands only at the fn end misses every inlined callee: the replay row read 117 = 40 + 77 stale. Any per-fn
"what the end of the body means" rule is a two-site change.

Into their classes (no new item): the replay parses the callee in the CALLER's fn context while its jumps land at the
replay's end — any per-fn fact recorded inside a replay must test GIDEP (MV-3, MV-4, MV-11r) ->
struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #5; "does this call return a pair" has two askers, so a
spelling rule lands through one helper (MV-8, `_pair_unwrap`), and the multi-value predicates disagree on what a
multi-value callee is (MV-1, MV-6, MV-18) -> one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 #6a, #6b; a
lookahead asked at every nesting level must be linear in the depth (MV-19) ->
tuples_anonymous_structs_and_the_multi_value_bridge_v677 #12; the struct-pair / multi-value crossing runs one way now
(MV-12) -> the same entry's aarch64 crossing note. User-facing: language/features.cyml ret2_rethi, bare_return_in_fn,
struct_byval_int_pair_return_abi, struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, async_await_futures_v6311,
stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555, tuples_v677, const_and_const_fn_v672, default_and_named_arguments_v677;
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml multi_value_returns_since_6_7_13; field_notes/language/parser_syntax.cyml
parentheses_pair_calls_and_callptr_forms_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (MV-1 .. MV-19, MV-11r, MV-12r).

[[entries]]
name = "tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_passes_v6713"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.13's tools lane (TL-1 .. TL-19, TL-18r: the aarch64 Linux CLI's own cycc, macOS `--pie`, `cyrius header`'s vector parameters and `?` pairs, the kill handler's reach and its re-raise, the lock's stale rows and moved pins, the refused resolve that writes nothing, distlib's thin profile, the LSP's loose files): which compiler a selector runs is a per-host choice; a fn becomes pair-returning three ways; an async-signal handler can walk a vec; blocking signals around fork is half the fix; re-raising a caught signal takes SIG_DFL, raise, unblock; a dry pass is invisible only when three things hold; cycc honours `#@incdir` only at byte 0; a gate's census reads shell text, not meaning."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "cbt", "current_cc", "_cc_arm", "run_binary_timed", "cyrius header", "_pair_body_scan", "_hdr_paren_end", "signals", "_cbt_fork_tracked", "sigprocmask", "SIG_DFL", "_cbt_sig_reraise", "WIFSIGNALED", "exec_capture_status", "SetStdHandle", "_dep_stdlib_seen", "_dep_closure_lib", "#@incdir", "cyrius-lsp", "gates_never_write_tree"]
---
1. WHICH COMPILER A SELECTOR RUNS IS A PER-HOST CHOICE (TL-2). `cycc_aarch64` is the x86_64-hosted cross compiler;
on an aarch64 host the host's own cycc IS the aarch64 emitter. Every site that picks a compiler or a runner by
`_arch` has to ask the host first: `current_cc()`, compile()'s bare-metal arm, distlib's per-target cc, and
`run_binary_timed`'s qemu exec. The triage said "one line (`_cc_arm = _cc`) covers every site"; it missed the RUN
path — `run --aarch64` on pi then exec'd an absent qemu-aarch64 and gave rc 127 with no output. Grep every
`_arch == ARCH_AARCH64` arm, not just the compiler pickers. 6.7.14 (TL-1, TL-2): the per-host choice reaches two more
places — the CLI's REFUSALS (a target selector a host's compiler fork ignores builds the host format silently, so the
macOS CLIs refuse `--win` by name) and the deps arch axis (on the aarch64 Linux CLI `_arch` stays ARCH_X86 by design,
so the emitted arch is asked through a helper that knows the host): tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_
inherited_ignores_v6714 #1, #2.

2. A FN BECOMES PAIR-RETURNING THREE WAYS, AND THE COMPILER ITSELF FOLLOWS ONLY TWO IN ORDER (TL-5). The three: a
constructor return, a return of a pair fn's call, and a `?` on a pair call (its Err path returns the callee's pair).
A tool mirroring GFLG 256 needs all three. cycc's own `_pair_body_scan` follows `return f(..)` only, so a fn whose
ONLY pair route is a `?` is flagged when pass 2 parses it — a caller ABOVE it sees a word: `qb(x)?` faults and `var v
= qb(x);` binds the tag (filed, High, 6.7.14 — fixed there, RC-15: two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_
pass_one_the_authority, 6.7.14 (b)). A source scanner with a fixpoint was ahead of the compiler there until then.
Scanner traps met on the way: an expression-bodied closure (`|a| f(a)?`, `|| …`) must be stepped over as a block
closure is, or its `?` is charged to the enclosing fn; and inside call arguments `<` / `>` are operators, so a
parameter-list matcher (`_hdr_close`) cannot find a call's `)` — it needs a paren matcher of its own
(`_hdr_paren_end`).

3. AN ASYNC-SIGNAL HANDLER CAN WALK A VEC — BUT ONLY WHAT WAS RECORDED BEFORE IT EXISTED (TL-7). Plain loads are safe
(data at +0, len at +8; the arena never frees an old buffer). A signal inside `vec_push` can expose a 0 slot, because
len is bumped before the store — skip it. An owner pid keeps a forked child off its parent's tree. And RECORD, THEN
CREATE: `_distlib_copy_whole` pushed `dst` after `file_write_all` made it, so a kill between the two left a file
nothing knew; the round's `entry.cyr`, written into the tree's root but in neither list, was the one file left after
the first fix. 6.7.14 (TL-7): RECORD, THEN CREATE reaches the resolver's scratch, where the file is a sibling temp
another module names — record its predicted name BEFORE it exists, and only the temp the CLI created:
tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_ignores_v6714 #5.

4. BLOCKING SIGNALS AROUND FORK IS HALF THE FIX (TL-8). The child inherits the blocked mask and execve keeps it, so the
child must restore the old mask FIRST, or every exec'd tool runs with HUP / INT / TERM blocked (mutation M2 shows
it); the parent restores only AFTER the track (restoring before it reopens the window). A window a few instructions
wide is testable: put a test-only hook between fork and track (a fn pointer, 0 in production) that signals its own
process. And a new tracked-fork helper must be taught to every fork-site detector — `cbt_fork_sites_have_pe_arm.sh`,
`cli_fork_failure_named.sh` axis 4, `cli_temp_dir_no_leak.sh` axis 5: without that, the first fell below its floor
and the third read 7 child-side exits as PARENT.

5. RE-RAISING A CAUGHT SIGNAL AFTER CLEANUP TAKES THREE STEPS: SIG_DFL, RAISE, THEN UNBLOCK (TL-18, D39). The handler
runs with the signal — and its sa_mask — blocked, so a raise without the unblock stays PENDING and the process falls
through to its `exit(128+N)` (measured: 143 WIFEXITED). Darwin behaves the same, with the handler installed as its
own trampoline (no sigreturn): the kill is process-directed, and the per-thread `__pthread_sigmask` unblock delivers
it. A shell's `$?` reads 128+N for BOTH endings; only a parent reading the wait status (`exec_capture_status`'s st[1],
perl's WIFSIGNALED) tells them apart — which is why the gate row has a cyrius parent: every `$?` row stays green on
the 6.7.12 handler. Supersedes the 6.7.12 entry's #1 last sentence.

6. A DRY PASS IS INVISIBLE ONLY WHEN THREE THINGS HOLD (TL-12, D41). (1) Its output is muted at the FD level and
nothing it prints is needed — take its reachable exits before muting; on Windows the mute is `SetStdHandle(-11 /
-12, NUL)`, since `sys_write` calls `GetStdHandle` on every write and dup2 is a no-op there. (2) Every per-pass
accumulator is reset: a carried `_dep_stdlib_seen` made the real resolve skip every leaf and lock a false row. (3)
Side effects the real pass reports are carried over: a clone the dry pass fetched is still `(fetched)`. And a
scratch-root resolve's closure holds SCRATCH paths — map it to lib/ paths before comparing it with lib/
(`_dep_closure_lib`): the triage's plan used `_dep_closure` directly, and every lib/ file read as out of scope
(TL-10).

7. cycc HONOURS `#@incdir` ONLY AT BYTE 0, RELATIVE AND `..`-FREE (TL-14). A tool feeding cycc must name the entry
relative to cycc's own working directory, or no marker can apply. Let a FORKED child write the pipe, so a cycc that
stops reading kills the writer, not the tool; fall back to the plain file if the pipe or fork fails.

8. A GATE'S CENSUS READS SHELL TEXT, NOT MEANING (TL-6, TL-12, TL-18r). Four gates misread the lane's own test code:
`check_sh_targeted_path_cleans_up.sh` axis 3 read `{ …; exec sleep 60; }` on one line, and a string `"… && exec …"`,
as a top-level exec past a trap; `gates_never_write_tree.sh` axis 6 read a `"/tmp/<name>"` literal as a fixed name
runs share, even with the pid appended later; `check_stale_home_reaper.sh` derives its reaper vocabulary from cbt/'s
`_cbt_tmpfile` names, so a new DIRECTORY scratch (`pin-move-deps`) has to join its exclusions; and
`manifest_read_whole_file.sh` axis 4 had to learn `_cbt_scratch_drop` as an unlink. The fixes: write a test's program
through a heredoc, spell a per-process /tmp name as a concatenation, and run the WHOLE check.sh after a bite that
adds a scratch name or a fork. Also: a gate that assumes the default SIGHUP disposition goes RED under `nohup` with no
defect behind it (filed, 6.7.14 — fixed there, TL-5: tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_
ignores_v6714 #6). 6.7.14, THREE MORE: `gates_never_write_tree.sh` axis 6 read the new test's `"/tmp/csk."` literal
as a fixed name (spelled `"/tmp/"` + `"csk."` now — TL-7); `check_sh_targeted_path_cleans_up.sh`'s exec census flags
an `exec` at the START of a continuation line in a gate (don't write `exec` there — the stdlib lane); and
`darwin_syscall_literals_routed.sh` reads `lib/` FILE BY FILE, with no `#ifdef` frame, so `lib/alloc_cx.cyr` —
included only under `lib/alloc.cyr`'s `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_CX` — read its new `syscall(12, …)` as an unrouted brk on
both Macs (LIB-7). A target-only file reached through another file's `#ifdef` is exempted BY NAME, with the reason, and
a ledger row proves the exemption is load-bearing.

Into their classes (no new item): the 6.7.12 entry tools_lane_signals_pairs_paths_and_the_lock_v6712 #1 (the
re-raise), #4 (D42; the stale-row trigger, TL-9), #7 (`_cc_arm = _cc` on an aarch64 Linux host). User-facing:
language/tooling.cyml cyrius_cli, compiler_env_vars, compiler_flags, lib_sync_relock_v6617, lsp_and_plugins,
cyrius_cyml_git_first_local_switch_v676; ecosystem.cyml (`ok_via` / `err_via`); field_notes/language/
platform_abi.cyml cyrius_header_return_types_and_agnos_hosts_v6711, pie_on_macos_is_arm64_only_v6713;
field_notes/language/shell_runtime.cyml pin_bump_order_with_a_full_vendored_lib_v6712,
a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (TL-1 .. TL-19, TL-18r).

[[entries]]
name = "receives_post_parse_records_and_the_capture_prescan_v6713"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.13's receives + closures lane (RC-1 .. RC-15: 6.7.11's D24 at every path, a by-value struct into a pointer annotation, parenthesised `&f` callees, integer-result operators, the known-callee chain and dyn source, the typed-pointer warning everywhere, narrow captures, a declaration's own name in a closure): a post-parse refusal must run before the type check that would misname it; the declaration zone is its own path and its `*T` global has pscale 0; a type clear at a shared tail clears every operator that reaches it; two hooks that can refuse one line must pin which one reports; a refusal that calls a callee unknown is silent under `--syntax-only`; a capture copies a slot, and a narrow local's slot is its low bytes; a defensive arm no mutant can reach is dead."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "receives", "closures", "_scv_rec_ann", "_sc_whole", "_bx_decl", "_tn_once", "_tn_seen_at", "_scv_refd", "_gvi_scalar", "_bx_gvi_expr", "sit_lit", "_int_rest", "_scv_icall_ann", "--syntax-only", "_cl_emit_cap_word", "EFLLOAD_W", "_var_rhs_wsid"]
---
1. A POST-PARSE REFUSAL MUST RUN BEFORE THE TYPE CHECK THAT WOULD MISNAME IT (D13, RC-6). `_scv_rec_ann` reads the
struct-result record (`_sc_whole`) after the initialiser's parse; placed in `_sc_var_receive` / `_sc_global_receive`
(the brief), it ran AFTER `_bx_decl`, so a `: bool` was refused first as "not a bool". One PARSE_VAR call right after
`_scv_disarm` and before `_bx_decl` serves the fn and the after-statement top level; the first token is marked
(`_tn_once`) so the bool check stays quiet, and a NON-marking twin (`_tn_seen_at`) asks "already reported?" without
claiming the token. A refused declaration must also take no struct (`_scv_refd`), or `var x: cstring = b.mk();`
receives the struct behind its annotation.

2. THE DECLARATION ZONE IS ITS OWN PATH, AND ITS `*T` GLOBAL HAS PSCALE 0 (D13, D18; RC-6, RC-11). `_sc_global_pair`
is never reached for `gps == 0`, so the zone needed its own arm (`_gvi_scalar`) — and that arm must arm NOTHING:
arming `_sc_want` there lets a dyn result past V4 (`_dyn_want`); it clears `_sc_beg` so only this parse's record is
read. A `*T` global's replay gps is 0 (`_gvi_ann` answers 0 for a pointer annotation), so a check keyed on `gps > 0`
never sees it — read the slot's GVTYPE where the entry is known (`_bx_gvi_expr`). And a BAKED initialiser (`sit_lit`,
a folded constant) is never replayed at all and needs its own check at its `;` (`_tp_warn_baked`). 6.7.14 (RC-12,
6.7.13's D14 at top level): AGAIN — `_gvi_ann` gives a pointer annotation `gps` 0, so the zone judged `**P2` / `*P2`
as SCALARS and gave D24's remedy where D14's (a struct over 8 B into a `*T` global is refused) was right. Every zone
check that branches on a pointer reads the annotation's first TOKEN (`_gvi_ptr_ann`).

3. A TYPE CLEAR AT A SHARED TAIL CLEARS EVERY OPERATOR THAT REACHES THE TAIL (D12, RC-4). PARSE_TERM's `if (go == 1)`
tail also runs after the `* /` f64 arms: clearing SESTYPE there turned `x * 2.0 + 1.0` into an integer add (0 where 4
is right). The clear is per operator (`_int_rest(S, typ)`: `% << >> >>>` only).

4. TWO HOOKS THAT CAN REFUSE ONE LINE: PIN WHICH ONE REPORTS (D14, RC-7). A known `&f` call leaves a struct-result
record, so `_scv_icall_ann`'s pointer arm overlapped `_scv_rec_ann`; mutant N27 (the arm dropped) showed only the
CARET moving (`fncall0` instead of `mkp`). The column is the row (PT10 at the callee), as D28 reports.

5. A REFUSAL THAT CALLS A CALLEE "UNKNOWN" MUST BE SILENT UNDER `--syntax-only` (D16, RC-9, 389e715d). Lint's
pre-pass runs `--syntax-only --allow-undef`, where a callee from a sibling file is unknown too: the new "its callee
is a value, not `&f`" refusal fired on `fncall1(&mk3sib, 5).b` where `mk3sib(5).b`, `_method_chain` and
`_call_field` stay silent. Gate on `_panic + _syntax_only == 0`, as they do. And at top level over 16 B `_sc_pre`
has already refused the call, so the chain must not report again (the method arm still does — filed).

6. A CAPTURE COPIES A SLOT, AND A NARROW LOCAL'S SLOT IS ONLY ITS LOW 1 / 2 / 4 BYTES (RC-12). The env copy
(`_cl_emit_cap_word`) took `EFLLOAD`, the whole 8 bytes, so a captured `i8` -3 read 253 and the slot's stale upper
bytes came along (-249 on aarch64 / cx). Load at the local's width and signedness (`EFLLOAD_W`, signed when
`GSLICE_W < 0`), exactly as the local-read arm does; then `_cl_cap_load`'s `ELOAD64` and a nested re-copy are right.
A narrow PARAMETER was already right, which is why no corpus file had caught it.

7. A DEFENSIVE ARM A MUTANT CANNOT REACH IS DEAD (RC-2). `_var_rhs_wsid`'s explicit `_dyn_is` test was dropped after
a mutant showed it unreachable: a dyn is 16 B, so the "8 B or less" size test already excludes it; the refusal is
pinned by row D1 instead.

Into their classes (no new item): RC-13 / RC-14 (a declaration's own name and a nested literal's parameters in the
closure pre-scan) -> generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711 #13; RC-1's wrap peel and RC-3's
moved caret -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #11; RC-5's f64 leading call -> the same
entry's #4. User-facing: language/features.cyml struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612, dyn_v678,
closures_v637_v638, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11, struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13. CHANGELOG [6.7.13]
(RC-1 .. RC-15).

[[entries]]
name = "vector_values_receives_arguments_and_dereferences_v6713"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.13's vectors lane (VC-1 .. VC-12, VC-5r, VC-6r, VC-6r2: the f32v8 parameter, explicit instances, methods and `*p` into a vector local, a vector call into a value-form parameter, a vector into another pointer kind or a struct, captured vectors, a struct into `*V`, `*&b` / `**ppb`, the refusal text): a name the parameter classifier does not know is silently an integer; a call-shape recogniser that tests `IDENT (` misses `name<..>(`; a lookahead that resets the declared type turns a refusal into a silent word; a receive that cannot emit the call takes its Win64 destination through a one-shot hook; Win64's own-call receives skip overload routing; refuse before consuming, then skip; a multi-word capture's value is its address; a refusal keyed on the parameter's recorded struct is blind where none is recorded; recognise by tokens, type after the parse; a diagnostic decided after the parse cannot ask FINDFN."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "vectors", "SIMD", "f32v8", "_classify_param_type", "_pv32_param", "_gcall_open", "_gen_resolve_call", "_vcall_fi", "pscale", "_sc_vbeg", "_sc_vdst", "_icall_vbeg", "Win64", "_vrcv_pe_call", "_arg_next", "_sarg_pe_vword", "_cl_cap_vec", "_sarg_type_err", "_pvec_param", "_vrcv_demit", "GFDS", "FINDFN", "mulh64"]
---
1. A NAME THE PARAMETER CLASSIFIER DOES NOT KNOW IS SILENTLY AN INTEGER PARAMETER (VC-2). `_classify_param_type` had no
class for `f32v8`, so `v: f32v8` was class 0 — one integer word; the caller pushed lane 0 and every read past it was
0, no diagnostic, on every target. Every vector type needs its own class. And the per-fn SIMD mask is 2 bits per
parameter: two vectors of one size class (f64v4 / f32v8) share a code, so an EXACT-type check must read the
definition's tokens (`_pv32_param`), never the mask.

2. A CALL-SHAPE RECOGNISER THAT TESTS `IDENT (` MISSES `name<..>(` (VC-3). Read the head with `_gcall_open`, the
callee with `_gen_resolve_call`, and end the call at the `(` it returned (`_vcall_fi` / `_vcall_o`). The struct
receives learned this in 6.6.10, the vector receives in 6.7.13. Resolve the instance only once the base is declared
to return a vector, or every generic call grows an instantiation.

3. A LOOKAHEAD THAT RESETS THE DECLARED TYPE TURNS A REFUSAL INTO A SILENT ONE-WORD LOCAL (VC-3). PARSE_VAR's struct
lookahead set `pscale = 0` to re-derive the type (`_gen_decl_check` answered 0 for a vector), so an unhandled `var v:
f64v2 = mkg<i64>(..)` became an untyped word that read 0 / 154 / 195 instead of being refused. A reset must never drop
a vector annotation (`pscale <= -2048`).

4. A RECEIVE THAT CANNOT EMIT THE CALL ITSELF TAKES ITS WIN64 DESTINATION THROUGH A ONE-SHOT HOOK (VC-4). A method's
call is the method arm's, and the receiver's `self` push sits between the hidden result pointer and the arguments; the
receive arms `_sc_vbeg` (keyed on the call's first token) / `_sc_vdst`, and `_sc_pre` consumes it before `self` — as
`_icall_vbeg` does for `callptr(&f)`. An unannotated declaration's vector inference must require the WHOLE
initialiser to be the call: `var v = callptr(&mkv, 1, 2) + 3` stays the scalar path.

5. WIN64'S OWN-CALL VECTOR RECEIVES SKIP OVERLOAD ROUTING (VC-6). `_f2c` / `_f4c`, `_try_vector_call_assign`,
`_vrcv_pe_call` and PARSE_RETURN's PE arm emit their call without PARSE_FNCALL's `_ptr` routing, so `var r: f64v2 =
f64v2_add(&a, &b)` reaches the VALUE form on PE with addresses — right only because PE's value-form parameter is by
address. A PE check refusing non-vector pushes into a value-form parameter must exempt `&x` (refusing it broke 6
corpus files on PE, measured). ⚠ SUPERSEDED in 6.7.14 (VC-6): exempting `&x` was the workaround; ROUTING is the fix —
the own-call receives now route `f(&a, ..)` to `f_ptr` (`_pe_venter` / `_pe_vroute`), and `lanes(&w)` into a
value-form parameter is refused on Windows too: vector_values_win64_parity_inline_frames_and_post_parse_types_v6714 #2.

6. REFUSE BEFORE CONSUMING, THEN SKIP TO THE ARGUMENT'S END — ON EVERY PATH (VC-6, VC-6r). The v5.10.38 value-form path
refused a non-local AFTER consuming its first token: the "expected ';'" cascade. The fix refuses, then skips to the
`,` / `)` (`_arg_next`). The PE twin (`_sarg_pe_vword`) first refused and then let the value parse run, which printed
its own second error (`lanes(G)`, `lanes(nosuch)`); it now answers 1 and its caller skips too. A refusal and the parse
that follows it are one decision.

7. A MULTI-WORD CAPTURE'S VALUE IS ITS ADDRESS (VC-8). A capture's value push is `&env[woff]`, so an aggregate capture
no arm recognises "works" into ANY pointer parameter, silently (42 on every target). `_CL_CAP_FIELD_IDX` answers for
struct captures only; every argument rule needs its own capture arm (`_cl_cap_vec`). 6.7.14 (FE-26) — THE SAME RULE
FOR A VALUE-TYPED MULTI-WORD LOCAL: a closure's READ of a captured `u128` took the aggregate rule and got the env copy's
ADDRESS. The snapshot now carries the slot's marks (GLW128, bit 60 of the depth word), so the read copies the two words
into a temporary and stamps it; a captured u128 is its value since 6.7.14.

8. A REFUSAL KEYED ON THE PARAMETER'S RECORDED STRUCT IS BLIND TO A PARAMETER THAT RECORDS NONE (VC-9). A `*V`
parameter records no GFPSID, so `_sarg_type_err` returned at `ps <= 0` and a struct passed to it was never compared;
ask the definition's tokens (`_pvec_param`) there.

9. RECOGNISE THE SHAPE BY TOKENS, READ THE TYPE AFTER THE PARSE (VC-10). The `*p` receive needs the operand's vector,
which no token-level typer knows: recognise one whole `* E` by tokens, parse E, read the vector from its step word,
and refuse after the parse with the binary withheld (`_vrcv_demit`). 6.7.14 (VC-10) — THE ARGUMENT-POSITION INSTANCE:
a rule that judges a dereference BY ITS TOKENS misses every other spelling — `_svo_vderef` took `* NAME` of a local
only, so `**ppb`, `*gp(pb)`, `*h.pv`, `*GPV`, `*(pb + 0)` and `*&b` pushed lane 0 as the address. Watch the argument's
`*` across the parse (`_sarg_vdo`) and let the dereference note its operand's vector from the step word once parsed
(`_dro_vnote`); nothing is emitted, so the struct path stays byte-identical. NEW TRAP: SAVE AND RESTORE the watch
around the parse, or a nested call's own argument steals or lends the note (`ri(*h2(**ppb))`) — the first measurement
of that mutant was GREEN with a bare count; count each refusal by its WORDS.

10. A DIAGNOSTIC DECIDED AFTER THE PARSE CANNOT ASK FINDFN WHETHER A NAME EXISTS (VC-12). PARSE_FNCALL `REGFN`s an
unknown name, so FINDFN finds it afterwards (and in every later replay of a generic body); a missing fn is the entry
with no definition stamp (`GFDS` 0 — the prescan stamps every defined fn) and no code. And `sizeof` / `mulh64` are
`NAME (` intrinsics FINDFN never finds: a "names no fn" test must exclude them, or `var v: f64v2 = mulh64(a, b);`
leaves the refusal and stores one word.

Into their classes (no new item): a refusal keyed on "a named local of type V" (VC-7) ->
one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_walker_v677 #6c; typing `&x` only where a dereference consumes it
(VC-11) -> struct_values_one_dereference_typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713 #1; the pin keyed by its struct
(VC-11, M-DR3) -> typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678 #1; the new `_HTEMP` user (VC-10) ->
struct_values_one_dereference_typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713 #9; `grep -qF "- got"` -> a_green_gate_can_be_
measuring_nothing… (6.7.13); one output path per test (ETXTBSY) -> a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code… (6.7.13); the
rows covered the callee shapes, not every callee × source pair — the replayed one-vector callee given `callptr` read
lane 0 wrong on x86_64 -> inline_replay_indirect_call_spill_below_the_frame_v6713 (INT-1, the fix). User-facing:
language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd, generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310,
closures_v637_v638, struct_argument_type_checked_v673, async_await_futures_v6311; field_notes/language/
semantics_runtime.cyml vector_value_return_binding_forms, call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11,
generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11, vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.13]
(VC-1 .. VC-12, VC-5r, VC-6r, VC-6r2).

[[entries]]
name = "windows_flock_relock_and_lock_state_in_the_kernel_v6713"
description = "6.7.13's stdlib lane (LIB-1, LIB-1r): Windows `xflock` re-taking the mode its handle already held unlocked first, and the kernel handed the lock to a waiter in the gap (patra lost a second handle's INSERTs). A same-handle re-lock must not unlock; Windows has no lock-query API, so `UnlockFileEx` on a private byte IS the query; a static gate that pins a call site but not the callee's answer is blind; a lock-race row must fail, never hang."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "windows", "pe", "LockFileEx", "UnlockFileEx", "xflock", "flock", "patra", "cass", "wine", "gates", "mutation"]
---
1. A SAME-HANDLE RE-LOCK MUST NOT UNLOCK FIRST. Emulating flock with Windows byte-range locks: a range relocked on the
handle that holds it blocks (exclusive) or stacks (shared), so 6.7.12 unlocked before every lock. But the kernel
grants a pending `LockFileEx` waiter AT the unlock — deterministic on real Windows (cass: every run), only sometimes
under wine (1 of 4 runs). patra re-takes `LOCK_EX` per statement inside a transaction, so a second handle's INSERTs
got in, returned `PATRA_OK` and were lost (40 of 60 rows). Fix: a re-lock to the HELD mode is a no-op; only a mode
CHANGE drops and re-takes (flock's own conversion is not atomic either).

2. WINDOWS HAS NO LOCK-QUERY API: `UnlockFileEx` IS THE QUERY. To know "does this handle hold mode M?" without touching
the real lock, record per-handle state as a lock on a PRIVATE byte that only lock holders touch (taken after the real
lock, released before it): 2^63 − 2 for exclusive, 2^63 − 3 for shared, beside the 2^63 − 1 sentinel. Probe with
`UnlockFileEx` on it and re-lock at once on success; nobody ever waits on that byte, so the probe has no usable gap.
Kernel-held beats a per-handle table: per handle and thread-safe by construction, freed by `CloseHandle` (no stale
record for a reused handle value, so no close hook and no close caveat), no capacity. TRAP: the record byte must never
be the sentinel itself — locking the sentinel twice exclusive on one handle self-deadlocks the first `LOCK_EX` (the
measured mutant).

3. A STATIC GATE THAT PINS A CALL SITE BUT NOT THE CALLEE'S ANSWER IS BLIND (LIB-1r). Axis 6 grepped
`_xflock_win_unlock(fd, mode) == 0` but never what the helper returns; a helper answering 1 for a granted unlock never
fires the probe and silently restores the bug while the gate stays 13 / 13 green (and wine's behaviour axis misses it
too). Pin the helper's return contract (0 held / 1 `ERROR_NOT_LOCKED`) beside the call site — axis 4 does now, and
axis 4's pin with axis 6 is the deterministic local guard (a helper answering 0 for `ERROR_NOT_LOCKED` instead hangs
axis 5: an unheld mode byte reads as held).

4. A LOCK-RACE ROW MUST FAIL, NEVER HANG. The waiter thread releases the lock the moment it gets it (so the buggy arm
completes the holder's re-lock and the row reads RED), every wait has a deadline, and the gate's wine runs are bounded
(`timeout 120`, exit 124): before the bound, the deadlocking mutant hung check.sh's axis 5 and had to be stopped by
PID.

Into their classes (no new item): wine granting the waiter only sometimes -> two_fields_of_a_foreign_api_constrain_
each_other_and_the_emulator_does_not_enforce_it_6_6_6 (6.7.13, the fifth); the conversion sentence ->
windows_locks_links_and_fixtures_that_carry_linux_v6712 #1. User-facing: language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking;
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml xflock_on_windows_waits_and_answers_minus_11_v6712. Gates:
tests/tcyr/crossos/xflock_relock_keeps_lock.tcyr, pe_lockfileex_reroute.sh axis 4 / 6. CHANGELOG [6.7.13] LIB-1,
LIB-1r.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_replay_indirect_call_spill_below_the_frame_v6713"
description = "6.7.13 integration (INT-1): an indirect call (`callptr` / `fncallN`) as an argument of an inline-replayed fn spilled its callee into frame slots counted by GFLC alone; the replay releases GFLC back to its parameters after each argument and the frame is sized from the final GFLC and the `_flc_hwm` high-water, so a spill slot lay one slot BELOW the frame and the call's own first argument push landed on it — the callee got its code address as `a`. Every transient frame slot must raise the high-water, AND every frame sizer must take max(GFLC, `_flc_hwm`) (6.7.14: a closure's frame was sized from GFLC alone, fixed); AND every GFLC release must record the peak first (6.7.15, VC-1: a plain fn's frame temporary taken while an `#inline` fn's arguments are evaluated lay past the frame — the open half, closed); a regression row for a frame-layout bug needs a tight caller and both frame parities."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "integration", "INT-1", "inline replay", "#inline", "_PINDIRECT_CALL_IN", "_icall_slot", "_flc_hwm", "GFLC", "frame", "callptr", "fncallN", "f64v2", "value-form", "x86_64", "v6.7.14", "_cl_frame_bytes", "closure", "_w128_tmp", "_HTEMP", "v6.7.15", "VC-1", "_inl_flc_release", "SFLC"]
---
THE BUG. D30 (6.7.13) made a vector call result a legal value-form argument, and the release-docs pass tried the new
shape through a fn pointer: `la(callptr(&mkv, 4, 2))` with `fn la(x: f64v2)` — small enough to be replayed inline —
exited 2 on x86_64 where 42 is right (`fncall2(&mkv, 4, 2)` too), and the two-parameter `lc(callptr(&mkv, 4, 2), 0)`
faulted (139); aarch64, cx and PE read 42. `_PINDIRECT_CALL_IN` spills the callee into two frame slots of its own,
counted by GFLC alone. The inline replay releases GFLC back to its parameters after each argument, and the frame is
sized from the FINAL GFLC and the `_flc_hwm` high-water — so the second spill slot lay one slot below the frame,
exactly where the call's own first argument `push` went. The store of the callee pointer overwrote that argument,
and `mkv` received its own code address as `a` (0x400480 × 10 + 2 ≡ 2 mod 256, read off the disassembly).

THE FIX. `_icall_slot` (src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr) allocates each spill slot AND records it in `_flc_hwm`, as
`_agg_temp_t` and the replay's own parameter slots already did. cycc's size is unchanged. An integer argument's spill
stayed inside the frame (a register and an in-frame slot), so scalar callers were not affected in the measured shapes.

THE LESSONS. (1) EVERY TRANSIENT FRAME SLOT MUST RAISE THE HIGH-WATER. Anything that allocates by bumping GFLC and
can outlive a later release of GFLC (the replay's per-argument release, a hidden temp) is invisible to the frame size
unless it records itself in `_flc_hwm` — the same reason top_level_has_no_frame_so_a_lowering_that_needs_one_must_
refuse_or_bring_its_own sizes its micro-frame from max(GFLC, `_flc_hwm`). Grep every `GFLC` bump for its high-water
line. 6.7.14 (VC-4, VC-5, VC-4r) ADDS THE SECOND HALF: EVERY FRAME SIZER MUST TAKE max(GFLC, `_flc_hwm`). An `#inline`
replay evaluates its arguments with its parameter slots reserved, then releases GFLC back to them (`SFLC(saved_flc +
_ptot)`); a frame temporary (`_HTEMP`, `_w128_tmp`, `_agg_temp_t`) taken during an inline argument is inside the frame
only if the frame is sized from `_flc_hwm`. PARSE_FN_DEF and the top-level micro-frames did that; the CLOSURE LITERAL
sized its frame from GFLC alone, so the copy's slot lay below rsp and the next push (`_sar_copy_ra`'s, PE's
`_sarg_snap` temp) overwrote it — D31 / D32's newly accepted sources exposed it: `|k| li(lanes(b), k)` read 40 for 42 on
x86_64 / IR=3 and faulted (139) on aarch64, `|k| li(two(b, setb(pb, 7)), k)` 64 / 192 / 139 / PE 5 for 114. Fix:
`_cl_frame_bytes` takes the larger of the two (`_fnst_save` zeroes the high-water at the closure's entry,
`_fnst_restore` brings back the enclosing fn's; a helper because `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` is at cybs's per-fn reference
ceiling; cyrius 986123203). It also fixed the PRE-EXISTING PE `|k| li(lanes(mkv(4, 2)), k)` (82 / 242 for 42) and
integer-only nested inline calls in a closure (26 for 57 on x86_64 / IR=3 / PE). The corpus had no inline call inside
a closure — a blind spot, now rows (`sp_irows`, `simd_pointer_param.sh` VCi1-VCi5; mutation M-VCi1, the fix reverted:
RED 20). Meanwhile, copy through registers and the stack (`_sar_copy_ra`), never through a hidden temporary, inside an
inline argument. THE THIRD HALF, CLOSED IN 6.7.15 (VC-1; it was the open half, High, pre-existing, filed at cyrius
1b0f84d4b, issues/archived/2026-10-17-inline-replay-argument-temps-lie-past-the-frame.md): in a PLAIN fn a frame
temporary taken while an `#inline` fn's arguments are evaluated was lost by the inline path's own `SFLC`, which no sizer
can cover — `id(a) + id(a + 1) + 2` with `a: u128 = 40` read 84 for 83 on x86_64 / IR=3 / aarch64 / PE (cx 83). EVERY
GFLC RELEASE MUST RECORD THE PEAK FIRST: `_inl_flc_release` writes GFLC into `_flc_hwm` before lowering it. So the rule
has three halves — a slot raises the high-water (6.7.13), a sizer reads it (6.7.14), a release records it (6.7.15):
vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715 #1; rows tests/tcyr/crossos/inline_argument_
temps.tcyr (17, each with a one-extra-local twin, since rounding the frame to 16 hides a one-slot overhang at half the
parities). (2) A REGRESSION ROW FOR A FRAME-LAYOUT BUG NEEDS A TIGHT CALLER: a caller with locals of its own padded the
frame and hid the overlap, so each of the 15 rows (`tests/tcyr/crossos/inline_replay_indirect_call_argument.tcyr`) is
its own minimal caller. (3) THE ROWS COVERED THE CALLEE SHAPES, NOT EVERY CALLEE × SOURCE PAIR (the vectors lane's own
finding at the docs pass): D30's rows passed `callptr(&mkv, ..)` to a non-replayed callee and to the SECOND parameter
of a two-parameter `#inline` fn, never to a replayed one-parameter callee's first slot. Matrix the replay paths
(auto-inline, `#inline`; one vs several vector parameters; first vs later position) against every source kind.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/inline_replay_indirect_call_argument.tcyr (15 rows; 15 / 15 on x86_64, aarch64 (qemu), cx
and PE (wine); the pre-fix compiler fails 2 and faults on x86_64); tests/gates/codegen/inline_replay_indirect_call_
spill.sh (S1 / S2 controls, V1 – V3 on plain / IR=3 / DCE, the crossos file; mutation M-H1, the high-water line
removed: RED 10). Related: inline_replay_is_a_prerequisite_for_simd_residency_and_it_binds_params_by_slot (the
replay binds parameters by slot). User-facing: language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi. CHANGELOG [6.7.13]
INT-1.

[[entries]]
name = "ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's infra lane (IN-1 .. IN-18, IN-4r, IN-6r, IN-6r2: the IR arena, IR=3's field emits, DCE compaction past 4,096 runs, a live fn inside a dead one, a redefined fn's losing body, `CYRIUS_SYMS` after compaction, cybs's EOF skippers / names / preprocessor caps, asm `in` / `out` and whole-name mnemonics, the aarch64 W^X cost, two timing gates, the instruction-aligned call graph): a fn's code range is not a closed box; code outside every fn range is not necessarily top-level code; a byte scan for E8 is not a call graph — walk the instructions, and walk lazily; a mnemonic that is a keyword never reaches an IDENT-only dispatch, and an emitter that skips its operands assembles another instruction; a mirror comment carries its own target's constant."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "infra", "IR arena", "CYRIUS_DCE", "CYRIUS_IR", "wp_compact", "_fn_redirect", "_instantiate_generic_fn", "dead code", "call graph", "DECODE_LEN", "dead_code_floor", "ASM_MNEMONIC", "ASM_IN", "_wx_data_vaddr", "W^X", "aarch64"]
---
1. A FN'S CODE RANGE IS NOT A CLOSED BOX (IN-5). Inline generic instantiation (`_instantiate_generic_fn`, the
jump-over) emits an instance INSIDE the fn that first needs it, so ranges nest; an inline replay can leave the outer
fn dead and the inner instance live, and a sweep that fills or removes `[fn_start, fn_end)` takes the live body with
it (`CYRIUS_DCE=1` removed a live generic instance). Any pass that acts on a fn's range must ask whether another fn
starts inside it — a sorted index of live starts, binary-searched; the dead fn is now kept whole. A `CYRIUS_DCE=1` run
of the corpus would have found it: the corpus file failed under the flag, and no gate built the corpus with it.
Cross-referenced from any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream (6.7.14).

2. CODE OUTSIDE EVERY FN RANGE IS NOT NECESSARILY TOP-LEVEL CODE (IN-6). The DCE seed takes a call with no host fn as a
ROOT, because top-level code is live. A redefined fn's losing body is outside every range once `_fn_redirect` turns
its entry into a jump to the winner (6.6.20: every call binds the last definition), so its calls rooted their callees
and kept them alive. Record such ranges and seed nothing from them. Their IR=3 trap — recorded at parse time, then
moved by the seam's `wp_compact`, so live fns were removed (SIGILL) — is in
compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not (6.7.14 (a)).

3. A BYTE SCAN FOR E8 IS NOT A CALL GRAPH (IN-18). The x86 dead-code reachability counted every `E8` whose rel32
landed on a fn start as an edge — including one inside an imm32 / disp32. Data addresses move with every global, so
the dead set moved with the global COUNT: two appended globals flipped `ir_emit1` in the cx fork and turned
`dead_code_floor.sh` RED (generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711 #7, now historical). The graph
walks with the length decoder and counts a call only where an instruction starts. Cost: walking a span only when a
call in it would mark a NEW fn halves the walked bytes (+21 ms against +80 ms for an eager walk). Where the bytes may
not be instructions — a raw `asm` byte run, a switch's jump table — the byte scan stays, MEASURED not assumed:
any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes… (the 13th).

4. A MNEMONIC THAT IS A KEYWORD NEVER REACHES AN IDENT-ONLY DISPATCH, AND AN EMITTER THAT SKIPS ITS OPERANDS ASSEMBLES
ANOTHER INSTRUCTION (IN-13, D16). `in` is token 76, a keyword, so `asm { in al, dx; }` — the guide's own spelling —
never reached the mnemonic dispatch (`inb` was the only way to `ASM_IN`). And `out dx, eax` assembled `out dx, al`: an
emitter that does not read its operands emits one fixed form for any of them, silently. `in` / `out` now encode the
accumulator's width and refuse an operand they cannot take; `lgdt` / `mov crN` / `ltr` / `int N` still skipped theirs
(filed — closed in 6.7.15 by IT-6, five more instances: infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_
seed_pass_v6715 #4). The prefix half — `clix` was `cli` — is in grep_the_shape_not_the_name_a_rule_applied_inline_once_is_the_tell_
6_6_6 (6.7.14).

5. A MIRROR COMMENT CARRIES ITS OWN TARGET'S CONSTANT (IN-15). x86's `_wx_data_vaddr` said its W^X split "costs
near-zero file bytes" — true at x86's 4 KB p_align, false at aarch64's 64 KB, where a W^X executable is at least
~64 KB and moves in 64 KB steps. A comment copied into a twin backend restates the source target's numbers; re-derive
them for the twin (the aarch64 cost is now documented and pinned, the layout kept — 6.7.13's D45).

Into their classes (no new item): a grow that drops part of a table passes every exit-code test and the self-host
(IN-1, IN-4) -> phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap and a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing… (6.7.14 (a));
an unrecorded emit belongs to the node before it (IN-2) -> any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes… (the 11th; the
IR=3 caveat in typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678 #4 retired); the buffer is not pure
instructions (IN-18) -> the same entry (the 13th); a range recorded before the IR=3 seam, `CYRIUS_SYMS` before the
sweep, the realign pad (IN-6r, IN-8, IN-3) -> compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not; a reader left
on a fixed base (IN-7) -> phase0_growable…; the 4,096-run cap restated in five places (IN-4, IN-4r) ->
a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer; a cybs pass-0 skipper past EOF (IN-10) ->
toktyp_reads_past_gtcnt… (6.7.14 (a)); cybs's `loop` / `const` / `do` and its preprocessor caps (IN-11, IN-12) ->
cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt… (6.7.14); `clix` was `cli` (IN-14) -> grep_the_shape_not_the_name…; the two timing gates and
a helper that ends in `[ … ] && { … }` (IN-16, IN-17) -> methodology.cyml a_flaky_gate_on_a_shared_box_read_the_PROCESS_
TREE_before_theorising, a_gate_bar_derived_from_a_host_quantity…, a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_pipefail_v6714.
User-facing: language/tooling.cyml compiler_env_vars; language/features.cyml cyrius_dce_verbose_env_v51145;
field_notes/language/diagnostics_caps.cyml a_kept_dead_fn_is_ud2_and_the_syms_map_predates_compaction_v6711;
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml var_name_bracket_is_bytes_not_elements (the anuenue verdict: not a cyrius
bug). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (IN-1 .. IN-18, IN-4r, IN-6r, IN-6r2).

[[entries]]
name = "generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_per_token_errors_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's generics lane (GN-1 .. GN-18, GN-5r, GN-14r, GN-18r: nested instance names, the stub scan's tables, the replayed return, `var h: S = gpg<S>()`, `: *Box<T>`, non-name type arguments, refused explicit calls, Vec elements and signatures, struct-carrier and bounded forwards, a T field's bounded method, `.m<..>(`, `p: *T` inference, once-per-token errors): a reset written for one return class must test that class; a generic's return type sits between PARSE_FN_DEF's two bindings; a token's value is a name offset only for an identifier; a refused type argument must not mint an instance; a check that must not mint must put everything back; the stub scan's forwarding test follows every binding position; a judgement that recurses into a scan must not nest it, and its 'seen' is per judgement; a second kind of site keyed by one token needs no flag when the positions cannot overlap; `.m<..>(` must not read `<` as less-than; one argument reader for two parameter shapes must be told which; the error emitters latch per (token, identity)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "generics", "_gen_ret_tp", "_gen_decl_check", "TOKTYP", "PEEKV", "_ta_refd", "_targ_chk", "_parse_one_type_arg", "_bbs_fwd_bad", "_bnd_base_bad", "_bnd_has", "_fnt_bnd", "_field_load_on", "_infer_conc_at_cursor", "_ic_ptr_param", "_err_once", "_err_dup", "_err_quiet", "ERR_UNDEF", "_undef_var_report"]
---
1. A RESET WRITTEN FOR ONE RETURN CLASS MUST TEST THAT CLASS — AND "NOT A STRUCT" IS NOT ONE CLASS (GN-5, GN-5r).
PARSE_VAR's B-explicit reset dropped `var h: S = ..`'s annotation whenever `_gen_decl_check` did not keep it — which
is also true of every return that is no struct at all (`: *T`, an untyped value), so `var h: S = gpg<S>();` lost its
handle. The first fix skipped the reset for every `GFRS <= 0`, and `: T` is one of those whose instance may be a
SCALAR: `var a: P3 = gid(5);` became a handle on a scalar and SIGSEGV'd (the review). Test the declared shape
(`_gen_ret_tp`), not the struct check's answer. REVISED in 6.7.15 (GN-25; 6.7.15's D13 (a), a language decision): a
word instance into a struct-annotated local IS a handle, as `var b: P3 = wid(5);` has been since 6.7.10 — the fault
above is the non-generic call's own — so the reset is keyed on GFRS of the instance the call REACHES, not on the
declared `: T` shape (type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715 #15).

2. A GENERIC'S RETURN TYPE SITS BETWEEN PARSE_FN_DEF'S TWO BINDINGS (GN-6). Parameters are bound and the body is
bound, but the return annotation is read in between — so its reader binds for itself (the instance since 6.6.20, the
base since 6.7.14, T -> i64 as the base body); that is what lets `fn g<T>(..): *Box<T>` compile. Its pass-1 half (stay
quiet, refuse nothing pass 2 knows) is two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority
(6.7.14 (d)).

3. A TOKEN'S VALUE IS A NAME-POOL OFFSET ONLY FOR AN IDENTIFIER (GN-7). Read TOKTYP before PEEKV: a number's, a
keyword's or a string's value read as a name printed "truct", "struct", "enum" or crashed — and a number that happened
to equal a type name's offset (`Box<7>`) compiled AS that type. A refusal of a non-name type argument must also step
the argument's own tokens (paren / bracket depth apart from angle depth), or `id<1 + 2>(3)` leaves `+ 2>(3)` to the
statement parser.

4. A REFUSED TYPE ARGUMENT MUST NOT MINT AN INSTANCE (GN-8). The instance's own parse clears the panic latch, so the
caller's arguments were then judged against a type the program never asked for (6.7.12's D27 against `g$i`). A
dedicated per-call latch (`_ta_refd`, set by every type-argument refusal, cleared when the list opens) sends the call
to the base with its arguments consumed under the panic latch: one error.

5. A CHECK THAT MUST NOT MINT, READING A LIST A DEFINITION IS IN THE MIDDLE OF (GN-10, GN-11). `_targ_chk` reads a Vec
element's `<..>` (and a signature's) the way `_parse_one_type_arg` does, but must not instantiate (a struct
definition's fields must stay contiguous), must report once per token, must read the fn's / struct's own type
parameters as names, and must put back the cursor, the owed `>` closers and any panic latch it set — so every site
consumes the list exactly as before and the two passes agree. Its "no instance" guard has no measured shape that shows
the difference (mutant MVP5 stays green): kept, recorded unproven.

6. THE STUB SCAN'S FORWARDING TEST MUST FOLLOW EVERY POSITION THAT BINDS THE CALLEE'S T (GN-12). Direct carriers and
struct carriers (6.7.8's GFGM bits 24 + 20w) alike: a position the inference reads but the scan does not leaves the
forwarder an i64 base that the inference then refuses at its definition — called or not.

7. A JUDGEMENT THAT RECURSES INTO A SCAN MUST NOT NEST THE SCAN, AND ITS "SEEN" IS PER JUDGEMENT (GN-14, GN-14r). The
stub scan's tables are globals, so `_bbs_fwd_bad` collects the forwarded generics first, on its own swapped-in tables —
all eight words (pointers, counts, capacities, cursors), since restoring counts alone does not restore contents — and
judges them after. A visited list, not a depth cap alone: a cycle that branches twice per level is 2^depth scans (F8d
does not finish in 60 s without the dedupe). The depth cap that remained (64) was a REACH limit that refused valid chains
past it — stated in the guide and filed (memoising `_bnd_base_bad` per generic lets it go: 3000-deep 39.8 s uncapped vs
2.5 s capped). 6.7.15 (GN-20) did exactly that and removed the cap: type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_
site_diagnostics_v6715 #11 (a "not bad" answer is kept only from a clean judgement; the CALLED chain still meets the
instance-nesting bound at about 120). And `_fnt_bnd` is alloc'd for every generic's "no bound" mark, so "a table exists" is no
test for "a bound exists" (`_bnd_has`).

8. A SECOND KIND OF SITE KEYED BY THE SAME TOKEN NEEDS NO FLAG WHEN THE POSITIONS CANNOT OVERLAP; A SCALAR `self` FROM
A NON-NAME (GN-15). A bounded site is keyed by its method-name token; `x.f.m(`'s m sits where no `recv.m(` site can,
so the site itself is the flag. A method on a scalar that is not a name (a field leaf) gets `self` by parking the value
in an anonymous frame slot and entering the method arm on it — the arm's existing scalar path needs no new code, and
the giant `_field_load_on` gains no reference.

9. `.m<..>(` AFTER A RECEIVER (GN-16). The method arm read `<` as less-than. Recognise the call only when m names no
field of the receiver's struct and a generic method of it, with `(` after its `<..>` — so `_field_load_on`'s field
lookup always runs first (mutant ME2, dropping the field test, stays green: unproven, the corpus differential its
guard). Every lookahead keyed on `x.m(` exactly (multi-value destructure, pair `?`, dyn / vector sources) must learn
`x.m<..>(` too (filed — 17 of them, one helper since 6.7.15's GN-21: type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_
site_diagnostics_v6715 #12); pass the method-name token explicitly into the arm, or name-keyed checks key the `>`.

10. ONE ARGUMENT READER FOR TWO PARAMETER SHAPES (GN-17). `_infer_conc_at_cursor` served `x: T` and `p: *T` alike and
never knew which; its comment's premise ("a `*Pt` local is not struct-typed") went stale when 6.6.17 gave a `*S` local
slot type `0 - sid`, so a `*S` local bound S through `x: T` against the guide, while `&G`, a `*S` global, field,
parameter or call bound nothing through `p: *T`. Pass the carrying parameter's shape (`_ic_arg` / `_ic_ptr_param`);
re-read a reader's premises when a slot-type encoding changes. The token-level scans (`_tok_gen_tp_sid`, the stub
scan's receiver typing, the pair scans) carry their own copy of the rule and did not follow (filed; `_tok_gen_tp_sid`
follows since 6.7.15's GN-16 — type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715 #10).

11. THE ERROR EMITTERS LATCH PER (TOKEN, IDENTITY) SINCE 6.7.14 (GN-18, GN-18r, GN-13). A generic body is parsed for
the base, each instance and an inline replay; `_err_once` / `_err_dup` (util.cyr) record each report and print a
repeat as nothing (`_had_error` and `_panic` still set). A new error writer heads with one of them; a NOTE printed
after an error must test `_err_quiet` (or the panic latch), or a repeat prints its note alone (a generic struct's field
refusal gave 1 error + 4 notes; a bound refusal swallowed by the panic latch printed its note under another error,
GN-13). The identity is what the text names — the emitter, the expected token, the message pointer / length, the names
— so a different message at the same token still prints. Warnings and the 61 direct `"error:"` writers were not
latched (filed — latched since 6.7.15's GN-22 / GN-23, `_warn_seen` / `_derr_seen`: type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_
and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715 #13). ONE MESSAGE, ONE WRITER (the wave-1 integration, a273b27e): this lane routed "undefined variable" through
ERR_UNDEF (once per token and message) while the frontend lane routed it through `_undef_var_report` (a const fn's
stand-down); two dedupe mechanisms on one message met at the merge, and the merge made them one path
(`_undef_var_report` -> `_ce_name_said` -> ERR_UNDEF).

Into their classes (no new item): two-parameter instance names that collide (GN-1, D1 (a)) ->
generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711 #1 (closed); a replay judged by the caller's struct class
(GN-4) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #5 (6.7.14 (a)); the stub scan's silent 64-entry
tables (GN-3) -> a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer (6.7.14 (b)) and the 6.7.11
entry's #4; a reader shared by pass 1 (GN-11) and the return type's pass-1 read (GN-6) -> two_passes… (6.7.14 (d));
backticks that ran in a gate's PASS line (GN-5r) -> a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing… (6.7.14 (c)). (GN-2, a gate
blind spot — `slice_position_refused.sh` K2h now reaches `_sl_ann_at`'s FINDSTRUCT test — needs no item.) User-facing:
language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617, trait_bounds_v671,
slice_type_v58x; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG
[6.7.14] (GN-1 .. GN-18, GN-5r, GN-14r, GN-18r).

[[entries]]
name = "frontend_cascade_latches_direct_reports_and_the_bake_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's frontend lane (FE-1 .. FE-26, FE-14r, FE-15r: TOKNAME's operator names, one report per private call, the refused const's silenced errors, local consts in replays, one mistake one error, `--syntax-only` chains, the declaration zone's pointer annotations / named literals / baked constants / float literals / redeclarations, `#pure` across definitions, call-site spellings, struct literals in expressions, declarations after statements, a captured u128): a top-context evaluation must not inherit or leave the cascade latch; two walkers of one body report one token twice unless deduped by token; a diagnostic printed directly needs its own per-site memory; a failed expectation must not step a recovery point; a const fn body is parsed twice; a refusal must step what it refuses and end its statement in sync; `--syntax-only` must consume what the resolved path consumes; a bake does not make a store redundant by itself; the replay-time baker covers what the integer folder folds plus a float literal; the fn table holds the mangled spelling, and mangling is lossy."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "frontend", "diagnostics", "_panic", "_sync_skip", "_ce_top_expr", "_ce_xpend_one", "_tl_pass1", "_ce_said", "_vis_seen", "_expect_step", "_sync_stop_at", "_ce_name_said", "_ce_fn_refd", "_cst_skip_sync", "_pair_sync_ti", "_slit_whole", "_synonly_chain_call", "_gvk_quiet", "_gvk_prev", "_gvk_flt", "_fn_src_name"]
---
1. A TOP-CONTEXT EVALUATION MUST NOT INHERIT OR LEAVE THE CASCADE LATCH (FE-3, FE-5). ERR_* return at once while
`_panic == 1` and set it otherwise; only the statement loop's `_sync_skip` clears it. The end-of-pass-1 const sweep,
an enum value, an array size and the end-of-sweep re-walk evaluate a declaration's OWN tokens outside any statement, so
the first refusal latched and every later evaluation's message was swallowed — and pass 2 started latched, its first
resync eating a statement (a false "undefined variable 'L'"). `_ce_top_expr` / `_ce_xpend_one` save, clear and restore
`_panic`; `_tl_pass1` clears it before pass 2. The in-place sites (`_cst_ctx_val`) still latched (filed — closed in
6.7.15 by FE-11: a refused in-place context hands the latch back only in sync, frontend_redeclarations_baked_literals_
and_refused_names_v6715 #8).

2. TWO WALKERS OF ONE BODY REPORT ONE TOKEN TWICE (FE-3). The sweep runs a const fn's body for a const that calls it,
and pass 2's definition check walks the same body; once the latch no longer hid the second, the same token was refused
twice. Dedupe BY TOKEN (`_ce_said` over `_tn_once`): count the refusal exactly as ERR_* would (`_ce_err`,
`_had_error`, `_panic`) and do not print; mark only when it WOULD print.

3. A DIAGNOSTIC PRINTED DIRECTLY NEEDS ITS OWN PER-SITE MEMORY (FE-2). A body re-parsed per replay (its definition,
each `#inline` expansion, each generic instance) re-runs every check in it; a report that prints directly — not through
ERR_*, which a per-token latch can cover — needs a (token, callee) memory in `_dep_seen`'s shape (`_vis_seen`) that
keeps the verdict and prints once: a call of another file's `private` fn is reported once per call site.

4. A FAILED EXPECTATION MUST NOT STEP A RECOVERY POINT (FE-7). `ERR_EXPECT` then an unconditional step moved past the
very token the resync stops at: a `)` miss stepped the `;`, the statement's `;` check stepped the `for`, and
`_sync_skip` re-entered INSIDE the loop header (`expected '=', got '<'`). `_expect_step` reports and steps only a token
that is not `;` / `}` / end of file / a statement keyword (`_sync_stop_at`, `_sync_kw` shared with `_sync_skip`); an
unclosed argument list gets no arity verdict.

5. A CONST FN BODY IS PARSED TWICE (FE-7, FE-8) — once by its definition check, once as an ordinary fn. A report about
the body needs a stand-down keyed to the check's verdict, BY NAME (`_ce_name_said`, `_ce_fn_refd`: another unknown
name is still reported), and the check must evaluate with the latch clear and keep its own latch out of the body. The
leak's remaining effects were filed (issues/archived/2026-10-16-refused-const-fn-check-latch-leaks-into-its-body.md) and
closed in 6.7.15 by FE-10: the check hands the body the caller's latch and only the token and name it refused
(frontend_redeclarations_baked_literals_and_refused_names_v6715 #7).

6. A REFUSAL MUST STEP WHAT IT REFUSES AND END ITS STATEMENT IN SYNC (FE-8, FE-11, FE-24). A statement refused at its
first name was stepped to its `;` with the latch still set, so PARSE_STMT's resync skipped the NEXT statement whole —
every other refused statement in a row lost, since 6.7.2. Clear at the `;` through `_pair_sync_ti` (6.7.11's in-sync
rule) — `_cst_skip_sync`; a read refused mid-expression likewise. In an EXPRESSION position: `P { .. }` read as an
expression was "undefined variable 'P'", and the `{` then cascaded two to five invented errors. The refusal must step
the `{ .. }` and any selector tail, and tell the enclosing walk (a nested literal, a return class) it took the whole
element (`_slit_whole`, `_slit_at`), or the walk invents the next error. (D12 (a): one error, "a struct literal is an
initializer"; a wrapped initializer `var q: P = (P { .. });` is the literal.)

7. `--syntax-only` MUST CONSUME WHAT THE RESOLVED PATH CONSUMES (FE-12). The v6.5.19 rule extends to a `*T` hop whose T
this file does not declare: the hop loop stopped and the leftover `.g` became a syntax error the file does not have.
Every `--syntax-only` arm that stops on an unresolved type must walk the rest of the chain (`_synonly_chain_call`,
each `(args)` through PCMPE, so real grammar errors still report).

8. A BAKE DOES NOT MAKE A STORE REDUNDANT BY ITSELF (FE-15, FE-15r). 6.6.16 baked `var X = C;` into the image and kept
the replayed store "so no code changes"; but the store also RE-RUNS the expression (an `INT_MIN % -1` trap on x86 / cx /
PE) and re-imposes the value after earlier writers. Drop it only when the image value is the entry's own and no earlier
LIVE writer of the slot differs (`_gvk_quiet` / `_gvk_prev` over `_gvx_*`); keep it for a list blob, a bool global (the
replay judges it), a private-name initializer (the replay reports it). A literal static init is not a replayed writer,
while a folded ZERO is a deferred entry ("A folded ZERO still falls through"), which is why `= 0` orders like a named
constant (the guide's ordering paragraph corrected, FE-15r).

9. THE REPLAY-TIME BAKER COVERS `_CF_TRY` PLUS A FLOAT LITERAL (FE-17). `_gvk_one` baked only what the integer folder
`_CF_TRY` folds; a float scalar's bare literal now has `_gvk_flt` (FLIT bits; an f32 rounded with `_gai_f32`). In a
`kernel;` build the "runs after the top-level program" warning names exactly what is left (non-literal float constants
were filed; since 6.7.15's FE-16 / D46 a constant f64 EXPRESSION is baked too, through the const evaluator as a quiet
probe — frontend_redeclarations_baked_literals_and_refused_names_v6715 #6).

10. THE FN TABLE HOLDS THE MANGLED SPELLING, AND MANGLING IS LOSSY (FE-23). `T_m` is also a legal free fn name, a `mod`
fn is `m_f`, an instance `g$i32`, a trait collision `T_Tr_m`: a diagnostic's spelling must come from a record kept
where the compiler KNOWS it (GFMETH at the impl / the method call, GFIC at the instance; `_fn_src_name`), never from
splitting the name. Changing a message family another lane's rows pin is done after the merge, with the rows.

Into their classes (no new item): a replay is a scope with no SCOPE_POP (FE-6) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_
replayed_returns_v6711 #5 (6.7.14 (b)); clamp a diagnostic's token at one point (FE-7) -> toktyp_reads_past_gtcnt…
(6.7.14 (b)); a replay reader steps a shape as its registering pass does (FE-13) -> one_reading_for_both_passes_and_
for_every_list_walker_v677 #7b; the zone replay is a second parser (FE-14) -> struct_destinations… #9; peek, don't emit
then rewind (FE-16) -> cfo_const_fold_rewind_class… (6.7.14); a per-slot mark compared at the fold (FE-19) ->
redeclared_name_new_slot_orphans_per_slot_data_and_name_replays_drift; every attribute a call site reads is recorded in
pass one (FE-21) -> two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority (6.7.14 (a)); one named
error after the first statement and an include's line from the file map (FE-25) ->
cyrius_top_level_statement_closes_the_declaration_window; a captured u128 (FE-26) -> vector_values_receives_arguments_
and_dereferences_v6713 #7; one "undefined variable" writer (the wave-1 merge) -> generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_
per_token_errors_v6714 #11; `grep -c` under pipefail (FE-14r) and the `git archive HEAD` gate in a codegen lane ->
methodology.cyml a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_pipefail_v6714 and worktree_lanes_share_more_than_the_tree….
User-facing: language/features.cyml const_and_const_fn_v672, diagnostics_column_caret_multierror, relaxed_fn_ordering,
struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612, visibility_public_private_v650, inline_directive_v6563;
language/core.cyml type_names_v6616; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml global_init_order_silent_zero,
a_same_value_redeclaration_is_silent_by_design_v6712, multi_value_returns_and_struct_words_since_6_7_11. CHANGELOG
[6.7.14] (FE-1 .. FE-26, FE-14r, FE-15r).

[[entries]]
name = "tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_ignores_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's tools lane (TL-1 .. TL-8: the macOS CLIs refuse `--win`, the aarch64 Linux CLI resolves a dep's target for its own arch, `cyrius header` reads a wrapped parameter list, `sock_send_a` / `sock_recv_a` typed `: Result`, the SIGHUP rows under `nohup`, a killed `cyrius deps` stops its git, a killed dry pass leaves no scratch resolve, cyrius-lsp's loose files): a target selector a host fork ignores builds the host format silently; on the aarch64 Linux CLI `_arch` is not the emitted arch; a source scanner reading a declaration line by line must bound its spans by the buffer; a child with cleanup of its own is forwarded the signal, and a forwarded wait needs a bound; record-before-create must name the sibling temp before it exists, and only the temp it created; a POSIX sh cannot reset a signal ignore it inherited at entry; turning off one reader of a project's pin surfaces the next."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "cbt", "signals", "SIGHUP", "SigIgn", "nohup", "signal_default", "_cbt_fork_tracked", "_cbt_scratch_track", "_io_tmp_name", "_dep_scr_next_tmp", "_cbt_sig_tree_set", "cyrius header", "_hdr_close", "_dep_arch_is_a64", "CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN", "cyrius-lsp", "CYRIUS_RESOLVED", "CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT"]
---
1. A TARGET SELECTOR A HOST FORK IGNORES BUILDS THE HOST FORMAT SILENTLY (TL-1). Neither Mach-O compiler fork
(src/main_x86_macho.cyr, src/main_aarch64_macho.cyr) reads `CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`. The CLI's compile() injects it, and the
fork wrote a Mach-O under the `.exe` name at rc 0. The fix is in the CLI, not the fork: every target selector a host's
compiler ignores is refused in `_cli_foreign_target` (via `_cli_host_refuses_win`), or it builds the host format — the
class of 6.7.11's D30 -> 6.7.12's D6 -> 6.7.13 -> 6.7.14. A refusal composed at run time sits in the binary as PIECES
(prefix, `_cli_host_emits_only()`, tail), so a strings-based gate row (S6m) checks each piece and the absence of the
other host's text.

2. ON THE aarch64 LINUX CLI `_arch` IS NOT "THE ARCH THIS BUILD EMITS" (TL-2). `_arch` stays ARCH_X86 there by design:
it selects the compiler binary (`current_cc()`, cycc vs cycc_aarch64) and the run path, and flipping it globally breaks
those. A question about the emitted arch goes through a helper that knows the host (`arch_name()`,
`_dep_arch_is_a64()`, under `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` + `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`), never `_arch` directly — a dep's
`target` is now resolved for the host's own arch. The crossos row reads the expected answer from `uname` at run time,
not from the same `#ifdef`, so the check does not share the fix's defect.

3. A SOURCE SCANNER READING A DECLARATION LINE BY LINE MUST BOUND ITS SPANS BY THE BUFFER (TL-3). `cmd_header` cut a
wrapped parameter list at the newline (`_hdr_close(.., le, ..)` returns its limit when nothing closes before it), while
its return-type reader was already buffer-bounded — so the return class came out right and the parameters wrong, which
hid the bug. Once a span crosses lines, every walker inside it treats LF / CR / tab as blanks: the leading skip, the
trailing trim, the `...` test. The empty test is "blanks alone", not `pe == ps`, or a wrapped `fn e0(` / `)` prints
`e0()`, a C non-prototype declaration; the same test fixed a one-line `( )`.

4. A CHILD WITH CLEANUP OF ITS OWN IS FORWARDED THE SIGNAL, AND A FORWARDED WAIT NEEDS A BOUND (TL-6). git removes its
`index.lock` and a partial clone directory on SIGTERM; a SIGKILL (fwd 0) leaves both. So `_git_run` and the clone fork
use `_cbt_fork_tracked(1)`. But `_cbt_sig_stop` then blocks in waitpid with no limit, and HUP / INT / TERM are in the
handler's sa_mask, so a second Ctrl-C cannot cut it short: a git that ignores TERM held the CLI 7.75 s (filed; closed
in 6.7.15 by IT-11 and 6.7.15's D49 — fwd 2: a WNOHANG grace period of 2 s, then SIGKILL, infratools_mode_pairs_key_
tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715 #8). And a scratch NAME reused across calls (`git_err`, one per process) needs a
de-duplicating track, or the 32-slot table fills — `_cbt_scratch_track` now skips a pointer it holds.

5. RECORD BEFORE CREATE MUST NAME THE SIBLING TEMP BEFORE IT EXISTS, AND ONLY THE TEMP IT CREATED (TL-7). lib/io.cyr's
`_io_tmp_name` is predictable (pid + counter), so `_dep_scr_next_tmp` calls it and puts `_io_atomic_ctr` back, naming
the temp the next `_aw_open(dst)` creates. Traps met: a mkdir-p walk must record only the prefixes AT OR UNDER the
scratch root, or the handler's rmdir removes an empty `$TMPDIR` (the no-root-filter mutation removed the temp base and
every later run failed to start); when `_aw_open` skips a taken name, APPENDING the real temp leaves the prediction
recorded, so the kill unlinks a file the CLI never created (filed; closed in 6.7.15 by IT-12 not with the planned slot
overwrite but by holding the signals across create-to-record, so only names this process created are ever recorded —
infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715 #8); a new caller of the
predictable name generator trips `atomic_temp_exclusive.sh` axis 5 by design — allowlist it only when it opens nothing;
and a FIFO held write-only to stall a copy flaked under load, because the resolver opens a source three times
(file_exists, `_file_size`, the copy) — hold it read-write.

6. A POSIX sh CANNOT RESET A SIGNAL IGNORE IT INHERITED AT ENTRY (TL-5). `trap - HUP` does nothing for an ignore
inherited at entry, so under `nohup` every row that signals a child reads the inherited ignore — and the CLI keeps it,
by contract. A gate that sends SIGHUP reads the disposition it inherited (SigIgn bit 0 of /proc/self/status) and resets
it in a helper before exec: a cyrius launcher compiled into the gate's mktemp dir, `signal_default(1)`, then execve
with the same pid and environment. The helper needs a premise row of its own: under an ignore a self-SIGHUP shell
lives (rc 0), under the helper it dies (rc 129). (Closes the last sentence of tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_
passes_v6713 #8.)

7. TURNING OFF ONE READER OF A PROJECT'S PIN SURFACES THE NEXT (TL-8). With the CLI's version redirect off
(`CYRIUS_RESOLVED=1`), cycc's `_check_cyml_pin_drift` read the same cwd cyrius.cyml and put a line-1 drift warning on
the loose file. "This file is in no project" has to switch off EVERY reader of the cwd's pin (`CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT=1`
too), and drop inherited entries of both names, because each reader takes the first. A probe met at the docs pass:
with the pin INSTALLED, 6.7.13's server ran the PINNED CLI on a loose file — the redirect changes WHICH toolchain checks
the file, not only whether an error shows.

Into their classes (no new item): tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_passes_v6713 #1 (the per-host choice, now
also the CLI's refusals and the deps arch axis), #3 (record, then create — now also the resolver's scratch), #8 (a
gate's census reads shell text: `gates_never_write_tree.sh` axis 6 read the new test's `"/tmp/csk."` literal as a fixed
name, so spell it `"/tmp/"` + `"csk."`). TL-4 (`sock_send_a` / `sock_recv_a` declared `: Result`, callers
byte-identical) needs no item. User-facing: language/tooling.cyml cyrius_cli, compiler_env_vars, lsp_and_plugins;
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml cyrius_header_return_types_and_agnos_hosts_v6711;
field_notes/language/shell_runtime.cyml a_signalled_cyrius_dies_by_the_signal_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (TL-1 ..
TL-8).

[[entries]]
name = "pe_errno_stubs_region_declarations_and_the_ctl_signature_hash_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's platform lane (PL-1 .. PL-11, PL-3r: Windows read / write / seek / open failures as errnos, the whole corpus on cass, arm64 macOS mkdirat / fchmodat, the native-region warning's included files and constants, `_win_widen` removed, cyrius-watch through `cyrius build`, the aarch64 tarball's bins, the disallowed CTL's 48-byte entries): an inline table on a reroute every syscall site emits multiplies by the site count — emit it once, register the placeholder, assert it drained; the count is not the answer, the BOOL is; a folded constant keeps no trace of its spelling, so a declaration judgement needs a side record and one forward pass; two region rules again, now on purpose; a gate's model of the compat rows can be narrower than the rows; a CTL identifier's length follows the certificate's own signature digest. Extended with 6.7.15's platform lane (PL-7 .. PL-10, PL-14; #4, #6, #7): a declared constant's region rule follows the include NAME, a fn-local const needs its own declaration record; a reroute that returns a BOOL must fold it, every fd-taking reroute maps the std fds, and a register wine keeps is not one Win64 preserves."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "windows", "pe", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "native region", "tls", "ctl", "EPE_ERRNO_STUBS", "_wpjs_add_raw", "_pew_stubs_drained", "ReadFile", "WriteFile", "SetFilePointerEx", "_vdecl_base", "SVDECL", "syscall_peer_kernel_agreement", "a64_call", "CERT_SIGNATURE_HASH", "v6.7.15", "CloseHandle", "GetStdHandle", "_pe_fd_to_handle_rcx", "_pe_w32_errno_site", "_esx_rg_decl", "PP_FMARK", "_lc_decl"]
---
1. AN INLINE TABLE ON A REROUTE EVERY SYSCALL SITE EMITS MULTIPLIES BY THE SITE COUNT (PL-1). The planned per-site
cmp/je GetLastError table was about 230 B a site: +156,672 B (+6.4 %) on `cycc_win.exe` (about 670 reroutes) and +9 %
on a 130 KB probe. Emit it ONCE, after EEXIT (`EPE_ERRNO_STUBS`, every x86 driver, beside `EMACHO_SYSX_STUBS`); each
site becomes a 13-byte `call .` placeholder: +13,824 B. Register the placeholder in the whole-program disp32 registry
(`_wpjs_add_raw`): the per-fn NOP compaction moves code after it is emitted, so a site offset recorded any other way
goes stale (any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…, the 10th's rule). Make FIXUP assert every placeholder was
patched (`_pew_stubs_drained`): a driver that forgets the stub call then fails at compile time, by name, instead of
shipping a `call .` that calls itself until the stack faults.

2. THE COUNT IS NOT THE ANSWER (PL-1, PL-2). ReadFile / WriteFile zero `*lpNumberOfBytes` BEFORE any error check, and
SetFilePointerEx leaves `*lpNewFilePointer` untouched on failure, so a reroute that returns the count or the pre-zeroed
slot reads every failure as 0: end of file, 0 bytes written, a seek to the start. Only the BOOL tells failure from
success. ReadFile's FALSE with ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE (109: the writer closed) or ERROR_HANDLE_EOF (38) IS end of file and
must stay 0 — `type x | cycc.exe` and `process_win`'s drains end that way, and cycc.exe's own stdin goes through this
emitter, so a mis-mapped EOF breaks the Windows self-host. ERROR_MORE_DATA (234) is a delivered read and keeps its
count.

3. A FOLDED CONSTANT KEEPS NO TRACE OF ITS SPELLING (PL-6). ESCPOPS sees only the value, so "was this number written
inside a native region?" needs a side record at registration: `_vdecl_base` / `SVDECL`, the name token's offset per var
slot, alloc()'d at the var-table cap like `_vecp_base`. Judge declarations as ONE forward pass in slot (= declaration)
order on a second region-scanner instance, cached per slot: querying per call site restarts the scan for every
out-of-order constant, quadratic on a stdlib build (6.7.12's lesson again — native_region_rules_and_the_macho_fold_
report_v6712 #2). The scanner became an INSTANCE (an alloc()'d state block) so the call-site and declaration scans do
not share a cursor.

4. TWO REGION RULES AGAIN, NOW ON PURPOSE (PL-6, D61). Call sites use the INHERITED region (the user's ruling: the
region carries through include). Declarations use the FILE-LOCAL one: the stdlib peers declare their x86-numbered
`SYS_*` at their own top level, inside `lib/syscalls.cyr`'s aarch64 region, and judging them by the inherited region
warned on `SYS_FSYNC` (mutation f). The cost: a consumer's per-arch file included under the guard that declares `enum {
NR_X = 39; }` at its top level is the peers' shape and stayed silent (filed: it needed a rule); a fn-local `const`
lives in `_lc_tab`, which recorded no declaration token, so it was not judged (filed). Supersedes item 1 of
native_region_rules_and_the_macho_fold_report_v6712. BOTH CLOSED IN 6.7.15 (PL-9, PL-10; 6.7.15's D39 "yes, for files
outside lib/"): (a) A DECLARED CONSTANT'S RULE DEPENDS ON THE INCLUDE NAME AS WRITTEN. `_esx_rg_decl` judges a file
under lib/ (its include-stack top's `#@file` name starts `lib/` or has a `/lib/` component) by the file-local rule and
any other file by the carried region. The `#@file` name is the include string as written (`PP_FMARK(fname)`), and the
store-slot fallback, the overlay and the `#@incdir` retry all keep it — so "under lib/" is a property of how the file
was INCLUDED, not of where it sits on disk; `vendor/lib/` counts, and a test of a leading `lib/` alone would miss it
(the mutation that does is RED). (b) A FN-LOCAL CONST IS NOT A VAR SLOT: `_esx_const_slot` answered -1 for a `_lc_find`
hit. A parallel `_lc_decl` (u32 per entry, `alloc()`'d beside `_lc_tab`) records the name token's offset, leaving the
48-byte stride sites untouched. An `#inline` replay re-parses the body's own tokens (`STI(S, GFBS(S, fi))`), so a
replayed local const records its declaration in the inline body, never the call site — the region test is right for
free; the side effect is that a warning in an inline body prints once for the definition and once per replay (filed,
6.7.16, cosmetic).

5. A GATE'S MODEL OF THE COMPAT ROWS CAN BE NARROWER THAN THE ROWS (PL-6). `syscall_peer_kernel_agreement.sh` rule (b)
assumed every compat row keeps the call's name. The two arg-extending rows do not: poll 7 -> ppoll 73 (ms ->
timespec) and epoll_wait 232 -> epoll_pwait 22 (a NULL sigmask). The gate blocked the decided respelling until
`a64_call()` learned exactly those two pairs.

6. A CTL IDENTIFIER'S LENGTH FOLLOWS THE CERTIFICATE'S OWN SIGNATURE DIGEST (PL-10, D57). Windows' disallowed CTL holds
public-key MD5s (16 B) AND signature hashes (prop 15: the tbsCertificate hashed with the certificate's OWN signature
digest — 20 / 32 / 48 / 64 B); the six 48-byte entries are the SHA-384-signed DarkMatter intermediates, applied since
6.7.14 (tls_the_obvious_reading_of_a_protocol_field_was_wrong_seven_times #14; the other lengths were filed, Low,
latent — and applied in 6.7.15, PL-14: the 20, 32 and 64-byte lists, the policy 64 -> 112 B; today's CTL has none of
them). How an unknown identifier was pinned from public data in ~20 minutes: methodology.cyml identify_an_unknown_hash_
by_hashing_a_public_corpus_in_every_form_v6714.

7. 6.7.15 — THE REROUTE CLASS AGAIN: A BOOL, THE STD FDS, AND A REGISTER WINE HAPPENS TO KEEP (PL-7, PL-8; 6.7.15's
D55). (a) A KERNEL32 REROUTE THAT RETURNS A BOOL MUST FOLD IT. CloseHandle's 1 / 0 read as success / success under
the POSIX `< 0` test, so every `sys_close(..) < 0` on Windows was dead code and `file_close_r` was `Ok(1)` / `Ok(0)`,
never `Err`; read, write, open, seek and close now all go through the one GetLastError table stub
(`_pe_w32_errno_site`, item 1's emit-once table). (b) EVERY fd-TAKING PE REROUTE MAPS fd 0 / 1 / 2 THROUGH
GetStdHandle (`_pe_fd_to_handle_rcx`) — the seek missed it until 6.7.15 (it seeked the raw value 1 as a HANDLE);
close and SetEndOfFile (ftruncate) still pass the raw values (filed, 6.7.16). A new fd-taking reroute starts from the
map. (c) WIN64 DOES NOT PROMISE r9 ACROSS A CALL; WINE'S GetStdHandle HAPPENS TO KEEP IT. Dropping the whence reload
after the GetStdHandle call passed under wine — only the objdump axis (the reload must be there) caught it. A register
that survives a call under wine is not one the ABI preserves: pin the reload structurally. (d) WINE IS NOT WINDOWS,
AGAIN: `SetFilePointerEx` on a pipe or console handle SUCCEEDS on real Windows (the stream does not move;
GetLastError 0) and fails ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION under wine. A gate run under wine pins wine's answer; record the
hardware answer beside it (two_fields_of_a_foreign_api_constrain_each_other_and_the_emulator_does_not_enforce_it_6_6_6
is the class).

Into their classes (no new item): the errno-stub placeholder (PL-1) -> any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes…
(6.7.14); CREATE_NEW over a directory (PL-3) -> two_fields_of_a_foreign_api_constrain_each_other_and_the_emulator_does_
not_enforce_it_6_6_6 (the sixth); a row that passes through the bug beside it (PL-2) -> a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_
nothing… (6.7.14 (b)); mkdirat / fchmodat keep the dirfd (PL-5) -> a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64_is_not_the_
number_you_wrote #8 (closed); a public name hands a raw number to every program's enum (PL-6) and a program's raw
`SYS_OPEN` on aarch64 (PL-9) -> the same entry #10, #11; a test whose every arm can SKIP (PL-5, 08a190069) ->
a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6 (6.7.14 (a)); the aarch64
drivers skip unknown flags (PL-9) -> unhandled_flag_falls_through_to_compile_stdin_6_6_6; the cross-OS leg's hosts
(PL-4, PL-11) -> a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus; `wait` on a killed child
returns 143 under `bash -e` (PL-8, 812c304e5) -> methodology.cyml a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_pipefail_v6714.
User-facing: language/stdlib_modules.cyml file_locking, tls_module_contract_v6614; language/features.cyml
aarch64_xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers_and_native_region_exemption_v6612; language/tooling.cyml
audit_test_check_pipeline, helper_scripts; field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml
raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence,
windows_native_tls_trusts_what_the_chain_engine_trusts_minus_fetching_v6712. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (PL-1 .. PL-11, PL-3r).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_shared_readers_and_inline_str_copies_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's structs lane (ST-1 .. ST-16, ST-7r, ST-7r2: the float literal under IR=3, top-level `mk().n = 5;`, the for-step store, a program's own `Result`, `n + &x`, `**pp`, struct values into other pointer kinds, a `*V` if branch, `*(&s) OP=`, `return &q;`, inline `Str` copies and headers, `var H: Str = gt;`): a refusal that does not latch the panic flag lets the statement form add a second error; every statement form has a for-step twin, armed by token; two classifiers for one type name must agree on declaration-before-name; a site skipped as 'no consumer of this rule' needs a row proving it; a type that lives only in the parsed step word is invisible to token readers; a new refusal in the shared argument tail must push exactly as the fallback does; note the operand's type before a load replaces it; removing a decision's carve-out is not a one-line deletion; `&` of a name is two things; a copy-operand exclusion by name is per pair; a declaration's annotation must reach the hook after the right side; two lanes adding arms to one dispatch merge as both, and floors are re-derived; check.sh dying at 141 can be the regression library."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "structs", "Str", "_refuse_toplevel_struct_call", "_tsc_n", "_step_call_field", "_cpf_step", "_classify_return_type", "_classify_param_type", "_hl_rcarry", "_hl_amp_var", "_hl_dref_psid", "_pp_sid", "_sarg_sval_ptr", "_dvd_note", "_dst_cop_err", "_ret_slot_amp", "_agc_plain_big", "_istr_hop", "_istr_pdecl", "_var_rhs_post", "regression_pipe_to_bin", "SIGPIPE"]
---
1. A REFUSAL THAT DOES NOT LATCH `_panic` LETS THE STATEMENT FORM ADD A SECOND ERROR (ST-2, ST-3).
`_refuse_toplevel_struct_call` reported and returned with the cursor still on `=`, so the statement then reported "the
result is a temporary" too. Fix: a COUNTER in the refusal (`_tsc_n`), read by every caller around its parse, rather than
a flag in one caller; the method form and the for step read the same counter. Step over the WHOLE rest of the
statement: stopping only before `=` left `mkp().p[0] = 5;` with a false "cannot subscript field". Still double-reported:
`o.mk().p[0] = 5;`, whose second error was filed as written inside PARSE_FIELD_LOAD's chain — closed in 6.7.15 (ST-19,
ST-20), where the second error turned out to come from `_stmt_qmark` -> `_stmt_call_sub` and `_sub_post`, not from
PARSE_FIELD_LOAD (forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715 #12).

2. EVERY STATEMENT FORM HAS A FOR-STEP TWIN, AND A STEP IS ARMED BY TOKEN, NEVER BY A TERMINATOR GLOBAL (ST-4). 6.7.5
makes every assignment a statement takes a step, so 6.7.13's `gp().n = v` statement store needed `_step_call_field`;
its terminator is the for's `)` (term 11), not `;`. TRAP 1: a global "current terminator" (the brief's `_cpf_term`)
leaks into a nested statement inside the step's own call — a closure body `{ gp().m = 2; .. }`; arming with the step's
first token (`_cpf_step`) cannot leak. TRAP 2: disarming on any store kills the step after an inner store (mutant RED
7, `st_nested`). PARSE_FOR and `_PARSE_STMT_IMPL` sit at cybs's per-fn reference ceiling, so new code goes in helpers.

3. TWO CLASSIFIERS FOR ONE TYPE NAME MUST AGREE ON DECLARATION-BEFORE-NAME (ST-5). `_classify_return_type` looked the
struct up first (FINDSTRUCT); `_classify_param_type` matched the NAME `Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` as the stdlib's
handle — so a program's own `struct Result` was a struct as a return and a one-word handle as a parameter. TRAP: the
classifier has many readers (`_param_wants_struct_ptr`, `_psid_plain`, `_agc_plain_big`, `_local_is_sptr_param`, impl
`self` typing, the masks); fixing it changes assignment, return and by-value copy semantics for that struct, a HANDLE of
it included, not only the filed parameter — a heap handle passed by value became a copy where it had aliased. Probe
every reader's shape, not only the filed one. (FINDSTRUCT in the classifier is still cybs-safe: no global refs.)

4. A SITE SKIPPED AS "NO CONSUMER OF THIS RULE" NEEDS A ROW PROVING IT (ST-6). 6.7.13's brief listed `_hl_rcarry` among
the `_hl_dpsid` routes, and it was skipped as "not a dereference consumer". It is `_hl_p1`'s right-carry, and asked only
`_hl_psid`, which answers 0 for `&x` — so `bs(n + &x)` with a narrow `n` passed the address as the struct. Fix:
`_hl_amp_var` (a variable's `&x`, never a fn's `&f`). Mutation lesson: only the narrow-LOCAL rows go RED with the fix
reverted (14 of 102) — parameters, captures and top level pass before too, because a narrow local is what `_hl_p1`'s
value test reads.

5. A TYPE THAT LIVES ONLY IN THE PARSED STEP WORD IS INVISIBLE TO TOKEN READERS (ST-7, ST-7r, ST-7r2). 6.7.13's D9 (a)
typed `*pp` through the step word GPSC AFTER the parse; `_hl_psid` types an operand from its TOKENS, before or without
parsing it, so every struct-value consumer read `**pp` as a word. Fix: mirror the pin in the token reader
(`_hl_dref_psid` / `_hl_pw` -> `_pp_sid`), including the field chain (`_hl_cw`) and a generic call under its binding
(`_gen_call_word`; `_gen_call_targs` factored out, byte-identical). Teaching the SHARED reader moved every consumer at
once — by decided precedents `**pp = u` copies, `**pp OP=` is refused (6.7.11's D4), a 16 B `{ **pp }` branch is refused
(6.7.13's D1), and `return *pp;` / `l = *pp + 0` see the struct. Review lesson: the guide first said `*pp` is a `*S`
"everywhere a typed pointer is read"; the whole-source copy arms (`_asg_dstar`, `_hl_dstar_t`) key on a different test
and still take the pointer word. Probe each consumer CLASS before writing "everywhere" (struct_values_one_dereference_
typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713 #1 is the operand-typer rule this extends).

6. A NEW REFUSAL IN THE SHARED ARGUMENT TAIL MUST PUSH EXACTLY AS THE FALLBACK DOES (ST-8). `_sarg_sval_ptr` parses the
argument (`_expr_struct_sid`, then `_ess_deref`) and emits PCMPE + EPUSHR, byte-identical to the caller's fallback
across the corpus, `lib/`, `programs/`, `benches/`, `fuzz/` and the compiler's sources. It reports without `_panic`, so
the parse stays in step and one compile reports every argument. `_dx_plain` (sid < 2048) excludes `Str` / tuple / dyn
(mutant without it: the `Str` field refused, RED 1). A GPSC test first written beside it was an equivalent mutant (no
shape reaches it) and was dropped.

7. WHEN A LOAD REPLACES THE OPERAND'S STEP WORD, NOTE THE OPERAND'S TYPE BEFORE THE LOAD (ST-9). `_deref_read` loads
lane 0 and sets the result's word, so the if-expression's typer saw an integer branch for a whole `*V`. `_dvd_note`
records the dereference's span and the code pointer after it (6.7.13's `_ppd_note` pattern); `_ie_struct_sid` matches a
whole `* E` branch against it, with nothing emitted since (`_dvd_whole`). The code-pointer test is defensive (a note
matches only its own span): no row kills it, and it is recorded so in the gate's ledger rather than given an invented
row.

8. REMOVING A DECISION'S CARVE-OUT IS NOT A ONE-LINE DELETION (ST-10, D17 (a)). 6.7.13 held `*(&s) OP= v` back with
`_hl_damp` in `_dst_struct`, pending D17. The triage's plan (delete the carve-out line) gives the `*p` words — "the
struct 's' points at … (write `*p = *p + b`)" — wrong for `*(&s)`, which IS s (mutant RED 11). `_dst_cop_err` gives the
struct-value words for the `&` arm. Also: the brief's row `(*(&s)) -= 1` never reaches the check, because no statement
starts with `(` ("unexpected '('", filed); the row is spelled `*((&s)) -= 1`. (Since 6.7.15's ST-15 / D19 a
parenthesised destination is one error that names the rule — "an assignment's destination is not parenthesised" — and
`(n + 1) = 7;`, `(f()) = 3;`, a tuple literal keep "unexpected '('".)

9. `&` OF A NAME IS TWO THINGS, AND THE REFUSAL MUST RUN BEFORE BOTH RETURN CLASSES' OWN (ST-11). An inline struct's
`&s` is the struct (6.7.13's D4 (a)); a handle's, a `*T`'s or an untyped `self`'s `&q` is its SLOT. `_ret_slot_amp`
(`_hl_name_ty` + `_hl_cp` + `_hl_amp_sid`) runs first in `_ret_small_ptr` AND `_ret_ptr_src`, so the pair class's "two
registers" and the retptr class's "bare local identifier" never fire on it; `_tn_once` reports a generic's instances
once. Wording trap: the advice "return q" is wrong for a pointer or handle of ANOTHER struct (`return pu;` is the
another-struct refusal).

10. A COPY-OPERAND EXCLUSION BY NAME IS PER PAIR (ST-12, ST-13). `_agc_plain_big` excludes `Str` / `Result` / `Option` /
`Tagged` by name, so a handle into a handle is a rebind; only an inline-`Str` DESTINATION takes the copy (`_istr_hop`).
Every source shape needs its own arm: a handle local / parameter / global (the aggregate copy); a `: Str` call
(`_istr_call`, `_try_struct_call_assign`'s class-0 arm); a method result or a `Str` field (`_istr_hcp`, the no-record
arm). Once the decision landed (D18 (c): an inline `Str` passes its header only where a type says `Str`; the stdlib's
`Str` methods typed `Str`), 6.7.13's interim review-fix marks (`_istr_bare` and the rest) were RETIRED, not kept beside
the new rule.

11. A DECLARATION'S ANNOTATION MUST REACH THE HOOK THAT RUNS AFTER THE RIGHT SIDE (ST-13). `_istr_pdecl` (`var p: *Str =
t;`) needed the annotation token; PARSE_VAR is at cybs's ceiling, so it passes `ann_ti` down through `_var_rhs_post`
(one argument) rather than adding body code. Without the SPSC the typed-pointer warning still fires (mutant e3, RED 4).
Byte-identity proof for a stdlib type change (`lib/str.cyr`'s five methods typed `Str`): build a program against the
file and against a copy with the annotations stripped, and compare (the CMP row).

12. TWO LANES ADDING ARMS TO ONE DISPATCH MERGE AS BOTH ARMS, AND FLOORS ARE RE-DERIVED FROM THE MERGED FILE (the wave-2
merge, d267050c4). `_try_struct_call_assign`'s class-0 arm took this lane's `_istr_call` (D20) AND the receives lane's
`_asa_call` (D27); `struct_value_codegen.sh` FLOORA 244 and `call_ptr_field_store.sh` FLOOR_ROWS 35 are the MERGED
files' counts, not either side's.

13. check.sh's DRIVER DYING AT EXIT 141 CAN BE `lib/regression.cyr`, NOT THE GATE (S1). `regression_pipe_to_bin` dies of
SIGPIPE when the child exits without reading its stdin — a race below 64 KB, deterministic above. Run 1 died in
`_monomorph_substrate_gate`, run 2 was clean. Filed (Medium,
issues/archived/2026-10-17-regression-pipe-to-bin-dies-of-sigpipe.md) and fixed first thing in 6.7.15 (W0-1, on main
before the lanes forked): `regression_pipe_write_all` runs its write with SIGPIPE ignored when the disposition is
SIG_DFL and puts it back after, so a child that exits unread no longer kills the CALLER; a caller's own ignore or
handler stands. Before 6.7.15, read a 141 as this before blaming a gate.

Into their classes (no new item): the float literal's unrecorded `movabs` (ST-1) -> any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_
emitted_bytes… (the 12th) and its wrong filed cause -> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times
(6.7.14 (a)); the zone deciding a global's shape in pass 1 (ST-14) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_
returns_v6711 #3; the new `_HTEMP` site (ST-12) -> struct_values_one_dereference_typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713 #9.
User-facing: language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, if_expression_v674, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617,
struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616, field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612; language/core.cyml
type_names_v6616; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13. CHANGELOG
[6.7.14] (ST-1 .. ST-16, ST-7r, ST-7r2).

[[entries]]
name = "receives_multi_value_capture_first_and_pass1_counts_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's receives + multi-value lane (RC-1 .. RC-22, RC-9r: capture writes beside a same-named global, refused annotations over struct results, top-level method chains, typed closure parameters, wrapped `Str` / f64 calls, nested literal elements, `callptr` field stores, a struct result into a scalar, known `&f` results, wrapped struct values, D14 at top level, the typed-pointer warning, untyped top-level struct bindings, `?`-only pair fns, `ret2` counts, tuple parameters, wrapped destructures and stores): 'not refused' is not 'say nothing'; two reporters on one path — the class test belongs where the callers converge; a shared end check is not yours to widen; a scale fix that resets every result erases a typed one; a declaration walk that steps over `( )` skips a for header; a builtin token has no name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "receives", "multi-value", "closures", "_rcv_ann_hit", "_rcv_ann_refd", "_tn_seen_at", "_method_chain", "_sc_pre", "_body_ends_in_return", "_ar_exits", "_call_scale_fix", "_icall_ps_beg", "_var_rhs_if64", "_asa_back", "_asa_for_hdr", "_tcap_callee_pr", "callptr", "ret2"]
---
1. "NOT REFUSED" IS NOT THE SAME AS "SAY NOTHING" (RC-2). Answering 0 from `_rcv_ann_hit` to silence the cascade after
"unknown type" let PARSE_VAR's receive infer the struct OVER the refused annotation, and the next use drew a new
cascade. The quiet answer is "refused, already reported" (`_rcv_ann_refd` -> `_tn_seen_at`), so the declaration parses
on as the first refusal left it.

2. TWO REPORTERS ON ONE PATH: THE CLASS TEST BELONGS WHERE THE CALLERS CONVERGE (RC-3). `_sc_pre` refuses a > 16 B
result at top level; `_method_chain`'s top-level arm refused it again (the 6.7.13 entry's "the method arm still does —
filed"). The known-callee chain had its own skip; the test belongs in the function all callers converge on
(`_method_chain`), not in one caller.

3. A SHARED END CHECK IS NOT YOURS TO WIDEN (RC-16). `_body_ends_in_return` serves the one-value proof too; the arity
count's end check — a final `ret2(..);`, an if / elif / else of exits — is its own (`_ar_exits`). Widening the shared
check also counted an if / else of `return (a, b);` arms with no `ret2` at all. (`ret2(a, b)` is two values to pass 1's
count since 6.7.14.)

4. A SCALE FIX THAT RESETS EVERY RESULT ERASES A TYPED ONE (RC-10). The factor's `_call_scale_fix` reset every
`fncallN` / `callptr` result's pointer scale to 0; a `: *T` callee's step must be recorded against the call token
(`_icall_ps_beg`) and kept. And `_var_rhs_post` took the peek's own type (fncall0's declared `: i64`) before the
callee's `: f64`: ask the callee first (`_var_rhs_if64`). A known `&f` callee's `: f64` and `: *T` results are the
direct call's.

5. A DECLARATION WALK THAT STEPS OVER `( )` SKIPS A FOR HEADER (RC-9r). D27's `_asa_back` stepped over a closed `( )`,
but a classic-for header's `var` is in scope in the loop body: at the body's `{`, ask whether the `)` before it closes a
`for (` and scan the init (`_asa_for_hdr`). Found by the review, not by the rows the bite wrote.

6. A BUILTIN TOKEN HAS NO NAME (RC-19). A refusal naming the callee printed TOKVAL of `callptr` (token 137) — garbage
from the name pool; name builtins explicitly (`_tcap_callee_pr`).

Into their classes (no new item): a write to a captured name with a same-named global, and the pre-scan's closure
grammar (RC-1, RC-4) -> generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711 #13 (closed); the zone's `*T` is
`gps` 0 again (RC-12) -> receives_post_parse_records_and_the_capture_prescan_v6713 #2; a zone global's type at the end
of pass 1, the pair flag's three sources and a brace-less closure's `?` (RC-14, RC-15, RC-22) -> two_passes_computing_
the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority (6.7.14 (b), (c)), closing tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_
passes_v6713 #2's filed High; peel in, close after (RC-7, RC-20) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_
v6711 #11; a whole-wrap peel that must stay linear (RC-21) -> tuples_anonymous_structs_and_the_multi_value_bridge_v677
#12; the gate harness's clobbered loop variable (RC-12) -> methodology.cyml a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_
pipefail_v6714. User-facing: language/features.cyml closures_v637_v638, ret2_rethi, tuples_v677,
struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676, struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711,
typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555; field_notes/language/
semantics_runtime.cyml multi_value_returns_since_6_7_13, struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13. CHANGELOG [6.7.14]
(RC-1 .. RC-22, RC-9r).

[[entries]]
name = "cx_guest_branch_reach_vm_bounds_and_the_lib_leaf_order_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's stdlib lane (LIB-1 .. LIB-10, LIB-7r: cx branches past 128 KiB, the TLS SHA-256 mirror, the ws tests' stand-ins, `epoll_event_set`, cx's raw mmaps, cxvm's scalar-only list, the guest-space break, the 1.2 hello residue, distlib's pinned stdlib, patra 1.16.4): a fixed-width patched offset needs a range check or a lowering — lower, don't refuse; a VM bound on a guest register is no bound; cycc's `lib/<leaf>.cyr` order lets the CLI's own store slot shadow an `#@incdir` copy."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "stdlib", "cx", "cxvm", "EPATCH", "jz", "jnz", "_cx_guest_brk", "_CX_MEM_SIZE", "_CX_STACK_RESERVE", "brk", "CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY", "#@incdir", "distlib"]
---
1. A FIXED-WIDTH PATCHED OFFSET NEEDS A RANGE CHECK OR A LOWERING (LIB-1). cx `jz` / `jnz` carry a signed 16-bit
offset (±128 KiB), and `EPATCH` wrote `rel & 0xFF` / `(rel >> 8) & 0xFF` with no check, so a body over 128 KiB compiled
at rc 0 and cxvm jumped to a wrapped offset — a negative-pc trap here, the wrong instructions when the wrap lands inside
the code. The class: 6.6.4's cx address past 64 KiB and the lexer's string-length pack were the same shape. LOWER,
DON'T REFUSE: the inverted short branch (`jz r0,+2` / `jnz r0,+2`) skips an unconditional 24-bit `jmp` — one more
instruction and no format change, because the VM's decode already reads `+2` as "skip the next instruction". The one
remaining narrow arm (fixed-size vector loops) keeps a by-name refusal, so the silent wrap cannot come back. Gate
lesson: give a "far" fixture a size FLOOR (`.cyx` past 132 KiB), so a body that shrinks cannot pass vacuously.

2. A VM BOUND ON A GUEST REGISTER IS NO BOUND (LIB-7r, found in review). cxvm's `_cx_guest_brk` bounded the break by the
live guest sp (r254 minus 1 MiB). But r254 is an ordinary guest register — `mov`, `add` and `movhi` write it; only `sub
sp` checks it — and the growth's zeroing loop wrote through `_cx_mem + z` unchecked. So a guest that raised sp past
8 MiB was granted a break past the segment, and cxvm zeroed its OWN heap past it (SIGSEGV at brk(1 GiB)). Bound every
VM-side write by the segment (`_CX_MEM_SIZE - _CX_STACK_RESERVE`), whatever a guest register says. The row asserts the
patch site's bytes (`50 01 00 00`, a `jmp +1`), patches one instruction (`movhi r254, 0x8000`) and expects the refusal,
so a stale patch site cannot pass silently.

3. cycc's `lib/<leaf>.cyr` RESOLUTION ORDER LETS THE CLI'S OWN SLOT SHADOW AN `#@incdir` COPY (LIB-9). cycc resolves
`lib/<leaf>.cyr` in this order: the overlay (CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY), the CWD, the store slot of cycc's OWN version, and
only then `#@incdir` (the entry's directory). distlib's mirror of the pinned snapshot sat where only `#@incdir` reached
it, so the CLI's own slot shadowed it, and the bundle self-check had no copy at all. A tool that must read a SPECIFIC
stdlib puts it in the overlay — and since the overlay outranks `./lib`, the mirror must exclude what `./lib` vendors, or
a project's own leaf is shadowed. The CLI strips an inherited CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY in `main()` (since 6.7.6), so only the
CLI's own overlay ever reaches a compile.

Into their classes (no new item): cxvm forwarding guest x86 numbers through its host compiler's renumbering (found,
filed; fixed in 6.7.15, PL-11 / PL-12) -> a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64_is_not_the_number_you_wrote #12; `mmap_stack` / `_fl_mmap`
beside the wrapper census (LIB-5) -> grep_the_shape_not_the_name_a_rule_applied_inline_once_is_the_tell_6_6_6; a lib/
scan with no `#ifdef` frame and an `exec` on a continuation line (LIB-7, the lane gate) -> tools_lane_host_compilers_
masks_and_dry_passes_v6713 #8; cxvm's guest-fault exit 1 (LIB-1) -> a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_
nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6 (6.7.14 (b)); the 1.2 ServerHello walk keyed to the buffered hello (LIB-8) ->
methodology.cyml read_what_you_sent_and_put_an_over_read_at_a_prot_none_page_v6712; the scalar-only allow list (LIB-6)
-> methodology.cyml an_allow_list_derived_from_one_corpus_regresses_the_next_v6714; the 128 KB static heap in every
`.cyx` (LIB-7) -> patterns.cyml cx_has_no_bss_a_static_buffer_costs_its_size_in_every_cyx_v6714. User-facing:
language/features.cyml cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612; language/stdlib_modules.cyml
tls_module_contract_v6614, epoll_event_readers_v6712; language/tooling.cyml
distlib_sidecar_is_what_the_compile_verify_proves_v6618. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (LIB-1 .. LIB-10, LIB-7r).

[[entries]]
name = "vector_values_win64_parity_inline_frames_and_post_parse_types_v6714"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.14's vectors lane (VC-1 .. VC-17, VC-4r: the `**S` pin table, Windows' value-form gate, wrapped arguments, captured vectors and `*V` dereferences into value-form parameters, `&w` on Windows, undefined-call refusals, `callptr` of another vector, typed-pointer warnings, `*V` into other kinds, method-result names, struct fields into `*V`, `return *p` / `var v = *p`, vector methods' destinations, `**T` inference, a struct named like a vector, the closure frame-size review fix): Win64 routes a value-form vector parameter through the struct mask, so every SIMD-mask argument rule needs its Win64 twin; Win64's own-call receives need PARSE_FNCALL's routing twin; a declaration whose type is known only after its initialiser is parsed watches one token and takes the type after; a new generic-carrier shape when the carrier bits are full is read lazily from the definition; a source file at the tools' read cap shapes where code lands."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "vectors", "SIMD", "Win64", "_sarg_pe_vform", "_sar_src", "_try_push_struct_addr_arg", "_pe_venter", "_pe_vroute", "_fnc_route", "_dvi_want", "_dvi_park", "_dvi_take", "GFGM", "_pp_tparam", "_gs_param", "cyrfmt", "cyrlint", "_cl_frame_bytes"]
---
1. WIN64 ROUTES A VALUE-FORM VECTOR PARAMETER THROUGH THE STRUCT MASK (VC-2). Win64 passes a value-form vector
parameter by address, so the prescan sets its struct-mask bit, not a SIMD mask code — and every argument check that
hangs off the SIMD mask (`_pm_simd` -> `_simd_arg_record` -> `_sar_src`) never ran on PE; the struct arms took the
argument. A vector of another class, a struct local, a struct call or `*ps`, and an expression led by a name (its VALUE
pushed as the address) all compiled on Windows only. Fix: one gate at the top of `_try_push_struct_addr_arg`,
`_sarg_pe_vform`, that takes exactly what `_sar_src` takes; every later source rule lands in BOTH readers (the wrap
`_sar_take` / `_spv_one`, the capture, the dereference, `&x`, the missing fn). RULE: a value-form argument rule without
its Win64 twin is a Windows-only acceptance.

2. WIN64'S OWN-CALL VECTOR RECEIVES NEED PARSE_FNCALL'S ROUTING TWIN (VC-6). `_try_vector_call_assign`, `_f2c` / `_f4c`,
`_vrcv_pe_call` and PARSE_RETURN's `_rc` arm emit the call themselves (the hidden result pointer), past PARSE_FNCALL, so
`_fnc_route`'s `_ptr` routing never ran: the six corpus files' `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` reached the VALUE form with
addresses. Fix: route there too (`_pe_venter` / `_pe_vroute`: a plain `name(&x, ..)` whose `<name>_ptr` returns the same
vector), so `lanes(&w)` into a value-form parameter is refused on Windows as elsewhere. This SUPERSEDES item 5 of
vector_values_receives_arguments_and_dereferences_v6713 (exempting `&x` was the workaround; routing is the fix). RULE:
any routing PARSE_FNCALL does (`_ptr` / `_str` / `_int`) needs its twin in every path that emits its own call.

3. A DECLARATION WHOSE TYPE IS KNOWN ONLY AFTER ITS INITIALISER IS PARSED (VC-13). `var v = *p;` is a vector only if the
operand's step word names one; tokens cannot decide it, and the emission cannot be rewound (a parse registers fixups,
temps and errors). Let the scalar path run unchanged and watch the one token (`_dvi_want`); the dereference parks its
pointer only when the operand turns out to be the type (`_dvi_park`), and the declaration takes it after the parse
(`_dvi_take`), nothing emitted since. The other case's code stays byte-identical by construction. (`return *p;` with `p:
*V` is the vector too. The new parked temporary moves `hidden_temp_census.sh`'s spec —
struct_values_one_dereference_typer_and_implicit_addresses_v6713 #9.)

4. A NEW GENERIC-CARRIER SHAPE WHEN THE CARRIER BITS ARE FULL (VC-15). GFGM's carrier bits are full (bits 8-23 the
direct carriers, 24-63 two struct-carrier fields). A new carrier shape is read LAZILY from the definition's tokens
(`_pp_tparam` reads `* * T`, as `_pvec_param` reads a `*V`) and consulted only where no recorded carrier exists, which
keeps every existing call's binding unchanged by construction: a generic's `pp: **T` called with no `<..>` binds T to
the struct a `**S` argument points through to. (The filing's root cause was wrong — a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_
is_not_evidence_seven_times, 6.7.14 (b).)

5. A SOURCE FILE AT THE TOOLS' READ CAP SHAPES WHERE CODE LANDS (VC-15, VC-16). `src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr` reached
1,054,367 B in a first cut, past cyrfmt / cyrlint's 1,052,672-byte read cap: both refuse the file, and
`tracked_sources_canonically_formatted.sh` requires every src file clean. The new helpers went to `parse_types.cyr` (fn
order matters to neither cycc nor cybs). A merge of other lanes' growth could cross the cap (filed, Low) — closed
first thing in 6.7.15 (W0-2, 6.7.15's D25 A): `parse_fn.cyr` (1,049,325 B, 3,347 B under the cap) was split, its
top-level scans and `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL` moved unchanged to `parse_fn_def.cyr`, included right after it — the token
stream is the same, so every compiler built byte-identical (patterns.cyml byte_identity_through_a_driver_refactor_needs_
the_blocks_own_positions_v6715); a headroom row keeps every swept `.cyr` outside lib/ 64 KB under the cap.

Into their classes (no new item): an inline replay gives its argument temporaries back, and a closure's frame ignored
the high-water (VC-4, VC-5, VC-4r) -> inline_replay_indirect_call_spill_below_the_frame_v6713 lesson (1), with the open
half; a dereference judged by its tokens in argument position (VC-10) -> vector_values_receives_arguments_and_
dereferences_v6713 #9; a classifier both passes call (VC-16, D41 A) -> one_reading_for_both_passes_and_for_every_list_
walker_v677 #7a; the pin field's width (VC-1) -> typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678 #1; the PASS
line's backticks (VC-17) -> a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing… (6.7.14 (c)). User-facing: language/features.cyml
typed_simd_value_type_abi, overload_dispatch_pointer_vs_value, closures_v637_v638, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617,
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, struct_argument_type_checked_v673, slice_type_v58x; field_notes/language/
semantics_runtime.cyml vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (VC-1 .. VC-17,
VC-4r).

[[entries]]
name = "type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's generics lane (GN-1 .. GN-25, GN-12r, GN-15r, GN-20r, GN-25r: the nested `>>`-then-`,` High, `dyn` and non-generic type arguments, a `*T` local / field in an instance, a late field's type arguments, the `**` return pin in pass 1, trait member signatures and the impl's return, a Vec element's bound, scalar / wrapped / token-scan pointer binding, stub carriers, the bounded-forward memo, `x.m<..>(` in every lookahead, warnings and direct writers once, generic-method names, a `: T` word instance into a struct local): an owed close belongs to the enclosing list and a re-parse must carry it; a `*T` re-read inside an instance needs the instance's binding; a declaration-time check of a late field reads without sizing; the trait-member reader is a second signature grammar; compare types as annotations read them; a check-only read never reaches where a bound is judged; read a step word's kind rather than widen a test; a shared step is documented for every kind it reads; the token scans carry their own copy of the binding rule; memoise a judgement over cycles only on a clean answer; a new call spelling must reach the lookaheads; latch warnings and direct writers per (token, identity); premise-check a name helper; a declaration's reset is decided by the fn the call reaches."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "generics", "_ta_pending_gt", "_ta_comma", "_instantiate_generic_struct", "_ptr_ann_sid_tp", "_fld_pb_note", "_ta_late", "_ret_ptr_unres", "_trs_type", "_ty_norm_eq", "_targ_chk", "_vbnd_judge", "_ic_pscalar", "_pwrap_in", "_tok_gen_tp_sid", "_bbm_put", "_bbf_none", "_gm_gen", "_rd_enter", "_mcall_open", "_warn_seen", "_derr_seen", "_err_quiet", "_gen_msp", "_gen_decl_reset"]
---
1. AN OWED CLOSE BELONGS TO THE ENCLOSING LIST, AND A RE-PARSE MUST CARRY IT (GN-1, one of the slot's two Highs). The
lexer folds a nested list's closes into one `>>` / `>>>`; the reader pays the inner `>` and owes the enclosing list the
rest (`_ta_pending_gt`), so a `,` after it is the ENCLOSING list's. 6.3.33 – 6.7.14 read it as the inner one's, on every
target: `g2<P<P<R>>, i16>` minted a `P` with i16 as its second argument, left B unbound and took the i64 binding with no
diagnostic (8 for 2), `sizeof(Two<P<P<R>>, i16>)` was 24 for 18, and `fn f(a: Box<Box<Pt>>, b: i64)` was "unknown type
'b' as a type argument". Test for a second argument only while nothing is owed (`_ta_comma`), at EVERY list reader (ten
of them). And a nested re-parse — an instance's PARSE_STRUCT_DEF — resets that state: `_instantiate_generic_struct`
saves and restores it, or the answer depends on whether the inner instance was MINTED or FOUND. So the rows must cover
both orders: the half-2 mutant stayed green on every row (A, S2, V, D3, O, C) whose explicit call's lookahead minted the
inner instance first, and went RED on the 35 that did not.

2. A `*T` RE-READ INSIDE AN INSTANCE MUST BE READ WITH THE INSTANCE'S BINDING (GN-5, GN-6). `_ptr_ann_sid` /
`_ptr_tok_sid` look up a struct literally named `T`; the parameter path, the `: *T` return (6.7.11) and the step were
binding-aware, but a `var t: *T` local and a generic struct's `p: *T` field were not — one `*T` was T's receiver in one
position and a plain pointer in the next. Use `_ptr_ann_sid_tp`. A late field also needs the binding recorded per pool
entry (`_fld_pb_note`), because the use re-reads the definition's tokens under its own binding.

3. A DECLARATION-TIME CHECK OF A LATE FIELD READS WITHOUT SIZING (GN-7, 6.7.15's D8). A late `*S` field (6.6.17) records
T's token and is sized at each use, so its type arguments were checked only where read. Checking them at the declaration
must not mint (the field pool is contiguous), must take a struct declared further down as a NAME (`_ta_late`) or it
breaks the mutual-pointer case the late form exists for, and can leave the use's re-read to the per-token latch (same
token, same identity: one line).

4. PASS 1 MUST NOT READ WHAT ONLY PASS 2 KNOWS — A SECOND READER OF THE SAME RETURN (GN-8). 6.7.14 gated the one-star
pointee read; the `**` pin read went through another path (`_ek_pin` -> `_pp_psid` -> `_gen_ann_sid`) with no `quiet`.
One predicate (`_ret_ptr_unres`) now gates both: two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority
(6.7.15 (a)). The forward-call fault it exposed (`**mk()` above `mk`, 139) was filed by this lane and resolved in the
same release by the structs lane's signature re-read
(forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715 #1).

5. THE TRAIT-MEMBER READER IS A SECOND SIGNATURE GRAMMAR (GN-9 .. GN-12; 6.7.15's D3, D4, D7, D9). `_trs_type` sits
beside PARSE_FN_DEF: every rule a fn signature gains (type arguments read at the definition, the return vocabulary — `:
Vec`, `: u128`) must be mirrored there, or the trait accepts what no impl can write. Two traps: a `*fn(..): u128` type's
own return is read at the reader's level 0 with no parameter name, so a "level-0 u128" test refuses a fn pointer's
return — the check belongs in the member's own-return arm; and 6.7.0 – 6.7.14 compared an impl to its trait by parameter
COUNT while dyn formation (6.7.8) compared types, so a mismatched return was an error only where a dyn was formed — a
bounded generic caller took the wrong register class silently.

6. COMPARE TYPES AS ANNOTATIONS READ THEM, NOT TOKEN BY TOKEN (GN-12r). `Box<Box<i64>>` is one `>>` token, `Box<Box<i64>
>` two; `Box<i64>` IS `Box` (6.6.10). A token comparison refuses one type spelled two ways: expand the closers and fold
all-i64 lists before comparing (`_ty_norm_eq`). The dyn pair check still compares tokens (filed, 6.7.16) — a check
shared in spirit but not in code drifts.

7. A CHECK-ONLY READ NEVER REACHES WHERE A BOUND IS JUDGED (GN-13, 6.7.15's D10). `_targ_chk` (used where nothing may be
minted) never calls `_instantiate_generic_struct`, which is where `_bnd_check_struct` runs; judge the bound beside the
read, minting nothing (`_vbnd_judge`). And the element's own list is read from its `<` by `_vec_el_args`, so the ELEMENT
struct is judged by the caller — the nested arm the triage named never sees it.

8. A TYPED POINTER'S STEP WORD CARRIES ITS POINTEE'S KIND — READ IT, DON'T WIDEN AN UNRELATED TEST (GN-14, 6.7.15's
D11). The scalar binding of `p: *T` / `pp: **T` rides `_ic_pscalar` on the new pointer answers only; widening
`_gen_infer_tp`'s `c == -9` test to every narrow scalar would have changed `g(f())` for a `fn f(): i32`, which calls the
base with `_gh_inf0` -4 on purpose.

9. A STEP THAT APPLIES TO EVERY ARGUMENT IS DOCUMENTED FOR EVERY ARGUMENT (GN-15, GN-15r, 6.7.15's D12). `_pwrap_in` in
`_ic_arg` peels a wrap from scalars as well as structs: `g((h))` with `h: f64` changed instance (7 -> 3). The lane's
text said "struct"; the review caught it. When a reader is shared, its description names every kind it now reads.

10. THE TOKEN SCANS CARRY THEIR OWN COPY OF THE BINDING RULE (GN-16; closes
generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_per_token_errors_v6714 #10's filed half). `_tok_gen_tp_sid` read a pointer
argument the same way through `p: *T` and `x: T`; it must take the carrying parameter's shape (`_ic_ptr_param`) and the
same wrap rule, or it makes FALSE stub verdicts — a refusal naming a stub method the parser never calls at i64. Answer 0
for anything unmirrored: a missed flag, never a false one. (The vectors lane met the same rule for `p ± n`:
vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715 #10.)

11. MEMOISE A REACHABILITY JUDGEMENT OVER CYCLES ONLY ON A CLEAN ANSWER (GN-20, GN-20r). The bounded-forward judgement
carried a 64-deep cap (6.7.14's GN-14r) to stay sub-quadratic; it is memoised per generic instead (`_bbm_get` /
`_bbm_put`, an alloc()'d per-fn table). A "bad" verdict is a fact wherever found; a "not bad" computed while an ancestor
is still in progress (a cycle) may be wrong — keep it only when the whole judgement found nothing (`_bbf_none`), and tag
entries with the epoch of the facts they read (`_gm_gen`). Verified by a random-forwarding-graph differential against an
uncapped, unmemoised reference compiler (6,000 generated programs). Removing one cap leaves the OTHER bound: the
instance-nesting recursion (`_rd_enter`, 256) still refuses a CALLED chain at about 120 forwards — say so wherever the
cap's removal is described (GN-20r).

12. A NEW CALL SPELLING MUST REACH THE LOOKAHEADS (GN-21; closes 6.7.14's #9 filed half). 6.7.14 taught the method arm
`x.m<..>(`; 17 token-level lookaheads keyed on `x . m (` (destructure, pair `?`, vector receives, dyn sources, refusal
wording) did not learn it — the pair `?` took the boxed lowering and crashed. One helper (`_mcall_open`) for every site;
a token-level `<` must confirm a generic method of the receiver's struct with no field of that name, or `p.n < k >> (j)`
reads as a call.

13. LATCH WARNINGS AND DIRECT WRITERS PER (TOKEN, IDENTITY) TOO (GN-19, GN-22, GN-23; 6.7.14 #11's class, its filed half
closed). Warnings get their own (negative) identity band in the same table (`_warn_seen`); a direct `error:` writer
heads with `_derr_seen`, setting `_had_error` / `_panic` on a repeat as the print would. Key on the REPORTED token:
`_CHECK_ARITY` ran at three cursors for one call (the tail arm after `);`, the call, the inline replay after `)`), so it
keys on the call's `(`. A note after a swallowed error tests `_err_quiet`: four bound-contract refusals swallowed by
pass 1's latch printed four notes under one error (GN-19).

14. A NAME HELPER CAN NAME MORE SPELLINGS THAN YOU THINK — PREMISE-CHECK IT (GN-24). The brief said `_fn_src_name` keeps
a free spelling's mangled name; measured, it renames `S_two(..)` to `S.two` (6.7.14 pinned that). A flag set while a
METHOD spelling is judged (`_gen_msp`, cleared inside an instance's body) scopes the rename instead. Key a latch on the
fn's own name, never on the spelling's scratch (vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715 #7
is the mutation that shows why).

15. A DECLARATION'S RESET IS DECIDED BY WHAT THE REACHED FN RETURNS, NOT BY THE GENERIC'S SPELLING (GN-25, GN-25r;
6.7.15's D13 (a); revises generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_per_token_errors_v6714 #1). `_gen_decl_reset` keyed on
the declared `: T` shape dropped the annotation for a scalar instance; it now keys on GFRS of the instance the call
reaches (a struct by value: a receive; a word: a handle, as the non-generic call). Typing the local as the struct also
brings the struct checks with it (a pass to another struct's parameter is refused, as for `wid(5)`) — measure the code
that already compiled, not only the code that starts compiling.

Into their classes (no new item): the dyn cascade (GN-2) -> generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_per_token_errors_v6714
#4 (a refused type argument must not mint); the explicit all-i64 instance (GN-4) -> one rule for annotation and type
argument (6.6.10); the depth-0 carrier (GN-17) -> the 6.7.14 entry's #6 (the stub scan's forwarding test follows every
binding position); the `**` pin read in pass 1 (GN-8) ->
two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority (6.7.15 (a)); the brief's premise about
`_fn_src_name` (GN-24) and the lane's "struct" (GN-15r) ->
a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times (6.7.15 (d), (e)). User-facing: language/features.cyml
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617, traits_checked_v670, trait_bounds_v671,
pointer_fields_returns_and_linked_lists_v6617, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, dyn_v678; field_notes/language/
semantics_runtime.cyml generics_since_6_7_15. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (GN-1 .. GN-25, GN-12r, GN-15r, GN-20r, GN-25r).

[[entries]]
name = "frontend_redeclarations_baked_literals_and_refused_names_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's frontend lane (FE-1 .. FE-18: a literal before `*%` / `*|` / `*?` / `>>>`, `var b: = 0;`, an unclosed literal wrap, struct-literal commas, const fn bodies and const contexts, redeclared globals in the zone, baked struct / tuple / f64 initializers, declarations below module scope, uses of what a refused declaration names): a run-time fold's term-operator list must be the parser's; a step-one-token ladder eats the next grammar token, and the zone replay must read as pass 1 did; the zone replay resolves names after pass 1 has registered everything; a supersede rule must hold for every spelling the bake folds; a dropped startup store must take back everything its walk emitted; the const evaluator as a quiet probe; a latch carried between a const fn's two compiles hides or invents errors; an in-place const context hands the latch back only in sync; PARSE_PROG is the module, block and fn-body parser; a refusal that steps a declaration enters what it would have named; a shared store carries its separator rule to every caller."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "frontend", "diagnostics", "_cf_excl", "_cf_term_at", "PARSE_TERM", "_gvi_ann", "EMIT_GVAR_INITS", "_gvr_hide", "_gvk_chain", "_gvc_rec", "_gvs_mark", "_gvs_undo", "_gvs_later", "_ce_quiet", "_ce_fn_rtok", "_ce_name_said", "_cst_ctx_val", "_ce_ctx_open", "_ce_ctx_close", "PARSE_PROG", "_dl_quiet", "EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD_W", "_tup_elems"]
---
1. A RUN-TIME FOLD'S "IS THE NEXT TOKEN A TERM OPERATOR?" LIST MUST BE THE PARSER'S (FE-1). The `+ - & | ^` arms of
`_PEXPR_IMPL` fold a literal into an armed constant left operand when the next token is not a term operator, and
`_cf_excl` listed only `* / % << >>`. `*%` `*|` `*?` (6.7.3's wrapping / saturating / checked) and `>>>` (6.7.5) joined
PARSE_TERM later, so `2 + 3 *% 4` folded `2 + 3`, stepped the `3`, and the expression loop met an operator it has no arm
for: "expected ';', got '*%'" on every compiler from 6.6.0 through 6.7.14. The `-` arm hid it in the filed control only
because `2 - 3` goes under zero and takes the run-time path. A table that classifies tokens by tier must be derived from
(or gated against) the parser's own tier — a new operator joins one and silently misses the other
(two_sites_that_rederive_one_mapping_drift_record_it_where_it_is_decided). Not the `_cfo` rewind class: nothing was
rewound, the fold simply took too little.

2. A STEP-ONE-TOKEN LADDER EATS THE NEXT GRAMMAR TOKEN, AND THE ZONE REPLAY MUST READ AS PASS 1 DID (FE-6, 6.7.14's D15
(a)). The annotation ladders step one token after the `:` whatever it is, so `var b: = 0;` took the `=` as the type and
reported "expected '='" at the `0`; worse, in the declaration zone `var c: ; var d = 2;` took the `;` and COMPILED with
c holding d's initializer and d never declared. A head reader that refuses leaves the cursor where the ladder's step
lands it ON the token (step back one), and the zone REPLAY (`_gvi_ann`) reads the same way pass 1 did — two readers of
one declaration that disagree by one token give a silent miscompile, not an error. 6.7.14's D15 (a) had closed the same
ladder for non-type tokens only.

3. THE ZONE REPLAY RESOLVES NAMES AFTER PASS 1 HAS REGISTERED EVERYTHING (FE-12). EMIT_GVAR_INITS runs after pass 1, and
FINDVAR's last match sees declarations that come LATER in the source — including a refused one, which keeps its own
registration (its replay stores there). A reader between two declarations of a name read the refused second one and was
refused again. Hiding the refused registration from EVERY reader made the code after it cascade the other way (`G + 1`
on the struct G: undefined `P_add`); hide it only while the entries declared BEFORE it replay (`_gvr_hide`), so each
reader sees what the source says at its place.

4. A SUPERSEDE RULE MUST HOLD FOR EVERY SPELLING THE BAKE FOLDS (FE-13, FE-14; 6.7.15's D2 (a)). Pass 1 folds no names
(`_cf_enum` is on only at the replay), so a const- or enum-named initializer was a deferred entry baked by `_gvk_pre`,
while a literal was a pass-1 static init: the two spellings of one constant ordered differently, and neither side of a
named pair could be compared. A second walk at the replay (`_gvk_chain`) applies pass 1's supersede bits to every
integer fold and compares the pairs pass 1 could not (`_gvc_rec` records which, in declaration order, so every other
pair keeps its pass-1 stderr position). Where a rule is applied in two passes, check what each pass can SEE.

5. A DROPPED STARTUP STORE MUST TAKE BACK EVERYTHING ITS WALK EMITTED (FE-15, 6.7.14's D11 (b)). A baked struct / tuple
literal's element walk still has to run (it gives the elements' diagnostics), so the code it emitted is undone
afterwards (`_gvs_mark` / `_gvs_undo`): the code position, the fixups (a stale one patches later code — SIGSEGV /
SIGILL, 48 rows RED in the mutation that skips them), the IR nodes and the current block's count, the jump-source
records, the five peephole trackers, the push depth and the constant folder's arming. A new piece of per-emit state that
a constant's walk can touch belongs in that list. And "no earlier writer" is not enough to drop a literal's stores: a
LATER literal of other bytes bakes over the image, so the earlier one keeps its stores for a read between them
(`_gvs_later`).

6. THE CONST EVALUATOR AS A QUIET PROBE (FE-16, 6.7.15's D46). To ask "is this initializer a constant f64?" without its
refusals: a token pre-scan first, so a run-time initializer never reaches the evaluator; then evaluate at the TOP
context with reports suppressed (`_ce_quiet`: no print, no `_had_error`, no latch, no recorded token); and refuse a NaN
(its bits are the target's) rather than bake it. Keep out of the pre-scan anything whose replay WARNS (an integer
operand in f64 arithmetic): baking it lets the drop rule remove the replay and its warning.

7. A CONST FN BODY IS COMPILED TWICE, AND A LATCH CARRIED BETWEEN THE TWO HIDES OR INVENTS ERRORS (FE-10; closes
frontend_cascade_latches_direct_reports_and_the_bake_v6714 #5's filed leak). 6.7.14 kept the definition check's `_panic`
into the run-time body, deduping its first statement by accident; the latched resync then skipped the next statement
whole (a false "undefined variable 'k'"). The check now hands the body the caller's latch and only the token and the
name it refused (`_ce_fn_rtok`, `_ce_name_said`) — the body stands down at exactly those.

8. AN IN-PLACE CONST CONTEXT HANDS THE LATCH BACK ONLY IN SYNC (FE-11; closes the 6.7.14 entry's #1 filed half). Pass
1's declaration loop never resyncs, so a refusal latched in an enum value, an array size or an `#assert` swallowed the
next declaration's error (and `_cst_ctx_val` memoises a refused value, so pass 2 never re-reported it). Evaluate with
the latch clear (`_ce_ctx_open`) and put the caller's back (`_ce_ctx_close`) only when the refused expression's extent —
by the evaluator's grammar, else where it stopped — ends at the context's own `,` / `;` / `}` / `]`; a malformed one
keeps the latch.

9. PARSE_PROG IS THE MODULE, BLOCK AND FN-BODY PARSER (FE-17). A rule keyed on module scope (`_pp_depth == 1`, GINFN 0)
leaves the same keyword one level down to the statement parser's generic "unexpected X" and its resync, which re-enters
the declaration's braces and can take the enclosing body's `}` ("return outside a fn body"). A below-module-scope arm
must step the declaration without stepping a `}` that closes the enclosing body.

10. A REFUSAL THAT STEPS A DECLARATION MUST ENTER WHAT IT WOULD HAVE NAMED, AS REPORTED (FE-18). Its fns (6.7.14's
`_dl_quiet`), its struct / union / enum names and members, a payload constructor, and the variables a refused type
annotates — else each use is a second error. Key the stand-down on the exact names, and on a variable by name within its
fn (a `.field` arm knows the variable, not its type), so an unrelated undefined name is still reported. (The receives
lane met its width: receives_known_callee_records_assignment_judge_and_zone_sweep_v6715 #4.)

11. A SHARED STORE CARRIES ITS SEPARATOR RULE TO EVERY CALLER (FE-8, 6.7.15's D1). EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD_W serves positional
and named struct literals, the zone replay AND tuple literals (`_tup_elems`): its new comma rule needed a `)` exception
for a tuple's last element, and a commaless tuple now reads the struct-literal message (filed, 6.7.16). Grep a helper's
callers before giving it a grammar rule.

Into their classes (no new item): the zone replay as a second parser (FE-6, FE-12) ->
struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #9; a rule applied in two passes (FE-13, FE-14) ->
two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority; the zone replay's latch printing only a
program's first error (filed, 6.7.16) -> the vectors lane's #6
(vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715). User-facing: field_notes/language/
parser_syntax.cyml struct_literal_elements_need_commas_v6715; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
a_zone_redeclaration_is_its_last_constant_from_program_start_v6715; language/features.cyml const_and_const_fn_v672,
relaxed_fn_ordering. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (FE-1 .. FE-18).

[[entries]]
name = "infratools_mode_pairs_key_tables_asm_operands_and_the_linear_seed_pass_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's infra / tools lane (IT-1 .. IT-12, IT-9r: IR=3's fold over a dead store, the IR=3 + DCE corpus, cybs's last-fn locals and include-once list, include-once's whole spelling, x86 system-instruction operands, aarch64 barriers and their option, the DCE seed pass, a forwarded git's grace period, a scratch resolve's records): one mode's corpus leg is not the pair's; a qword store copying a string writes 7 bytes past it; a fixed-width key table must refuse or store whole; an emitter that steps over its operand unread encodes its default register; qemu-user does not validate system-instruction words; an optional operand must not eat the next mnemonic; ask the question the caller asks — 'whether', not 'which'; forward a signal only to a process that promises to exit on it, and record what you made, not what you expect to make."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "infra", "tools", "CYRIUS_IR", "CYRIUS_DCE", "ir3_dce_corpus", "cybs", "pp_mark", "movb", "PP_MARK_INCLUDED", "PP_ALREADY_INCLUDED", "include-once", "ASM_MNEMONIC", "lgdt", "mov crN", "_asm_x86_reg", "dmb", "dsb", "wfe", "_asm_a64_barrier_word", "llvm-mc", "_dce_host_fn", "_dce_in_any_fn", "_dce_cover_merge", "self_compile", "_cbt_sig_stop", "_cbt_sig_hold", "_aw_open"]
---
1. ONE MODE'S CORPUS LEG IS NOT THE PAIR'S (IT-2). A class of defect shows only with two opt-in flags together: the IR=3
seam compacts code before FIXUP reads parse-time positions, and only `CYRIUS_DCE=1` acts on what FIXUP concludes; under
IR=3 alone a stale table moves only the `N unreachable fns` note, which no gate compared. So 6.7.14's `_wp_fnr_shift`
defect (IN-6r: compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not) passed every gate and that lane's full
check.sh. A corpus gate per mode PAIR (`ir3_dce_corpus.sh`: every `.tcyr` under `CYRIUS_IR=3 CYRIUS_DCE=1` must exit 0),
with an expected-failure list that can only shrink, is the fix — it is EMPTY at 6.7.15. Its anti-vacuous plant has to be
the COMPILER (a shim that miscompiles only under both flags): no predefine shows `CYRIUS_IR` / `CYRIUS_DCE` to a source.
(The IR=3 fold that faulted, IT-1, is the emitted-bytes class's 14th: any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes….)

2. A QWORD STORE COPYING A STRING WRITES 7 BYTES PAST IT (IT-4). cybs's hand assembly copied bytes with `mov [r], rax`
(8 bytes, the byte zero-extended), so every copy and NUL write spilled 7 bytes. Inside a table that is overwritten later
it is invisible; at the table's END it lands in whatever follows — here depth 0's include name buffer, so the 256th
distinct include with a 249 – 255 character path had its own name zeroed and was skipped silently (the program exited 0
where it sets 42). Use `movb` for byte copies into fixed-width slots: the refusal's promised width (255 characters fit)
is only true once the LAST slot holds it too.

3. A FIXED-WIDTH KEY TABLE MUST REFUSE OR STORE WHOLE — A TRUNCATING STORE PAIRED WITH A WHOLE-KEY COMPARE NEVER MATCHES
(IT-5, IT-4). cycc's include-once entries kept 127 bytes and the lookup compared the whole spelling, so a spelling of
128+ characters (easy through directories or an absolute deps path) never matched its own entry and was included again —
its top-level statements run twice, its fns redefined, silently, on every target since v4.8.4-alpha2. cybs's 256 B list
had the same unchecked width. cycc now stores a POINTER to the whole spelling (`alloc(n + 1)`, the same band at `S +
0x1C0000`: no layout change, no two-step); cybs, which has no allocator to spare, refuses past the width by name.
`PP_ALREADY_INCLUDED` is the historical regalloc victim: prove a change to it with the compiler sources compiled
byte-identical (the differential). The cap-class view of it:
a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer (the fourteenth).

4. AN EMITTER THAT STEPS OVER ITS OPERAND UNREAD ENCODES ITS DEFAULT REGISTER (IT-6, 6.7.14's D16; closes
ir_arena_dce_ranges_and_the_instruction_aligned_call_graph_v6714 #4's filed half). The 6.7.14 `in` / `out` find had five
more instances: `lgdt`, `lidt`, `invlpg`, `mov crN`, `ltr` each skipped the operand token and emitted the rax / ax
encoding (`mov cr8, rax` wrote CR0; a two-digit crN wrapped into the reg field), and `int` masked the vector to 8 bits.
Every operand slot needs a reader that refuses what it cannot encode, and register names are matched WHOLE (through the
NUL), or `raxx` / `r8d` pass as rax / r8 — `_asm_x86_reg` still does that for `param_load` (filed, 6.7.16). REX: R for
the cr8 slot, B for r8 – r15 in rm; rsp / r12 need a SIB (0x24), rbp / r13 a disp8 0. Check every new encoding against
llvm-mc.

5. qemu-user DOES NOT VALIDATE SYSTEM-INSTRUCTION WORDS — DECODE EVERY HAND-ENCODED aarch64 WORD (IT-7). The bare `dsb`
was SSBB and `wfe` an MSR to a system register for as long as they existed, and the tests that EXECUTED them
(`inline_asm_fences.tcyr` under qemu and on pi) passed. A reserved barrier option executes as the full-system barrier,
so a wrong CRm is invisible at run time too. Only a decode (`llvm-mc --disassemble`, objdump) shows it; a gate that pins
the words is the only row that catches a regression (`asm_mnemonics_whole.sh` axes 6 / 9) — a tcyr that runs them
cannot.

6. AN OPTIONAL OPERAND MUST NOT EAT THE NEXT MNEMONIC (IT-8, 6.7.15's D37). `dmb` / `dsb` / `isb` on three lines (the
corpus's spelling) are three barriers, so a barrier takes the next token as its option only when it is an identifier
that is NOT a mnemonic. The mnemonic test reads the same lookups `ASM_MNEMONIC` dispatches through (`_asm_a64_word`,
`_asm_a64_barrier_word`, `param_load`), so the list has one source and a new mnemonic cannot become an "option".
Mutation: the option read without the test makes `dmb dsb isb` refuse 'dsb'.

7. ASK THE QUESTION THE CALLER ASKS — "WHETHER", NOT "WHICH" (IT-9). The DCE seed pass needed to know WHETHER a call
site lies inside any fn, but called `_dce_host_fn`, which answers WHICH fn — a scan of the whole fn table per E8 / E9
(BL / B) site, O(call sites × fns). "Which" (first match in index order, with nested generic-instance ranges) has no
cheap index; "whether" is the union of the ranges: pack `start << 32 | end`, sort, merge overlapping or touching ranges,
binary-search (`_dce_cover_build`, `_dce_in_any_fn`) — exact by construction, with the linear helper kept as the
fallback (alloc failure, an end ≥ 2^31) and for the two callers that need the host's index. 6.7.14's nested-instance
shape (IN-5) is what makes the merge keep the OUTER end: the gate's exactness row E, whose mutation (take each later
range's end) names a live call dead. The quadratic had cost EVERY build, DCE or not, because the `N unreachable fns`
note runs the walk: x86 FIXUP at 16,000 fns 9,934 -> 114 ms, cycc's own self_compile min 1,980 -> 1,278 ms (`prof:
fixup=` 820 -> 112), and the release's same-input A/B −34.6 % (6.7.14's sources: 1,765 -> 1,154 ms). One patch
dominated, so the CHANGELOG names it. Proven byte-identical by a differential of stdout + stderr + rc (the note and the
`dead:` list included, which scripts/differential.sh does not compare) over 763 inputs in 12 modes.

8. FORWARD A SIGNAL ONLY TO A PROCESS THAT PROMISES TO EXIT ON IT, AND RECORD WHAT YOU MADE (IT-11, IT-12; 6.7.15's D49;
closes tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_ignores_v6714 #4 and #5's filed halves). The CLI's
own forked copies exit on a forwarded signal (their handler always exits); an exec'd external program does not, and with
HUP / INT / TERM in sa_mask nothing else breaks the wait, not even a second Ctrl-C. fwd 2: send, poll `waitpid(WNOHANG)`
with an async-signal-safe sleep (nanosleep on Linux; `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` on Darwin) for 2 s, SIGKILL, reap. And a
predicted temp name is wrong exactly when the exclusive create skips it — the name it skipped is someone else's file.
Hold the signals across create-to-record instead (`_cbt_sig_hold` / `_cbt_sig_release`, one `_aw_open` long), so the
handler only ever sees names this process created; that also closes the window a slot overwrite would leave. Still open
(filed, 6.7.16): the record outlives the rename.

Into their classes (no new item): the IR=3 fold over a dead store (IT-1) ->
any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream (the 14th); cybs's last fn's locals and
its include-once list (IT-3, IT-4) -> cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt_the_cycc_fixpoint_does_not_substitute_for_seed_derive
(6.7.15); the gates that stop under `bash -eo pipefail` (IT-9r, IT-10) -> methodology.cyml
a_gate_green_under_sh_can_stop_under_pipefail_v6714; the commit text that called IT-9's gate green under pipefail
(IT-9r) -> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times (6.7.15 (e)). User-facing:
field_notes/language/diagnostics_caps.cyml cyrius_ir_3_is_still_opt_in_v6714; field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml
inline_asm_mnemonics_match_whole_and_out_writes_its_width_v6714; language/ tooling.cyml compiler_env_vars, cyrius_cli.
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (IT-1 .. IT-12, IT-9r).

[[entries]]
name = "forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's structs lane (ST-1 .. ST-20, ST-2r, ST-7r: a forward call into a fn whose signature names a struct declared below it, `*pp` whole-source copies, `**pp OP= v`, a generic's `pp: **Box<T>`, inline `Str` sources and destinations, `w.s.len()`, `*pu` of a `*u128`, `&` of a non-name, `return &` refusals, parenthesised destinations and reads, call-result statements, `cyrius lint` on a sibling module's call) and its wave-2 merge: a pass-1 fact keyed on FINDSTRUCT must be re-read once every struct is registered, and the re-read must be idempotent; a copy arm keys on the source's shape; a generic's parameter annotation is read with no binding for the base; a new destination class stays out of the parked-store path; a dereference argument is judged by the parameter; a handle field's method receiver is the field's value; the step word is the only reliable `*u128` signal; a refusal that does not return reads on; a generic's base body is also its i64 instance; registered is not defined; premise-check a filing's location; two lanes each changing one fn's arity is a semantic conflict only the merged build shows."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "structs", "Str", "pass 1", "CO-01", "_psig_note", "_psig_reread", "_psig_one", "_prescan_sig_facts", "FINDSTRUCT", "GFDS", "_impl_tnoff", "_asg_dstar", "_hl_dstar_t", "_ret_ptr_base_bind", "_istr_dst", "_agc_ovr", "_sarg_istr_dx", "_load_str_hop", "_deref_u128", "_ret_gen_base", "_pf_read_stmt", "_fn_nodef", "_method_chain", "_ret_sid_refuse", "merge", "semantic conflict"]
---
1. "DECLARED BELOW" IS NOT ONLY TEXTUAL, AND A PASS-1 FACT KEYED ON FINDSTRUCT MUST BE RE-READ (ST-1; 6.7.14's D41 A now
holds above the fn too). Pass 1 records a fn's ABI facts as it meets the fn (CO-01, 6.3.5). A struct registered later
leaves the record without it, and a call parsed before the fn's own pass-2 definition reads that record: a 16 / 24 B
by-value argument pushed as one word (139 on x86_64 and aarch64), a `: Pq` return with no hidden result pointer (139),
"pass &t for its address" refusals, a struct named `f32v4` read as the vector. And include-once makes "below" a matter
of include ORDER, not of one file: `include "lib/tls_native.cyr"` then `include "lib/str.cyr"` expands str.cyr at the
main file's position (bayan's own include of it is then skipped), so bayan's 96 fns naming `Str` are prescanned with no
`Str` registered — seven corpus programs changed codegen when the fix landed (21 register / stack swaps, all passing on
both compilers). The fix (`_psig_note`, `_psig_reread`, `_psig_one`) re-reads, once every struct is registered and
before the end-of-pass-1 sweeps, only a definition that is still its fn's record (`GFDS(fi) == ti + 1`) and whose
signature names a struct registered after its note, with the noted module / impl context restored — without the
`_impl_tnoff` restore `H_take(h, t)` faulted on every leg. THE TRAP IS IDEMPOTENCY: the re-read must not re-run the
one-time parts (the slot, the visibility stamp, the notes, the generic records), and every part it re-runs must give the
same facts twice — proved by a variant compiler that re-reads EVERY definition and compiles cycc and the 1,045-input
corpus byte-identically, stderr included. A pass-1 fact keyed on a TOKEN scan of struct names (`_cpt_vscan`) is
order-free by construction. Not covered: an `impl` header's type, so an untyped `self` above its struct (filed, 6.7.16).
Resolved as a side effect: the forward-call pointer-return twin the generics lane filed (`**mk()`, `*Box<Later>`,
`mk().x` — type_argument_lists_pointer_binding_and_once_per_site_diagnostics_v6715 #4).

2. A COPY ARM KEYS ON THE SOURCE'S SHAPE, SO EVERY NEW SPELLING OF A `*S` NEEDS ITS ARM TOLD (ST-2). 6.7.14 taught the
typed-pointer reader that `*pp` is a `*S`; the whole-source copy arms (`_asg_dstar`, `_hl_dstar_t`) still asked for a
struct POINTEE and the `*S`-name copy ran on a NAME only, so `l = *pp` stored the pointer and `var t: S = *pp;` bound a
handle. When a spelling becomes a `*S`, probe EVERY destination — local, global, `*q`, field, declaration, top level,
for step: the field destination and the top-level declaration were found this way and filed (6.7.16).

3. A GENERIC'S PARAMETER ANNOTATION IS READ WITH NO BINDING FOR THE BASE (ST-4). Only an instance binds before the
parameter loop, so a reader there that resolves a type argument (`_ek_pin` -> `_gen_ann_sid`) binds the base's T -> i64
itself, as the return reader does (`_ret_ptr_base_bind`) — `pp: **Box<T>` was refused for this alone. (The return
annotation's own place between PARSE_FN_DEF's two bindings: generic_names_type_arguments_and_once_per_token_errors_v6714
#2.)

4. A NEW DESTINATION CLASS STAYS OUT OF THE PARKED-STORE PATH (ST-6, ST-8; 6.7.15's D14, D16). `_istr_dst` answers 3 / 4
for a `*Str` local / global; `*p = <src>`'s parked destination (`_agc_ovr`) must never take them, and a `**Str` keeps 0
through its step word (16 bytes is the header, not the pointee). The `*Str` source copy reuses the same answers as copy
SOURCES.

5. A DEREFERENCE ARGUMENT IS JUDGED BY THE PARAMETER, BEFORE THE WORD READ HAPPENS (ST-7). `_sarg_istr_dx` runs first in
`_sarg_byval_small` (a cybs-ceiling fn: one helper call) and parses only E of `* E` into a `Str` parameter; a `**Str`
NAME is no `*Str` to `_hl_psid`, so `str_len(*pp)` keeps passing the `*Str` that `*pp` reads.

6. A HANDLE FIELD'S METHOD RECEIVER IS THE FIELD'S VALUE (ST-10). The inline-field method arm passes the field's ADDRESS
as `self`; for a `Str` field (a pointer slot) that made `self` the slot, so `w.s.len()` read the NEXT field (the mutant
gave 1). `_load_str_hop` loads the field's word and parks it as a `*Str` receiver.

7. THE STEP WORD IS THE ONLY RELIABLE `*u128` SIGNAL, AND A ROW'S DESCRIPTION CAN LIE (ST-11, 6.7.15's D20). `var p =
&b;` steps 1 (an untyped pointer), so `silent_values_checked.sh` U34, described as "a deref through a `*u128`", pinned
an untyped word read all along; `_deref_u128` keys on step 16 with element width 16.

8. A REFUSAL THAT DOES NOT RETURN READS ON WITH GARBAGE (ST-12). The `&` factor's ERR_EXPECT fell through to `PEEKV`,
and a `(` token's value 0 is name offset 0 — the source's first word — hence "undefined variable 'fn'" for `&(x)`.
(6.7.14's generics item 3 is the same read from the other side: a token's value is a name offset only for an
identifier.) In a cybs-ceiling fn the fix is one call swapped for one.

9. A GENERIC'S BASE BODY IS ALSO ITS T = i64 INSTANCE (ST-14, 6.7.15's D18). Refusing `return &n;` at the base refused a
valid program used only at `<S8>`; `_ret_gen_base` skips the base, which leaves `hg(3)` alone unjudged (filed, 6.7.16) —
the stub-base answer (`_gen_stub`) belongs to the generics machinery. And a return-class refusal that does not consult
the value's own refusal adds a second error beside it: the pair class's `return zz;` (filed), and — found at the
release-docs pass, not by the lane — `return &o;` of a dyn in an 8 B struct fn now prints the dyn's "not addressable"
AND this bite's "got `&o` (a `*dyn Show`)", where `8b4d44602` printed the first alone.

10. A STATEMENT JUDGED AFTER ITS PARSE NEEDS ITS OWN EXITS, AND SOME TESTS ARE ONLY DEFENSIVE (ST-16, 6.7.15's D60).
`_pf_read_stmt` refuses `(s).a;` only when the read parsed whole (GTI on the `;`), carries no struct operator's result
stamp (`_lsc_beg`) and has no latched refusal of its own; its panic test kills no row (the inner latch already silences
the error) — record such a test in the gate's ledger rather than invent a row.

11. "REGISTERED" IS NOT "DEFINED" (ST-18). An earlier plain call registers an undefined name (offset < 0, no owner), so
a `--syntax-only` test for "this file defines it" uses `_warn_undefined_prepass`'s test (`_fn_nodef`), as `cyrius
lint`'s `gp().n = 5;` on a sibling module's call needed; the full compile's `fi >= 0` for such a name is the filed "does
not return a struct" twin.

12. PREMISE-CHECK A FILING'S LOCATION (ST-19, ST-20; closes struct_values_shared_readers_and_inline_str_copies_v6714
#1's filed half). The top-level `o.mk().p[0] = 5;` second error was not "inside PARSE_FIELD_LOAD" as filed but in
`_stmt_method_call`'s `_stmt_qmark` -> `_stmt_call_sub` (the statement) and the factor's `_sub_post` (the value),
judging the `[` against a field never typed. Stopping the chain's walk in `_method_chain`'s top-level arm covers both
and every `_method_chain` caller; the free call's value twin (`var x = mk().p[0];`) was found beside it and repaired in
its own commit.

13. TWO LANES EACH CHANGING ONE FN'S CONTRACT IS A SEMANTIC CONFLICT ONLY THE MERGED BUILD SHOWS (the wave-2 merge,
77dade9d; 4862a462). The vectors lane gave `_ret_sid_refuse` an `fi` argument (a method result named as the source
spells it) while this lane added a NEW 5-argument call for `return &t` of an inline Str / tuple / dyn: git merged both
cleanly, and the merged tree did not compile (the new site passes `0 - 1`, a name, as the other name sites do). The
first build of the merged tree failed on it — and the empty output that failed build left was copied over `build/cycc`
by an unguarded build chain (restored from git before anything read it):
a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing_and_the_ways_it_happens_are_enumerable (6.7.15). Likewise rows written in one lane
quote messages another lane re-spells: the vectors lane's `_fn_src_name` names a real `impl O { fn mk }` method `O.mk`,
so this lane's `call_ptr_field_store.sh` T6 – T15 (written before the merge, expecting `O_mk`) went RED only in the
merged check.sh. RULE: after a merge, grep every caller of a fn either side changed the arity or meaning of — including
call sites the OTHER lane added — and every gate for a message either side re-spelled; build the merged tree through a
stage-checking script before anything reads its output. (The 6.7.14 shape — two lanes adding arms to one dispatch — is
the 6.7.14 entry's #12.)

Into their classes (no new item): the pass-1 record re-read (ST-1) ->
two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority (6.7.15 (c)); a filing's wrong location (ST-19)
-> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times (6.7.15 (a)); a parenthesised destination refused by
name (ST-15, 6.7.15's D19) closes struct_values_shared_readers_and_inline_str_copies_v6714 #8's "unexpected '('".
User-facing: field_notes/language/ semantics_runtime.cyml struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_15;
language/features.cyml typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711. CHANGELOG
[6.7.15] (ST-1 .. ST-20, ST-2r, ST-7r) and § Integration — the waves.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_receives_one_recogniser_inline_frames_and_pointer_typing_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's vectors lane (VC-1 .. VC-15, VC-1r, VC-6r: the inline-replay frame High, the replay's panic latch, the hashed pin intern, field-chain vector methods, wrapped / annotated / global / top-level vector receives, method-result and dereference-argument names, `*(&p)`, wrapped and computed pointer arguments into a generic, `***T`): every GFLC release must record the peak first; a replay of statements inside an expression must preserve the panic latch; one recogniser for every receive, or the spellings drift; 'no annotation' is `ann_ti == 0`, never width 0; a check keyed on an assignment's destination resolves it as the store does; a top-level declaration has two parsers; a dedupe key built from a scratch-written name is a scratch offset; two scratch writers in one message write apart; a dereference-typing rule needs both halves; the stub scan must read every shape the inference reads; a pointer-depth rule generalises along the pin chain; a hashed intern keeps first-come numbers."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "vectors", "SIMD", "inline replay", "#inline", "GFLC", "SFLC", "_flc_hwm", "_inl_flc_release", "_w128_tmp", "_HTEMP", "_panic", "_inl_replay_body", "_vcall_vt", "_vmc_open", "_mch_open", "ann_ti", "_vin_decl", "_vasg_gnv", "_err_ident_fn", "_fn_src_name", "_src_span_nm", "_amp_pw", "_hl_pw", "_tok_ta_arg", "_pp_is_tp", "_ic_pp_deep", "_pp_hix"]
---
1. EVERY GFLC RELEASE MUST RECORD THE PEAK FIRST (VC-1, one of the slot's two Highs; closes
inline_replay_indirect_call_spill_below_the_frame_v6713's open half). The inline replay reserves its parameter slots,
evaluates the arguments — which may take `_w128_tmp` or `_HTEMP` (`_dlo_park`) at GFLC and above — then releases GFLC
back to the parameters (`SFLC(S, saved_flc + _ptot)`). It recorded only the parameter slots in `_flc_hwm`, so the frame
ended below the temps, over the pushed left operand of `id(a) + id(a + 1) + 2` (84 for 83 on x86_64, aarch64 and PE; the
closure twin 43 / 84; cx right). `_inl_flc_release` records GFLC in `_flc_hwm` BEFORE lowering it — a helper, because
PARSE_FNCALL sits at cybs's per-fn reference ceiling. This was the last site that lowered GFLC without recording the
peak: 6.7.13's INT-1 (`_icall_slot`, a slot that did not raise the high-water) and 6.7.14's VC-4r (`_cl_frame_bytes`, a
sizer that did not read it) were the other two halves. RULE: grep every `SFLC` that LOWERS the count for a high-water
line just before it. A row needs a TIGHT caller and BOTH frame parities — rounding the frame to 16 hides a one-slot
overhang at half of them, so every row has a one-extra-local twin (`tests/tcyr/crossos/inline_argument_temps.tcyr`, 17
rows; 13 of 17 failed on x86_64 before).

2. A REPLAY OF STATEMENTS INSIDE AN EXPRESSION MUST PRESERVE THE PANIC LATCH (VC-2; closes
struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #5's filed half). The `#inline` replay re-parses the callee's
body as statements, and each statement's end clears `_panic` (`_stmt_resync`). So a refusal in the caller's argument
stopped latching, and the next statement's refusal printed too on every target that replays — Windows makes a real call
for a vector parameter and printed one. `_inl_replay_body` saves `_panic` and sets it again if the caller had set it.
RULE: anything that runs PARSE_STMT inside an expression saves and restores the latch.

3. ONE RECOGNISER FOR EVERY RECEIVE, OR THE SPELLINGS DRIFT (VC-4, VC-5, VC-7, VC-8, VC-9; 6.7.15's D26 – D29). The
vector receives keyed a method on the TOKENS `x . m (`, and each receive tested its own subset of spellings: a chain
`w.s.mv()` fell to the scalar path (one word) or to a refusal naming the chain's head; the ACCEPTANCE check peeled
parentheses (6.7.11) while the MISMATCH check tested the unwrapped token, so a wrapped call of another vector got the
generic refusal; the untyped inference read no wrap for four releases after the annotated one did. `_vcall_vt(S, ti,
term, start)` answers "the vector the call at ti declares" for every spelling — by name, `g<T>`, a method on a name or a
field chain (`_vmc_open` -> `_mch_open`), `fncallN(&f)` / `callptr(&f)` — for a whole source or one STARTING it, wrapped
or not, and every receive asks it. A new receive or receiver spelling extends `_vcall_vt` / `_vmc_open`, never a site. A
lookahead that now runs on every scalar assignment must not resolve what the parse resolves later (`_vmc_vfi` asks the
base's declared return before it would instantiate a generic method), and one walking new shapes must leave side outputs
(`_cap_fld_sid`) as it found them. Do NOT widen `_tok_method_open` / `_pair_callee_at`: the pair `?` machinery types no
chain on purpose.

4. "NO ANNOTATION" IS `ann_ti == 0`, NEVER `scalar_type == 0` (VC-6, VC-6r). The decoded width is 0 for every annotation
that is not an integer — f64, f32, bool, cstring, an enum, Vec, Result, Option — so a test on the width read them all as
unannotated, and `var x: f64 = mkv(..)` silently declared a vector local labelled f64. The review found the lane's own
newly-refused list incomplete (`Vec` / `Result` / `Option` unnamed). RULE: enumerate EVERY annotation that decodes to
the same width before writing the "newly refused" list.

5. A CHECK KEYED ON AN ASSIGNMENT'S DESTINATION RESOLVES IT AS THE STORE DOES (VC-8). `_try_vector_call_assign` and
6.7.14's D38 arm asked FINDLOCAL alone, so a global destination fell through to the 8-byte store, and every vector
refusal skipped it. FINDLOCAL, then `_findvar_core` (`_vasg_gnv`), with a vector global left to its own rule. A local's
`IDENT (` that is no by-name vector call — `fncall2(` is an IDENT — was also returned unjudged.

6. A TOP-LEVEL DECLARATION HAS TWO PARSERS (VC-9). PARSE_VAR's global arm runs after the first statement; the
declaration zone's replay (EMIT_GVAR_INITS -> `_gvi_expr`) runs before it; the in-fn checks sat under `GINFN == 1`.
Every initialiser rule needs an arm in both zones, and rows in both. The replay knows every fn and pass 1 does not: a
callee declared below the global is known only to the replay. The zone replay's latch prints only a program's first
error (filed by the frontend lane, 6.7.16). Moving PARSE_VAR's vector block into `_vin_decl` was also cybs headroom:
PARSE_VAR and PARSE_FNCALL sit at cybs's per-fn reference ceiling, so new logic there goes into a helper.

7. A DEDUPE KEY BUILT FROM A SCRATCH-WRITTEN NAME IS A SCRATCH OFFSET (VC-10). `_fn_src_name` writes at the pool's
uncommitted scratch (GNPOS), so every name it writes has the same offset. Key the report on the record's own name and
print the spelling (`_err_ident_fn`). When the spelling IS the name (a free fn `Pt_add`), the second key equals the
first and the report swallowed itself (rc 1, no error printed: the DNK1 mutation). The rename also moved rows in seven
other gates; one (`call_result_field.sh`) went red only in the full check.sh, and the structs lane's
`call_ptr_field_store.sh` T6 – T15 only on the merged tree — grep every gate for the old spelling of a renamed message,
across lanes, before the merge (forward_call_signatures_dereference_copies_and_inline_str_destinations_v6715 #13).

8. TWO SCRATCH WRITERS IN ONE MESSAGE MUST WRITE APART (VC-12). `_fn_src_name` owns [GNPOS, GNPOS + 961) and
`_src_span_nm` owns [GNPOS + 1024, + 1985). A method callee and a rendered argument share one ERR_IDENT: written at the
same place, the callee's `Tk.take` overwrote the argument (the OKN6 mutation). A token's source text is GTOFF up to the
next token's GTOFF, cut at a space or `#` (`_rcv_ann_pr` reads an annotation the same way).

9. A DEREFERENCE-TYPING RULE NEEDS BOTH HALVES (VC-13, 6.7.15's D21). The VALUE side: `_deref_read`'s step word
(`_amp_pw`: `*(& NAME)` takes NAME's word), read by every vector and narrow consumer through `_pk_word`. The TOKEN side:
`_hl_psid` / `_hl_pw` / `_hl_dref_psid`, read by the field, struct-return and operator paths before anything is parsed.
With only the value side, `**(&p)` was the vector but `(*(&ps)).y` stayed refused (the third mutation). The
compound-assignment path is a third reader that did not follow (`*(&pi) += 1`, filed): enumerate every consumer of a
dereference's type, including `OP=`. (6.7.14's structs #5 is the same split from the other side: a type that lives only
in the parsed step word is invisible to token readers.)

10. THE STUB SCAN MUST READ EVERY SHAPE THE PARSER'S INFERENCE READS (VC-14, 6.7.15's D22 / D23). A generic's pointer
inference reads an argument as the typed-pointer readers type it: a wrap, `p ± n`, `n + p` (`_hl_psid` / `_hl_pw`). The
pass-1 stub scan (`_tok_ta_arg`, from the tokens, before any local exists) must read the same shapes, or the parser
binds P where the scan answered 0 — a forwarding generic's i64 base becomes a stub, and valid source is refused
("generic 'P.pick' has no i64 instance"). `_gz_split`'s loosest operator decides whether a whole argument IS arithmetic:
`p + 1 & 3` is a bitwise result and binds nothing. The `**T` rung is still missing from the scan (filed by the generics
lane, 6.7.16).

11. A POINTER-DEPTH RULE GENERALISES ALONG THE PIN CHAIN (VC-15, 6.7.15's D24). `_pp_is_tp` read exactly `* * T`. Any
depth d >= 2 reads `*` d times (`_ppt_d`), and the argument's pin chain is walked d − 2 pointees down (`_ic_pp_deep`,
`_pp_inner` per star), because each `**`-or-deeper pin's pointee word carries its own pin. `_gen_infer_ind` must read d
BEFORE the argument's inference can re-enter and overwrite it.

12. A HASHED INTERN MUST KEEP FIRST-COME NUMBERS (VC-3; closes typed_subscripts_element_kinds_and_the_bounds_mode_v678
#1's filed linear search). Pins are baked into step words, so the open-addressing index (`_pp_hix`, `alloc()`'d at twice
`_pp_cap`, at most half full, rebuilt on grow) changes only the lookup, never the numbering — the proof is a
byte-identical corpus (657 `.tcyr`) and generator output. The hash folds each word's high half down, because a step
word's pin and kind sit in bits 40 – 61: hashing the low bits alone collides. 15,360 pins: 4,543 -> 1,333 ms.

Into their classes (no new item): the triage's counts stale after waves 0 / 1 (the filed repros printed all their lines
on the lane base, where the triage saw two) and the review's corrections of the lane's own bullets (VC-1r: a per-target
before-value; VC-6r: an incomplete newly-refused list) -> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times
(6.7.15 (c), (e)). User-facing: field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml vector_values_since_6_7_15; language/
features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, inline_directive_v6563, generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310. CHANGELOG
[6.7.15] (VC-1 .. VC-15, VC-1r, VC-6r).

[[entries]]
name = "receives_known_callee_records_assignment_judge_and_zone_sweep_v6715"
description = "Gotchas met building 6.7.15's receives lane (RC-1 .. RC-14, RC-13r, RC-14r, RC-5r: `ret2` counts past an endless loop, a wrapped tuple-field store, literal tuple arguments, a wrapped `: Str` call at top level, refused-annotation uses, string literals into `*T`, the mixed-return warning on impls and trait defaults, known `&f` bool and narrow `*T` results, struct results over 8 B into `*T` / untyped names, top-level untyped struct results, a zone generic instance): a token-keyed record answers only the readers that ask it; a proof by token search misses a builtin that is an exit; two reports on two tokens pass a per-token dedupe; a stand-down keyed on (fn, name) is wider than a slot, and a garbage id never reaches it; the zone sweep and the replay must agree, and a later walk can move a verdict; a per-global scan inside a per-global check is quadratic and taxes the old shapes too; minting a generic struct instance at the end of pass 1 is safe through pass 2's own maker; 'not judged' needs its own answer; a decided flip reddens every gate that pinned the old shape."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "receives", "multi-value", "fncallN", "callptr", "_icall_ps", "_sub_word", "_pk_icall", "_icall_fi", "_loop_exits_by_return", "_ret2_stmt", "_tn_once", "_tfc_rep", "_fsc_paren", "_ann_refd_mark", "_gu_sweep", "_gz_agree", "_gu_agps", "_gz_walk", "_gu_recheck", "_gu_find", "_inst_conc0", "_asa_ann", "globals_scale_linear"]
---
1. A TOKEN-KEYED RECORD ANSWERS ONLY THE READERS THAT ASK IT (RC-10, 6.7.15's D31). 6.7.14's D26 recorded a known `&f`
callee's step word against the fncallN / callptr token (`_icall_ps_beg` / `_icall_ps`), and the scale fix kept it, so
`fncall0(&gp) + 1` stepped right. But the element KIND is vouched elsewhere: `_pk_post` / `_sub_word` ask the DIRECT
callee's `_fn_rps` — fncallN's own (0) — and `callptr` (token 137) failed the direct-call test, so `*fncall0(&bp)` read
a word and `fncall0(&bp)[1]` was refused while `callptr(&bp)[1]` already read the byte. A fact recorded for one reader
(the step) looks complete until a second reader (the element) asks the old source: every reader of "the call's `*T`"
asks the record (`_sub_word`'s call arm, `_pk_icall`, `_sub_ret_sid` through `_icall_fi`).

2. A PROOF BY TOKEN SEARCH MISSES A BUILTIN THAT IS AN EXIT (RC-1; 6.7.14's D42, 6.7.15's D62). `_loop_exits_by_return`
proved a loop-ending body is left only through its exits by finding a `return` token and no `break`; `ret2(..);`
statements are exits with no `return` token, so the loop was "not provably left" and the count unrecorded (the third
name read 88). Count by statement shape (`_ret2_stmt`: `;` / `{` / `}` before, `);` after), in the nested helper too
(`_has_return_tok`). The same shape test misses a `ret2(..);` right after a `case N:` label (the token before is `:`;
filed, 6.7.16).

3. TWO REPORTS ON TWO TOKENS PASS A PER-TOKEN DEDUPE (RC-2). `_tn_once` dedupes per token; R17 is reported at the
wrapped call's token and R12 at the wrap's `(`, so both printed for `h.p = (two(3));`. The inner reporter notes that it
spoke (`_tfc_rep`) and the wrap marks its own token (`_fsc_paren`), as a literal already did. (The brief guessed the
inner returns 0 here; it returns 2 — mark on the note, not on the return value.)

4. A STAND-DOWN KEYED ON (fn, name) IS WIDER THAN A SLOT, AND A GARBAGE ID NEVER REACHES IT (RC-5, RC-5r). The frontend
lane's `_ann_refd_mark` / `_ann_refd_said` match by (fn, name), so a same-named untyped variable in a nested block has
its genuine error counted, not printed (filed). And the stand-down sits on the `sid == 0` arms: a top-level `var P:
*Foo` global takes a GARBAGE struct id instead (whatever an unrelated table row holds — with a first struct that has the
field, `P.a` adds no error at all), so its uses still report; standing down at one reporter would hide the garbage id at
reads only — the review narrowed the texts instead of patching (filed by the frontend lane). (The frontend side of the
same rule: frontend_redeclarations_baked_literals_and_refused_names_v6715 #10.)

5. THE ZONE SWEEP AND THE REPLAY MUST AGREE, AND A LATER WALK CAN MOVE A VERDICT (RC-13, 6.7.15's D34 / D35). A zone
global is typed at the end of pass 1 from tokens (`_gu_sweep` -> `_gu_wsid`, `_gz_ty`) because fn bodies read it before
its replay; the replay parses the line for real. `_gz_agree` checked annotated globals only — it must check an untyped
global the sweep made an inline struct too (`_gu_agps`). The handle-line walk can turn an operand into a handle after
the sweep (`var A: P1 = gp1(); var G = A + A;` — `A + A` becomes pointer arithmetic, G untyped), so walk and recheck
loop to a fixpoint (`_gz_walk`, `_gu_recheck`). A GENERIC method's generic struct base is not the instance the replay
makes: leave it to the replay (`_gz_mfi`; filed).

6. A PER-GLOBAL SCAN INSIDE A PER-GLOBAL CHECK IS QUADRATIC — AND IT TAXES THE OLD SHAPES TOO (RC-13r, the review's
must-fix; no released compiler had it). `_gu_agps` looked each global's note up with a scan of every note, so 16k
globals of 6.7.14's plain `var Gi = mk1(i);` went 137 -> 815 ms. Pass 1 pushes the notes in token order, so bisect
(`_gu_find`, with a scan fallback if the order ever breaks). Found by the review, not by the bite's rows:
`globals_scale_linear.sh` gained a `structcall` row (10,000 / 20,000 globals, ratio limit 3.0).

7. MINTING A GENERIC STRUCT INSTANCE AT THE END OF PASS 1 IS SAFE THROUGH PASS 2'S OWN MAKER (RC-14, RC-14r; closes
two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority 6.7.14 (c)'s filed half). The sweep reads the
type arguments as `_gen_resolve_call` / `_gen_infer_tp` do and reads the return with `_gtype_sid` under `_inst_conc0/1`
(set and put back), so `_instantiate_generic_struct`'s name dedup gives pass 2's call the same `G$..` — no second
struct. A bounded G is not minted (a bound would be judged at the sweep); tokens that bind nothing keep the old answer.

8. "NOT JUDGED" NEEDS ITS OWN ANSWER (RC-12, 6.7.15's D33). D33's untyped arm reads `_asa_ann` 0 as "untyped"; a name
that is no variable also answered 0 and would add a refusal on top of its "undefined variable" error. `_asa_ann` answers
−1 (not judged) for no variable and for a local the walk does not find.

9. A DECIDED FLIP REDDENS EVERY GATE THAT PINNED THE OLD SHAPE, NOT ONLY THE FILED ONE (D35, `d8c3a4d1c`). D35 named
SATK2; `struct_result_type_refused.sh` ZK2 and a codegen gate's probe (`stack_param_homing_matrix.sh`, "keeps the first
word and must NOT count") pinned the same word. Grep every gate for the decided shape before the unit's check.sh.

Into their classes (no new item): the zone global typed at the end of pass 1 (RC-13, RC-14) ->
two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority (6.7.15 (b)); peel the wrap, close after (RC-4's
`_pair_unwrap_k`) -> struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711 #11; a closure parameter reading an
earlier fn's slot type (filed from RC-12) -> receives_post_parse_records_and_the_capture_prescan_v6713; the brief's
guessed return value (RC-2) -> a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times (6.7.15 (b)). User-facing:
field_notes/language/ semantics_runtime.cyml receives_since_6_7_15; language/features.cyml closures_v637_v638,
ret2_rethi, tuples_v677. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (RC-1 .. RC-14, RC-13r, RC-14r, RC-5r).
